// C/P Practice Test flashcards: original cards that practice what the Chem/Phys questions of AAMC Practice Exams 1,
// 3, 4, 5 and 6 ask (the pe1-cp … pe6-cp cards of data/qb-aamc-fl.js), plus the cards (cpt-st-*) that twin the daily
// quiz's Sample Test and QBank-pack questions. TODAY's C/P flashcard step uses only these cards (masteryFlashPool in js/mastery.js).
// Same ask, same setup: `twinOf` names the question(s) each card mirrors, asked the same way with the same kind of
// choices; a passage question's card carries a short twin passage (`passage`, 3–6 sentences), a stand-alone's has none.
// `from` names the spec items (peX-cp:Q#n) the card teaches; `aamc`, `disc` and `skill` come from a source's spec line.
// Original wording, numbers and compounds only: no AAMC passage, question, number or answer choice is reproduced.
// Format: {id, s, disc, aamc, skill, from, twinOf, passage?, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, elim, rule, fast, exp, how, teach}
// (tools/test-cp-test-cards.cjs checks all of it).
const CP_TEST_CARDS = [
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-01a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:1#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:1"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"Which reference compound shown will travel the shortest distance from the origin?","choices":["Cyclohexanone","Tetrahydropyran","Hexane-1,5-diol","Octane"],"choiceFigs":["cyclohexanone","tetrahydropyran","hexane-1-5-diol","n-octane"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"An alkane has no polar group, so it stays with the nonpolar toluene and runs closest to the front."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone C=O can only accept H-bonds; with no O–H it binds the water less than a compound with donors."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An ether oxygen only accepts H-bonds and has no O–H to donate, so it is held only weakly."}],"rule":"On a polar stationary phase, count O–H/N–H donors: the most H-bonding compound moves least. Spot size reflects the amount loaded, not the distance.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Water-coated paper is a polar phase, so look for O–H groups. Only one compound has O–H donors (two of them); the ketone and ether only accept, and the alkane does neither."},"exp":"The bound water is a **polar stationary phase** and toluene is a nonpolar mobile phase. Whichever compound forms the **strongest hydrogen bonds** with the water lags most and travels the **shortest distance** (smallest Rf). Hexane-1,5-diol carries **a pair of O–H groups** that both donate and accept H-bonds; cyclohexanone and tetrahydropyran only accept, and octane does neither. Spot size only reflects how much was loaded.","how":{"ask":"Find the compound held most strongly by the polar stationary phase.","know":"• A polar stationary phase holds compounds that hydrogen-bond with it; they move least (small Rf).\n• O–H and N–H donate H-bonds; C=O and ether O only accept.\n• Spot size depends on the amount loaded, not on Rf.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says water bound to the cellulose is the stationary phase: a polar phase.","hl":"water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase"},{"text":"The alkane has no polar group at all, so it rides with toluene near the front.","strike":["Octane"]},{"text":"The ketone and the ether can only accept H-bonds; neither has an O–H.","strike":["Cyclohexanone","Tetrahydropyran"]},{"text":"Two O–H groups give the strongest hold on the water. Answer: hexane-1,5-diol."}],"trap":"Linking spot size to distance, or picking the ketone for its strong C=O dipole: on a water phase, O–H donors hold hardest."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase","note":"polar phase"},{"t":"nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase","note":"nonpolar mobile phase"},{"t":"hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water","note":"held back"},{"t":"the spots differed in size","note":"amount, not distance"}]},{"text":"**Rank H-bonding** with the bound water.","calc":["two O–H: donor and acceptor","C=O or ether O: acceptor only","alkane: neither"]},{"text":"**Most H-bonding = shortest distance = smallest Rf.**"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a polar stationary phase, the compound with the most O–H/N–H donors moves least; spot size only shows how much was loaded."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-02a","s":"Research Design & Reasoning","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:2#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:2"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"According to the passage, the team's conclusion requires that:","choices":["those reactants existed then.","the ester formed in high yield.","the products were optically pure.","heat speeds up every reaction."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Any yield, even a trace, shows the ester can form; the amount isn't what the conclusion rests on."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Optical purity isn't needed: the claim is only that condensation happened, in whatever form."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too sweeping: the argument concerns these reactions only, so a claim about every reaction isn't needed."}],"rule":"A required assumption bridges the lab result to the claim's real setting; details of the experiment's yield or purity don't.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Lab result = 'could happen'; 'did happen' needs the same inputs back then. Pick the choice saying the ingredients existed on early Earth; yield, purity and all-reactions claims are irrelevant."},"exp":"A lab result shows what **can** happen. To conclude it **did** happen on the early Earth, the **starting materials and conditions** (here N-acetylglycine, ethanol, acidic water and clay) must have existed then. Yield, purity and a claim about all reactions do not bear on that.","how":{"ask":"Find the unstated premise that must be true for the lab result to support the historical claim.","know":"• **Assumption**: a premise the conclusion needs but does not state; if it were false, the conclusion would fail.\n• A lab model of a past event supports the claim only if its **inputs and conditions** existed at that time.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the conclusion, about the primitive Earth; sentence 1 shows the evidence is a lab run.","hl":"such condensations took place on the primitive Earth"},{"text":"Test each choice: if it were false, would the argument fail?","calc":["reactants absent then → the lab run says nothing about then → fails","low yield → some ester still formed → stands"]},{"text":"Yield, optical purity and all reactions are not needed for the conclusion.","strike":["the ester formed in high yield.","the products were optically pure.","heat speeds up every reaction."]},{"text":"Answer: the reactants must have been present on the early Earth."}],"trap":"Choosing a detail about the experiment's quality; the gap is between the lab and the early Earth."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"on the primitive Earth","note":"the conclusion"},{"t":"a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol","note":"the evidence: a lab run"},{"t":"requires","note":"find the assumption"}]},{"text":"**Split conclusion from evidence.** The evidence is a lab result; the conclusion is about the past."},{"text":"**Bridge the gap.** For the lab pool to stand in for the early Earth, the same ingredients had to be there."},{"text":"**Negate to test.** If those reactants were absent, the argument collapses, so that is the assumption.","calc":["negate the choice → does the conclusion fail?"]},{"text":"**Also:** an assumption is the unstated premise whose denial would break the conclusion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lab model supports a claim about the past only if its starting materials and conditions existed then."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-03a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:3#1","pe1-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:46#1","pe6-cp:23#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:3"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"What is the retention factor (Rf) of the ester on the silica plate?","choices":["0.36","0.40","0.47","2.5"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Rf can never exceed 1: a spot can't travel farther than the solvent front."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's 4.2 ÷ 9.0, the raw readings; both distances must be measured from the baseline, not the plate edge."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"The baseline was taken off the spot but not the front; subtract it from both readings."}],"rule":"Rf is between 0 and 1, and both distances are measured from the baseline. Round each distance to 1 digit, then divide.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Shift both readings down by 1: 3.2 over 8. Halve twice: 0.8 over 2 = 0.40. Anything above 1 (2.5) is out instantly."},"exp":"**Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent-front distance, both from the baseline.** Spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm. Front: 9.0 − 1.0 = 8.0 cm. Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = **0.40**.","how":{"ask":"Measure both distances from the baseline, then divide.","know":"**Rf = distance moved by the compound ÷ distance moved by the solvent front**, both measured from the **baseline** where the sample was spotted. Rf is between 0 and 1.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the readings from the plate's edge, but the sample started at the baseline.","hl":"the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge","fig":"cpt-tlc-plate"},{"text":"Subtract the baseline from both readings.","calc":["spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = **3.2 cm**","front: 9.0 − 1.0 = **8.0 cm**"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = **0.40**"]},{"text":"0.47 skips the baseline (4.2 ÷ 9.0), 0.36 subtracts it from only the spot (3.2 ÷ 9.0), and 2.5 is inverted (8.0 ÷ 3.2).","strike":["0.47","0.36","2.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.40."}],"trap":"Dividing the raw readings (4.2 ÷ 9.0 = 0.47): both distances must start at the baseline."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm","note":"where the sample started"},{"t":"9.0 cm","note":"solvent front, from the edge"},{"t":"4.2 cm","note":"spot, from the edge"}]},{"text":"**Start at the baseline.** The race starts where the sample was spotted, not at the plate's edge.","calc":["spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm","front: 9.0 − 1.0 = 8.0 cm"]},{"text":"**Divide spot by front.**","calc":["Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = 0.40"]},{"text":"**Also:** on silica, the compound with the strongest polar groups (O–H or N–H first) has the smallest Rf."},{"text":"**Also:** a low Rf means strong binding to the stationary phase; a high Rf means the compound prefers the mobile phase."},{"text":"**Also:** only N–H, O–H and F–H donate hydrogen bonds; C=O and ether oxygens can only accept them."},{"text":"**Also:** in chromatography, Rf is characteristic of the compound under fixed conditions; spot size only reflects how much was applied."},{"text":"**Also:** every Rf lies between 0 and 1; a larger value means the ratio is inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** sugar-based column materials (polysaccharides such as dextran, agarose and cellulose) are covered in –OH groups, so they interact with proteins mainly by hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Also:** in a binding pocket, sort each ligand atom by what it carries. An oxygen with lone pairs but no H of its own (a ketone or amide C=O, a carboxylate –COO⁻, an ether O) can only receive an H-bond, for instance from arginine's N–H; an O–H or N–H on the ligand can also give one; a C–H does neither. In acetic acid, drawn here, the C=O oxygen is a pure acceptor and the O–H is a donor.","fig":"acetic-acid","calc":["C=O oxygen: lone pairs, no H → acceptor only","O–H: H on O plus lone pairs → donor and acceptor","C–H: no lone pair, no polar H → neither"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Rf = (spot − baseline) ÷ (front − baseline), and it always falls between 0 and 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-04e","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:4#1","pe1-cp:4#3","pe1-cp:4#4","pe1-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:4"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"Which statement about the pentan-2-ol dehydration is NOT correct?","fig":"cpt-pentan-2-ol","choices":["Via a carbocation","Loses one water","Tertiary alcohols react faster","Only trans alkene forms"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"True: the protonated –OH leaves as water and E1 runs through a carbocation, so this one stays."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"True: one H₂O leaves for each alkene formed, as the passage describes."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"True: a 3° cation forms most easily, so tertiary alcohols dehydrate fastest."}],"rule":"For a NOT question, test each statement against E1: a carbocation, one H₂O lost, 3° fastest, and a planar cation that gives both cis and trans.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"A planar carbocation can lose H⁺ toward either face, so E1 is never stereospecific. A choice claiming a single geometry is the false one."},"exp":"Acid-catalyzed dehydration is **E1**: the –OH is protonated, **one water** leaves and a **planar carbocation** forms; **tertiary** alcohols react most easily because they give the most stable cation. Because the cation is planar, the C–H can break on either side, so **both cis and trans** pent-2-ene form, as sentence 3 reports. The claim that only the trans alkene forms is the false statement.","how":{"ask":"Find the statement that contradicts the E1 mechanism or the passage.","know":"• E1: protonate the –OH, lose one H₂O, form a planar carbocation, lose H⁺.\n• Ease of dehydration: 3° > 2° > 1°.\n• A planar cation gives cis and trans alkenes, so the reaction is not stereospecific.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 names the planar carbocation and the single water lost: both statements are true.","hl":"lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation","strike":["Via a carbocation","Loses one water"]},{"text":"The tertiary alcohol reacts at a lower temperature: true.","hl":"reacted at a much lower temperature","strike":["Tertiary alcohols react faster"]},{"text":"Both cis and trans pent-2-ene were collected, so a trans-only claim is false. Answer: only trans alkene forms.","hl":"both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene"}],"trap":"Treating the major (trans) alkene as the only one: a planar cation gives both isomers."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"lost one water molecule","note":"one H₂O"},{"t":"planar carbocation","note":"E1; either face"},{"t":"both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene","note":"not stereospecific"},{"t":"NOT correct","note":"three are true"}]},{"text":"**Check each statement** against E1 and the passage; cross off the true ones.","calc":["carbocation: true","one H₂O: true","3° fastest: true","a single geometry: false"]},{"text":"**Also:** in E1, the C=C always forms between the cationic carbon and a carbon directly bonded to it."},{"text":"**Also:** a direct E1 alkene always has the former C–OH carbon in its C=C."},{"text":"**Also:** E1 through a planar carbocation gives both E and Z alkenes, with the more stable E usually major."},{"text":"**Also:** tertiary alcohols dehydrate most easily because they form the most stable carbocation; primary alcohols dehydrate slowest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in E1 dehydration a planar carbocation forms, one water is lost, 3° alcohols react fastest, and both cis and trans alkenes form."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-05a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:27#3","pe3-cp:27#1","pe3-cp:27#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:5"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"The product is drawn. Which functional group now links the two former reactants?","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","choices":["Ester","Amide","Ether","Carboxylic acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide's carbonyl carbon is bonded to N; the highlighted carbonyl carbon is bonded to O."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An ether is C–O–C with no C=O; the highlighted O sits right on a carbonyl carbon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylic acid ends in O–H; this oxygen carries an ethyl group instead of H."}],"rule":"Name a carbonyl by the atom on its carbon: N makes an amide, O–H an acid, O–C the alkoxy kind; no C=O at all means an ether.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name a carbonyl by its neighbors: C=O next to O–R is ester, next to N is amide, next to OH is acid, no C=O is ether. Here C=O touches O–CH2CH3, so ester."},"exp":"A carbonyl carbon bonded to an **–O–R** group is an **ester**, C(=O)–O–C: here an ethyl ester. The other carbonyl in the molecule is bonded to N, so that one is the **amide**. An ether has C–O–C with no carbonyl, and a carboxylic acid has C(=O)–O**H**.","how":{"ask":"Find the atom on the new carbonyl carbon, then name the linkage it makes.","know":"• Name a carbonyl group by its other neighbor on the C=O carbon.\n• C(=O)–**O–C** → **ester**; C(=O)–**N** → **amide** (the peptide bond); C(=O)–**OH** → carboxylic acid.\n• **Ether**: C–O–C with no C=O on either carbon.\n• Carboxyl + alcohol → ester; carboxyl + amine → amide.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says the acid was heated with ethanol, an alcohol: the new bond joins the carboxyl carbon to its oxygen.","hl":"with ethanol","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","calc":["–C(=O)–O–CH₂CH₃","C=O carbon + O–R → **ester**"]},{"text":"Sentence 2: the amide C=O band was already there and no new N–H appears, so the new link is not an amide.","hl":"showed no new N–H stretch","strike":["Amide"]},{"text":"An ether has no carbonyl next to its oxygen, and an acid's oxygen carries H, not an ethyl group.","strike":["Ether","Carboxylic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: an ester."}],"trap":"Calling every new carbonyl link an amide because amino acids are involved: check whether the carbonyl carbon is bonded to N or to O–R."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"with ethanol","note":"an alcohol partner"},{"t":"with loss of a water molecule","note":"condensation"},{"t":"one new C=O stretch","note":"a new carbonyl link"},{"t":"no new N–H stretch","note":"not a new amide"}]},{"text":"**Name a carbonyl by its other neighbor.**","calc":["C(=O)–O–R → ester","C(=O)–N → amide (peptide bond)","C(=O)–OH → carboxylic acid","C–O–C, no C=O → ether"]},{"text":"**Read the product.** The old carbonyl sits on N; the new one sits on O–ethyl.","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","calc":["CH₃C(=O)–NH– → amide (old)","–C(=O)–O–CH₂CH₃ → ester (new)"]},{"text":"**Also:** forming a peptide (amide) bond is a condensation that releases one H₂O, 18 amu, so a dipeptide's mass is the sum of its two amino acids minus 18."},{"text":"**Also:** a peptide bond is an amide, C(=O)–N, made when a carboxyl group condenses with an amino group and releases water; carboxyl + alcohol gives an ester instead.","calc":["R–COOH + H₂N–R′ → R–CO–NH–R′ + H₂O","R–COOH + HO–R′ → R–CO–O–R′ + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name the new link by the partner: carboxyl + alcohol gives an ester (C(=O)–O–R); carboxyl + amine gives an amide, the peptide bond."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-06a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:6#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:6"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P2","passage":"A teaching lab studied how alcohols become alkyl halides and how those halides react. First, 2-methylpentan-3-ol was heated with concentrated HBr; the protonated –OH left as water, giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH, and this cation could rearrange before it captured bromide or lost a proton. The isolated products were a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes. Next, four alkyl chlorides were each added to sodium iodide in acetone at 50 °C, where iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant. NaI dissolves in acetone but NaCl does not, so the first cloudiness marks the fastest chloride. A crude sample of 1-chloropentane, made from pentan-1-ol, was analyzed by gas chromatography on a nonpolar column at 105 °C; it held pent-1-ene (70 g/mol), pentanal (86 g/mol), pentan-1-ol (88 g/mol) and the chloride (107 g/mol). Each compound spends less time in the column's liquid coating the more easily it evaporates. Finally, (R)-2-chlorobutane was stirred with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa, in dry acetone; doubling either reactant doubled the rate, and the thiolate attacks carbon readily but is only weakly basic.","q":"Which of the four chlorides would turn the iodide solution cloudy first?","choices":["Chlorocyclopentane","1-Chloropentane","2-Chloro-2-methylbutane","1-Chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"A tertiary carbon is too crowded for backside attack, so it cannot react by this one-step path at all."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"It is primary, but the next carbon carries three alkyl groups that block the backside; that check was skipped."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A secondary ring carbon is more crowded than an open CH₂, so it reacts more slowly."}],"rule":"SN2 rate: methyl > 1° > 2°, and 3° does not react. Check the neighboring carbon too: heavy branching there blocks the backside.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Concerted and second order means SN2: pick the least crowded carbon. An unbranched CH₂–Cl with an unbranched neighbor wins."},"exp":"A one-step reaction that is first order in both reactants is **SN2**, so the rate depends on **steric crowding at the carbon bearing Cl**. 1-Chloropentane is primary with no branching nearby, so iodide reaches its back side most easily and NaCl precipitates first. Chlorocyclopentane is secondary (slower), 2-chloro-2-methylbutane is tertiary (no SN2), and 1-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane is primary but its quaternary neighbor blocks the back side.","how":{"ask":"Identify the mechanism, then pick the least hindered carbon.","know":"• Concerted + second order = SN2.\n• SN2 rate: methyl > 1° > 2°; 3° does not react.\n• Branching on the next carbon (neopentyl-type) also blocks backside attack.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step, first order in each reactant: SN2.","hl":"one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"A tertiary carbon is too crowded for backside attack.","strike":["2-Chloro-2-methylbutane"]},{"text":"Primary, but the neighboring carbon carries two methyls and an ethyl: blocked.","strike":["1-Chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane"]},{"text":"A secondary ring carbon is slower than an open primary one.","strike":["Chlorocyclopentane"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-chloropentane."}],"trap":"Assuming every primary chloride is fast: check the next carbon for bulky branches."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"one concerted step","note":"SN2"},{"t":"first order in each reactant","note":"bimolecular"},{"t":"NaCl does not","note":"precipitate = reaction"}]},{"text":"**Classify each carbon bearing Cl.**","calc":["1-chloropentane: 1°, open","chlorocyclopentane: 2°","2-chloro-2-methylbutane: 3°","1-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane: 1°, crowded neighbor"]},{"text":"**Least crowded reacts fastest** and clouds the solution first."},{"text":"**Also:** SN2 slows with crowding at the carbon being attacked (methyl > 1° > 2°, no 3°), not at the leaving group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 slows with crowding at the attacked carbon and its neighbor: methyl > 1° > 2°, and 3° does not react."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-07b","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:7#2","pe1-cp:7#1","pe1-cp:7#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:7"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P2","passage":"A teaching lab studied how alcohols become alkyl halides and how those halides react. First, 2-methylpentan-3-ol was heated with concentrated HBr; the protonated –OH left as water, giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH, and this cation could rearrange before it captured bromide or lost a proton. The isolated products were a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes. Next, four alkyl chlorides were each added to sodium iodide in acetone at 50 °C, where iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant. NaI dissolves in acetone but NaCl does not, so the first cloudiness marks the fastest chloride. A crude sample of 1-chloropentane, made from pentan-1-ol, was analyzed by gas chromatography on a nonpolar column at 105 °C; it held pent-1-ene (70 g/mol), pentanal (86 g/mol), pentan-1-ol (88 g/mol) and the chloride (107 g/mol). Each compound spends less time in the column's liquid coating the more easily it evaporates. Finally, (R)-2-chlorobutane was stirred with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa, in dry acetone; doubling either reactant doubled the rate, and the thiolate attacks carbon readily but is only weakly basic.","q":"Which product of the HBr reaction could form only by way of a rearranged carbocation?","choices":["3-Bromo-2-methylpentane","2-Bromo-2-methylpentane","4-Methylpent-2-ene","2-Methylpent-2-ene"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Br sits on C3, the carbon that held the –OH: direct capture of the first carbocation."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing an H from C4, next to the C3 cation, gives this alkene with no shift."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing an H from C2, next to the original C3 cation, gives this alkene directly."}],"rule":"Find the carbon that held the –OH. Br on it, or a C=C touching it, is direct; Br on a more substituted neighbor means the cation shifted first.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mark C3, where the –OH was. Both alkenes include C3 in the C=C, so they can form directly; only a bromide with Br off C3 needs a hydride shift."},"exp":"The first carbocation forms on **C3**, the carbon that lost the –OH. Capture there gives 3-bromo-2-methylpentane, and losing H from C2 or C4 gives 2-methylpent-2-ene or 4-methylpent-2-ene: all **direct**. Moving a hydride from C2 (a **1,2-shift**) puts the + charge on the **tertiary** C2; only that cation gives **2-bromo-2-methylpentane**, with Br on a carbon that never held the –OH.","how":{"ask":"Find the product whose Br position needs the carbocation to move first.","know":"• Direct products keep Br on, or a C=C touching, the carbon that held the –OH.\n• A 1,2-hydride or methyl shift moves the + charge to a more substituted neighbor.\n• Br on that neighbor means a rearrangement happened.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the first cation sits on C3, the carbon that held the –OH.","hl":"giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH"},{"text":"Br on C3 is direct capture of that cation.","strike":["3-Bromo-2-methylpentane"]},{"text":"Both alkenes have C3 in their C=C, so each forms by losing an H next to the C3 cation.","strike":["4-Methylpent-2-ene","2-Methylpent-2-ene"]},{"text":"A hydride shift from C2 makes a 3° cation on C2; Br there needs that shift. Answer: 2-bromo-2-methylpentane."}],"trap":"Picking the most substituted alkene: 2-methylpent-2-ene can form directly from the C3 cation, so it does not prove a shift."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH","note":"start here"},{"t":"this cation could rearrange","note":"look for a shift"},{"t":"a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes","note":"sort four products"},{"t":"only by way of a rearranged carbocation","note":"the ask"}]},{"text":"**Sort the products** by where the new group sits.","calc":["Br on C3 → direct","C=C at C2–C3 or C3–C4 → direct","Br on C2 (3°) → needs a hydride shift"]},{"text":"**Also:** when the neighboring carbon has no H, a methyl moves instead, as in 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol, drawn here: its 2,3-dimethyl products need a methyl shift.","fig":"cpt-33-dimethylbutan-2-ol"},{"text":"**Also:** a carbocation shifts H or CH₃ from a neighboring carbon whenever that makes a more stable cation."},{"text":"**Also:** a new group on a carbon that never held the leaving group is the fingerprint of a carbocation rearrangement."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a product with Br (or another group) on a carbon that never held the OH can only come from a shifted carbocation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-08d","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:8#3","pe1-cp:8#1","pe1-cp:8#2","pe4-cp:9#1","pe4-cp:9#2","pe4-cp:9#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:8"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P2","passage":"A teaching lab studied how alcohols become alkyl halides and how those halides react. First, 2-methylpentan-3-ol was heated with concentrated HBr; the protonated –OH left as water, giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH, and this cation could rearrange before it captured bromide or lost a proton. The isolated products were a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes. Next, four alkyl chlorides were each added to sodium iodide in acetone at 50 °C, where iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant. NaI dissolves in acetone but NaCl does not, so the first cloudiness marks the fastest chloride. A crude sample of 1-chloropentane, made from pentan-1-ol, was analyzed by gas chromatography on a nonpolar column at 105 °C; it held pent-1-ene (70 g/mol), pentanal (86 g/mol), pentan-1-ol (88 g/mol) and the chloride (107 g/mol). Each compound spends less time in the column's liquid coating the more easily it evaporates. Finally, (R)-2-chlorobutane was stirred with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa, in dry acetone; doubling either reactant doubled the rate, and the thiolate attacks carbon readily but is only weakly basic.","q":"In the gas chromatogram of the crude 1-chloropentane, what emerges first, and why?","fig":["cpt-pent-1-ene","pentanal","cpt-pentan-1-ol","cpt-1-chloropentane"],"choices":["Pentan-1-ol, strongest H-bonds","Pent-1-ene, weakest forces","1-Chloropentane, heaviest","Pentanal, most polar C=O"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"H-bonding raises the boiling point, so the alcohol stays on the column longest, not shortest."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"More mass means stronger dispersion forces and a later peak, not the first one."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A polar C=O adds dipole attractions, which hold a compound back rather than speed it up."}],"rule":"GC on a nonpolar column runs in boiling-point order: weakest intermolecular forces and lowest mass come out first; H-bonders come out last.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"First peak = most volatile. Strike anything with O–H, C=O or a heavy halogen; the light hydrocarbon with only dispersion forces is left."},"exp":"In GC the **most volatile** compound (lowest boiling point) spends the least time in the stationary liquid and reaches the detector **first**. Pent-1-ene is the lightest and has only weak dispersion forces. Pentanal adds a C=O dipole, 1-chloropentane is heavier and polar, and pentan-1-ol **hydrogen-bonds**, so it comes out last.","how":{"ask":"Rank volatility; the most volatile compound elutes first.","know":"• GC separates by volatility (boiling point).\n• Weaker intermolecular forces and lower mass → lower boiling point → earlier peak.\n• At similar size: alkene < aldehyde or alkyl halide < alcohol in boiling point.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says compounds that evaporate easily leave the coating fastest: on a nonpolar column the order follows boiling point.","hl":"the more easily it evaporates"},{"text":"Strong H-bonds, a C=O dipole and the largest mass all raise the boiling point and delay the peak.","strike":["Pentan-1-ol, strongest H-bonds","Pentanal, most polar C=O","1-Chloropentane, heaviest"]},{"text":"Answer: pent-1-ene, which has the weakest intermolecular forces."}],"trap":"Thinking the most polar or heaviest compound comes out first: on GC, strong attractions mean a late peak."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"nonpolar column","note":"order by boiling point"},{"t":"70 g/mol","note":"lightest"},{"t":"the more easily it evaporates","note":"volatile = first"}]},{"text":"**Rank the forces.**","calc":["pent-1-ene: dispersion only","pentanal, 1-chloropentane: dipoles","pentan-1-ol: H-bonds"]},{"text":"**Also:** in GC, compounds come off in order of increasing boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** with similar sizes, an alkane boils lowest and an alcohol highest, because hydrogen bonding is the strongest force."},{"text":"**Also:** an O–H or N–H group raises the boiling point far more than added mass does, because of hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Also:** fractional distillation separates liquids by the same property, boiling point. An alcohol hydrogen-bonds, so it boils higher than an alkyl halide or ether of similar mass, and the lower-boiling liquid distills over first.","calc":["dipropyl ether or 1-chlorohexane vs hexan-1-ol","ether / chloride: lower bp → collects first","alcohol: H-bonding, higher bp → stays behind"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in GC the most volatile compound (weakest forces, lowest mass) gives the first peak; alcohols come out last."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-09b","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:9#1","pe1-cp:9#2","pe1-cp:9#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:9"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P2","passage":"A teaching lab studied how alcohols become alkyl halides and how those halides react. First, 2-methylpentan-3-ol was heated with concentrated HBr; the protonated –OH left as water, giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH, and this cation could rearrange before it captured bromide or lost a proton. The isolated products were a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes. Next, four alkyl chlorides were each added to sodium iodide in acetone at 50 °C, where iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant. NaI dissolves in acetone but NaCl does not, so the first cloudiness marks the fastest chloride. A crude sample of 1-chloropentane, made from pentan-1-ol, was analyzed by gas chromatography on a nonpolar column at 105 °C; it held pent-1-ene (70 g/mol), pentanal (86 g/mol), pentan-1-ol (88 g/mol) and the chloride (107 g/mol). Each compound spends less time in the column's liquid coating the more easily it evaporates. Finally, (R)-2-chlorobutane was stirred with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa, in dry acetone; doubling either reactant doubled the rate, and the thiolate attacks carbon readily but is only weakly basic.","q":"Which compound forms as the main product of the thiolate reaction?","choices":["(S)-2-(Methylthio)butane","(R)-2-(Methylthio)butane","1-(Methylthio)butane","(E)-But-2-ene"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Keeping R ignores backside attack: SN2 inverts the stereocenter, and here the label flips with it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"SN2 puts the new group on the carbon that lost Cl; a CH₂ end group would need a different starting chloride."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A weakly basic thiolate favors substitution, so an alkene is not the main product."}],"rule":"SN2 at a stereocenter inverts it: redraw, then re-rank priorities before naming R or S, since the new group may change the order.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Second order → SN2 → inversion. SCH₃ takes Cl's place as the top-priority group, so the label flips from R to S."},"exp":"Doubling either reactant doubles the rate: **second order, SN2**. Thiolate attacks from the back and the stereocenter **inverts**. S outranks ethyl and methyl just as Cl did, so the priority order is unchanged and the inverted carbon is **(S)**: (S)-2-(methylthio)butane. The new group stays on C2, and a weakly basic thiolate gives little alkene.","how":{"ask":"Decide SN1 or SN2 from the rate data, then name the product and its configuration.","know":"• Rate depends on both reactants → SN2 → inversion.\n• After inversion, re-rank priorities: the label flips only if the new group holds the same rank as the leaving group.\n• SN2 keeps the new group on the same carbon.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 8 says doubling either reactant doubled the rate: SN2.","hl":"doubling either reactant doubled the rate","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"Inversion rules out keeping R; the group stays on C2; a weak base gives little alkene.","strike":["(R)-2-(Methylthio)butane","1-(Methylthio)butane","(E)-But-2-ene"]},{"text":"S ranks first just as Cl did, so inversion flips R to S. Answer: (S)-2-(methylthio)butane.","calc":["before: Cl > ethyl > methyl > H","after: SCH₃ > ethyl > methyl > H"]}],"trap":"Assuming the label never changes: here it flips only because S takes Cl's top rank."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"(R)-2-chlorobutane","note":"start R"},{"t":"doubling either reactant doubled the rate","note":"SN2"},{"t":"only weakly basic","note":"little E2"}]},{"text":"**Invert, then re-rank.**","calc":["backside attack → inversion","SCH₃ keeps Cl's top rank → R becomes S"]},{"text":"**Also:** CIP priority is set by the first atom's atomic number; go farther out only to break a tie."},{"text":"**Also:** after a substitution, the product keeps the substrate's carbon chain and the new group's position; only the group's name changes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 inverts the stereocenter; re-rank priorities with the new group before naming R or S, and keep the group on the same carbon."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:10#1","pe1-cp:10#2","pe1-cp:10#3","pe3-cp:10#1","pe3-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:10"],"q":"Ethanol whose oxygen is ¹⁸O is esterified with acetic acid under acid catalysis. Where does the ¹⁸O end up?","fig":["cpt-ethanol","cpt-acetic-acid"],"choices":["In the ester","In the water","In both products","In neither product"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"There is only one ¹⁸O per ethanol, and one atom can't be split between two products."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Atoms aren't destroyed: the labeled O must end up in some product."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The water forms from the acid's –OH plus H⁺, so it carries the acid's ordinary oxygen."}],"rule":"In acid-catalyzed esterification the acid loses its OH; the alcohol's O–C bond never breaks.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Fischer esterification: acid loses its OH, alcohol keeps its O. So the alcohol's labeled oxygen becomes the ester's bridging O, never the water."},"exp":"In Fischer esterification the **alcohol's oxygen is the nucleophile**: it attacks the protonated carbonyl carbon and stays bonded to it, becoming the ester's bridging –O–. The water that leaves takes an oxygen from the **acid**. So the ¹⁸O ends up **in the ester**.","how":{"ask":"Find which oxygen attacks and which one leaves, then follow the labeled one.","know":"• **Fischer esterification**: RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O (acid catalyst).\n• The **alcohol O attacks** the carbonyl carbon and is kept; **water leaves from the acid** side.\n• So the ester's C–O–R′ oxygen comes from the alcohol, and the water's O from the acid.","steps":[{"text":"The labeled O is ethanol's, the nucleophile.","hl":"oxygen is ¹⁸O","calc":["CH₃CH₂–¹⁸OH attacks the C=O carbon"]},{"text":"The ethyl–¹⁸O bond never breaks, and one ¹⁸O can't be in two places or vanish.","strike":["In both products","In neither product"]},{"text":"The water forms from the acid's –OH plus H⁺, so it carries the acid's ordinary oxygen.","strike":["In the water"]},{"text":"Answer: in the ester, as its bridging oxygen."}],"trap":"Thinking the alcohol loses its OH as water, as in an SN1 reaction: in esterification the acid loses the OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"esterified with acetic acid","note":"Fischer"},{"t":"oxygen is ¹⁸O","note":"the nucleophile's O"}]},{"text":"**Activate.** H⁺ protonates the acid's C=O, making the carbon more electrophilic."},{"text":"**Attack and leave.**","calc":["EtO–H attacks C → tetrahedral intermediate","acid's –OH → –OH₂⁺ → leaves as H₂O"]},{"text":"**Also:** in Fischer esterification the ester's bridging oxygen comes from the alcohol, its C=O oxygen from the acid, and the water from the acid."},{"text":"**Also:** an H on O or N exchanges rapidly with solvent water (faster with acid or base), so it cannot keep a label, so it cannot show which bond broke."},{"text":"**Also:** an H bonded to carbon, such as an aromatic ring C–H or a CH₃ hydrogen, is far too weakly acidic to swap with solvent protons, so a ³H or D placed there stays put even after hours in water. Only H on O, N or S trades places with the solvent.","calc":["O–H, N–H, S–H: swap with H₂O → label lost","C–H (aromatic or alkyl): no swap → label kept"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in Fischer esterification the alcohol keeps its oxygen as the ester's –O–, and the acid loses an oxygen as water; follow a label by asking which O attacks and which leaves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-11a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:11#1","pe1-cp:11#2","pe1-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:11"],"q":"A camera images a slide 20 cm from its lens onto a sensor 4.0 cm behind the lens. What is the lens's power?","choices":["+30 D","+0.30 D","+20 D","+25 D"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"That adds 1/20 and 1/4.0 in centimetres; diopters are inverse metres."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Subtracting the reciprocals treats the image as virtual; a real image on a sensor adds them."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That uses only 1/i; the object distance adds its own 1/o term to the power."}],"rule":"Convert both distances to metres and add both reciprocals for a real image: P = 1/o + 1/i, positive for a converging lens.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"In metres: 1/0.20 = 5 and 1/0.040 = 25. Add them: 30 D. A camera or eye lens making a real image is always +."},"exp":"**Power P = 1/f (f in metres) = 1/o + 1/i.** o = 0.20 m and i = 0.040 m, so P = 5 + 25 = **+30 D**. The sensor plays the retina's role: the image distance is the lens-to-sensor distance.","how":{"ask":"Find 1/f in m⁻¹ from the two distances.","know":"• 1/f = 1/o + 1/i.\n• Power (D) = 1/f with f in metres.\n• Image distance = lens to screen (or retina).","steps":[{"text":"Convert both distances to metres.","hl":"4.0 cm behind the lens","calc":["o = 0.20 m","i = 0.040 m"]},{"text":"Add the reciprocals.","calc":["P = 1/0.20 + 1/0.040 = 5 + 25 = 30 D"]},{"text":"Centimetres give 0.30; subtracting gives 20; dropping the 1/o term gives 25.","strike":["+0.30 D","+20 D","+25 D"]},{"text":"Answer: +30 D."}],"trap":"Leaving the distances in cm (0.30 D): diopters are inverse metres."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"20 cm from its lens","note":"object distance"},{"t":"4.0 cm behind the lens","note":"image distance"}]},{"text":"**Metres, then reciprocals.**","calc":["1/0.20 m = 5 D","1/0.040 m = 25 D"]},{"text":"**Add** for a real image on a screen, and keep the sign positive."},{"text":"**Also:** power in diopters = 1 ÷ (focal length in metres); converging lenses are +, diverging lenses are −."},{"text":"**Also:** in any eye problem, the image distance is the lens-to-retina distance; the object distance is lens-to-object."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** power in diopters = 1/o + 1/i with both distances in metres; a lens forming a real image on a screen has positive power."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:12#1","pe1-cp:12#3","pe1-cp:12#2","pe3-cp:14#1","pe3-cp:14#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:12"],"q":"The plot shows free energy along the reaction path for peptide hydrolysis, without and with a protease (kJ/mol). Which conclusion fits the plot?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide hydrolysis with and without the protease","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No enzyme","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,40],[5,90],[7,30],[9,-30],[10,-30]]},{"name":"With protease","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,20],[5,50],[7,10],[9,-30],[10,-30]]}]},"choices":["Ea drops by 40","ΔG° drops by 40","Ea drops to 40","ΔG° turns positive"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That reads the size of the drop as the new barrier; the catalyzed peak sits at 50."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Reactants and products sit at the same levels on both curves, so ΔG° is unchanged."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Products stay below reactants on both curves; a catalyst never changes ΔG°."}],"rule":"Ea = peak − reactants; ΔG° = products − reactants. A catalyst lowers only the peak, so only Ea changes.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The endpoints never move with a catalyst, so strike any ΔG° change. Then subtract the two peaks: 90 − 50."},"exp":"Read the plot: reactants at 0, products at −30, uncatalyzed peak at 90 and catalyzed peak at 50 kJ/mol. **Ea = peak − reactants**: 90 without and 50 with the protease, so the enzyme **lowers Ea by 40**. **ΔG° = products − reactants = −30** on both curves: an enzyme stabilizes the **transition state** and leaves ΔG° and the equilibrium unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Read Ea and ΔG° from each curve and compare them.","know":"• Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy.\n• ΔG° = product energy − reactant energy.\n• A catalyst lowers the transition state only; ΔG° and K stay the same.","steps":[{"text":"Read the endpoints: the same reactants and products on both curves.","calc":["ΔG° = −30 − 0 = −30 kJ/mol on both curves"],"strike":["ΔG° drops by 40","ΔG° turns positive"]},{"text":"Read the peaks.","calc":["Ea without = 90","Ea with = 50","drop = 90 − 50 = 40"]},{"text":"The new barrier is 50, not the size of the drop.","strike":["Ea drops to 40"]},{"text":"Answer: Ea drops by 40."}],"trap":"Reading the drop as the new barrier, or thinking the catalyst also changes ΔG°."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **read the plot and the question**.","marks":[{"t":"without and with a protease","note":"two curves"},{"t":"Which conclusion fits the plot?","note":"compare Ea and ΔG°"}]},{"text":"**Measure two gaps on each curve.**","calc":["Ea: peak − start","ΔG°: end − start"]},{"text":"**A catalyst lowers only the peak.** It stabilizes the transition state, so the barrier falls while the start and end stay put."},{"text":"**Also:** a catalyst speeds both directions equally, so it shortens the time to equilibrium but leaves K, ΔG° and the equilibrium yield unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** catalysis needs the transition state stabilized more than the substrate; extra substrate binding alone raises the barrier."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = peak − reactants and ΔG° = products − reactants; an enzyme lowers only the peak, never ΔG° or K."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12b","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:50"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"What is the most likely result for the hydrogenation product, and why?","choices":["Pure; solid filtered off","Contaminated; Pd stays in solution","Higher yield; K increases","Lower yield; catalyst is consumed"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Contamination happens when a catalyst dissolves with the product; this one is an insoluble solid."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A catalyst speeds both directions equally, so it never shifts the equilibrium or the yield."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A catalyst is regenerated, which is why it can be reused in the next batch."}],"rule":"A solid (heterogeneous) catalyst is filtered off; a dissolved (homogeneous) one stays with the product. No catalyst changes the equilibrium yield.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask what phase the catalyst is in. An insoluble powder in a liquid is caught by a filter, so the product leaves clean."},"exp":"Pd on charcoal is a **heterogeneous** catalyst: an insoluble solid in a liquid reaction. Filtration removes it, so the product is **free of catalyst**, and the recovered solid can be reused because catalysts are **regenerated**. A dissolved (homogeneous) catalyst that cannot be separated would stay in the product. No catalyst shifts the equilibrium or the yield; it only shortens the time to reach equilibrium.","how":{"ask":"Decide the catalyst's phase, then whether filtration can separate it.","know":"• Heterogeneous (solid) catalysts are filtered off; homogeneous (dissolved) ones stay with the product.\n• Catalysts are regenerated, not consumed.\n• Catalysts change the rate, not K or the equilibrium yield.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the catalyst is an insoluble powder, a different phase from the solution.","hl":"a black powder that does not dissolve"},{"text":"A filter catches it, so it does not contaminate the product.","hl":"passed through a filter","strike":["Contaminated; Pd stays in solution"]},{"text":"Catalysts neither move the equilibrium nor get used up.","strike":["Higher yield; K increases","Lower yield; catalyst is consumed"]},{"text":"Answer: pure; solid filtered off."}],"trap":"Assuming a metal catalyst always ends up in the product: only a dissolved one does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a black powder that does not dissolve","note":"heterogeneous"},{"t":"passed through a filter","note":"separated"},{"t":"reused in the next batch","note":"not consumed"}]},{"text":"**Phase decides separation.**","calc":["solid in liquid → filter → clean product","dissolved → same phase → stays in product"]},{"text":"**Also:** an inseparable homogeneous catalyst contaminates the product; a catalyst never shifts the equilibrium."},{"text":"**Also:** an enzyme is a catalyst too. A lipase making an ester (acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water) only brings the mixture to equilibrium sooner; doubling the enzyme leaves K and the equilibrium amount of ester unchanged. Changing concentrations (adding acid or alcohol, taking water out) shifts the equilibrium position; only a temperature change alters K.","calc":["2× enzyme → equilibrium reached faster","K and [ester] at equilibrium: unchanged"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a solid catalyst is filtered off and a dissolved one stays with the product; neither is consumed, and neither changes K or the yield."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-13a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:13#1","pe1-cp:13#2","pe1-cp:13#3","pe1-cp:13#4"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:13"],"q":"A 0.20 M solution of formic acid is prepared. Adding which substance would lower the percent ionization of the formic acid?","choices":["Nitric acid","Pure water","Sodium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: diluting a weak acid makes it ionize to a larger fraction, not a smaller one."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A base removes H⁺, pulling HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻ to the right: more ionization, not less."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Bicarbonate is a base; it consumes H⁺ and pushes ionization up, not down."}],"rule":"To lower a weak acid's ionization, add one of its products (H⁺ or its conjugate base); bases and water make it ionize more.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Common-ion rule: adding the ion already produced (H+ from a strong acid) pushes the weak acid back left. Bases and water pull it right, so nitric acid."},"exp":"HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻. Nitric acid is a **strong acid**, so it floods the solution with **H⁺, an ion already in the equilibrium**. By **Le Châtelier** the equilibrium shifts **left** and a smaller fraction of formic acid ionizes (the **common-ion effect**). Water dilutes the acid, and the two bases remove H⁺; all three raise ionization.","how":{"ask":"For each added substance, decide whether the formic acid equilibrium shifts left (less ionized) or right (more ionized).","know":"• **Percent ionization** = [A⁻] at equilibrium ÷ starting [HA] × 100.\n• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product pushes the equilibrium toward reactants; removing a product pulls it toward products.\n• **Common-ion effect**: a strong acid adds H⁺, a product of HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, so HA ionizes less.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium for the weak acid in the stem.","hl":"0.20 M solution of formic acid","calc":["HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻   (Ka ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁴)","alone: x = √(Ka·C) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁵) = 6.0 × 10⁻³ M","% = 6.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **3.0%**"]},{"text":"Nitric acid is a strong acid: it adds H⁺, a product, so the equilibrium shifts left. With 0.050 M HNO₃ present:","calc":["x ≈ Ka·C ÷ [H⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20 ÷ 0.050","x = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ M","% = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **0.36%**  (down from 3.0%)"]},{"text":"Water dilutes the acid, and a diluted weak acid ionizes to a larger fraction.","strike":["Pure water"]},{"text":"Both bases remove H⁺ (OH⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O; HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃), so the equilibrium shifts right: more ionization.","strike":["Sodium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"]},{"text":"Answer: nitric acid."}],"trap":"Choosing a base because it \"neutralizes\" the acid: removing H⁺ pulls the equilibrium to the right, so the weak acid ionizes more, not less."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"formic acid","note":"weak acid: HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻"},{"t":"lower the percent ionization","note":"shift the equilibrium left"}]},{"text":"**Percent ionization** is the fraction of the acid molecules that have given up their H⁺.","calc":["% ionization = [A⁻] ÷ [HA]₀ × 100"]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier:** add a product and the equilibrium moves back toward the reactant. H⁺ is a product, so an extra source of H⁺ makes less HA ionize.","calc":["add H⁺ → shift left → % ionization down","remove H⁺ → shift right → % ionization up"]},{"text":"**Also:** diluting a weak acid lowers [H⁺] but raises its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Also:** an electron-withdrawing substituent (such as Cl) near the acidic group makes the acid stronger, so it ionizes more."},{"text":"**Also:** a drop of indicator is too small an amount to change an acid's equilibrium or its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a strong acid (a common ion, H⁺) lowers a weak acid's percent ionization; adding a base raises it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-14a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:14"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P3","passage":"A point-of-care glucose meter pairs an enzyme electrode with an optical check. The enzyme-coated electrode keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates; its adjustable supply drives 15 mA through it at a low setting and 45 mA at a higher one. For the optical check, light from an LED reaches the sample along a plastic light guide. In vacuum this light travels at 300,000 km/s, but inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s, and its frequency, set by the LED, does not change on entering the guide. The meter's indicator dye fluoresces only when a single photon brings it at least 3.5 eV. LED X emits at 750 THz and LED Y at half that frequency; Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Photodetectors show that the two LEDs deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre.","q":"How does the voltage across the enzyme electrode change between the two supply settings?","choices":["Rises from 0.6 V to 1.8 V","Falls from 1.8 V to 0.6 V","Stays at 0.6 V","Rises from 600 V to 1800 V"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That multiplies 40 Ω by 15 and 45 without turning milliamps into amps first."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"At fixed R, V = IR rises with I; a falling voltage has the proportion upside down."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Constant resistance does not mean constant voltage; V tracks the current."}],"rule":"At fixed R, V = IR is proportional to I: convert mA to A, then multiply each current by R.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The current triples (15 → 45 mA) at fixed R, so V triples. 40 Ω × 0.015 A = 0.6 V, then × 3 = 1.8 V."},"exp":"By **V = IR** at a fixed 40 Ω: V₁ = 0.015 A × 40 Ω = **0.6 V** and V₂ = 0.045 A × 40 Ω = **1.8 V**. Tripling the current triples the voltage, because **V is proportional to I** when R is constant.","how":{"ask":"Apply V = IR at each setting with the same R.","know":"• V = IR.\n• At constant R, V is proportional to I.\n• 1 mA = 10⁻³ A.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says R stays at 40 Ω while the current changes: R is fixed.","hl":"keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates"},{"text":"Convert and multiply.","calc":["V₁ = 0.015 A × 40 Ω = 0.6 V","V₂ = 0.045 A × 40 Ω = 1.8 V"]},{"text":"Unconverted mA gives hundreds of volts; a fall reverses V ∝ I; a constant voltage ignores the change in current.","strike":["Rises from 600 V to 1800 V","Falls from 1.8 V to 0.6 V","Stays at 0.6 V"]},{"text":"Answer: rises from 0.6 V to 1.8 V."}],"trap":"Thinking constant resistance means constant voltage: at fixed R, V follows I."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"40 Ω","note":"R fixed"},{"t":"15 mA","note":"low setting"},{"t":"45 mA","note":"high setting"}]},{"text":"**Same R, so V follows I.**","calc":["V = IR","3 × I → 3 × V"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant R, V = IR rises in proportion to I; convert mA to A before multiplying."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-15a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:15#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:15"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P3","passage":"A point-of-care glucose meter pairs an enzyme electrode with an optical check. The enzyme-coated electrode keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates; its adjustable supply drives 15 mA through it at a low setting and 45 mA at a higher one. For the optical check, light from an LED reaches the sample along a plastic light guide. In vacuum this light travels at 300,000 km/s, but inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s, and its frequency, set by the LED, does not change on entering the guide. The meter's indicator dye fluoresces only when a single photon brings it at least 3.5 eV. LED X emits at 750 THz and LED Y at half that frequency; Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Photodetectors show that the two LEDs deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre.","q":"What is the light guide's index of refraction, and what happens to the light's wavelength inside it?","choices":["1.5; it shortens","1.5; it lengthens","0.67; it shortens","0.67; it lengthens"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"An index below 1 would mean light moves faster than in vacuum; that is v/c upside down."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Below 1 again, and a slower wave at the same frequency cannot have a longer wavelength."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"λ = v/f: with f fixed and v smaller, the wavelength gets shorter, not longer."}],"rule":"n = c/v is at least 1; with the frequency fixed, λ = v/f shrinks by the same factor n.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"n can never be below 1, which kills two choices. Slower light at a fixed frequency packs its waves closer together: shorter λ."},"exp":"**n = c/v** = 300,000 ÷ 200,000 = **1.5**. The frequency does not change at the boundary and **λ = v/f**, so the wavelength shrinks by the same factor: λ in the plastic = λ in vacuum ÷ 1.5. Any index below 1 is impossible.","how":{"ask":"Divide c by v, then use λ = v/f with f fixed.","know":"• n = c/v, and n ≥ 1 always.\n• Frequency is unchanged crossing a boundary.\n• λ = v/f, so λ falls by the factor n.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the two speeds: c in vacuum and v in the plastic.","hl":"inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s","calc":["n = c/v = 300,000 ÷ 200,000 = **1.5**"]},{"text":"An index below 1 is impossible.","strike":["0.67; it shortens","0.67; it lengthens"]},{"text":"f fixed and v smaller → λ smaller.","hl":"does not change on entering the guide","strike":["1.5; it lengthens"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.5; it shortens."}],"trap":"Writing v/c instead of c/v, or thinking slower light stretches its waves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two speeds and what stays fixed**.","marks":[{"t":"300,000 km/s","note":"c"},{"t":"200,000 km/s","note":"v in the plastic"},{"t":"does not change","note":"f fixed"}]},{"text":"**Fast over slow.** The bigger speed goes on top, so n comes out at least 1.","calc":["300,000 ÷ 200,000 = 1.5"]},{"text":"**Same f, slower v, shorter λ.**","calc":["λ = v/f"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = c/v ≥ 1, and with f fixed the wavelength inside a material is the vacuum wavelength ÷ n."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:16#1","pe5-cp:54#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:16"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P3","passage":"A point-of-care glucose meter pairs an enzyme electrode with an optical check. The enzyme-coated electrode keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates; its adjustable supply drives 15 mA through it at a low setting and 45 mA at a higher one. For the optical check, light from an LED reaches the sample along a plastic light guide. In vacuum this light travels at 300,000 km/s, but inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s, and its frequency, set by the LED, does not change on entering the guide. The meter's indicator dye fluoresces only when a single photon brings it at least 3.5 eV. LED X emits at 750 THz and LED Y at half that frequency; Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Photodetectors show that the two LEDs deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre.","q":"Compared with LED Y, what is doubled for LED X?","choices":["Energy of each photon","Photon arrival rate","Speed of the photons","Wavelength of the light"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: equal intensity with more energy per photon means fewer photons each second, not more."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: λ = c/f, so doubling f halves the wavelength."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"All light in air moves at c, whatever its frequency."}],"rule":"E = hf: frequency sets each photon's energy; speed stays c, λ = c/f shrinks, and equal intensity means fewer photons.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"E = hf: frequency sets each photon's energy, so double f means double energy per photon. Speed stays c, λ halves, and equal intensity means fewer photons."},"exp":"A photon's energy is set by its frequency: **E = hf**. Doubling f **doubles the energy of each photon**. The wavelength halves (λ = c/f), the speed stays c, and since both beams have the same intensity, X needs only **only half the photons each second** to carry the same power.","how":{"ask":"Which property of a single photon depends on frequency, and what does equal intensity then force on the photon count?","know":"• **E = hf = hc/λ**: one photon's energy is proportional to its frequency.\n• **c = fλ**: higher f means shorter λ; light speed in air stays c ≈ 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.\n• **Intensity** = (photons per second) × (energy per photon) ÷ area.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says X's frequency is twice Y's; apply E = hf to both.","hl":"LED Y at half that frequency","calc":["E_Y = hf","E_X = h(2f) = **2E_Y**","e.g. f = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz: E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.0 × 10¹⁴) = 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","2f = 1.0 × 10¹⁵ Hz: E = 6.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy rises in proportion to frequency (E = hf)","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.66],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.97],[10,6.6]]}]}},{"text":"Equal intensity with twice the energy per photon means half the photons each second, so the arrival rate halves.","hl":"equal intensity","calc":["I = (photons/s) × E ÷ A","same I, 2E → photons/s × ½"],"strike":["Photon arrival rate"]},{"text":"Light speed in air is c at every frequency, and c = fλ means doubling f halves λ.","calc":["λ = c ÷ f → λ_X = λ_Y ÷ 2"],"strike":["Speed of the photons","Wavelength of the light"]},{"text":"Answer: the energy of each photon."}],"trap":"Thinking higher frequency means more photons arriving. Frequency sets each photon's energy; at the same intensity a higher frequency actually means fewer photons per second."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is the same and what differs**.","marks":[{"t":"equal intensity","note":"same energy per second"},{"t":"LED Y at half that frequency","note":"X has twice f"},{"t":"what is doubled","note":"the ask"}]},{"text":"**Light comes in photons.** Each photon's energy is set by its frequency alone.","calc":["E = hf = hc/λ","2f → 2E"]},{"text":"**Intensity belongs to the beam**: energy per photon times photons per second, per area.","calc":["I = (photons/s × E) ÷ A","fixed I and 2E → ½ the photons/s"]},{"text":"**See it:** photon energy is a straight line through zero against frequency.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"E = hf: doubling the frequency doubles the photon energy","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.66],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.97],[10,6.6]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** at one fixed color, intensity is proportional to the number of photons per second; speed, wavelength and polarization don't set it."},{"text":"**Also:** one photon has an energy, a frequency and a wavelength, linked by E = hf = hc/λ. Intensity and amplitude belong to the beam (how many photons arrive each second), so a photon with twice the energy has twice the f and half the λ, but that tells you nothing about amplitude.","calc":["one photon: E, f, λ","the whole beam: photons per second → intensity, amplitude"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf, so photon energy rises in step with frequency (and λ falls); the photon rate sets intensity at a fixed color, and speed stays c."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-17a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:17#1","pe6-cp:51#1","pe6-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:17"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P3","passage":"A point-of-care glucose meter pairs an enzyme electrode with an optical check. The enzyme-coated electrode keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates; its adjustable supply drives 15 mA through it at a low setting and 45 mA at a higher one. For the optical check, light from an LED reaches the sample along a plastic light guide. In vacuum this light travels at 300,000 km/s, but inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s, and its frequency, set by the LED, does not change on entering the guide. The meter's indicator dye fluoresces only when a single photon brings it at least 3.5 eV. LED X emits at 750 THz and LED Y at half that frequency; Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Photodetectors show that the two LEDs deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre.","q":"How much energy does each photon from LED X carry, and is it enough to make the dye fluoresce?","choices":["3.1 eV; no","3.1 eV; yes","0.0031 eV; no","5.5 × 10⁻³⁰ eV; no"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Tera read as giga (10⁹): that leaves the energy a thousand times too small."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Dividing h by f gives eV·s², not an energy; E = hf multiplies them."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The comparison is flipped: the photon's energy falls short of the threshold."}],"rule":"E = hf with tera = 10¹² and h in eV·s: multiply the leading digits, add the exponents, then compare one photon's energy with the stated threshold.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"4 × 7.5 = 30 and 10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹, so about 3 eV. That is below 3.5 eV, so one photon cannot do it."},"exp":"**E = hf** with **tera = 10¹²**: f = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, so E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s)(7.5 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹) ≈ **3.1 eV**. That is **less** than the 3.5 eV the photoinitiator needs, so a single photon cannot start the curing, however bright the lamp.","how":{"ask":"Convert THz to Hz, compute E = hf, then compare E with the threshold.","know":"• E = hf.\n• tera = 10¹², giga = 10⁹.\n• One photon either carries enough energy or it does not; a brighter lamp only adds photons.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives LED X at 750 THz; convert to hertz.","hl":"LED X emits at 750 THz","calc":["750 THz = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"Multiply.","calc":["E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵)(7.5 × 10¹⁴)","4.14 × 7.5 ≈ 31; 10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹","E ≈ 3.1 eV"]},{"text":"Giga instead of tera gives 0.0031 eV; dividing h by f gives 10⁻³⁰.","strike":["0.0031 eV; no","5.5 × 10⁻³⁰ eV; no"]},{"text":"Compare: 3.1 eV is below the 3.5-eV threshold.","hl":"at least 3.5 eV","strike":["3.1 eV; yes"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.1 eV; no."}],"trap":"Getting E right but flipping the comparison, or thinking a brighter lamp makes each photon stronger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers and the threshold**.","marks":[{"t":"750 THz","note":"f of X"},{"t":"at least 3.5 eV","note":"threshold, one photon"},{"t":"4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s","note":"h in eV"}]},{"text":"**Tera means 10¹².** Write f in scientific notation, then multiply.","calc":["f = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz","E = hf ≈ 3.1 eV"]},{"text":"**Compare one photon with the threshold.** Brightness changes how many photons arrive, never the energy of each."},{"text":"**Also:** E = hf covers radio waves too; only the prefix changes. Mega = 10⁶, so an FM station at 100 MHz emits photons at 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz, each carrying millions of times less energy than a visible photon.","calc":["E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s × 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁶ J","green light (5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz): ≈ 3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","FM-band photons (88–108 MHz): about 6–7 × 10⁻²⁶ J"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf with f in hertz (THz × 10¹²); compare one photon's energy with the threshold, since brightness never makes a single photon stronger."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-18a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:18"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P4","passage":"A patient with kidney failure is assessed before her first dialysis session. Her blood pressure reads 118/74 mmHg, and her pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. Alveolar ventilation, the fresh air reaching the alveoli each minute, is V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T the tidal volume and V_D the dead-space volume of the airways. She breathes 14 times per minute, with a dead space of 0.15 L and an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min. For a kidney scan she receives a radioactive tracer that decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter; its activity was measured every 5 hours after preparation, and the first four readings are graphed. During dialysis, the machine's pump moves 37.5 L of fluid in 7.5 min at a steady flow. The pump's power curve passes through 9 W at 0 L/min, 14 W at 2.5 L/min, 19 W at 5 L/min and 24 W at 7.5 L/min.","q":"What tidal volume does the passage's formula give for this patient?","choices":["0.45 L","0.30 L","0.15 L","59 L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplying by the rate instead of dividing gives tens of litres per breath, far beyond a lung."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is V_T − V_D; the dead space still has to be added back to get V_T."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the dead-space volume copied from the passage, not the tidal volume."}],"rule":"Isolate the unknown: divide by the factor outside the bracket, then undo the subtraction inside it; check that the size is sensible.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Undo the outside first: 4.2 ÷ 14 = 0.30 L. Then undo the subtraction: 0.30 + 0.15 = 0.45 L."},"exp":"Rearrange V_A = f(V_T − V_D): divide by f, then add V_D. V_T − V_D = 4.2 ÷ 14 = 0.30 L, so **V_T = 0.30 + 0.15 = 0.45 L**, a normal-sized breath.","how":{"ask":"Isolate V_T in the given formula, then substitute.","know":"• Undo operations in reverse order: divide by the factor outside the bracket, then add what was subtracted.\n• Check units and size: a resting breath is about half a litre.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the formula; sentence 4 gives f, V_D and V_A.","hl":"V_A = f × (V_T − V_D)","calc":["f = 14 /min, V_D = 0.15 L, V_A = 4.2 L/min"]},{"text":"Divide by f.","calc":["V_T − V_D = 4.2 ÷ 14 = 0.30 L"]},{"text":"Add V_D.","calc":["V_T = 0.30 + 0.15 = **0.45 L**"]},{"text":"59 L multiplies instead of dividing; 0.30 L stops before adding V_D; 0.15 L is just the dead space.","strike":["59 L","0.30 L","0.15 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.45 L."}],"trap":"Stopping at 0.30 L: that is V_T − V_D, not V_T."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its symbol.","marks":[{"t":"14 times per minute","note":"f"},{"t":"a dead space of 0.15 L","note":"V_D"},{"t":"4.2 L/min","note":"V_A"}]},{"text":"**Undo the outside first.** Divide both sides by f.","calc":["V_T − V_D = V_A ÷ f"]},{"text":"**Then undo the inside.** Add V_D to both sides.","calc":["V_T = V_A ÷ f + V_D"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a fluid column, h = P/(ρg): atmospheric pressure can push water up an evacuated tube about 10 m.","calc":["h = P/(ρg)","1 atm ≈ 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa; water: 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ (1000 × 10) ≈ 10 m"]},{"text":"**Also:** when a question gives a formula, name each variable and plug the values in. In a blood pressure written systolic/diastolic, the diastolic is the lower number.","calc":["given pulse pressure = P_s − P_d","120/76 mmHg: P_s = 120, P_d = 76","pulse pressure = 120 − 76 = 44 mmHg"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same plug-in works for a formula you have never met, such as a gyromagnetic ratio γ = 2πf/B. Match each symbol to its value in the passage (f, frequency; B, field), keep the units the choices use (MHz stays MHz for an answer in MHz/T), and leave 2π standing as a factor when every choice carries it.","calc":["¹H at 400 MHz in a 9.4 T field","γ = 2π × 400 MHz ÷ 9.4 T ≈ 42.6 × 2π MHz/T"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to isolate a variable inside a bracket, divide by the outside factor first, then undo the operation inside; check that the answer's size makes sense."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-19d","s":"Fluids","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:19#4","pe1-cp:19#1","pe1-cp:19#2","pe1-cp:19#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:19"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P4","passage":"A patient with kidney failure is assessed before her first dialysis session. Her blood pressure reads 118/74 mmHg, and her pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. Alveolar ventilation, the fresh air reaching the alveoli each minute, is V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T the tidal volume and V_D the dead-space volume of the airways. She breathes 14 times per minute, with a dead space of 0.15 L and an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min. For a kidney scan she receives a radioactive tracer that decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter; its activity was measured every 5 hours after preparation, and the first four readings are graphed. During dialysis, the machine's pump moves 37.5 L of fluid in 7.5 min at a steady flow. The pump's power curve passes through 9 W at 0 L/min, 14 W at 2.5 L/min, 19 W at 5 L/min and 24 W at 7.5 L/min.","q":"The patient's pulse pressure is about what percent of an atmosphere?","choices":["5.8%","16%","9.7%","59%"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That divides the systolic value alone; pulse pressure is systolic minus diastolic."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That uses the diastolic value alone; subtract the two readings first."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That divides by the diastolic pressure instead of by 760 mmHg, the pressure of one atmosphere."}],"rule":"Pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic; percent of 1 atm = (mmHg ÷ 760) × 100.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"118 − 74 = 44 mmHg. 38 mmHg is 5% of 760, so 44 is a bit more: about 5.8%."},"exp":"**Pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic** = 118 − 74 = 44 mmHg. **1 atm = 760 mmHg**, so 44 ÷ 760 × 100 ≈ **5.8%**. 16% and 9.7% use one reading alone, and 59% divides by the diastolic value.","how":{"ask":"Subtract to get the pulse pressure, then divide by 760 mmHg.","know":"• Blood pressure is systolic/diastolic; a typical adult is about 120/80 mmHg.\n• Pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic.\n• 1 atm = 760 mmHg; % = value ÷ 760 × 100.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the reading and the definition: subtract diastolic from systolic.","hl":"118/74 mmHg","calc":["pulse = 118 − 74 = 44 mmHg"]},{"text":"Divide by 760 and multiply by 100.","calc":["44 ÷ 760 = 0.058","× 100 = **5.8%**"]},{"text":"16% and 9.7% skip the subtraction; 59% divides by the diastolic pressure.","strike":["16%","9.7%","59%"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.8%."}],"trap":"Dividing one reading by 760 instead of the difference between them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers and the definition**.","marks":[{"t":"118/74 mmHg","note":"systolic/diastolic"},{"t":"systolic minus diastolic","note":"pulse pressure"}]},{"text":"**Subtract, then compare with 760 mmHg.**","calc":["44 ÷ 760 × 100 ≈ 5.8%"]},{"text":"**Also:** a typical healthy adult's blood pressure is about 120/80 mmHg (systolic/diastolic)."},{"text":"**Also:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic."},{"text":"**Also:** 1 atm = 760 mmHg; multiply atm by 760 to get mmHg."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic; percent of 1 atm = (mmHg ÷ 760) × 100."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-20b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:20#2","pe1-cp:20#1","pe1-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:45#1","pe4-cp:45#2","pe6-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:57#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:20"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P4","passage":"A patient with kidney failure is assessed before her first dialysis session. Her blood pressure reads 118/74 mmHg, and her pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. Alveolar ventilation, the fresh air reaching the alveoli each minute, is V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T the tidal volume and V_D the dead-space volume of the airways. She breathes 14 times per minute, with a dead space of 0.15 L and an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min. For a kidney scan she receives a radioactive tracer that decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter; its activity was measured every 5 hours after preparation, and the first four readings are graphed. During dialysis, the machine's pump moves 37.5 L of fluid in 7.5 min at a steady flow. The pump's power curve passes through 9 W at 0 L/min, 14 W at 2.5 L/min, 19 W at 5 L/min and 24 W at 7.5 L/min.","q":"Assuming the trend continues, what percentage of the tracer will have decayed 35 hours after preparation, and what activity will remain?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Tracer activity after preparation","x":{"label":"Time (h)","min":0,"max":15,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Activity (Bq)","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,640],[5,320],[10,160],[15,80]]}]},"choices":["99.2%; 5 Bq","0.8%; 5 Bq","98.4%; 10 Bq","75%; 160 Bq"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the fraction still left; the question asks how much has decayed."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That stops one half-life short: count every 5-h step out to the stated time."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Those are the values read at 10 h on the graph, not extrapolated to the stated time."}],"rule":"Read the half-life off the graph, count n = t ÷ t½, then remaining = (½)ⁿ and decayed = 1 − remaining; activity falls by the same factor.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The graph halves every 5 h, so 35 h is 7 halvings: 640 → 5 Bq, and only 1 part in 128 (about 0.8%) is left, so about 99.2% has decayed."},"exp":"The activity halves every **5 h** (640 → 320 → 160 → 80 Bq), so the half-life is 5 h. At 35 h, **n = 35 ÷ 5 = 7** half-lives: remaining = (½)⁷ = 1/128 ≈ 0.8%, so **about 99.2% has decayed**, and the activity is 640 ÷ 128 = **5 Bq**.","how":{"ask":"Find the half-life from the graph, count half-lives, then compute the decayed fraction and the activity.","know":"• n = time ÷ half-life.\n• remaining = (½)ⁿ; decayed = 1 − remaining.\n• Activity tracks how many undecayed nuclei are left.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the activity was read every 5 hours; the graph halves at each reading, so t½ = 5 h.","hl":"measured every 5 hours after preparation","calc":["640 → 320 → 160 → 80 Bq","t½ = 5 h"]},{"text":"Count half-lives and compute.","hl":"35 hours","calc":["n = 35 ÷ 5 = 7","remaining = 1/128 ≈ 0.8%","decayed ≈ 99.2%","activity = 640 ÷ 128 = 5 Bq"]},{"text":"0.8% is what remains; 98.4% stops at six half-lives; 75% is the 10-h reading.","strike":["0.8%; 5 Bq","98.4%; 10 Bq","75%; 160 Bq"]},{"text":"Answer: 99.2% decayed; 5 Bq."}],"trap":"Reporting the fraction remaining as the fraction decayed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"every 5 hours","note":"time step"},{"t":"35 hours","note":"target time"},{"t":"first-order kinetics","note":"constant half-life"}]},{"text":"**Half-life from the graph**, then count.","calc":["t½ = 5 h","n = 35 ÷ 5 = 7"]},{"text":"**Decayed = 1 − remaining.**","calc":["1 − 1/128 ≈ 99.2%"]},{"text":"**Also:** fraction decayed = 1 − (½)ⁿ, where n = time ÷ half-life."},{"text":"**Also:** to find the starting amount, run it backward: double once per half-life, so original = remaining × 2ⁿ.","calc":["n = 18 days ÷ 6 days = 3","original = 7 mg × 2³ = 7 × 8 = 56 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** activity (decays per second, in Bq or Ci) is proportional to the number of nuclei left, so it falls on the same schedule: A = A₀ × (½)ⁿ, with n = elapsed time ÷ half-life.","calc":["t½ = 8.0 days, t = 24 days → n = 3","A = 600 Bq × (½)³ = 600 ÷ 8 = 75 Bq"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = t ÷ t½, remaining = (½)ⁿ, decayed = 1 − remaining, and activity falls by the same factor as the nuclei."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-21b","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:21#2","pe1-cp:21#1","pe1-cp:21#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:21"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P4","passage":"A patient with kidney failure is assessed before her first dialysis session. Her blood pressure reads 118/74 mmHg, and her pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. Alveolar ventilation, the fresh air reaching the alveoli each minute, is V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T the tidal volume and V_D the dead-space volume of the airways. She breathes 14 times per minute, with a dead space of 0.15 L and an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min. For a kidney scan she receives a radioactive tracer that decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter; its activity was measured every 5 hours after preparation, and the first four readings are graphed. During dialysis, the machine's pump moves 37.5 L of fluid in 7.5 min at a steady flow. The pump's power curve passes through 9 W at 0 L/min, 14 W at 2.5 L/min, 19 W at 5 L/min and 24 W at 7.5 L/min.","q":"How much work does the dialysis pump do during the 7.5 min?","choices":["8.6 kJ","143 J","11 kJ","4.1 kJ"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"19 W × 7.5 keeps the time in minutes; W × s gives joules, so convert the time to seconds."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That reads the curve near zero flow, as if the per-second rate (about 0.08 L/s) sat on a per-minute axis."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That reads the curve at x = 7.5, the number of minutes, instead of at the computed flow."}],"rule":"Get the flow in the axis's units, read the power there, then W = P × t with t in seconds.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Flow = 37.5 ÷ 7.5 = 5 L/min → 19 W. Time = 450 s. 19 × 450 ≈ 8600 J."},"exp":"Flow = 37.5 L ÷ 7.5 min = **5.0 L/min**, where the curve gives **19 W**. Work = P × t = 19 W × 450 s = 8550 J ≈ **8.6 kJ**.","how":{"ask":"Find the flow, read the power at that flow, then multiply by the time in seconds.","know":"• Flow = volume ÷ time, in the axis units (L/min).\n• Read y at that x on the curve.\n• W = P × t, with t in seconds for joules.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the volume and time; turn them into a flow rate in the curve's units.","hl":"moves 37.5 L of fluid in 7.5 min","calc":["Q = 37.5 ÷ 7.5 = 5.0 L/min"]},{"text":"Sentence 7: read the power at 5.0 L/min.","hl":"19 W at 5 L/min","calc":["P = 19 W"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pump power versus flow rate","x":{"label":"Flow rate (L/min)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2.5},"y":{"label":"Power (W)","min":0,"max":35,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Power","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,9],[2.5,14],[5,19],[7.5,24],[10,29]]}]}},{"text":"Multiply by the time in seconds.","calc":["t = 7.5 × 60 = 450 s","W = 19 × 450 = 8550 J ≈ 8.6 kJ"]},{"text":"143 J keeps minutes; 4.1 kJ reads near zero flow; 11 kJ reads the curve at 7.5.","strike":["143 J","4.1 kJ","11 kJ"]},{"text":"Answer: 8.6 kJ."}],"trap":"Reading the graph at a number from the question (7.5 min), or multiplying watts by minutes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"37.5 L","note":"volume"},{"t":"7.5 min","note":"time"},{"t":"power curve","note":"read P at that flow"}]},{"text":"**Get the x value**, then read the curve.","calc":["37.5 L ÷ 7.5 min = 5.0 L/min → 19 W"]},{"text":"**Work = power × time**, in seconds.","calc":["19 W × 450 s ≈ 8.6 kJ"]},{"text":"**Also:** compute a rate in exactly the units of the graph's axis before reading the graph."},{"text":"**Also:** work = power × time, with power in watts and time in seconds, giving joules."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compute the x value in the axis's units, read the curve there, then W = P × t with t in seconds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-22a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:22#1","pe1-cp:22#2","pe1-cp:22#3","pe6-cp:30#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:22"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P5","passage":"A designed peptide is spun into fibers for a wound-dressing gel. Its cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) joins neighboring strands, and its cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp) gives migrating cells a surface to grip through electrostatic contacts at body pH. A Ser–Met linker joins region B to an assembly region of six glutamates, whose side-chain pKa is about 4.3. Samples equilibrated at different pH values were tested for the percentage of peptide found in fibers, and the results are graphed. When the wet gel is left in air, links form between neighboring strands and the gel turns stiff. Soaking the stiff gel in mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent, makes it soft again. In the wound, a kinase adds a regulatory phosphate to the peptide: mass spectrometry places it on a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol, and replacing that residue with alanine prevents the labeling.","q":"Which atom forms the links that stiffen the gel?","choices":["Sulfur of cysteine","Sulfur of methionine","Oxygen of serine","Nitrogen of lysine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Methionine's sulfur is a thioether with no S–H, so it cannot form a disulfide."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Serine's –OH does not form links that air makes and a reducing agent breaks."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine's amine can form salt bridges, but those are not made by air oxidation or undone by reduction."}],"rule":"Links that form on oxidation (air) and break on reduction are disulfides, which need two cysteine thiols (–SH).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Oxidized together, reduced apart = S–S bond. Only a thiol S–H can form one, and only cysteine has one."},"exp":"Air **oxidizes** cysteine thiols (–SH) on neighboring chains into **disulfide (S–S) bonds**: covalent links between different chains, so the fiber stiffens. A **reducing agent** such as mercaptoethanol breaks them back into two –SH groups. Methionine's S carries no H, and serine and lysine cannot form redox-reversible links.","how":{"ask":"Match \"forms in air, breaks with a reducing agent\" to the bond and the atom that makes it.","know":"• Oxidizing two Cys –SH groups gives a covalent S–S bond.\n• Between two chains, the disulfide is an intermolecular link.\n• Reducing agents (DTT, mercaptoethanol) break S–S bonds.","steps":[{"text":"Sentences 5 and 6: links form in air and break with a reducing agent, so they are disulfides.","hl":"mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent","fig":"cysteine"},{"text":"Only a thiol can form one; methionine's S sits between two carbons with no H.","strike":["Sulfur of methionine"]},{"text":"O–H and N–H groups make no redox-reversible links.","strike":["Oxygen of serine","Nitrogen of lysine"]},{"text":"Answer: sulfur of cysteine."}],"trap":"Picking any sulfur: methionine's thioether has no S–H to oxidize."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"left in air","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"links form between neighboring strands","note":"intermolecular"},{"t":"a reducing agent","note":"breaks S–S"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation joins two –SH into S–S; reduction splits it.**","calc":["2 R–SH → R–S–S–R (air)","R–S–S–R → 2 R–SH (reducing agent)"]},{"text":"**Also:** a disulfide is always covalent; it is intermolecular when its two cysteines belong to different molecules."},{"text":"**Also:** reducing agents such as β-mercaptoethanol or DTT break disulfides back into two thiols."},{"text":"**Also:** a disulfide can form only between two cysteine thiols (–SH). If a protein has no Cys, or its Cys are already paired, any oxidative cross-link in it must come from a different side chain, so look past cysteine for the residue the oxidant attacked."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** links made by air oxidation and undone by a reducing agent are disulfides, formed only by cysteine thiols."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-23c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:23#3","pe1-cp:23#1","pe1-cp:23#2","pe5-cp:42#1","pe5-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:42#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:23"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P5","passage":"A designed peptide is spun into fibers for a wound-dressing gel. Its cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) joins neighboring strands, and its cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp) gives migrating cells a surface to grip through electrostatic contacts at body pH. A Ser–Met linker joins region B to an assembly region of six glutamates, whose side-chain pKa is about 4.3. Samples equilibrated at different pH values were tested for the percentage of peptide found in fibers, and the results are graphed. When the wet gel is left in air, links form between neighboring strands and the gel turns stiff. Soaking the stiff gel in mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent, makes it soft again. In the wound, a kinase adds a regulatory phosphate to the peptide: mass spectrometry places it on a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol, and replacing that residue with alanine prevents the labeling.","q":"Which residue gives migrating cells a charged group to bind?","choices":["Arg in region B","Cys in region A","Gly in region A","Cys in region B"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Region A is the cross-linker: Cys forms disulfides, and its –SH is uncharged at pH 7."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycine's side chain is just H; it carries no charged group."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Read the passage: region B is Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp, with no Cys in it."}],"rule":"Charged side chains at pH 7 are Lys and Arg (+) and Asp and Glu (−); Gly and Cys carry no charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Charged at body pH: K, R, D, E (and H partly). Gly and Cys carry no charge, and region B has no Cys, so Arg in B."},"exp":"Match each residue to its region first. Region A holds **Gly** (no side chain) and **Cys** (a thiol for disulfide cross-links), so it offers no charge. Region B holds Lys, Glu, Arg and Asp, all **charged** at body pH; **Arg** is one of them. There is no Cys in region B.","how":{"ask":"List the residues in each region, then find a charged one in the region where it actually sits.","know":"• Charged at body pH: Asp −, Glu −, Lys +, Arg +.\n• **Cys** = thiol for **disulfide cross-links**, not a charge.\n• **Gly** = no side chain.\n• A choice must name a residue that is really in the region it names.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 lists the residues of each region.","hl":"Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys","calc":["region A: Gly, Cys → cross-linking","region B: Lys, Glu, Arg, Asp → all charged"]},{"text":"Region A has no charged side chain: Gly has none, and Cys forms disulfides.","strike":["Cys in region A","Gly in region A"]},{"text":"Cys does not appear in region B, so that pairing is wrong.","hl":"Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp","strike":["Cys in region B"]},{"text":"Answer: Arg in region B."}],"trap":"Choosing cysteine because its sulfur looks reactive: it belongs to the cross-linking region and gives a covalent link between strands, not a charge for cells."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cross-linking region A","note":"Cys, Gly"},{"t":"cell-binding region B","note":"charged residues"},{"t":"electrostatic contacts","note":"need a charge"}]},{"text":"**Sort by job.** Cys makes S–S cross-links; Gly is a spacer; charged residues bind cells.","fig":["cysteine","glycine"]},{"text":"**Check the charged ones.**","calc":["Lys +, Arg +","Glu −, Asp −"],"fig":["arginine","glutamate"]},{"text":"**Check the location too.** A right residue in the wrong region is a wrong answer."},{"text":"**Also:** for an electrostatic contact, choose a side chain charged opposite to the partner: Lys or Arg for a − site, Asp or Glu for a + site."},{"text":"**Also:** glycine has no side chain (R = H), so it adds no charge or functional group to a binding site."},{"text":"**Also:** a binding pocket can name two contacts at once. A phosphate (−) makes a salt bridge only with a + side chain (Lys or Arg), and a flat aromatic ring π-stacks with an aromatic side chain (Phe, Tyr, Trp or His). The right pair satisfies both: Asp or Glu would repel the phosphate, and Ser or Ala cannot stack.","calc":["phosphate (−) → Lys⁺ or Arg⁺","aromatic ring → Phe, Tyr, Trp or His","pair: one from each list, e.g. Lys + Trp"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match each residue to its labeled region and its job: Cys cross-links, Gly adds nothing, and Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg give charges; for a phosphate in a ring pocket, pair a + residue (Lys/Arg) with an aromatic one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-24c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:24#3","pe1-cp:24#1","pe1-cp:24#2","pe6-cp:10#1","pe6-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:24"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P5","passage":"A designed peptide is spun into fibers for a wound-dressing gel. Its cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) joins neighboring strands, and its cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp) gives migrating cells a surface to grip through electrostatic contacts at body pH. A Ser–Met linker joins region B to an assembly region of six glutamates, whose side-chain pKa is about 4.3. Samples equilibrated at different pH values were tested for the percentage of peptide found in fibers, and the results are graphed. When the wet gel is left in air, links form between neighboring strands and the gel turns stiff. Soaking the stiff gel in mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent, makes it soft again. In the wound, a kinase adds a regulatory phosphate to the peptide: mass spectrometry places it on a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol, and replacing that residue with alanine prevents the labeling.","q":"Which residue of the peptide carries the phosphate the kinase adds?","choices":["Serine","Asparagine","Tyrosine","Cysteine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Asparagine ends in an amide, –C(=O)NH₂; it has no –OH at all."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Tyrosine's OH sits on a ring, which makes it a phenol, not a primary alcohol."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Cysteine has –SH, a thiol, not an alcohol."}],"rule":"Primary alcohol means –CH₂OH; Thr's OH is secondary, Tyr's is a phenol, Cys has SH.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphate goes on OH residues: Ser, Thr, Tyr. Primary alcohol = CH2–OH, which is serine; Tyr's OH sits on a ring (phenol)."},"exp":"A **primary alcohol** has its –OH on a carbon bonded to only one other carbon: **–CH₂–OH**. That is serine's side chain. Tyrosine's –OH is on a ring (a phenol), asparagine ends in an amide with no –OH, and cysteine has –SH, not –OH.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain matches the description the report gives: an –OH on a carbon with one carbon neighbor?","know":"• **Primary alcohol**: –CH₂–OH (1 C on the carbinol carbon).\n• Ser –CH₂OH (1°); Thr –CH(OH)CH₃ (2°); Tyr ring–OH (phenol); Asn –CH₂CONH₂ (amide, no OH); Cys –CH₂SH (thiol).\n• Use the description given, not the residue you expect to see phosphorylated.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7's clue is the class of alcohol: primary.","hl":"side chain is a primary alcohol"},{"text":"Find the side chain that ends in –CH₂–OH.","fig":["serine-sc","t1-phosphoserine"],"calc":["Ser: –CH₂–OH → C bearing OH has 1 C neighbor → **1°** ✓"]},{"text":"Tyrosine's OH sits on a ring (a phenol), and asparagine ends in an amide with no OH.","strike":["Asparagine","Tyrosine"]},{"text":"Cysteine ends in –CH₂–SH: a thiol, not an alcohol.","strike":["Cysteine"]},{"text":"Answer: serine."}],"trap":"Picking tyrosine because it is also phosphorylated: the report names a primary alcohol, and only serine fits."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"primary alcohol","note":"–CH₂–OH"},{"t":"regulatory phosphate","note":"goes on an –OH"},{"t":"replacing that residue with alanine","note":"remove the –OH, lose the label"}]},{"text":"**Translate the description into a structure.** Primary means the –OH carbon has one carbon neighbor.","calc":["primary: R–CH₂–OH","secondary: R–CH(OH)–R′","phenol: ring–OH"]},{"text":"**Match it to a side chain.** Only serine ends in –CH₂–OH.","fig":"serine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** serine is a primary alcohol, threonine a secondary alcohol, tyrosine a phenol, and alanine only a methyl group."},{"text":"**Also:** phosphorylation goes on a side-chain –OH, so look for Ser, Thr or Tyr and reject side chains with no hydroxyl."},{"text":"**Also:** in a kinase assay with [γ-³²P]ATP, the label ends up only where the enzyme can build a phosphoester, on a side-chain hydroxyl. Look at the one residue that differs between the peptides: Ser, Thr or Tyr can take the ³²P, while a residue with no hydroxyl, such as alanine, leucine or valine, leaves its peptide unlabeled.","calc":["–OH present → phosphoester → ³²P in the peptide","no –OH (Ala, Leu, Val) → no ³²P"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** turn the description into a structure first (primary alcohol → –CH₂OH → serine), then pick the residue that matches it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-25c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:25#3","pe1-cp:25#1","pe1-cp:25#2","pe3-cp:43#1","pe5-cp:31#3","pe5-cp:31#1","pe5-cp:31#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:25"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P5","passage":"A designed peptide is spun into fibers for a wound-dressing gel. Its cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) joins neighboring strands, and its cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp) gives migrating cells a surface to grip through electrostatic contacts at body pH. A Ser–Met linker joins region B to an assembly region of six glutamates, whose side-chain pKa is about 4.3. Samples equilibrated at different pH values were tested for the percentage of peptide found in fibers, and the results are graphed. When the wet gel is left in air, links form between neighboring strands and the gel turns stiff. Soaking the stiff gel in mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent, makes it soft again. In the wound, a kinase adds a regulatory phosphate to the peptide: mass spectrometry places it on a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol, and replacing that residue with alanine prevents the labeling.","q":"Which explanation best fits the fiber data in the graph?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide found in fibers after equilibration","x":{"label":"pH","min":2,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"In fibers (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Fibers","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[2,95],[3,90],[4,65],[5,20],[6,5],[7,2],[8,2]]}]},"choices":["Protonated glutamates stop repelling","Deprotonated glutamates attract","Protonated glutamates form ion pairs","Deprotonated glutamates shed water"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Deprotonated glutamates all carry −1, so they repel; that is why fibers vanish at high pH."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Charged carboxylates hold water more tightly, not less, and fibers fall rather than rise as they form."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A protonated –COOH is neutral, so it has no charge with which to form an ion pair."}],"rule":"Compare pH with the side chain's pKa: charged chains repel and stay apart; when the pH neutralizes them, they can pack together.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Fibers appear below about pH 4, near the glutamate pKa. Below it COO⁻ becomes neutral COOH, so the strands stop repelling and pack."},"exp":"Fibers form **below pH ≈ 4.3**, the glutamate side-chain pKa. Under that pH, the side chains are **protonated and neutral (–COOH)**, so the strands **no longer repel** and can pack into fibers. Above it they are **–COO⁻**, all negative, and like charges push the strands apart. A neutral –COOH has no charge to make an ion pair.","how":{"ask":"Link the pH where fibers form to the side chain's pKa and charge.","know":"• Acidic side chain: pH < pKa → –COOH (0); pH > pKa → –COO⁻ (−1).\n• Like charges repel and keep strands apart; neutral strands can pack.\n• A salt bridge needs opposite charges.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the glutamate pKa; the graph (sentence 4) shows fibers only at pH below it.","hl":"side-chain pKa is about 4.3"},{"text":"Above the pKa every glutamate is −1: they repel rather than attract, and they hold water more tightly.","strike":["Deprotonated glutamates attract","Deprotonated glutamates shed water"]},{"text":"Below the pKa the –COOH is neutral, so it has no charge to pair.","strike":["Protonated glutamates form ion pairs"]},{"text":"Answer: protonated glutamates stop repelling."}],"trap":"Reaching for ion pairs whenever charge matters: a protonated –COOH is neutral and has no charge to pair."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"six glutamates","note":"acidic side chains"},{"t":"pKa is about 4.3","note":"charge switch"},{"t":"percentage of peptide found in fibers","note":"read the graph"}]},{"text":"**Charge on each side of the pKa.**","calc":["pH < 4.3: –COOH, 0","pH > 4.3: –COO⁻, −1"]},{"text":"**Neutral strands pack; charged strands repel.**"},{"text":"**Also:** a group grafted onto a residue sets its charge the same way: an added –COOH (pKa ≈ 4) is ionized at pH 7, so the residue becomes acidic; an added amine (pKa ≈ 9–10) is protonated, so it becomes basic."},{"text":"**Also:** a side chain's charge changes only when the pH moves past its pKa; acids go 0 → −1, bases go +1 → 0 as pH rises."},{"text":"**Also:** charged side chains repel and keep peptides apart; when the pH neutralizes them, the strands can pack together."},{"text":"**Also:** at a fixed pH, lowering a group's pKa means less of it is protonated, so a structure held together by the protonated form (such as C·C⁺ pairs in an i-motif) is less stable; compare pK values to see which change lowered it.","calc":["pKa 6.5 → 6.0 at pH 6.5: 50% → 24% protonated → less stable"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same pKa-versus-pH test works for a group added to a residue. A grafted –CH₂NH₃⁺ with pKa 9.5 sits about 2 units above pH 7.4, so it stays protonated (+1) and makes the side chain basic.","calc":["9.5 − 7.4 = 2.1 units","[–CH₂NH₂] : [–CH₂NH₃⁺] = 10^(7.4 − 9.5) ≈ 1 : 126","≈ 99% carries +1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare the pH with each side chain's pKa: charged chains repel and stay apart, and neutralized chains can pack together."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-26a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:50#1","pe3-cp:52#1","pe3-cp:52#2","pe5-cp:28#1","pe5-cp:28#2","pe3-cp:50#2","pe3-cp:50#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:26"],"q":"In a muscle bed, blood moves at 2.0 cm/s through arterioles with a combined cross-sectional area of 40 cm². The capillaries they feed total 800 cm². What is the capillary blood speed, and why?","choices":["0.10 cm/s, larger combined area","0.10 cm/s, each capillary is thinnest","40 cm/s, larger combined area","2.0 cm/s, equal volume per second"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"The same speed through twenty times the area would carry twenty times the flow; flow cannot grow."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: more combined area means slower blood (Q = Av), so the speed must fall below 2.0."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The number is right but not the reason: one thin tube would speed blood up; the many capillaries in parallel slow it."}],"rule":"Q = A·v is the same in every section, so speed scales by old area ÷ new area: N times the area gives N times slower flow.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Speed ∝ 1/total area. Area grows 800/40 = 20×, so speed drops 20×: 2.0 ÷ 20 = 0.10 cm/s."},"exp":"**Continuity: Q = A·v is the same at every level.** Q = 40 cm² × 2.0 cm/s = 80 cm³/s, so v = 80 ÷ 800 = **0.10 cm/s**. Blood crawls through capillaries because their **total** area is twenty times larger, not because each one is thin: one thin tube alone would speed blood up.","how":{"ask":"The same volume per second passes the arterioles and the capillaries. How fast must it move through twenty times the total area?","know":"• **Continuity: Q = A·v** (Q = flow rate in cm³/s, A = cross-sectional area, v = mean speed). For blood, Q is the same at every level of the circulation.\n• Vessels in parallel: use their **combined** area.\n• Larger total A → smaller v. Capillaries have the largest total area, so blood is slowest there.","steps":[{"text":"Find the flow rate in the arterioles.","hl":"2.0 cm/s through arterioles with a combined cross-sectional area of 40 cm²","calc":["Q = A × v","Q = 40 cm² × 2.0 cm/s = **80 cm³/s**"]},{"text":"The same Q passes the capillaries; divide by their total area.","hl":"total 800 cm²","calc":["v = Q ÷ A = 80 cm³/s ÷ 800 cm²","v = **0.10 cm/s**","area × 20 → speed ÷ 20"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Same flow (80 cm³/s): speed falls as total area grows","x":{"label":"Total cross-sectional area (cm²)","min":0,"max":800,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"v = Q ÷ A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,2],[80,1],[160,0.5],[400,0.2],[800,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"Equal speed would need equal area; 40 cm/s treats the capillaries like a nozzle; \"each capillary is thinnest\" is the wrong reason, since one thin tube would speed blood up.","strike":["2.0 cm/s, equal volume per second","40 cm/s, larger combined area","0.10 cm/s, each capillary is thinnest"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.10 cm/s, because the combined area is larger."}],"trap":"Thinking narrow vessels speed blood up. One narrow tube would, but the blood is split among countless capillaries whose combined area is far larger than the arterioles'."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 cm/s","note":"arteriole speed"},{"t":"40 cm²","note":"arteriole total area"},{"t":"800 cm²","note":"capillary total area"}]},{"text":"**Continuity:** blood is not lost between levels, so the volume per second is the same everywhere.","calc":["Q = A·v = constant"]},{"text":"**Combined area decides the speed.** More total area for the same Q means slower blood.","calc":["v = Q ÷ A"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Speed versus total area at a fixed flow of 80 cm³/s","x":{"label":"Total cross-sectional area (cm²)","min":0,"max":800,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"v = Q ÷ A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,2],[80,1],[160,0.5],[400,0.2],[800,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** given n vessels each of area a, total area = n·a, so u = Av/(na).","calc":["n = 1.6 × 10⁹ capillaries, a = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ cm² each","n·a = 1.6 × 10⁹ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 800 cm²","u = Av/(na) = (40 × 2.0) ÷ 800 = 0.10 cm/s"]},{"text":"**Also:** for one tube that narrows (no branching), A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ with A = πd²/4, so area goes as d² and v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)². Halving the diameter gives 4× the speed; a diameter one-third as wide gives 9×.","calc":["d: 1.2 cm → 0.6 cm, v₁ = 15 cm/s","v₂ = 15 × (1.2/0.6)² = 15 × 4 = 60 cm/s","d₁/d₂ = 3 → (3)² = 9× faster"]},{"text":"**Also:** when a passage gives a tube's diameter instead of its area, halve it to get r and use A = πr². If the speed is in mm/s and the area in μm², multiply the speed by 1000 (1 mm = 1000 μm) before Q = A·v. Using d in place of r makes A four times too big.","calc":["d = 20 μm → r = 10 μm","A = π(10 μm)² ≈ 314 μm²","v = 0.75 mm/s × 1000 = 750 μm/s","Q = 314 μm² × 750 μm/s ≈ 2.4 × 10⁵ μm³/s"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = A·v is the same at every level, so speed is smallest where the total cross-sectional area is largest: the capillaries."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-27a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:27#1","pe1-cp:27#2","pe1-cp:27#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:27"],"q":"On a potential–pH diagram, the boundary between Cu²⁺ and copper metal stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5. Which species is absent from its half-reaction?","choices":["H⁺ and OH⁻","The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A boundary at a set potential is a redox couple, so electrons must appear."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Cu²⁺ is one of the two species the line separates, so it must be in the half-reaction."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Cu metal is one of the two species the line separates, so it must be in the half-reaction."}],"rule":"On a potential–pH diagram, a flat line has electrons but no H⁺/OH⁻; a vertical line has H⁺/OH⁻ but no electrons.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Flat line vs pH means pH has zero effect, so the half-reaction contains no H+ or OH−. Electrons and both copper forms must still be there."},"exp":"A **horizontal** boundary keeps the same potential whatever the pH, so the equilibrium **does not depend on pH**: its half-reaction, Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu, contains **no H⁺ or OH⁻**. It still transfers electrons, and both copper forms appear in it.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a boundary at one fixed potential across a range of pH says about the half-reaction.","know":"• Pourbaix diagram: potential E (y) versus pH (x); each line is an equilibrium between two forms.\n• **Horizontal line**: E does not change with pH → **pH-independent** → no H⁺ or OH⁻ in the half-reaction, but electrons are transferred.","steps":[{"text":"The boundary is flat: one potential across several pH values.","hl":"stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5","calc":["E constant while pH changes → pH-independent"]},{"text":"Write the half-reaction it represents.","calc":["Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu   (E° = +0.34 V)","no H⁺, no OH⁻"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"A line set at a potential is a redox couple, so electrons must be in it; Cu²⁺ and Cu are the two forms the line separates.","strike":["The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"]},{"text":"Answer: H⁺ and OH⁻."}],"trap":"Thinking a flat line means \"no electrons\": that describes a vertical line. A flat line is a redox couple with no H⁺ or OH⁻."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"potential–pH diagram","note":"E on y, pH on x"},{"t":"stays at +0.34 V","note":"horizontal line"},{"t":"from pH 0 to 5","note":"pH changes, E does not"}]},{"text":"**Read the slope of each boundary.** A flat line sits at one potential, whatever the pH.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"**Flat = pH has no say.** If changing pH does not move the line, H⁺ and OH⁻ are not in the equilibrium.","calc":["horizontal → electrons yes, H⁺/OH⁻ no"]},{"text":"**Also:** a vertical Pourbaix boundary means the equilibrium depends on pH but transfers no electrons."},{"text":"**Also:** the higher a region lies on a potential–pH diagram, the more oxidized the species stable there."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a horizontal Pourbaix boundary is a redox equilibrium that does not depend on pH (no H⁺ or OH⁻ involved)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-28a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:28#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:28"],"q":"Phosphorus pentachloride, PCl₅, has five chlorine atoms bonded to a central phosphorus atom that carries no lone pairs. Which hybridization describes the phosphorus?","choices":["dsp³","sp³d²","sp²","sp"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That set makes six orbitals, for six domains; PCl₅'s phosphorus has five bonds and no lone pair."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"sp² gives 3 orbitals: enough for the 3 equatorial Cl only, not the 2 axial ones."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"sp gives only 2 orbitals; phosphorus needs 5, one for each P–Cl bond."}],"rule":"Count domains on the central atom (bonds + lone pairs); the hybrid set must have exactly that many orbitals.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count domains, add up orbitals: 2 sp, 3 sp², 4 sp³, 5 sp³d (dsp³), 6 sp³d². Five Cl, no lone pairs = 5 → dsp³."},"exp":"Five bonded atoms and no lone pairs = **5 electron domains**, so phosphorus needs **5 hybrid orbitals**: dsp³, also written **sp³d** (1 s + 3 p + 1 d = 5), pointing to the corners of a **trigonal bipyramid**. sp³d² makes 6 (octahedral), sp² makes 3 and sp makes 2.","how":{"ask":"Count phosphorus's electron domains, then pick the hybridization label that makes exactly that many orbitals.","know":"• Hybrid orbitals needed = electron domains (bonded atoms + lone pairs).\n• **2 → sp; 3 → sp²; 4 → sp³ (tetrahedral); 5 → sp³d = dsp³ (trigonal bipyramidal); 6 → sp³d² or d²sp³ (octahedral)**.\n• Count the orbitals in a label: s = 1, then read each superscript for p and d (no superscript = 1).","steps":[{"text":"Count phosphorus's domains: five bonds and no lone pairs.","hl":"five chlorine atoms bonded to a central phosphorus atom that carries no lone pairs","calc":["domains = 5 bonds + 0 lone pairs = **5**","5 domains → **5** hybrid orbitals → trigonal bipyramidal"]},{"text":"Count the orbitals each label makes.","calc":["dsp³ (= sp³d): 1 + 1 + 3 = **5** → trigonal bipyramidal","sp³d²: 1 + 3 + 2 = 6 → octahedral","sp²: 1 + 2 = 3 → trigonal planar","sp: 1 + 1 = 2 → linear"]},{"text":"sp³d² makes six orbitals, which would need a sixth domain; phosphorus's 5 valence electrons all go into the five P–Cl bonds, leaving no lone pair.","strike":["sp³d²"]},{"text":"sp² covers only the three equatorial Cl, and sp only the two axial ones; five bonds need five orbitals.","strike":["sp²","sp"]},{"text":"Answer: dsp³ (sp³d)."}],"trap":"sp³d² also uses d orbitals, so it looks right for any expanded octet, but it makes 6 orbitals: the octahedral set of SF₆, not the 5-orbital trigonal bipyramidal set of PCl₅."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"five chlorine atoms","note":"5 bonded atoms"},{"t":"no lone pairs","note":"0 extra domains"},{"t":"hybridization","note":"how many orbitals mix"}]},{"text":"**Electron domains** = atoms bonded + lone pairs. Each domain needs one hybrid orbital.","calc":["PCl₅: 5 + 0 = 5 domains → 5 orbitals"]},{"text":"**Match the count to a label.**","calc":["4 → sp³ (CH₄, tetrahedral)","5 → sp³d = dsp³ (PCl₅, trigonal bipyramidal)","6 → sp³d² or d²sp³ (SF₆, octahedral)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the order of letters doesn't change the count: dsp³ and sp³d both mix one s, three p and one d orbital; d²sp³ and sp³d² both make six, the octahedral set seen in six-coordinate metal complexes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 5 domains → trigonal bipyramidal → sp³d (dsp³); 6 → sp³d²; 4 → sp³."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-29b","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:29#2","pe1-cp:29#1","pe5-cp:15#1","pe5-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:29"],"q":"A chemist compares 0.200 mol of neon at 127 °C and 0.820 atm with the ideal-gas model, taking R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K). Which option lists one ideal-gas assumption and the volume the model gives?","choices":["No attractions; 8.0 L","No attractions; 4.5 L","Weak attractions; 8.0 L","Weak attractions; 4.5 L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"22.4 L/mol holds only at STP; this gas is hot and below 1 atm, so use PV = nRT."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The ideal model assumes no intermolecular attractions at all, not weak ones."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong premise and an STP volume: ideal particles do not attract, and 22.4 L/mol needs 0 °C and 1 atm."}],"rule":"Ideal gas: point particles with no attractions. Use V = nRT/P with T in kelvin; 22.4 L/mol works only at STP.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"nRT ≈ 0.2 × 0.082 × 400 ≈ 6.6; ÷ 0.82 ≈ 8 L. Then pick the premise with zero attractions."},"exp":"The ideal-gas model treats particles as points with **no intermolecular attractions** (and no volume of their own). Volume: V = nRT/P = (0.200)(0.0821)(400 K) ÷ 0.820 ≈ **8.0 L**. 4.5 L is 0.200 × 22.4, valid only at STP.","how":{"ask":"Pick the true premise, then compute V = nRT/P.","know":"• Ideal gas: negligible particle volume, no attractions, elastic collisions.\n• V = nRT/P with T in K.\n• 22.4 L/mol only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm).","steps":[{"text":"Convert T to kelvin.","hl":"127 °C","calc":["T = 127 + 273 = 400 K"]},{"text":"Compute V.","calc":["V = (0.200)(0.0821)(400) ÷ 0.820","= 6.57 ÷ 0.820 ≈ 8.0 L"]},{"text":"22.4 L/mol is for STP only.","strike":["No attractions; 4.5 L","Weak attractions; 4.5 L"]},{"text":"Ideal particles have no attractions, not weak ones.","strike":["Weak attractions; 8.0 L"]},{"text":"Answer: no attractions; 8.0 L."}],"trap":"Using 22.4 L/mol away from STP, or calling weak attractions ideal."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"0.200 mol","note":"n"},{"t":"127 °C","note":"convert to K"},{"t":"0.820 atm","note":"P"}]},{"text":"**Premise:** ideal particles occupy no volume and feel no attractions."},{"text":"**Volume:** V = nRT/P in kelvin.","calc":["V ≈ 8.0 L"]},{"text":"**Also:** a gas is most ideal at high T and low P, where particle volume and intermolecular forces are both negligible."},{"text":"**Also:** run it backward to count moles: at STP, n = V ÷ 22.4 L/mol, the same answer PV/RT gives. Put the volume in litres first: 1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L.","calc":["112 cm³ = 112 mL = 0.112 L","n = 0.112 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 0.0050 mol","check: n = PV/RT = (1 atm)(0.112 L) ÷ (0.0821 × 273) = 0.0050 mol ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ideal gas = point particles with no attractions; V = nRT/P in kelvin, and 22.4 L/mol only at STP."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-30a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:30"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P6","passage":"Biologists studied how a deep-sea fish senses its dim surroundings. Its rods contain a single visual pigment, whose absorbance spectrum is graphed, and each rod reports only how many photons its pigment absorbs. In one test, a 450-nm light and then a 530-nm light were adjusted so that the pigment captured equal numbers of photons per second from each. The fish also smells limonene, a small hydrocarbon odorant held by an odorant-binding protein in a buried cavity sealed off from the surrounding water; mutations that put charged residues into the cavity wall abolish binding. Males release (E)-4-chlorooct-4-ene as a mating signal, while its Z isomer is inactive. A pure sample of either isomer stays unchanged for weeks at room temperature, and the two interconvert only under ultraviolet light, which briefly breaks the π bond. A gill parasite switches off the fish's proteins by AMPylation: its enzyme attaches an AMP unit from ATP to a threonine side chain of the target and releases pyrophosphate. To detect this, researchers supply ATP carrying ³³P at one or more phosphate positions and check only the target protein for radioactivity.","q":"Can the fish tell the 450-nm and 530-nm lights apart?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance of the fish's rod pigment","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]}]},"choices":["No; one pigment, one signal","Yes; 530-nm light signals more","Yes; bluer photons signal more","Yes; by comparing two pigments"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Absorption was matched on purpose, so the longer-wavelength light gives the same signal, not a bigger one."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A rod counts photons; once the counts match, photon energy does not change the signal."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"There is only one pigment here, so there is no second signal to compare."}],"rule":"One pigment reports only photons absorbed; telling wavelengths apart needs two or more pigments with different peaks, compared by ratio.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Equal photons absorbed by the only pigment means identical signals. With nothing to compare, the two lights look the same."},"exp":"A photoreceptor reports only **how many photons it absorbs**, not their wavelength. The intensities were set so the single pigment absorbs equal numbers from both lights, so the rods send **identical signals** and the fish cannot tell them apart. Color vision needs **a second pigment with a different peak**, so the brain can compare the two responses.","how":{"ask":"Does anything differ between the two signals once absorption is matched?","know":"• A pigment signals photons absorbed, not wavelength (univariance).\n• Color vision needs two or more pigments with different peaks; the brain compares their ratio.\n• Matching absorption for the only pigment erases any difference.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the rods hold one pigment, and each rod reports only how many photons it absorbs.","hl":"a single visual pigment"},{"text":"Sentence 3: absorption was matched for the two lights, so the signals are equal whatever the photon energy.","hl":"captured equal numbers of photons per second from each","strike":["Yes; 530-nm light signals more","Yes; bluer photons signal more"]},{"text":"With one pigment there is no second signal to compare.","strike":["Yes; by comparing two pigments"]},{"text":"Answer: no; one pigment, one signal."}],"trap":"Thinking the wavelength itself is signaled: only the count of absorbed photons is."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a single visual pigment","note":"one signal only"},{"t":"equal numbers of photons per second","note":"matched absorption"},{"t":"only how many photons","note":"no color information"}]},{"text":"**One pigment sends one number**, so matched lights look identical."},{"text":"**Add a second pigment** with another peak and the two lights give different ratios: that comparison is color vision.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two pigments, two peaks","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]},{"name":"Second pigment (560 nm)","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[450,0.01],[480,0.1],[510,0.4],[530,0.7],[545,0.92],[560,1],[575,0.92],[590,0.7],[610,0.4],[640,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a single pigment reports only photons absorbed; color vision needs two or more pigments with different peaks whose responses the brain compares."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-31a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:31#1","pe1-cp:31#2","pe4-cp:36#1","pe4-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:31"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P6","passage":"Biologists studied how a deep-sea fish senses its dim surroundings. Its rods contain a single visual pigment, whose absorbance spectrum is graphed, and each rod reports only how many photons its pigment absorbs. In one test, a 450-nm light and then a 530-nm light were adjusted so that the pigment captured equal numbers of photons per second from each. The fish also smells limonene, a small hydrocarbon odorant held by an odorant-binding protein in a buried cavity sealed off from the surrounding water; mutations that put charged residues into the cavity wall abolish binding. Males release (E)-4-chlorooct-4-ene as a mating signal, while its Z isomer is inactive. A pure sample of either isomer stays unchanged for weeks at room temperature, and the two interconvert only under ultraviolet light, which briefly breaks the π bond. A gill parasite switches off the fish's proteins by AMPylation: its enzyme attaches an AMP unit from ATP to a threonine side chain of the target and releases pyrophosphate. To detect this, researchers supply ATP carrying ³³P at one or more phosphate positions and check only the target protein for radioactivity.","q":"Which residue would best suit the wall of the odorant cavity?","fig":"limonene","choices":["Methionine","Asparagine","Glutamine","Histidine"],"choiceFigs":["methionine-sc","asparagine-sc","glutamine-sc","histidine-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Asparagine's amide side chain is polar and H-bonds to water, a poor match for a hydrocarbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glutamine's amide is polar too; it would rather face water than a nonpolar guest."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine's imidazole ring is polar and can be charged, so it suits a water-facing surface."}],"rule":"Match the pocket to the ligand: a nonpolar guest away from water needs nonpolar side chains (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met); polar or charged ones face water.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydrocarbon ligand + no water = hydrophobic lining. Only one choice has a side chain with no N–H, O–H or charge."},"exp":"Limonene is a **hydrocarbon** with no polar groups, and the cavity is **sealed from water**, so it is held by **hydrophobic (dispersion) contacts**. Methionine's side chain (–CH₂CH₂–S–CH₃) is nonpolar. Asparagine and glutamine carry polar amides, and histidine has a polar, ionizable ring; these line water-facing surfaces.","how":{"ask":"Classify the ligand and the pocket, then pick the side chain that matches.","know":"• Nonpolar ligand in a water-free pocket → hydrophobic lining (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met).\n• Polar (Asn, Gln, Ser, Thr) and charged or ionizable (Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, His) side chains prefer water.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the odorant is a hydrocarbon held in a cavity away from water.","hl":"sealed off from the surrounding water","fig":"limonene"},{"text":"Amide side chains are polar.","strike":["Asparagine","Glutamine"]},{"text":"Histidine's ring is polar and can carry a + charge.","strike":["Histidine"]},{"text":"Answer: methionine, whose thioether side chain is nonpolar."}],"trap":"Picking any long side chain: polar amides do not suit a hydrocarbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a small hydrocarbon odorant","note":"nonpolar guest"},{"t":"sealed off from the surrounding water","note":"water-free pocket"},{"t":"charged residues","note":"polar walls fail"}]},{"text":"**Like holds like.** A nonpolar guest is held by nonpolar side chains through dispersion contacts.","calc":["nonpolar: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met","polar: Asn, Gln, Ser, Thr","ionizable: Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, His"]},{"text":"**Also:** charged or polar side chains don't fit a pocket for a nonpolar ligand; swapping in Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, Ser or Thr weakens binding."},{"text":"**Also:** when a nonpolar part of a drug sits in a water-free pocket, its main interaction is hydrophobic contact (London dispersion) with nonpolar side chains such as Leu, Ile, Val and Phe, not hydrogen bonds or salt bridges."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a nonpolar ligand in a water-free pocket is lined by nonpolar side chains (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met); polar and charged side chains face water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-32a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:32#1","pe1-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:32"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P6","passage":"Biologists studied how a deep-sea fish senses its dim surroundings. Its rods contain a single visual pigment, whose absorbance spectrum is graphed, and each rod reports only how many photons its pigment absorbs. In one test, a 450-nm light and then a 530-nm light were adjusted so that the pigment captured equal numbers of photons per second from each. The fish also smells limonene, a small hydrocarbon odorant held by an odorant-binding protein in a buried cavity sealed off from the surrounding water; mutations that put charged residues into the cavity wall abolish binding. Males release (E)-4-chlorooct-4-ene as a mating signal, while its Z isomer is inactive. A pure sample of either isomer stays unchanged for weeks at room temperature, and the two interconvert only under ultraviolet light, which briefly breaks the π bond. A gill parasite switches off the fish's proteins by AMPylation: its enzyme attaches an AMP unit from ATP to a threonine side chain of the target and releases pyrophosphate. To detect this, researchers supply ATP carrying ³³P at one or more phosphate positions and check only the target protein for radioactivity.","q":"Which ATP label would end up on the parasite's target protein?","fig":"atp","choices":["[α-³³P]ATP","[β-³³P]ATP","[γ-³³P]ATP","[β,γ-³³P]ATP"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The γ phosphate is what kinases transfer; in AMPylation it leaves with the pyrophosphate."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The β phosphate stays bonded to γ and leaves as pyrophosphate, not on the protein."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both labeled phosphates sit in the pyrophosphate that is released."}],"rule":"Find which piece of ATP the enzyme moves: kinases move the γ phosphate; AMP transfer moves adenosine plus the α phosphate, and β + γ leave as PPi.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"AMP = adenosine + the α phosphate. Pyrophosphate = β + γ. Only a label in the AMP part reaches the protein."},"exp":"AMPylation transfers **AMP**, adenosine plus the **α phosphate**, to the threonine and releases **pyrophosphate (PPi)**, which holds the β and γ phosphates. So only an **α label** ends up on the protein. A kinase is different: it transfers the terminal **γ phosphate**, and the kinase itself is the catalyst, not the source.","how":{"ask":"Split ATP into the part transferred and the part released, then follow each label.","know":"• ATP = adenosine–Pα–Pβ–Pγ.\n• Kinases move Pγ; ADP is left behind.\n• AMP transfer moves adenosine–Pα; Pβ–Pγ leaves as pyrophosphate.\n• The enzyme is the catalyst, not the source of the group.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says the parasite's enzyme moves AMP and releases pyrophosphate.","hl":"attaches an AMP unit from ATP","fig":"atp"},{"text":"γ and β labels leave in the pyrophosphate.","hl":"releases pyrophosphate","strike":["[γ-³³P]ATP","[β-³³P]ATP","[β,γ-³³P]ATP"]},{"text":"Answer: [α-³³P]ATP."}],"trap":"Reaching for γ by habit: that is the kinase answer; here AMP, with the α phosphate, is transferred."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is moved and what is released**.","marks":[{"t":"attaches an AMP unit","note":"α phosphate + adenosine"},{"t":"releases pyrophosphate","note":"β + γ leave"},{"t":"only the target protein","note":"what is counted"}]},{"text":"**Map each phosphate.**","calc":["Pα → with AMP → protein","Pβ, Pγ → PPi → released"]},{"text":"**Also:** a kinase is the catalyst; the phosphate it attaches comes from ATP's γ phosphate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** follow the piece of ATP the enzyme transfers: γ for a kinase, α (with adenosine) for AMP transfer; labels elsewhere leave in ADP or PPi."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-33a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:33#1","pe1-cp:33#2","pe6-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:13#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:33"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P6","passage":"Biologists studied how a deep-sea fish senses its dim surroundings. Its rods contain a single visual pigment, whose absorbance spectrum is graphed, and each rod reports only how many photons its pigment absorbs. In one test, a 450-nm light and then a 530-nm light were adjusted so that the pigment captured equal numbers of photons per second from each. The fish also smells limonene, a small hydrocarbon odorant held by an odorant-binding protein in a buried cavity sealed off from the surrounding water; mutations that put charged residues into the cavity wall abolish binding. Males release (E)-4-chlorooct-4-ene as a mating signal, while its Z isomer is inactive. A pure sample of either isomer stays unchanged for weeks at room temperature, and the two interconvert only under ultraviolet light, which briefly breaks the π bond. A gill parasite switches off the fish's proteins by AMPylation: its enzyme attaches an AMP unit from ATP to a threonine side chain of the target and releases pyrophosphate. To detect this, researchers supply ATP carrying ³³P at one or more phosphate positions and check only the target protein for radioactivity.","q":"Which carbon of the mating signal is sp² and belongs to the non-rotating bond?","fig":"cpd-e-4-chlorooct-4-ene","choices":["C1","C4","C7","C8"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"C1 is the CH₃ end carbon: sp³, tetrahedral and free to rotate."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C7 is a CH₂ in the chain with four single bonds, so it is sp³."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"C8 is the terminal CH₃: sp³ and tetrahedral."}],"rule":"Only the two carbons of the C=C are sp² (trigonal planar, about 120°) and locked against rotation; every carbon with four single bonds is sp³.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The name says where the double bond is: oct-4-ene means C4=C5. Pick C4 or C5."},"exp":"Geometric isomers exist because the **C4=C5 double bond** cannot rotate without breaking its π bond, which the UV light does briefly. Each carbon of a C=C has three electron domains, so it is **sp², trigonal planar**. C4 is one of them; C1, C7 and C8 have four single bonds and are sp³.","how":{"ask":"Find the C=C from the name, then pick a carbon in it.","know":"• E/Z (cis/trans) isomers come from a C=C, which cannot rotate.\n• C=C carbons have 3 domains → sp², trigonal planar.\n• Carbons with four single bonds are sp³, tetrahedral.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the isomers interconvert only when the π bond breaks, so the C=C is the locked bond.","hl":"briefly breaks the π bond","fig":"cpd-e-4-chlorooct-4-ene"},{"text":"Sentence 5 names the signal: \"oct-4-ene\" puts the double bond between C4 and C5.","hl":"(E)-4-chlorooct-4-ene"},{"text":"C1, C7 and C8 have only single bonds: sp³.","strike":["C1","C7","C8"]},{"text":"Answer: C4."}],"trap":"Picking a carbon next to the double bond: an allylic CH₂ is still sp³."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"its Z isomer","note":"cis/trans pair"},{"t":"stays unchanged for weeks","note":"no rotation"},{"t":"briefly breaks the π bond","note":"the C=C is locked"}]},{"text":"**Count domains on each carbon.**","calc":["C4, C5: three domains → sp², planar","C1–C3, C6–C8: four → sp³"]},{"text":"**Also:** CO₂'s carbon works the same way: two O atoms, two double bonds, each counted once, no lone pair → 2 domains → sp, linear, 180°."},{"text":"**Also:** cis/trans isomers exist because a C=C cannot rotate without breaking its π bond."},{"text":"**Also:** a Schiff base is an imine, R₂C=N–R′, made when an amine condenses with an aldehyde or ketone. Count its C=N carbon like an alkene carbon: two single-bonded neighbors plus the N, with the double bond counted once, and no lone pair. So it is sp², trigonal planar, with angles near 120°.","calc":["imine C: 3 atoms + 0 lone pairs = 3 → sp², ≈120°","imine N: 2 atoms + 1 lone pair = 3 → sp² as well"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the carbons of a C=C are sp² and trigonal planar, and the C=C cannot rotate, which is why cis/trans isomers exist."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-34b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:34#2","pe1-cp:34#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:34"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P7","passage":"A fungal lipase is being engineered for a laundry detergent. Incubated with triolein in an unbuffered emulsion, the enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact, and the pH of the emulsion falls as the reaction runs. So that the lipase binds a positively charged purification resin more tightly, engineers made a variant with four replacements: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His; it folds normally and keeps its activity. Two other mutants, A and B, carry changes in a flexible surface loop. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%; unheated samples of all three had equal activity. Finally, the enzymes are tested in a rinse with a pH of 5.8 and in a wash solution with a pH of 7.8, both read at the same temperature.","q":"Apart from oleic acid, what product does the lipase release from triolein?","fig":"triacylglycerol","choices":["1,3-Dioleoylglycerol","1,2-Dioleoylglycerol","2-Oleoylglycerol","1-Oleoylglycerol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is what pancreatic lipase leaves (ends cut, middle kept); this enzyme does the opposite."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Cutting the middle ester frees C2's –OH; a 1,2-diester would still hold the middle ester."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A monoester at C1 would need an end ester cut too, and this enzyme leaves both ends intact."}],"rule":"Lipases hydrolyze ester bonds: remove only the acyl groups at the positions the passage says are cut, and keep the rest.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only C2 is cut: the two end oleates stay, so the glycerol keeps its acyl groups at 1 and 3."},"exp":"A lipase **hydrolyzes ester bonds**, releasing fatty acids, so the pH drops as oleic acid forms. This enzyme cuts **only the C2 ester**, so the glycerol keeps its oleoyl groups on **C1 and C3**: **1,3-dioleoylglycerol**. Pancreatic lipase does the reverse, cutting at C1 and C3 and leaving a 2-monoacylglycerol.","how":{"ask":"Remove only the acyl groups the passage says are cut, then name what is left.","know":"• Lipases hydrolyze the ester bonds of triacylglycerols, freeing fatty acids.\n• Number glycerol C1–C3; the middle carbon is C2.\n• Pancreatic lipase cuts at 1 and 3 and leaves a 2-monoacylglycerol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says only the middle ester is cut.","hl":"only the ester on the middle carbon","fig":"triacylglycerol"},{"text":"Both end esters stay, so the product still has acyl groups at C1 and C3.","strike":["2-Oleoylglycerol","1-Oleoylglycerol"]},{"text":"A 1,2-diester would still hold the middle ester.","strike":["1,2-Dioleoylglycerol"]},{"text":"Answer: 1,3-dioleoylglycerol."}],"trap":"Answering with the pancreatic-lipase product (a 2-monoacylglycerol) by habit."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"only the ester on the middle carbon","note":"cut at C2"},{"t":"leaves the two end esters intact","note":"C1 and C3 keep their chains"},{"t":"the pH of the emulsion falls","note":"a fatty acid is freed"}]},{"text":"**Cut, then name.**","calc":["triolein − C2 oleate → oleic acid + 1,3-dioleoylglycerol"]},{"text":"**Also:** pancreatic lipase cuts only the esters at positions 1 and 3, freeing two fatty acids and leaving the chain on C2: a 2-monoacylglycerol.","calc":["3 chains − 2 cut = 1 left, on C2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lipase hydrolyzes the ester bonds the passage names; remove those acyl groups and name the glycerol by the positions still esterified."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-35b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:35#2","pe1-cp:35#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:35"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P7","passage":"A fungal lipase is being engineered for a laundry detergent. Incubated with triolein in an unbuffered emulsion, the enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact, and the pH of the emulsion falls as the reaction runs. So that the lipase binds a positively charged purification resin more tightly, engineers made a variant with four replacements: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His; it folds normally and keeps its activity. Two other mutants, A and B, carry changes in a flexible surface loop. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%; unheated samples of all three had equal activity. Finally, the enzymes are tested in a rinse with a pH of 5.8 and in a wash solution with a pH of 7.8, both read at the same temperature.","q":"What is the change in the resin-binding variant's net charge at pH 7?","choices":["A decrease of 6","A decrease of 5","No change","An increase of 6"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: + residues (Lys, Arg) become − ones (Glu, Asp), so the net charge must fall."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Counts His as +1, but with a pKa near 6 it is mostly neutral at pH 7."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Each Lys/Arg → Glu/Asp swap is +1 → −1, a change of −2, so the total can't be zero."}],"rule":"At pH 7 Lys and Arg are +1, Asp and Glu −1, His about 0; add up each swap's change (new minus old).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At pH 7: K, R = +1; D, E = −1; A, H ≈ 0. Each + to − swap is −2; three swaps = −6. Ala→His adds nothing."},"exp":"At pH 7, Lys and Arg are **+1**, Glu and Asp are **−1**, and Ala and His are **≈ 0**. Each basic-to-acidic swap goes from +1 to −1, a change of **−2**; there are three of them. Ala→His changes nothing. Total: −2 − 2 − 2 + 0 = **−6**.","how":{"ask":"For each substitution, subtract the old side-chain charge from the new one at pH 7, then add the four changes.","know":"• Charges at pH 7: Arg/Lys **+1**, Asp/Glu **−1**, His **≈ 0**, others **0**.\n• Change at each site = **new − old**.\n• Net change = sum of the site changes.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 lists the four swaps.","hl":"Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His"},{"text":"Compute new − old at each site.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc","glutamate","aspartate-sc","histidine-sc"],"calc":["Lys→Glu: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Arg→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Lys→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Ala→His: 0 − 0 = **0**"]},{"text":"Add them.","calc":["Δ = −2 + (−2) + (−2) + 0 = **−6**"]},{"text":"Counting His as +1 gives −2 − 2 − 2 + 1 = −5, but at pH 7 His is ≈ 0.","strike":["A decrease of 5"]},{"text":"Seeing three charged residues swapped for three charged residues as no change ignores the sign: each swap trades +1 for −1, a change of −2.","strike":["No change"]},{"text":"Positive charges are replaced by negative ones, so the net charge falls; an increase has the sign flipped.","strike":["An increase of 6"]},{"text":"Answer: a decrease of 6 (Δ = −6)."}],"trap":"Counting His as +1 at pH 7: its side-chain pKa is about 6, so at pH 7 it is mostly neutral and Ala→His adds nothing."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"four replacements","note":"four sites"},{"t":"pH 7","note":"charges at neutral pH"},{"t":"positively charged purification resin","note":"aim: a more negative protein"}]},{"text":"**Assign charges at pH 7.**","calc":["Lys, Arg: +1","Glu, Asp: −1","Ala, His: 0"]},{"text":"**Change per site = new − old.** A + → − swap is worth 2.","calc":["(−1) − (+1) = −2"]},{"text":"**Also:** at pH 7, count Arg and Lys as +1, Asp and Glu as −1, and His and everything else as 0."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for each substitution take new charge minus old charge at the stated pH, then add all the site changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-36a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:36#1","pe1-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:36"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P7","passage":"A fungal lipase is being engineered for a laundry detergent. Incubated with triolein in an unbuffered emulsion, the enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact, and the pH of the emulsion falls as the reaction runs. So that the lipase binds a positively charged purification resin more tightly, engineers made a variant with four replacements: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His; it folds normally and keeps its activity. Two other mutants, A and B, carry changes in a flexible surface loop. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%; unheated samples of all three had equal activity. Finally, the enzymes are tested in a rinse with a pH of 5.8 and in a wash solution with a pH of 7.8, both read at the same temperature.","q":"Which conclusion about the loop mutants do these results support?","choices":["Only mutant A is more stable","Both mutants are more stable","Only mutant B is more stable","Neither mutant is more stable"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Mutant B kept 15%, below the wild type's 35%, so B is less stable, not more."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"B kept 15% vs 35% for wild type: less activity left means less stable, not more."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mutant A kept 70% vs the wild type's 35%, clearly more activity left, so a mutant did gain stability."}],"rule":"Compare each mutant with the wild type on its own: more activity left after heating means more stable.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Higher retained % = more heat-stable. Compare to wild type's 35: A at 70 beats it, B at 15 falls short. So only A."},"exp":"Retained activity is the measure of heat stability: more activity left means less damage. Mutant A kept **70% vs the wild type's 35%**, so it is more stable. Mutant B kept **15%**, **less** than the wild type, so it is not more stable.","how":{"ask":"Line up each mutant's retained activity against the wild type's and decide which are higher.","know":"• **% activity kept after heating**: higher → more heat-stable.\n• Compare every variant with the **wild type**, the reference.\n• A conclusion about \"both\" fails if even one variant does not fit.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the reference and each mutant's retained activity after heating; unheated activity was equal.","hl":"wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%"},{"text":"Compare each with the wild type.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Activity kept after heating","x":{"label":"Enzyme"},"y":{"label":"Activity kept (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Wild type","Mutant A","Mutant B"],"series":[{"name":"Kept","values":[35,70,15]}]},"calc":["A: 70% > 35% → **more stable**","B: 15% < 35% → **not more stable**"]},{"text":"Mutant B kept less than the wild type, so it is neither more stable alone nor along with A.","strike":["Both mutants are more stable","Only mutant B is more stable"]},{"text":"Mutant A clearly kept more, so it is wrong that neither is more stable.","strike":["Neither mutant is more stable"]},{"text":"Answer: only mutant A is more stable."}],"trap":"Assuming every mutant behaves alike: check each one against the wild type separately."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"55 °C","note":"heating step"},{"t":"35%","note":"wild type, the reference"},{"t":"70%","note":"mutant A"},{"t":"15%","note":"mutant B"}]},{"text":"**Heat, cool, assay.** The activity that survives measures how well the enzyme resists heat.","calc":["% kept ↑ → heat stability ↑"]},{"text":"**Subtract the reference.**","calc":["A: 70 − 35 = +35","B: 15 − 35 = −20"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep only the claim the numbers show; reject absolute words like all, fully or every when any activity remains."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare each variant's retained activity with the wild type's; higher means more heat-stable, lower means less."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-37a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:37#1","pe6-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:37"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P7","passage":"A fungal lipase is being engineered for a laundry detergent. Incubated with triolein in an unbuffered emulsion, the enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact, and the pH of the emulsion falls as the reaction runs. So that the lipase binds a positively charged purification resin more tightly, engineers made a variant with four replacements: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His; it folds normally and keeps its activity. Two other mutants, A and B, carry changes in a flexible surface loop. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%; unheated samples of all three had equal activity. Finally, the enzymes are tested in a rinse with a pH of 5.8 and in a wash solution with a pH of 7.8, both read at the same temperature.","q":"How does [H⁺] in the wash solution compare with that in the rinse?","choices":["1/100 as much","100 times as much","1/2 as much","1/20 as much"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: higher pH means less H⁺, and the wash has the higher pH."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Multiplies 2 × 10 instead of raising 10 to the power 2; each pH unit is its own factor of ten."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"pH is a log scale; a 2-unit gap is a power of ten, not a factor of 2."}],"rule":"Each pH unit is a factor of 10 in [H⁺], and higher pH means less H⁺: the factor is 10 raised to the pH difference.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each pH unit = 10× in [H+], and higher pH = less H+. 7.8 − 5.8 = 2 units → 10² = 1/100."},"exp":"Because **pH = −log[H⁺]**, one pH unit corresponds to a **tenfold** change in [H⁺]. The wash is 7.8 − 5.8 = 2 units higher than the rinse, so its [H⁺] is 10² = 100 times lower, **1/100 as much**: about 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M versus 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M.","how":{"ask":"Find the pH difference, turn it into a power of ten, and decide whether a higher pH means more or less H⁺.","know":"• **pH = −log[H⁺]** → [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).\n• A difference of Δ pH units = a factor of **10^Δ** in [H⁺].\n• **Higher pH = less H⁺**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives both pH values; find the change from rinse to wash.","hl":"a pH of 5.8","calc":["ΔpH = 7.8 − 5.8 = **2** units"]},{"text":"Convert to a factor and check with real concentrations.","hl":"a pH of 7.8","calc":["factor = 10² = **100**","pH 5.8: [H⁺] = 10^−5.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M","pH 7.8: [H⁺] = 10^−7.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M","1.6 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ = **100**"],"strike":["1/2 as much","1/20 as much"]},{"text":"Direction: the wash has the higher pH, so it has less H⁺.","strike":["100 times as much"]},{"text":"Answer: 1/100 as much."}],"trap":"\"1/2 as much\" treats pH as linear; every pH unit is a tenfold change, so 2 units is 10 × 10 = 100."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and what you must compare.","marks":[{"t":"a pH of 5.8","note":"sample A"},{"t":"a pH of 7.8","note":"sample B"},{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"H⁺ concentration"}]},{"text":"**pH counts powers of ten:** [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).","calc":["pH 5 → 10⁻⁵ M","pH 6 → 10⁻⁶ M (10 times less)"]},{"text":"**Subtract, then raise 10 to that power.** Then set the direction: higher pH, less H⁺.","calc":["factor = 10^(ΔpH)"]},{"text":"**Also:** compare any solution with pure water (pH 7.0 at 25 °C) the same way. Each pH unit above 7 divides [H⁺] by 10, and since [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ stays fixed, [OH⁻] grows by that same factor. A fractional gap works too: 10 raised to the gap.","calc":["pH 9.0 vs water: gap 2.0 → [H⁺] 100 times lower","[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 10⁻⁹ = 10⁻⁵ M, 100 times water's 10⁻⁷ M","gap 0.5 → 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a change of Δ pH units changes [H⁺] by a factor of 10^Δ, and a lower pH means more H⁺."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-38b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:38#2","pe1-cp:38#1","pe1-cp:38#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:38"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"To which carbon of glucose does phosphorylase attach the phosphate?","choices":["C-1","C-4","C-5","C-6"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"C-6 belongs to glucose 6-phosphate, the later product named in the passage, not to phosphorylase's product."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"C-4 is the ring carbon that links to the next glucose in a glycogen chain; no phosphate goes there."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C-5 bonds the ring oxygen and carries the CH₂OH; neither compound has a phosphate on it."}],"rule":"Read the number in the name: glucose n-phosphate carries its phosphate on Cn. Number from the anomeric C1; C6 is the CH₂OH outside the ring.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The passage names phosphorylase's product: glucose 1-phosphate. The number in the name is the carbon."},"exp":"Phosphorylase makes **glucose 1-phosphate**, so the phosphate sits on **C1, the anomeric carbon**, bonded to the ring O and to an O outside the ring. C6 is the familiar position, but it belongs to glucose 6-phosphate, the later product. In glucopyranose, C1 is anomeric, C2–C4 are ring carbons, C5 bonds the ring O, and C6 is the CH₂OH.","how":{"ask":"Take the carbon number from the compound the passage names for this enzyme.","know":"• Glucopyranose numbering: C1 anomeric; C2–C4 in the ring; C5 bonded to the ring O; C6 = CH₂OH.\n• Glucose n-phosphate → phosphate on Cn.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 names the phosphorylase product.","hl":"the product is glucose 1-phosphate","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose"},{"text":"C-6 belongs to the later product, glucose 6-phosphate.","strike":["C-6"]},{"text":"C-4 and C-5 carry no phosphate in either compound.","strike":["C-4","C-5"]},{"text":"Answer: C-1."}],"trap":"Defaulting to C6 because glucose 6-phosphate is the familiar one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"glucose 1-phosphate","note":"the phosphorylase product"},{"t":"glucose 6-phosphate","note":"a later product"},{"t":"pyranose form","note":"six-membered ring"}]},{"text":"**Number the ring.**","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose","calc":["C1: anomeric","C2–C4: ring","C5: bonded to ring O","C6: CH₂OH"]},{"text":"**Also:** in glucopyranose, C1 is anomeric, C2–C4 are ring carbons, C5 is bonded to the ring O, and C6 is the CH₂OH."},{"text":"**Also:** always put the phosphate where the passage says, then number the ring from C1; do not default to C6."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** glucose n-phosphate has its phosphate on Cn; number from the anomeric C1 and use the compound the passage names, not the familiar C6."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:39#2","pe1-cp:39#1","pe1-cp:43#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:43"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"Compared with normal glycogen of the same mass, the patients' glycogen has fewer:","choices":["Nonreducing ends","α-1,4 glycosidic links","β-1,4 glycosidic links","Reducing ends"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Fewer branches leave longer chains, so the α-1,4 links do not fall; they slightly rise."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Each glycogen molecule has one reducing end, however much it branches."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycogen has no β-1,4 links at all; those belong to cellulose."}],"rule":"Each branch point is one α-1,6 bond and adds one more nonreducing end; fewer branches means fewer of both.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count ends: a molecule with b branch points has b + 1 nonreducing ends and still one reducing end."},"exp":"Each branch hangs from an **α-1,6 bond** made by the branching enzyme, and each one starts a new chain with its own **nonreducing end**. With few branches the glycogen has **fewer α-1,6 bonds and fewer nonreducing ends**. It still has one reducing end per molecule, more α-1,4 links in its long chains, and no β-1,4 links at all.","how":{"ask":"Trace what each branch point adds, then take most of them away.","know":"• Branch points are α-1,6 bonds; chains are α-1,4.\n• Each branch adds one nonreducing end; a molecule has one reducing end.\n• β-1,4 links are in cellulose, not glycogen.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1: the branching enzyme is missing, so few α-1,6 branch points form.","hl":"very few branch points","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose"},{"text":"Fewer branches → fewer chain tips → fewer nonreducing ends."},{"text":"α-1,4 links do not drop, the reducing end stays at one, and glycogen has no β-1,4 links.","strike":["α-1,4 glycosidic links","Reducing ends","β-1,4 glycosidic links"]},{"text":"Answer: nonreducing ends."}],"trap":"Thinking every bond count drops: only the branch-linked features (α-1,6 bonds, nonreducing ends) fall."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"branching enzyme is deficient","note":"fewer α-1,6 links"},{"t":"very few branch points","note":"fewer chain tips"},{"t":"of the same mass","note":"compare like with like"}]},{"text":"**Count what one branch adds.**","calc":["1 branch = 1 α-1,6 bond + 1 nonreducing end","reducing ends: always 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** glycogen synthase forms α-1,4 bonds; the branching enzyme forms α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** branch points are α-1,6 bonds, so fewer branches means fewer α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** each branch point is one α-1,6 bond and one extra nonreducing end; fewer branches means fewer of both, while the single reducing end stays."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39c","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:39#3","pe1-cp:39#1","pe1-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:39"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"The linkage in isomaltose is best classified as:","choices":["α-1,4","α-1,6","β-1,4","β-1,6"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"β would put C1's bond on the same face as the CH₂OH; here they are on opposite faces."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong on both counts: opposite faces means α, and the partner is the CH₂OH carbon, not a ring C4."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A 1,4 link joins C1 to a ring C4–OH; this oxygen sits on the CH₂OH carbon instead."}],"rule":"Haworth: C1's bond on the same face as CH₂OH is β, on the opposite face α; the partner carbon (C4 in the ring, C6 = CH₂OH) gives the number.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Opposite faces → α. Linked to the CH₂OH carbon → 1,6. A branch-point bond, like glycogen's."},"exp":"On a Haworth drawing, **C1's bond on the face opposite the CH₂OH** marks an **α** link; the same face would be β. The bridging O joins the second glucose's **CH₂OH carbon, C6**, so the bond is **α-1,6**, the branch-point bond of glycogen and amylopectin. Glycogen synthase makes the α-1,4 chain bonds.","how":{"ask":"Decide α or β from the faces, then read the partner carbon's number.","know":"• Haworth: C1–O bond and CH₂OH on the same face → β; opposite faces → α.\n• C6 is the CH₂OH carbon; C4 is a ring carbon.\n• Glycogen synthase makes α-1,4; the branching enzyme makes α-1,6.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4: CH₂OH above, C1 bond below: opposite faces, so α.","hl":"points below the ring","strike":["β-1,4","β-1,6"]},{"text":"The partner is the CH₂OH carbon, C6, not a ring C4.","hl":"exocyclic –CH₂– carbon","strike":["α-1,4"]},{"text":"Answer: α-1,6."}],"trap":"Reading \"below the ring\" as β: compare C1's bond with the CH₂OH, not with up or down alone."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₂OH group above the ring","note":"reference face"},{"t":"points below the ring","note":"opposite face → α"},{"t":"exocyclic –CH₂– carbon","note":"C6"}]},{"text":"**Compare faces** on the glucose that donates C1.","fig":["alpha-glucopyranose","beta-glucopyranose"],"calc":["same face → β","opposite faces → α"]},{"text":"**Number the partner.** A ring C4–OH gives 1,4; the CH₂OH carbon gives 1,6."},{"text":"**Also:** glycogen synthase makes the α-1,4 bonds of each chain, and the branching enzyme makes the α-1,6 branch points."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Haworth drawing, C1's bond on the same face as CH₂OH is β and on the opposite face α; the partner carbon gives 1,4 or 1,6."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-40a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:40#1","pe1-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:40"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"Apart from galactose, which sugar ring is part of UDP-galactose?","choices":["Ribofuranose","Deoxyribofuranose","Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"UDP has no \"d\" prefix, so its sugar keeps the 2′ –OH: it is not deoxyribose."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A nucleotide's own sugar is a five-carbon pentose; glucose is a six-carbon hexose."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Fructose is a hexose too; a nucleotide's sugar is always ribose or deoxyribose."}],"rule":"A nucleotide's own sugar is a pentose: ribose in UDP, ATP and the like; only the 'd' nucleotides (dATP, dTTP) use deoxyribose.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"U in UDP is uridine, an RNA nucleoside, so its sugar is ribose (5-ring, furanose). DNA ones are deoxy, and dUDP isn't the carrier."},"exp":"UDP is **uridine diphosphate**. Uridine is uracil bonded to **ribose**, a five-membered (furanose) ring with –OH groups on C2′ and C3′. UDP is an RNA-type nucleotide, so its sugar is ribose, not deoxyribose; glucose and fructose are not part of it.","how":{"ask":"What does UDP stand for, and which sugar is in that nucleoside?","know":"• **UDP** = uridine diphosphate; **uridine** = uracil + **ribose**.\n• Nucleotides without a \"d\" (ATP, UTP, UDP, CTP) carry **ribose**.\n• Deoxyribose appears in DNA nucleotides (dATP, dTTP…).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5: the molecule is galactose attached to UDP.","hl":"UDP-galactose"},{"text":"Expand the name.","fig":["uracil","ribose"],"calc":["UDP = uridine diphosphate","uridine = uracil + **ribose** (furanose)"]},{"text":"UDP has no \"d\" prefix, so its sugar keeps the 2′ –OH: it is not deoxyribose.","strike":["Deoxyribofuranose"]},{"text":"Glucose and fructose are not part of a uridine nucleotide.","strike":["Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"]},{"text":"Answer: ribofuranose."}],"trap":"Picking glucose because UDP-sugars are usually UDP-glucose: the attached sugar varies, but the nucleotide's own sugar is always ribose."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"UDP-galactose","note":"uridine diphosphate + galactose"},{"t":"C₅H₁₀O₅","note":"a pentose with all its –OH groups"},{"t":"Apart from galactose","note":"find the other sugar"}]},{"text":"**Read UDP.** Uridine diphosphate: uracil + ribose + two phosphates.","fig":"ribose","calc":["U = uridine","D = di (2 phosphates)","P = phosphate"]},{"text":"**Ribose vs deoxyribose.** Ribose has –OH on C2′ and C3′; deoxyribose lacks the 2′ –OH.","fig":["t1-ribose-c2oh","t1-dr-c2"]},{"text":"**Also:** deoxyribose is found in DNA nucleotides such as dTTP; nucleotides without the \"d\" carry ribose."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** UDP is uridine diphosphate, and uridine contains ribose."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:41#1","pe1-cp:41#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:41"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"After the phosphatase step, which label remains on the phosphorylase?","choices":["¹³¹I only","³²P only","Both labels","Neither label"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the phosphatase removes exactly the phosphate that carries ³²P, so ³²P can't be what's left."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A phosphatase strips the phosphate, so ³²P can't still be there alongside the tyrosine label."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing the tyrosine label would mean cutting the protein; a phosphatase only cleaves phosphate esters."}],"rule":"A phosphatase removes only phosphate groups (a kinase adds them); labels anywhere else on the protein stay.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphatases remove only phosphate groups. ³²P leaves with the phosphate; the iodine on tyrosine rings is untouched, so it stays."},"exp":"A phosphatase **hydrolyzes phosphate esters**, releasing the phosphate as free Pᵢ. The ³²P rides on that phosphate, so it leaves the enzyme. The ¹³¹I is bonded to the tyrosine rings, which the phosphatase does not touch, so **¹³¹I is the label that stays**. (The ³H is on glucose, not on the enzyme.)","how":{"ask":"Place each label on its chemical group, then ask which group the phosphatase leaves alone.","know":"• **Phosphatase**: R–O–PO₃²⁻ + H₂O → R–OH + Pᵢ (removes phosphate).\n• A radioactive label goes wherever its atom goes.\n• Kinase adds phosphate; phosphatase takes it off.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 places the two labels on phosphorylase.","hl":"³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines","calc":["³²P → phosphate groups","¹³¹I → tyrosine rings"]},{"text":"Sentence 8: the phosphatase cuts off the phosphate.","hl":"protein phosphatase","calc":["Ser–O–PO₃²⁻(³²P) + H₂O → Ser–OH + Pᵢ(³²P)","³²P **leaves**; ¹³¹I **stays**"]},{"text":"Removing the phosphate takes ³²P with it, so ³²P cannot remain, alone or with the other label.","strike":["³²P only","Both labels"]},{"text":"The tyrosines are untouched, so the iodine label is not lost.","strike":["Neither label"]},{"text":"Answer: ¹³¹I only."}],"trap":"Mixing up kinase and phosphatase: a kinase adds phosphate (³²P would stay or grow); a phosphatase removes it, leaving only labels on the rest of the protein."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"³²P in its phosphoserines","note":"label on phosphate"},{"t":"¹³¹I on its tyrosines","note":"label on the protein"},{"t":"protein phosphatase","note":"removes phosphate"}]},{"text":"**Each isotope tags one part.** Phosphorus is only in the phosphate; the iodine sits on the tyrosine ring, drawn here.","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Phosphatase = phosphate off.** The phosphate leaves as free Pᵢ.","calc":["phospho-protein + H₂O → protein + Pᵢ"]},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase removes only phosphate, so a ¹⁴C or ³H label in a sugar backbone stays with the sugar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a phosphatase removes phosphate groups, so a ³²P label on them leaves while labels elsewhere on the protein (or in a sugar) stay."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-42b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:42#2","pe1-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:49#1","pe4-cp:49#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:42"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"Which daughter nuclides do the two β⁻ emitters form?","choices":["Helium-3 and xenon-131","Helium-3 and tellurium-131","Helium-4 and xenon-132","Hydrogen-2 and iodine-130"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Helium is right, but tellurium is one place left of iodine; β⁻ moves one place right."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Mass numbers rose by 1, but the emitted electron carries no nucleons; β⁻ leaves A unchanged."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mass numbers fell by 1, as if a neutron left; β⁻ keeps A and raises Z instead."}],"rule":"β⁻ turns a neutron into a proton: the mass number stays, Z rises by one, so step one place right on the periodic table.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"β⁻: mass same, Z + 1, so one step right on the periodic table. H → He and I → Xe, keeping their mass numbers."},"exp":"β⁻ decay keeps **A** and raises **Z by 1**. ³H (Z = 1) → **³He (Z = 2)**; ¹³¹I (Z = 53) → **¹³¹Xe (Z = 54)**. Tellurium lowers Z (that would be β⁺), and the choices with A changed by 1 forget that the electron carries no nucleons.","how":{"ask":"Apply A unchanged, Z + 1 to each parent, then name the element with the new Z.","know":"• **β⁻ decay**: A unchanged, **Z + 1**.\n• Z sets the element: H 1, He 2; Te 52, I 53, Xe 54.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says both labels are β⁻ emitters: keep A, add 1 to Z.","hl":"Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters","calc":["A → A, Z → Z + 1"]},{"text":"Tritium (Z = 1).","calc":["³₁H → ³₂He + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 1 = 2 + (−1) ✓   A: 3 = 3 ✓"]},{"text":"Iodine (Z = 53).","calc":["¹³¹₅₃I → ¹³¹₅₄Xe + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 53 = 54 + (−1) ✓   A: 131 = 131 ✓"]},{"text":"Tellurium has Z − 1 (positron emission); helium-4 with xenon-132 adds a mass unit; hydrogen-2 with iodine-130 loses one.","strike":["Helium-3 and tellurium-131","Helium-4 and xenon-132","Hydrogen-2 and iodine-130"]},{"text":"Answer: helium-3 and xenon-131."}],"trap":"Moving one step left on the periodic table (tellurium): that is β⁺ decay. β⁻ moves one step right."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the parents and the decay type** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"tagged with ³H","note":"Z = 1, A = 3"},{"t":"¹³¹I on its tyrosines","note":"Z = 53, A = 131"},{"t":"β⁻ emitters","note":"A same, Z + 1"}]},{"text":"**Keep the mass number.** The emitted electron carries no nucleons.","calc":["daughters: A = 3 and A = 131"]},{"text":"**Step one element to the right.** Z + 1 is the next element in the periodic table.","calc":["H (1) → He (2)","I (53) → Xe (54)"]},{"text":"**Also:** in β⁻ decay one neutron turns into a proton, emitting the electron and an antineutrino, so the daughter has one more proton and one fewer neutron.","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino","H-3: 1 p, 2 n → He-3: 2 p, 1 n"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for β⁻ decay, keep A and move one element right (Z + 1) to name the daughter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-44c","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:44#3","pe1-cp:44#1","pe1-cp:44#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:44"],"q":"Arsenic acid, H₃AsO₄, is triprotic. Which of these acids dissociates to the smallest extent when dissolved in water?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30.0 mL of 0.050 M H₃AsO₄ with 0.10 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":45,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1.8],[3,2],[7.5,2.4],[12,2.9],[14,3.4],[15,4.6],[16,5.9],[18,6.4],[22.5,7],[27,7.6],[29,8.1],[30,9.2],[31,10.3],[33,10.8],[37.5,11.3],[42,11.7],[45,11.8]]}]},"choices":["HAsO₄²⁻","H₂AsO₄⁻","H₃AsO₄","HCl"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"HCl is a strong acid: it dissociates completely, the most, not the least."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The neutral parent acid always has the largest Ka (Ka1) of a polyprotic series."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its Ka2 is weaker than Ka1 but stronger than the next step, so it isn't the weakest."}],"rule":"In a polyprotic series each step is weaker: the more negative the species, the less it gives up H⁺; strong acids dissociate fully.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each H+ pulled off a more negative ion is harder. The 2− ion (HAsO4²⁻) holds its last H+ hardest, so it dissociates least; HCl is strong."},"exp":"HCl is a strong acid. Arsenic acid's three Ka values fall steeply (pKa ≈ 2.2, 7.0, 11.5) because each H⁺ comes off a more negative ion. **HAsO₄²⁻** must lose H⁺ from a 2− ion, so it has the smallest Ka and dissociates least, just as HPO₄²⁻ does in the phosphate series.","how":{"ask":"Rank the four by Ka: the strong acid first, then each successive arsenic-acid step.","know":"• A strong acid (HCl) ionizes completely.\n• Triprotic acid: H₃A → H₂A⁻ → HA²⁻ → A³⁻, with Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃.\n• The most negative species that still has an H⁺ is the weakest acid.","steps":[{"text":"HCl is a strong acid: it dissociates completely.","strike":["HCl"]},{"text":"Write the three arsenic-acid steps.","hl":"is triprotic","calc":["H₃AsO₄ ⇌ H⁺ + H₂AsO₄⁻   pKa₁ ≈ 2.2","H₂AsO₄⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + HAsO₄²⁻   pKa₂ ≈ 7.0","HAsO₄²⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + AsO₄³⁻   pKa₃ ≈ 11.5"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":7.5,"text":"buffer 1","at":"above"},{"series":"pH","x":22.5,"text":"pH 7.0 = pKa₂","at":"left"},{"series":"pH","x":37.5,"text":"buffer 3","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Each step is about 10⁴–10⁵ times weaker than the one before, because H⁺ leaves an ever more negative ion.","calc":["Ka₁ ≈ 6 × 10⁻³ > Ka₂ ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁷ > Ka₃ ≈ 3 × 10⁻¹²"],"strike":["H₃AsO₄","H₂AsO₄⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: HAsO₄²⁻."}],"trap":"H₃AsO₄ is the weak acid named in the stem, but it is the strongest of the three arsenic species; its ions are weaker still."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"triprotic","note":"three H⁺ to lose"},{"t":"smallest extent","note":"weakest acid, smallest Ka"}]},{"text":"**Strong acids are out:** HCl ionizes completely, so it dissociates most."},{"text":"**Line up the arsenic species by charge:** neutral H₃AsO₄, then 1− H₂AsO₄⁻, then 2− HAsO₄²⁻. The more negative the ion, the more tightly it holds H⁺.","calc":["0 → 1− → 2−","Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃"]},{"text":"**Also:** HCl and H₂SO₄ (first H⁺) are strong acids; HF, H₃PO₄ and H₂CO₃ are weak."},{"text":"**Also:** each successive Ka of a polyprotic acid is much smaller, because H⁺ is harder to remove from a more negative ion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a polyprotic series, the most negative ion that still carries an H⁺ (HPO₄²⁻, HAsO₄²⁻, HCO₃⁻) is the weakest acid."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-45a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:45#1","pe1-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:45"],"q":"An object sits 15 cm from a converging lens (f = 12 cm). How many times taller is the image, and which way up is it?","choices":["4.0, inverted","4.0, upright","0.25, upright","0.80, inverted"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A real image from a converging lens has m = −i/o below zero, so it is inverted, not upright."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is o/i with the sign dropped: the ratio is flipped and the orientation is wrong."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is f/o; it skips finding the image distance from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i."}],"rule":"Solve 1/f = 1/o + 1/i for i, then m = −i/o: |m| is the height ratio and a negative m means inverted.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"1/i = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60, so i = 60 cm. m = −60/15 = −4: four times taller, inverted."},"exp":"1/i = 1/f − 1/o = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60, so **i = 60 cm**, a real image. **m = −i/o = −60/15 = −4**: the image is **4.0 times** as tall as the object, and the minus sign marks it as **inverted**. An object between f and 2f always gives an enlarged, inverted real image.","how":{"ask":"Find i from the thin-lens equation, then m = −i/o.","know":"• 1/f = 1/o + 1/i.\n• m = −i/o = image height ÷ object height.\n• m < 0 → inverted (real); m > 0 → upright (virtual).","steps":[{"text":"Solve for the image distance.","hl":"sits 15 cm from","calc":["1/i = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60","i = 60 cm"]},{"text":"Magnification.","calc":["m = −60/15 = −4.0"]},{"text":"Upright ignores the sign; 0.25 is o/i; 0.80 is f/o.","strike":["4.0, upright","0.25, upright","0.80, inverted"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.0, inverted."}],"trap":"Dropping the minus sign: a real image from a single converging lens is always inverted."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"f = 12 cm","note":"focal length"},{"t":"sits 15 cm from","note":"o, between f and 2f"}]},{"text":"**Image distance first**, then the ratio.","calc":["i = 60 cm","m = −i/o = −4.0"]},{"text":"**Also:** solve 1/f = 1/o + 1/i for i, then m = −i/o: |m| scales the height and a negative m means inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** for an object beyond 2f, find i from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i and use m = −i/o: the real image is inverted and smaller, shrinking as the object moves away."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find i from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i, then m = −i/o: |m| is the height ratio and a negative m means an inverted image."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-46a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:46"],"q":"Phosphorus-32 decays to sulfur-32, raising Z by one at constant A, and its radiation bends toward a positive plate. Which particle is emitted?","choices":["An electron","A photon","A positron","An alpha particle"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A positron would lower the atomic number by one and bend toward the negative plate."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A photon carries no charge, so the plates could not bend it, and it would leave Z unchanged."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An alpha particle would cut the mass number by four and the atomic number by two."}],"rule":"Read A and Z: same A with Z + 1 is β⁻; same A with Z − 1 is β⁺; A − 4 is α; no change at all is γ. Check the charge from the deflection.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Z up by one with A fixed means a neutron became a proton and threw out a negative charge; bending toward + confirms it."},"exp":"Same A and **Z up by one** means a neutron turned into a proton, and the nucleus emitted an **electron (β⁻)** plus an antineutrino. Its negative charge explains the bend **toward the positive plate**. A photon (γ) has no charge and changes neither A nor Z; a positron lowers Z; an alpha particle lowers A by 4.","how":{"ask":"Use the change in A and Z, then the charge shown by the deflection.","know":"• α: A − 4, Z − 2 (charge +2).\n• β⁻: A same, Z + 1 (an electron, −1).\n• β⁺: A same, Z − 1 (a positron, +1).\n• γ: no change in A or Z (a photon, no charge).","steps":[{"text":"Same mass number, atomic number one higher.","hl":"raising Z by one at constant A","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino"]},{"text":"A positron lowers Z; an alpha lowers A; a photon changes nothing and is not deflected.","strike":["A positron","An alpha particle","A photon"]},{"text":"Bending toward + means a negative charge. Answer: an electron."}],"trap":"Picking γ whenever A is unchanged: γ leaves Z unchanged too, and it is not deflected."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"decays to sulfur-32","note":"A same, Z + 1"},{"t":"raising Z by one","note":"n → p"},{"t":"bends toward a positive plate","note":"negative charge"}]},{"text":"**Match the change to the decay.**","calc":["A same, Z + 1 → β⁻","A same, Z − 1 → β⁺","A − 4, Z − 2 → α","no change → γ"]},{"text":"**Also:** gamma emission is the one decay that changes neither A nor Z: an excited nucleus drops to a lower state and releases a photon, which has no mass and no charge (X* → X + γ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** identify an emission from the change in A and Z and from its charge: β⁻ raises Z with A fixed, β⁺ lowers Z, α lowers A by 4, and γ changes neither."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-47c","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:47#3","pe1-cp:47#1","pe1-cp:47#2","pe4-cp:1#1","pe4-cp:1#2","pe4-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:47"],"q":"Magnesium carbonate does not decompose at room temperature but does when strongly heated: MgCO₃(s) → MgO(s) + CO₂(g), ΔH° ≈ +100 kJ/mol. At room temperature, what are the signs of ΔG° and ΔS°?","choices":["ΔG°: positive; ΔS°: positive","ΔG°: negative; ΔS°: positive","ΔG°: positive; ΔS°: negative","ΔG°: negative; ΔS°: negative"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"No change at room temperature means not spontaneous there, so ΔG° is not negative."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A gas forms from a solid, so the products are more disordered and ΔS° cannot be negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both signs are off: nothing happens at room temperature, and a gas forms from a solid."}],"rule":"No reaction under the stated conditions means ΔG > 0 there; a gas formed from solids or solution means ΔS > 0. Then check ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Observation first: nothing happens at room T, so ΔG° > 0. Gas made from a solid, so ΔS° > 0. Heating works because −TΔS grows until it beats ΔH."},"exp":"Magnesium carbonate is stable at room temperature, so the decomposition is **non-spontaneous there: ΔG° > 0**. It makes a **gas from a solid**, so **ΔS° > 0**. With ΔH° > 0 and ΔS° > 0, ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is positive at low T and turns negative only at high T, which is why strong heating works.","how":{"ask":"Read spontaneity from the observation and entropy from the phases, then check with ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","know":"• Spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0; no reaction ⇒ ΔG > 0.\n• A gas formed from solids or solution ⇒ ΔS > 0.\n• ΔG = ΔH − TΔS: ΔH > 0 with ΔS > 0 → spontaneous only at high T.","steps":[{"text":"Nothing happens at room temperature: ΔG° > 0.","hl":"does not decompose at room temperature","strike":["ΔG°: negative; ΔS°: positive","ΔG°: negative; ΔS°: negative"]},{"text":"A gas forms from a solid: ΔS° > 0.","hl":"CO₂(g)","strike":["ΔG°: positive; ΔS°: negative"]},{"text":"Check: +ΔH and +ΔS give ΔG > 0 at low T and ΔG < 0 at high T, matching the heating. Answer: both positive."}],"trap":"Assuming any gas-forming reaction is spontaneous: the observation at room temperature says otherwise."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"does not decompose at room temperature","note":"ΔG > 0 there"},{"t":"when strongly heated","note":"spontaneous at high T"},{"t":"CO₂(g)","note":"gas formed: ΔS > 0"}]},{"text":"**Fit the signs to ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.**","calc":["ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0","low T: ΔG > 0","high T: ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** spontaneous means ΔG < 0, however slow the reaction."},{"text":"**Also:** a reaction that makes gas from a solid or solution has ΔS > 0."},{"text":"**Also:** for an endothermic reaction (ΔH > 0) with ΔS > 0, ΔG = ΔH − TΔS stays positive, so the reaction is non-spontaneous, as long as ΔH is greater than TΔS. It becomes spontaneous once TΔS > ΔH, i.e. above T = ΔH/ΔS = 90 ÷ 0.20 = 450 K.","calc":["ΔH = +90 kJ/mol, ΔS = +0.20 kJ/(mol·K), T = 300 K","TΔS = 300 × 0.20 = 60 kJ/mol < ΔH","ΔG = 90 − 60 = +30 kJ/mol → non-spontaneous","T > 90 ÷ 0.20 = 450 K → TΔS > ΔH → ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read ΔG from what happens (no reaction → ΔG > 0) and ΔS from the phases (gas formed → ΔS > 0), then check against ΔG = ΔH − TΔS."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:48#1","pe1-cp:48#2","pe3-cp:29#1","pe3-cp:29#2","pe3-cp:29#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:48"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"Based on the passage, what is the overall reaction of the iron-catalyzed oxidation?","choices":["S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂","S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","S₂O₈²⁻ + I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Fe²⁺ appears on both sides and was never cancelled; a species on both sides drops out of a net equation."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the second step alone: it keeps Fe³⁺, an intermediate, and leaves out persulfate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Unbalanced: one I on the left but two in I₂, and the charges (−3 vs −4) do not match."}],"rule":"Add the steps, then cancel anything on both sides (catalysts and intermediates); the net equation shows neither.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Add the steps and strike what shows on both sides: Fe²⁺ (catalyst) and Fe³⁺ (intermediate) cancel, leaving only persulfate, iodide and their products."},"exp":"Add the two steps and cross out anything on both sides: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺ + 2Fe²⁺ + I₂ becomes **S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂**. Fe²⁺ is used and then regenerated (a catalyst), and Fe³⁺ is made and then used (an intermediate), so neither appears overall. Leaving Fe²⁺ on both sides is an unfinished sum, the second step alone keeps an intermediate, and a single I⁻ cannot balance I₂.","how":{"ask":"Add the steps, then cancel each species that appears on both sides.","know":"• **Overall reaction** = sum of the elementary steps (multiply a step if needed so shared species cancel).\n• Cancel species found on both sides: **catalysts** (used, then regenerated; a regenerated cofactor such as enzyme-bound FAD → FADH₂ → FAD cancels like a catalyst) and **intermediates** (made, then used).\n• What's left holds only the true reactants and products.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives both steps; write them and add them.","hl":"S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","calc":["S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺","2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","sum: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺ + 2Fe²⁺ + I₂"]},{"text":"Cancel 2Fe²⁺ and 2Fe³⁺, each found on both sides.","fig":"cpt-fe-cycle","calc":["cancel Fe²⁺ (catalyst)","cancel Fe³⁺ (intermediate)","**S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂**","check: I 2 = 2; charge −2 + (−2) = −4 = 2 × (−2) ✓"]},{"text":"Leaving Fe²⁺ on both sides is a sum that was never simplified.","strike":["S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + I₂"]},{"text":"The second step alone keeps Fe³⁺, an intermediate, and drops persulfate.","strike":["2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂"]},{"text":"One I⁻ cannot make I₂: atoms and charge fail to balance.","strike":["S₂O₈²⁻ + I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂"]},{"text":"Answer: persulfate plus two iodide ions gives two sulfate ions and I₂."}],"trap":"The sum with Fe²⁺ on both sides is balanced and looks complete, but a species on both sides must be cancelled; an overall equation never shows a catalyst."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two steps","note":"a mechanism"},{"t":"S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺","note":"step one"},{"t":"2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","note":"step two"},{"t":"overall reaction","note":"sum and cancel"}]},{"text":"**A mechanism** is the list of elementary steps a reaction actually goes through. Adding them gives the overall equation."},{"text":"**Cancel like terms**, just as in algebra: a species on both sides drops out.","calc":["A + B → C","C + D → A + E","sum: B + D → E"]},{"text":"**Also:** after summing a mechanism, confirm that atoms and total charge (coefficient × charge) balance on both sides."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** add the steps, then cancel every species on both sides (catalysts and intermediates) to get the overall reaction."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48c","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:48#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:48"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"What roles do Fe³⁺ and I₂ play in the iron-catalyzed oxidation?","choices":["Intermediate; product","Catalyst; product","Intermediate; catalyst","Reactant; intermediate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Fe³⁺ is made first and used up later; a catalyst is the reverse (used first, then regenerated)."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"I₂ is formed and never consumed, so it is a final product, not a catalyst."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Fe³⁺ never appears as a starting material, and I₂ is never used up, so neither label fits."}],"rule":"Catalyst: consumed in an early step and regenerated later. Intermediate: made in an early step and consumed later. Product: made and kept.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Check where each species first appears: on the right first → intermediate or product (is it used later?); on the left first → reactant or catalyst (does it come back?)."},"exp":"**Fe³⁺** is **made in the first step and used up in the second**, so it is an **intermediate**, which is why its concentration stays tiny. **I₂** is formed in the second step and never consumed: a **product**. Fe²⁺, used in the first step and regenerated in the second, is the catalyst.","how":{"ask":"Track each species: where it first appears and whether it comes back.","know":"• Catalyst: used early, regenerated later; absent from the overall equation.\n• Intermediate: made early, used later; absent from the overall equation.\n• Product: made and kept; on the right of the overall equation.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4: Fe³⁺ is made in the first step and consumed in the second.","hl":"2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","fig":"cpt-fe-cycle","strike":["Catalyst; product"]},{"text":"I₂ is never consumed, so it is a product, not a catalyst or an intermediate.","strike":["Intermediate; catalyst","Reactant; intermediate"]},{"text":"Answer: intermediate; product."}],"trap":"Swapping catalyst and intermediate: a catalyst is consumed first and regenerated; an intermediate is formed first and consumed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺)","note":"catalyst, regenerated"},{"t":"[Fe³⁺] stayed very small","note":"made, then used"},{"t":"[I₂] rose steadily","note":"made and kept"}]},{"text":"**Add the steps and cancel.**","calc":["S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂","Fe²⁺ cancels (catalyst); Fe³⁺ cancels (intermediate)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst is used and then regenerated, an intermediate is made and then used, and a product is made and kept; only products and reactants survive in the overall equation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-49b","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:49#2","pe1-cp:49#1","pe1-cp:49#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:49"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"In the sucrose hydrolysis, H⁺ is best described as which kind of catalyst?","choices":["Homogeneous; regenerated","Heterogeneous; regenerated","Homogeneous; used up","Heterogeneous; used up"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Heterogeneous means a different phase; H⁺ is dissolved in the same solution as the sucrose."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong phase and wrong fate: the H⁺ is dissolved, and all of it is still there at the end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The pH ends where it started, so all the H⁺ added is still present: it was not consumed."}],"rule":"Same phase as the reactants = homogeneous; a different phase (such as a solid acting on gases) = heterogeneous. A catalyst is regenerated, never used up.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Dissolved catalyst in a dissolved reaction → same phase → homogeneous. Same pH at the end → not consumed."},"exp":"H⁺ is **dissolved in the same aqueous phase** as the sucrose, so it is a **homogeneous** catalyst. It speeds the reaction and is **regenerated**: the pH, and so the amount of H⁺, is unchanged at the end. A solid catalyst acting on gases, like the platinum in a catalytic converter, would be heterogeneous.","how":{"ask":"Compare the catalyst's phase with the reactants', then check whether it is consumed.","know":"• Homogeneous: same phase as the reactants; heterogeneous: a different phase.\n• A catalyst is regenerated, not consumed.\n• A catalytic converter is a heterogeneous solid catalyst: it oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons to CO₂ and H₂O and reduces NOₓ to N₂.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says H⁺ mixes evenly through the solution: the same phase as the sucrose.","hl":"mix evenly through the solution","strike":["Heterogeneous; regenerated","Heterogeneous; used up"]},{"text":"The pH is unchanged at the end, so the H⁺ is not consumed.","hl":"the pH is the same","strike":["Homogeneous; used up"]},{"text":"Answer: homogeneous; regenerated."}],"trap":"Calling every metal catalyst heterogeneous: dissolved ions share the reactants' phase."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"mix evenly through the solution","note":"one phase"},{"t":"the pH is the same","note":"H⁺ not used up"}]},{"text":"**Two questions classify any catalyst.**","calc":["same phase as reactants? → homogeneous","different phase? → heterogeneous","consumed? → never"]},{"text":"**Also:** a solid catalyst acting on gases (a catalytic converter) is a heterogeneous catalyst."},{"text":"**Also:** a catalytic converter reduces nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) to N₂ while it oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst in the reactants' phase is homogeneous and one in a different phase is heterogeneous; either way it is regenerated, not used up."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-51a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:51#1","pe1-cp:51#2","pe1-cp:51#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:51"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"By what factor does cutting the resin speed up the hydrolysis?","choices":["30","900","27000","0.033"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Smaller pieces expose more surface, so the rate must rise, not fall."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That squares the edge ratio; at fixed mass the total area grows by the edge ratio itself."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the number of small cubes; each one has far less surface than the big cube."}],"rule":"Same mass cut into small cubes: total area grows by the ratio of the edge lengths (big ÷ small), not by its square or cube.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Edge ratio = 1.5 ÷ 0.050 = 30. Total surface, and so the rate, goes up by that same factor."},"exp":"Number of small cubes = (1.5/0.050)³ = 27,000, each with area 6(0.050)² = 0.015 cm², for 405 cm² in total. The big cube has 6(1.5)² = 13.5 cm². Ratio = 405 ÷ 13.5 = **30**, the **edge ratio**. Rate ∝ area, so the hydrolysis runs **30 times faster**; the mass of resin is unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Compare the total surface area before and after, at the same mass.","know":"• Rate ∝ exposed surface area.\n• Cutting a cube of edge L into cubes of edge l: N = (L/l)³, and the total area grows by L/l.\n• The mass of catalyst does not change.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives both edge lengths; take their ratio.","hl":"0.050 cm on each side","calc":["1.5 ÷ 0.050 = 30"]},{"text":"Total area.","calc":["big: 6 × 1.5² = 13.5 cm²","small: 27,000 × 6 × 0.050² = 405 cm²","405 ÷ 13.5 = 30"]},{"text":"900 squares the ratio; 27000 counts cubes; 0.033 has the rate falling.","strike":["900","27000","0.033"]},{"text":"Answer: 30."}],"trap":"Using the number of pieces (27,000) as the area factor."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"1.5 cm on each side","note":"L"},{"t":"0.050 cm on each side","note":"l"},{"t":"rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area","note":"rate ∝ area"}]},{"text":"**Area factor = edge ratio** at fixed mass.","calc":["N × 6l² ÷ 6L² = (L/l)³ × (l/L)² = L/l"]},{"text":"**Also:** grinding or cutting a catalyst raises its surface area but leaves its mass unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** cutting a cube into smaller cubes multiplies the total surface area by the ratio of the edge lengths."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** cutting a solid catalyst into cubes at fixed mass multiplies its surface, and the rate, by the edge ratio L/l."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:52#1","pe1-cp:52#2","pe1-cp:52#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:52"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"Which replacement coil would draw the largest current from the 9.0-V battery?","choices":["A 12-Ω coil","A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"45 Ω is more than the present 30 Ω, so by I = V/R it lowers the current."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"60 Ω is twice the present 30 Ω, so by I = V/R it halves the current."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"I = V/R: at fixed V a bigger R gives a smaller current, and 90 Ω is the biggest R offered."}],"rule":"At fixed voltage I = V/R: more resistance means less current.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"I ∝ 1/R at fixed voltage, so the smallest resistance draws the most: 12 Ω. No division needed."},"exp":"By **Ohm's law, I = V/R**, a fixed battery voltage drives more current through a smaller resistance. The 12-Ω coil draws 9.0 V ÷ 12 Ω = **0.75 A**, more than the 0.30 A now.","how":{"ask":"With the battery voltage fixed, which resistance gives the biggest I = V/R?","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R**.\n• Fixed V → current is **inversely proportional** to resistance: lower R, more current.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the battery voltage stays constant; only R changes.","hl":"the battery's voltage stays constant","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"Current now.","hl":"30-Ω coil","calc":["I = 9.0 V ÷ 30 Ω = **0.30 A**"]},{"text":"Compare the replacements.","calc":["12 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 12 = **0.75 A**","45 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 45 = 0.20 A","60 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 60 = 0.15 A","90 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 90 = 0.10 A"]},{"text":"The larger coils all lower the current below 0.30 A.","strike":["A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"]},{"text":"Answer: the 12-Ω coil."}],"trap":"Thinking a bigger resistor \"uses more\" current. At fixed voltage, more resistance means less current."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"9.0-V battery","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"30-Ω coil","note":"present R"},{"t":"largest current","note":"smallest R"}]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** the battery's push divided by the resistance gives the current.","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"**Smaller R, bigger I.** Halve R and the current doubles.","calc":["R ↓ → I ↑"]},{"text":"**Also:** a longer, thinner or hotter metal wire has more resistance; shorter, thicker or cooler has less."},{"text":"**Also:** lowering the ion concentrations a cell runs on lowers its voltage, and at fixed R that lowers the current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to raise the current from a fixed voltage, lower the resistance (I = V/R)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-53a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:53"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"Will H₂ gas form at the silver strip in the acid?","choices":["No; Ag holds its electrons","Yes; Ag gives electrons to H⁺","Yes; chloride attacks silver","No; Ag⁺ forms but no gas"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Silver's reduction potential is above hydrogen's, so electrons would flow the other way."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Chloride is not an oxidizing agent; the only species here that could be reduced is H⁺."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"If silver were oxidized, its electrons would have to reduce H⁺ to H₂; ions cannot form without a gas."}],"rule":"A metal releases H₂ from acid only if it is easier to oxidize than H₂ (E° below zero); above zero, nothing happens.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Compare with zero: Ag⁺/Ag is +0.80 V, above hydrogen, so silver stays put; magnesium, far below zero, fizzes."},"exp":"For H₂ to form, the metal must give its electrons to H⁺, which happens only if the metal is **easier to oxidize than H₂**, i.e. its **E° is below 0 V**. Silver's E° is **+0.80 V**, so silver will not reduce H⁺ and **no H₂** forms. Magnesium (−2.37 V) is far below zero, which is why it fizzes.","how":{"ask":"Compare the metal's reduction potential with hydrogen's (0 V).","know":"• E° < 0: the metal is oxidized by H⁺, releasing H₂.\n• E° > 0: the metal does not react with a non-oxidizing acid.\n• Cl⁻ is not an oxidizing agent.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 puts silver's E° above zero, the hydrogen electrode.","hl":"Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V"},{"text":"So silver cannot hand electrons to H⁺, chloride oxidizes nothing, and any silver oxidation would have to make H₂.","strike":["Yes; Ag gives electrons to H⁺","Yes; chloride attacks silver","No; Ag⁺ forms but no gas"]},{"text":"Answer: no; Ag holds its electrons."}],"trap":"Assuming every metal dissolves in acid: only metals below hydrogen (E° < 0) release H₂."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"Ag: above H₂"},{"t":"−2.37 V","note":"Mg: below H₂, fizzes"},{"t":"defined as zero","note":"the H⁺/H₂ reference"}]},{"text":"**Compare each metal with hydrogen.**","calc":["E° < 0 → oxidized by H⁺ → H₂ forms","E° > 0 → no reaction"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a metal releases H₂ from acid only if its reduction potential is below hydrogen's (E° < 0): it is oxidized and H⁺ is reduced."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-54a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:54#1","pe1-cp:54#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:54"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"What is the balanced net ionic equation for the reaction in the silver nitrate solution?","choices":["Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag","Mg + Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Ag","Mg²⁺ + 2Ag → Mg + 2Ag⁺","2Mg + Ag⁺ → 2Mg²⁺ + Ag"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That runs the reaction in reverse; magnesium, with the lower E°, is the metal oxidized."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Atoms balance but charge does not: +1 on the left and +2 on the right."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Charge is off: +1 on the left and +4 on the right; electrons lost must equal electrons gained."}],"rule":"The metal with the lower E° is oxidized; multiply the half-reactions so electrons lost equal electrons gained, then check the charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mg gives 2e⁻ and each Ag⁺ takes 1e⁻, so two Ag⁺ per Mg. Charge check: +2 on both sides."},"exp":"Magnesium has the **lower E°**, so it is **oxidized**: Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻. Silver ions are reduced: Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, which is the silver seen growing. Balance the electrons with **two Ag⁺ per Mg**: **Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag**, with a charge of +2 on each side.","how":{"ask":"Pick the oxidized metal from E°, then balance electrons and charge.","know":"• Lower E° → oxidized; higher E° → reduced.\n• Electrons lost = electrons gained.\n• Check that atoms and total charge balance.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2: magnesium has the lower E°, so magnesium metal is oxidized and silver ions are reduced.","hl":"Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V","strike":["Mg²⁺ + 2Ag → Mg + 2Ag⁺"]},{"text":"Balance electrons: Mg gives 2, each Ag⁺ takes 1.","calc":["Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻","2Ag⁺ + 2e⁻ → 2Ag"]},{"text":"The one-to-one and two-to-one versions leave the charge unbalanced.","strike":["Mg + Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Ag","2Mg + Ag⁺ → 2Mg²⁺ + Ag"]},{"text":"Answer: Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag."}],"trap":"Balancing atoms but not charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"gray-black crystals of silver","note":"Ag⁺ reduced"},{"t":"−2.37 V","note":"Mg: lower, oxidized"},{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"Ag: higher, reduced"}]},{"text":"**Balance electrons, then charge.**","calc":["Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻","2 × (Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag)","sum: Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag"]},{"text":"**Also:** balance a redox equation by multiplying the half-reactions so the electrons lost equal the electrons gained, then check the charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** oxidize the metal with the lower E°, scale the half-reactions until electrons lost equal electrons gained, and check that charge balances."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-55a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:55#1","pe1-cp:55#2","pe1-cp:55#3","pe6-cp:41#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:55"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"What is the standard potential of the student's cell, and does its reaction run spontaneously?","choices":["+1.24 V, spontaneous","−0.36 V, not spontaneous","+0.36 V, spontaneous","+1.24 V, not spontaneous"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That puts the anode first and drops iron's minus sign; the silver cathode, which gains mass, goes first."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That adds iron's −0.44 to 0.80; subtracting a negative E° adds its size instead."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A positive E°cell means ΔG° < 0, so the reaction is spontaneous."}],"rule":"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, with the higher reduction potential as the cathode; positive means spontaneous.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Silver gains mass, so it is the cathode. 0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V, and positive means spontaneous."},"exp":"Silver gains mass, so Ag⁺ is reduced there: silver is the **cathode**, with the higher E°. Subtract the anode's value from the cathode's: **0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V**. A **positive** E°cell means ΔG° = −nFE° < 0, so the reaction is **spontaneous**, as a working galvanic cell must be.","how":{"ask":"Identify the cathode and anode, subtract, then read the sign.","know":"• E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode.\n• The cathode has the higher reduction potential (and gains mass when metal plates out).\n• E°cell > 0 → spontaneous.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says silver gains mass: it is the cathode, and iron is the anode.","hl":"the silver electrode gains mass"},{"text":"Subtract, anode from cathode.","calc":["E°cell = +0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V"],"strike":["−0.36 V, not spontaneous","+0.36 V, spontaneous"]},{"text":"Positive means spontaneous.","strike":["+1.24 V, not spontaneous"]},{"text":"Answer: +1.24 V, spontaneous."}],"trap":"Adding the potentials as written, or swapping cathode and anode."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"gains mass","note":"cathode"},{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"Ag, higher"},{"t":"−0.44 V","note":"Fe, lower"}]},{"text":"**Cathode minus anode, then the sign.**","calc":["0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V","+ → spontaneous"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a galvanic cell, the cathode is the half-cell with the higher standard reduction potential."},{"text":"**Also:** a positive E°cell means spontaneous as written; a negative E°cell means not spontaneous (the reverse reaction is)."},{"text":"**Also:** when E°cell and one electrode are known, solve the same formula for the other: E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell. A strongly negative result marks a metal that gives up electrons easily.","calc":["this cell backward: +0.80 − (+1.24) = −0.44 V (iron ✓)","Ag cathode, E°cell = +3.17 V: E°anode = 0.80 − 3.17 = −2.37 V (magnesium)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode with the higher reduction potential as the cathode; a positive E°cell means spontaneous."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:56"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"About how much current does the thin heater strip draw?","choices":["18 A","18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Treats 250 mΩ as 250 Ω, a resistance a thousand times too big, so the current comes out in milliamps."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Divides 250 by 4.5: R ÷ V with the milli left off, so it is both upside down and a thousand off."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That's R ÷ V, upside down; with R under 1 Ω the current must exceed 4.5 A."}],"rule":"I = V ÷ R with R in ohms (mΩ ÷ 1000); a resistance under 1 Ω draws more amps than the volts. Round both to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"250 mΩ is a quarter ohm; dividing by 1/4 is multiplying by 4: 4.5 × 4 = 18 A. R under 1 Ω means I bigger than the volt number, so not mA."},"exp":"Convert first: **250 mΩ = 0.250 Ω**. Then **Ohm's law, I = V/R** = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω = **18 A**. A resistance under 1 Ω gives a current larger than the voltage number.","how":{"ask":"Put the resistance in ohms, then divide the voltage by it.","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R** (A = V ÷ Ω).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**: 1 mΩ = 0.001 Ω. Capital **M (mega) = 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives R in milliohms; convert to ohms.","hl":"a resistance of 250 mΩ","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 × 10⁻³ Ω = **0.250 Ω**"]},{"text":"Apply Ohm's law.","hl":"4.5-V supply","calc":["I = V ÷ R","I = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω","I = **18 A**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Current from a 4.5-V supply versus resistance","x":{"label":"Resistance (Ω)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Current (A)","min":0,"max":20,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"I = V ÷ R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.25,18],[0.5,9],[0.75,6],[1,4.5],[1.5,3],[2,2.25],[3,1.5]]}]}},{"text":"18 mA divides by 250 Ω, skipping the milli conversion. 56 A divides 250 by 4.5, which is R ÷ V and unconverted. 0.056 A is R ÷ V in ohms, the ratio upside down.","strike":["18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"]},{"text":"Answer: 18 A."}],"trap":"Treating 250 mΩ as 250 Ω gives 18 mA, a thousand times too small."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"250 mΩ","note":"0.250 Ω"},{"t":"4.5-V supply","note":"V"},{"t":"how much current","note":"I = V/R"}]},{"text":"**Milli means one thousandth.** Convert before dividing.","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 ÷ 1000 Ω = 0.250 Ω"]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** current = voltage ÷ resistance.","calc":["I = V ÷ R = 4.5 ÷ 0.250 = 18 A"]},{"text":"**Also:** at constant resistance, V = IR makes voltage directly proportional to current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** I = V/R with R in ohms; convert mΩ by dividing by 1000 first, and expect more amps than volts when R is under 1 Ω."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-57a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:57#1","pe1-cp:57#2","pe1-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:56#1","pe3-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:57"],"q":"Solid potassium iodide is dissolved in a saturated lead(II) iodide solution standing over undissolved PbI₂. Which best describes the result?","choices":["PbI₂ forms; Q exceeds Ksp","Solid PbI₂ forms; Ksp drops","More solid enters solution; Ksp rises","No shift; Ksp is unchanged"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Adding iodide raises Q above Ksp, which pushes PbI₂ out of solution, not into it."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Ksp is a constant at a given temperature; adding an ion changes Q, not Ksp."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ksp stays fixed, but Q does not: the added iodide raises Q, so the system must respond."}],"rule":"Adding a common ion raises Q above Ksp, so the salt precipitates until Q = Ksp again; Ksp itself changes only with temperature.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Common ion added → Q goes up → Q > Ksp → shift left → solid forms. Ksp never moves at constant temperature."},"exp":"Dissolving KI adds **I⁻, a common ion**. The ion product **Q = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² rises above Ksp**, so PbI₂(s) ⇌ Pb²⁺ + 2I⁻ **shifts left** and PbI₂ precipitates until Q = Ksp again. Ksp itself is unchanged at constant temperature.","how":{"ask":"Compare Q with Ksp after the common ion is added.","know":"• Ksp is fixed at a given T.\n• Q > Ksp → precipitation; Q < Ksp → more dissolves.\n• A common ion raises Q (Le Châtelier: adding a product shifts the equilibrium left).","steps":[{"text":"KI adds iodide, a common ion.","hl":"potassium iodide","calc":["Q = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² rises"]},{"text":"Q > Ksp, so the shift is toward the solid.","strike":["More solid enters solution; Ksp rises","No shift; Ksp is unchanged"]},{"text":"Ksp does not change; only Q does.","strike":["Solid PbI₂ forms; Ksp drops"]},{"text":"Answer: PbI₂ forms; Q exceeds Ksp."}],"trap":"Saying Ksp drops: the constant stays put; Q is what moves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"potassium iodide","note":"common ion I⁻"},{"t":"saturated lead(II) iodide solution","note":"Q = Ksp at the start"},{"t":"undissolved PbI₂","note":"solid present"}]},{"text":"**Compare Q with Ksp.**","calc":["add I⁻ → Q > Ksp → PbI₂(s) forms"]},{"text":"**Also (Le Châtelier):** adding a product, here the common ion, shifts the equilibrium left, so the sparingly soluble salt precipitates."},{"text":"**Also:** adding a common ion lowers a salt's solubility, but Ksp itself doesn't change unless temperature does."},{"text":"**Also:** a pure solid is left out of K and Q (its activity is 1), so adding or removing one (more undissolved AgCl, an ion-exchange resin, a precipitate filtered off) doesn't shift the equilibrium. Only changes in dissolved concentrations or gas pressures do.","calc":["AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻: Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻], no AgCl term","more AgCl(s) → Q unchanged → no shift"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a common ion raises Q above Ksp, so the salt precipitates; Ksp itself changes only with temperature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-58a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:58#1","pe3-cp:21#3","pe5-cp:1#1","pe5-cp:24#1","pe6-cp:35#2","pe3-cp:21#1","pe3-cp:21#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:58"],"q":"A recipe needs 2.22 g of calcium chloride (molar mass 111 g/mol), measured out from a 0.400 M CaCl₂ stock. What volume of stock is required?","choices":["50.0 mL","8.0 mL","20.0 mL","5.55 L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong operation: 8.0 comes from n × M (0.0200 × 0.400); volume is n ÷ M."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops at the moles (0.0200) and reads them as litres; volume still needs ÷ molarity."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips the grams-to-moles step: 5.55 L of 0.400 M would hold about 246 g, not 2.22 g."}],"rule":"Grams → moles (÷ molar mass), then volume = moles ÷ molarity, then L → mL. Round every number to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"2.22 ÷ 111 = 0.02 mol (111 × 2 = 222). Then 0.02 ÷ 0.4 = 0.05 L = 50 mL."},"exp":"First grams to moles: 2.22 g ÷ 111 g/mol = **0.0200 mol**. Then **V = n ÷ M** = 0.0200 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L = 0.0500 L = **50.0 mL**.","how":{"ask":"Convert grams to moles, then divide moles by molarity to get litres, then convert to mL.","know":"• **n = mass ÷ molar mass**.\n• **M = n ÷ V**, so **V = n ÷ M** (V in litres).\n• 1 L = 1000 mL.","steps":[{"text":"Note the mass, molar mass and molarity.","hl":"2.22 g of calcium chloride","calc":["m = 2.22 g","molar mass = 111 g/mol","M = 0.400 mol/L"]},{"text":"Grams to moles.","calc":["n = 2.22 g ÷ 111 g/mol","n = 0.0200 mol"]},{"text":"Volume from V = n ÷ M, then mL.","calc":["V = 0.0200 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L","V = 0.0500 L","V = 0.0500 × 1000 = **50.0 mL**"]},{"text":"Multiplying n × M instead of dividing gives 8.0 mL, and stopping at the moles (0.0200 read as litres) gives 20.0 mL.","calc":["0.0200 × 0.400 = 0.0080 L = 8.0 mL ✗","0.0200 → 20.0 mL skips ÷ M ✗"],"strike":["8.0 mL","20.0 mL"]},{"text":"Skipping the grams-to-moles step divides grams by molarity.","calc":["2.22 ÷ 0.400 = 5.55 ✗"],"strike":["5.55 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 50.0 mL."}],"trap":"Dividing grams by molarity: molarity is moles per litre, so the mass must become moles first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"2.22 g","note":"mass"},{"t":"111 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"0.400 M","note":"mol per L"}]},{"text":"**Grams → moles.**","calc":["n = mass ÷ molar mass"]},{"text":"**Moles → litres → mL.** Rearrange M = n/V.","calc":["V = n ÷ M","mL = L × 1000"]},{"text":"**Also:** going the other way, mass = M × V(L) × molar mass. Turn both prefixes into base units before multiplying: μM × 10⁻⁶ = M and mL × 10⁻³ = L.","calc":["40 μM = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ M; 750 mL = 0.750 L","n = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L × 0.750 L = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol","mass = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol × 342 g/mol ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻² g ≈ 10 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** when only the moles are wanted, n = M × V(L). A millimolar value must become molar first (mM ÷ 1000), and mL ÷ 1000 gives litres.","calc":["600 mM = 0.600 M; 400 mL = 0.400 L","n = 0.600 mol/L × 0.400 L = 0.240 mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** to make a solution from the solid, chain the units forward: mass = M × V × molar mass, with V in litres. Change mL to L first, or the mass comes out 1000 times too big.","calc":["0.050 M CaCl₂ in 300 mL = 0.300 L","mass = 0.050 mol/L × 0.300 L × 111 g/mol ≈ 1.7 g"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a concentration given in g/L, mass = concentration (g/L) × volume (L), and no molar mass enters. A microlitre is 10⁻⁶ L (a millilitre is 10⁻³ L); mixing them up makes the mass 1000 times too big.","calc":["40 g/L protein; drop = 8.0 μL = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ L","mass = 40 g/L × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ L = 3.2 × 10⁻⁴ g = 0.32 mg","μL read as mL: 40 × 8.0 × 10⁻³ = 0.32 g ✗ (1000× too big)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = n ÷ M gives litres; convert grams to moles first and litres to mL at the end; mass = M × V(L) × molar mass, or (g/L) × V(L) with μL = 10⁻⁶ L."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-59c","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:59#3","pe1-cp:59#1","pe1-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:7#1","pe3-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:59"],"q":"An engineer reports a lab centrifuge motor's output using different unit expressions. Which one is not equivalent to the watt?","choices":["V·A","Pa·m³·s⁻¹","kg·m·s⁻²","A²·Ω"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Volts × amps = (J/C)(C/s) = J/s, which is a watt."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Pressure × volume flow = (N/m²)(m³/s) = N·m/s = J/s, a watt."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Current squared × resistance = I²R = A × V = J/s, a watt."}],"rule":"Reduce each expression to base units and compare with kg·m²·s⁻³ (J/s). A newton has one metre; a joule has m² and s⁻²; power needs m² and s⁻³.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Power always carries m² (J = N·m). An expression whose base units show only one metre is a force, not a power."},"exp":"A watt is a joule per second, which in base units is **kg·m²·s⁻³**. V·A = (J/C)(C/s) = J/s; Pa·m³·s⁻¹ = (N/m²)(m³/s) = N·m/s = J/s; A²·Ω = I²R = J/s. **kg·m·s⁻²** is a **newton**, a unit of force: it lacks the extra metre that makes a joule and the extra s⁻¹ that makes power.","how":{"ask":"Convert each expression to base units and compare with kg·m²·s⁻³.","know":"• W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³.\n• J = N·m = kg·m²·s⁻²; N = kg·m·s⁻².\n• V = J/C, A = C/s, Ω = V/A, Pa = N/m².","steps":[{"text":"Electrical products: V·A and A²·Ω both reduce to J/s.","calc":["V·A = (J/C)(C/s) = J/s","A²·Ω = A·(A·Ω) = A·V = J/s"],"strike":["V·A","A²·Ω"]},{"text":"Pressure × volume per time is energy per time.","calc":["Pa·m³·s⁻¹ = N·m·s⁻¹ = J/s"],"strike":["Pa·m³·s⁻¹"]},{"text":"kg·m·s⁻² is a newton, a force. Answer: kg·m·s⁻²."}],"trap":"Stopping at \"it has kg, m and s\": count the powers; one metre with s⁻² makes a force."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the ask**.","marks":[{"t":"not equivalent to the watt","note":"find the odd one out"},{"t":"different unit expressions","note":"reduce each to base units"}]},{"text":"**Build the ladder.**","calc":["N = kg·m·s⁻²","J = N·m = kg·m²·s⁻²","W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³"]},{"text":"**Also:** W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³; one more s⁻¹ than a joule means power."},{"text":"**Also:** kg·m²·s⁻² is a joule (energy), not power; power needs kg·m²·s⁻³."},{"text":"**Also:** with no change in speed there is no acceleration (ΣF = 0), so the driving force equals the resisting force; then P = F·v.","calc":["constant speed: driving force = resisting force = 120 N","P = F·v = 120 N × 2.5 m/s = 300 W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** reduce every unit expression to base units: power is kg·m²·s⁻³ (J/s); kg·m²·s⁻² is energy and kg·m·s⁻² is force."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-01a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:1#2","pe3-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:1"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P1","passage":"Base-promoted aldol reactions were run in three experiments. In Experiment 1, propanal was stirred with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product was isolated before any water was eliminated; it has the formula C₆H₁₂O₂ and IR bands near 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹. In Experiment 2, 1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, whose chain carbons are numbered C1 (the phenyl ketone carbon) through C6 (the methyl carbon), was treated with KOH; one ring closed and water was lost to give 3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, whose C=C is conjugated with its C=O. Its isomer 3-phenylcyclohex-3-en-1-one, whose C=C lies one carbon farther from the C=O, also formed. A run stopped after 3 min at 0 °C gave mostly the 3-en isomer, while a run heated for 12 h at 78 °C, where the two enones interconvert, gave mostly the 2-en isomer. In Experiment 3, the enolate of cyclohexanone added to pent-1-en-3-one, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in a Michael addition, and an intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water closed a new six-membered enone ring. In that new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 is the alkene carbon beside it, C3 is the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 is the CH₂ bonded to C1.","q":"In Experiment 2, which two chain carbons of the dione become the C=C of the ring product?","choices":["C1 and C6","C2 and C3","C4 and C5","C5 and C6"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"C2 and C3 stay CH₂ groups in the new ring; neither ends up in the C=C."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C5 is the ketone that survives as the ring C=O, so a pair that includes it can't be the alkene."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Again C5: it keeps its oxygen as the enone's C=O, so it is not an alkene carbon."}],"rule":"In an intramolecular aldol condensation, the C=C joins the attacking α-carbon to the carbonyl carbon it attacked; the other C=O survives.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for a 5- or 6-membered ring: only the CH₃ end can reach the far carbonyl and close six atoms. Dehydration then puts the C=C right on that new bond."},"exp":"Sentence 3 numbers the dione. Only the CH₃ carbon, **C6**, can reach a carbonyl and close a six-membered ring: its enolate bonds to **C1**, the phenyl ketone carbon. The OH left on C1 then leaves with an H from C6, so the **C=C joins C1 and C6**, conjugated with the C5 ketone, which stays a C=O.","how":{"ask":"Find which α-carbon attacks which carbonyl, then place the C=C that dehydration makes.","know":"• **Aldol bond**: the enolate's **α-carbon** (nucleophile) bonds to the **other carbonyl carbon** (electrophile).\n• **Ring size**: an intramolecular aldol closes a **five- or six-membered** ring.\n• **Dehydration**: the C=C forms between the **former α-carbon** and the **former carbonyl carbon**, conjugated with the remaining C=O.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the dione's structure and its numbering: C1 is the phenyl ketone carbon, C5 the methyl ketone carbon, C6 the CH₃.","hl":"C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃","calc":["C₆H₅–C1(=O)–C2H₂–C3H₂–C4H₂–C5(=O)–C6H₃"]},{"text":"Two ketones plus base and loss of water point to an intramolecular aldol condensation, which closes a five- or six-membered ring. C6 attacking C1 makes six atoms; C2 or C4 attacking the nearer carbonyl makes only four.","calc":["C6 → C1: ring = C6, C5, C4, C3, C2, C1 = **6 atoms** ✓","C2 → C5 or C4 → C1: **4 atoms** ✗"]},{"text":"The new C6–C1 bond leaves an OH on C1, β to the C5 ketone. Dehydration removes that OH with an H from C6.","calc":["C=C: **C6 = C1**, conjugated with C5=O"]},{"text":"Check by neighbors: the phenyl starts on C1, and the product named in sentence 3 carries it on ring C3, an alkene carbon.","hl":"3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one"},{"text":"C2 and C3 stay CH₂ groups in the ring; C5 keeps its oxygen as the ring C=O, so it is never part of the C=C.","strike":["C2 and C3","C4 and C5","C5 and C6"]},{"text":"Answer: C1 and C6."}],"trap":"Putting the surviving C=O carbon (C5) into the C=C: it keeps its oxygen; the alkene forms at C1, the carbon that lost its OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione","note":"two ketones, 1,5 apart"},{"t":"one ring closed and water was lost","note":"aldol, then dehydration"},{"t":"3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one","note":"six-membered enone"}]},{"text":"**Number and label.** Write C1–C6 under the chain; mark the carbonyls (C1, C5) and the α-carbons (C2, C4, C6)."},{"text":"**Make the aldol bond.** An α-carbon (as its enolate) bonds to the other carbonyl carbon; pick the pair that closes a five- or six-membered ring.","calc":["C6 + C1 → 6-membered ring"]},{"text":"**Dehydrate.** The OH on the former carbonyl carbon leaves with an H from the former α-carbon, so the C=C joins those two and is conjugated with the remaining C=O."},{"text":"**Also:** track carbons by their neighbors: the carbon that carries the phenyl keeps it, so you can find it again in the ring."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an intramolecular aldol condensation, the α-carbon bonds to the other carbonyl carbon, and the C=C forms between those same two carbons."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-02a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:2#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:2"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P1","passage":"Base-promoted aldol reactions were run in three experiments. In Experiment 1, propanal was stirred with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product was isolated before any water was eliminated; it has the formula C₆H₁₂O₂ and IR bands near 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹. In Experiment 2, 1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, whose chain carbons are numbered C1 (the phenyl ketone carbon) through C6 (the methyl carbon), was treated with KOH; one ring closed and water was lost to give 3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, whose C=C is conjugated with its C=O. Its isomer 3-phenylcyclohex-3-en-1-one, whose C=C lies one carbon farther from the C=O, also formed. A run stopped after 3 min at 0 °C gave mostly the 3-en isomer, while a run heated for 12 h at 78 °C, where the two enones interconvert, gave mostly the 2-en isomer. In Experiment 3, the enolate of cyclohexanone added to pent-1-en-3-one, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in a Michael addition, and an intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water closed a new six-membered enone ring. In that new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 is the alkene carbon beside it, C3 is the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 is the CH₂ bonded to C1.","q":"Which conclusion do the two Experiment 2 runs support?","choices":["The 2-en isomer is lower in energy","The 3-en isomer is lower in energy","The 2-en isomer forms with the lower Ea","The two isomers are equal in energy"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the 3-en isomer wins only the short, cold run, which shows rate, not stability."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the faster-forming product wins the cold run, and there the 3-en isomer wins."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Equal energies would give a near-even mix at equilibrium, not one isomer at over 90%."}],"rule":"Short, cold run = kinetic product (lower Ea); long, hot run where the products interconvert = thermodynamic product (more stable).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cold and brief = kinetic (fastest-forming); hot and long with interconversion = thermodynamic (most stable). Read stability only from the hot run."},"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Enone mix from the two Experiment 2 runs","x":{"label":"Conditions"},"y":{"label":"Percent of product","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["3 min, 0 °C","12 h, 78 °C"],"series":[{"name":"2-en isomer","values":[22,93]},{"name":"3-en isomer","values":[78,7]}]},"exp":"Sentence 5 says the enones **interconvert** only in the long, hot run, so only that run reaches **equilibrium**, where the **more stable** product dominates: the conjugated 2-en isomer. The 3 min, 0 °C run shows **kinetic control**: the 3-en isomer forms faster, so it has the lower Ea.","how":{"ask":"Decide which run reached equilibrium, then read stability from that run only.","know":"• **Thermodynamic control** (reversible, long time, higher T): the major product is the **more stable** one.\n• **Kinetic control** (irreversible, short time, low T): the major product is the one that **forms faster** (lower Ea).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the two enones interconvert only in the long, hot run.","hl":"where the two enones interconvert","calc":["interconversion → equilibrium → thermodynamic control"]},{"text":"At equilibrium the major product is the more stable one. The 12 h bars are mostly the 2-en isomer.","calc":["equilibrium → major = more stable = **2-en isomer**"],"graph":{"bars":[["12 h, 78 °C","2-en isomer"]]}},{"text":"The cold, brief run cannot reverse, so it shows rate: the 3-en isomer forms faster.","hl":"stopped after 3 min at 0 °C","calc":["kinetic → major = faster = 3-en isomer → lower Ea"],"graph":{"bars":[["3 min, 0 °C","3-en isomer"]]}},{"text":"The 3-en isomer wins only under kinetic control, so it is not the more stable one; and since it forms faster, it, not the 2-en isomer, has the lower Ea.","strike":["The 3-en isomer is lower in energy","The 2-en isomer forms with the lower Ea"]},{"text":"Equal energies would give a near-even mix once the enones interconvert, not 93% of one.","strike":["The two isomers are equal in energy"]},{"text":"Answer: the 2-en isomer is lower in energy."}],"trap":"Reading stability from the cold, brief run: there the faster-forming product wins, whatever its stability."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"stopped after 3 min at 0 °C","note":"kinetic conditions"},{"t":"heated for 12 h at 78 °C","note":"thermodynamic conditions"},{"t":"interconvert","note":"equilibrium reached"}]},{"text":"**Sort the runs.** Interconversion means equilibrium; no interconversion means the first-formed product stays."},{"text":"**Equilibrium → stability.** The product favored at equilibrium has the lower free energy.","calc":["K = [2-en]/[3-en] > 1 → 2-en more stable"]},{"text":"**Also:** a C=C conjugated with the C=O is lower in energy, which fits the 2-en isomer winning at equilibrium."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** under equilibrium (thermodynamic) conditions the more stable product is major; under kinetic conditions the faster-forming product is major."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:3#1","pe3-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:3"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P1","passage":"Base-promoted aldol reactions were run in three experiments. In Experiment 1, propanal was stirred with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product was isolated before any water was eliminated; it has the formula C₆H₁₂O₂ and IR bands near 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹. In Experiment 2, 1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, whose chain carbons are numbered C1 (the phenyl ketone carbon) through C6 (the methyl carbon), was treated with KOH; one ring closed and water was lost to give 3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, whose C=C is conjugated with its C=O. Its isomer 3-phenylcyclohex-3-en-1-one, whose C=C lies one carbon farther from the C=O, also formed. A run stopped after 3 min at 0 °C gave mostly the 3-en isomer, while a run heated for 12 h at 78 °C, where the two enones interconvert, gave mostly the 2-en isomer. In Experiment 3, the enolate of cyclohexanone added to pent-1-en-3-one, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in a Michael addition, and an intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water closed a new six-membered enone ring. In that new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 is the alkene carbon beside it, C3 is the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 is the CH₂ bonded to C1.","choiceFigs":["cpt-3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","cpt-2-methylpent-2-enal","cpt-2-hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","cpt-3-hydroxyhexanal"],"q":"Which compound is isolated in Experiment 1?","choices":["3-Hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","2-Methylpent-2-enal","2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"OH and methyl swapped: the OH goes on the β-carbon (C3), the α-carbon's methyl on C2."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No OH: \"-enal\" names the dehydrated product, and the passage says no water was lost."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Straight chain: propanal's α-carbon carries a CH₃, so the aldol product must branch at C2."}],"rule":"Aldol addition: one aldehyde's α-carbon bonds to the other's C=O carbon; the OH ends on C3 and the α-substituent on C2.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Aldol dimer of an n-carbon aldehyde: 2n carbons, OH on C3, the α-carbon's substituent on C2. Propanal: 6 C, R = methyl."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says no water was lost (C₆H₁₂O₂ is two whole propanals), so this is **aldol addition**. The enolate carbon of one propanal (C2, which carries the CH₃) bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second, leaving a **β-OH**: CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO, **3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal**. The O–H band near 3400 cm⁻¹ fits; losing water would give 2-methylpent-2-enal.","how":{"ask":"Join an α-carbon of one aldehyde to the carbonyl carbon of the other, and keep the OH.","know":"• **Aldol addition** gives a **β-hydroxy carbonyl**: the old C=O becomes C–OH, two carbons from the remaining C=O.\n• **Dehydration** (heat or more base) removes that OH and an α-H, giving the **α,β-unsaturated carbonyl**.\n• IR: broad ~3400 cm⁻¹ = O–H; ~1715 cm⁻¹ = C=O.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the product was isolated before any water was eliminated, and its formula is C₆H₁₂O₂: two whole propanal units.","hl":"isolated before any water was eliminated","calc":["2 C₃H₆O → C₆H₁₂O₂ (no H₂O lost)"]},{"text":"That points to aldol addition, not condensation, so the product is a β-hydroxy aldehyde. The IR bands back this up: an O–H and a C=O.","hl":"3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹"},{"text":"Base removes an α-H from C2 of propanal; that carbon bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second propanal, and the O⁻ picks up H⁺.","calc":["CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO"]},{"text":"Name it: five-carbon chain with CHO as C1, CH₃ on C2, OH on C3.","calc":["3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal (C₆H₁₂O₂ ✓)"]},{"text":"2-Methylpent-2-enal has lost water and has no O–H, so sentence 2 rules it out.","strike":["2-Methylpent-2-enal"]},{"text":"The OH must be on the β-carbon and the methyl on the α-carbon, so the swapped name is wrong; a straight six-carbon chain would need the terminal CH₃ to attack, but it is not α to the C=O.","strike":["2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"]},{"text":"Answer: 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal."}],"trap":"Choosing the α,β-unsaturated aldehyde out of habit: it forms only after dehydration, which the passage rules out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"cold, dilute NaOH","note":"mild: aldol addition"},{"t":"before any water was eliminated","note":"no dehydration"},{"t":"C₆H₁₂O₂","note":"two whole propanals"}]},{"text":"**Find the α-carbon.** In CH₃CH₂CHO the α-carbon is the CH₂ next to C=O; its enolate is the nucleophile."},{"text":"**Join and protonate.** α-Carbon to the other C=O carbon; the O⁻ picks up H⁺, giving an OH on the β-carbon.","calc":["α-C–C(OH) next to CHO → β-hydroxy aldehyde"]},{"text":"**Also:** if the mixture is heated, the β-OH and an α-H leave as water, giving the conjugated α,β-unsaturated aldehyde.","calc":["β-hydroxy carbonyl − H₂O → α,β-unsaturated carbonyl"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** aldol addition gives a β-hydroxy carbonyl; only dehydration turns it into the α,β-unsaturated carbonyl."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-04a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:4#1","pe3-cp:4#2","pe3-cp:4#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:4"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P1","passage":"Base-promoted aldol reactions were run in three experiments. In Experiment 1, propanal was stirred with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product was isolated before any water was eliminated; it has the formula C₆H₁₂O₂ and IR bands near 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹. In Experiment 2, 1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, whose chain carbons are numbered C1 (the phenyl ketone carbon) through C6 (the methyl carbon), was treated with KOH; one ring closed and water was lost to give 3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, whose C=C is conjugated with its C=O. Its isomer 3-phenylcyclohex-3-en-1-one, whose C=C lies one carbon farther from the C=O, also formed. A run stopped after 3 min at 0 °C gave mostly the 3-en isomer, while a run heated for 12 h at 78 °C, where the two enones interconvert, gave mostly the 2-en isomer. In Experiment 3, the enolate of cyclohexanone added to pent-1-en-3-one, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in a Michael addition, and an intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water closed a new six-membered enone ring. In that new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 is the alkene carbon beside it, C3 is the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 is the CH₂ bonded to C1.","q":"In Experiment 3, on which carbon of the new ring does the extra methyl group of pent-1-en-3-one end up?","choices":["C1","C2","C3","C6"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"C1 keeps its C=O and is bonded to two ring carbons, so it has no free bond left for a methyl."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"C6 comes from the vinyl end, the carbon the enolate attacks; the extra CH₃ sits on the ethyl side instead."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C3 is the old cyclohexanone carbonyl carbon at the ring fusion; nothing from the ethyl group reaches it."}],"rule":"Map atoms: the Michael donor bonds to the enone β-carbon, the far α-carbon closes the aldol ring, and any extra group stays on the atom that carried it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The extra methyl rides on the ethyl CH₂. That carbon becomes the enolate that closes the ring, so after dehydration it is the alkene carbon right next to the C=O."},"exp":"Sentence 6 names the acceptor, pent-1-en-3-one. **Michael**: the cyclohexanone enolate bonds to its **β-carbon** (the CH₂= end). **Aldol**: the ketone's other α-carbon, the CH₂ of the ethyl group, attacks the ring C=O and closes a six-membered ring; **dehydration** puts the C=C between that carbon and the old ring carbonyl carbon. The extra CH₃ rides along on that α-carbon, so by sentence 7's numbering it sits on **C2**.","how":{"ask":"Track the carbon that carries the extra methyl through the Michael addition, the ring-closing aldol and the dehydration.","know":"• **Michael addition**: an enolate bonds to the **β-carbon** of an α,β-unsaturated ketone.\n• **Intramolecular aldol + dehydration**: an α-carbon attacks the other C=O to close a six-membered ring, and the C=C forms between the former α-carbon and the former carbonyl carbon.\n• A group that does not react **rides along** on the atom that carried it.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the acceptor: pent-1-en-3-one, whose ketone carries an ethyl group where methyl vinyl ketone has a methyl.","hl":"CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃","calc":["extra CH₃ sits on the ethyl CH₂"]},{"text":"Michael step: the enolate adds to the vinyl end, the β-carbon of the enone, so a chain hangs off the ring.","calc":["ring C–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₂–CH₃"]},{"text":"Aldol step: the ethyl CH₂, the α-carbon that carries the CH₃, attacks the ring C=O; that closes a six-membered ring.","calc":["ring = old C=O carbon, old α-carbon, CH₂, CH₂, C=O, CH(CH₃) = **6 atoms**"]},{"text":"Dehydration: water leaves, and the C=C joins the CH(CH₃) carbon to the old ring carbonyl carbon. Sentence 7 calls that carbon beside the C=O C2.","hl":"C2 is the alkene carbon beside it","calc":["C1 = C=O, **C2 = C(CH₃)=**, C3 = ring fusion"]},{"text":"C1 keeps its C=O and has no spare bond; C6 comes from the vinyl end; C3 is the old ring carbonyl carbon.","strike":["C1","C6","C3"]},{"text":"Answer: C2."}],"trap":"Putting the extra group on the vinyl end: the enolate attacks the CH₂= carbon, but the methyl rides on the ethyl side, which becomes the ring-closing α-carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"Michael addition","note":"enolate → β-carbon"},{"t":"intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water","note":"ring closes, then C=C forms"},{"t":"pent-1-en-3-one","note":"one extra CH₃"}]},{"text":"**Michael first.** The enolate carbon bonds to the β-carbon, the far end of the C=C, so a chain hangs off the ring with its ketone three atoms out."},{"text":"**Ring closure.** The α-carbon that can reach the ring C=O to make a six-membered ring is the one that attacks; water then leaves and the C=C forms at the new bond, conjugated with the C=O."},{"text":"**Ride-along groups.** A substituent that does not react stays on its own carbon, so follow that carbon, not the substituent."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a Michael-then-aldol ring closure, the enolate hits the β-carbon, the far α-carbon closes a six-membered ring, and every extra group stays on the atom it started on."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-05a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:5#2","pe3-cp:5#3","pe3-cp:5#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:5"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P2","passage":"A battery locomotive that hauls ore through a straight, level mine tunnel was checked on one test run. Its warning strobe runs from a 25 mF capacitor, which is charged from the locomotive's 16 V battery (internal resistance 0.3 Ω) until no current flows. Through the main tunnel, the 8000-kg locomotive held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N. Halfway along, it passed a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz, and the driver heard the horn as she approached it and again after she passed it; sound travels at 346 m/s in the tunnel air. At the end of the run the motor was switched off, and the locomotive coasted into a magnetic braking zone, where eddy currents in its steel wheels slowed it and rolling friction was negligible. In that zone its speed fell from 5.0 m/s to half that value in the first 3 s and halved again in each later 3 s interval, so it never quite stopped (Figure 1).","q":"Once the strobe capacitor is fully charged, what charge does it hold and what energy does it store?","choices":["0.40 C; 3.2 J","0.40 C; 6.4 J","400 C; 3200 J","0.40 C; 0.20 J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"mF is 10⁻³ F; using 25 F makes both values a thousand times too large."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the ½: CV² is twice the energy the capacitor stores."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"½CV is not an energy: the stored energy needs V squared."}],"rule":"Fully charged means no current, so internal resistance drops out; Q = CV and U = ½CV², with C in farads.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mF × V = mC: 25 × 16 = 400 mC. Then U = ½QV = ½ × 0.40 C × 16 V."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says charging runs **until no current flows**, so no voltage drops across the 0.3 Ω internal resistance and the capacitor sits at the full 16 V. **Q = CV** = (25 × 10⁻³ F)(16 V) = **0.40 C**; **U = ½CV²** = ½(0.025 F)(16 V)² = **3.2 J**.","how":{"ask":"Find the voltage across the charged capacitor, then apply Q = CV and U = ½CV².","know":"• **Q = CV** and **U = ½CV² = ½QV**.\n• Fully charged: **no current**, so the internal resistance drops no voltage.\n• **1 mF = 10⁻³ F**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the capacitor charges until no current flows: with zero current, the battery's internal resistance drops no voltage.","hl":"until no current flows","calc":["V across C = 16 V (I = 0, so Ir = 0)"]},{"text":"That points to the capacitor relations, with mF converted to F.","calc":["C = 25 mF = 0.025 F","Q = CV = 0.025 × 16 = **0.40 C**"]},{"text":"Now the energy stored.","calc":["U = ½CV² = ½ × 0.025 × 256 = **3.2 J**","check: ½QV = ½ × 0.40 × 16 = 3.2 J ✓"]},{"text":"Treating 25 mF as 25 F makes both values a thousand times too large.","strike":["400 C; 3200 J"]},{"text":"Dropping the ½ doubles the energy, and ½CV leaves out a factor of V.","strike":["0.40 C; 6.4 J","0.40 C; 0.20 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.40 C and 3.2 J."}],"trap":"Subtracting a voltage drop across the internal resistance: once charged, no current flows, so Ir = 0."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"25 mF capacitor","note":"C = 0.025 F"},{"t":"16 V battery","note":"full charging voltage"},{"t":"until no current flows","note":"internal resistance drops out"}]},{"text":"**No current, no drop.** A resistor drops V = IR; with I = 0, the capacitor gets the battery's whole voltage."},{"text":"**Charge, then energy.**","calc":["Q = CV","U = ½CV² = ½QV = Q²/2C"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert prefixes first: m = 10⁻³, µ = 10⁻⁶."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a fully charged capacitor, use the source voltage, Q = CV and U = ½CV², with C in farads."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-06a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:6#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:6"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P2","passage":"A battery locomotive that hauls ore through a straight, level mine tunnel was checked on one test run. Its warning strobe runs from a 25 mF capacitor, which is charged from the locomotive's 16 V battery (internal resistance 0.3 Ω) until no current flows. Through the main tunnel, the 8000-kg locomotive held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N. Halfway along, it passed a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz, and the driver heard the horn as she approached it and again after she passed it; sound travels at 346 m/s in the tunnel air. At the end of the run the motor was switched off, and the locomotive coasted into a magnetic braking zone, where eddy currents in its steel wheels slowed it and rolling friction was negligible. In that zone its speed fell from 5.0 m/s to half that value in the first 3 s and halved again in each later 3 s interval, so it never quite stopped (Figure 1).","q":"About what frequency does the driver hear while approaching the horn, and then after passing it?","choices":["About 528 Hz, then 512 Hz","About 512 Hz, then 528 Hz","About 528 Hz, then 528 Hz","About 520 Hz, then 520 Hz"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: closing in raises the pitch and moving away lowers it, so the order is flipped."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the sign change: after passing she moves away, so the shift must reverse."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the horn's own frequency; a moving listener hears a shift even when the source is still."}],"rule":"Closing in → above the source frequency; separating → below it. Shift ≈ f × (listener speed ÷ sound speed).","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"Shift ≈ 520 × 5/346 ≈ 7.5 Hz. Add it while closing in, subtract it after passing."},"exp":"Sentence 4 puts a **fixed** horn beside the track and a **moving** listener. Approaching at 5.0 m/s, she meets crests more often: f′ = f(v + v_o)/v ≈ **528 Hz**, higher. After passing she moves away: f′ = f(v − v_o)/v ≈ **512 Hz**, lower.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the driver and horn are closing in or separating, before and after passing.","know":"• **Doppler effect**: closing in → higher f; separating → lower f.\n• Moving listener, still source: f′ = f(v ± v_o)/v, + toward, − away.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the horn is fixed and the driver hears it both as she approaches and after she passes.","hl":"a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz","calc":["source still, listener moving"]},{"text":"That points to the Doppler effect for a moving listener. Sentence 3 gives her speed.","hl":"held a steady 5.0 m/s","calc":["v_o = 5.0 m/s, v = 346 m/s"]},{"text":"Approaching: she runs into the crests, so she meets more per second.","calc":["f′ = 520 × (346 + 5.0)/346 ≈ 528 Hz → **higher**"]},{"text":"After passing: she runs away from the crests.","calc":["f′ = 520 × (346 − 5.0)/346 ≈ 512 Hz → **lower**"]},{"text":"Reversed order, the same shift both times, or no shift at all each break the toward-then-away rule.","strike":["About 512 Hz, then 528 Hz","About 528 Hz, then 528 Hz","About 520 Hz, then 520 Hz"]},{"text":"Answer: about 528 Hz approaching, about 512 Hz after passing."}],"trap":"Thinking only a moving source shifts the frequency: a moving listener shifts it too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a fixed alarm horn","note":"source at rest"},{"t":"as she approached it and again after she passed it","note":"toward, then away"}]},{"text":"**Closing in or separating?** That one question sets the sign of the shift."},{"text":"**Size of the shift.**","calc":["f′ = f(v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the size of the shift barely changes with distance at constant speed; it flips sign as she goes by."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** closing in raises the heard frequency and separating lowers it, whether the source or the listener moves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-07a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:7#1","pe3-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:7"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P2","passage":"A battery locomotive that hauls ore through a straight, level mine tunnel was checked on one test run. Its warning strobe runs from a 25 mF capacitor, which is charged from the locomotive's 16 V battery (internal resistance 0.3 Ω) until no current flows. Through the main tunnel, the 8000-kg locomotive held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N. Halfway along, it passed a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz, and the driver heard the horn as she approached it and again after she passed it; sound travels at 346 m/s in the tunnel air. At the end of the run the motor was switched off, and the locomotive coasted into a magnetic braking zone, where eddy currents in its steel wheels slowed it and rolling friction was negligible. In that zone its speed fell from 5.0 m/s to half that value in the first 3 s and halved again in each later 3 s interval, so it never quite stopped (Figure 1).","q":"What power must the motor deliver to hold the locomotive at its steady speed in the main tunnel?","choices":["280 W","1.4 kW","7.0 kW","40 kW"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Force divided by speed gives N·s/m, not watts."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stopped at the force: 1400 N becomes a power only when multiplied by the speed."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Copied the mass: 8000 kg × 5.0 m/s is momentum, and mass plays no part at steady speed."}],"rule":"At constant speed the driving force equals the resisting force, and P = Fv.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"W = N·m/s, so multiply the resisting force (N) by the speed (m/s): 1400 × 5.0."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says the speed is **steady**, so the net force is zero and the motor's driving force equals the **1400 N** of resistance. **P = Fv** = (1400 N)(5.0 m/s) = 7000 W = **7.0 kW**. The 8000 kg mass plays no part.","how":{"ask":"Find the driving force at constant speed, then multiply by the speed.","know":"• **Constant velocity** → net force zero → driving force = resisting force.\n• **P = Fv** (watts = newtons × m/s).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the locomotive holds a steady speed against a known resisting force.","hl":"held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N","calc":["v = 5.0 m/s, F_resist = 1400 N"]},{"text":"Steady speed means zero net force (Newton's first law), so drive force and resistance match.","calc":["F_drive = 1400 N"]},{"text":"Power at constant speed is force times speed.","calc":["P = Fv = 1400 × 5.0 = 7000 W = **7.0 kW**"]},{"text":"Dividing force by speed gives N·s/m, not watts; 1400 N alone is a force, not a power; 8000 kg × 5.0 m/s is momentum.","strike":["280 W","1.4 kW","40 kW"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.0 kW."}],"trap":"Bringing in the mass: at constant speed no net force acts, so mass doesn't enter the power."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"held a steady 5.0 m/s","note":"zero net force"},{"t":"opposed it with 1400 N","note":"resisting force"},{"t":"8000-kg","note":"distractor at steady speed"}]},{"text":"**Balance the forces.** Constant velocity → F_drive = F_resist."},{"text":"**Power.**","calc":["P = W/t = F·d/t = Fv"]},{"text":"**Also:** check units: N × m/s = J/s = W."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant speed, the power needed is the resisting force times the speed, P = Fv."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-08a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:8#1","pe3-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:8"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P2","passage":"A battery locomotive that hauls ore through a straight, level mine tunnel was checked on one test run. Its warning strobe runs from a 25 mF capacitor, which is charged from the locomotive's 16 V battery (internal resistance 0.3 Ω) until no current flows. Through the main tunnel, the 8000-kg locomotive held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N. Halfway along, it passed a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz, and the driver heard the horn as she approached it and again after she passed it; sound travels at 346 m/s in the tunnel air. At the end of the run the motor was switched off, and the locomotive coasted into a magnetic braking zone, where eddy currents in its steel wheels slowed it and rolling friction was negligible. In that zone its speed fell from 5.0 m/s to half that value in the first 3 s and halved again in each later 3 s interval, so it never quite stopped (Figure 1).","q":"What does Figure 1 show about the magnetic braking force on the locomotive?","choices":["Same at every speed","Weakens as it slows","Largest just before it stops","Zero once the speed halves"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"With no force the speed would level off, but the curve keeps falling after the speed has halved."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the drop per second shrinks near the end, so the force is smallest there."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A constant force gives a constant deceleration and a straight line; this curve flattens."}],"rule":"Straight-line drop on a speed–time graph = constant force. Halving in equal times = force proportional to speed (exponential slowing).","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Compare the drop in the first second with a late second: about 1 m/s versus about 0.2 m/s. Shrinking drops mean shrinking deceleration, so F = ma shrinks with v."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1: speed of the locomotive in the braking zone","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":9,"step":3},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Locomotive","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,5],[1,3.97],[2,3.15],[3,2.5],[4,1.98],[5,1.57],[6,1.25],[7,0.99],[8,0.79],[9,0.63]]}]},"exp":"Sentence 6 says the speed **halves in every 3 s interval**: equal fractions lost in equal times, which is exponential slowing. That happens when the **braking force is proportional to speed**, as in eddy-current braking: large at entry and smaller as the locomotive slows, which is why it never quite stops. A constant force would give a straight line.","how":{"ask":"Read how the slope of the speed–time curve changes, then turn slope into force with F = ma.","know":"• On a speed–time graph, **slope = acceleration**, and F = ma.\n• **Straight line** → constant force.\n• **Halving in equal times** → exponential slowing → force ∝ speed (eddy-current braking).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the speed halves in the first 3 s and halves again in each later 3 s interval.","hl":"halved again in each later 3 s interval","calc":["5.0 → 2.5 → 1.25 m/s every 3 s"]},{"text":"Equal fractions lost in equal times is exponential decay, the signature of a braking force proportional to speed.","calc":["F = −kv → a = −(k/m)v → v = v₀e^(−kt/m)"]},{"text":"Check Figure 1: the curve is steep at entry and flattens later, so the deceleration, and the force, shrink as the speed falls.","calc":["first second: ≈ 1.0 m/s lost","a late second: ≈ 0.2 m/s lost"]},{"text":"A constant force would give a straight line; a force largest near the end would steepen the curve there; zero force would stop the speed from falling.","strike":["Same at every speed","Largest just before it stops","Zero once the speed halves"]},{"text":"Answer: the force weakens as it slows."}],"trap":"Reading the flat tail of the curve as a strong force: a flatter slope means less deceleration, so less force."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"eddy currents in its steel wheels","note":"force grows with speed"},{"t":"rolling friction was negligible","note":"the magnets are the only brake"},{"t":"so it never quite stopped","note":"exponential tail"}]},{"text":"**Slope is acceleration.** Steep at entry means a large force; nearly flat later means a small force."},{"text":"**Equal halving times** are the signature of a force proportional to speed.","calc":["v = v₀(½)^(t / 3 s)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a straight speed–time line means a constant force; a curve that halves in equal times means a force proportional to speed."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:9#1","pe3-cp:9#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:9"],"q":"Four C₄H₈ isomers, W–Z, each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O. Their heats of combustion are W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol. Which isomer is most stable?","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"W releases the most heat, so it starts highest in energy: the least stable, not the most."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"X releases more heat than two other isomers, so it starts above them in energy."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Y still releases more heat than one other isomer, so it isn't the lowest in energy."}],"rule":"Same products, so less heat released means a lower starting energy: the smallest heat of combustion is the most stable.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same products, so less heat out means it started lower in energy. Smallest heat of combustion = most stable: 2701 kJ/mol, Z."},"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Heat released on burning one mole of each C₄H₈ isomer","x":{"label":"Isomer"},"y":{"label":"Heat released (kJ/mol)","min":2690,"max":2720,"step":10},"categories":["W","X","Y","Z"],"series":[{"name":"Heat released","values":[2717,2710,2707,2701]}]},"exp":"All four isomers end at the **same products**, so the difference in heat released is the difference in their starting enthalpies. Z releases the **least heat**, so it began lowest in energy and is the most stable; W, releasing the most, is the least stable.","how":{"ask":"Compare the heats of combustion; the smallest one marks the most stable isomer.","know":"• Isomers that burn to the **same products** can be ranked by heat of combustion: **less heat released = lower starting enthalpy = more stable**.\n• Boiling points, UV spectra and mass spectra do **not** measure thermodynamic stability.","steps":[{"text":"Same formula, same products, so all four reactions end at the same enthalpy.","hl":"each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O"},{"text":"Rank by heat released; the gap from the products is the starting enthalpy.","hl":"W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol","calc":["Z 2701 < Y 2707 < X 2710 < W 2717 kJ/mol","most stable: **Z**; Z is 2717 − 2701 = 16 kJ/mol below W"],"graph":{"bars":[["Z","Heat released"]]}},{"text":"W releases the most heat, so it starts highest and is least stable; X and Y lie in between.","strike":["W","X","Y"]},{"text":"Answer: Z."}],"trap":"Thinking more heat released means a 'stronger' or more stable molecule: more heat out means it started higher in energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"C₄H₈ isomers","note":"same formula"},{"t":"4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O","note":"same products"},{"t":"W 2717","note":"heat released, kJ/mol"},{"t":"Z 2701 kJ/mol","note":"smallest heat"}]},{"text":"**Common endpoint.** Products are identical, so each heat of combustion measures how far its isomer sits above the same floor.","calc":["ΔH_comb = H(products) − H(isomer)"]},{"text":"**Smaller drop, lower start.** The isomer giving off the smallest amount of heat starts lowest in enthalpy: most stable.","calc":["2701 kJ/mol → lowest start → Z"]},{"text":"**Also:** boiling points reflect intermolecular forces, UV absorption reflects conjugation, and mass-spec patterns reflect mass and fragmentation; none of them measures stability."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** among isomers, the smallest heat of combustion marks the most stable one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:10#1","pe3-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:10"],"q":"Tritiated N-(2-hydroxyethyl)acetamide, with atoms labeled I–IV, is kept in water for two days. Only labeled atoms bearing H can hold tritium. Which site best keeps its label?","choices":["Site I","Site II","Site III","Site IV"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Site I is the carbonyl oxygen, which carries no hydrogen, so it cannot hold a tritium label at all."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Site II is an O–H; hydrogens on oxygen trade places with water's hydrogens within seconds."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Site III is the amide N–H; hydrogens on nitrogen also exchange with the solvent."}],"rule":"In water, H on O or N swaps with the solvent; only H on carbon keeps an isotope label.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan for the one labeled atom that is a carbon bearing H. O–H and N–H exchange with water; C–H does not."},"fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites","exp":"Hydrogens on **O or N** are acidic enough to **exchange with water** quickly, so tritium there is washed into the solvent. Hydrogens on **carbon** do not exchange, so a label on the **CH₃ (site IV)** stays put. Site I, the C=O oxygen, has no H to label.","how":{"ask":"Classify each labeled atom by what it is and what it carries, then keep only a C–H.","know":"• **O–H and N–H** hydrogens exchange with solvent water (fast proton transfer).\n• **C–H** hydrogens, alkyl or aromatic, do not exchange under these conditions.\n• An atom with no H cannot carry a hydrogen label.","steps":[{"text":"Identify each labeled atom: I is the C=O oxygen, II the O–H, III the N–H, IV the CH₃ carbon.","hl":"atoms labeled I–IV"},{"text":"Site I bears no hydrogen, so it cannot carry tritium.","strike":["Site I"]},{"text":"Sites II and III are H on O and on N: they swap with water's H over two days.","strike":["Site II","Site III"]},{"text":"Answer: Site IV."}],"trap":"Choosing a polar O–H or N–H because it looks reactive: those are exactly the hydrogens that wash out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"kept in water for two days","note":"time for exchange"},{"t":"best keeps its label","note":"non-exchangeable H"}]},{"text":"**Exchangeable H.** Any H on O or N is passed back and forth with water, so its tritium ends up in the solvent.","fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites"},{"text":"**Also:** an aromatic ring C–H is just as stable as an alkyl C–H, so on a drawn ring the C–H site is the one that keeps a label."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only hydrogens on carbon keep an isotope label in water; H on O or N exchanges."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-11a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:11#1","pe3-cp:11#2","pe3-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:11"],"q":"A balloon of volume V holds gas at pressure P and 25 °C. Using R and N_A, how many gas molecules does it hold?","choices":["PV·N_A/(R·298)","PV/(R·298)","PV·N_A/(R·25)","PV/(R·298·N_A)"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"25 is the Celsius reading; the gas law needs kelvin, so 273 must be added first."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dividing by N_A shrinks the count; molecules = moles × N_A, so N_A belongs on top."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"PV/RT stops at moles; one more step, × N_A, turns moles into molecules."}],"rule":"Molecules = (PV/RT) × N_A with T in kelvin: check that N_A multiplies and that 273 was added to the Celsius value.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"PV/RT is moles; mol × (molecules/mol) = molecules, so N_A multiplies. T must be 25 + 273 K."},"exp":"**n = PV/RT** gives moles, with T in **kelvin**: 25 + 273 = 298 K. Multiplying by **N_A** (molecules per mole) gives the number of molecules: **PV·N_A/(R·298)**.","how":{"ask":"Build the count from n = PV/RT, then convert moles to molecules, with T in kelvin.","know":"• **Ideal gas law**: n = PV/RT, with T in **kelvin** (K = °C + 273).\n• **Molecules = n × N_A**.\n• Units: (Pa·m³) / (J/(mol·K) × K) = mol.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the temperature.","hl":"25 °C","calc":["T = 25 + 273 = **298 K**"]},{"text":"Moles from the gas law.","calc":["n = PV/(R·298)"]},{"text":"Molecules = moles × N_A.","calc":["N = PV·N_A/(R·298)"]},{"text":"25 is a Celsius value; dividing by N_A runs the conversion backwards; PV/(R·298) stops at moles.","strike":["PV·N_A/(R·25)","PV/(R·298·N_A)","PV/(R·298)"]},{"text":"Answer: PV·N_A/(R·298)."}],"trap":"Stopping at moles: PV/RT counts moles, and the question asks for molecules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"25 °C","note":"convert to kelvin"},{"t":"how many gas molecules","note":"not moles: × N_A"}]},{"text":"**Kelvin first.** Gas laws use absolute temperature.","calc":["25 °C + 273 = 298 K"]},{"text":"**Moles to molecules.**","calc":["N = n·N_A = PV·N_A/(RT)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** particles = PV·N_A/RT, with T in kelvin."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-12a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:12#1","pe3-cp:12#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:12"],"q":"H₂SO₃ has Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻² and Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ (Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴). In water, HSO₃⁻ is best classified as:","choices":["an acid; H⁺ loss wins.","a base; H⁺ gain wins.","a base; its Ka1 is largest.","neither; the two balance."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"This takes Kw/Ka2 as HSO₃⁻'s Kb, but Ka2 is its own acid constant; its Kb comes from Kw/Ka1."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Ka1 belongs to H₂SO₃, not to HSO₃⁻; the biggest number in the stem is not the ion's own constant."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The ion's Ka and Kb differ by a factor of about 10⁵, so one process clearly dominates."}],"rule":"For an amphoteric ion HA⁻: its Ka is Ka2, its Kb is Kw/Ka1 (from its conjugate acid H₂A). Whichever is larger decides acid or base.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Kb(HSO₃⁻) = Kw/Ka1 = 10⁻¹⁴/1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ 7 × 10⁻¹³. Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ is about 10⁵ times larger, so it acts as an acid."},"exp":"As an acid, HSO₃⁻ has **Ka = Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸**. As a base it becomes H₂SO₃, so its **Kb = Kw/Ka1** = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ **7.1 × 10⁻¹³**. Ka is far larger, so HSO₃⁻ behaves as an **acid**: losing H⁺ wins over gaining it.","how":{"ask":"Get the ion's Ka and its Kb, then compare them.","know":"• For HA⁻ made from H₂A: **Ka(HA⁻) = Ka2**.\n• **Kb(HA⁻) = Kw/Ka1**, because its conjugate acid is H₂A.\n• Ka > Kb → acts as an acid; Kb > Ka → acts as a base.","steps":[{"text":"The ion's own acid constant is Ka2.","hl":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸"},{"text":"Its base constant comes from its conjugate acid, H₂SO₃.","hl":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","calc":["Kb = Kw/Ka1 = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.4 × 10⁻²","Kb ≈ **7.1 × 10⁻¹³**"]},{"text":"Compare: Ka2 is about 10⁵ times Kb, so losing H⁺ wins.","calc":["6.3 × 10⁻⁸ ≫ 7.1 × 10⁻¹³"]},{"text":"Kw/Ka2 is not this ion's Kb; Ka1 belongs to H₂SO₃; the two constants are nowhere near equal.","strike":["a base; H⁺ gain wins.","a base; its Ka1 is largest.","neither; the two balance."]},{"text":"Answer: an acid."}],"trap":"Dividing Kw by Ka2 to get the ion's Kb: that gives the Kb of SO₃²⁻, not of HSO₃⁻."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","note":"H₂SO₃ → HSO₃⁻"},{"t":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸","note":"the ion's own Ka"},{"t":"HSO₃⁻","note":"amphoteric ion"}]},{"text":"**Two jobs, two constants.** As an acid HSO₃⁻ uses Ka2; as a base it uses Kw divided by the Ka of its conjugate acid, Ka1.","calc":["Kb = Kw/Ka1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an amphoteric ion's Ka is the constant for losing its H⁺, its Kb is Kw over the Ka of the acid it forms; the larger one decides."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-13a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:13#1","pe3-cp:13#2","pe3-cp:13#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:13"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P3","passage":"Cadmium sulfide, CdS, is a yellow pigment made by mixing two colorless solutions: Cd(NO₃)₂(aq) + Na₂S(aq) → CdS(s) + 2NaNO₃(aq). A chemist dissolved 7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol) and 1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol) in separate beakers of water and then poured them together (CdS: 144.5 g/mol). A bright yellow solid formed at once and was filtered off; the colorless filtrate still conducted electricity, since Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ stay dissolved, and it gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺. Cadmium (Z = 48) sits below zinc in group 12, and like zinc it forms only one ion, Cd²⁺; after the [Kr] core of 36 electrons, its 5s subshell fills before its 4d. Treating the precipitation as a single elementary step, the chemist sketched its energy diagram: the separated ions start at 15 kJ/mol, the highest point reaches 70 kJ/mol, and the solid product ends at −45 kJ/mol.","q":"Which reactant limits the synthesis, and what mass of CdS can form at most?","choices":["Na₂S; 2.9 g","Cd(NO₃)₂; 4.3 g","Na₂S; 4.3 g","Cd(NO₃)₂; 2.9 g"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: Cd²⁺ is left over in the filtrate, so cadmium nitrate was in excess, not limiting."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards again: the leftover Cd²⁺ rules out cadmium nitrate as the limiting reactant."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Right limiting reactant, but 4.3 g comes from the excess reactant's moles, not the limiting one's."}],"rule":"Convert each reactant to moles and divide by its coefficient; the smallest limits, sets the yield, and the other is left over.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The passage already names the excess reactant (leftover Cd²⁺), so the other one limits. Yield = its moles × 144.5 g/mol."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says the filtrate still held **Cd²⁺**, so Cd(NO₃)₂ was in excess and **Na₂S limits**. Check with sentence 2: 7.09 g ÷ 236.4 = 0.0300 mol Cd(NO₃)₂; 1.56 g ÷ 78.0 = **0.0200 mol Na₂S**, and the ratio is 1:1. Yield = 0.0200 mol × 144.5 g/mol = **2.9 g CdS**.","how":{"ask":"Find which reactant runs out, then base the yield on its moles.","know":"• **Moles** = mass ÷ molar mass.\n• Divide by each coefficient: the **smallest** value is the **limiting** reactant.\n• A reactant **left over** at the end was in **excess**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the filtrate gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺, so cadmium nitrate was in excess.","hl":"it gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺","calc":["Cd(NO₃)₂ in excess → Na₂S limits"]},{"text":"That is the limiting-reactant idea; confirm it with moles from sentence 2.","hl":"7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol) and 1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol)","calc":["Cd(NO₃)₂: 7.09 ÷ 236.4 = 0.0300 mol","Na₂S: 1.56 ÷ 78.0 = **0.0200 mol** (1:1, smaller → limiting)"]},{"text":"Base the yield on the limiting reactant.","calc":["CdS = 0.0200 mol × 144.5 g/mol = **2.9 g**"]},{"text":"Cadmium nitrate is left over, so it cannot be limiting, whatever mass is paired with it.","strike":["Cd(NO₃)₂; 4.3 g","Cd(NO₃)₂; 2.9 g"]},{"text":"4.3 g is 0.0300 mol × 144.5: the excess reactant's moles, more than the limiting one allows.","strike":["Na₂S; 4.3 g"]},{"text":"Answer: Na₂S; 2.9 g."}],"trap":"Calling the reactant with the smaller mass limiting, or basing the yield on the excess reactant's moles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"positive test for leftover Cd²⁺","note":"Cd(NO₃)₂ in excess"},{"t":"7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol)","note":"0.0300 mol"},{"t":"1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol)","note":"0.0200 mol"}]},{"text":"**Moles, not grams.** Divide each mass by its molar mass, then by its coefficient."},{"text":"**Yield from the limiting reactant.**","calc":["mol limiting × (product coefficient ÷ its coefficient) × M(product)"]},{"text":"**Also:** leftover reactant seen after the reaction is a direct clue to which reactant was in excess."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the reactant with the fewest moles per coefficient limits the reaction and sets the theoretical yield; any reactant left over was in excess."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-14a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:14#1","pe3-cp:14#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:14"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P3","passage":"Cadmium sulfide, CdS, is a yellow pigment made by mixing two colorless solutions: Cd(NO₃)₂(aq) + Na₂S(aq) → CdS(s) + 2NaNO₃(aq). A chemist dissolved 7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol) and 1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol) in separate beakers of water and then poured them together (CdS: 144.5 g/mol). A bright yellow solid formed at once and was filtered off; the colorless filtrate still conducted electricity, since Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ stay dissolved, and it gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺. Cadmium (Z = 48) sits below zinc in group 12, and like zinc it forms only one ion, Cd²⁺; after the [Kr] core of 36 electrons, its 5s subshell fills before its 4d. Treating the precipitation as a single elementary step, the chemist sketched its energy diagram: the separated ions start at 15 kJ/mol, the highest point reaches 70 kJ/mol, and the solid product ends at −45 kJ/mol.","q":"Based on the chemist's energy diagram, what is the forward activation energy per mole?","choices":["−60 kJ","55 kJ","70 kJ","115 kJ"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"An activation energy is a climb from reactants to the peak, so it can never be negative."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the subtraction: 70 kJ/mol is the peak's height, not the climb from the reactants."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Peak minus product is the reverse activation energy, not the forward one."}],"rule":"Ea(forward) = transition-state energy − reactant energy; ΔH = products − reactants; Ea(reverse) = transition state − products.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read two numbers only: the peak and the starting level. Forward Ea = peak − start."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Energy diagram for the one-step model of the precipitation","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":-60,"max":80,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Ions → CdS","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,15],[1,15],[3,40],[5,70],[7,20],[9,-45],[10,-45]]}]},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives the energies: reactant ions at **15 kJ/mol**, the transition state at **70 kJ/mol**, and the product at −45 kJ/mol. **Ea = E(transition state) − E(reactants)** = 70 − 15 = **55 kJ/mol**. ΔH = −45 − 15 = −60 kJ/mol, and the reverse Ea is 70 − (−45) = 115 kJ/mol.","how":{"ask":"Subtract the reactants' energy from the energy of the highest point.","know":"• **Ea (forward)** = transition state − reactants.\n• **ΔH** = products − reactants.\n• **Ea (reverse)** = transition state − products.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the starting energy of the reactants and the height of the highest point.","hl":"the separated ions start at 15 kJ/mol, the highest point reaches 70 kJ/mol","calc":["E(reactants) = 15 kJ/mol","E(transition state) = 70 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Activation energy is the climb from reactants to the transition state.","calc":["Ea = 70 − 15 = **55 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"A negative value is ΔH, product minus reactant, and no Ea can be negative.","calc":["ΔH = −45 − 15 = −60 kJ/mol"],"strike":["−60 kJ"]},{"text":"70 kJ/mol is only the peak's height, and 115 kJ/mol is the climb from the product, the reverse Ea.","strike":["70 kJ","115 kJ"]},{"text":"Answer: 55 kJ."}],"trap":"Reading the peak's height straight off the axis: Ea is measured from the reactants' level, not from zero."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"start at 15 kJ/mol","note":"reactant level"},{"t":"the highest point reaches 70 kJ/mol","note":"transition state"},{"t":"ends at −45 kJ/mol","note":"product level"}]},{"text":"**Label three levels**: reactants, transition state, products."},{"text":"**Subtract the right pair.**","calc":["Ea(fwd) = TS − R","ΔH = P − R","Ea(rev) = TS − P"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** forward activation energy is the transition-state energy minus the reactant energy, and it is always positive."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-15a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:15#1","pe3-cp:15#2","pe3-cp:15#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:15"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P3","passage":"Cadmium sulfide, CdS, is a yellow pigment made by mixing two colorless solutions: Cd(NO₃)₂(aq) + Na₂S(aq) → CdS(s) + 2NaNO₃(aq). A chemist dissolved 7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol) and 1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol) in separate beakers of water and then poured them together (CdS: 144.5 g/mol). A bright yellow solid formed at once and was filtered off; the colorless filtrate still conducted electricity, since Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ stay dissolved, and it gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺. Cadmium (Z = 48) sits below zinc in group 12, and like zinc it forms only one ion, Cd²⁺; after the [Kr] core of 36 electrons, its 5s subshell fills before its 4d. Treating the precipitation as a single elementary step, the chemist sketched its energy diagram: the separated ions start at 15 kJ/mol, the highest point reaches 70 kJ/mol, and the solid product ends at −45 kJ/mol.","q":"Which describes the outer subshells of a neutral cadmium atom in its ground state?","choices":["Filled 4d, filled 5s","Filled 4d, half-filled 5s","Half-filled 4d, filled 5s","Filled 4d, empty 5s"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"A full 4d with a half-filled 5s holds 11 electrons past [Kr], one short of the neutral atom's count."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"A half-filled 4d plus a full 5s is only 7 electrons past [Kr], far short of the neutral atom's count."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"An empty 5s describes Cd²⁺, the ion named in the passage; the neutral atom keeps its 5s pair."}],"rule":"Count electrons past the noble-gas core first; neutral atoms keep their s electrons, while cations lose the outer s electrons before any d.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Group 12 = d¹⁰s². Cd: 48 − 36 = 12 electrons past [Kr] = 4d¹⁰5s². The ion drops the 5s pair."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives Z = 48 and the [Kr] core of 36, so neutral Cd has **12** electrons past the core: **5s²** (filled) and **4d¹⁰** (filled), [Kr]4d¹⁰5s². The ion Cd²⁺ loses the two **5s** electrons first, leaving [Kr]4d¹⁰.","how":{"ask":"Count the electrons past the core, fill 5s and 4d, then describe each subshell.","know":"• Group 12 atoms (Zn, Cd) end in **d¹⁰s²**: both subshells filled (Zn is [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²).\n• Cations lose the outer **s electrons before d** electrons (Zn²⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰).\n• \"Half-filled\" means **d⁵** or **s¹**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives cadmium's atomic number and the size of its core, and says 5s fills before 4d.","hl":"Cadmium (Z = 48)","calc":["48 − 36 = 12 electrons past [Kr]"]},{"text":"Fill in aufbau order: 5s first (2), then 4d (10).","hl":"its 5s subshell fills before its 4d","calc":["5s² + 4d¹⁰ = 12 ✓"]},{"text":"A half-filled 4d (7 in all) or a half-filled 5s (11 in all) has too few electrons; an empty 5s is the Cd²⁺ ion, not the atom.","strike":["Half-filled 4d, filled 5s","Filled 4d, half-filled 5s","Filled 4d, empty 5s"]},{"text":"Answer: filled 4d, filled 5s."}],"trap":"Describing the ion when the question asks about the neutral atom: Cd²⁺ has an empty 5s, but the atom keeps both 5s electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"Cadmium (Z = 48)","note":"48 electrons"},{"t":"forms only one ion, Cd²⁺","note":"loses two electrons"},{"t":"the [Kr] core of 36 electrons","note":"subtract the core"}]},{"text":"**Count past the core.**","calc":["48 − 36 = 12 → 4d¹⁰5s²"]},{"text":"**Also:** the ion removes the 5s electrons first, giving [Kr]4d¹⁰ with every electron paired; zinc does the same, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s² → [Ar]3d¹⁰."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** group 12 atoms are d¹⁰s² with both subshells filled; their ions lose the s pair first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-16a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:16#1","pe3-cp:32#1","pe3-cp:32#2","pe3-cp:32#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:32"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Which species is reduced in enzyme Z's overall reaction?","choices":["O₂","NADH","FMN","RNH₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: NADH gives up a hydride and becomes NAD⁺, so it is oxidized."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the substrate's N gains an O and goes from −3 to −1, an oxidation."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The flavin is reduced in one step but reoxidized in the next, so it has no net change."}],"rule":"Reduced = gains electrons (oxidation state drops). Molecular oxygen ending up as H₂O or C–OH/N–OH goes from 0 to −2; regenerated cofactors don't count.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"In a reaction that consumes NADH and molecular oxygen, NADH is the reductant and the oxygen is what ends up reduced."},"exp":"Adding the steps in sentences 2 and 3 gives NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O. **O₂** (oxidation state 0) ends up as H₂O and as the N–OH oxygen, both **−2**: it gains electrons, so it is **reduced**. NADH and the substrate's nitrogen are oxidized, and FMN is regenerated.","how":{"ask":"Write the overall reaction, then find the species whose oxidation state drops.","know":"• **Reduced** = gains electrons; its oxidation state goes **down**.\n• O in O₂ is **0**; in H₂O or an OH group it is **−2**.\n• A **regenerated** cofactor has no net change.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the reduced enzyme uses O₂, and its oxygen ends up in H₂O and in RNHOH.","hl":"E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","calc":["O: 0 → −2 in both products"]},{"text":"An oxidation state that drops means a gain of electrons: reduction.","calc":["O₂ gains electrons → **reduced**"]},{"text":"NADH loses a hydride to become NAD⁺, and the substrate's N goes from −3 to −1: both are oxidized.","strike":["NADH","RNH₂"]},{"text":"Sentence 2 reduces FMN, but sentence 3 reoxidizes it, so it has no net change.","strike":["FMN"]},{"text":"Answer: O₂."}],"trap":"Naming the flavin because it is reduced in the first step: it is reoxidized in the second, so overall it is unchanged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"takes a hydride and a proton from NADH","note":"NADH is oxidized"},{"t":"uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen","note":"O₂ is reduced"}]},{"text":"**Track oxidation states.**","calc":["O: 0 (O₂) → −2 (H₂O, N–OH)","N: −3 (RNH₂) → −1 (RNHOH)"]},{"text":"**Cancel the cofactor.** A flavin that is reduced and then reoxidized each turn has no net change."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the species whose oxidation state falls is reduced; check the net reaction, not a single step."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-16b","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:16"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P3","passage":"Cadmium sulfide, CdS, is a yellow pigment made by mixing two colorless solutions: Cd(NO₃)₂(aq) + Na₂S(aq) → CdS(s) + 2NaNO₃(aq). A chemist dissolved 7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol) and 1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol) in separate beakers of water and then poured them together (CdS: 144.5 g/mol). A bright yellow solid formed at once and was filtered off; the colorless filtrate still conducted electricity, since Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ stay dissolved, and it gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺. Cadmium (Z = 48) sits below zinc in group 12, and like zinc it forms only one ion, Cd²⁺; after the [Kr] core of 36 electrons, its 5s subshell fills before its 4d. Treating the precipitation as a single elementary step, the chemist sketched its energy diagram: the separated ions start at 15 kJ/mol, the highest point reaches 70 kJ/mol, and the solid product ends at −45 kJ/mol.","q":"How is the reaction that makes the pigment best classified?","choices":["precipitation; no e⁻ move","redox; Cd²⁺ is reduced","redox; S²⁻ is oxidized","Lewis acid–base; adduct forms"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Cadmium stays +2 in CdS; a reduction would need its oxidation state to drop, and it does not."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Sulfur stays −2 in CdS; oxidized sulfide would become elemental sulfur, not a sulfide salt."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"No electron pair is donated to make a covalent adduct; the ions just pack into an insoluble lattice."}],"rule":"Assign oxidation states before and after: if none change, it is not redox; ions from two solutions forming an insoluble solid is a precipitation.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Check oxidation numbers: Cd +2 → +2, S −2 → −2. Nothing changes, and a solid drops out of mixed ion solutions."},"exp":"Net ionic: Cd²⁺(aq) + S²⁻(aq) → CdS(s). **No oxidation state changes**: Cd stays +2 and S stays −2, so it is not redox. Sentence 3 shows an **insoluble solid forming from two ion solutions**, a precipitation; Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ are spectators, so the filtrate still conducts.","how":{"ask":"Check the oxidation states first, then match the observations to a reaction type.","know":"• **Redox**: some oxidation state changes.\n• **Precipitation**: ions in solution combine into an insoluble solid; oxidation states stay put.\n• **Acid–base**: H⁺ transfer, or an electron-pair donor bonding to an acceptor to form an adduct.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says a yellow solid formed at once, while the filtrate kept conducting because Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ stay dissolved.","hl":"A bright yellow solid formed at once","calc":["Cd²⁺(aq) + S²⁻(aq) → CdS(s)"]},{"text":"To classify, assign oxidation states on both sides.","calc":["Cd: +2 → +2","S: −2 → −2","no change → not redox"],"strike":["redox; Cd²⁺ is reduced","redox; S²⁻ is oxidized"]},{"text":"No electron pair is shared into a new covalent adduct; the ions just pack into a lattice.","strike":["Lewis acid–base; adduct forms"]},{"text":"Answer: precipitation."}],"trap":"Calling any reaction of a metal ion redox: check whether an oxidation number actually changes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"two colorless solutions","note":"ions in water"},{"t":"A bright yellow solid formed at once","note":"insoluble product"},{"t":"Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ stay dissolved","note":"spectators"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation states decide redox.**","calc":["metal + element → salt: states change → redox","ion + ion → solid: no change → not redox"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** if no oxidation number changes it is not redox; ions combining into an insoluble solid is a precipitation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-17a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:17#1","pe3-cp:17#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:17"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P4","passage":"A portable water purifier kills microbes with ultraviolet light from a sealed, gas-filled lamp. The lamp emits every wavelength from 280 nm to 450 nm; take hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm. To protect the quartz envelope, the lamp gas must never exceed 131 °F, and the design team plugs this limit into PV = nRT to find the highest gas pressure. The lamp is driven by square pulses: the voltage is held at 2.4 kV for 15 µs, then switched off for 25 µs, and the cycle repeats. The lamp's cooling air passes between two large parallel plates 8.0 mm apart, the upper one at +150 V and the lower one grounded, which pull charged dust out of the airflow. Away from the plate edges, the field in the gap has one strength everywhere.","q":"At the passage's gas temperature limit, what value of T must be entered in PV = nRT?","choices":["55 K","328 K","346 K","404 K"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stopped at Celsius: the gas law needs kelvin, so 273 must still be added."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Added 273 to the Fahrenheit reading without converting it to Celsius first."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Divided by 1.8 without first subtracting 32, which overshoots the Celsius value."}],"rule":"°F → °C: subtract 32, then multiply by 5/9; °C → K: add 273. Gas laws always use kelvin.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor points: 32 °F = 0 °C and each °F step is 5/9 of a °C. (131 − 32) × 5/9 = 55 °C, then + 273."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the limit in **°F** and says it goes into PV = nRT, which needs **kelvin**. °F → °C: (131 − 32) × 5/9 = 55 °C. °C → K: 55 + 273 = **328 K**.","how":{"ask":"Convert the Fahrenheit limit to Celsius, then to kelvin.","know":"• **°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9**.\n• **K = °C + 273**.\n• Gas-law temperatures are always absolute (kelvin).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the limit in Fahrenheit and says it is used in the ideal gas law.","hl":"the lamp gas must never exceed 131 °F","calc":["T = 131 °F → must become kelvin"]},{"text":"Convert Fahrenheit to Celsius: subtract 32, then scale by 5/9.","calc":["(131 − 32) × 5/9 = 99 × 5/9 = 55 °C"]},{"text":"Convert Celsius to kelvin.","calc":["55 + 273 = **328 K**"]},{"text":"Stopping at Celsius gives 55; adding 273 to the °F reading gives 404; dividing 131 by 1.8 without subtracting 32 gives about 346.","strike":["55 K","404 K","346 K"]},{"text":"Answer: 328 K."}],"trap":"Adding 273 straight to a Fahrenheit number: the 273 offset belongs to the Celsius scale."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"131 °F","note":"Fahrenheit: convert twice"},{"t":"PV = nRT","note":"needs kelvin"}]},{"text":"**Fahrenheit to Celsius.**","calc":["°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9"]},{"text":"**Celsius to kelvin.**","calc":["K = °C + 273"]},{"text":"**Also:** sanity check: body temperature, 98.6 °F, is 37 °C, about 310 K."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** before any gas-law calculation, take °F to °C (subtract 32, × 5/9) and then add 273."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-18a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:18#2","pe4-cp:15#1","pe4-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:18"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P4","passage":"A portable water purifier kills microbes with ultraviolet light from a sealed, gas-filled lamp. The lamp emits every wavelength from 280 nm to 450 nm; take hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm. To protect the quartz envelope, the lamp gas must never exceed 131 °F, and the design team plugs this limit into PV = nRT to find the highest gas pressure. The lamp is driven by square pulses: the voltage is held at 2.4 kV for 15 µs, then switched off for 25 µs, and the cycle repeats. The lamp's cooling air passes between two large parallel plates 8.0 mm apart, the upper one at +150 V and the lower one grounded, which pull charged dust out of the airflow. Away from the plate edges, the field in the gap has one strength everywhere.","q":"What period and what frequency describe the lamp's drive pulses?","choices":["40 µs; 25 kHz","15 µs; 67 kHz","25 µs; 40 kHz","40 µs; 25 MHz"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a thousand: a period of microseconds gives a frequency in kilohertz, not megahertz."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Used only the on-time; one period is the on-time plus the off-time."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Used only the off-time; one period includes the on-time too."}],"rule":"One period is one full repeat, on-time plus off-time; f = 1/T, and 1/µs = MHz, so 1/(tens of µs) = tens of kHz.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Add on + off for T. 1/(1 µs) = 1 MHz, so 1/(40 µs) = 1/40 MHz = 25 kHz."},"exp":"Sentence 4 says each cycle is **15 µs on** and **25 µs off**, then repeats, so one period is **T = 15 + 25 = 40 µs**. **f = 1/T** = 1/(40 × 10⁻⁶ s) = 2.5 × 10⁴ Hz = **25 kHz**.","how":{"ask":"Add the on-time and the off-time for one period, then invert it.","know":"• **Period T**: one full cycle, on plus off.\n• **f = 1/T**.\n• 1 µs = 10⁻⁶ s; 1 kHz = 10³ Hz.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the on-time and the off-time and says the cycle repeats.","hl":"held at 2.4 kV for 15 µs, then switched off for 25 µs","calc":["on = 15 µs, off = 25 µs"]},{"text":"One period is one full repeat: both parts together.","calc":["T = 15 + 25 = **40 µs**"]},{"text":"Frequency is the inverse of the period.","calc":["f = 1/(40 × 10⁻⁶ s) = 2.5 × 10⁴ Hz = **25 kHz**"]},{"text":"Using only the on-time or only the off-time misses half the cycle; a microsecond period gives kilohertz, not megahertz.","strike":["15 µs; 67 kHz","25 µs; 40 kHz","40 µs; 25 MHz"]},{"text":"Answer: 40 µs; 25 kHz."}],"trap":"Taking the pulse width as the period: the period runs from the start of one pulse to the start of the next."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"for 15 µs","note":"on-time"},{"t":"switched off for 25 µs","note":"off-time"},{"t":"the cycle repeats","note":"period = on + off"}]},{"text":"**One full repeat.** Measure from the start of one pulse to the start of the next."},{"text":"**Invert.**","calc":["f = 1/T; µs ↔ MHz, ms ↔ kHz"]},{"text":"**Also:** the voltage height (2.4 kV) does not affect the period or frequency."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the period of a pulse train is on-time plus off-time, and the frequency is its inverse."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-19a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:19#1","pe3-cp:19#2","pe3-cp:19#3","pe4-cp:6#1","pe4-cp:6#2","pe4-cp:6#3","pe5-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:19"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P4","passage":"A portable water purifier kills microbes with ultraviolet light from a sealed, gas-filled lamp. The lamp emits every wavelength from 280 nm to 450 nm; take hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm. To protect the quartz envelope, the lamp gas must never exceed 131 °F, and the design team plugs this limit into PV = nRT to find the highest gas pressure. The lamp is driven by square pulses: the voltage is held at 2.4 kV for 15 µs, then switched off for 25 µs, and the cycle repeats. The lamp's cooling air passes between two large parallel plates 8.0 mm apart, the upper one at +150 V and the lower one grounded, which pull charged dust out of the airflow. Away from the plate edges, the field in the gap has one strength everywhere.","q":"What is the greatest energy carried by a single photon from the lamp, and at what wavelength?","choices":["4.4 eV, at 280 nm","2.8 eV, at 450 nm","4.4 × 10⁻⁹ eV, at 280 nm","0.23 eV, at 280 nm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"With hc in eV·nm, λ goes in nm; converting λ to meters mixes the units."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: photon energy falls as wavelength grows, so the longest wavelength gives the weakest photon."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"λ/hc inverts the relation; photon energy is hc divided by λ."}],"rule":"E = hc/λ: the shortest wavelength in a band carries the most energy per photon; with hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm, put λ in nm.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm, so E (eV) = 1240/λ (nm). Pick the shortest λ in the band."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives the band, **280 nm to 450 nm**, and hc. Since **E = hc/λ**, the most energetic photon has the **shortest** wavelength: E = 1240 eV·nm ÷ 280 nm ≈ **4.4 eV**. The 450 nm end gives only about 2.8 eV.","how":{"ask":"Pick the shortest wavelength in the band, then compute E = hc/λ.","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: energy and wavelength are inversely related.\n• hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm (or 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m).\n• 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the lamp's wavelength range and the value of hc to use.","hl":"every wavelength from 280 nm to 450 nm","calc":["λ range: 280–450 nm; hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm"]},{"text":"E = hc/λ, so the largest energy goes with the smallest wavelength.","calc":["λ_min = 280 nm"]},{"text":"Compute, keeping λ in nm to match hc.","calc":["E = 1240/280 ≈ **4.4 eV**"]},{"text":"The long end gives the least energy; putting λ in meters mixes units; λ/hc turns the formula upside down.","strike":["2.8 eV, at 450 nm","4.4 × 10⁻⁹ eV, at 280 nm","0.23 eV, at 280 nm"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.4 eV, at 280 nm."}],"trap":"Choosing the longest wavelength because its number is bigger: a longer wavelength means a less energetic photon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"from 280 nm to 450 nm","note":"shortest = 280 nm"},{"t":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm","note":"λ stays in nm"}]},{"text":"**Inverse relation.** Shorter wavelength → higher frequency → more energy per photon."},{"text":"**Match units.**","calc":["E (eV) = 1240 / λ (nm)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a band of wavelengths, the highest photon energy is hc divided by the shortest wavelength."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-20a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:20#1","pe3-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:20"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P4","passage":"A portable water purifier kills microbes with ultraviolet light from a sealed, gas-filled lamp. The lamp emits every wavelength from 280 nm to 450 nm; take hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm. To protect the quartz envelope, the lamp gas must never exceed 131 °F, and the design team plugs this limit into PV = nRT to find the highest gas pressure. The lamp is driven by square pulses: the voltage is held at 2.4 kV for 15 µs, then switched off for 25 µs, and the cycle repeats. The lamp's cooling air passes between two large parallel plates 8.0 mm apart, the upper one at +150 V and the lower one grounded, which pull charged dust out of the airflow. Away from the plate edges, the field in the gap has one strength everywhere.","q":"Away from the edges, how do the electric field lines in the gap run?","choices":["Evenly spaced; upper to lower","Evenly spaced; lower to upper","Denser near plates; upper to lower","Curved outward; lower to upper"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: field lines run from higher potential to lower, so they leave the +150 V plate."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A field with one strength everywhere has evenly spaced lines; crowding would mean a stronger field there."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Curved lines appear only near the edges; the passage describes the region away from them."}],"rule":"Between large parallel plates the field is uniform: straight, parallel, evenly spaced lines from the higher-potential plate to the lower.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same strength everywhere = same line spacing everywhere. Lines point downhill in potential: + plate → grounded plate."},"exp":"Sentence 6 says the field has **the same strength everywhere** away from the edges, so the lines are **parallel and evenly spaced**. Sentence 5 puts the upper plate at **+150 V** and the lower one at 0 V; field lines point from higher to lower potential, so they run **from the upper plate to the lower**.","how":{"ask":"Use the field's strength to set the spacing, then the plate potentials to set the direction.","know":"• Line **density** shows field **strength**: equal strength → equal spacing.\n• Field lines point from **+ (higher V)** to **− (lower V)**.\n• Large parallel plates give a **uniform** field away from the edges.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the field has one strength throughout the gap, away from the edges.","hl":"the field in the gap has one strength everywhere","calc":["uniform E → evenly spaced, parallel lines"]},{"text":"Direction: sentence 5 puts the upper plate at the higher potential.","hl":"the upper one at +150 V and the lower one grounded","calc":["E points high V → low V: upper → lower"]},{"text":"Lower-to-upper points uphill in potential; crowding near the plates would mean a stronger field there; curved lines belong only at the edges.","strike":["Evenly spaced; lower to upper","Denser near plates; upper to lower","Curved outward; lower to upper"]},{"text":"Answer: evenly spaced, from the upper plate to the lower."}],"trap":"Drawing the lines toward the positive plate: they start on positive charge and end on negative."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"large parallel plates","note":"uniform field"},{"t":"one strength everywhere","note":"even spacing"},{"t":"the upper one at +150 V","note":"lines start here"}]},{"text":"**Spacing = strength.** Closer lines mean a stronger field, so a uniform field has evenly spaced lines."},{"text":"**Direction.** Lines run from higher to lower potential, the way a positive charge would be pushed.","calc":["E = ΔV/d = 150 V ÷ 8.0 mm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** between parallel plates, field lines are straight, evenly spaced and point from the higher-potential plate to the lower."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-21a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:21#1","pe3-cp:21#2","pe3-cp:21#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:21"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P5","passage":"An amperometric sensor tracks 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), a solvent residue, in factory wastewater. On its electrode, an engineered oxidase converts the alcohol into its ketone, acetophenone, by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons. A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion, held tightly in the active site, accepts both hydrogens and then passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂; no other substrate is consumed. The electrode then oxidizes each H₂O₂, collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted (F = 96,500 C/mol). To calibrate the sensor, a 6.0 µL drop of a 3.0 mM standard of the alcohol is placed on the electrode, and every alcohol molecule in the drop reacts. In one field reading, 0.0965 C of charge was collected.","q":"What mass of 1-phenylethanol does one calibration drop deliver to the electrode?","choices":["2.2 × 10⁻⁶ g","2.2 × 10⁻³ g","1.8 × 10⁻⁸ g","1.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ g"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a thousand: µL is 10⁻⁶ L, not 10⁻³ L."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divided by the molar mass instead of multiplying: grams = moles × g/mol."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stopped at moles: that number still has to be multiplied by the molar mass to give grams."}],"rule":"Mass in a small volume: moles = M × V (in L), then grams = moles × molar mass; µL = 10⁻⁶ L and mM = 10⁻³ M.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mM × µL = nmol: 3.0 × 6.0 = 18 nmol. Then 18 nmol × 122 g/mol ≈ 2200 ng."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives the drop: **6.0 µL** of a **3.0 mM** standard. Moles = (3.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L)(6.0 × 10⁻⁶ L) = 1.8 × 10⁻⁸ mol. Sentence 1 gives the molar mass, so mass = 1.8 × 10⁻⁸ mol × 122 g/mol ≈ **2.2 × 10⁻⁶ g**.","how":{"ask":"Turn concentration × volume into moles, then moles into grams.","know":"• **mol = M × V** (V in liters).\n• **g = mol × molar mass**.\n• **1 µL = 10⁻⁶ L**; **1 mM = 10⁻³ M**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the drop volume and the standard's concentration.","hl":"a 6.0 µL drop of a 3.0 mM standard","calc":["V = 6.0 × 10⁻⁶ L, c = 3.0 × 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"Molarity times liters gives moles.","calc":["n = 3.0 × 10⁻³ × 6.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 1.8 × 10⁻⁸ mol"]},{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the molar mass; multiply to get grams.","hl":"1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol)","calc":["m = 1.8 × 10⁻⁸ × 122 ≈ **2.2 × 10⁻⁶ g**"]},{"text":"Treating µL as mL gives a thousand times too much; stopping at moles skips the molar mass; dividing by it inverts the conversion.","strike":["2.2 × 10⁻³ g","1.8 × 10⁻⁸ g","1.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ g"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.2 × 10⁻⁶ g."}],"trap":"Reading µ as milli: a microliter is a millionth of a liter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"6.0 µL drop","note":"6.0 × 10⁻⁶ L"},{"t":"3.0 mM standard","note":"3.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L"},{"t":"(122 g/mol)","note":"moles → grams"}]},{"text":"**Moles first.**","calc":["n = c × V"]},{"text":"**Then grams.**","calc":["m = n × M"]},{"text":"**Also:** if the concentration is already in g/L, skip the molar mass: m = (g/L) × L."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass of solute = concentration × volume in liters, then × molar mass when the concentration is in mol/L."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-22a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:22#1","pe3-cp:22#2","pe3-cp:40#1","pe3-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:22"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P5","passage":"An amperometric sensor tracks 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), a solvent residue, in factory wastewater. On its electrode, an engineered oxidase converts the alcohol into its ketone, acetophenone, by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons. A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion, held tightly in the active site, accepts both hydrogens and then passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂; no other substrate is consumed. The electrode then oxidizes each H₂O₂, collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted (F = 96,500 C/mol). To calibrate the sensor, a 6.0 µL drop of a 3.0 mM standard of the alcohol is placed on the electrode, and every alcohol molecule in the drop reacts. In one field reading, 0.0965 C of charge was collected.","fig":["1-phenylethanol","acetophenone"],"q":"To which enzyme class does the sensor's oxidase belong?","choices":["Oxidoreductase: H removed","Transferase: passes H to O₂","Lyase: forms a C=O","Isomerase: same formula"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"The ketone has two fewer H atoms than the alcohol, so the formula changes; an isomerase keeps it the same."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A lyase makes or breaks a double bond without redox; here two H go to the cofactor and then O₂, an oxidation."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Transferases move a whole group between substrates; handing hydrogens to O₂ is an oxidation–reduction."}],"rule":"Name the change: H removed or O added → oxidoreductase; group moved → transferase; water splits a bond → hydrolase; atoms rearranged → isomerase.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count H: alcohol → ketone loses 2 H, which end up on O₂. Losing hydrogen is oxidation, so the class is the redox one."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says the enzyme turns the alcohol into a ketone by **removing two hydrogens**, and sentence 3 says they end up reducing O₂ to H₂O₂. An enzyme that catalyzes an **oxidation–reduction** is an **oxidoreductase**, just like glucose oxidase in a glucose meter.","how":{"ask":"Compare substrate and product, decide what kind of change it is, and match it to one of the six classes.","know":"• **Oxidoreductase**: oxidation–reduction (H removed, O added, electrons moved).\n• **Transferase**: moves a group (methyl, phosphate) from one molecule to another.\n• **Hydrolase**: splits a bond with water.\n• **Lyase**: makes or breaks a double bond or ring by adding or removing a group, without hydrolysis or redox.\n• **Isomerase**: rearranges atoms within one molecule.\n• **Ligase**: joins two molecules, using ATP.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the oxidase strips two hydrogen atoms from the alcohol to make the ketone.","hl":"by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons","calc":["C₈H₁₀O → C₈H₈O + 2 H"]},{"text":"Losing hydrogen (electrons) is oxidation, and sentence 3 says O₂ is reduced to H₂O₂: a redox reaction.","hl":"passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂"},{"text":"The formula changed, so it is not an isomerase.","strike":["Isomerase: same formula"]},{"text":"Removing H and reducing O₂ is oxidation–reduction, not a group transfer or a lyase addition.","strike":["Transferase: passes H to O₂","Lyase: forms a C=O"]},{"text":"Answer: oxidoreductase."}],"trap":"Calling it a transferase because hydrogens move to O₂: moving electrons with hydrogens is redox; transferases move whole groups such as methyl or phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"removing two hydrogen atoms","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"passes them to O₂","note":"O₂ is reduced"},{"t":"no other substrate is consumed","note":"no group donor"}]},{"text":"**Six classes, six kinds of change.**","calc":["redox → oxidoreductase","group moved → transferase","water splits a bond → hydrolase","double bond made or broken → lyase","atoms rearranged → isomerase","two joined with ATP → ligase"]},{"text":"**Also:** an enzyme that moves a methyl group from SAM onto a substrate is a transferase; one that then oxidizes that methyl with O₂ is an oxidoreductase."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** decide what changed (redox, group transfer, hydrolysis, double bond, rearrangement, joining), then name the class."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-23a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:23#1","pe3-cp:23#2","pe3-cp:23#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:23"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P5","passage":"An amperometric sensor tracks 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), a solvent residue, in factory wastewater. On its electrode, an engineered oxidase converts the alcohol into its ketone, acetophenone, by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons. A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion, held tightly in the active site, accepts both hydrogens and then passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂; no other substrate is consumed. The electrode then oxidizes each H₂O₂, collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted (F = 96,500 C/mol). To calibrate the sensor, a 6.0 µL drop of a 3.0 mM standard of the alcohol is placed on the electrode, and every alcohol molecule in the drop reacts. In one field reading, 0.0965 C of charge was collected.","q":"What mass of 1-phenylethanol was oxidized during the field reading?","choices":["31 µg","61 µg","122 µg","61 mg"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a thousand: 5 × 10⁻⁷ mol × 122 g/mol is in micrograms, not milligrams."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the electron ratio: each alcohol molecule supplies two electrons, so divide the electron moles by 2."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Divided by four: the passage gives two electrons per molecule, not four."}],"rule":"Charge → mol e⁻ = Q/F; mol substance = mol e⁻ ÷ electrons per molecule; mass = mol × molar mass.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Q/F = 0.0965/96,500 = 1.0 µmol e⁻ → 0.50 µmol alcohol (2 e⁻ each) → × 122 µg/µmol."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives the charge; sentence 4 gives **two electrons per alcohol**. mol e⁻ = Q/F = 0.0965 C ÷ 96,500 C/mol = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol. mol alcohol = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 2 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol. Mass = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol × 122 g/mol = 6.1 × 10⁻⁵ g = **61 µg**.","how":{"ask":"Turn the charge into moles of electrons, then into moles of alcohol, then into mass.","know":"• **mol e⁻ = Q/F** (F ≈ 96,500 C/mol).\n• Divide by the **electrons per molecule** to get moles of substance.\n• **g = mol × molar mass**; 1 g/mol = 1 µg/µmol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the electrode collects two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted.","hl":"collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted","calc":["2 e⁻ per molecule"]},{"text":"That is Faraday's law: charge counts electrons. Sentence 6 gives the charge.","hl":"0.0965 C of charge was collected","calc":["mol e⁻ = 0.0965 ÷ 96,500 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol"]},{"text":"Divide by the electrons per molecule, then multiply by the molar mass.","calc":["mol alcohol = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 2 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol","m = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ × 122 = 6.1 × 10⁻⁵ g = **61 µg**"]},{"text":"Skipping the ÷2 doubles the mass; dividing by four halves it; milligrams is a thousand times too large.","strike":["122 µg","31 µg","61 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 61 µg."}],"trap":"Treating moles of electrons as moles of alcohol: each molecule gives two electrons, so there are half as many molecules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"two electrons for every alcohol molecule","note":"÷ 2"},{"t":"0.0965 C","note":"Q"},{"t":"F = 96,500 C/mol","note":"C → mol e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Charge to electrons.**","calc":["n(e⁻) = Q/F"]},{"text":"**Electrons to molecules to grams.**","calc":["n = n(e⁻)/z","m = n × M"]},{"text":"**Also:** if the passage gives moles of electrons directly, skip Q/F and start at the ÷ z step."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass from charge = (Q/F) ÷ electrons per molecule × molar mass."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-24a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:24#1","pe3-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:24"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P5","passage":"An amperometric sensor tracks 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), a solvent residue, in factory wastewater. On its electrode, an engineered oxidase converts the alcohol into its ketone, acetophenone, by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons. A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion, held tightly in the active site, accepts both hydrogens and then passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂; no other substrate is consumed. The electrode then oxidizes each H₂O₂, collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted (F = 96,500 C/mol). To calibrate the sensor, a 6.0 µL drop of a 3.0 mM standard of the alcohol is placed on the electrode, and every alcohol molecule in the drop reacts. In one field reading, 0.0965 C of charge was collected.","q":"Which cofactor takes up the two hydrogens, and how does it change?","choices":["FMN; it gains two H","FMNH₂; it loses two H","NAD⁺; it gains two H","Heme; Fe gains two e⁻"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"FMNH₂ is the form that already holds the hydrogens; the acceptor has to start oxidized and gain them."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"NAD⁺ is a loosely bound cosubstrate that takes a hydride; it is not the riboflavin-derived cofactor described."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Heme is an iron porphyrin, not made from riboflavin, and its iron takes electrons, not hydrogen atoms."}],"rule":"The acceptor of 2 H starts oxidized and ends reduced: FAD → FADH₂, FMN → FMNH₂. Tightly bound and riboflavin-derived means a flavin.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Riboflavin-derived, tightly bound, carries 2 H → a flavin. No AMP portion → FMN. The acceptor is the oxidized form, so it gains the 2 H."},"exp":"Sentence 3 describes a **flavin** made from riboflavin but **lacking FAD's AMP portion**: **FMN**. Like FAD in glucose oxidase, it **accepts 2 H (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻)** and is **reduced to FMNH₂**; it then hands them to O₂ and returns to FMN.","how":{"ask":"Identify the bound cofactor from the clues, then decide whether taking the hydrogens reduces or oxidizes it.","know":"• **FAD** (glucose oxidase, succinate dehydrogenase) and **FMN** are flavins made from riboflavin; they are tightly bound and carry **2 H**: FAD → FADH₂, FMN → FMNH₂.\n• **NAD⁺/NADP⁺** are loosely bound cosubstrates that take a hydride (H⁻).\n• Gaining H (electrons) = **reduction**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the cofactor is riboflavin-derived, lacks FAD's AMP portion and is held tightly.","hl":"A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion","calc":["→ FMN, a flavin"]},{"text":"NAD⁺ is not riboflavin-based and is not tightly bound; heme is an iron porphyrin.","strike":["NAD⁺; it gains two H","Heme; Fe gains two e⁻"]},{"text":"The acceptor gains the hydrogens, so it starts as FMN, not FMNH₂.","calc":["FMN + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻ → FMNH₂ (reduced)"],"strike":["FMNH₂; it loses two H"]},{"text":"Answer: FMN, which gains 2 H."}],"trap":"Picking the reduced form (FMNH₂ or FADH₂) as the acceptor: the acceptor has to start oxidized."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"riboflavin-derived","note":"a flavin"},{"t":"held tightly in the active site","note":"not NAD⁺"},{"t":"accepts both hydrogens","note":"gets reduced"}]},{"text":"**Flavins carry two hydrogens.**","calc":["FAD + 2 H → FADH₂","FMN + 2 H → FMNH₂"]},{"text":"**Also:** glucose oxidase uses bound FAD in the same way: the flavin takes two hydrogens from glucose, is reduced, and O₂ then reoxidizes it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a bound flavin (FAD or FMN) accepts 2 H and is reduced to its H₂ form; O₂ or another acceptor reoxidizes it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-25a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:25#1","pe3-cp:25#2","pe5-cp:21#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:25"],"q":"A hormone enters its target cell and binds an intracellular steroid receptor. Which molecule has the ring skeleton of a steroid?","choices":["Estradiol","Epinephrine","Thyroxine","Prostaglandin E₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Epinephrine is an amine (catecholamine) hormone with one ring; one ring can't be a four-ring core."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Thyroxine has two benzene rings linked through an O, not four fused rings."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Prostaglandins come from a 20-C fatty acid; one five-membered ring, not four fused rings."}],"rule":"Steroid core: 6-6-6-5 fused ring system; sex hormones and cortisol are steroids, amines and eicosanoids are not.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Steroid = four fused rings, 6-6-6-5, like cholesterol. Sex hormones (estr-, andr-, -diol, -one) are steroids; catecholamines, thyroid hormones and eicosanoids aren't."},"exp":"A steroid is defined by its **6-6-6-5 fused ring core** (rings A, B and C of six atoms, ring D of five). Estradiol has it. Epinephrine has one benzene ring, thyroxine has two separate rings joined by an ether O, and prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two chains.","how":{"ask":"Look for four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five, in each molecule.","know":"• **Steroid skeleton**: four **fused** rings, **6-6-6-5** (rings A, B, C, D), 17 ring carbons, derived from cholesterol.\n• Fused = neighboring rings share a bond.\n• Rings joined by a chain or an O are not fused, and a single ring is never a steroid.","steps":[{"text":"A steroid receptor binds molecules built on the steroid core.","hl":"steroid receptor"},{"text":"Draw the core to search for: three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring, all fused.","fig":["t3-steroid-abcd","cholesterol"],"calc":["rings: 6 + 6 + 6 + 5","fused: each pair shares one bond"]},{"text":"Estradiol has rings A–D (ring A aromatic): the 6-6-6-5 core."},{"text":"Epinephrine is a catecholamine with one benzene ring; thyroxine has two benzene rings linked through an O, not fused.","strike":["Epinephrine","Thyroxine"]},{"text":"Prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two side chains, a fatty-acid derivative.","strike":["Prostaglandin E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: estradiol."}],"trap":"Choosing any lipid-soluble or ring-containing hormone: only the fused 6-6-6-5 skeleton makes a steroid."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"steroid receptor","note":"needs a steroid"},{"t":"ring skeleton","note":"count and fuse the rings"}]},{"text":"**Know the core.** Four rings fused edge to edge: three six-membered (A, B, C) and one five-membered (D).","fig":"t3-steroid-abcd","calc":["6 + 6 + 6 + 5 = four rings, 17 ring C"]},{"text":"**Spot it in a drawing.** Find the five-membered ring, then check that it is fused to three six-membered rings in a row; extra groups (OH, C=O, a side chain) do not matter.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule out look-alikes.** One ring, rings joined by a linker, or no rings at all: not a steroid."},{"text":"**Also:** every steroid hormone (estradiol, cortisol, progesterone) is built from cholesterol, which already has the 6-6-6-5 core; the body shortens its 8-carbon side chain (to 2 carbons in progesterone and cortisol, none in estradiol) and keeps rings A–D. Asked for a steroid's precursor, choose the lipid drawn with those four rings, not a fatty acid or another open-chain lipid.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a steroid has four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five; look for that 6-6-6-5 skeleton and ignore the groups on it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-26a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:26#2","pe3-cp:26#3","pe4-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:26"],"q":"A carbonate buffer (HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3) reads pH 10.8. What should be added to reach its greatest buffer capacity, and how must base:acid change?","choices":["NaHCO₃; ratio 3.2 → 1.0","Na₂CO₃; ratio 3.2 → 1.0","NaHCO₃; ratio 0.32 → 1.0","NaOH; ratio 0.32 → 1.0"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Na₂CO₃ adds more of the base form, so the ratio and the pH climb further above pKa instead of falling."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The ratio is base over acid; with the pH above pKa it is greater than one now, not below one."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"NaOH turns HCO₃⁻ into CO₃²⁻, raising the ratio and the pH: the wrong direction, from a ratio read upside down."}],"rule":"Buffer capacity peaks at pH = pKa. If the pH is above pKa, add the acid form; if below, add the base form.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Capacity peaks at pH = pKa. pH 10.8 sits above 10.3, so there is too much base form: add the acid form, HCO₃⁻ (NaHCO₃), to pull pH down."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30.0 mL of 0.040 M NaHCO₃ with 0.080 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":15,"step":3},"y":{"label":"pH","min":9,"max":12,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1.5,9.35],[3,9.7],[6,10.12],[7.5,10.3],[9,10.48],[11.4,10.8],[13.5,11.25]]}]},"exp":"**pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])**. At pH 10.8 the ratio [CO₃²⁻]/[HCO₃⁻] = 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2. Capacity is greatest at **pH = pKa**, where the ratio is 1, so the pH must fall: add more of the **acid form**, NaHCO₃.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the pH must rise or fall to reach pKa, find the base:acid ratio now and at pKa, then add the form that moves it that way.","know":"• **Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]).\n• A buffer works best at **pH = pKa** ([base] = [acid]).\n• To **lower** pH, raise the share of the **acid** form; to raise pH, add the base form.","steps":[{"text":"Read the buffer pair and its pKa.","hl":"HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3","calc":["acid = HCO₃⁻, base = CO₃²⁻"]},{"text":"Find the current base:acid ratio.","hl":"pH 10.8","calc":["10.8 = 10.3 + log(ratio)","log(ratio) = 0.5","ratio = 10^0.5 ≈ **3.2**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":11.4,"text":"now: pH 10.8","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Best capacity is at pH = pKa = 10.3, ratio 1. The ratio must fall, so the pH must drop by 0.5: add the acid form.","calc":["pH 10.8 → 10.3: add HCO₃⁻ (as NaHCO₃)"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":7.5,"text":"pH = pKa 10.3","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Na₂CO₃ and NaOH raise the base share and push the pH higher; a ratio below one would mean the pH is already under pKa.","strike":["Na₂CO₃; ratio 3.2 → 1.0","NaOH; ratio 0.32 → 1.0","NaHCO₃; ratio 0.32 → 1.0"]},{"text":"Answer: NaHCO₃, so the ratio falls from about 3.2 to 1.0."}],"trap":"Adding the base form because the pH must \"reach\" pKa: here the buffer is above its pKa, so it needs more acid form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 10.3","note":"best buffering pH"},{"t":"pH 10.8","note":"current pH, above pKa"},{"t":"greatest buffer capacity","note":"pH = pKa"}]},{"text":"**Write Henderson–Hasselbalch.** It links pH to the base:acid ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])","10.8 − 10.3 = 0.5 → ratio ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Aim for pH = pKa.** There the ratio is 1, and the buffer resists added acid and base equally well."},{"text":"**Lower pH = more acid form.** The pH is too high, so raise [HCO₃⁻]."},{"text":"**Also:** for a phosphate buffer, the acid form is H₂PO₄⁻ (the monosodium salt, NaH₂PO₄); adding it lowers the pH, while Na₂HPO₄ raises it."},{"text":"**Also:** a buffer works only within pKa ± 1, where the base:acid ratio stays between one-tenth and ten; check each pH against that window.","calc":["pKa 10.3 → useful from pH 9.3 to 11.3"]},{"text":"**Also:** write Henderson–Hasselbalch with the two forms named before plugging in: the conjugate base (A⁻, the salt form) goes on top and the weak acid (HA) on the bottom. Then undo the log to get the ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","phosphate, pKa 7.2, target pH 6.9: log ratio = −0.3","[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^−0.3 ≈ 0.5, i.e. 1 : 2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]); to lower the pH add the acid form, to raise it add the base form, and capacity peaks at pH = pKa."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-27a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:27#1","pe3-cp:27#2","pe3-cp:27#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:27"],"q":"Glycine (75 Da) and leucine (131 Da) join by one peptide bond into the dipeptide shown, 188 Da. What mass is released?","choices":["18 Da","17 Da","206 Da","36 Da"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Adding the two amino-acid masses gives the starting total, not what leaves; the dipeptide is lighter than that."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Ammonia is not lost: the amine N stays in the new amide bond; the carboxyl gives up OH and the amine gives up one H."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"One peptide bond releases one water, not one water per amino acid."}],"rule":"Each peptide bond is a condensation: one water leaves, so the dipeptide = sum of the amino acids − one water.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Lost mass = (75 + 131) − 188. Then check that it matches a small molecule you know."},"fig":"cpt-gly-leu-sites","exp":"Forming a peptide bond is a **condensation**: the carboxyl's OH and one H from the amine leave together as **H₂O**. Check: 75 + 131 = 206; 206 − 188 = **18 Da**, the mass of one water.","how":{"ask":"Find the mass lost when the two amino acids join, and name what left.","know":"• A peptide bond links the **carbonyl C** of one amino acid to the **N** of the next.\n• Forming it releases **one H₂O** (18 Da): a condensation (dehydration) reaction.\n• Dipeptide mass = sum of the amino acids − 18.","steps":[{"text":"Add the two amino-acid masses.","hl":"Glycine (75 Da) and leucine (131 Da)","calc":["75 + 131 = 206 Da"]},{"text":"Subtract the dipeptide's mass.","hl":"188 Da","calc":["206 − 188 = **18 Da** = H₂O"]},{"text":"206 Da is the starting total; one bond releases one water, not two; the N stays in the amide.","strike":["206 Da","36 Da","17 Da"]},{"text":"Answer: 18 Da."}],"trap":"Thinking the amine nitrogen leaves as NH₃: the N stays in the amide bond, and only H and OH leave, as water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Glycine (75 Da)","note":"amino acid 1"},{"t":"leucine (131 Da)","note":"amino acid 2"},{"t":"188 Da","note":"after joining"}]},{"text":"**Condensation.** The bond forms between glycine's carbonyl C and leucine's N; the carboxyl OH and an amine H leave together.","fig":"cpt-gly-leu-sites"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** every peptide bond formed releases one water, 18 Da."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-28a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:28#1","pe3-cp:28#2","pe3-cp:28#3","pe3-cp:28#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:28"],"q":"A student is planning Michaelis–Menten measurements on a purified enzyme. Which condition is NOT required for valid data?","choices":["Initial rates measured","Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate","Reaction run to equilibrium"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Initial rates (v₀) are the very data a Michaelis–Menten plot uses, so they are required."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Enzyme rates shift with pH, so a fixed, buffered pH is required for comparable rates."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"[E] ≪ [S] keeps free [S] at its set value, an assumption the Michaelis–Menten equation needs."}],"rule":"Michaelis–Menten needs initial rates under fixed conditions with [E] ≪ [S]; the odd one out is the condition that is not part of that setup.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Picture the end point: at equilibrium [S] has dropped and the net rate is zero, so no v₀ is left to measure. The other three are the standard set-up; equilibrium is the odd one."},"exp":"Michaelis–Menten analysis needs **initial rates (v₀)** under **steady state**, a **constant pH**, and **[E] ≪ [S]** so [S] stays at its set value. Letting the reaction reach **equilibrium** is not wanted: by then [S] has fallen, product has built up and the net rate is zero.","how":{"ask":"Check each condition against the assumptions behind v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]).","know":"• **v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S])** assumes **initial rates** (little product, [S] ≈ starting value) and a **steady state** of ES.\n• **Constant pH** (a buffer) keeps the enzyme's charges and activity fixed.\n• **[E] ≪ [S]** so binding barely depletes free substrate.\n• At **equilibrium** the net rate is zero, so it gives no v₀.","steps":[{"text":"This is a NOT question: find the condition the analysis does not want.","hl":"NOT required"},{"text":"Initial rates are the data Michaelis–Menten uses: the slope at the start of the product-versus-time curve.","strike":["Initial rates measured"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Product formed over time in one enzyme run","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Product (μM)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Product","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,22],[5,46],[10,68],[15,80],[20,86],[25,88],[30,89]]}]}},{"text":"A buffered pH holds the enzyme's ionizable groups steady, and [E] ≪ [S] keeps free [S] at its set value.","strike":["Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate"]},{"text":"At equilibrium the curve is flat: net rate zero, [S] no longer the starting value. That is not wanted."},{"text":"Answer: reaction run to equilibrium."}],"trap":"Thinking a reaction must be “complete” to be measured: Michaelis–Menten uses only the first, straight part of the curve."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Michaelis–Menten","note":"uses v₀"},{"t":"NOT required","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Initial rates, steady state.** Measure v₀ while [ES] is steady and little product has formed; later the curve bends over.","calc":["v₀ = slope at t ≈ 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep the pH constant with a buffer; a changing pH changes the enzyme's activity during the run."},{"text":"**Also:** keep [E] much less than [S], so the enzyme does not tie up a significant share of the substrate.","calc":["[E] ≪ [S] → free [S] ≈ total [S]"]},{"text":"**Also:** letting the reaction reach equilibrium is not wanted: the net rate there is zero and [S] has changed, so rates must be initial."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** valid Michaelis–Menten data use initial rates at steady state, constant pH and [E] ≪ [S]; never wait for equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-29a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:29#1","pe3-cp:29#2","pe3-cp:29#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:29"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Which equation is the balanced net reaction of enzyme Z's two-step cycle?","choices":["NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O","E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","NADH + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O","NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ + FMN → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O + FMNH₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Unbalanced: the left side lacks one H and one + charge that the right side has."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is only the second step; the net reaction adds both steps together."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the cancelling: FMN is used in one step and remade in the other, so it drops out."}],"rule":"Net reaction of a catalytic cycle: add the steps and cancel every species that appears on both sides, including the regenerated enzyme form.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cross out what the cycle regenerates (E–FMN and E–FMNH₂); everything left over is the net reaction."},"exp":"Sentences 2 and 3 give the two steps. **Adding** them puts E–FMN and E–FMNH₂ on **both sides**, so they **cancel**, as a regenerated cofactor should. What remains: **NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O**, balanced in atoms and charge (+1 on each side).","how":{"ask":"Add the two steps, cancel what is regenerated, and check atoms and charge.","know":"• A **catalyst or bound cofactor** that is regenerated cancels from the net equation.\n• **Add** the steps; **cancel** species found on both sides.\n• Check **atoms** and **charge**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the first step, in which NADH reduces the bound flavin.","hl":"E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺"},{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the second step, which returns the enzyme to E–FMN; add the two.","hl":"E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","calc":["E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ + E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺ + E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O"]},{"text":"Cancel the enzyme forms on both sides.","calc":["net: NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O","charge: +1 = +1 ✓"]},{"text":"The step alone is not the net reaction; leaving FMN and FMNH₂ in skips the cancelling; dropping H⁺ breaks the charge balance.","strike":["E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ + FMN → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O + FMNH₂","NADH + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O"]},{"text":"Answer: NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O."}],"trap":"Keeping the cofactor in the net equation: a species made in one step and used in the next is regenerated and cancels."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"tightly bound flavin","note":"regenerated each cycle"},{"t":"E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺","note":"step one"},{"t":"E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","note":"step two"}]},{"text":"**Add, then cancel.** Anything on both sides, such as the enzyme's two flavin forms, drops out."},{"text":"**Check.** Count each element and the total charge on both sides."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalytic cycle's net equation is the sum of its steps with every regenerated species cancelled."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-30a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:30"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Which structure shows the hydroxamic acid formed by the second enzyme?","choices":["HC(=O)–N(OH)–R","HC(=O)–O–NH–R","HC(=O)–NH–R","HC(=O)–N(OCHO)–R"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the N–OH: a plain amide has no hydroxyl on the nitrogen, so it is not a hydroxamic acid."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The OH is formylated too, so no N–OH remains to grip the iron."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The formyl group sits on the oxygen here, making an ester, not an amide."}],"rule":"Hydroxamic acid = an amide whose nitrogen also carries an OH: R′–C(=O)–N(OH)–R.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydroxamic acid = C(=O) on N, and that N keeps its OH. Find the choice with C(=O)–N and N–OH together."},"exp":"Sentence 3 makes the hydroxylamine RNHOH, and sentence 4 puts the formyl group on **that same nitrogen**. The product is an **amide whose nitrogen keeps its OH**: **HC(=O)–N(OH)–R**, a hydroxamic acid whose C=O and N–OH oxygens bind Fe³⁺.","how":{"ask":"Put the acyl group on the hydroxylamine's nitrogen and keep the N–OH.","know":"• **Hydroxamic acid**: R′–C(=O)–N(OH)–R, an amide with an OH on the nitrogen.\n• **Amide**: C(=O)–N; **ester**: C(=O)–O.\n• Both oxygens (C=O and N–OH) chelate metal ions such as Fe³⁺.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the formyl group is attached to the same nitrogen that sentence 3 hydroxylated.","hl":"attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen","calc":["RNHOH + formyl on N → HC(=O)–N(OH)–R"]},{"text":"That is the hydroxamic acid pattern: an amide nitrogen that also carries OH."},{"text":"Acyl on O makes an ester; acyl on N with no OH is an ordinary amide; formylating the OH too removes the N–OH.","strike":["HC(=O)–O–NH–R","HC(=O)–NH–R","HC(=O)–N(OCHO)–R"]},{"text":"Answer: HC(=O)–N(OH)–R."}],"trap":"Putting the acyl group on the oxygen: the passage says it goes on the nitrogen, which keeps its OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"RNHOH","note":"hydroxylamine"},{"t":"to that same nitrogen","note":"N-acylation"},{"t":"grips Fe³⁺","note":"C=O and N–OH bind"}]},{"text":"**Name the pattern.**","calc":["amide: C(=O)–N","hydroxamic acid: C(=O)–N–OH"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a hydroxamic acid is an amide whose nitrogen also bears an OH, R′C(=O)N(OH)R."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-31a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:31"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Under the assay conditions, how many times faster is hydroxylation than the side reaction that releases H₂O₂?","choices":["0.17","5.5","6.0","7.3"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: that is the side reaction's rate divided by the hydroxylation rate."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Subtracting rate constants gives a difference in s⁻¹, not how many times faster."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Multiplying the two constants gives s⁻², not a ratio of rates."}],"rule":"With equal supply of the shared reactant, rate ratio = ratio of rate constants (rate = k × [shared reactant]).","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"6.6/1.1: 1.1 × 6 = 6.6 exactly."},"exp":"Sentence 7 says both reactions draw on the **same supply** of reduced enzyme, so each rate = k × [E–FMNH₂] and the concentration cancels. **Ratio = k(hydroxylation)/k(side)** = 6.6 s⁻¹ ÷ 1.1 s⁻¹ = **6.0**.","how":{"ask":"Divide the hydroxylation rate constant by the side-reaction rate constant.","know":"• **Rate = k × [reactant]**.\n• With the **same** reactant supply, the concentration cancels: **rate ratio = k ratio**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives both rate constants and says both reactions draw on the same reduced enzyme.","hl":"the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹","calc":["k₁ = 6.6 s⁻¹, k₂ = 1.1 s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Same supply means the concentration cancels in the ratio of rates.","calc":["rate₁/rate₂ = k₁[E]/k₂[E] = k₁/k₂"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["6.6 ÷ 1.1 = **6.0**"]},{"text":"The inverse ratio is upside down; a difference or a product is not a ratio of rates.","strike":["0.17","5.5","7.3"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.0."}],"trap":"Dividing the smaller constant by the larger: \"how many times faster\" puts the faster reaction on top."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"6.6 s⁻¹","note":"hydroxylation"},{"t":"1.1 s⁻¹","note":"side reaction"},{"t":"both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme","note":"concentration cancels"}]},{"text":"**Ratios cancel shared terms.**","calc":["rate₁/rate₂ = k₁/k₂ when [shared reactant] is equal"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when two reactions use the same reactant at the same concentration, their rates compare as their rate constants."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-33a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:33#1","pe3-cp:33#2","pe3-cp:33#3","pe3-cp:33#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:33"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Which pair of residues could hold the substrate's carboxylate in enzyme Z?","choices":["Arginine and histidine","Aspartate and glutamate","Leucine and valine","Serine and threonine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Aspartate and glutamate carry side-chain carboxylates: acidic, the opposite of basic, and they would repel the substrate's COO⁻."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Leu and Val side chains are only C and H: nonpolar, not hydrophilic."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ser and Thr side chains end in a neutral OH: polar but not basic."}],"rule":"Basic side chains carry extra N (Lys, Arg, His); acidic ones carry COO⁻ (Asp, Glu); side chains of only C and H are nonpolar; Ser/Thr are polar but neutral.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Basic side chains = the three that take H⁺: Lys (amine), Arg (guanidino), His (imidazole). Only one pair is drawn from those three."},"exp":"Sentence 5 says the two residues are **basic and hydrophilic**, as needed to pair with a COO⁻. **Arginine** (guanidino) and **histidine** (imidazole) have nitrogen side chains that take protons, as does lysine. Aspartate and glutamate are acidic; leucine and valine are nonpolar; serine and threonine are polar but neutral.","how":{"ask":"Sort each amino acid by its side chain: basic, acidic, polar neutral or nonpolar.","know":"• **Basic, hydrophilic**: Lys, Arg, His (N-containing side chains that take H⁺).\n• **Acidic**: Asp, Glu (side-chain COOH/COO⁻).\n• **Polar, neutral**: Ser, Thr (OH), Asn, Gln (amide).\n• **Nonpolar**: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe (hydrocarbon side chains).\n• Arg's guanidino side chain (pKa ≈ 12.5) stays protonated at ordinary pH, so free Arg is **+1** overall near pH 7–9.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the carboxylate is held by residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic.","hl":"two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic"},{"text":"Basic side chains contain nitrogen that takes H⁺: arginine's guanidino group and histidine's imidazole both qualify.","fig":"arginine"},{"text":"Aspartate and glutamate have side-chain carboxylates, so they are acidic.","strike":["Aspartate and glutamate"]},{"text":"Leucine and valine are hydrocarbons (nonpolar); serine and threonine carry a neutral OH (polar, not basic).","strike":["Leucine and valine","Serine and threonine"]},{"text":"Also check arginine's charge in the buffer: compare the pH with each pKa.","calc":["α-COOH (≈2): pH above → COO⁻, −1","α-NH₂ (≈9): pH just below → mostly NH₃⁺, +1","guanidino (≈12.5): pH below → protonated, +1","net ≈ **+1**"]},{"text":"Answer: arginine and histidine."}],"trap":"Calling histidine nonpolar because of its ring: its imidazole nitrogens take H⁺, so it is grouped with the basic amino acids."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"basic and hydrophilic","note":"N-containing side chains"},{"t":"the substrate's carboxylate","note":"negative: needs a + partner"}]},{"text":"**Sort by side chain.**","calc":["basic: Lys, Arg, His","acidic: Asp, Glu","polar neutral: Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln","nonpolar: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to pair with a negative group, look for the basic residues (Lys, Arg, His), whose nitrogen side chains carry + charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:34#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:34"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"What is [OH⁻] in the assay buffer?","choices":["2.0 × 10⁻⁹ M","5.0 × 10⁻⁶ M","5.3 × 10⁻⁶ M","8.7 × 10⁻⁶ M"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: 10^−8.7 is [H⁺]; hydroxide uses the pOH."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the antilog: 5.3 is the pOH itself, not a concentration's leading digits."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Copied the pH into the mantissa; [OH⁻] comes from 10^−pOH."}],"rule":"pOH = 14 − pH; [OH⁻] = 10^−pOH. Split the exponent: 10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"pOH = 5.3 → 10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶, and 10^0.7 ≈ 5 (log 5 ≈ 0.70)."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives **pH 8.7** and pH + pOH = 14, so **pOH = 5.3**. [OH⁻] = 10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ **5.0 × 10⁻⁶ M**. [H⁺] = 10^−8.7 ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻⁹ M is much smaller, as expected in a basic buffer.","how":{"ask":"Turn the pH into pOH, then into [OH⁻].","know":"• **pH + pOH = 14** at 25 °C.\n• **[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH**; **[H⁺] = 10^−pH**.\n• log 2 ≈ 0.30, log 5 ≈ 0.70.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the buffer's pH and the relation to pOH.","hl":"pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14","calc":["pOH = 14 − 8.7 = 5.3"]},{"text":"Convert pOH to concentration.","calc":["[OH⁻] = 10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ **5.0 × 10⁻⁶ M**"]},{"text":"10^−8.7 is [H⁺], not [OH⁻]; 5.3 and 8.7 are the pOH and pH themselves, not mantissas.","strike":["2.0 × 10⁻⁹ M","5.3 × 10⁻⁶ M","8.7 × 10⁻⁶ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ M."}],"trap":"Using 10^−pH for hydroxide: that gives [H⁺]."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 8.7","note":"basic buffer"},{"t":"pH + pOH = 14","note":"get pOH"}]},{"text":"**pH to pOH to [OH⁻].**","calc":["pOH = 14 − pH","[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH"]},{"text":"**Split fractional exponents.**","calc":["10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁶"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** [OH⁻] = 10^−(14 − pH); write the exponent as a whole number plus a fraction to estimate the mantissa."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-35a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:35#1","pe3-cp:36#1","pe3-cp:36#2","pe3-cp:36#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:35"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","q":"In the first experiment, what species does one catalytic step produce from 2-mercaptoethanol?","choices":["HOCH₂CH₂S•","HOCH₂CH₂SH•⁺","•OCH₂CH₂SH","HOCH₂CH•SH"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the proton: losing only an electron leaves a radical cation that still holds its H."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The enzyme cannot take hydrogen from an O–H bond, so no oxygen radical forms."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"C–H bonds are off-limits to this enzyme too, so no carbon radical forms."}],"rule":"Removing H• (H⁺ + e⁻) from X–H leaves a neutral radical X•; removing only H⁺ leaves an anion. The enzyme's allowed X–H sets which radical forms.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"H atom = H⁺ + e⁻, so the product is neutral with an unpaired electron on the atom that lost H. Only S–H is allowed here."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says each step takes **a whole hydrogen atom (H⁺ + e⁻)** off an **S–H**, and sentence 4 rules out O–H and C–H. So 2-mercaptoethanol loses the H on sulfur and keeps its electron count one short: a neutral **thiyl radical, HOCH₂CH₂S•**. Two of these then join into the S–S cross-link. Classic peroxidases do the same to phenols, making phenoxyl radicals.","how":{"ask":"Find which X–H the enzyme can break, then remove a whole hydrogen atom from it.","know":"• **H atom = H⁺ + e⁻**: removing it leaves a **neutral radical** (X•).\n• Removing **H⁺ only** leaves an **anion** (X⁻); removing **e⁻ only** leaves a radical cation.\n• Peroxidases usually take H• from **phenols** (Ar–OH → Ar–O•); among side chains that is **tyrosine**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says each step removes a hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group.","hl":"removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group","calc":["R–S–H − (H⁺ + e⁻) → R–S•"]},{"text":"That is H-atom abstraction, which leaves a neutral radical. Sentence 4 limits it to S–H.","hl":"cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds"},{"text":"Apply it to 2-mercaptoethanol: only the sulfur's H is taken.","calc":["HS–CH₂CH₂–OH → **HOCH₂CH₂S•** (thiyl radical)"]},{"text":"The radical cation lost only an electron; the oxygen and carbon radicals need O–H or C–H abstraction, which the enzyme cannot do.","strike":["HOCH₂CH₂SH•⁺","•OCH₂CH₂SH","HOCH₂CH•SH"]},{"text":"Answer: HOCH₂CH₂S•."}],"trap":"Choosing an oxygen or carbon radical because the molecule has O–H and C–H bonds: this enzyme can break only the S–H."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a proton plus an electron","note":"a whole H atom"},{"t":"from an S–H group","note":"where the radical forms"},{"t":"both an S–H and an O–H group","note":"only S–H reacts"}]},{"text":"**H atom vs proton.**","calc":["X–H − H⁺ → X⁻","X–H − H• → X•"]},{"text":"**Also:** a classic peroxidase takes H• from a phenol, Ar–OH → Ar–O•; among side chains only tyrosine is a phenol (lysine is an amine, leucine an alkyl, glutamine an amide).","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** two radicals pair their unpaired electrons to form a new bond: two R–S• make R–S–S–R; two phenoxyl radicals make a C–C or C–O link.","fig":["lysine-sc","leucine-sc","glutamine-sc"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an enzyme that removes H• (H⁺ + e⁻) leaves a neutral radical on the atom that held the H; check which X–H it is allowed to break."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-36a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:36#1","pe3-cp:36#2","pe3-cp:36#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:36"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","choiceFigs":["cysteine-sc","methionine-sc","serine-sc","lysine-sc"],"q":"Which amino-acid side chain could the enzyme act on?","choices":["Cysteine","Methionine","Serine","Lysine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Methionine's sulfur is a thioether, C–S–C, with no S–H to lose."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Serine's side chain ends in an O–H, an alcohol, which the enzyme cannot use."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine ends in an amine, with N–H bonds, not a thiol."}],"rule":"Match the group the enzyme needs to the side chain: thiol = Cys, thioether = Met, alcohol = Ser/Thr, phenol = Tyr, amine = Lys, amide = Asn/Gln.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only one standard amino acid has an S–H: find it."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says the enzyme needs an **S–H (thiol)**, and sentence 4 rules out O–H, N–H, C–H and any sulfur without an H. **Cysteine** is the only amino acid with a thiol side chain, –CH₂SH. Methionine has sulfur but as a **thioether** (no S–H); serine has an O–H; lysine has N–H bonds.","how":{"ask":"Identify the group the enzyme acts on, then find the side chain that carries it.","know":"• Side-chain groups: **Cys** thiol (–SH); **Met** thioether (–S–CH₃); **Ser/Thr** alcohol; **Tyr** phenol; **Lys** amine; **Asn/Gln** amide; **Leu** alkyl.\n• A **peroxidase** removes H from a **phenol**, so among amino acids it acts on **Tyr**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says each step takes a hydrogen atom from an S–H group, and sentence 4 rules out every other X–H.","hl":"removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group"},{"text":"Sentence 4 also excludes a sulfur with no hydrogen, so the side chain must be a thiol.","hl":"it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen"},{"text":"Scan each side chain: only cysteine carries –SH.","calc":["Cys: –CH₂–SH ✓"]},{"text":"Methionine's S has no H; serine is O–H; lysine is N–H.","strike":["Methionine","Serine","Lysine"]},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Choosing methionine because it contains sulfur: its sulfur is bonded to two carbons, with no S–H."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"from an S–H group","note":"thiol"},{"t":"does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen","note":"rules out a thioether"},{"t":"cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds","note":"rules out Ser, Lys"}]},{"text":"**Group → residue.**","calc":["S–H → Cys","C–S–C → Met","O–H → Ser","N–H → Lys"]},{"text":"**Also:** a peroxidase removes H from a phenol O–H, so the residue it attacks is tyrosine, not lysine (amine), leucine (alkyl) or glutamine (amide).","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the functional group the enzyme acts on, then pick the one side chain that carries it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-37a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:37#1","pe6-cp:42#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:37"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","fig":"asparagine-sc","q":"Which side-chain atom of the loop's asparagine is best placed to bind the Ca²⁺?","choices":["The C=O oxygen","The NH₂ nitrogen","The C=O carbon","The β-carbon"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"A CH₂ carbon has no lone pair to donate, so it cannot coordinate a metal ion."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The carbonyl carbon carries a partial positive charge and has no lone pair, so it repels a cation."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide N's lone pair is delocalized into the C=O, which makes the N a poor donor and the O electron-rich."}],"rule":"Ca²⁺ binds lone-pair oxygens: carboxylate O (Asp, Glu), carbonyl O (backbone, Asn, Gln) and water; not amide N, and never carbon.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ca²⁺ is a hard cation that favors oxygen donors. Pick the electron-rich O."},"exp":"Sentence 5 names the ligand: an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂. Ca²⁺ is held by **oxygen** lone pairs: carboxylate O of Asp/Glu and **carbonyl O** of the backbone or of Asn/Gln side chains. In the amide, the N lone pair is delocalized into the C=O, which makes the **carbonyl oxygen** electron-rich (δ−) and the best donor.","how":{"ask":"Find the atom with an available lone pair and a partial negative charge.","know":"• **Ca²⁺ binds oxygen**: carboxylate O (Asp, Glu), carbonyl O (backbone, Asn, Gln), water.\n• Amide N lone pairs are tied up in resonance with the C=O.\n• Carbon atoms have no lone pair to give a metal.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says an asparagine side chain is one of the Ca²⁺ ligands and gives its structure.","hl":"–CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂","calc":["–CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂: candidates are C, C, O, N"]},{"text":"A 2+ cation needs an atom with a lone pair and a partial negative charge."},{"text":"Carbons have no lone pair, and the C=O carbon is δ+.","strike":["The β-carbon","The C=O carbon"]},{"text":"Amide resonance pulls the N lone pair toward O, so the O is the electron-rich donor.","calc":["O=C–NH₂ ↔ ⁻O–C=NH₂⁺"],"strike":["The NH₂ nitrogen"]},{"text":"Answer: the C=O oxygen."}],"trap":"Picking the nitrogen because amines bind metals: an amide N is not basic; its lone pair is shared with the carbonyl."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein","note":"coordination"},{"t":"–CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂","note":"carboxamide"},{"t":"is one of the ligands","note":"donates a lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Oxygen donors.** Carbonyl and carboxylate oxygens point lone pairs at the metal.","fig":"asparagine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** aspartate and glutamate bind Ca²⁺ or Zn²⁺ through their carboxylate oxygens; lowering the pH protonates those groups and releases the metal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** metal cations bind lone-pair donors with partial negative charge, carboxylate and carbonyl oxygens first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-38a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:38#1","pe3-cp:38#2","pe3-cp:38#3","pe5-cp:14#2","pe5-cp:14#3","pe5-cp:14#1","pe5-cp:14#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:38"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","q":"Which ring is the building block of the enzyme's porphyrin?","choices":["Five-membered, one N","Five-membered, two N","Six-membered, one N","Six-membered, two N"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Five-membered with two N is imidazole, histidine's ring; it binds the heme iron but isn't part of the porphyrin."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Six-membered with one N is pyridine; a porphyrin is made of pyrroles, which are five-membered."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Six-membered with two N is a pyrimidine, a nucleobase ring, not a porphyrin unit."}],"rule":"A porphyrin is four pyrrole rings joined by CH bridges. Know the rings: pyrrole, imidazole, pyridine, pyrimidine.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Porphyrin = 4 pyrroles. Ring key: pyrrole 5/1 N, imidazole 5/2 N, pyridine 6/1 N, pyrimidine 6/2 N. So five-membered, one N."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says the porphyrin is four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each giving one N to the iron. Those rings are **pyrroles**, and a pyrrole is **five-membered with one N**. The five-membered ring with two N is imidazole (histidine's side chain, which binds the heme iron from below); six-membered with one N is pyridine, and with two N a pyrimidine.","how":{"ask":"Name the unit heme is made of, then describe that ring by its size and its count of nitrogens.","know":"• **Heme** = a porphyrin ring (four **pyrrole** units joined by =CH– bridges) with Fe²⁺ held by the four inward-facing N.\n• **Pyrrole**: 5 ring atoms, **1 N** (an N–H).\n• **Imidazole**: 5 ring atoms, **2 N**; the side chain of **His**, whose free N binds the heme Fe.\n• **Pyridine**: 6 ring atoms, 1 N. **Pyrimidine**: 6 ring atoms, 2 N.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the porphyrin is built from four identical rings, each pointing one nitrogen at the iron.","hl":"four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges"},{"text":"That unit is pyrrole. Count its ring atoms and its nitrogens.","fig":"pyrrole","calc":["ring atoms: 4 C + 1 N = **5**","nitrogens: **1** (N–H)"]},{"text":"Five-membered with two N is imidazole, histidine's ring. It is not part of the porphyrin; it is the residue whose free N lone pair binds the heme iron from below.","fig":["imidazole","t3-his-ring"],"strike":["Five-membered, two N"]},{"text":"Six-membered rings are pyridine (one N) and pyrimidine (two N); neither builds a porphyrin.","fig":"pyridine","strike":["Six-membered, one N","Six-membered, two N"]},{"text":"Answer: five-membered, one N (pyrrole)."}],"trap":"Picking imidazole because histidine is drawn next to heme: His only binds the iron; the ring itself is made of pyrroles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"held in a porphyrin","note":"the ring of heme"},{"t":"four identical aromatic rings","note":"the repeating unit"},{"t":"each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron","note":"one N per ring"}]},{"text":"**Heme = porphyrin + Fe.** The porphyrin is four pyrrole rings joined by one-carbon bridges, with their four N pointing at the central iron."},{"text":"**Tell the rings apart by size and N count.**","fig":["pyrrole","imidazole","pyridine"],"calc":["pyrrole: 5 atoms, 1 N","imidazole: 5 atoms, 2 N","pyridine: 6 atoms, 1 N"]},{"text":"**Also:** imidazole (two N) is the ring on histidine's R group; in heme proteins, His binds the heme iron through its N that carries a free lone pair.","fig":"t3-his-ring"},{"text":"**Also:** to name any ring, count its atoms first (5 or 6), then count the nitrogens (1 or 2)."},{"text":"**Also:** to write a ring's formula, give every ring atom its full set of bonds: pyrrole has four C–H and one N–H, so it is C₄H₅N; imidazole is C₃H₄N₂ and pyridine C₅H₅N."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heme's porphyrin is four pyrroles (5 atoms, 1 N); imidazole (5 atoms, 2 N) is His, which binds the iron; pyridine has 6 atoms and 1 N."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-39a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:39#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:39"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","q":"From the second experiment, what is the resistivity of the worm's body fluid?","choices":["2.5 Ω·cm","25 Ω·cm","4.0 × 10⁻² Ω·cm","2.0 × 10⁵ Ω·cm"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That is the conductivity with its units relabeled; resistivity is its reciprocal."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Copied the wrong row: that is the reciprocal of the tube wall's conductivity, not the fluid's."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Off by ten: 1 ÷ 0.04 is in the tens, not the ones."}],"rule":"Resistivity is the reciprocal of conductivity: ρ = 1/σ, and (Ω·cm)⁻¹ becomes Ω·cm.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"(Ω·cm)⁻¹ → Ω·cm means take 1/x: 1/(4.0 × 10⁻²) = 100/4."},"exp":"Sentence 7 gives the body fluid's **conductivity**, σ = 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹. **Resistivity is its reciprocal**: ρ = 1/σ = 1/(0.040 (Ω·cm)⁻¹) = **25 Ω·cm**. The tube wall, with far lower conductivity, has a far higher resistivity, 2.0 × 10⁵ Ω·cm.","how":{"ask":"Pick the body fluid's conductivity and take its reciprocal.","know":"• **ρ = 1/σ**: resistivity (Ω·cm) is the reciprocal of conductivity ((Ω·cm)⁻¹).\n• High conductivity ↔ low resistivity.\n• R = ρL/A for a sample of length L and area A.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the body fluid's conductivity, along with the tube wall's.","hl":"the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹","calc":["σ(fluid) = 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹"]},{"text":"Resistivity is the reciprocal of conductivity; the units flip too.","calc":["ρ = 1/σ = 1/0.040 = **25 Ω·cm**"]},{"text":"Keeping the conductivity's number is not a reciprocal; 2.0 × 10⁵ belongs to the tube wall; 2.5 slips a factor of ten.","strike":["4.0 × 10⁻² Ω·cm","2.0 × 10⁵ Ω·cm","2.5 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Answer: 25 Ω·cm."}],"trap":"Taking the reciprocal of the wrong medium's value: read which material the question names."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"σ of the fluid"},{"t":"the hardened tube wall","note":"the other medium"}]},{"text":"**Flip it.**","calc":["ρ = 1/σ; (Ω·cm)⁻¹ → Ω·cm"]},{"text":"**Also:** ionic fluids conduct well (low ρ); dry, cross-linked protein conducts poorly (high ρ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** resistivity is one over conductivity; choose the value for the material asked about, then invert it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-40a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:40#1","pe3-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:40"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"In the phage pathway, what classes are enzyme X and enzyme Y?","choices":["Oxidoreductase; lyase","Transferase; lyase","Oxidoreductase; isomerase","Lyase; oxidoreductase"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards order: the O₂-using oxidation comes first, then the group removal."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A transferase moves a group from a donor; enzyme X adds an O from O₂, which is oxidation."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing a carbon as formaldehyde changes the formula, so enzyme Y cannot be an isomerase."}],"rule":"Name each change: O₂ adds O or removes H → oxidoreductase; group removed without water or redox, often leaving a double bond or a small molecule → lyase; group moved from a donor → transferase.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"O₂ consumed → redox class. A carbon leaves as a small molecule with no water and no redox → lyase."},"fig":["thymine","hydroxymethyluracil","uracil"],"exp":"Sentence 3 describes both steps. **Enzyme X** uses **O₂** to turn CH₃ into CH₂OH: an oxidation, so an **oxidoreductase**. **Enzyme Y** cuts off that carbon as formaldehyde **with no water added and no redox change**: breaking a C–C bond that way is the work of a **lyase**.","how":{"ask":"Classify each step by what changes: redox, group transfer, hydrolysis, or removal without water.","know":"• **Oxidoreductase**: oxidation–reduction (O₂ used, O added, H removed).\n• **Transferase**: moves a group (methyl, phosphate) from a donor to the substrate.\n• **Hydrolase**: breaks a bond by adding water.\n• **Lyase**: breaks a bond without water or redox, releasing a group or forming a double bond.\n• **Isomerase**: same formula, atoms rearranged.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says enzyme X uses O₂ to turn thymine's methyl into CH₂OH.","hl":"enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH","calc":["–CH₃ + [O] → –CH₂OH: oxidation"]},{"text":"A reaction that consumes O₂ and adds an oxygen is redox, so enzyme X is an oxidoreductase."},{"text":"Sentence 3 also says enzyme Y releases the CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde with no water and no change in oxidation state.","hl":"with no water added and no change in oxidation state","calc":["5-hydroxymethyluracil → uracil + HCHO: lyase"]},{"text":"The order is fixed by the pathway; no donor group is moved; and losing a carbon changes the formula.","strike":["Lyase; oxidoreductase","Transferase; lyase","Oxidoreductase; isomerase"]},{"text":"Answer: oxidoreductase, then lyase."}],"trap":"Calling enzyme Y a hydrolase because a bond breaks: hydrolysis needs water, and the passage rules water out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"uses O₂ to oxidize","note":"oxidoreductase"},{"t":"releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde","note":"group removed"},{"t":"no water added and no change in oxidation state","note":"not hydrolase, not redox"}]},{"text":"**One question per step:** did electrons move, did a group move between molecules, did water split a bond, or did a group simply leave?"},{"text":"**Also:** adding a methyl group from a donor such as SAM, as when uracil becomes thymine, is a transferase step; oxidizing that methyl with O₂ is an oxidoreductase step.","fig":["uracil","thymine"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** O₂-dependent oxidation → oxidoreductase; group lost without water or redox → lyase; group moved from a donor → transferase; water splits a bond → hydrolase."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-41a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:41#1","pe3-cp:41#2","pe3-cp:41#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:41"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"For the unfolding of Strand Q (F ⇌ U) at pH 6.4 and 37 °C, which expression gives ΔG°′, and what is its value?","choices":["−R·T·ln([U]/[F]); zero","−R·T·ln([U]/[F]); positive","+R·T·ln([U]/[F]); negative","−R·T·e^([U]/[F]); zero"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"ΔG°′ uses the natural log of K, not e raised to K."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards sign: the relation is ΔG°′ = −RT ln K, and ln 1 gives no sign at all."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A positive ΔG°′ needs K < 1, fewer unfolded than folded; here the two are equal."}],"rule":"For F ⇌ U, K = [U]/[F] and ΔG°′ = −RT ln K; K > 1 → negative, K < 1 → positive, K = 1 (half unfolded) → zero.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Half unfolded means K = 1, and ln 1 = 0, so ΔG°′ = 0 whatever the temperature."},"exp":"Sentence 6 says that at pH 6.4 **exactly half** of Strand Q is unfolded, so **K = [U]/[F] = 1**. With **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K**, ln 1 = 0, so **ΔG°′ = 0**: at the transition midpoint neither form is favored.","how":{"ask":"Write K for unfolding from the passage's equilibrium data, then use ΔG°′ = −RT ln K.","know":"• For F ⇌ U: **K = [U]/[F]** (products over reactants).\n• **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K** (natural log).\n• K > 1 → ΔG°′ < 0; K < 1 → ΔG°′ > 0; **K = 1 → ΔG°′ = 0**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium under these conditions.","hl":"exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium","calc":["[U] = [F]"]},{"text":"Write K for the reaction as written, then apply the free-energy relation.","calc":["K = [U]/[F] = 1","ΔG°′ = −RT ln 1 = **0**"]},{"text":"e^K is not the relation; a positive sign or a negative sign would need K below or above 1.","strike":["−R·T·e^([U]/[F]); zero","−R·T·ln([U]/[F]); positive","+R·T·ln([U]/[F]); negative"]},{"text":"Answer: −R·T·ln([U]/[F]), equal to zero."}],"trap":"Assuming an unfolding ΔG°′ is always positive: its sign depends on K, and at the midpoint K = 1."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded","note":"K = 1"},{"t":"the pH at which half the molecules are folded","note":"the midpoint"}]},{"text":"**K from the data.**","calc":["K = [U]/[F] = fraction unfolded ÷ fraction folded"]},{"text":"**Sign from K.**","calc":["ln K > 0 → ΔG°′ < 0","ln K < 0 → ΔG°′ > 0","ln 1 = 0 → ΔG°′ = 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** below the midpoint pH most molecules are folded, K < 1, so ΔG°′ for unfolding is positive; above it, negative."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG°′ = −RT ln K with K = [U]/[F] for unfolding; at the transition midpoint K = 1 and ΔG°′ = 0."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-42a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:42#1","pe3-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:42"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"Which strand unfolds more cooperatively, and what is its pK?","choices":["Q; pK 6.4","Q2; pK 6.9","Q; pK 6.9","Q2; pK 6.4"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: Q2's transition spreads over more than two pH units, the mark of low cooperativity."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards again: the broad transition belongs to Q2, the less cooperative strand."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Right strand, but 6.9 is copied from Strand Q2's midpoint, not Strand Q's."}],"rule":"Narrow transition (steep curve) = cooperative; broad transition = less cooperative. Read the midpoint of the same curve for its pK.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Compare widths: 6.2–6.6 is 0.4 unit; 5.7–8.1 is 2.4 units. Narrower = more cooperative."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives the transition widths: Strand Q unfolds **between pH 6.2 and 6.6** (0.4 unit), Strand Q2 **between 5.7 and 8.1** (2.4 units). A **narrow, steep** transition means the molecule unfolds all at once: **cooperative**. Strand Q's own midpoint is **pK 6.4**.","how":{"ask":"Compare the widths of the two transitions, then read that strand's midpoint.","know":"• **Cooperative** unfolding: all-or-none, so the curve drops over a **narrow** range.\n• Splitting a structure into separate units makes unfolding **less cooperative**: a broad curve.\n• **pK** (or Tm) = the midpoint, where half are folded.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says Strand Q unfolds sharply over a narrow pH range and Strand Q2 gradually over a wide one.","hl":"Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6","calc":["Q: 6.6 − 6.2 = 0.4 unit","Q2: 8.1 − 5.7 = 2.4 units"]},{"text":"A narrow transition means cooperative unfolding; the extra loops in Q2 let its tracts unfold separately.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fraction folded of Strands Q and Q2 versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Strand Q","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,1],[6,0.99],[6.2,0.95],[6.4,0.5],[6.6,0.05],[7,0.01],[9,0]]},{"name":"Strand Q2","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.99],[5.7,0.95],[6.3,0.8],[6.9,0.5],[7.5,0.2],[8.1,0.05],[9,0.02]]}]}},{"text":"Read Strand Q's midpoint from sentence 5.","hl":"is 6.4 for Strand Q","calc":["Q: pK 6.4"]},{"text":"Q2 is the broad, less cooperative strand, and 6.9 is its midpoint, not Q's.","strike":["Q2; pK 6.9","Q2; pK 6.4","Q; pK 6.9"]},{"text":"Answer: Q; pK 6.4."}],"trap":"Calling the strand with the higher midpoint more cooperative: cooperativity is about the steepness of the curve, not where it sits."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"split into three short tracts by extra loops","note":"units unfold separately"},{"t":"unfolded sharply","note":"cooperative"},{"t":"unfolded gradually","note":"less cooperative"}]},{"text":"**Width = cooperativity.** Measure each curve from about 95% folded to about 5% folded."},{"text":"**Midpoint = pK.** Read it on the same curve you chose."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the steeper (narrower) unfolding transition is the more cooperative one; its midpoint is its pK or Tm."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-43a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:43#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:43"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"Compared with the original Strand Q at pH 5.5, the i-motif built from the analog is:","choices":["more stable; its N3 holds H⁺ more.","less stable; its N3 holds H⁺ more.","less stable; its N3 holds H⁺ less.","no different; only H-bonds matter."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"More protonated N3 means more C·C⁺ pairs, which hold the structure together; it cannot make it less stable."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A higher pKa means the N3 holds its H⁺ more tightly, so the analog is protonated more, not less."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The C·C⁺ pairs need a protonated N3, so how readily N3 takes up H⁺ directly sets stability."}],"rule":"Higher pKa = more protonated at a given pH. If a structure needs the protonated form, raising the pKa stabilizes it and lowering it destabilizes it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Compare pH with each pKa: at pH 5.5 the analog (pKa 5.1) is about 28% protonated, normal C (4.4) only about 7%. More C⁺, more pairs."},"exp":"Sentence 1 says the i-motif needs **protonated N3**, and sentence 7 gives the analog the **higher pKa**. A higher pKa keeps N3 protonated at higher pH: at pH 5.5 about 28% of analog N3 is protonated versus about 7% for normal cytosine. More C·C⁺ pairs form, so the analog i-motif is **more stable**.","how":{"ask":"Use each pKa to judge how much protonated cytosine exists at pH 5.5, then link that to stability.","know":"• At pH < pKa a site is mostly protonated; at pH > pKa mostly not.\n• Fraction protonated = 1/(1 + 10^(pH − pKa)).\n• **Higher pKa → more protonated** at a given pH; lower pKa → less.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3 to hold the i-motif together.","hl":"must be protonated at N3","calc":["stability needs C⁺"]},{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the two pKa values; compare them.","hl":"whose N3 pKa is 5.1","calc":["analog 5.1 > normal 4.4"]},{"text":"At pH 5.5, find the protonated fraction of each.","calc":["analog: 1/(1 + 10^0.4) ≈ 0.28","normal: 1/(1 + 10^1.1) ≈ 0.07"]},{"text":"More protonated N3 → more C·C⁺ pairs → more stable.","strike":["less stable; its N3 holds H⁺ more.","less stable; its N3 holds H⁺ less."]},{"text":"Stability depends on that protonation, so it cannot be unchanged.","strike":["no different; only H-bonds matter."]},{"text":"Answer: more stable."}],"trap":"Thinking a higher pKa means a stronger acid that gives up its H⁺: a higher pKa is a weaker acid, so it keeps its H⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"must be protonated at N3","note":"needs C⁺"},{"t":"N3 pKa of 4.4","note":"normal"},{"t":"N3 pKa is 5.1","note":"analog holds H⁺ more"},{"t":"at pH 5.5","note":"above both pKa values"}]},{"text":"**pKa vs pH.** The closer the pKa sits to (or above) the pH, the larger the protonated share."},{"text":"**Also:** a modification that lowers the pKa has the reverse effect: fewer C⁺, a less stable structure."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** if a structure needs a protonated base, raising that base's pKa stabilizes it and lowering the pKa destabilizes it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-44a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:44#1","pe3-cp:44#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:44"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"When Strand Q is copied into a complementary strand, which nucleoside is absent from the new strand, and what percentage of it is deoxyguanosine?","choices":["Deoxythymidine; 46%","Deoxyadenosine; 46%","Deoxythymidine; 21%","Deoxyguanosine; 33%"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Every C in the template pairs with a G in the copy, so the new strand cannot lack G."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the template has T, so the copy has A; the missing template base is A, so T is what's absent."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Copied the template's own G percentage; the new strand's G pairs with the template's C."}],"rule":"Complementary strand: each base's share equals its partner's share in the template (A↔T, G↔C); a base missing from the template means its partner is missing from the copy.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Swap partners: template A 0 → copy T 0; template C → copy G. Read both from sentence 2."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives Strand Q as **46% C, 33% T, 21% G and no A**. In the copy, each base pairs with its partner: template C → **dG (46%)**, T → dA (33%), G → dC (21%), and since the template has **no A**, the copy has **no dT**.","how":{"ask":"Pair each template base with its partner, then read off the missing nucleoside and the share of G.","know":"• Base pairing: **A ↔ T**, **G ↔ C**.\n• In the copy, each base's % = its partner's % in the template.\n• A base absent from the template → its **partner is absent** from the copy.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives Strand Q's base composition and says it has no A.","hl":"Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A","calc":["template: C 46, T 33, G 21, A 0"]},{"text":"Pair each base to build the complementary strand.","calc":["C 46 → **dG 46%**","T 33 → dA 33%","G 21 → dC 21%","A 0 → **dT 0%**"]},{"text":"The copy has dA (from the template's T) and dG (from its C); 21% is the template's G, which becomes dC in the copy.","strike":["Deoxyadenosine; 46%","Deoxyguanosine; 33%","Deoxythymidine; 21%"]},{"text":"Answer: deoxythymidine is absent, and the strand is 46% deoxyguanosine."}],"trap":"Reporting the base missing from the template itself: the copy is missing that base's partner."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"46% C, 33% T and 21% G","note":"template composition"},{"t":"with no A","note":"copy has no T"}]},{"text":"**Partner swap.**","calc":["template A → copy T","template T → copy A","template G → copy C","template C → copy G"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a complementary strand's base percentages are the template's partner percentages; a base missing from the template removes its partner from the copy."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-45a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:45#1","pe3-cp:45#2","pe3-cp:45#3","pe5-cp:35#1","pe5-cp:35#2","pe6-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:45"],"q":"Proteins with pI 4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4 are loaded onto a cation-exchange column (negative beads). At which pH will exactly three bind?","choices":["pH 6.8","pH 7.8","pH 7.3","pH 9.8"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"At this pH three proteins are negative and only two are positive; negative beads hold the positive ones."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Above every pI all five proteins are negative, so none sticks to negative beads."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"A pI copied as the buffer pH: that protein is neutral at its own pI, so only two proteins carry a positive charge."}],"rule":"A protein is positive when the buffer pH is below its pI. For a cation exchanger, pick the pH that sits below exactly the needed number of pIs.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count the pIs above each candidate pH: pH 6.8 sits below 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4, so three proteins are positive and bind."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Net charge versus pH for the five proteins","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":11,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Net charge","min":-10,"max":10,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Protein A (pI 4.6)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,6.4],[4,2.9],[4.6,0],[5,-2],[6,-5.9],[6.8,-7.6],[7,-7.8],[8,-8.6],[9,-8.9],[10,-9],[11,-9]]},{"name":"Protein B (pI 5.9)","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[3,5.5],[4,4.7],[5,2.8],[5.9,0],[6,-0.3],[6.8,-2.8],[7,-3.3],[8,-4.9],[9,-5.6],[10,-5.9],[11,-6]]},{"name":"Protein C (pI 7.3)","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,8],[4,7.9],[5,7.6],[6,6.2],[6.8,3],[7,1.9],[7.3,0],[8,-4.1],[9,-7],[10,-7.8],[11,-8]]},{"name":"Protein D (pI 8.2)","marker":"circle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[3,6],[4,6],[5,5.9],[6,5.7],[6.8,4.8],[7,4.5],[8,1],[8.2,0],[9,-3.4],[10,-5.4],[11,-5.9]]},{"name":"Protein E (pI 9.4)","marker":"diamond","line":"smooth","points":[[3,7],[4,7],[5,6.9],[6,6.7],[6.8,6.3],[7,6.1],[8,4.6],[9,1.5],[9.4,0],[10,-2.3],[11,-5]]}]},"exp":"Below its pI a protein is **positive** and binds the **negative beads** of a cation exchanger. At **pH 6.8**, only the pI 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4 proteins are below their pI, so exactly **three** bind; the pI 4.6 and 5.9 proteins are negative and flow through.","how":{"ask":"For each candidate pH, count the proteins whose pI lies above it (the positive ones), and find the pH that gives three.","know":"• **pH < pI → net positive**; **pH > pI → net negative**; pH = pI → no net charge.\n• **Cation exchanger** = negative beads → binds **positive** proteins.\n• **Anion exchanger** = positive beads → binds **negative** proteins.","steps":[{"text":"Read the pIs.","hl":"4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4"},{"text":"Count positive proteins (pI above the buffer pH) at each candidate pH.","calc":["pH 6.8: pI 7.3, 8.2, 9.4 → **3**","pH 7.3: 8.2, 9.4 → 2 (the pI 7.3 protein is neutral)","pH 7.8: 8.2, 9.4 → 2","pH 9.8: none → 0"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Protein A (pI 4.6)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein B (pI 5.9)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein C (pI 7.3)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein D (pI 8.2)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein E (pI 9.4)","x":6.8}]}},{"text":"At pH 7.8 three proteins are negative, not positive; at pH 9.8 all five are negative; at pH 7.3 one protein is neutral, leaving two.","strike":["pH 7.8","pH 9.8","pH 7.3"]},{"text":"Answer: pH 6.8."}],"trap":"Counting the negative proteins: a cation exchanger has negative beads, so it holds the proteins that are positive, those with pI above the buffer pH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4","note":"the pIs"},{"t":"cation-exchange column","note":"negative beads"},{"t":"exactly three bind","note":"three positive proteins"}]},{"text":"**Charge from pI.** Below its pI a protein holds extra H⁺ and is positive; above its pI it is negative.","calc":["pH < pI → +","pH > pI → −"]},{"text":"**Also:** a cation exchanger has negative beads, so it binds the positive proteins: at pH 6.8 those with pI 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4, three of them."},{"text":"**Also:** an anion exchanger has positive beads and binds the negative proteins: at pH 6.8 those with pI 4.6 and 5.9, two of them."},{"text":"**Also:** two forms of one protein with almost the same mass but different net charge (say −3 and −5 at the buffer pH) cannot be split by size, so use ion exchange. Both are negative, so an anion exchanger holds both, and the −5 form clings tighter: it stays on the column longer and comes off only at a higher salt concentration.","calc":["same size → size exclusion fails","−3 form: released first, at lower salt","−5 form: released later, at higher salt"]},{"text":"**Also:** in electrophoresis the same sign rule sets the direction. When the buffer pH equals the pI, the charges cancel and the protein stays in its well; below its pI it is positive and drifts to the negative electrode (cathode); above its pI it is negative and drifts to the positive electrode (anode).","calc":["pH 6.8, pI 4.6: negative → toward the anode (+)","pH 6.8, pI 9.4: positive → toward the cathode (−)","a protein with pI 6.8 would not migrate"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** proteins are positive below their pI and negative above it; a cation exchanger binds the positive ones, an anion exchanger the negative ones."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-46a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:46"],"q":"Cellulose beads, whose repeating unit is shown, are used to separate peptides by size. A few small peptides come off later than their size predicts. What weak interaction with the beads most likely explains this?","choices":["H-bonds to ring hydroxyls","Salt bridges to carboxylates","Aromatic π–π contacts","Hydrophobic contact with CH₂ chains"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"The repeating unit has no carboxylate or other charged group, so there is nothing to form an ion pair with."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"The rings drawn are saturated sugar rings, not aromatic, so there are no π systems to stack on."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Only short CH and CH₂ pieces sit between polar OH groups; there is no long nonpolar chain."}],"rule":"Read the polymer's functional groups first: sugar resins are covered in OH groups, so peptides interact with them by hydrogen bonding.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Sugar polymer = –OH everywhere = hydrogen bonding. No charge, no aromatic ring, no long alkyl chain."},"fig":"p1-cellobiose","exp":"Cellulose is a chain of **glucose rings** covered in **–OH groups**, with no charges, aromatic rings or long alkyl chains. Polar peptide groups (N–H, C=O, side-chain OH) can form **hydrogen bonds** with those hydroxyls, which slows them slightly on the column.","how":{"ask":"Identify the functional groups on the resin, then match the interaction they allow.","know":"• **Polysaccharides** (cellulose, dextran, agarose) are covered in **–OH**, so they hydrogen-bond.\n• Ionic attraction needs **charged groups**; π-stacking needs **aromatic rings**; hydrophobic contact needs **long nonpolar chains**.\n• Polyacrylamide resins offer **amide** groups, which also hydrogen-bond.","steps":[{"text":"Scan the repeating unit: glucose rings joined by an O bridge, with OH on almost every carbon.","hl":"whose repeating unit is shown"},{"text":"No ionized group, no aromatic ring, no long CH₂ chain.","strike":["Salt bridges to carboxylates","Aromatic π–π contacts","Hydrophobic contact with CH₂ chains"]},{"text":"The OH groups both give and accept H-bonds with peptide N–H and C=O groups.","calc":["resin O–H ··· O=C (peptide)"]},{"text":"Answer: H-bonds to ring hydroxyls."}],"trap":"Calling the saturated sugar rings aromatic: π-stacking needs flat, conjugated rings such as phenylalanine's."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Cellulose beads","note":"a polysaccharide"},{"t":"later than their size predicts","note":"weak attraction"}]},{"text":"**Groups decide interactions.**","calc":["–OH → hydrogen bonding","–COO⁻ → ionic","aryl ring → π-stacking","long CH₂ chain → hydrophobic"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** list the resin's functional groups, then name the only interaction they can make."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-47a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:47#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:47"],"q":"Of four nonmetals in groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4, which needs the most energy to lose its first electron, and why?","choices":["Sulfur; it is the smallest atom","Chlorine; its electrons sit closest","Selenium; it has more protons than S","Bromine; it has the most protons"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Atoms shrink across a period toward group 17, so sulfur is larger than its period-3 neighbor, not smaller."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Selenium's extra protons are outweighed by an extra shell: down a group, ionization energy falls."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Its extra protons come with another shell that shields them; bromine sits below its period-3 group-mate, so its IE is lower."}],"rule":"First ionization energy rises up a group and to the right across a period: pick the element highest and farthest right.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ionization energy climbs toward the upper right of the table. Of S, Cl, Se, Br the upper-right one is Cl (group 17, period 3)."},"exp":"First ionization energy **rises across a period** (more protons, same shell) and **rises up a group** (fewer shells, so the outer electrons sit nearer the protons). The group 17, period 3 atom, chlorine, is the smallest of the four and holds its electrons most tightly.","how":{"ask":"Place the four atoms on the table and apply both ionization-energy trends.","know":"• First ionization energy **increases left → right** across a period and **increases bottom → top** in a group.\n• Smaller atom → outer electrons nearer the protons → harder to remove.\n• Among halogens, **F** (smallest) is the hardest to ionize.","steps":[{"text":"Place the atoms: period 3 holds S (16) and Cl (17); period 4 holds Se (16) and Br (17).","hl":"groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4"},{"text":"Across a period, group 17 beats group 16, and the group 16 atoms are the larger ones: S and Se lose.","strike":["Sulfur; it is the smallest atom","Selenium; it has more protons than S"]},{"text":"Up a group, period 3 beats period 4: Br has the most protons but one more shell, so it loses.","strike":["Bromine; it has the most protons"]},{"text":"**Also:** extend the trend up group 17: F > Cl > Br > I, so fluorine, the smallest halogen, is the hardest of them all to ionize."},{"text":"Answer: chlorine, because its valence electrons sit closest to the nucleus."}],"trap":"Thinking a heavier atom with more protons holds its electrons more tightly: extra shells put them farther away and shield them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"lose its first electron","note":"first ionization energy"},{"t":"groups 16 and 17","note":"across a period"},{"t":"periods 3 and 4","note":"up and down a group"}]},{"text":"**Across a period:** more protons pull on the same shell, so ionization energy rises toward group 17 (and 18)."},{"text":"**Up a group:** fewer shells, a smaller atom, less shielding, so ionization energy rises toward the top."},{"text":"**Also:** of the halogens, fluorine is the hardest atom to ionize, because it is the smallest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** first ionization energy rises up a group and across a period; the smallest atom, top right, holds its electrons tightest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-48a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:48#1","pe3-cp:48#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:48"],"q":"A toy sled, released from rest, glides down a ramp with steady acceleration; the graph plots its position x against time. Which relationship fits the data, and what is the sled's acceleration?","choices":["x ∝ t²; a = 3 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 1.5 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 6 m/s²","x ∝ t; constant speed"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That reads x/t² as a; from rest x = ½at², so a = 2x/t², double that ratio."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"6 is the position reading at the 2 s mark, in meters, not an acceleration."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Equal time steps cover growing distances, so the graph curves upward; a straight line would mean no acceleration."}],"rule":"From rest at constant a, x = ½at² (a parabola); read one point and use a = 2x/t². A straight x–t line means constant velocity.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Pick a clean point: x = 6 m at t = 2 s. a = 2x/t² = 12/4."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Position of the sled","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position x (m)","min":0,"max":24,"step":6},"series":[{"name":"Sled","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,1.5],[2,6],[3,13.5],[4,24]]}]},"exp":"From rest with constant acceleration, **x = ½at²**, so x grows with **t²** and the curve bends upward. At t = 2 s, x = 6 m, so a = 2x/t² = 2(6)/2² = **3 m/s²**. Check: at t = 4 s, x = ½(3)(16) = 24 m.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the curve is a line or a parabola, then read a point and solve x = ½at² for a.","know":"• From rest, constant a: **x = ½at²** → x ∝ t² (parabola).\n• Constant velocity: x = vt → x ∝ t (straight line).\n• Solve for a: **a = 2x/t²**.","steps":[{"text":"The curve bends upward: the distance covered each second keeps growing.","calc":["0 → 1.5 → 6 → 13.5 → 24 m"],"strike":["x ∝ t; constant speed"]},{"text":"Read a point and solve x = ½at² for a.","calc":["x = 6 m at t = 2 s","a = 2x/t² = 2(6)/4 = **3 m/s²**"]},{"text":"1.5 m/s² drops the ½ (a = x/t²); 6 m/s² is the position reading itself.","strike":["x ∝ t²; a = 1.5 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 6 m/s²"]},{"text":"Answer: x ∝ t²; a = 3 m/s²."}],"trap":"Forgetting the ½ in x = ½at², which halves the acceleration."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"released from rest","note":"v₀ = 0"},{"t":"steady acceleration","note":"x = ½at²"}]},{"text":"**Shape first.** A parabola that bends upward means constant acceleration; a straight line means constant velocity."},{"text":"**Also:** for the same motion, the velocity–time graph is a straight line through the origin (v = at), while the position–time graph is the parabola."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** from rest, x = ½at², so x ∝ t² and a = 2x/t²."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-49a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:49#1","pe3-cp:51#1","pe5-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:49"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P9","passage":"A sonographer studies blood flow in the kidney with a probe whose crystal vibrates at 6.4 MHz. Each pulse passes from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s, into a layer of fat, where it travels at 1450 m/s, before reaching the kidney. The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second. In one image, an arteriole segment 150 µm long has a 6.0 × 10³ µm² cross section, and blood moves through it at an average speed of 3.5 mm/s. Downstream, the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section, so their combined area is larger than the arteriole's. Blood is treated as an incompressible fluid in steady flow.","q":"Once a pulse passes from the muscle into the fat, how do its frequency and wavelength change?","choices":["Same f; λ shrinks","Same f; λ grows","Lower f; λ unchanged","Higher f; λ unchanged"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: sound is slower in fat, and λ = v/f, so the wavelength gets shorter, not longer."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The source sets the frequency, and it does not change when a wave enters a new medium."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Frequency stays fixed at the boundary; the change in speed shows up in the wavelength."}],"rule":"Across a boundary, frequency stays fixed (set by the source); speed changes with the medium, so λ = v/f changes with it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"f is locked to the source. Slower medium → shorter λ, faster medium → longer λ."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says the **crystal alone sets** how many crests leave each second, so the **frequency stays 6.4 MHz** in every tissue. Sentence 2 says sound is **slower in fat** (1450 m/s) than in muscle (1600 m/s); since **λ = v/f**, the wavelength **shrinks** in the fat.","how":{"ask":"Decide which wave property the source fixes, then use λ = v/f for the one that changes.","know":"• The source fixes the **frequency**; it does not change between media.\n• **Speed** depends on the medium.\n• **λ = v/f**: with f fixed, λ follows v.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the crystal alone sets the number of crests per second: the frequency is fixed.","hl":"The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second","calc":["f = 6.4 MHz in muscle and in fat"]},{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the speeds: the pulse slows when it enters fat.","hl":"where it travels at 1450 m/s","calc":["v: 1600 → 1450 m/s"]},{"text":"With f fixed, λ = v/f falls with v.","calc":["λ(muscle) = 1600/6.4 × 10⁶ = 0.25 mm","λ(fat) = 1450/6.4 × 10⁶ ≈ 0.23 mm → **shrinks**"]},{"text":"The frequency cannot rise or fall at the boundary, and a slower wave has a shorter λ, not a longer one.","strike":["Lower f; λ unchanged","Higher f; λ unchanged","Same f; λ grows"]},{"text":"Answer: same f; λ shrinks."}],"trap":"Letting the frequency change at the boundary: the boundary cannot add or remove crests, so f stays and λ adjusts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second","note":"f fixed"},{"t":"1600 m/s","note":"muscle"},{"t":"1450 m/s","note":"fat: slower"}]},{"text":"**What stays, what changes.** Frequency stays; speed and wavelength change together."},{"text":"**Direction.**","calc":["slower medium → shorter λ","faster medium → longer λ"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a wave crosses into a new medium, its frequency stays the same and its wavelength scales with its speed."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-50a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:50#1","pe3-cp:50#2","pe3-cp:50#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:50"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P9","passage":"A sonographer studies blood flow in the kidney with a probe whose crystal vibrates at 6.4 MHz. Each pulse passes from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s, into a layer of fat, where it travels at 1450 m/s, before reaching the kidney. The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second. In one image, an arteriole segment 150 µm long has a 6.0 × 10³ µm² cross section, and blood moves through it at an average speed of 3.5 mm/s. Downstream, the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section, so their combined area is larger than the arteriole's. Blood is treated as an incompressible fluid in steady flow.","q":"What volume of blood flows through the arteriole segment each second?","choices":["2.1 × 10⁴ µm³/s","2.1 × 10⁷ µm³/s","3.2 × 10⁹ µm³/s","5.8 × 10⁻¹ µm³/s"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a thousand: mm/s must become µm/s (× 10³) before multiplying by an area in µm²."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divided speed by area instead of multiplying them; flow rate is area times speed."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Copied in the segment's length, which does not enter the volume flow rate."}],"rule":"Volume flow rate Q = A × v, with A and v in matching length units; the length of the tube does not enter.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"µm² × µm/s = µm³/s. Convert 3.5 mm/s to 3.5 × 10³ µm/s, then multiply by 6.0 × 10³ µm²."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives the area, **6.0 × 10³ µm²**, and the speed, **3.5 mm/s = 3.5 × 10³ µm/s**. **Q = Av** = (6.0 × 10³ µm²)(3.5 × 10³ µm/s) = **2.1 × 10⁷ µm³/s**. The 150 µm length plays no part.","how":{"ask":"Multiply the cross-sectional area by the average speed, in matching units.","know":"• **Q = A × v** (volume per time).\n• Match units: 1 mm = 10³ µm.\n• A tube's length does not affect Q.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the arteriole's cross-sectional area and the blood's average speed.","hl":"a 6.0 × 10³ µm² cross section","calc":["A = 6.0 × 10³ µm²","v = 3.5 mm/s"]},{"text":"Flow rate is area times speed; first convert the speed to µm/s.","calc":["v = 3.5 × 10³ µm/s"]},{"text":"Multiply.","calc":["Q = 6.0 × 10³ × 3.5 × 10³ = **2.1 × 10⁷ µm³/s**"]},{"text":"Skipping the mm → µm step loses a factor of 10³; dividing v by A inverts the relation; multiplying by the length adds a quantity that doesn't belong.","strike":["2.1 × 10⁴ µm³/s","5.8 × 10⁻¹ µm³/s","3.2 × 10⁹ µm³/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.1 × 10⁷ µm³/s."}],"trap":"Multiplying in the tube's length: Q = Av needs only the area and the speed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"6.0 × 10³ µm²","note":"A"},{"t":"3.5 mm/s","note":"v, convert to µm/s"},{"t":"150 µm long","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"**Flow rate.**","calc":["Q = A·v"]},{"text":"**Also:** if a diameter is given instead, find the area first: A = πr² = πd²/4."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** volume flow rate is cross-sectional area times average speed, in matching units; length doesn't enter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-51a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:51#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:51"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P9","passage":"A sonographer studies blood flow in the kidney with a probe whose crystal vibrates at 6.4 MHz. Each pulse passes from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s, into a layer of fat, where it travels at 1450 m/s, before reaching the kidney. The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second. In one image, an arteriole segment 150 µm long has a 6.0 × 10³ µm² cross section, and blood moves through it at an average speed of 3.5 mm/s. Downstream, the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section, so their combined area is larger than the arteriole's. Blood is treated as an incompressible fluid in steady flow.","q":"In the muscle, how long is one wavelength of the ultrasound?","choices":["0.25 mm","25 mm","250 m","1.0 × 10¹⁰ m"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Used 6.4 Hz instead of 6.4 × 10⁶ Hz: MHz means a million cycles per second."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplied v by f; the wavelength is v divided by f."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Off by a factor of 100: 1600 ÷ 6.4 × 10⁶ gives a fraction of a millimeter."}],"rule":"λ = v/f, with f in hertz (1 MHz = 10⁶ Hz); use the speed in the medium asked about.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"m/s ÷ MHz = µm: 1600 ÷ 6.4 = 250 µm."},"exp":"Sentence 1 gives **f = 6.4 MHz** and sentence 2 gives **v = 1600 m/s** in muscle. **λ = v/f** = 1600 m/s ÷ 6.4 × 10⁶ Hz = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.25 mm**.","how":{"ask":"Divide the speed in muscle by the probe's frequency, in hertz.","know":"• **v = fλ**, so **λ = v/f**.\n• 1 MHz = 10⁶ Hz; 1 mm = 10⁻³ m.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the speed of sound in muscle, and sentence 1 the frequency.","hl":"from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s","calc":["v = 1600 m/s, f = 6.4 × 10⁶ Hz"]},{"text":"Wave speed equals frequency times wavelength, so λ = v/f.","calc":["λ = 1600 ÷ (6.4 × 10⁶) = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.25 mm**"]},{"text":"Leaving out 10⁶ gives meters by the hundred; v × f is not a length; 25 mm slips two powers of ten.","strike":["250 m","1.0 × 10¹⁰ m","25 mm"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.25 mm."}],"trap":"Dropping the mega- prefix: 6.4 MHz is 6.4 million hertz."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"vibrates at 6.4 MHz","note":"f"},{"t":"where sound travels at 1600 m/s","note":"v in muscle"}]},{"text":"**Rearrange.**","calc":["v = fλ → λ = v/f"]},{"text":"**Also:** use the speed for the tissue the question names; in fat (1450 m/s) the same probe gives a shorter wavelength."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** wavelength = speed in that medium ÷ frequency in hertz."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-52a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:52#1","pe3-cp:52#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:52"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P9","passage":"A sonographer studies blood flow in the kidney with a probe whose crystal vibrates at 6.4 MHz. Each pulse passes from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s, into a layer of fat, where it travels at 1450 m/s, before reaching the kidney. The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second. In one image, an arteriole segment 150 µm long has a 6.0 × 10³ µm² cross section, and blood moves through it at an average speed of 3.5 mm/s. Downstream, the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section, so their combined area is larger than the arteriole's. Blood is treated as an incompressible fluid in steady flow.","q":"What is the average blood speed in each capillary?","choices":["0.0089 mm/s","2.5 mm/s","4.9 mm/s","700 mm/s"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: a larger total area means a lower speed, not a higher one."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the number of capillaries: the arteriole's flow is shared among all of them."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Divided by the capillary count twice; continuity uses the combined area once."}],"rule":"Continuity: A₁v₁ = (N·a)·v₂, so v₂ = A₁v₁/(N·a). A larger total area means a lower speed.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Speed scales inversely with total area: 6.0 × 10³ ÷ 8.4 × 10³ ≈ 0.71, so the speed falls to about 0.71 × 3.5 mm/s."},"exp":"Sentence 6 makes blood **incompressible**, so the flow into the capillaries equals the flow in the arteriole: **A·v = N·a·u**. Sentence 5 gives N·a = 280 × 30 µm² = 8.4 × 10³ µm². u = (6.0 × 10³)(3.5 mm/s) ÷ (8.4 × 10³) = **2.5 mm/s**, slower than in the arteriole because the total area is larger.","how":{"ask":"Set the arteriole's flow equal to the combined capillary flow, then solve for the speed.","know":"• **Continuity** (incompressible flow): A₁v₁ = A₂v₂.\n• For N identical branches: A₂ = **N·a**.\n• Larger total area → lower speed.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the number of capillaries and each one's area, and says their combined area is larger.","hl":"the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section","calc":["N·a = 280 × 30 = 8.4 × 10³ µm²"]},{"text":"Incompressible flow means the volume per second is the same before and after the branch.","calc":["A·v = N·a·u"]},{"text":"Solve for u, using the arteriole's values from sentence 4.","calc":["u = (6.0 × 10³ × 3.5) ÷ 8.4 × 10³ = **2.5 mm/s**"]},{"text":"Using one capillary's area ignores the sharing; a speed above the arteriole's has the area ratio upside down; dividing by N again double-counts.","strike":["700 mm/s","4.9 mm/s","0.0089 mm/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.5 mm/s."}],"trap":"Thinking narrow capillaries make blood faster: each is narrow, but together they have more area, so the blood slows."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"280 identical capillaries","note":"N"},{"t":"30 µm²","note":"a, each"},{"t":"their combined area is larger than the arteriole's","note":"speed drops"},{"t":"incompressible fluid in steady flow","note":"continuity"}]},{"text":"**Continuity with branches.**","calc":["A·v = N·a·u → u = Av/(Na)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at a branch point the flow is conserved, so the speed in each branch is the parent flow divided by the total branch area."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-53a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:53#1","pe3-cp:53#2","pe3-cp:53#3","pe4-cp:2#1","pe4-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:53"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P10","passage":"A laboratory treats its tap water before feeding it to a small boiler. The scale that builds up in the boiler is mainly magnesium fluoride, MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq); in water saturated with it, [Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M. The most abundant dissolved metal ion in the feed water comes from a metal whose first three ionization energies are 420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol. A cation-exchange resin in its acid form removes that ion: M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq), where R is an anionic site fixed to the resin beads. At 28 °C, where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol (use e ≈ 2.7). When the resin is spent, it is restored by rinsing it with a concentrated solution that drives the exchange back to the left.","q":"What are the Ksp expression and value for the boiler scale?","choices":["[Mg²⁺][F⁻]² = 3.7 × 10⁻¹¹","[Mg²⁺][F⁻] = 8.8 × 10⁻⁸","[Mg²⁺]²[F⁻]² = 7.8 × 10⁻¹⁵","[Mg²⁺] + [F⁻] = 6.3 × 10⁻⁴"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"An equilibrium constant multiplies concentrations; it never adds them."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped the exponent: the coefficient 2 on F⁻ makes [F⁻] squared."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Only F⁻ has a coefficient of 2; Mg²⁺ has 1, so [Mg²⁺] is not squared."}],"rule":"Ksp = product of the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient; the solid is left out.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"MgF₂ → 1 Mg²⁺ + 2 F⁻, so Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻]². Estimate: 2 × (4 × 10⁻⁴)² × 10⁻⁴ ≈ 3 × 10⁻¹¹."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives the equilibrium, **MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺ + 2F⁻**, and the saturated concentrations. Leave out the solid and raise every ion's concentration to its coefficient: **Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻]²** = (2.1 × 10⁻⁴)(4.2 × 10⁻⁴)² = (2.1 × 10⁻⁴)(1.76 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ **3.7 × 10⁻¹¹**.","how":{"ask":"Write Ksp from the dissolution equation, then plug in the saturated concentrations.","know":"• **Ksp** = product of ion concentrations, each to the power of its **coefficient**.\n• **Solids** (and pure liquids) are left out.\n• Use the concentrations of a **saturated** solution.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the dissolution equation and the concentrations in a saturated solution.","hl":"MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq)","calc":["coefficients: Mg²⁺ 1, F⁻ 2; MgF₂(s) left out"]},{"text":"Write the solubility product with each concentration raised to its coefficient.","calc":["Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻]²"]},{"text":"Plug in the values from sentence 2.","hl":"[Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M","calc":["(4.2 × 10⁻⁴)² = 1.76 × 10⁻⁷","Ksp = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ × 1.76 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ **3.7 × 10⁻¹¹**"]},{"text":"Adding concentrations is never an equilibrium constant; leaving [F⁻] unsquared skips its coefficient; squaring [Mg²⁺] gives it a coefficient it doesn't have.","strike":["[Mg²⁺] + [F⁻] = 6.3 × 10⁻⁴","[Mg²⁺][F⁻] = 8.8 × 10⁻⁸","[Mg²⁺]²[F⁻]² = 7.8 × 10⁻¹⁵"]},{"text":"Answer: [Mg²⁺][F⁻]² = 3.7 × 10⁻¹¹."}],"trap":"Squaring every concentration: only the ion with coefficient 2 is squared."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq)","note":"coefficients 1 and 2"},{"t":"in water saturated with it","note":"use these for Ksp"}]},{"text":"**Expression first.**","calc":["AₓBᵧ(s) ⇌ x A + y B → Ksp = [A]ˣ[B]ʸ"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a 1:1 salt such as CaCO₃, both exponents are 1: Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻]."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ksp multiplies the dissolved ions' concentrations, each raised to its coefficient, and leaves the solid out."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-54a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:54#1","pe3-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:20#1","pe4-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:54"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P10","passage":"A laboratory treats its tap water before feeding it to a small boiler. The scale that builds up in the boiler is mainly magnesium fluoride, MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq); in water saturated with it, [Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M. The most abundant dissolved metal ion in the feed water comes from a metal whose first three ionization energies are 420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol. A cation-exchange resin in its acid form removes that ion: M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq), where R is an anionic site fixed to the resin beads. At 28 °C, where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol (use e ≈ 2.7). When the resin is spent, it is restored by rinsing it with a concentrated solution that drives the exchange back to the left.","q":"To which family of the periodic table does the metal of the feed water's main ion belong?","choices":["alkali metals","alkaline earth metals","inner transition metals","halogens"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Group 2 metals rise only modestly from IE₁ to IE₂ and jump after the 2nd electron; here the jump comes after the 1st."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Inner transition metals usually form 3+ ions, with a jump after the 3rd electron; here IE₂ to IE₃ is a modest rise."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The passage calls it a metal that forms a cation; halogens are nonmetals that gain electrons."}],"rule":"Find the big jump between successive ionization energies: the electrons removed before it are the valence electrons, which sets the group.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Electrons before the big jump = valence electrons. 420 → 3050 is about 7×, right after the 1st, so one valence electron."},"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Successive ionization energies of the feed-water metal","x":{"label":"Electron removed"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":5000,"step":1000},"categories":["IE₁","IE₂","IE₃"],"series":[{"name":"Ionization energy","values":[420,3050,4410]}]},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the ionization energies: they leap **right after the first electron** (420 → 3050 kJ/mol, about 7×), so the atom has one valence electron and forms **1+ ions**, matching the M⁺ in sentence 4: group 1, the **alkali metals**.","how":{"ask":"Find where the big jump in ionization energy falls; that counts the valence electrons and names the group.","know":"• **Group names:** alkali metals = group 1; alkaline earth metals = group 2; halogens = group 17; noble gases = group 18; metalloids = the staircase elements.\n• A huge jump in successive ionization energies comes once the valence shell is empty.\n• Group 1 loses 1 electron → **1+ ions**; group 2 loses 2 → **2+ ions** (Ca, Mg), with the jump after the 2nd.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the metal's first three ionization energies; compare each with the one before.","hl":"420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol","calc":["3050 ÷ 420 ≈ 7.3 (**big jump**)","4410 ÷ 3050 ≈ 1.4 (modest)"],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"],["IE₂","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"The jump comes after electron 1: one valence electron, group 1. Sentence 4's M⁺ agrees.","hl":"M⁺(aq) + H–R(s)"},{"text":"Alkaline earth metals jump after the 2nd electron; inner transition metals typically form 3+ ions; halogens are nonmetals that gain electrons.","strike":["alkaline earth metals","inner transition metals","halogens"]},{"text":"Answer: alkali metals."}],"trap":"Reading the biggest value as the jump: the jump is the biggest ratio between neighbors, here IE₂ ÷ IE₁."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol","note":"IE₁, IE₂, IE₃"},{"t":"M⁺(aq)","note":"a 1+ ion"}]},{"text":"**Find the jump.** Divide each ionization energy by the one before it.","calc":["3050/420 ≈ 7.3 → jump after the 1st electron","4410/3050 ≈ 1.4"]},{"text":"**Count valence electrons.** One before the jump → group 1 → forms 1+ ions (Li⁺, Na⁺, K⁺)."},{"text":"**Also:** hardness ions Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ come from group 2, the alkaline earth metals, whose jump comes after the 2nd electron."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the big ionization-energy jump comes right after the last valence electron; after the 1st means group 1 (1+ ions), after the 2nd means group 2 (2+ ions)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-55a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:55#1","pe3-cp:41#1","pe3-cp:41#2","pe3-cp:41#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:55"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P10","passage":"A laboratory treats its tap water before feeding it to a small boiler. The scale that builds up in the boiler is mainly magnesium fluoride, MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq); in water saturated with it, [Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M. The most abundant dissolved metal ion in the feed water comes from a metal whose first three ionization energies are 420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol. A cation-exchange resin in its acid form removes that ion: M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq), where R is an anionic site fixed to the resin beads. At 28 °C, where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol (use e ≈ 2.7). When the resin is spent, it is restored by rinsing it with a concentrated solution that drives the exchange back to the left.","q":"For the resin exchange at 28 °C, what is K, and which side does it favor?","choices":["K ≈ 0.37; reactants win","K ≈ 1.0; neither wins","K ≈ 2.7; products win","K ≈ 7.4; products win"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards sign: a negative ΔG° gives K above 1, not below."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"K = 1 needs ΔG° = 0; here ΔG° is negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That is e², which would need ΔG° twice as negative as given."}],"rule":"K = e^(−ΔG°/RT): ΔG° < 0 → K > 1 (products favored); ΔG° > 0 → K < 1; ΔG° = 0 → K = 1.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"−ΔG°/RT = 2.5/2.5 = 1, so K = e¹."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives **ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol** and **RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol**. Rearranging **ΔG° = −RT ln K** gives ln K = −ΔG°/RT = 1, so **K = e ≈ 2.7**. K > 1, so the **products** (resin holding M⁺, H⁺ released) are favored.","how":{"ask":"Turn ΔG° into ln K with −ΔG°/RT, then exponentiate and read the side.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K**, so **K = e^(−ΔG°/RT)**.\n• ΔG° < 0 → K > 1 → products favored.\n• Keep ΔG° and RT in the same units.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives ΔG° and RT in the same units.","hl":"where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol","calc":["−ΔG°/RT = 2.5/2.5 = 1"]},{"text":"Use ΔG° = −RT ln K.","calc":["ln K = 1 → **K = e ≈ 2.7**"]},{"text":"K > 1, so the products are favored.","calc":["M⁺ binds the resin; H⁺ is released"]},{"text":"A K below 1 flips the sign of ΔG°; K = 1 needs ΔG° = 0; 7.4 is e², twice the exponent.","strike":["K ≈ 0.37; reactants win","K ≈ 1.0; neither wins","K ≈ 7.4; products win"]},{"text":"Answer: K ≈ 2.7; products win."}],"trap":"Dropping the minus sign: a negative ΔG° means ln K is positive and K > 1."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol","note":"same units as ΔG°"},{"t":"ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol","note":"negative → K > 1"},{"t":"use e ≈ 2.7","note":"e¹"}]},{"text":"**From ΔG° to K.**","calc":["K = e^(−ΔG°/RT)"]},{"text":"**Also:** with R = 8.314 J/mol·K, convert kJ to J first; at 298 K, RT ≈ 2.48 kJ/mol."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG° = −RT ln K; a negative ΔG° means K > 1 and products are favored."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-56a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:56#1","pe3-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:56"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P10","passage":"A laboratory treats its tap water before feeding it to a small boiler. The scale that builds up in the boiler is mainly magnesium fluoride, MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq); in water saturated with it, [Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M. The most abundant dissolved metal ion in the feed water comes from a metal whose first three ionization energies are 420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol. A cation-exchange resin in its acid form removes that ion: M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq), where R is an anionic site fixed to the resin beads. At 28 °C, where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol (use e ≈ 2.7). When the resin is spent, it is restored by rinsing it with a concentrated solution that drives the exchange back to the left.","q":"Which flushing solution regenerates the resin, and why?","choices":["HCl; extra H⁺ reverses it","NaOH; OH⁻ removes H⁺","Pure water; it dilutes H⁺","Fresh resin; more H–R"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: removing H⁺, a product, pulls the exchange further to the right."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: lowering [H⁺] also favors the forward direction, not the reverse."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Solids don't appear in Q or K, so adding resin does not shift the equilibrium."}],"rule":"Le Châtelier: adding a product shifts an equilibrium left; removing one shifts it right; added solids don't shift it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"To push left, add a product in high concentration. The dissolved product is H⁺, so use a strong acid."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives the exchange, **M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq)**, and sentence 6 says regeneration drives it **left**. Adding a **product** does that, and the only dissolved product is **H⁺**: a concentrated strong acid such as **HCl** loads H⁺ back onto the resin and releases M⁺. Removing or diluting H⁺ shifts the other way, and the solid resin doesn't enter K.","how":{"ask":"Find which dissolved species, added in excess, pushes the exchange to the left.","know":"• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product shifts left; removing a product shifts right.\n• **Solids** (the resin) don't appear in K, so adding more doesn't shift it.\n• A strong acid supplies a high [H⁺].","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says regeneration must drive the exchange back to the left.","hl":"drives the exchange back to the left","calc":["need: shift ←"]},{"text":"Sentence 4 shows H⁺(aq) as the dissolved product; adding a product shifts the equilibrium left.","hl":"M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq)","calc":["add H⁺ → Q > K → shift ←"]},{"text":"NaOH and pure water both lower [H⁺], which shifts it right; added solid resin doesn't shift it at all.","strike":["NaOH; OH⁻ removes H⁺","Pure water; it dilutes H⁺","Fresh resin; more H–R"]},{"text":"Answer: HCl, because extra H⁺ reverses the exchange."}],"trap":"Thinking more resin helps: solids don't enter the equilibrium expression."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"in its acid form","note":"H–R"},{"t":"drives the exchange back to the left","note":"add a product"}]},{"text":"**Which side is H⁺ on?** It is a product, so adding it pushes the reaction backward."},{"text":"**Also:** a water softener works the same way: a Na⁺-form resin takes up Ca²⁺, and a concentrated salt brine pushes Na⁺ back on."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to drive an ion exchange backward, flood it with the ion it released; solids don't shift an equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-57a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:57#1","pe3-cp:57#2","pe3-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:57#4","pe3-cp:57#5"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:57"],"q":"In each species below, the central atom is bonded only to halogen atoms. Which one has an octahedral molecular geometry?","fig":"cpt-halide-centers","choices":["TeF₆","ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"ICl₄⁻ has only four bonded atoms (two lone pairs on opposite corners): square planar, not octahedral."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Octahedral molecular geometry needs six bonded atoms; ClF₃ has only three."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Octahedral needs six bonded atoms; SiCl₄ has four and no lone pairs: tetrahedral."}],"rule":"Molecular geometry counts bonded atoms only: octahedral needs six atoms around the center with no lone pairs (AX₆).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Octahedral = 6 domains, 0 lone pairs (AX₆): only TeF₆ has 6 bonds. ICl₄⁻ is square planar, ClF₃ T-shaped, SiCl₄ tetrahedral."},"exp":"Count electron domains on the central atom. **TeF₆**: 6 bonds, 0 lone pairs → **octahedral**. ICl₄⁻: 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs → square planar. ClF₃: 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs → T-shaped. SiCl₄: 4 bonds → tetrahedral.","how":{"ask":"For each central atom, count bonds and lone pairs, then name the shape.","know":"• **Lone pairs** = (valence e⁻ − bonding e⁻) ÷ 2, after adding 1 e⁻ per negative charge and subtracting 1 per positive charge; each single bond uses 1 e⁻ of the central atom.\n• 6 bonds, 0 LP → **octahedral**.\n• 4 bonds + 2 LP → **square planar**.\n• 3 bonds + 2 LP → **T-shaped**.\n• 4 bonds, 0 LP → **tetrahedral**.","steps":[{"text":"Count the central atom's electron domains in each species.","hl":"octahedral molecular geometry","calc":["TeF₆: Te 6 e⁻, 6 bonds → 0 LP → 6 domains","ICl₄⁻: I 7 + 1 = 8 e⁻, 4 bonds → 2 LP → 6 domains","ClF₃: Cl 7 e⁻, 3 bonds → 2 LP → 5 domains","SiCl₄: Si 4 e⁻, 4 bonds → 0 LP → 4 domains"]},{"text":"Only TeF₆ has all six domains as bonds, so atoms sit at all six octahedral corners.","calc":["TeF₆ → **octahedral**"]},{"text":"ICl₄⁻ is square planar (two lone pairs take opposite corners), ClF₃ is T-shaped, and SiCl₄ is tetrahedral.","strike":["ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"]},{"text":"Answer: TeF₆."}],"trap":"Picking ICl₄⁻ because it has six electron domains: its electron geometry is octahedral, but with two lone pairs the molecular shape is square planar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"central atom","note":"count its domains"},{"t":"octahedral molecular geometry","note":"6 bonds, no lone pairs"}]},{"text":"**Count electron domains.** Bonds plus lone pairs on the central atom set the electron geometry; only the bonded atoms set the molecular shape."},{"text":"**6 bonds, 0 lone pairs = octahedral.** TeF₆: Te's six valence electrons all go into six Te–F bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs is square planar: in ICl₄⁻ the lone pairs sit opposite each other."},{"text":"**Also:** 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs is T-shaped: ClF₃'s lone pairs take two equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds and no lone pairs is tetrahedral, as in SiCl₄ (109.5° angles)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count domains on the central atom; 6 bonds/0 LP octahedral, 4 bonds/2 LP square planar, 3 bonds/2 LP T-shaped, 4 bonds/0 LP tetrahedral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-58a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:58"],"q":"A tube sealed at the upper end stands in ethanol (ρ = 790 kg/m³), and the air inside is pumped out. Air pressure is 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa and g = 9.8 m/s². How high can the ethanol rise?","choices":["13 m","0.76 m","1.3 m","7.8 × 10⁸ m"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a factor of 10: recheck the powers of ten when dividing 10⁵ Pa by about 7.7 × 10³."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplying P by ρ and g gives a height far beyond the atmosphere; solve P = ρgh for h by dividing."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"0.76 m is the mercury barometer height; ethanol is far less dense, so it rises much higher."}],"rule":"An evacuated column rises until ρgh equals air pressure: h = P/(ρg). Less dense liquids rise higher (water about 10 m, mercury 0.76 m).","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"h = P/(ρg) ≈ 10⁵ / (800 × 10) ≈ 12.5 m; the exact numbers land just above that."},"exp":"Nothing presses down on the liquid inside the tube, so it climbs until the pressure of the column at its base matches air pressure, **P = ρgh**, so the height is h = P/(ρg) = 1.01 × 10⁵ / (790 × 9.8) ≈ **13 m**, higher than water's 10.3 m because ethanol is less dense.","how":{"ask":"Set the column's pressure ρgh equal to air pressure and solve for h.","know":"• A column under vacuum is held up by air pressure: **P_atm = ρgh**.\n• **h = P/(ρg)**: about 10 m for water and 0.76 m for mercury at 1 atm.\n• Lower density → taller column.","steps":[{"text":"Set air pressure equal to the column's pressure.","hl":"Air pressure is 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa","calc":["P = ρgh"]},{"text":"Solve for h.","calc":["h = 1.01 × 10⁵ / (790 × 9.8)","= 1.01 × 10⁵ / 7.7 × 10³ ≈ **13 m**"]},{"text":"1.3 m slips a power of ten; 7.8 × 10⁸ m multiplies instead of dividing; 0.76 m is mercury's height.","strike":["1.3 m","7.8 × 10⁸ m","0.76 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 13 m."}],"trap":"Recalling the 0.76 m mercury height: that works only for mercury's high density."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pumped out","note":"vacuum above the liquid"},{"t":"ρ = 790 kg/m³","note":"ρ"},{"t":"1.01 × 10⁵ Pa","note":"P"}]},{"text":"**Pressure balance.** Air pushes on the open surface; nothing pushes down inside the tube except the liquid's own weight.","calc":["P_atm = ρgh"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** h = P/(ρg); the less dense the liquid, the taller the column air pressure can hold up."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:59#1","pe3-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:59#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:59"],"q":"Consider these period-2 elements and their melting points: Li 180 °C; B 2076 °C; F₂ −220 °C; Ne −249 °C. Which one is a covalent network solid in its standard state, and why?","choices":["Boron: a 3-D net of B–B bonds","Lithium: it is solid at 25 °C","Fluorine: F–F bonds join atoms","Neon: its octet is complete"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Lithium is a metal: its atoms are held by a sea of delocalized electrons, not by localized covalent bonds."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Each F–F bond joins only two atoms into an F₂ molecule; weak forces between molecules give the very low melting point."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A full octet leaves neon as lone, unbonded atoms held only by weak dispersion forces."}],"rule":"Network solids melt very high because melting breaks covalent bonds; small molecules and noble gases melt far below 0 °C, and metals bond metallically.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The highest melting point among the nonmetals flags a covalent network."},"exp":"**Boron** forms a covalent network: each B atom is covalently bonded to several neighbors in a 3-D framework, so it melts only when covalent bonds break, hence **2076 °C**. F₂ and Ne are discrete molecules or atoms held by weak dispersion forces; lithium is a **metallic** solid.","how":{"ask":"Sort the elements by bonding type (network, molecular, atomic, metallic), using the melting points as a check.","know":"• **Covalent network solids** (diamond and graphite C, B, Si, SiO₂): every atom bonded covalently to several neighbors; very high melting points.\n• **Molecular** elements (N₂, O₂, F₂) and noble gases: weak forces between particles; low melting points.\n• **Metals** (Li, Be): metallic bonding, not covalent.","steps":[{"text":"Sort by melting point: thousands of degrees signals a network.","hl":"B 2076 °C"},{"text":"F₂ and Ne melt far below zero: separate molecules and lone atoms.","strike":["Fluorine: F–F bonds join atoms","Neon: its octet is complete"]},{"text":"Lithium is solid, but its bonding is metallic, not covalent.","strike":["Lithium: it is solid at 25 °C"]},{"text":"Answer: boron."}],"trap":"Taking \"solid at room temperature\" as proof of a network: metals and ionic solids are solids too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"B 2076 °C","note":"very high: network"},{"t":"F₂ −220 °C","note":"molecular"},{"t":"covalent network solid","note":"atoms linked in a 3-D web"}]},{"text":"**Bonding type sets the melting point.** Breaking covalent bonds takes far more energy than separating molecules."},{"text":"**Also:** in period 2, carbon (diamond, graphite) is the classic network solid alongside boron; N₂ and O₂ are small molecules, and P or I can be ruled out at once because they are not in period 2."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a covalent network solid has every atom covalently bonded to several neighbors, so it melts at very high temperature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-01a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:1#1","pe4-cp:1#2","pe4-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:1"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P1","passage":"Diammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₂HPO₄, is a fertilizer that slowly gives off ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g) (Reaction I). In Reaction I, a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion. For Reaction I, ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K). A bag stored in a warehouse at 320 K under standard conditions lost no measurable mass over a whole season. In a sealed flask, the solid was allowed to reach equilibrium with its ammonia, and extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature. On strong heating, both ammonium ions give up their ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g) (Reaction II). In one run, 59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol) was decomposed this way, and the ammonia released was measured at STP.","q":"Which statement best describes Reaction I in the 320 K warehouse?","choices":["Nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +25 kJ/mol","Spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −6.2 × 10⁴ kJ/mol","Spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −25 kJ/mol","Nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +149 kJ/mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Multiplying 320 K by 195 without turning J into kJ makes TΔS a thousand times too big and flips the sign."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is TΔS − ΔH: subtracting the wrong way flips the sign of ΔG and clashes with the unchanged mass."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Adding TΔS to ΔH treats the entropy gain as a cost; ΔG = ΔH − TΔS subtracts it."}],"rule":"A passage that reports no change means ΔG > 0. Check it: put ΔS in kJ, multiply by T, subtract from ΔH; spontaneity starts only above T = ΔH/ΔS.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"ΔS = 0.195 kJ/(mol·K), so TΔS = 320 × 0.195 ≈ 62 kJ/mol, below ΔH = 87. ΔG ≈ +25 kJ/mol > 0, matching the unchanged mass."},"exp":"Sentence 4 reports no mass loss at 320 K, so ΔG must be positive there. Check it with sentence 3: TΔS = 320 K × 0.195 kJ/(mol·K) ≈ **62 kJ/mol**, less than ΔH = **87 kJ/mol**, so ΔG = ΔH − TΔS ≈ **+25 kJ/mol**: Reaction I is **nonspontaneous** at 320 K. It turns spontaneous only above T = ΔH/ΔS ≈ 446 K.","how":{"ask":"Decide the sign and size of ΔG for Reaction I at the warehouse temperature.","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; ΔG > 0 means no net reaction.\n• ΔS is given in J: divide by 1000 before combining it with ΔH in kJ.\n• With ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0, the reaction turns spontaneous only above **T = ΔH/ΔS**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: at 320 K the bag lost no mass, so no ammonia escaped and ΔG must be positive.","hl":"lost no measurable mass over a whole season"},{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the two terms; put ΔS in kJ and multiply by the warehouse temperature.","hl":"ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K)","calc":["ΔS = 0.195 kJ/(mol·K)","TΔS = 320 × 0.195 ≈ **62 kJ/mol** < ΔH = 87 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Subtract: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","calc":["ΔG ≈ 87 − 62 = **+25 kJ/mol** > 0"]},{"text":"TΔS only beats ΔH if J is never turned into kJ.","strike":["Spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −6.2 × 10⁴ kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Subtracting the wrong way gives a negative ΔG that clashes with sentence 4; adding the terms treats TΔS as a cost.","strike":["Spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −25 kJ/mol","Nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +149 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: Nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +25 kJ/mol, as sentence 4 observed."}],"trap":"Calling a gas-releasing, entropy-raising decomposition spontaneous without multiplying ΔS by T: at 320 K the endothermic ΔH still wins."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol","note":"endothermic"},{"t":"ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K)","note":"J, not kJ"},{"t":"at 320 K","note":"the T to use"},{"t":"lost no measurable mass","note":"the observation: ΔG > 0"}]},{"text":"**Compare the two terms** in ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, in the same units.","calc":["TΔS = 320 × 0.195 ≈ 62 kJ/mol","ΔG ≈ 87 − 62 = +25 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"**Find the crossover.** Above T = ΔH/ΔS the entropy term wins, and the fertilizer would lose ammonia.","calc":["T = 87 ÷ 0.195 ≈ 446 K"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a passage observation of \"no change\" means ΔG > 0; for ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0 that holds while ΔH > TΔS, below T = ΔH/ΔS, with ΔS in kJ first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-02a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:2#1","pe4-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:2"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P1","passage":"Diammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₂HPO₄, is a fertilizer that slowly gives off ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g) (Reaction I). In Reaction I, a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion. For Reaction I, ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K). A bag stored in a warehouse at 320 K under standard conditions lost no measurable mass over a whole season. In a sealed flask, the solid was allowed to reach equilibrium with its ammonia, and extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature. On strong heating, both ammonium ions give up their ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g) (Reaction II). In one run, 59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol) was decomposed this way, and the ammonia released was measured at STP.","q":"In the sealed flask, which expression gives K for Reaction I, and how does P_NH₃ respond to the added powder?","choices":["K = P_NH₃; no change","K = P_NH₃; P_NH₃ falls","K includes both solids; P_NH₃ falls","K = (P_NH₃)²; no change"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Reaction I makes one NH₃ per formula unit, so the pressure enters K to the first power."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Pure solids have activity 1 and are left out of K, so neither phosphate salt appears."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Adding a pure solid product leaves Q equal to K, so nothing shifts back and the pressure holds."}],"rule":"Only gases and dissolved species enter K, each to its own coefficient. Adding a pure solid at constant T never shifts the equilibrium.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Strike every (s): only NH₃(g) is left, coefficient 1. A pure solid stays at activity 1, so more NH₄H₂PO₄ leaves Q = K and the ammonia pressure stays put."},"exp":"Sentence 1 shows the phases in Reaction I: two solids and one gas. Pure solids are left out, so **K = P_NH₃**, to the first power. Sentence 5 stirs in more NH₄H₂PO₄ at constant temperature, but a pure solid's activity stays 1, so Q still equals K and the ammonia pressure **does not change**.","how":{"ask":"Write K for Reaction I from the phases, then decide whether adding more solid product shifts it.","know":"• K contains gas pressures (or solute concentrations) raised to their coefficients.\n• **Pure solids and liquids are left out** (activity = 1).\n• Adding a pure solid, even a product, changes neither Q nor K.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the phase of each species in Reaction I: two solids and one gas.","hl":"(NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g)","calc":["both phosphate salts (s): left out of K","NH₃(g): in K, coefficient 1"]},{"text":"Write K from the gas alone.","calc":["K = P_NH₃"]},{"text":"The coefficient of NH₃ is 1, and the solids stay out of K.","strike":["K = (P_NH₃)²; no change","K includes both solids; P_NH₃ falls"]},{"text":"Sentence 5 adds only more pure solid at constant temperature, so Q still equals K and nothing shifts.","hl":"extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature","strike":["K = P_NH₃; P_NH₃ falls"]},{"text":"Answer: K = P_NH₃; no change."}],"trap":"Applying Le Châtelier to a solid product: more NH₄H₂PO₄ does not raise its \"concentration\", so it pushes the equilibrium nowhere."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"(NH₄)₂HPO₄(s)","note":"solid: out of K"},{"t":"NH₄H₂PO₄(s)","note":"solid: out of K"},{"t":"NH₃(g) (Reaction I)","note":"the only term in K"},{"t":"at constant temperature","note":"K is fixed"}]},{"text":"**Build K.** Gases and dissolved species only, each raised to its coefficient.","calc":["K = P_NH₃"]},{"text":"**Test the change.** A pure solid's activity is always 1, so adding it changes neither Q nor K. Only a change in a gas pressure, a concentration or the temperature could shift it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pure solids and liquids are left out of K, and adding or removing them never shifts the equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-03a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:3#1","pe4-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:3"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P1","passage":"Diammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₂HPO₄, is a fertilizer that slowly gives off ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g) (Reaction I). In Reaction I, a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion. For Reaction I, ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K). A bag stored in a warehouse at 320 K under standard conditions lost no measurable mass over a whole season. In a sealed flask, the solid was allowed to reach equilibrium with its ammonia, and extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature. On strong heating, both ammonium ions give up their ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g) (Reaction II). In one run, 59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol) was decomposed this way, and the ammonia released was measured at STP.","q":"In Reaction I, what happens to phosphorus's oxidation state, and what type of reaction is it?","choices":["Stays +5; acid–base","Stays +5; redox","Rises to +6; redox","Rises to +7; acid–base"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"P is in group 15, with five valence electrons, so +5 is its highest oxidation state; +6 cannot occur."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"P cannot exceed +5, and a real change of oxidation state would make it redox anyway."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No element changes oxidation state when only a proton moves, so the reaction is not redox."}],"rule":"If only H⁺ moves between species, no oxidation state changes, so it is acid–base, not redox. A group 15 element tops out at +5.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Sentence 2 says only a proton moves (staying +1): acid–base. P is +5 both times (1 + P − 8 = −2)."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says a proton moves from NH₄⁺ to HPO₄²⁻. In HPO₄²⁻: (+1) + P + 4(−2) = −2, so P = +5. In H₂PO₄⁻: 2(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −1, so P = +5. Only an H⁺ moves, keeping its +1, so no element changes oxidation state: Reaction I is **acid–base, not redox**.","how":{"ask":"Assign phosphorus's oxidation state before and after Reaction I, then decide whether anything was oxidized or reduced.","know":"• Oxidation states: H = +1, O = −2; they add up to the species' charge.\n• **Redox** needs an element whose oxidation state changes. Moving H⁺ (still +1) changes nothing: that is **acid–base**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: Reaction I just moves a proton from ammonium to hydrogen phosphate.","hl":"a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion"},{"text":"Assign P before and after.","calc":["HPO₄²⁻: 1 + P − 8 = −2 → P = **+5**","H₂PO₄⁻: 2 + P − 8 = −1 → P = **+5**"]},{"text":"P stays +5 and cannot exceed it, so the rising values are wrong.","strike":["Rises to +6; redox","Rises to +7; acid–base"]},{"text":"Nothing changes oxidation state; only a proton is passed along, so the reaction is not redox.","strike":["Stays +5; redox"]},{"text":"Answer: Stays +5; acid–base."}],"trap":"Thinking that gaining a bond to H changes the central atom: the H arrives as H⁺, bringing no electrons, so P keeps its oxidation state."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a proton moves","note":"H⁺ transfer"},{"t":"(NH₄)₂HPO₄(s)","note":"HPO₄²⁻, charge −2"},{"t":"NH₄H₂PO₄(s)","note":"H₂PO₄⁻, charge −1"}]},{"text":"**Assign oxidation states.** H = +1, O = −2, and the sum equals the charge.","calc":["HPO₄²⁻: P = +5","H₂PO₄⁻: P = +5"]},{"text":"**Also:** nitrogen behaves the same way in the other ion. In NH₄⁺, N + 4(+1) = +1, so N = −3; in NH₃, N = −3 too. Losing H⁺ leaves N unchanged."},{"text":"**Classify.** No oxidation state changes and only H⁺ moves: acid–base, not redox."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reaction is redox only if some element's oxidation state changes; a pure H⁺ transfer is acid–base."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-04a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:4#1","pe4-cp:4#2","pe6-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:4"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P1","passage":"Diammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₂HPO₄, is a fertilizer that slowly gives off ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g) (Reaction I). In Reaction I, a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion. For Reaction I, ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K). A bag stored in a warehouse at 320 K under standard conditions lost no measurable mass over a whole season. In a sealed flask, the solid was allowed to reach equilibrium with its ammonia, and extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature. On strong heating, both ammonium ions give up their ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g) (Reaction II). In one run, 59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol) was decomposed this way, and the ammonia released was measured at STP.","q":"In the strong-heating run, what volume of NH₃ was released?","choices":["5.0 L","10.1 L","20.2 L","22.1 L"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"5.0 L divides by 2 instead of multiplying: each (NH₄)₂HPO₄ releases two NH₃."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"10.1 L treats each formula unit as giving one NH₃; Reaction II's 1 : 2 ratio was skipped."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"22.1 L uses 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume near 25 °C; at STP one mole of gas fills 22.4 L."}],"rule":"Grams ÷ molar mass, then × the coefficient ratio (product ÷ reactant), then × 22.4 L/mol, which holds at STP only.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"59.4 ÷ 132 = 0.45 mol salt; × 2 = 0.90 mol NH₃; 0.9 × 22.4 ≈ 20 L."},"exp":"Sentence 7 gives the mass and molar mass: 59.4 g ÷ 132 g/mol = **0.450 mol**. Reaction II releases 2 NH₃ per formula unit: **0.900 mol NH₃**. At STP one mole of ideal gas fills 22.4 L, so 0.900 × 22.4 = 20.16 ≈ **20.2 L**.","how":{"ask":"Convert the salt's mass to moles, use Reaction II's ratio to get moles of NH₃, then use the STP molar volume.","know":"• moles = mass ÷ molar mass.\n• The coefficients give the **mole ratio**: 1 (NH₄)₂HPO₄ → 2 NH₃.\n• **V = n × 22.4 L/mol** at STP (0 °C, 1 atm) only; near 25 °C it is about 24.5 L/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the mass decomposed, its molar mass, and the STP condition.","hl":"59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol)","calc":["n = 59.4 ÷ 132 = **0.450 mol**"]},{"text":"Sentence 6's Reaction II sets the ratio: each formula unit gives two NH₃.","hl":"(NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g)","calc":["n(NH₃) = 0.450 × 2 = **0.900 mol**"]},{"text":"Convert to volume at STP.","hl":"measured at STP","calc":["V = 0.900 × 22.4 = 20.16 L"]},{"text":"5.0 L halves instead of doubling, 10.1 L skips the ratio, and 22.1 L uses the 25 °C molar volume instead of STP's.","strike":["5.0 L","10.1 L","22.1 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 20.2 L."}],"trap":"Taking one mole of salt as one mole of gas: Reaction II, not Reaction I, describes the strong-heating run, and it releases two NH₃ per unit."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"59.4 g","note":"mass decomposed"},{"t":"M = 132 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"2NH₃(g)","note":"2 mol NH₃ per mol salt"},{"t":"measured at STP","note":"22.4 L/mol applies"}]},{"text":"**Grams to moles.**","calc":["59.4 ÷ 132 = 0.450 mol"]},{"text":"**Moles of reactant to moles of product** with the coefficients of the right reaction.","calc":["0.450 × 2 = 0.900 mol NH₃"]},{"text":"**Moles to liters at STP.** One mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L at 0 °C and 1 atm; at other conditions use PV = nRT.","calc":["0.900 × 22.4 = 20.16 L"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same coefficients give a gas used up. For a fuel, moles of O₂ = moles of fuel × (O₂ coefficient ÷ fuel coefficient), then × 22.4 L/mol at STP."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V(gas, STP) = moles of reactant × (product coefficient ÷ reactant coefficient) × 22.4 L/mol, and 22.4 L/mol holds at STP only."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-05a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:5#1","pe4-cp:5#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:5"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P2","passage":"Iron in well water can be measured with ferrozine, which binds Fe²⁺ to form a magenta complex that absorbs most strongly at 562 nm (hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm). The spectrophotometer is zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette. Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66, a straight line through the origin. A sample from a well near a rusted pipe reads A = 0.93, above the top standard. A sample from a second well was read in a cuvette with a scratched wall and gave A = 0.71. When the scratched cuvette was rinsed and filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.08.","q":"What absorbance comes from the iron complex in the second well's sample?","choices":["0.63: the scratch adds 0.08","0.71: the zero was set","0.79: add the cuvette’s share","0.08: only the gap counts"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The scratch adds its own absorbance to the reading, so it must be subtracted, not added."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"The zero was set on the clean cuvette, so it never removed the scratched cuvette's own absorbance."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the scratched cuvette's blank reading, which contains no iron complex at all."}],"rule":"A reading is analyte plus everything else in the light path. Measure the flawed cuvette with blank alone and subtract it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Absorbances add along the light path: sample in the scratched cuvette − blank in the scratched cuvette = complex alone."},"exp":"Sentence 2 sets the zero on a **clean** cuvette, so the scratched cuvette's own absorbance is still in the 0.71 reading of sentence 5. Sentence 6 measures that share by itself: 0.08. Absorbances add, so the complex gives 0.71 − 0.08 = **0.63**. Matched cuvettes avoid the problem: then sample and reference differ only in the analyte.","how":{"ask":"Separate the iron complex's absorbance from the extra absorbance of the scratched cuvette.","know":"• Absorbances along one light path add: A(total) = A(analyte) + A(cuvette, blank).\n• Zeroing removes only what was in the zeroing cuvette.\n• Matched (optically identical) cuvettes make the sample and reference differ only in the analyte.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the zero was set on a different, clean cuvette, so the scratch was never zeroed out.","hl":"zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette"},{"text":"Sentences 5 and 6 give the total in the scratched cuvette and the scratched cuvette alone.","hl":"filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.08","calc":["A(total) = 0.71","A(cuvette + blank) = 0.08"]},{"text":"Subtract the cuvette's share.","calc":["A(complex) = 0.71 − 0.08 = **0.63**"]},{"text":"Adding the scratch is backwards, the clean-cuvette zero never removed it, and the blank-only reading holds no iron.","strike":["0.79: add the cuvette’s share","0.71: the zero was set","0.08: only the gap counts"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.63: the scratch adds 0.08."}],"trap":"Trusting the zero: zeroing on a clean cuvette does not cancel a flaw in a different cuvette."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"in a clean glass cuvette","note":"zero set on another cuvette"},{"t":"scratched wall","note":"adds its own A"},{"t":"filled with reagent blank alone","note":"measures the flaw by itself"}]},{"text":"**Absorbances add.** The beam passes the wall, the reagent and the complex; each takes a share.","calc":["0.71 = A(complex) + 0.08"]},{"text":"**Why matched cuvettes:** if the cuvettes used for sample and reference are matched, the cuvette's share cancels and only the analyte is left."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** sample and reference must differ only in the analyte; any extra absorber in the sample's path (a scratch, a smudge, a different cuvette) must be measured and subtracted."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-06a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:6#1","pe4-cp:6#2","pe4-cp:6#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:6"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P2","passage":"Iron in well water can be measured with ferrozine, which binds Fe²⁺ to form a magenta complex that absorbs most strongly at 562 nm (hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm). The spectrophotometer is zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette. Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66, a straight line through the origin. A sample from a well near a rusted pipe reads A = 0.93, above the top standard. A sample from a second well was read in a cuvette with a scratched wall and gave A = 0.71. When the scratched cuvette was rinsed and filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.08.","q":"About how much energy does one photon at the complex's absorption peak carry?","choices":["0.45 eV","2.2 eV","22 eV","3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ eV"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That is the energy in joules with an eV label; a visible photon carries a few eV."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"A power-of-ten slip in the wavelength; a visible photon carries a few eV, not tens."},{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dividing λ by hc inverts the relation; energy falls as wavelength grows, E = hc/λ."}],"rule":"E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm). Visible light (400–700 nm) gives about 1.8–3.1 eV, so any other size is a units slip.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"E (eV) ≈ 1240 ÷ λ (nm) = 1240 ÷ 562 ≈ 2.2 eV, inside the visible range of about 1.8–3.1 eV."},"exp":"Sentence 1 gives the peak, 562 nm, and hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm. E = hc/λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 562 ≈ **2.2 eV**. In joules that is about 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, which must still be divided by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV.","how":{"ask":"Convert the complex's absorption wavelength into photon energy in electron-volts.","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: shorter wavelength, more energy.\n• hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm, so **E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm)**.\n• In joules: E = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴)(3.0 × 10⁸)/λ in m; divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV.\n• Visible photons: about 1.8–3.1 eV.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 holds both numbers: the peak wavelength and hc in eV·nm.","hl":"absorbs most strongly at 562 nm"},{"text":"Use the eV·nm shortcut.","hl":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm","calc":["E = 1240 ÷ 562 ≈ **2.2 eV**"]},{"text":"A joule value labeled eV and a tenfold slip both fall outside the visible range.","strike":["3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ eV","22 eV"]},{"text":"Dividing λ by hc inverts E = hc/λ.","strike":["0.45 eV"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.2 eV."}],"trap":"Stopping at joules: hc/λ in SI units gives about 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, which still has to be divided by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"562 nm","note":"λ to use"},{"t":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm","note":"shortcut"},{"t":"magenta complex","note":"absorbs green light"}]},{"text":"**Long route, in joules.**","calc":["E = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)(3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (562 × 10⁻⁹ m) ≈ 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV ≈ 2.2 eV"]},{"text":"**Sanity check.** Red ≈ 700 nm ≈ 1.8 eV; violet ≈ 400 nm ≈ 3.1 eV. Green light, which a magenta solution absorbs, must fall in between."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm); to go from joules to eV, divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-07a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:7#1","pe4-cp:7#2","pe4-cp:8#1","pe4-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:7"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P2","passage":"Iron in well water can be measured with ferrozine, which binds Fe²⁺ to form a magenta complex that absorbs most strongly at 562 nm (hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm). The spectrophotometer is zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette. Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66, a straight line through the origin. A sample from a well near a rusted pipe reads A = 0.93, above the top standard. A sample from a second well was read in a cuvette with a scratched wall and gave A = 0.71. When the scratched cuvette was rinsed and filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.08.","q":"The first well's sample is diluted 5-fold with reagent blank and then reads A = 0.33. What was its original iron concentration?","choices":["2.4 μM","12 μM","34 μM","60 μM"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"2.4 μM divides by the dilution factor; undoing a dilution means multiplying."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"12 μM is the diluted solution; the 5-fold dilution was never multiplied back."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"34 μM reads the line at 0.93, above the top standard, where the straight line was never tested."}],"rule":"Use only a reading inside the standards' range; convert it with the standards' slope, then multiply by the dilution factor.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"A ∝ c: the standards give 0.22 per 8 μM. 0.33 is 1.5 × 0.22, so 1.5 × 8 = 12 μM diluted; × 5 = 60 μM."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the line: 0.22 per 8 μM, or **0.0275 per μM**. Sentence 4's reading, 0.93, lies above the top standard, so only the diluted reading is used: 0.33 ÷ 0.0275 = **12 μM** in the cuvette. Undo the 5-fold dilution: 12 × 5 = **60 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Read the in-range reading off the standard line, then multiply by the dilution factor.","know":"• **Beer's law**: A = εbc, so at a fixed path **A ∝ c**; the standards give the slope.\n• Only **interpolate**: a reading above the top standard must be diluted into range, never read off an extended line.\n• Original concentration = **diluted concentration × dilution factor**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the standards fix a straight line through the origin, so find its slope.","hl":"Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66","calc":["slope = 0.22 ÷ 8 = **0.0275 per μM**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Iron standards with ferrozine, read in the clean cuvette","x":{"label":"Fe²⁺ (μM)","min":0,"max":40,"step":8},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Standards (with blank)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[8,0.22],[16,0.44],[24,0.66]]}]}},{"text":"Sentence 4 shows the first reading lies above the top standard, outside the proven range, so it is not used.","hl":"reads A = 0.93, above the top standard"},{"text":"Use the diluted reading from the stem, which falls inside the range.","hl":"reads A = 0.33","calc":["c(diluted) = 0.33 ÷ 0.0275 = **12 μM**"]},{"text":"Undo the dilution.","hl":"diluted 5-fold","calc":["c(original) = 12 μM × 5"]},{"text":"Dividing by 5 runs the dilution backwards, 12 μM forgets it, and 34 μM extrapolates the line to the out-of-range 0.93.","strike":["2.4 μM","12 μM","34 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 60 μM."}],"trap":"Reporting the concentration in the cuvette: the sample was diluted, so multiply by 5."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8, 16 and 24 μM","note":"standards"},{"t":"0.22, 0.44 and 0.66","note":"their absorbances"},{"t":"above the top standard","note":"do not extrapolate"},{"t":"diluted 5-fold","note":"multiply back by 5"}]},{"text":"**Absorbance to concentration** with the standards' slope.","calc":["0.33 ÷ 0.0275 per μM = 12 μM"]},{"text":"**Undo the dilution.**","calc":["12 μM × 5 = 60 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** this is why the first reading was not used. A line is trusted only between its lowest and highest standards; a sample above the top standard is diluted into range, reread, and the result multiplied back."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** original concentration = (in-range absorbance ÷ slope of the standards) × dilution factor; never read a value off the line beyond the top standard."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-08a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:8#1","pe4-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:8"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P2","passage":"Iron in well water can be measured with ferrozine, which binds Fe²⁺ to form a magenta complex that absorbs most strongly at 562 nm (hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm). The spectrophotometer is zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette. Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66, a straight line through the origin. A sample from a well near a rusted pipe reads A = 0.93, above the top standard. A sample from a second well was read in a cuvette with a scratched wall and gave A = 0.71. When the scratched cuvette was rinsed and filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.08.","q":"Which procedure gives the most reliable iron value for the sample from the well near the rusted pipe?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Iron standards with ferrozine, read in the clean cuvette","x":{"label":"Fe²⁺ (μM)","min":0,"max":40,"step":8},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Standards (with blank)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[8,0.22],[16,0.44],[24,0.66]]}]},"choices":["Extrapolate beyond A = 0.66","Equate it with the 24 μM standard","Dilute, reread, scale up","Use a cuvette with a longer path"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A longer path raises A (A = εbc), pushing the reading even further above the standards."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"No standard was run above 0.66, so the line is not known to stay straight that high."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the top standard; the sample read well above it, so it holds more iron."}],"rule":"Read unknowns only inside the calibrated range: interpolate, never extrapolate. Too high → bring it into range, then multiply back.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Bring the reading inside the standards: 0.93 ÷ 2 ≈ 0.47, inside 0–0.66. Read that off the line, then multiply by 2."},"exp":"Sentence 3 shows the standards stop at A = 0.66, and sentence 4 says the sample reads 0.93, above them. The line is proven only between its standards, so **dilute** the sample by a known factor into that range, reread it, read the concentration off the line, and **multiply by the dilution factor**.","how":{"ask":"Choose the step that brings the sample's reading into the range the standards cover, then recovers the original concentration.","know":"• A calibration line is trusted only **between** its lowest and highest standards (interpolation).\n• Above the top standard: **dilute** (or shorten the path) to bring A into range.\n• Multiply the result by the dilution factor to get the original concentration.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the sample reads above the top standard.","hl":"reads A = 0.93, above the top standard"},{"text":"Sentence 3 shows the standards reach only A = 0.66.","hl":"give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66"},{"text":"A longer path makes A larger still, and the line beyond 0.66 was never tested.","strike":["Use a cuvette with a longer path","Extrapolate beyond A = 0.66"]},{"text":"The sample holds more iron than the top standard, so it cannot be reported as that standard.","strike":["Equate it with the 24 μM standard"]},{"text":"A twofold dilution puts it near A ≈ 0.47, inside the range; multiply the result by 2.","calc":["0.93 ÷ 2 ≈ 0.47 → ≈ 17 μM on the line","17 μM × 2 ≈ 34 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: Dilute, reread, scale up."}],"trap":"Extending the calibration line: Beer's law can bend at high absorbance, and no standard checks it there."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a straight line through the origin","note":"proven only up to the top standard"},{"t":"24 μM","note":"top standard"},{"t":"above the top standard","note":"out of range"}]},{"text":"**Interpolate, never extrapolate.** The standards prove the line only between 0 and 0.66; above that it may bend."},{"text":"**Bring it into range.** Dilute by a known factor (or shorten the path), reread, convert with the line, then multiply back."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reading above the top standard is diluted into range, reread, and multiplied by the dilution factor."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-09a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:9#1","pe4-cp:9#2","pe4-cp:9#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:9"],"q":"The four isomers of formula C₅H₁₀O shown are separated by fractional distillation at atmospheric pressure. Which isomer leaves the column first?","choices":["Tetrahydropyran","Pentan-3-one","Pentanal","Cyclopentyl alcohol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"The O–H group hydrogen-bonds, giving the highest boiling point, so this isomer distills last, not first."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone C=O makes a strong dipole, so dipole–dipole attraction raises its boiling point above that of an ether."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An aldehyde C=O is strongly polar too, so it boils higher than a weakly polar ether of the same mass."}],"rule":"Same mass, so rank by intermolecular forces: H-bonding (O–H, N–H) > strong dipoles (C=O) > weak dipoles (ethers). Lowest boiling point distills first.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Equal masses: the fraction that comes over first is the one with the weakest attractions. No O–H and only a weak C–O–C dipole: the cyclic ether."},"exp":"Fractional distillation separates liquids by **boiling point**; the lowest-boiling one distills first. All four have the same formula, so dispersion forces are similar and polarity decides. Cyclopentanol **hydrogen-bonds** (bp ≈ 140 °C). The ketone and aldehyde have strong C=O dipoles (bp ≈ 102–103 °C). The cyclic ether tetrahydropyran has only a weak C–O–C dipole and no O–H (bp ≈ 88 °C), so it comes over **first**.","how":{"ask":"Find the isomer with the lowest boiling point, since it vaporizes and reaches the top of the column first.","know":"• Fractional distillation separates by **boiling point**.\n• At similar molar mass: **H-bonding** (alcohols, acids, amines) > **dipole–dipole** (ketones, aldehydes) > weak dipoles (ethers, alkyl halides).\n• An alcohol boils far higher than an ether or alkyl halide of similar mass.","steps":[{"text":"All four share a formula, so their masses are equal.","hl":"formula C₅H₁₀O"},{"text":"Classify each by its strongest attraction.","calc":["cyclopentanol: O–H → H-bonds","3-pentanone, pentanal: C=O → strong dipoles","tetrahydropyran: C–O–C → weak dipole only"]},{"text":"The H-bonding alcohol boils highest, so it comes over last.","strike":["Cyclopentyl alcohol"]},{"text":"The C=O compounds boil above the ether.","strike":["Pentan-3-one","Pentanal"]},{"text":"Answer: Tetrahydropyran."}],"trap":"Thinking a ring or a compact shape raises the boiling point here: with equal masses, polarity and H-bonding decide the order."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"isomers of formula C₅H₁₀O","note":"equal molar mass"},{"t":"fractional distillation","note":"separates by boiling point"},{"t":"leaves the column first","note":"lowest boiling point"}]},{"text":"**Rank the forces.** H-bonding beats dipole–dipole, which beats a weak dipole.","fig":"cyclopentanol","calc":["bp: cyclopentanol ≈ 140 °C","pentanal ≈ 103 °C, 3-pentanone ≈ 102 °C","tetrahydropyran ≈ 88 °C"]},{"text":"**Also:** an alcohol boils well above an alkyl halide or ether of similar mass, because only the alcohol has an O–H to hydrogen-bond."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a distillation, the liquid with the weakest intermolecular forces (lowest boiling point) collects first."}],"choiceFigs":["tetrahydropyran","3-pentanone","pentanal","cyclopentanol"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:10#1","pe4-cp:10#2","pe4-cp:10#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:10"],"q":"Four alcohols are each heated with chromic acid, H₂CrO₄, a strong oxidizing agent. Which alcohol is NOT oxidized?","choices":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol","2-Methylbutan-2-ol"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"'-1-ol' puts the OH on an end CH₂: a primary alcohol, readily oxidized."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Unbranched pentan-2-ol has its OH on a CH between two carbons: secondary, so it becomes a ketone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"In 3-methylbutan-2-ol the OH carbon is a CH bonded to two carbons: secondary, so it oxidizes to a ketone."}],"rule":"Chromic acid needs an H on the carbon holding the OH: primary and secondary alcohols oxidize; tertiary alcohols do not.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Chromic acid needs an H on the C bearing the OH. A tertiary alcohol (that carbon has three C's, no H) won't oxidize: 2-methylbutan-2-ol."},"exp":"Oxidizing an alcohol removes **the H on the carbon bearing the OH** and turns C–OH into C=O. In **2-methylbutan-2-ol** that carbon carries three carbon groups and no H, so it is a tertiary alcohol and resists oxidation. Pentan-1-ol (primary) goes to pentanal and then pentanoic acid; the two secondary alcohols become ketones.","how":{"ask":"Classify each alcohol as 1°, 2° or 3° by counting the carbons on the carbinol carbon, then find the one with no H there.","know":"• **Carbinol carbon** = the carbon holding the OH. Oxidation needs an **H on that carbon**.\n• **1° alcohol → aldehyde → carboxylic acid** (a strong oxidant like H₂CrO₄ goes all the way to the acid).\n• **2° alcohol → ketone** (stops there).\n• **3° alcohol → no reaction** (no H on the carbinol carbon).","steps":[{"text":"The reagent is a strong oxidant, so any alcohol that can be oxidized will be.","hl":"chromic acid, H₂CrO₄"},{"text":"Pentan-1-ol: the OH carbon is CH₂, bonded to one carbon, so it is primary. It is oxidized to an aldehyde and then on to the acid.","calc":["CH₃(CH₂)₃CH₂OH → CH₃(CH₂)₃CHO → CH₃(CH₂)₃COOH"],"strike":["Pentan-1-ol"]},{"text":"Pentan-2-ol and 3-methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon is CH, bonded to two carbons, so both are secondary and become ketones.","calc":["CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₂CH₃ → pentan-2-one","CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)₂ → 3-methylbutan-2-one"],"strike":["Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol"]},{"text":"2-Methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon holds two CH₃ groups and an ethyl, three carbons and no H. It is tertiary and cannot form a C=O without breaking a C–C bond.","calc":["(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂CH₃: carbinol C has 0 H → no oxidation"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-Methylbutan-2-ol."}],"trap":"Picking a branched secondary alcohol because it looks crowded: branching elsewhere does not matter; only the H on the carbinol carbon does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"chromic acid","note":"strong oxidant"},{"t":"NOT oxidized","note":"look for the alcohol that cannot lose an H"}]},{"text":"**Find the carbinol carbon** in each name: the number before \"-ol\" tells you which carbon holds the OH. Count how many carbons are bonded to it: one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°."},{"text":"**Primary alcohols** have two H's on the carbinol carbon: they go to an **aldehyde**, and a strong oxidant carries them on to the **carboxylic acid**.","calc":["RCH₂OH → RCHO → RCOOH"]},{"text":"**Secondary alcohols** have one H there: they become a **ketone** and stop.","calc":["R₂CHOH → R₂C=O"]},{"text":"**Tertiary alcohols** have no H there: nothing to remove, so **no oxidation**.","calc":["R₃COH → no reaction"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an alcohol is oxidized only if its carbinol carbon carries an H: 1° gives aldehyde then acid, 2° gives ketone, 3° does not react."}],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pentan-1-ol","cpt-pentan-2-ol","cpt-3-methylbutan-2-ol","cpt-2-methylbutan-2-ol"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-11a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:11#1","pe4-cp:11#2","pe4-cp:11#3","pe4-cp:11#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:11"],"q":"In CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃, shown, the bromine and the terminal CH₃ bonded to the CH carbon are cis to each other. What is the compound's name?","choices":["(Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The higher-priority groups, Br and the C1 CH₃, are on the same side; E would need them opposite."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both wrong: Br and the C1 CH₃ are cis, not E, and 'pent-3-ene' renumbers to a lower locant from the other end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Locant rule: number from the end nearer the C=C; in a 5-carbon chain a C=C at 3 is at 2 counted from the other end."}],"rule":"Number from the end nearest the C=C, then compare the higher-priority group on each alkene carbon: same side = Z, opposite = E.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"E/Z compares the higher-priority group on each alkene C, not the main chain. C2: CH₃ > H; C3: Br > ethyl. Br cis to CH₃ → Z; lowest C=C locant → pent-2-ene."},"exp":"The five-carbon chain holds the C=C, and numbering from the CH₃–CH= end puts it at **C2**, with Br on **C3**: 3-bromopent-2-ene. On C2, **CH₃ beats H**; on C3, **Br (atomic number 35) beats the ethyl carbon (6)**. Those two winners are on the same side, so the alkene is **Z**, even though the methyl and the ethyl are on opposite sides.","how":{"ask":"Number the chain to give the C=C the lowest locant, name the substituent, then compare the higher-priority group on each alkene carbon.","know":"• **Parent chain**: the longest chain that contains the C=C, numbered from the end **nearer the C=C**.\n• **Substituents** get the locant of the carbon they sit on.\n• **CIP priority**: on each alkene carbon, the atom of higher atomic number wins.\n• **Z** = the two winners on the **same side**; **E** = opposite sides.","steps":[{"text":"The longest chain through the C=C has five carbons. From the left, the C=C starts at C2; from the right, at C3. Number from the left.","hl":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","calc":["left: C1H₃–C2H=C3(Br)–C4H₂–C5H₃ → C=C at 2 ✓","right: C=C at 3 ✗"]},{"text":"Numbering from the ethyl end gives the C=C a locant of 3, which is higher than needed.","strike":["(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"]},{"text":"The only substituent is Br, on C3.","calc":["3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Rank the groups on each alkene carbon by the atomic number of the first atom.","calc":["C2: CH₃ (C, 6) > H (1) → CH₃ wins","C3: Br (35) > CH₂CH₃ (C, 6) → Br wins"]},{"text":"The winners, Br and the C1 CH₃, are on the same side, so the alkene is Z. Calling it E comes from comparing the two alkyl groups instead of Br.","hl":"are cis to each other","strike":["(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Answer: (Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene."}],"trap":"Reading E/Z as cis/trans of the carbon chain: the ethyl and the methyl are on opposite sides, but Br outranks the ethyl, so the winners are cis and the name is Z."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","note":"five carbons, one Br on an alkene carbon"},{"t":"the bromine and the terminal CH₃","note":"the groups whose positions are given"},{"t":"cis to each other","note":"same side; compare with the priorities"}]},{"text":"**Pick and number the chain.** Take the longest chain that includes both alkene carbons, and number from the end that reaches the C=C first.","calc":["C=C at 2 from the left vs 3 from the right → left"]},{"text":"**Name the substituents** with their locants and put them in front of the parent, in alphabetical order if there are several.","calc":["Br on C3 → 3-bromo + pent-2-ene"]},{"text":"**Rank by CIP.** On each alkene carbon, compare the two attached atoms; higher atomic number wins. If they tie, move out to the next atoms.","calc":["Br (35) > C (6) > H (1)"]},{"text":"**Assign Z or E.** Winners on the same side = Z (zusammen); on opposite sides = E (entgegen)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number the longest chain containing the C=C from its nearer end, name the substituents, then put Z when the higher-priority groups on the two alkene carbons share a side and E when they do not."}],"fig":"cpt-z-3-bromopent-2-ene"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-12a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:12#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:12"],"q":"A hiker walks 11 km in a straight line, turns through any angle, then walks 7 km more. Which distance from the start is impossible?","choices":["4 km","10 km","18 km","19 km"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"4 km = 11 − 7, the turn-around case, so it can happen."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"10 km lies between 11 − 7 and 11 + 7, so some turn angle gives it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"18 km = 11 + 7, walking straight on, so it can happen."}],"rule":"After two straight legs, the distance from the start lies between the difference and the sum of the legs; anything outside cannot happen.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Test the two extremes: turn straight back gives 11 − 7 = 4 km; keep going gives 11 + 7 = 18 km. Every result lies in 4–18, so 19 km can't happen."},"exp":"Two displacements add as vectors: the resultant lies between **|11 − 7| = 4 km** (turning back) and **11 + 7 = 18 km** (going straight on). **19 km** is outside that range, so it can't happen.","how":{"ask":"Find the smallest and largest possible magnitudes of the vector sum.","know":"• For vectors of magnitudes A and B: **|A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B**.\n• Minimum when they point opposite (180°), maximum when parallel (0°); every value between is reachable at some angle.","steps":[{"text":"The two legs are vectors of 11 km and 7 km at an adjustable angle.","hl":"turns through any angle"},{"text":"Compute the two extremes.","calc":["min = |11 − 7| = **4 km** (walks back)","max = 11 + 7 = **18 km** (keeps straight)","at 90°: √(11² + 7²) = √170 ≈ 13 km"]},{"text":"4 km and 18 km are the ends of the range, and 10 km lies inside it.","strike":["4 km","18 km","10 km"]},{"text":"Answer: 19 km."}],"trap":"Thinking the endpoints are impossible: turning fully back or not at all gives exactly 4 km or 18 km."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"11 km","note":"vector A"},{"t":"7 km","note":"vector B"},{"t":"any angle","note":"the sum can vary"},{"t":"impossible","note":"look outside the range"}]},{"text":"**Smallest sum:** the vectors point opposite, so they subtract.","calc":["|A − B| = |11 − 7| = 4 km"]},{"text":"**Largest sum:** the vectors point the same way, so they add.","calc":["A + B = 11 + 7 = 18 km"]},{"text":"**Everything between is possible** at some angle; anything outside is not.","calc":["4 km ≤ R ≤ 18 km","19 km > 18 km → impossible"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** |A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B; a magnitude outside that range is impossible."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-13a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:13#1","pe4-cp:13#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:13"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P3","passage":"In a study of myopia, the eyes of nearsighted rabbits bring light from distant objects to a focus in front of the retina. The team first delivers a gene into the outer corneal cells by electroporation. Each cornea sits between two parallel plate electrodes 4.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse is applied across them. The central corneal surface is then made flatter with a pulsed infrared laser. The laser starts a new pulse every 40 ms, and each pulse lasts 12 ns and delivers 0.18 J. Heat deposited in the treated spot spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds. In trial runs at the same peak power, continuous output scorched the tissue around the spot, while pulsed output left it near body temperature.","q":"What does the laser's flattening of the central cornea change?","choices":["Larger radius; less power","Larger radius; more power","Smaller radius; less power","Smaller radius; more power"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"More power means a shorter focal length, which would pull the focus further forward and worsen the myopia."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Flatter means less curved, part of a bigger sphere: the radius grows, not shrinks."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A smaller radius means more curvature, the opposite of flattening."}],"rule":"A flatter curve belongs to a bigger circle, and a gentler curve bends light less: refracting power falls as the radius grows.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Flatter = part of a bigger sphere = larger R. Less curvature bends light less → longer f → lower power (P = 1/f), which moves the focus back."},"exp":"Sentence 1 says the eyes focus **in front of** the retina, so they converge light too strongly. Sentence 4 makes the cornea **flatter**: a flatter surface is part of a bigger sphere, so its **radius of curvature grows**. A larger radius bends light less, giving a longer focal length and **lower power** (P = 1/f), which moves the image back onto the retina.","how":{"ask":"Decide how flattening changes the cornea's radius of curvature and its focusing power.","know":"• **Flatter = larger radius of curvature** (a flat surface has an infinite radius).\n• For a refracting surface or lens, **f grows with R**; power P = 1/f falls.\n• **Myopia**: the eye is too converging, so the image forms in front of the retina; weakening it moves the focus back.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the laser makes the central surface flatter.","hl":"The central corneal surface is then made flatter"},{"text":"Flatter means less curved, part of a bigger sphere, so R grows.","calc":["flatter → larger R"]},{"text":"A smaller radius would be a steeper, more curved surface.","strike":["Smaller radius; less power","Smaller radius; more power"]},{"text":"A larger R bends light less: f grows and power falls. Sentence 1 shows the eye was too strong, so this is the fix.","hl":"to a focus in front of the retina","strike":["Larger radius; more power"]},{"text":"Answer: Larger radius; less power."}],"trap":"Pairing a larger radius with more power: a flatter surface is a weaker refractor, not a stronger one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"nearsighted","note":"too converging"},{"t":"in front of the retina","note":"focus too short"},{"t":"made flatter","note":"less curved surface"}]},{"text":"**Flatter means a bigger circle.** A gently curved surface belongs to a sphere with a large radius.","calc":["more curved → small R","flatter → large R"]},{"text":"**Bigger radius, weaker focusing.** Focal length rises with R, so the cornea converges light less.","calc":["R up → f up → power down"]},{"text":"**Also:** a nearsighted eye focuses too strongly, in front of the retina; a longer focal length moves the image back onto the retina."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** flattening a refracting surface raises its radius of curvature, lengthens its focal length and lowers its power."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-14a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:14"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P3","passage":"In a study of myopia, the eyes of nearsighted rabbits bring light from distant objects to a focus in front of the retina. The team first delivers a gene into the outer corneal cells by electroporation. Each cornea sits between two parallel plate electrodes 4.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse is applied across them. The central corneal surface is then made flatter with a pulsed infrared laser. The laser starts a new pulse every 40 ms, and each pulse lasts 12 ns and delivers 0.18 J. Heat deposited in the treated spot spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds. In trial runs at the same peak power, continuous output scorched the tissue around the spot, while pulsed output left it near body temperature.","q":"How strong is the electric field between the electrodes during the electroporation pulse?","choices":["0.29 V/m","18 V/m","1.8 × 10⁴ V/m","1.8 × 10⁷ V/m"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Leaving the gap in millimetres while V is in volts makes E a thousand times too small."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Treating mm as 10⁻⁶ m (a micrometre) makes the field a thousand times too large."},{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That multiplies voltage by gap; the field is voltage divided by gap, E = V/d."}],"rule":"Uniform field between plates: E = V/d, with V in volts and d in metres (mm = 10⁻³ m). V/m means volts over metres.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Convert first: 4.0 mm = 4.0 × 10⁻³ m. Then E = 72 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁻³) = 18 × 10³ V/m."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives parallel plates, so the field is uniform: **E = V/d**. With d = 4.0 mm = 4.0 × 10⁻³ m, E = 72 V ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻³ m = **1.8 × 10⁴ V/m**.","how":{"ask":"Find the uniform field between the two plates from the voltage and the gap.","know":"• Parallel plates: **E = V/d** (V/m = N/C).\n• **mm = 10⁻³ m**; kV = 10³ V.\n• Pulse timing does not enter E.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: parallel plates, the gap and the voltage.","hl":"two parallel plate electrodes 4.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse","calc":["d = 4.0 × 10⁻³ m","V = 72 V"]},{"text":"Divide voltage by gap.","calc":["E = 72 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁻³) = **1.8 × 10⁴ V/m**"]},{"text":"A gap left in mm, or read as μm, puts the power of ten off by 10³.","strike":["18 V/m","1.8 × 10⁷ V/m"]},{"text":"Multiplying V by d gives V·m, not a field.","strike":["0.29 V/m"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.8 × 10⁴ V/m."}],"trap":"Using the gap in millimetres: E = V/d needs d in metres, or the power of ten is off by 10³."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"parallel plate electrodes","note":"uniform field, E = V/d"},{"t":"4.0 mm apart","note":"d, convert to m"},{"t":"72 V","note":"V"}]},{"text":"**Prefixes:** kilo = 10³, milli = 10⁻³, micro = 10⁻⁶."},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["E = 72 V ÷ (4.0 × 10⁻³ m)","= 18 × 10³ = 1.8 × 10⁴ V/m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for parallel plates, E = V/d in V/m; convert kV to V and mm to m before dividing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-15a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:15#1","pe4-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:15"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P3","passage":"In a study of myopia, the eyes of nearsighted rabbits bring light from distant objects to a focus in front of the retina. The team first delivers a gene into the outer corneal cells by electroporation. Each cornea sits between two parallel plate electrodes 4.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse is applied across them. The central corneal surface is then made flatter with a pulsed infrared laser. The laser starts a new pulse every 40 ms, and each pulse lasts 12 ns and delivers 0.18 J. Heat deposited in the treated spot spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds. In trial runs at the same peak power, continuous output scorched the tissue around the spot, while pulsed output left it near body temperature.","q":"What are the laser's pulse repetition frequency and average output power?","choices":["25 Hz; 4.5 W","0.025 Hz; 4.5 mW","25 Hz; 15 MW","83 MHz; 15 MW"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Using 40 instead of 0.040 s leaves the ms prefix unconverted, so both values come out a thousand times too small."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Energy ÷ pulse length is the peak power during a pulse; the average spreads that energy over the whole period."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That takes the nanosecond pulse length as the period; pulses start every 40 ms, not every few ns."}],"rule":"Repetition rate f = 1/T, with T the start-to-start time. Mean power = (energy of one pulse) × f; peak power = that energy ÷ the pulse length.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"T = 40 ms = 0.040 s → f = 1/0.040 = 25 Hz. Average P = 0.18 J × 25 /s = 4.5 W."},"exp":"Sentence 5 says a new pulse **starts every 40 ms**, so T = 0.040 s and f = 1/T = **25 Hz**. Each pulse carries 0.18 J, so the average power is 0.18 J × 25 s⁻¹ = **4.5 W**. The 12 ns length gives the peak power instead: 0.18 J ÷ 1.2 × 10⁻⁸ s = 1.5 × 10⁷ W.","how":{"ask":"Find how often pulses start, then the energy delivered per second on average.","know":"• **f = 1/T**, with T in seconds (ms = 10⁻³ s).\n• **Average power** = energy per pulse × f.\n• **Peak power** = energy per pulse ÷ pulse duration (much larger).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: the start-to-start time, the pulse length and the energy per pulse.","hl":"starts a new pulse every 40 ms","calc":["T = 0.040 s → f = 1/0.040 = **25 Hz**"]},{"text":"Average power is energy per pulse times pulses per second.","hl":"delivers 0.18 J","calc":["P(avg) = 0.18 J × 25 /s"]},{"text":"Leaving ms unconverted shrinks both by 10³.","strike":["0.025 Hz; 4.5 mW"]},{"text":"Dividing by the 12 ns pulse length gives the peak power, and 1/12 ns is not how often pulses start.","strike":["25 Hz; 15 MW","83 MHz; 15 MW"]},{"text":"Answer: 25 Hz; 4.5 W."}],"trap":"Using the pulse duration where the period belongs: the 12 ns is how long each flash lasts, not how often flashes repeat."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"every 40 ms","note":"period T"},{"t":"lasts 12 ns","note":"pulse duration, for peak power"},{"t":"0.18 J","note":"energy per pulse"}]},{"text":"**Two different powers.**","calc":["average: 0.18 J × 25 Hz = 4.5 W","peak: 0.18 J ÷ 1.2 × 10⁻⁸ s = 1.5 × 10⁷ W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** f = 1/(start-to-start time); average power = energy per pulse × f, and peak power = energy per pulse ÷ pulse duration."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-16a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:16"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P3","passage":"In a study of myopia, the eyes of nearsighted rabbits bring light from distant objects to a focus in front of the retina. The team first delivers a gene into the outer corneal cells by electroporation. Each cornea sits between two parallel plate electrodes 4.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse is applied across them. The central corneal surface is then made flatter with a pulsed infrared laser. The laser starts a new pulse every 40 ms, and each pulse lasts 12 ns and delivers 0.18 J. Heat deposited in the treated spot spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds. In trial runs at the same peak power, continuous output scorched the tissue around the spot, while pulsed output left it near body temperature.","q":"Why does pulsed output spare the tissue around the treated spot?","choices":["Off-time lets heat dissipate","Its mean output is larger","Pulses shorten the wavelength","Pulses reflect off the cornea"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The pulsed beam is dark most of the time, so its mean output is smaller, not larger."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Switching a beam on and off changes its timing, not its wavelength."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Pulsing changes only when the light arrives, not how much the surface reflects."}],"rule":"Pulsing at the same peak power lowers the average power and adds off-time; it does not change the light's wavelength or reflection.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Heat spreads out in a few ms, but pulses start only every 40 ms: each gap is far longer than the time heat needs to leave the spot."},"exp":"Sentence 6 says deposited heat spreads into neighboring tissue **within a few milliseconds**, and sentence 5 shows the beam is dark for nearly all of each 40 ms cycle. So between pulses the heat leaves the spot and the surroundings stay near body temperature (sentence 7). A continuous beam gives no off-time, so heat piles up and scorches the tissue.","how":{"ask":"Explain the burn difference from how fast heat spreads compared with the gap between pulses.","know":"• Heat flows from a warm spot into cooler surroundings over time.\n• Any off-time longer than that spreading time lets the tissue cool before the next burst.\n• At equal peak power, the pulsed beam's **average power** is far lower.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: heat leaves the spot within a few milliseconds.","hl":"spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds"},{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the off-time: each pulse lasts ns but pulses start every 40 ms, so the beam is off almost all the time.","hl":"starts a new pulse every 40 ms","calc":["off-time ≈ 40 ms ≫ a few ms"]},{"text":"With so much off-time the mean output is lower, not higher.","strike":["Its mean output is larger"]},{"text":"Timing does not alter wavelength or reflection.","strike":["Pulses shorten the wavelength","Pulses reflect off the cornea"]},{"text":"Answer: Off-time lets heat dissipate.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Neighboring tissue temperature (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":120,"step":40},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":35,"max":55,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Continuous","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[40,41],[80,45],[120,49]]},{"name":"Pulsed","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[1,38],[40,37.2],[41,38.2],[80,37.4],[81,38.4],[120,37.6]]}]}}],"trap":"Equal peak power does not mean equal energy per second: the pulsed beam is dark between pulses, so heat has time to leave."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"within a few milliseconds","note":"heat spreading time"},{"t":"every 40 ms","note":"one full cycle"},{"t":"at the same peak power","note":"same strength while on"},{"t":"near body temperature","note":"the outcome to explain"}]},{"text":"**Heat spreads** from a hot spot into cooler surroundings over time. Each off-time lets it leave before the next pulse arrives; a continuous beam never pauses."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulsed light at the same peak power heats nearby tissue less, because the gaps let heat dissipate and the average temperature stays lower."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-17a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:17#1","pe4-cp:17#2","pe4-cp:17#3","pe4-cp:17#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:17"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P4","passage":"A fish-processing plant vents air that carries dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor, along with propane from its heaters. The vent stream is bubbled through a water scrubber, which captures the dimethylamine but lets the propane pass through almost untouched. In a bench test, a few milliliters of water were squirted into a sealed flask of dry dimethylamine, and the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds. Dimethylamine is a weak base in water, (CH₃)₂NH + H₂O ⇌ (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻, with Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine. Traces of acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide were also found in the vent air. Before analysis, gas samples are dried over pellets of the oxide of a metal M, whose successive ionization energies were measured in a separate study. The plant's chemists want to know which ion M forms in that oxide.","q":"In its pure liquid state, which compound from the vent air is held together partly by hydrogen bonding?","choices":["Dimethylamine","Acetaldehyde","Hydrogen sulfide","Propane"],"choiceFigs":["1-propanol","tetrahydropyran","3-pentanone","ethyl-acetate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Acetaldehyde is polar, but its H atoms sit on C, so its C=O can only accept an H-bond, never donate one."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"S is not electronegative enough: an H on S makes only weak dipole–dipole contacts, not hydrogen bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Propane is a nonpolar hydrocarbon with only London dispersion forces."}],"rule":"A pure liquid hydrogen-bonds only if its molecules carry H on N, O or F (a donor) plus a lone pair (an acceptor). H on C or S does not count.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan each formula for N–H, O–H or F–H. Only (CH₃)₂NH has one, along with the N lone pair, so only it both donates and accepts."},"exp":"Sentence 1 gives the formula **(CH₃)₂NH**: an N–H bond plus a lone pair on N, so its molecules both donate and accept hydrogen bonds (on top of dipole–dipole and London forces). Acetaldehyde's C=O only accepts, hydrogen sulfide's S–H is too weakly polar, and propane has London forces alone.","how":{"ask":"Find the compound whose molecules carry an H on N, O or F as well as a lone pair.","know":"• Every molecule has **London dispersion** forces.\n• Polar molecules add **dipole–dipole** forces.\n• **Hydrogen bonding** is a strong dipole–dipole force that needs H on N, O or F in the same liquid.\n• Aldehydes, ketones and ethers accept H-bonds but cannot donate them.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 holds the clue: the formula shows an H bonded directly to N.","hl":"(CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor","fig":"diethylamine-nh-hl"},{"text":"Sentence 5 names the other candidates; check each for H on N, O or F.","hl":"acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide","calc":["dimethylamine: N–H ✓","acetaldehyde: CH₃–CHO, H only on C","hydrogen sulfide: H–S, S not N/O/F","propane: C–H only"]},{"text":"The aldehyde can only accept, the S–H is too weakly polar, and propane is nonpolar.","strike":["Acetaldehyde","Hydrogen sulfide","Propane"]},{"text":"Answer: Dimethylamine."}],"trap":"Counting any H or any lone pair: the donor H must sit on N, O or F, so an aldehyde's C=O or an S–H bond does not give a hydrogen-bonded liquid."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"(CH₃)₂NH","note":"N–H: a donor"},{"t":"acetaldehyde","note":"C=O: acceptor only"},{"t":"hydrogen sulfide","note":"S–H: no H-bond"},{"t":"propane","note":"London only"}]},{"text":"**Donor + acceptor.** Only an O–H, N–H or F–H can donate, like the N–H of a secondary amine.","fig":"cpt-methanol"},{"text":"**Also:** ammonia, NH₃, likewise has N–H bonds and a lone pair, so liquid NH₃ has hydrogen bonds plus London forces; a polar molecule without N–H, O–H or F–H has only dipole–dipole and London forces."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** list the forces from the bottom up: London (always), dipole–dipole (if polar), hydrogen bonding (only with H on N, O or F)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-18a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:18#1","pe4-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:18"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P4","passage":"A fish-processing plant vents air that carries dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor, along with propane from its heaters. The vent stream is bubbled through a water scrubber, which captures the dimethylamine but lets the propane pass through almost untouched. In a bench test, a few milliliters of water were squirted into a sealed flask of dry dimethylamine, and the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds. Dimethylamine is a weak base in water, (CH₃)₂NH + H₂O ⇌ (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻, with Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine. Traces of acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide were also found in the vent air. Before analysis, gas samples are dried over pellets of the oxide of a metal M, whose successive ionization energies were measured in a separate study. The plant's chemists want to know which ion M forms in that oxide.","q":"Approximately what is [(CH₃)₂NH₂⁺] in the spent scrub water at equilibrium?","choices":["1.1 × 10⁻⁴ M","5.4 × 10⁻⁴ M","1.0 × 10⁻² M","5.2 × 10⁻² M"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is √(Kb ÷ C): the concentration was divided into Kb instead of multiplied by it."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"No square root: 1.1 × 10⁻⁴ is just Kb·C, which equals x², not x."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies Kb itself, a ratio, not a concentration."}],"rule":"Weak base: [BH⁺] = [OH⁻] ≈ √(Kb × C). Rewrite Kb × C with an even power of ten before taking the root.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"x = √(Kb·C) = √(5.4 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20) = √(1.08 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻² M."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴ and C ≈ 0.20 M. Each base that reacts gives one (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ and one OH⁻, so Kb ≈ x²/C and x = √(Kb·C) = √(1.08 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ **1.0 × 10⁻² M**. That is about 5 % of C, small enough for the approximation.","how":{"ask":"Use Kb and the base concentration to find how much of the base is protonated.","know":"• B + H₂O ⇌ BH⁺ + OH⁻, so Kb = [BH⁺][OH⁻]/[B] = **x²/C** when x is small next to C.\n• **x = √(Kb·C)**; x is both [OH⁻] and [BH⁺].\n• To take a square root of 10ⁿ, make n even first, then halve it.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the base equation, its Kb and the scrub water's concentration.","hl":"Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine"},{"text":"Write the small-x expression and solve for x.","calc":["Kb = x² / C","x = √(Kb · C)","Kb · C = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20 = 1.08 × 10⁻⁴"]},{"text":"The exponent is already even, so halve it.","calc":["√1.08 ≈ 1.0; √10⁻⁴ = 10⁻²","x ≈ **1.0 × 10⁻² M**"]},{"text":"Check the approximation: x is small next to C.","calc":["0.010 ÷ 0.20 = 5 %"]},{"text":"Dividing by C instead of multiplying inverts the setup; Kb·C has no square root; Kb alone is not a concentration.","strike":["5.2 × 10⁻² M","1.1 × 10⁻⁴ M","5.4 × 10⁻⁴ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.0 × 10⁻² M."}],"trap":"Stopping at Kb·C: that product is x², so its square root is the concentration."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a weak base in water","note":"only a little reacts"},{"t":"Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴","note":"base constant"},{"t":"about 0.20 M","note":"starting concentration C"},{"t":"(CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻","note":"made 1 : 1, so both equal x"}]},{"text":"**Write Kb with x.** Each base that reacts makes one BH⁺ and one OH⁻, and the base left is still about C.","calc":["Kb = x·x / (C − x) ≈ x² / C"]},{"text":"**Solve for x** and take the root of the power of ten.","calc":["x = √(1.08 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻² M"]},{"text":"**Also:** with an odd exponent, shift one factor of 10 first, e.g. √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹) = √(36 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a weak base, [OH⁻] = [BH⁺] ≈ √(Kb·C); make the power of ten even, then halve it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-19a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:19#1","pe4-cp:19#2","pe5-cp:3#1","pe5-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:19"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P4","passage":"A fish-processing plant vents air that carries dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor, along with propane from its heaters. The vent stream is bubbled through a water scrubber, which captures the dimethylamine but lets the propane pass through almost untouched. In a bench test, a few milliliters of water were squirted into a sealed flask of dry dimethylamine, and the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds. Dimethylamine is a weak base in water, (CH₃)₂NH + H₂O ⇌ (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻, with Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine. Traces of acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide were also found in the vent air. Before analysis, gas samples are dried over pellets of the oxide of a metal M, whose successive ionization energies were measured in a separate study. The plant's chemists want to know which ion M forms in that oxide.","q":"If the bench test were repeated with dry sulfur dioxide, SO₂, in the flask, which result would be expected?","choices":["Falls; polar SO₂ dissolves","Holds; SO₂ is a nonpolar gas","Holds; no gas dissolves","Falls; SO₂ forms a base"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"The passage itself shows a gas dissolving in water, so \"no gas dissolves\" cannot be right."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"SO₂ dissolves to give sulfurous acid, an acid; calling it a base gets the chemistry backwards."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"SO₂ is bent, with an unshared pair on S, so its bond dipoles do not cancel: it is polar."}],"rule":"Like dissolves like: a polar or water-reactive gas dissolves and its pressure drops; a nonpolar gas (propane, methane, N₂) barely dissolves and its pressure holds.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask one thing: is the new gas polar like dimethylamine or nonpolar like propane? SO₂ is bent and polar, so it dissolves and the pressure falls."},"exp":"Sentences 2 and 3 show the pattern: polar dimethylamine dissolves, so its pressure falls, while nonpolar propane passes through untouched. **SO₂ is bent**, with a lone pair on S, so it is **polar** (and it reacts with water to give an acid). It dissolves like dimethylamine did, and **the pressure falls**.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether SO₂ behaves like the dimethylamine or like the propane in water.","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: polar or H-bonding molecules dissolve in water; nonpolar ones barely do.\n• A sealed container's gas pressure falls only if gas molecules leave the gas phase.\n• SO₂ is bent (polar); CO₂ is linear (nonpolar); hydrocarbons are nonpolar.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the pressure fell because the gas dissolved in the water.","hl":"the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds"},{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the contrast: the nonpolar gas is not captured.","hl":"lets the propane pass through almost untouched"},{"text":"Classify the replacement gas.","calc":["SO₂: bent, lone pair on S → polar","SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ (an acid)"]},{"text":"The passage already shows a gas dissolving, and SO₂ gives an acid, not a base.","strike":["Holds; no gas dissolves","Falls; SO₂ forms a base"]},{"text":"Bent SO₂ is polar, so it dissolves.","strike":["Holds; SO₂ is a nonpolar gas"]},{"text":"Answer: Falls; polar SO₂ dissolves."}],"trap":"Assuming any swap of the gas stops the effect: the outcome depends only on whether the new gas is polar (or water-reactive) enough to dissolve."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"captures the dimethylamine","note":"polar: dissolves"},{"t":"propane pass through almost untouched","note":"nonpolar: stays a gas"},{"t":"fell sharply","note":"gas left the gas phase"}]},{"text":"**Link solubility to pressure.** Dissolved gas no longer pushes on the walls; gas that stays above the water keeps the pressure up."},{"text":"**Contrast:** a nonpolar gas such as propane or methane barely dissolves in water, so its pressure stays near atmospheric."},{"text":"**Also:** the same rule ranks analogs of a drug. Replacing a ring C–H with N (benzene ring → pyridine ring), or adding an O or N that water can H-bond to, raises solubility; adding CH₃ groups or trading a polar group for C–H or C–Cl lowers it.","fig":"pyridine"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a polar or water-reactive gas dissolves and its pressure falls; a nonpolar gas barely dissolves and its pressure holds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-20a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:20#1","pe4-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:20#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:20"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P4","passage":"A fish-processing plant vents air that carries dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor, along with propane from its heaters. The vent stream is bubbled through a water scrubber, which captures the dimethylamine but lets the propane pass through almost untouched. In a bench test, a few milliliters of water were squirted into a sealed flask of dry dimethylamine, and the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds. Dimethylamine is a weak base in water, (CH₃)₂NH + H₂O ⇌ (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻, with Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine. Traces of acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide were also found in the vent air. Before analysis, gas samples are dried over pellets of the oxide of a metal M, whose successive ionization energies were measured in a separate study. The plant's chemists want to know which ion M forms in that oxide.","q":"Based on the ionization energies in the chart, which family does M belong to, and which ion does it usually form?","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Successive ionization energies of metal M","x":{"label":"Electron removed"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":6000,"step":1000},"categories":["IE₁","IE₂","IE₃","IE₄"],"series":[{"name":"M","values":[549,1064,4138,5500]}]},"choices":["Na's group; M⁺","Mg's group; M²⁺","Al's group; M³⁺","Si's group; M⁴⁺"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A group 1 metal shows its huge jump right after the first electron; here the second is still easy to remove."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A group 13 metal loses three electrons before the jump; here the jump already comes at the third."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Four easy removals would mean four valence electrons; the third already costs about four times the second."}],"rule":"Find the big jump in successive ionization energies: the number of electrons removed before it is the valence count, which gives the group and the usual ion charge.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Look for the cliff: IE₁ and IE₂ are low, then IE₃ is about four times IE₂. Two valence electrons → group 2, 2+ ion."},"exp":"Sentence 6 points to M's successive ionization energies. In the chart, IE₂/IE₁ ≈ 1.9, but **IE₃/IE₂ ≈ 3.9**: the jump comes after two electrons, so M has two valence electrons. It is an **alkaline earth metal (group 2)** and forms **M²⁺**, so its oxide is MO, like the CaO often used to dry basic gases.","how":{"ask":"Count how many electrons come off before the large jump in ionization energy.","know":"• Group 1 (alkali metals) jump after IE₁ → 1+; group 2 (alkaline earth metals) after IE₂ → 2+; group 13 after IE₃ → 3+.\n• Halogens (group 17) gain an electron; metalloids sit on the stair-step line; transition metals form several ions.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: M is identified by its successive ionization energies, so read the chart for the jump.","hl":"whose successive ionization energies were measured","calc":["IE₁ ≈ 549, IE₂ ≈ 1064","IE₃ ≈ 4138, IE₄ ≈ 5500 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Find the largest ratio between neighbors.","calc":["IE₂/IE₁ ≈ 1.9","IE₃/IE₂ ≈ 3.9 ← the jump","IE₄/IE₃ ≈ 1.3"]},{"text":"Two electrons come off before the jump, so one, three or four valence electrons are ruled out.","strike":["Na's group; M⁺","Al's group; M³⁺","Si's group; M⁴⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: Mg's group; M²⁺."}],"trap":"Picking the largest bar: IE₄ is the biggest value, but the group comes from where the jump happens, not from the tallest bar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"successive ionization energies","note":"look for the jump"},{"t":"which ion M forms","note":"charge = electrons before the jump"},{"t":"oxide of a metal M","note":"MO for a 2+ ion"}]},{"text":"**Why the jump:** after the valence electrons are gone, the next one comes from a filled inner shell, much closer to the nucleus."},{"text":"**Group names:** alkali metals (group 1, 1+), alkaline earth metals (group 2, 2+), transition metals (groups 3–12), halogens (group 17, 1−), noble gases (group 18). Calcium is an alkaline earth metal and forms Ca²⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the number of electrons removed before the big ionization-energy jump equals the valence count, the group (for main-group metals) and the usual positive charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-21a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:21#1","pe4-cp:21#2","pe4-cp:21#3","pe4-cp:23#1","pe4-cp:23#2","pe4-cp:24#1","pe4-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:21"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P5","passage":"Glioblastoma cells often survive the drug temozolomide (TMZ) by tolerating the DNA lesions it creates. After a dose of TMZ, these lesions take about 9 hours to build up to their full level. Compound V is harmless to cells without such lesions but kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose. The TMZ concentration needed to kill 90% of cells was measured without and with V: line G1, 150 μM alone and 15 μM with V; line G2, 330 μM and 110 μM; line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM; and line G4, 64 μM both ways. For healthy astrocytes, the values were 120 μM alone and 114 μM with V. In a binding assay, V bound its target protein with a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM.","q":"Which conclusion about compound V do the dose data best support?","choices":["Its effect differs widely among tumor lines.","It boosts TMZ to the same degree in every cell type.","It is toxic to tumor cells even without TMZ.","It protects astrocytes against TMZ."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"G3's dose falls thirtyfold, G4's not at all, and the astrocytes' barely, so the boost is far from uniform."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Untested: no condition gives V alone, so the data cannot show V killing cells by itself."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"With V the astrocytes need slightly less TMZ, not more, so V does not protect them."}],"rule":"Compare each cell type with and without the add-in as a ratio, then compare the types. Reject claims about conditions never tested.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Take each ratio: G1 10×, G2 3×, G3 30×, G4 1×, astrocytes ≈ 1×. The boost ranges from none to thirtyfold, so it depends on the line."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives fold changes (dose alone ÷ dose with V) of **10× (G1), 3× (G2), 30× (G3) and 1× (G4)**, and sentence 5 shows the astrocytes barely shift (≈ 1×). So V's boost **differs widely among the tumor lines**. No condition gives V alone, and the astrocytes need slightly less TMZ with V, not more, so neither of those claims is supported.","how":{"ask":"Compute each cell type's fold change with V, then pick the claim the numbers actually test.","know":"• A **sensitizer** lowers the dose of a drug needed for the same effect.\n• Compare **with vs without** within each cell type, then compare the types.\n• **Selective** = the dose falls for the target cells but not for the others.\n• **Fold change = dose alone ÷ dose with the add-in**, not the difference.\n• Reject any claim about a condition the data never tested.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: each tumor line's dose without and with V.","hl":"line G1, 150 μM alone and 15 μM with V; line G2, 330 μM and 110 μM; line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM; and line G4, 64 μM both ways","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"TMZ dose that kills 90% of cells, without and with V","x":{"label":"Cells"},"y":{"label":"TMZ dose (μM)","min":0,"max":350,"step":50},"categories":["G1","G2","G3","G4","Astrocytes"],"series":[{"name":"TMZ alone","values":[150,330,42,64,120]},{"name":"TMZ + V","values":[15,110,1.4,64,114]}]},"calc":["G1: 150/15 = 10×","G2: 330/110 = 3×","G3: 42/1.4 = 30×","G4: 64/64 = 1×"]},{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the healthy cells: 120 → 114 μM, about 1×.","hl":"the values were 120 μM alone and 114 μM with V"},{"text":"The fold changes run from none to thirtyfold, so the boost is not uniform.","strike":["It boosts TMZ to the same degree in every cell type."]},{"text":"No condition gives V alone, so its own toxicity is untested.","strike":["It is toxic to tumor cells even without TMZ."]},{"text":"With V the astrocytes need a little less TMZ, the opposite of protection.","strike":["It protects astrocytes against TMZ."]},{"text":"Answer: Its effect differs widely among tumor lines."}],"trap":"Reading a claim the data never tested (V alone) as supported, or treating every tumor line as responding the same way."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"without and with V","note":"the comparison"},{"t":"64 μM both ways","note":"no boost at all"},{"t":"120 μM alone and 114 μM with V","note":"healthy cells: ≈ 1×"},{"t":"harmless to cells without such lesions","note":"V alone not tested here"}]},{"text":"**Compare within each cell type first,** as a ratio.","calc":["G1 10×, G2 3×, G3 30×, G4 1×, astrocytes ≈ 1.05×"]},{"text":"**Selective means some types only.** Here the dose falls sharply for some tumor lines and barely for the healthy astrocytes, but even among tumor lines the effect varies."},{"text":"**Test only what was measured.** No condition gives V alone, so a claim about V killing cells by itself is unsupported either way."},{"text":"**Also:** order matters in a combination. The agent that creates the vulnerable state goes first; the one that exploits it follows once that state is in place."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** judge a combination by the fold change within each cell type, compare types, and reject claims about conditions the data never tested."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-22a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:22#1","pe4-cp:22#2","pe4-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:1#2","pe5-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:22"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P5","passage":"Glioblastoma cells often survive the drug temozolomide (TMZ) by tolerating the DNA lesions it creates. After a dose of TMZ, these lesions take about 9 hours to build up to their full level. Compound V is harmless to cells without such lesions but kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose. The TMZ concentration needed to kill 90% of cells was measured without and with V: line G1, 150 μM alone and 15 μM with V; line G2, 330 μM and 110 μM; line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM; and line G4, 64 μM both ways. For healthy astrocytes, the values were 120 μM alone and 114 μM with V. In a binding assay, V bound its target protein with a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM.","q":"What number goes into the database for V's dissociation constant?","choices":["0.027 μM","0.27 μM","270 μM","2.7 × 10⁵ μM"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Divided by 10,000; nano to micro is only a factor of 1000."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That keeps the number and swaps the unit, which would make the value 1000 times larger."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: converting to a larger unit (μM) must give a smaller number, not a larger one."}],"rule":"1 μM = 1000 nM, so nM → μM means divide by 1000: a bigger unit always gives a smaller number.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"1 μM = 1000 nM; a bigger unit means a smaller number. 270 ÷ 1000 moves the decimal three places left."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives the value in nM and says the database stores μM. Since 1 μM = 1000 nM, divide by 1000: 270 nM = **0.27 μM**. A bigger unit always gives a smaller number.","how":{"ask":"Convert the nanomolar value to micromolar.","know":"• **Prefixes**: m = 10⁻³, μ = 10⁻⁶, n = 10⁻⁹, p = 10⁻¹².\n• 1 μM = 1000 nM, so **nM → μM: divide by 1000**.\n• **[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M**; **M → μM: × 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: the value is in nM, and the database wants μM.","hl":"a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM"},{"text":"Write the prefixes as powers of ten.","calc":["n = 10⁻⁹, μ = 10⁻⁶: a factor of 10³ apart"]},{"text":"Divide by 1000.","calc":["270 nM ÷ 1000 = **0.27 μM**"]},{"text":"Dividing by 10⁴, or keeping or growing the number, misreads the factor of 1000 between nano and micro.","strike":["0.027 μM","270 μM","2.7 × 10⁵ μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.27 μM."}],"trap":"Multiplying by 1000: a micromole is larger than a nanomole, so the number must get smaller."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"270 nM","note":"value in nanomolar"},{"t":"every concentration in μM","note":"target unit"}]},{"text":"**Write the prefixes as powers of ten.** n = 10⁻⁹ and μ = 10⁻⁶: they differ by 10³."},{"text":"**Bigger unit, smaller number.** nM → μM: divide by 1000."},{"text":"**Also:** from pH to μM: [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M, then × 10⁶ for μM. At pH 6.3, [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁶·³ M ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁷ M = 0.5 μM."},{"text":"**Also:** in a weighing problem, chain the prefixes. Going in, μM → M (× 10⁻⁶) and mL → L (× 10⁻³); coming out, grams → milligrams (× 1000)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert through powers of ten: nM → μM ÷ 1000, M → μM × 10⁶, and [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-23a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:23#1","pe4-cp:23#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:23"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P5","passage":"Glioblastoma cells often survive the drug temozolomide (TMZ) by tolerating the DNA lesions it creates. After a dose of TMZ, these lesions take about 9 hours to build up to their full level. Compound V is harmless to cells without such lesions but kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose. The TMZ concentration needed to kill 90% of cells was measured without and with V: line G1, 150 μM alone and 15 μM with V; line G2, 330 μM and 110 μM; line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM; and line G4, 64 μM both ways. For healthy astrocytes, the values were 120 μM alone and 114 μM with V. In a binding assay, V bound its target protein with a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM.","q":"In which glioblastoma line does V enhance TMZ the most?","choices":["Line G1","Line G2","Line G3","Line G4"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Its dose did not move at all, so V did nothing there: that line shows the least effect, not the most."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"It has the biggest drop in μM, but enhancement is a fold change; 330 → 110 is only threefold."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"150 → 15 is a tenfold drop, which is smaller than another line's fold change."}],"rule":"Enhancement is a ratio: dose alone ÷ dose with the enhancer. Compute it for every line and pick the largest; never compare raw differences.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Divide each pair: G1 150/15 = 10, G2 330/110 = 3, G3 42/1.4 = 30, G4 64/64 = 1. The biggest fold change wins."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives each line's pair. Enhancement is **dose alone ÷ dose with V**: G1 = 10, G2 = 3, **G3 = 30**, G4 = 1. G2 has the largest drop in μM (220), but only a threefold change; G3's small numbers hide the largest fold change.","how":{"ask":"Compute the fold change in TMZ dose for each line and pick the largest.","know":"• Potency changes are compared as **ratios**, not differences.\n• Fold enhancement = dose alone ÷ dose with the enhancer; 1 means no effect.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: each line's dose without and with V.","hl":"line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM","calc":["G1: 150 ÷ 15 = 10","G2: 330 ÷ 110 = 3","G3: 42 ÷ 1.4 = 30","G4: 64 ÷ 64 = 1"]},{"text":"An unchanged dose means no enhancement at all.","strike":["Line G4"]},{"text":"The biggest μM drop is not the biggest fold change.","strike":["Line G2"]},{"text":"Tenfold is smaller than thirtyfold.","strike":["Line G1"]},{"text":"Answer: Line G3."}],"trap":"Subtracting instead of dividing: a line that starts at a high dose shows a big μM drop even when the fold change is small."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"without and with V","note":"a before/after pair per line"},{"t":"330 μM and 110 μM","note":"big drop, small ratio"},{"t":"42 μM and 1.4 μM","note":"small numbers, big ratio"}]},{"text":"**Ratios, not differences.**","calc":["G2: 330 − 110 = 220 μM, but 330/110 = 3","G3: 42 − 1.4 ≈ 41 μM, but 42/1.4 = 30"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to compare how much a treatment helps, divide the before value by the after value in each case and rank the ratios."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-24a","s":"Research Design & Reasoning","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:24#1","pe4-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:24"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P5","passage":"Glioblastoma cells often survive the drug temozolomide (TMZ) by tolerating the DNA lesions it creates. After a dose of TMZ, these lesions take about 9 hours to build up to their full level. Compound V is harmless to cells without such lesions but kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose. The TMZ concentration needed to kill 90% of cells was measured without and with V: line G1, 150 μM alone and 15 μM with V; line G2, 330 μM and 110 μM; line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM; and line G4, 64 μM both ways. For healthy astrocytes, the values were 120 μM alone and 114 μM with V. In a binding assay, V bound its target protein with a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM.","q":"Which schedule should kill the most glioblastoma cells?","choices":["V, then TMZ after 9 h","TMZ and V together","TMZ, then V after 9 h","TMZ, then V after 2 h"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"V acts only on cells TMZ has already damaged, so giving V first reverses the needed order."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Given together, V is cleared within about 2 hours, long before the lesions have built up."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"At 2 h the lesions are far from their full level, so most of V is cleared before its targets exist."}],"rule":"Order the doses by the mechanism: give the agent that creates the vulnerable state first, wait for that state to build, then give the agent that exploits it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask which drug makes the target and which hits it. TMZ makes the lesions (full after about 9 h); V hits lesioned cells and is short-lived. So TMZ first, V once the lesions peak."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says V kills only cells that already carry TMZ lesions and is gone within about 2 hours. Sentence 2 says the lesions take about **9 hours** to build. So give **TMZ first**, then V about 9 h later, when its targets are at their peak. Giving V first, together, or after only 2 h wastes it before the lesions exist.","how":{"ask":"Decide which drug creates the state the other needs, and how long that state takes to build.","know":"• A sequence works when the first agent sets up the state the second one needs.\n• Match the delay to how long the first agent takes to act.\n• A short-lived second agent must arrive after that state exists.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: V kills only lesioned cells, and it is cleared within about 2 hours.","hl":"kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose"},{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the build-up time of those lesions.","hl":"take about 9 hours to build up to their full level"},{"text":"V before TMZ reverses the dependence.","strike":["V, then TMZ after 9 h"]},{"text":"Together, or with only a 2 h gap, V is cleared before the lesions have built up.","strike":["TMZ and V together","TMZ, then V after 2 h"]},{"text":"Answer: TMZ, then V after 9 h."}],"trap":"Assuming the add-in always goes first: here V exploits damage TMZ creates, so TMZ must lead."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"about 9 hours","note":"time for the target state to build"},{"t":"kills cells whose DNA carries them","note":"V needs TMZ's lesions"},{"t":"within about 2 hours","note":"V is short-lived"}]},{"text":"**Mechanism → order.** First the agent that sets up the state; then, once the state exists, the agent that exploits it. If an add-in must block a pathway before a drug can work, the add-in goes first instead."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** give first the agent whose effect the other depends on, and time the second dose to arrive when that effect is fully in place."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-25a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:25#1","pe4-cp:25#2","pe4-cp:25#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:25"],"q":"N₂ has an N–N distance of 110 pm, versus 145 pm for the N–N single bond in hydrazine, H₂N–NH₂. Which bonding in N₂ explains its shorter bond?","choices":["σ + π","σ + 2π","3σ","2σ + π"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Two atoms share only one σ bond, along the axis between them; any further bonds must be π."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"A second σ bond between the same two atoms cannot form; extra bonds are π bonds."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"One σ plus one π is a double bond, which is shorter than a single bond but not as short as this one."}],"rule":"Two atoms share at most one σ bond; every extra bond between them is a π bond. More bonds between the same two atoms → shorter, stronger bond.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each N has 5 valence electrons: N≡N with one lone pair each. A triple bond is always one σ and two π, and it is the shortest."},"exp":"N₂ is **N≡N**: a triple bond made of **a σ bond plus two π bonds**. Only one σ bond can lie along the internuclear axis; each extra shared pair is a π bond from sideways p-orbital overlap. More bonding pairs pull the atoms closer, so the triple bond (110 pm) is much shorter than hydrazine's single bond (145 pm), and also shorter than an N=N double bond (σ + π, about 125 pm).","how":{"ask":"Match the very short bond to its bond order, then break that bond into σ and π parts.","know":"• Single bond = 1 σ; double = 1 σ + 1 π; triple = 1 σ + 2 π.\n• Only one σ bond can join two atoms.\n• Higher bond order → shorter, stronger bond (C–O 143 pm vs C=O ≈ 122 pm is the same pattern).","steps":[{"text":"Compare the two lengths.","hl":"110 pm, versus 145 pm","calc":["110 pm ≪ 145 pm → more than a single bond"]},{"text":"Two atoms can share only one σ bond.","strike":["3σ","2σ + π"]},{"text":"Lewis structure of N₂: :N≡N: (10 valence electrons), a triple bond.","strike":["σ + π"]},{"text":"Answer: σ + 2π."}],"trap":"Counting every shared pair as σ: after the first σ bond, each additional bond between the same atoms is π."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"N₂","note":"N≡N"},{"t":"N–N single bond","note":"σ only"},{"t":"shorter bond","note":"higher bond order"}]},{"text":"**σ vs π.** σ = head-on overlap along the axis (one per atom pair); π = side-by-side p overlap above and below the axis."},{"text":"**Also:** a carbonyl C=O is one σ plus one π, and it is shorter than a C–O single bond; a C≡N or C≡C is one σ plus two π."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** single = σ, double = σ + π, triple = σ + 2π; the more bonds, the shorter the bond."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-26a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:26#1","pe4-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:26"],"q":"Four neutral atoms have the ground-state configurations 1s²2s²2p⁶, [Ne]3s²3p⁵, [Ne]3s² and [Ar]4s¹. Which atom accepts an extra electron most readily, and why?","choices":["Ne, 2p not full","Cl, filling 3p","Mg, 3s has a vacancy","K, pairing in 4s"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"The configuration ends in 3s², a full s subshell, so it has no vacancy there."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"2p⁶ is a full subshell; an added electron would have to start a new, higher shell, which is unfavorable."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A 4s electron is far from a weakly shielded nucleus, so potassium gains little energy by taking one."}],"rule":"An atom one electron short of a filled p subshell (a halogen) gains an electron most readily. Full subshells (noble gases, s²) and metals do not.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for p⁵: one electron completes the subshell. That configuration belongs to the halogen."},"exp":"The [Ne]3s²3p⁵ atom is **chlorine**, a halogen: **one more electron fills its 3p subshell**, giving the stable [Ar] configuration and releasing much energy (the most favorable electron affinity of the four). Neon (2p⁶) is already full, so an extra electron must go into a new shell. Magnesium's 3s² is full too, and potassium, a metal, tends to lose its 4s electron rather than gain one.","how":{"ask":"Find the configuration that one added electron would complete, and say why that is favorable.","know":"• **Electron affinity** is most favorable for halogens (p⁵ → p⁶).\n• Noble gases (full p) and s² atoms have no low-energy vacancy.\n• Metals (low ionization energy) tend to lose electrons, not gain them.","steps":[{"text":"Name each configuration.","calc":["1s²2s²2p⁶ = Ne","[Ne]3s²3p⁵ = Cl","[Ne]3s² = Mg","[Ar]4s¹ = K"]},{"text":"Magnesium's 3s is full, so it has no vacancy there.","hl":"[Ne]3s²","strike":["Mg, 3s has a vacancy"]},{"text":"Neon's 2p is already full; potassium, a metal, holds an extra electron weakly.","hl":"1s²2s²2p⁶","strike":["Ne, 2p not full","K, pairing in 4s"]},{"text":"One electron completes chlorine's 3p subshell.","hl":"[Ne]3s²3p⁵"},{"text":"Answer: Cl, filling 3p."}],"trap":"Thinking any atom with room in an s orbital gains electrons easily: metals have low ionization energies and prefer to lose electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3p⁵","note":"one short of full"},{"t":"1s²2s²2p⁶","note":"already full"},{"t":"accepts an extra electron most readily","note":"most favorable electron affinity"}]},{"text":"**Electron affinity trend:** most favorable toward the upper right (halogens); noble gases are the exception, with no room in their shell."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the atom that gains an electron most readily is the one an extra electron would bring to a filled p subshell: a halogen, not a noble gas or a metal."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-27a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:27#1","pe4-cp:27#2","pe4-cp:27#3","pe4-cp:27#4","pe6-cp:54#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:27"],"q":"Compound Z's triple point is 0.40 atm and 15 °C. Its vapor, held at 0.10 atm, is cooled until frost forms at −10 °C. Which process makes the frost?","choices":["Deposition","Freezing","Condensation","Sublimation"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sublimation is solid → gas, made on warming; this vapor is cooling into a solid, the reverse crossing."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Freezing starts from a liquid, and no liquid exists below the triple-point pressure (0.10 < 0.40 atm)."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Condensation makes a liquid, which cannot exist below the triple-point pressure of 0.40 atm."}],"rule":"Below the triple-point pressure there is no liquid, so gas and solid convert directly: gas to solid on cooling, solid to gas on warming.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Below the triple-point pressure (0.10 < 0.40 atm) no liquid can exist, so gas goes straight to solid: deposition, the reverse of sublimation."},"exp":"At 0.10 atm, below the triple-point pressure, Z has no liquid phase, so cooling the vapor reaches only the **solid–gas line** (at −10 °C). There the gas turns straight into solid: **deposition**, the reverse of sublimation.","how":{"ask":"Which boundary does the cooling vapor cross below the triple-point pressure, and in which direction does it cross it?","know":"• A **phase diagram** plots pressure (up) against temperature (across). Solid sits at low T and high P, gas at high T and low P, liquid in between.\n• Each line is where two phases coexist, crossed one way or the other: **solid–gas = sublimation (solid → gas) / deposition (gas → solid)**, **solid–liquid = melting / freezing**, **liquid–gas = vaporization / condensation**.\n• The **triple point** is where all three lines meet; the **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the path with the triple point.","hl":"held at 0.10 atm","calc":["0.10 atm < 0.40 atm (below the triple-point pressure)","−10 °C < 15 °C (colder than the triple point)"]},{"text":"Below the triple-point pressure, liquid cannot exist, so the only border on this path is the solid–gas line, which the vapor reaches at −10 °C.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"Freezing and condensation both involve a liquid, which has no region below 0.40 atm.","strike":["Freezing","Condensation"]},{"text":"The vapor is cooling, so it crosses the solid–gas line from the gas side to the solid side. Sublimation (solid → gas) is the reverse crossing, made on warming.","hl":"frost forms at −10 °C","strike":["Sublimation"]},{"text":"Answer: deposition (gas → solid)."}],"trap":"Naming the right line but the wrong direction: sublimation and deposition share the solid–gas line, and vapor cooling into frost is deposition."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"triple point","note":"where all three lines meet"},{"t":"0.40 atm and 15 °C","note":"triple-point P and T"},{"t":"held at 0.10 atm","note":"the path's pressure"},{"t":"frost forms at −10 °C","note":"solid appears from the gas"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** up = more pressure, right = hotter. Cold and squeezed = solid; hot and low pressure = gas; liquid is the wedge in between.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"**Place the path:** cooling at a fixed 0.10 atm moves left along a horizontal line from the gas region into the solid region, crossing only the solid–gas line.","calc":["0.10 < 0.40 atm → no liquid region on this path","gas → solid at −10 °C: deposition"]},{"text":"**Also:** warming the solid back along the same line reverses the crossing: solid → gas with no liquid stage, which is **sublimation**."},{"text":"**Also:** a point on the solid–liquid line (nearly vertical, above the triple point) is **melting** or freezing; a point on the liquid–gas line is **boiling** (vaporization) or condensation, and at 1 atm it gives the normal boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** two points are special. The **triple point** is the one T and P where solid, liquid and gas all coexist. The **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line; beyond it liquid and gas merge into one supercritical fluid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find which line the path crosses and in which direction: solid–gas = sublimation (warming) or deposition (cooling), solid–liquid = melting or freezing, liquid–gas = vaporization or condensation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-28a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:28#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:28"],"q":"Electron levels: E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV. Which downward jump emits the longest-wavelength photon?","choices":["E₄ → E₃","E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Largest gap (7.0 eV) means the shortest λ, the opposite of what's asked."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Drops all the way to E₁ have big gaps (5.0 eV here): high energy, short λ."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"E₃ → E₂ spans 1.5 eV; another listed jump has a smaller gap, so its photon is longer than this one."}],"rule":"Longest wavelength = smallest energy gap (λ = hc/ΔE): find the two closest levels.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"λ ∝ 1/ΔE, so longest λ = smallest gap. Gaps: E₄→E₃ 0.5, E₃→E₂ 1.5, E₂→E₁ 5, E₄→E₁ 7 eV. Smallest is 0.5 eV: E₄ → E₃."},"exp":"**A photon carries the energy gap, and λ = hc/ΔE**, so the smallest gap gives the longest wavelength. The gaps are 0.5 eV (E₄ → E₃), 1.5 eV (E₃ → E₂), 5.0 eV (E₂ → E₁) and 7.0 eV (E₄ → E₁); the 0.5 eV drop emits light near 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm, the longest.","how":{"ask":"Find the energy gap of each transition and pick the smallest one.","know":"• An emitted photon's energy equals the gap: **ΔE = hc/λ**, so **λ = hc/ΔE**.\n• Wavelength is inversely proportional to the gap: **smallest ΔE → longest λ**.\n• Shortcut: λ(nm) ≈ 1240/ΔE(eV).","steps":[{"text":"List the levels.","hl":"E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV"},{"text":"Compute each choice's gap.","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = **0.5 eV**","E₃ → E₂: −5.5 − (−7.0) = 1.5 eV","E₂ → E₁: −7.0 − (−12.0) = 5.0 eV","E₄ → E₁: −5.0 − (−12.0) = 7.0 eV"]},{"text":"λ = hc/ΔE, so convert the smallest and largest gaps to wavelengths.","calc":["0.5 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm","7.0 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 7.0 ≈ 177 nm"]},{"text":"The three larger gaps all give shorter wavelengths; E₄ → E₁, the largest gap, gives the shortest.","strike":["E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: E₄ → E₃."}],"trap":"Picking the biggest drop: a large gap means a high-energy photon, which has the shortest wavelength, not the longest."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"E₁ = −12.0 eV","note":"lowest level"},{"t":"E₄ = −5.0 eV","note":"highest level"},{"t":"longest-wavelength photon","note":"smallest gap"}]},{"text":"**The photon carries the gap.** A falling electron emits one photon with ΔE = E(upper) − E(lower).","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = 0.5 eV"]},{"text":"**Flip to wavelength:** λ = hc/ΔE, so wavelength and gap are inversely related.","calc":["λ(nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ ΔE(eV)","1.5 eV → ≈ 830 nm; 0.5 eV → ≈ 2500 nm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ = hc/ΔE, so the transition with the smallest energy gap emits the longest wavelength, and the largest gap the shortest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-29a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:29#1","pe4-cp:29#2","pe4-cp:29#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:29"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"Which nitrogen of Compound 12 belongs to a tertiary amine?","choices":["Nα","Nβ","Nγ","Nδ"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"An N on a carbonyl that also bears an O–C is a carbamate nitrogen, N–C(=O)–O, not an amine."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An N bonded to the carbon of a C=O whose other partner is carbon is an amide nitrogen, not an amine."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A pyridine N is part of an aromatic ring with a C=N bond; it is not an amine with three single-bonded carbons."}],"rule":"Amine = N with only C and H neighbors by single bonds (no C=O, no C=N). Count the carbons on N: one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan each N for a C=O (amide, carbamate) or C=N (aromatic N) first; of what is left, pick the N with three carbons and no H."},"exp":"Sentence 2 lists each nitrogen's neighbors. **Nδ** is bonded to three carbons (two ring CH₂ groups and the benzyl CH₂) and no H, with no C=O: a **tertiary amine**. Nα sits on a carbonyl that also bears an O, a **carbamate**; Nβ sits on a C=O flanked by carbon, an **amide**; Nγ is the aromatic ring N of **pyridine**.","how":{"ask":"Classify each nitrogen by its neighbors, then find the amine with three carbons on N.","know":"• **Amine**: N–C and N–H single bonds only.\n• 1°, 2°, 3° = one, two or three carbons on N.\n• **Amide**: N–C(=O)–C; **carbamate**: N–C(=O)–O; **imine/aromatic N**: C=N.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: Nδ has three carbons, no H and no carbonyl.","hl":"is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group","calc":["3 C on N, no C=O → tertiary amine"]},{"text":"Nα sits on a carbonyl that also bears an O: a carbamate.","hl":"R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph","strike":["Nα"]},{"text":"Nβ sits on a C=O whose other partner is carbon: an amide.","hl":"the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon","strike":["Nβ"]},{"text":"Nγ is double-bonded within an aromatic ring.","strike":["Nγ"]},{"text":"Answer: Nδ."}],"trap":"Calling an amide or carbamate nitrogen an amine: once N is bonded to a C=O carbon, it is no longer an amine, however many carbons it carries."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group","note":"three C on N"},{"t":"R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph","note":"carbamate"},{"t":"whose other partner is a carbon","note":"amide"},{"t":"pyridine ring atom","note":"aromatic C=N"}]},{"text":"**A tertiary amine** has three carbon groups on N and a lone pair, like triethylamine.","fig":"triethylamine"},{"text":"**Also:** an ester is C(=O)–O–C; next to an N, a C=O makes an amide, and N–C(=O)–O makes a carbamate.","fig":"cpt-fix-amino-ester-co-hl"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** rule out amides and carbamates (N–C=O) and aromatic or imine N (C=N) first, then count carbons on the amine N: three carbons, no H = tertiary."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-30a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:30#1","pe4-cp:30#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:30"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"Which organic product forms from methyl 4-oxopentanoate in the side-chain steps?","fig":"cpt-methyl-4-oxopentanoate","choices":["γ-Valerolactone","δ-Valerolactone","Pentane-1,4-diol","Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-gamma-valerolactone","cpt-delta-valerolactone","cpt-pentane-14-diol","cpt-methyl-4-hydroxypentanoate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Warm acid lets a γ-OH close onto the ester, so the open hydroxy ester does not survive."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A δ-lactone would need the OH one carbon farther out, on C5; here the new OH is on C4."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones but not esters; a diol would need LiAlH₄."}],"rule":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones, not esters; a hydroxy ester with the OH γ or δ to the C=O then closes to a ring on warming with acid.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones, not esters. New OH on C4 (γ) closes onto C1: ring C1–C4 + O = 5 atoms. 5-ring lactone = γ, 6-ring = δ."},"exp":"Sentence 4 says NaBH₄ reduces the **ketone** at C4 to a secondary alcohol but leaves the **ester** alone. The new OH sits on the γ-carbon of the ester, so on warming with acid it attacks the ester carbonyl in the same molecule, expels **methanol** and closes a ring of C1–C4 plus the O: five atoms, **γ-valerolactone**.","how":{"ask":"Decide which carbonyl NaBH₄ reduces, then count the ring formed when the new OH attacks the ester.","know":"• **NaBH₄** reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols; it does **not** reduce esters (that needs LiAlH₄).\n• A **γ-hydroxy ester** (OH on C4) cyclizes to a **five-membered lactone**; a δ-hydroxy ester (OH on C5) gives a six-membered one. The ester's alcohol part leaves.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the structure and a reagent that reduces ketones but not esters. Number from the ester carbon: C1 is the CO₂CH₃ carbon, C4 the ketone carbon.","hl":"CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃","calc":["C5H₃–C4(=O)–C3H₂–C2H₂–C1(=O)OCH₃"]},{"text":"NaBH₄ reduces only the ketone, so the ester survives; a diol would need LiAlH₄.","hl":"reduces ketones but leaves esters intact","calc":["C4=O → C4H–OH"],"strike":["Pentane-1,4-diol"]},{"text":"The C4 OH is γ to the ester carbonyl. With acid and warmth it attacks C1 and methanol leaves, so the open hydroxy ester does not survive.","hl":"warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed","strike":["Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"]},{"text":"Count the ring atoms: C1, C2, C3, C4 and the O.","calc":["ring = 4 C + 1 O = 5 atoms → γ-lactone"]},{"text":"A δ-lactone would need the OH one carbon farther out, on C5.","strike":["δ-Valerolactone"]},{"text":"Answer: γ-Valerolactone."}],"trap":"Letting NaBH₄ reduce the ester as well: it is too mild, so the ester stays to be attacked by the new OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"4-oxopentanoate","note":"ketone at C4 of an ester"},{"t":"NaBH₄","note":"reduces the ketone only"},{"t":"warmed with a trace of acid","note":"ring closure"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: what does the reducing agent touch?** NaBH₄ turns a C=O of an aldehyde or ketone into C–OH and leaves esters, acids and amides unchanged.","calc":["R₂C=O → R₂CH–OH; RCO₂R′ unchanged"]},{"text":"**Step 2: find the hydroxy ester.** Locate the new OH relative to the ester carbon: on the γ-carbon (C4) or the δ-carbon (C5)."},{"text":"**Step 3: cyclize.** The OH attacks the ester C=O inside the molecule, the OR′ group leaves as R′OH, and a lactone forms; count its ring atoms."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** NaBH₄ reduces only the ketone; a γ-hydroxy ester closes to a five-membered lactone and a δ-hydroxy ester to a six-membered one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-31a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:31"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"What happens to the intermediate in the KOH reflux step?","choices":["All three are hydrolyzed","Only the ethyl esters react","Just the lactone is opened","Nothing is hydrolyzed"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Hot hydroxide is a strong nucleophile that attacks ester carbonyls; that is saponification."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A lactone is a cyclic ester, so excess hot base opens it just as it cleaves the open-chain esters."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The open-chain esters are not protected; with base in excess they are cleaved too."}],"rule":"Excess hot hydroxide saponifies every ester in the molecule, cyclic (lactone) or not, giving one carboxylate per ester group.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count the ester carbonyls, lactone included: with base in excess, every one becomes a carboxylate."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives two ethyl esters plus the lactone, heated with **about six equivalents** of KOH: base in excess. Hot hydroxide hydrolyzes esters (**saponification**), and a **lactone** is an ester closed into a ring, so it opens too. **All three** become carboxylates: two from the ethyl esters (plus ethanol) and one from the lactone, with an OH left on the chain.","how":{"ask":"Decide which ester groups excess hot base cleaves.","know":"• **Saponification**: RCOOR′ + OH⁻ → RCOO⁻ + R′OH, essentially irreversible.\n• A **lactone** is a cyclic ester and saponifies the same way (the ring opens).\n• Excess base → every ester reacts.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: the ester groups present and the amount of base.","hl":"two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH","calc":["2 esters + 1 lactone = 3 ester carbonyls","≈ 6 equiv KOH: excess"]},{"text":"Hot hydroxide does cleave esters.","strike":["Nothing is hydrolyzed"]},{"text":"Neither the lactone nor the open esters are spared when base is in excess.","strike":["Only the ethyl esters react","Just the lactone is opened"]},{"text":"Answer: All three are hydrolyzed."}],"trap":"Forgetting that a lactone is an ester: the ring opens under the same conditions that cleave the ethyl esters."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"two ethyl esters","note":"two esters"},{"t":"this lactone ring","note":"a cyclic ester"},{"t":"about six equivalents of aqueous KOH","note":"excess: saponifies all"}]},{"text":"**Saponification.** OH⁻ adds to the C=O, the alkoxide leaves, and the acid is deprotonated to a carboxylate.","calc":["R–CO–OEt + OH⁻ → R–COO⁻ + EtOH"]},{"text":"**Lactone:** the same reaction opens the ring, leaving –COO⁻ and –OH on one chain."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** excess hot hydroxide hydrolyzes every ester and lactone into a carboxylate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-32a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:32#1","pe4-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:32"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"In the SN2 step that formed Nδ, which species attacks the benzyl bromide?","choices":["Piperidinium ion","Free piperidine","Piperidide anion","Benzyl carbanion"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"In the ammonium ion the N lone pair is used in the N–H⁺ bond, so it has no free pair to attack carbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Removing the N–H proton (pKa about 35) needs a far stronger base than hydroxide."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Benzyl bromide is the electrophile; nothing here pulls a proton from its carbon to make a carbanion."}],"rule":"A base frees an amine from its salt only as far as pKa allows. The neutral amine, with its lone pair free, is the nucleophile in SN2.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydroxide (water pKa 15.7) easily deprotonates R₂NH₂⁺ (pKa 11.1), but not R₂NH (pKa ~35). So the lone-pair amine is what attacks."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the pKa values: water 15.7 > piperidinium 11.1, so hydroxide **removes H⁺ from the piperidinium ion** and frees **neutral piperidine**. Only the neutral amine has a **free lone pair**, so it attacks the benzylic carbon and displaces bromide, forming the tertiary amine Nδ. Hydroxide is far too weak to strip the N–H proton (pKa ≈ 35) to make the anion.","how":{"ask":"Use the pKa values to see what NaOH does to the salt, then find the species with a free lone pair.","know":"• An acid–base reaction favors the side with the weaker acid (higher pKa).\n• **Ammonium salts** are not nucleophilic: the N lone pair is tied up by H⁺.\n• **Free amines** (lone pair) are good SN2 nucleophiles.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the salt meets NaOH, and the two pKa values decide what happens.","hl":"piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7","calc":["11.1 < 15.7 → OH⁻ deprotonates piperidinium"]},{"text":"The salt's N has no free lone pair, so it cannot attack.","strike":["Piperidinium ion"]},{"text":"OH⁻ cannot remove the N–H of the neutral amine, and the electrophile is not deprotonated.","strike":["Piperidide anion","Benzyl carbanion"]},{"text":"Answer: Free piperidine.","fig":"cpt-piperidine"}],"trap":"Thinking the base makes an even stronger nucleophile (the amide anion): hydroxide only reaches the free amine."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"piperidine hydrochloride","note":"R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻"},{"t":"aqueous NaOH","note":"frees the amine"},{"t":"SN2 reaction","note":"needs a lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Why base first.** NaOH removes H⁺ from the ammonium salt, freeing the amine; only the free amine has a lone pair to attack carbon."},{"text":"**Too far:** the amide anion would need a base stronger than pKa ≈ 35.","fig":"cpt-piperidide"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an amine salt must be deprotonated by a base stronger than the ammonium ion; the resulting neutral amine is the nucleophile."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-33a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:33#1","pe4-cp:33#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:33"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"What does complete hydrolysis of the carbamate on Nα release?","choices":["R–NH₂, CO₃²⁻ and PhCH₂OH","R–NH–CO₂H plus toluene","R–NH₂ and benzyl formate","R–NH–CO₂⁻ plus PhCH₂OH"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"The carbamate anion is only halfway; it loses CO₂ and the free amine is set free, as the extraction shows."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Hydrolysis breaks the C–O bond to give benzyl alcohol, not toluene; no C–H bond forms."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The benzyl group leaves as an alcohol, and the carbonyl carbon ends up as carbonate, so no ester survives."}],"rule":"Fully hydrolyzing a carbamate cuts both sides of the carbonyl: amine + alcohol + CO₂ (carbonate in base).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Split at the C=O: the N side becomes the free amine, the O side becomes the alcohol, and the carbonyl carbon leaves as CO₂ (carbonate in base)."},"exp":"Sentence 2 shows the carbamate, **R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph**, and sentence 6 hydrolyzes it completely in hot KOH. Hydroxide releases **benzyl alcohol** and a carbamate ion, R–NH–CO₂⁻, which loses **CO₂** to set free the **amine R–NH₂**; in KOH the CO₂ is trapped as **carbonate**. That is why a free amine could be extracted into ether.","how":{"ask":"Break the carbamate on both sides of its carbonyl and name the three pieces.","know":"• **Carbamate** = R–NH–C(=O)–OR′: amide and ester on one carbonyl.\n• Hydrolysis gives **amine + CO₂ + alcohol**; in base, CO₂ → CO₃²⁻.\n• Hydrolysis needs strong base (or acid) and heat.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the carbamate's structure, with N on one side of the C=O and the benzyl O on the other.","hl":"R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph","calc":["N side → R–NH₂","O side → PhCH₂OH","C=O carbon → CO₂ → CO₃²⁻ in base"]},{"text":"Sentence 6 says the hydrolysis is complete and a free amine is extracted, so the carbamate anion cannot be the end point.","hl":"complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether","strike":["R–NH–CO₂⁻ plus PhCH₂OH"]},{"text":"The benzyl group leaves as benzyl alcohol, not toluene or an ester.","strike":["R–NH–CO₂H plus toluene","R–NH₂ and benzyl formate"]},{"text":"Answer: R–NH₂, CO₃²⁻ and PhCH₂OH."}],"trap":"Stopping at the carbamic acid or its anion: these lose CO₂ on their own, so complete hydrolysis frees the amine."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"benzyl carbamate","note":"N–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph"},{"t":"complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH","note":"hydrolysis conditions"},{"t":"the free amine was extracted","note":"the amine was released"}]},{"text":"**Two cleavages.** Ester side → alcohol; amide side → amine; the carbonyl carbon becomes CO₂."},{"text":"**Also:** only a step with strong base or acid and heat cleaves a carbamate; NaBH₄ in the cold or an SN2 step leaves it intact."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** complete hydrolysis of a carbamate gives the amine, the alcohol and CO₂ (as carbonate in base)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-34a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:34#1","pe4-cp:34#2","pe4-cp:34#3","pe4-cp:34#4","pe4-cp:34#5","pe6-cp:15#1","pe6-cp:15#2","pe6-cp:15#3","pe6-cp:15#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:34"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P7","passage":"Enzyme Q, a bacterial acetyltransferase, makes aminoglycoside antibiotics useless by moving an acetyl group onto them from acetyl-CoA. In kinetic studies, acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme Q and CoA was released before any antibiotic bound, and an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine was detected between the two steps. To block enzyme Q, chemists made a probe that contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups; the nitrile groups make the C=C electron-poor, so the cysteine thiol adds to it. In a crystal structure of the complex, the probe's benzene ring sits deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded. Initial rates measured with and without the probe gave a double-reciprocal plot in which the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹.","q":"Which type of inhibition does the probe show, and how does it affect KM?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Lineweaver–Burk plot with and without the probe","x":{"label":"1/[S] (μM⁻¹)","min":-0.25,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"1/v (s/μM)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"No probe","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-0.25,0],[0,0.2],[0.5,0.6],[1,1]]},{"name":"+ Probe","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-0.25,0],[0,0.5],[0.5,1.5],[1,2.5]]}]},"choices":["Noncompetitive; KM same","Competitive; KM rises","Uncompetitive; KM falls","Mixed; KM rises"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Competitive inhibition keeps the y-intercept (Vmax) and moves the x-intercept; here the reverse happened."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Uncompetitive inhibition shifts both intercepts, giving parallel lines; the x-intercept stayed put."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A KM change would move the x-intercept (−1/KM), and it did not move."}],"rule":"Lineweaver–Burk: y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = −1/KM. Only y moves → Vmax down, KM fixed; only x moves → KM changes, Vmax fixed.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"LB plot: y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = −1/KM. y from 0.20 to 0.50 → Vmax 5 → 2 μM/s; x fixed → KM stays 4 μM. Lines cross on the x-axis."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives the intercepts. The **y-intercept (1/Vmax)** rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM, so **Vmax fell from 5 to 2 μM/s**. Meanwhile the x-intercept, which equals −1/KM, held at −0.25 μM⁻¹, so **KM stays 4 μM**. Vmax down with KM unchanged is **noncompetitive** inhibition, as expected when a probe bonds to the active-site cysteine and takes enzyme out of action.","how":{"ask":"Translate each intercept into Vmax or KM, then match the pattern to an inhibitor type.","know":"• Lineweaver–Burk: 1/v = (KM/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax.\n• **y-intercept = 1/Vmax**; **x-intercept = −1/KM**; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Competitive: Vmax same, KM up (lines cross on the y-axis).\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax down, KM same (lines cross on the x-axis).\n• Mixed: Vmax down and KM changed; uncompetitive: both fall (parallel lines).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: which intercept moved and which stayed.","hl":"the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹","calc":["Vmax = 1/0.20 = 5 μM/s → 1/0.50 = **2 μM/s**","KM = 1/0.25 = **4 μM**, both lines"]},{"text":"Vmax fell while KM held: the lines cross on the x-axis.","calc":["Vmax ↓, KM =  → noncompetitive"]},{"text":"Competitive inhibition would keep Vmax and raise KM, the reverse of this plot.","strike":["Competitive; KM rises"]},{"text":"Any KM change, up or down, would move the x-intercept.","strike":["Uncompetitive; KM falls","Mixed; KM rises"]},{"text":"Answer: Noncompetitive; KM same."}],"trap":"Mixing up the axes: the y-intercept is 1/Vmax and the x-intercept is −1/KM, so a rising y-intercept means a lower Vmax, not a higher KM."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and which intercept it belongs to.","marks":[{"t":"y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM","note":"1/Vmax up → Vmax down"},{"t":"x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹","note":"−1/KM fixed → KM fixed"}]},{"text":"**Read the intercepts.**","calc":["Vmax: 5 → 2 μM/s","KM: 4 μM → 4 μM"]},{"text":"**Match the pattern.** Vmax down, KM unchanged: noncompetitive. A probe that bonds covalently to the enzyme acts the same way, by removing working enzyme."},{"text":"**Also:** competitive keeps Vmax and raises KM (lines meet on the y-axis); mixed lowers Vmax and changes KM (lines meet off both axes); uncompetitive lowers both (parallel lines)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a double-reciprocal plot, y-intercept = 1/Vmax and x-intercept = −1/KM; see which one moved, then name the inhibitor type."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-35a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:35#1","pe4-cp:35#2","pe4-cp:35#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:35"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P7","passage":"Enzyme Q, a bacterial acetyltransferase, makes aminoglycoside antibiotics useless by moving an acetyl group onto them from acetyl-CoA. In kinetic studies, acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme Q and CoA was released before any antibiotic bound, and an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine was detected between the two steps. To block enzyme Q, chemists made a probe that contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups; the nitrile groups make the C=C electron-poor, so the cysteine thiol adds to it. In a crystal structure of the complex, the probe's benzene ring sits deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded. Initial rates measured with and without the probe gave a double-reciprocal plot in which the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹.","q":"Which mechanism best fits the kinetic findings for enzyme Q?","choices":["Random sequential","Ordered, acetyl-CoA first","Ordered, antibiotic first","Ping-pong"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A sequential mechanism holds both substrates at once before any product leaves; here CoA left first."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Ordered binding still forms a ternary complex before release; CoA left before the antibiotic bound."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The antibiotic bound only after acetyl-CoA had reacted and CoA had left, so it cannot bind first."}],"rule":"A product leaving before the second substrate binds rules out every sequential path. Otherwise: ordered if only one substrate binds free enzyme, random if either can.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask first: does a product leave before the second substrate binds? Yes (CoA leaves, acetyl-enzyme forms) → ping-pong."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme and **CoA was released before the antibiotic bound**, with an **acetyl-cysteine enzyme intermediate** in between. A product leaving before the second substrate binds, through a modified enzyme, is the **ping-pong** mechanism.","how":{"ask":"Decide sequential vs ping-pong from when the first product leaves.","know":"• **Sequential**: a ternary complex (E·A·B) forms before any product leaves.\n• **Ordered**: one substrate must bind first; **random**: either can.\n• **Ping-pong**: the first product leaves (enzyme modified) before the second substrate binds.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: CoA left before the antibiotic bound, and a covalent acetyl-enzyme formed in between.","hl":"CoA was released before any antibiotic bound"},{"text":"No ternary complex forms, so no sequential mechanism fits.","strike":["Random sequential","Ordered, acetyl-CoA first"]},{"text":"The antibiotic binds only the acetylated enzyme, after CoA leaves.","strike":["Ordered, antibiotic first"]},{"text":"Answer: Ping-pong."}],"trap":"Reading \"acetyl-CoA reacts first\" as ordered sequential: in an ordered mechanism both substrates are bound together before any product leaves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"reacted with free enzyme Q","note":"first substrate alone"},{"t":"CoA was released before any antibiotic bound","note":"product out first"},{"t":"an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine","note":"modified enzyme"}]},{"text":"**Ping-pong:** E + A → F + P, then F + B → E + Q; a product leaves first, often with a covalent enzyme intermediate."},{"text":"**Ordered sequential:** substrate B binds only the E·A complex, never free enzyme. **Random:** E·A and E·B both form; either way, both are bound before release."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** product out before the second substrate = ping-pong; otherwise sequential, ordered if only one substrate binds free enzyme and random if either can."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-36a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:36#1","pe4-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:36"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P7","passage":"Enzyme Q, a bacterial acetyltransferase, makes aminoglycoside antibiotics useless by moving an acetyl group onto them from acetyl-CoA. In kinetic studies, acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme Q and CoA was released before any antibiotic bound, and an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine was detected between the two steps. To block enzyme Q, chemists made a probe that contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups; the nitrile groups make the C=C electron-poor, so the cysteine thiol adds to it. In a crystal structure of the complex, the probe's benzene ring sits deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded. Initial rates measured with and without the probe gave a double-reciprocal plot in which the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹.","q":"The pocket around the probe's benzene ring is most likely lined by which residue?","fig":"cpt-benzoyl-methylene-malononitrile","choices":["Valine","Glutamate","Threonine","Arginine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its side chain is a charged carboxylate, which needs water or a partner charge, not a dry pocket around a ring."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Its side-chain O–H is polar and would rather hydrogen-bond than sit against an all-carbon ring."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Its guanidinium group carries a positive charge, which is costly to bury against a nonpolar ring."}],"rule":"Match the ligand part to the residue: a nonpolar chain or ring in a water-free pocket sits against nonpolar side chains (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Ala, Met).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Benzene ring = all C and H, and no water: pick the only side chain that is pure hydrocarbon."},"exp":"Sentence 4 places the probe's **benzene ring**, all C–H, in a pocket that **excludes water**, so it is held by **hydrophobic (London) contacts** with nonpolar side chains such as **valine**, leucine and phenylalanine. Glutamate and arginine carry charges and threonine has a polar O–H; they belong at water-exposed surfaces.","how":{"ask":"Classify the part of the probe in the pocket, then pick the side chain that suits it in a dry pocket.","know":"• **Nonpolar side chains**: Ala, Val, Leu, Ile, Met, Phe, Trp, Pro.\n• **Polar**: Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln, Tyr, Cys; **charged**: Asp, Glu (−), Lys, Arg, His (+).\n• A hydrocarbon buried away from water makes hydrophobic contacts.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the benzene ring sits in a pocket with no water.","hl":"sits deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded","calc":["nonpolar ring + no water → hydrophobic contacts"]},{"text":"Charged and polar side chains do not suit a buried hydrocarbon ring.","strike":["Glutamate","Arginine","Threonine"]},{"text":"Answer: Valine."}],"trap":"Picking a residue that could react with or hydrogen-bond to another part of the probe: the question asks only about the pocket around the ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"benzene ring","note":"nonpolar"},{"t":"water is excluded","note":"hydrophobic pocket"}]},{"text":"**Like with like.** Nonpolar ligand parts pack against nonpolar side chains; the main force is hydrophobic (London dispersion) contact, and two aromatic rings can stack.","fig":"valine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a nonpolar part of a drug buried in a water-free pocket is lined by nonpolar side chains such as Leu, Ile, Val and Phe."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-37a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:37#1","pe4-cp:37#2","pe4-cp:37#3","pe4-cp:37#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:37"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P7","passage":"Enzyme Q, a bacterial acetyltransferase, makes aminoglycoside antibiotics useless by moving an acetyl group onto them from acetyl-CoA. In kinetic studies, acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme Q and CoA was released before any antibiotic bound, and an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine was detected between the two steps. To block enzyme Q, chemists made a probe that contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups; the nitrile groups make the C=C electron-poor, so the cysteine thiol adds to it. In a crystal structure of the complex, the probe's benzene ring sits deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded. Initial rates measured with and without the probe gave a double-reciprocal plot in which the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹.","q":"How many π bonds does the probe contain?","fig":"cpt-benzoyl-methylene-malononitrile","choices":["5","7","9","11"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Too few: the ring's 3 π plus the C=O and C=C already make 5 before any C≡N is counted."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"7 counts each C≡N as one π bond; a triple bond holds 2 π bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too many: a triple bond is 1 σ + 2 π, so 3 π per C≡N overcounts."}],"rule":"Count π bonds: each double bond 1, each triple bond 2, a benzene ring 3; then add them.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"π bonds = bonds beyond the first: double 1, triple 2, benzene ring 3. 3 + 1 (C=O) + 1 (C=C) + 2 × 2 (two C≡N) = 9."},"exp":"Sentence 3 lists the probe's multiple bonds. Any bonded pair shares one **σ bond**; every extra bond is a **π bond**. So the C=O gives 1 π, the C=C 1 π, each C≡N 2 π, and the benzene ring (three C=C) 3 π: **3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9**.","how":{"ask":"Give each multiple bond its π count, then add group by group.","know":"• **Single bond** = 1 σ, 0 π.\n• **Double bond** (C=O, C=C) = 1 σ + **1 π**.\n• **Triple bond** (C≡N, C≡C) = 1 σ + **2 π**.\n• **Benzene ring** = three C=C = **3 π**.\n• More shared pairs pull atoms closer: a **double bond is shorter than a single bond**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: it lists every group with multiple bonds.","hl":"one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups"},{"text":"The ketone C=O is a double bond: one σ bond holds the atoms, and the second is π.","calc":["C=O = 1 σ + 1 π → 1 π"]},{"text":"Each C≡N is a triple bond: one σ and two π.","calc":["2 × C≡N = 2 × 2 π = 4 π"]},{"text":"The ring has three C=C (3 π), and the extra C=C has 1 π. Add everything.","calc":["3 (ring) + 1 (C=O) + 1 (C=C) + 4 (C≡N) = 9 π"]},{"text":"Counting each C≡N as only 1 π gives 7; also counting the ring as a single π gives 5; counting all three lines of each triple bond as π gives 11.","calc":["3 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 7","1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 5","3 + 1 + 1 + 6 = 11"],"strike":["7","5","11"]},{"text":"**Also:** the π bond adds a second shared pair, so C=O (about 120 pm) is shorter than a C–O single bond (about 140 pm)."},{"text":"Answer: 9."}],"trap":"Counting a triple bond as one π bond: only its first bond is σ, so the other two are both π."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"one benzene ring","note":"three C=C"},{"t":"one ketone C=O","note":"a double bond"},{"t":"one C=C outside the ring","note":"a double bond"},{"t":"two C≡N groups","note":"two triple bonds"}]},{"text":"**σ first, then π.** Between two atoms the first bond is always σ (head-on overlap); every extra bond is π (side-by-side p overlap).","calc":["double = 1 σ + 1 π","triple = 1 σ + 2 π"]},{"text":"**Count by group.** Benzene ring 3 π; each C=O or C=C 1 π; each C≡N or C≡C 2 π. Then add.","calc":["ring 3 + C=O 1 + C=C 1 + C≡N 2 + C≡N 2 = 9"]},{"text":"**Also:** more bonds between the same two atoms means a shorter, stronger link: triple < double < single in length."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count one π per double bond and two per triple bond; a benzene ring has three; add them group by group."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-38a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:38#1","pe4-cp:38#2","pe4-cp:39#1","pe4-cp:5#1","pe4-cp:5#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:38"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"What is the p-nitrophenolate concentration in the well that reads A = 0.81?","choices":["11 μM","45 μM","180 μM","900 μM"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That multiplies A by the path length instead of dividing by it; c = A/(εb)."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That leaves out the 0.25 cm path length, as if the light crossed 1 cm."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That uses ε at 350 nm, but the well was read at 405 nm."}],"rule":"Beer's law: c = A/(εb), with ε for the wavelength actually read and b the real path length in cm.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"εb = 1.8 × 10⁴ × 0.25 = 4.5 × 10³ M⁻¹. c = 0.81 ÷ 4500 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M; × 10⁶ for μM."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives ε at the wavelength read, 405 nm: 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹, and the path, 0.25 cm. Sentence 3 gives A = 0.81. Beer's law: c = A/(εb) = 0.81 ÷ (1.8 × 10⁴ × 0.25) = 0.81 ÷ 4500 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M = **180 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange Beer's law for c, using the ε that matches the wavelength read and the real path length.","know":"• **A = εbc** (ε in M⁻¹cm⁻¹, b in cm, c in M).\n• Use ε at the wavelength actually measured.\n• 1 M = 10⁶ μM.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the reading and its wavelength.","hl":"In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81"},{"text":"Sentence 2 gives ε at that wavelength and the path length.","hl":"ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm","calc":["εb = 1.8 × 10⁴ × 0.25 = 4.5 × 10³ M⁻¹"]},{"text":"Solve for c.","calc":["c = 0.81 ÷ 4500 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M","= **180 μM**"]},{"text":"Multiplying by b, or leaving b out, mishandles the path; the 350 nm ε belongs to a different wavelength.","strike":["11 μM","45 μM","900 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 180 μM."}],"trap":"Grabbing the first ε in the passage: a compound has a different ε at each wavelength, so match ε to the wavelength read."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"at 405 nm, A = 0.81","note":"the reading"},{"t":"1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm","note":"the ε to use"},{"t":"3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm","note":"wrong wavelength"},{"t":"0.25 cm deep","note":"path length b"}]},{"text":"**Rearrange Beer's law.**","calc":["c = A/(εb) = 0.81/(4.5 × 10³ M⁻¹) = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M"]},{"text":"**Also:** an absorbance counts only what differs between sample and reference; a smudged or mismatched well adds its own A, which must be measured and subtracted."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c = A/(εb), with ε for the measured wavelength and b the true path length; convert M to μM at the end."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-39a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:39#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:39"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"How could the most active wells be read within the reader's straight-line range?","choices":["Halve the liquid depth","Double the liquid depth","Concentrate them twofold","Use a brighter lamp"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A deeper liquid lengthens the light path and raises A, pushing the reading further out of range."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Concentrating raises c and so raises A; the reading must come down."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Absorbance is a ratio of light in to light out, so a brighter lamp leaves A unchanged."}],"rule":"A = εbc: to lower A into range, lower c (dilute) or b (shorter path). Lamp brightness does not change A.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"A ∝ b: the depth is the path. Halving 0.25 cm to 0.125 cm halves A: 3.3 → about 1.65, under 1.8."},"exp":"Sentence 4 says the reader is linear only to A = 1.8, but the strongest wells read about 3.3. Sentence 2 says the liquid depth sets the light path, and A = εbc, so **halving the depth halves A** (3.3 → ≈ 1.65), within range. Diluting twofold would work for the same reason; a deeper or more concentrated sample raises A, and a brighter lamp changes nothing.","how":{"ask":"Find a change that lowers A by about half, using A = εbc.","know":"• **A = εbc**: A scales with path length b and concentration c.\n• In a microplate, the liquid depth is the path length.\n• A is a log ratio of intensities, so the lamp's brightness cancels.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the readings are about twice the reader's limit.","hl":"a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3","calc":["3.3 ÷ 1.8 ≈ 1.8: need A roughly halved"]},{"text":"Sentence 2 ties the path to the liquid depth.","hl":"holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path","calc":["b: 0.25 → 0.125 cm → A ≈ 1.65"]},{"text":"More depth or more concentration raises A.","strike":["Double the liquid depth","Concentrate them twofold"]},{"text":"A brighter lamp does not change the ratio that defines A.","strike":["Use a brighter lamp"]},{"text":"Answer: Halve the liquid depth."}],"trap":"Thinking a stronger signal (brighter lamp) helps: the problem is too much absorbance, which only a smaller b or c can fix."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"only up to A = 1.8","note":"the limit"},{"t":"read about 3.3","note":"too high"},{"t":"which sets the light path","note":"depth = b"}]},{"text":"**Use A = εbc.** Lower b or c and A falls in proportion.","calc":["A ∝ b × c"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reading above the detector's linear range is brought down by diluting the sample or shortening the light path."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-40a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:40#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:40"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"At which pH would a HEPES buffer NOT be useful?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of HEPES (acid form) with NaOH","x":{"label":"NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":25,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":13,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"HEPES + NaOH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,4.3],[2,6.55],[5,7.02],[10,7.5],[15,7.98],[18,8.45],[19,8.78],[19.8,9.5],[20,10.2],[20.5,11.3],[22,11.9],[25,12.3]]}]},"choices":["pH 6.7","pH 7.2","pH 8.4","pH 8.9"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"pH 6.7 is 0.8 unit below the pKa, so acid outnumbers base only about 6 : 1, inside the tenfold limit."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"pH 7.2 is close to the pKa, so both forms are present in similar amounts."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"pH 8.4 is 0.9 unit above the pKa, so base outnumbers acid about 8 : 1, still inside the limit."}],"rule":"A buffer works within pKa ± 1, where neither form outnumbers the other more than tenfold. Read the pKa at the half-equivalence point.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Useful window = pKa ± 1 = 6.5 to 8.5. Only one choice falls outside it."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives pKa 7.5 (the half-equivalence point on the curve) and the tenfold rule, which is **pKa ± 1**: from pH 6.5 to 8.5. At pH 8.9, base : acid = 10^1.4 ≈ 25 : 1, more than tenfold, so **pH 8.9** is outside the useful range; the other three lie inside.","how":{"ask":"Turn the tenfold rule into a pH window around the pKa, then find the choice outside it.","know":"• Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]).\n• A ratio between 1 : 10 and 10 : 1 means **pH = pKa ± 1**.\n• On a titration curve, pH = pKa at half-equivalence.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: the pKa and the tenfold limit.","hl":"HEPES (pKa 7.5)","calc":["window = 7.5 ± 1 = 6.5 to 8.5"]},{"text":"Check each choice against the window.","calc":["6.7, 7.2, 8.4: inside","8.9: outside (ratio ≈ 25 : 1)"]},{"text":"These lie within one unit of the pKa.","strike":["pH 6.7","pH 7.2","pH 8.4"]},{"text":"Answer: pH 8.9."}],"trap":"Picking the pH farthest from the assay's working values rather than the one outside pKa ± 1."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 7.5","note":"center of the window"},{"t":"by more than tenfold","note":"± 1 pH unit"}]},{"text":"**Henderson–Hasselbalch.** Each pH unit away from the pKa is a tenfold change in base : acid.","calc":["pH − pKa = log(base/acid)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a buffer is effective within pKa ± 1; outside that, one form outnumbers the other more than tenfold."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:41#1","pe4-cp:41#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:41"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"Which atom of p-nitrophenyl acetate does the serine oxygen attack?","choices":["Carbonyl carbon","Carbonyl oxygen","Bridging ester oxygen","Acetyl CH₃ carbon"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The carbonyl O is δ− and electron-rich, so a nucleophile won't attack it; it takes the electrons as the C=O opens."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The bridging O is electron-rich too; it is the leaving group, departing with the ring as p-nitrophenolate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A CH₃ carbon has no leaving group and no δ+ pull, so it is not an electrophile."}],"rule":"A nucleophile bonds to the most δ+ atom, the one pulled on by two oxygens; oxygens are electron-rich and never the target.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Nucleophiles go for the δ+ atom. In an ester that is the C=O carbon, tugged by two oxygens at once; O atoms donate electrons and a CH₃ is not electrophilic."},"exp":"Sentence 1 says the serine oxygen, made more nucleophilic by the histidine, forms an **acyl-enzyme** and releases p-nitrophenolate. So it bonds to the most **electrophilic** atom, the **carbonyl carbon**, made δ+ by two oxygens. A tetrahedral intermediate forms, the bond to the aryl oxygen breaks, p-nitrophenolate leaves, and the acetyl group sits on the serine.","how":{"ask":"The attacking oxygen has lone pairs; which atom of the ester is electron-poor enough to accept them, leaving the acyl group on the enzyme?","know":"• A nucleophile (Ser O, Cys S, His N, Asp O) attacks an **electrophilic** atom.\n• In an ester R–C(=O)–O–R′, the **carbonyl carbon** is bonded to two O atoms and is strongly δ+.\n• Oxygens are electron-rich: a nucleophile does not attack them.\n• In a phosphate ester the same role falls to **P**, bonded to four O atoms.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 holds the clue: an activated serine oxygen attacks and an acyl-enzyme forms while p-nitrophenolate leaves.","hl":"attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate","fig":"serine-sc"},{"text":"Find the electron-poor atom: the carbonyl carbon, pulled on by two oxygens.","calc":["C=O carbon bonded to 2 O → δ+ → the electrophile","Ser–OH + CH₃C(=O)OAr → Ser–O–C(=O)CH₃ + ArO⁻"]},{"text":"Oxygen atoms are electron-rich, so the nucleophile attacks neither the carbonyl nor the bridging oxygen; the bridging O leaves with the ring.","strike":["Carbonyl oxygen","Bridging ester oxygen"]},{"text":"The CH₃ carbon has no δ+ pull and no leaving group.","strike":["Acetyl CH₃ carbon"]},{"text":"Answer: Carbonyl carbon."}],"trap":"Picking the bridging oxygen because the bond that breaks touches it: the nucleophile forms its new bond to the carbonyl C, and the bridging O leaves with the ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"acyl-enzyme","note":"acyl group bonded to the enzyme"},{"t":"active-site serine","note":"hydroxyl O nucleophile"},{"t":"removed its O–H proton","note":"activated nucleophile"}]},{"text":"**Electrophile = δ+.** In an ester, the carbonyl C is bonded to two electronegative O atoms."},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase does the same at phosphorus: an Asp O or His N attacks the δ+ P of a phosphate ester, the alcohol leaves, and a phospho-enzyme forms.","fig":"histidine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an enzyme nucleophile attacks the most δ+ atom (the carbonyl C of an ester, the P of a phosphate), and the group on the other O leaves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-42a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:42"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"What is [H₃O⁺] in the stopped reactions, and how does it compare with the neutral value?","choices":["1000 μM; above neutral","3 μM; above neutral","1000 μM; below neutral","0.001 μM; above neutral"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That is 10⁻³ M written as μM without the × 10⁶ conversion going the right way."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Lower pH means more H₃O⁺, so an acidified mixture is above the neutral 0.1 μM, not below it."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the pH value as if it were a concentration."}],"rule":"[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M; × 10⁶ for μM. Lower pH → more H₃O⁺ than the 0.1 μM of neutral water.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"pH 3 → 10⁻³ M = 10⁻³ × 10⁶ μM. Neutral water is 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM, ten thousand times less."},"exp":"Sentence 7 gives pH 3 and asks for μM. [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻³ M; × 10⁶ μM/M = **1000 μM**. At 25 °C, neutral water holds only a tenth of a micromolar, so the stopped mixture is **far above neutral** (more acidic).","how":{"ask":"Convert the pH into molar [H₃O⁺], then into μM, and compare with 10⁻⁷ M.","know":"• **[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M**.\n• **M → μM: × 10⁶**.\n• Neutral water at 25 °C: 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 holds the clue: the pH and the unit wanted.","hl":"acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"Convert to μM.","calc":["10⁻³ M × 10⁶ μM/M = 10³ μM"]},{"text":"Dividing instead of multiplying gives a tiny number, and the pH itself is not a concentration.","strike":["0.001 μM; above neutral","3 μM; above neutral"]},{"text":"Lower pH means more H₃O⁺ than neutral water.","strike":["1000 μM; below neutral"]},{"text":"Answer: 1000 μM; above neutral."}],"trap":"Treating a lower pH as less H₃O⁺: pH is a negative log, so pH 3 has ten thousand times more H₃O⁺ than pH 7."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 3","note":"[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻³ M"},{"t":"in micromolar units","note":"× 10⁶"}]},{"text":"**Powers of ten.**","calc":["10⁻³ M = 10⁻³ × 10⁶ μM = 1000 μM","neutral: 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M, then × 10⁶ for μM; anything above 0.1 μM is more acidic than neutral water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-43a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:43#1","pe4-cp:43#2","pe4-cp:43#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:43"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"How do the catalytic efficiency (kcat/KM) and Vmax change when the pH rises from 7.2 to 8.4?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Initial rate vs [S] at 0.80 nM enzyme, from each pH's kcat and KM","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":560,"step":70},"y":{"label":"v₀ (nM/s)","min":0,"max":350,"step":50},"series":[{"name":"pH 7.2","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[35,56],[70,74.7],[140,89.6],[280,99.6],[560,105.4]]},{"name":"pH 8.4","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[35,112],[70,168],[140,224],[280,268.8],[560,298.7]]}]},"choices":["Efficiency ×1.5; Vmax ×3","Efficiency ×6; Vmax ×3","Efficiency ×1.5; Vmax same","Efficiency ÷2; Vmax ×3"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That multiplies by the KM change instead of dividing by it; a larger KM lowers kcat/KM."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Vmax = kcat × [E]; with the same enzyme concentration, tripling kcat triples Vmax."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That divides by the doubled KM but forgets that kcat tripled."}],"rule":"kcat/KM: divide the kcat change by the KM change. Vmax = kcat × [E]total, so at fixed [E] it follows kcat.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"kcat ×3, KM ×2 → kcat/KM ×3/2. [E] fixed → Vmax ×3, like kcat."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives the constants. **kcat/KM**: 140/35 = 4 → 420/70 = 6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹, up **1.5×**. **Vmax = kcat[E]**: with [E] fixed at 0.80 nM, Vmax rises from 112 to 336 nM/s, up **3×**. The chart shows the higher plateau at pH 8.4.","how":{"ask":"Compute kcat/KM at each pH, then use Vmax = kcat[E] at the fixed enzyme concentration.","know":"• **Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM**.\n• **Vmax = kcat × [E]total**.\n• A change in KM alone leaves Vmax unchanged.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: kcat and KM at both pH values, at one enzyme concentration.","hl":"kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4","calc":["kcat/KM: 4 → 6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ (×1.5)"]},{"text":"Vmax follows kcat at fixed [E].","hl":"At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM","calc":["Vmax: 140 × 0.80 = 112 → 420 × 0.80 = 336 nM/s (×3)"]},{"text":"Multiplying by the KM change, or dividing by it alone, misuses kcat/KM.","strike":["Efficiency ×6; Vmax ×3","Efficiency ÷2; Vmax ×3"]},{"text":"Vmax tracks kcat, so it cannot stay the same.","strike":["Efficiency ×1.5; Vmax same"]},{"text":"Answer: Efficiency ×1.5; Vmax ×3."}],"trap":"Treating a higher KM as higher efficiency: KM sits in the denominator of kcat/KM."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"kcat = 140 s⁻¹","note":"turnover at pH 7.2"},{"t":"KM = 70 μM","note":"larger KM at pH 8.4"},{"t":"0.80 nM","note":"[E] fixed"}]},{"text":"**Efficiency.**","calc":["pH 7.2: 140/35 = 4","pH 8.4: 420/70 = 6"]},{"text":"**Vmax.**","calc":["112 → 336 nM/s"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** kcat/KM changes by (kcat factor ÷ KM factor), and Vmax changes with kcat when [E] is fixed."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-44a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:44#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:44"],"q":"A 6.0 g cork floats on a pond, partly submerged, pushing aside 6.0 cm³ of water (1 g/cm³). Which property of water mainly holds the cork up?","choices":["Buoyancy","Surface tension","Viscosity","Capillary action"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Surface tension can hold only a few milligrams on a dimpled surface; this object sank in until it displaced a lot of water."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Viscosity resists flow and motion through a fluid; it supplies no upward force on an object at rest."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Capillary action lifts water up narrow tubes by adhesion; it does not hold a floating object up."}],"rule":"Compare the weight of displaced water with the object's weight: if they match, Archimedes' upward force does the job; if almost nothing is displaced, surface tension does.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Displaced mass = 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = 6.0 g, equal to the cork's mass: the buoyant force alone balances its weight."},"exp":"The displaced water has mass 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = **6.0 g**, the same as the cork. By Archimedes' principle the upward **buoyant force equals the weight of the displaced water**, so buoyancy alone balances the cork's weight. Surface tension matters only for very light objects, such as a water strider, whose legs dimple the surface while displacing almost no water.","how":{"ask":"Decide which property of water supplies the upward force, using the displaced volume as the clue.","know":"• **Buoyancy**: F_b = ρ_water × V_displaced × g (Archimedes).\n• **Surface tension**: cohesion among surface water molecules; supports only tiny weights on a dimpled surface.\n• Viscosity resists flow; capillary action lifts water in narrow tubes.","steps":[{"text":"Weigh the displaced water.","hl":"pushing aside 6.0 cm³ of water","calc":["m = 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = 6.0 g"]},{"text":"Compare with the cork.","hl":"A 6.0 g cork","calc":["6.0 g displaced = 6.0 g cork → buoyant force = weight"]},{"text":"Surface tension supports only tiny weights that displace almost nothing; viscosity and capillarity give no upward support.","strike":["Surface tension","Viscosity","Capillary action"]},{"text":"Answer: Buoyancy."}],"trap":"Seeing \"floats on water\" and thinking surface tension: check how much water the object displaces first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"partly submerged","note":"it sank in and displaced water"},{"t":"6.0 cm³","note":"displaced volume"},{"t":"1 g/cm³","note":"turns volume into mass"}]},{"text":"**Contrast:** a 10 mg insect whose legs displace a fraction of a cubic millimetre is held up by **surface tension**, the cohesion between water molecules at the surface; buoyancy there is far too small."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** displaced weight equal to the object's weight → buoyancy; displaced weight negligible and the surface dimpled → surface tension."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-45a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:45#1","pe4-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:45"],"q":"Fluorine-18 has a half-life of 110 min. A PET tracer dose made at 8:00 a.m. contains 12 mg of ¹⁸F at 3:20 p.m. the same day. How much ¹⁸F did it contain when made?","choices":["0.75 mg","48 mg","192 mg","384 mg"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dividing by 16 runs the decay forward; going back in time, the amount must be larger, not smaller."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Multiplying by the number of half-lives treats decay as linear; each half-life halves the amount, so use 2ⁿ."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That doubles once too often; 8:00 a.m. to 3:20 p.m. is 440 min, exactly four half-lives, not five."}],"rule":"n = elapsed time ÷ half-life; original = remaining × 2ⁿ. Count the elapsed time carefully across a.m./p.m.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"8:00 → 3:20 is 7 h 20 min = 440 min; 440 ÷ 110 = 4 half-lives. Original = 12 mg × 2⁴ = 12 × 16."},"exp":"From 8:00 a.m. to 3:20 p.m. is **440 min**, and 440 ÷ 110 = **4 half-lives**. Each half-life halves the amount, so the original was the remaining amount doubled four times: 12 mg × 2⁴ = **192 mg**.","how":{"ask":"Count the half-lives between the two times, then double the remaining amount that many times.","know":"• **n = t ÷ t½**.\n• Remaining = original ÷ 2ⁿ, so **original = remaining × 2ⁿ**.","steps":[{"text":"Elapsed time.","hl":"made at 8:00 a.m. contains 12 mg of ¹⁸F at 3:20 p.m.","calc":["8:00 a.m. → 3:20 p.m. = 7 h 20 min = 440 min"]},{"text":"Number of half-lives.","hl":"half-life of 110 min","calc":["n = 440 ÷ 110 = 4"]},{"text":"Work back in time.","calc":["12 mg × 2⁴ = 12 × 16 = **192 mg**"]},{"text":"Dividing goes the wrong way in time; ×4 is linear decay; ×32 counts five half-lives.","strike":["0.75 mg","48 mg","384 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 192 mg."}],"trap":"Multiplying by n instead of 2ⁿ: radioactive decay halves the amount each period; it does not lose a fixed amount."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"half-life of 110 min","note":"t½"},{"t":"8:00 a.m.","note":"start"},{"t":"3:20 p.m.","note":"end: 440 min later"},{"t":"contains 12 mg","note":"what is left"}]},{"text":"**Doubling ladder back in time:** 12 → 24 → 48 → 96 → 192 mg (four steps)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** original = remaining × 2^(t/t½); always check that the answer is larger than what remains."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-46a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:46"],"q":"Viewed from the side in white light, a 7 mm pencil standing in a glass of water (n = 1.33) looks broken at the water line, with no colored fringes. What causes this?","choices":["Refraction","Diffraction","Dispersion","Polarization"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Diffraction shows only for objects near the light's wavelength; a 7 mm pencil is about 10⁴ times bigger than visible λ."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Dispersion spreads white light into colors, but the stem says the image shows no colored fringes."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Polarization concerns the orientation of the light's field; it does not bend or shift an image."}],"rule":"An image bent at a surface between two media comes from light changing speed; colored fringes mean dispersion, wavelength-sized gaps mean diffraction.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Image bent where light crosses a boundary, no color: refraction (light slows in water). Color fringes → dispersion; slit-sized openings → diffraction; filters/glare → polarization."},"exp":"Light from the underwater part of the pencil **changes speed as it crosses the water–air surface** (v = c/n, slower in water) and bends away from the normal as it exits, so that part appears shifted. Bending at a boundary between two media is **refraction**, described by Snell's law, n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂.","how":{"ask":"Is the light crossing into a new medium, or bending around an edge close to its wavelength in size?","know":"• **Refraction**: light changes speed at a boundary between media (v = c/n) and bends: n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂.\n• **Diffraction**: bending around an edge or through a gap, strong only when its size is near the wavelength (visible λ ≈ 0.4–0.7 μm).\n• **Dispersion**: n varies with λ, so white light spreads into colors.\n• **Interference**: overlapping waves add (bright) or cancel (dark), making fringes.","steps":[{"text":"The light leaving the underwater pencil crosses from water into air, a change of medium, so its speed and direction change.","hl":"looks broken at the water line","calc":["v in water = c/n = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 1.33 ≈ 2.3 × 10⁸ m/s","faster in air → bends away from the normal at the surface"]},{"text":"Compare the pencil with the wavelength: diffraction is noticeable only for objects near λ in size.","hl":"7 mm pencil","calc":["visible λ ≈ 0.5 μm","7 mm ÷ 0.5 μm = 14,000 wavelengths → diffraction negligible"],"strike":["Diffraction"]},{"text":"Dispersion would fringe the image with colors, which the stem rules out; polarization changes the field's orientation, not the light's direction.","hl":"no colored fringes","strike":["Dispersion","Polarization"]},{"text":"Answer: refraction."}],"trap":"Choosing diffraction because the light bends: bending around an edge needs an object near the wavelength, while bending at a boundary between media is refraction."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"glass of water (n = 1.33)","note":"a second medium"},{"t":"7 mm pencil","note":"far larger than λ"},{"t":"broken at the water line","note":"image shifted at the boundary"},{"t":"no colored fringes","note":"not dispersion"}]},{"text":"**New medium → speed change → bending.** That is refraction, with Snell's law n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂."},{"text":"**Size versus wavelength.** Diffraction matters only when an object or gap is near λ: bright and dark rings around 1 μm specks in green laser light are diffraction, not refraction.","calc":["7 mm ≫ 0.5 μm → refraction dominates","1 μm ≈ 2 × 0.532 μm → diffraction rings"]},{"text":"**Also:** bright and dark fringes form by interference: waves in step add (bright), and waves half a wavelength out of step cancel (dark)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** bending where light enters a new medium is refraction (Snell's law); bending around an obstacle near λ in size is diffraction; colors split because n varies with λ is dispersion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-47a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:47#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:47"],"q":"Saturated fatty acids of molar mass 144, 172, 312 and 340 g/mol are compared. Which dissolves best in water at room temperature?","choices":["C₇H₁₅COOH","C₉H₁₉COOH","C₁₉H₃₉COOH","C₂₁H₄₃COOH"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"The longest tail is the most hydrophobic, so it is the least soluble of the four, the opposite of what is asked."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A twenty-carbon chain is almost all nonpolar surface, so it ranks near the bottom for water solubility."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its chain is two carbons longer than another choice's, so it is more hydrophobic and less soluble."}],"rule":"All saturated fatty acids share a single polar –COOH group; the shorter the hydrocarbon tail (smaller molar mass), the more water-soluble it is.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same head group, so solubility tracks tail length: pick the smallest molar mass, which has the fewest CH₂ groups."},"exp":"Each acid has the same polar **–COOH head**; they differ only in the length of the **nonpolar tail**. The longer the tail, the more hydrophobic the molecule and the less it dissolves. The 144 g/mol acid, **C₇H₁₅COOH** (octanoic acid, eight carbons), has the shortest tail and so the greatest water solubility.","how":{"ask":"Rank the acids by hydrocarbon chain length and pick the shortest.","know":"• Saturated fatty acid: CH₃(CH₂)ₙCOOH, one polar head.\n• Each extra CH₂ adds nonpolar surface → lower water solubility.\n• Molar mass rises with chain length.","steps":[{"text":"Match each formula to its chain length.","calc":["C₇H₁₅COOH: 8 C, 144 g/mol","C₉H₁₉COOH: 10 C, 172 g/mol","C₁₉H₃₉COOH: 20 C, 312 g/mol","C₂₁H₄₃COOH: 22 C, 340 g/mol"]},{"text":"The long chains are the most hydrophobic.","strike":["C₂₁H₄₃COOH","C₁₉H₃₉COOH"]},{"text":"Ten carbons is longer than eight.","strike":["C₉H₁₉COOH"]},{"text":"Answer: C₇H₁₅COOH."}],"trap":"Thinking a bigger molecule has more polar contacts: the head group is the same in all four, so extra size is all nonpolar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Saturated fatty acids","note":"same head, different tails"},{"t":"dissolves best in water","note":"least hydrophobic"}]},{"text":"**Head vs tail.** The –COOH head H-bonds with water; the tail does not.","fig":"palmitic-acid"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** among molecules with the same polar group, the one with the shortest hydrocarbon chain is the most water-soluble."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-48a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:48#1","pe4-cp:48#2","pe4-cp:51#1","pe4-cp:51#2","pe4-cp:51#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:48"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"Which form of guanine predominates in DNA under physiological conditions and makes the normal pair with cytosine?","fig":"guanine","choices":["Keto–amino form","Enol–amino form","Keto–imino form","Enol–imino form"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Moving the N1 hydrogen onto O6 gives the rare enol the passage blames for mispairing; it is a minor form."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Shifting an amino H onto a ring N gives a rare imino form, which also mispairs."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Two rare shifts at once give an even rarer form; it is not what sits in DNA."}],"rule":"Under physiological conditions G, T and U sit as keto (C=O, ring N–H) and A, C as amino (–NH₂). Enol and imino tautomers are rare and cause mispairing.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Default forms: C=O stays C=O (keto), –NH₂ stays –NH₂ (amino). Any choice with \"enol\" or \"imino\" is a rare, mutagenic form."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says G mispairs only when it **briefly** takes a **rare enol** form, so the normal form is the other one. At physiological pH guanine is almost entirely the **keto–amino** tautomer: C6=O, N1–H and a 2-NH₂ group. That face (acceptor, donor, donor) pairs with cytosine through **three hydrogen bonds**. Enol and imino forms swap a donor for an acceptor and mispair.","how":{"ask":"Pick the tautomer that dominates at physiological pH and gives standard pairing.","know":"• G, T and U are mainly **keto** (lactam: C=O with ring N–H); A and C are mainly **amino**.\n• **Enol** (C–OH) and **imino** (C=NH) forms are rare and change the donor/acceptor pattern → mispairing.\n• G·C: 3 H-bonds; A·T: 2.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the enol is the rare, brief form that mispairs.","hl":"only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer"},{"text":"The normal form keeps C6=O and the 2-NH₂ group.","calc":["keto at O6, amino at C2 → G·C with 3 H-bonds"]},{"text":"Any enol or imino shift is a rare, mutagenic form.","strike":["Enol–amino form","Keto–imino form","Enol–imino form"]},{"text":"Answer: Keto–amino form."}],"trap":"Thinking the enol is favored because it looks aromatic: in nucleobases the keto (lactam) form dominates."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"rare enol tautomer","note":"minor form, mutagenic"},{"t":"misinserts T opposite G","note":"mispairing"},{"t":"hydrogen-bonding faces","note":"donor/acceptor pattern"}]},{"text":"**Keto wins.** G, T and U sit mainly as keto (C=O, ring N–H); A and C as amino.","fig":"guanine"},{"text":"**Count the H-bonds pair by pair.** An H on N or O facing a lone-pair N or O is one hydrogen bond: keto G with C gives 3; A with T gives 2.","fig":"cytosine"},{"text":"**Also:** a rare enol tautomer mispairs. Enol guanine has O6–H (donor) and N1 (acceptor) in place of C6=O and N1–H, so its face changes and the H-bond count in a drawn pair changes with it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** G, T and U are mainly keto at physiological pH and A, C mainly amino; rare enol or imino tautomers mispair."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-49a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:49#1","pe4-cp:49#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:49"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"When a ³H atom in a labeled strand decays, what element takes its place, and how do its proton count Z and neutron count N change?","choices":["Helium; Z +1, N −1","Helium; Z +1, A +1","Hydrogen; Z same, N −1","Lithium; Z +2, N −2"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A β⁻ particle is an electron, with almost no mass, so the mass number A stays the same."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"β⁻ decay turns a neutron into a proton, so Z rises and the element changes."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"One β⁻ decay converts only one neutron, so Z rises by 1, not 2."}],"rule":"β⁻ decay: a neutron becomes a proton, Z + 1, N − 1, A unchanged; the atom becomes the next element up.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"β⁻: n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino. Z goes up by one (H → He), N down by one, A stays 3."},"exp":"Sentence 6 says tritium decays by **β⁻ emission**: a neutron turns into a proton and an electron, which leaves with an antineutrino. **Z rises by 1** (hydrogen, Z = 1, becomes helium, Z = 2), **N falls by 1** (2 → 1), and the mass number stays 3: ³H → ³He.","how":{"ask":"Apply the β⁻ change to Z, N and A, then name the new element.","know":"• **β⁻ decay**: n → p⁺ + e⁻ + antineutrino.\n• Z + 1, N − 1, A unchanged.\n• **β⁺ decay / electron capture**: Z − 1, N + 1.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: the label is tritium, and it decays by β⁻ emission.","hl":"tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission"},{"text":"β⁻ turns one neutron into one proton.","calc":["³H: Z = 1, N = 2","→ Z = 2, N = 1, A = 3: ³He"]},{"text":"A stays 3, and the element must change.","strike":["Helium; Z +1, A +1","Hydrogen; Z same, N −1"]},{"text":"One decay changes one neutron, not two.","strike":["Lithium; Z +2, N −2"]},{"text":"Answer: Helium; Z +1, N −1."}],"trap":"Treating β⁻ like α decay or losing mass: the electron carries almost no mass, so only Z and N trade by one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"tritium (³H)","note":"Z = 1, A = 3"},{"t":"β⁻ emission","note":"n → p + e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Balance the decay.**","calc":["³₁H → ³₂He + ⁰₋₁e + antineutrino"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** β⁻ decay raises Z by 1 and lowers N by 1 at constant A, so the atom becomes the next element in the periodic table."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-50a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:50#1","pe4-cp:50#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:50"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"Which change to one of the two active-site aspartates would most likely keep the polymerase working?","choices":["Asp → Glu","Asp → Asn","Asp → Lys","Asp → Phe"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Asparagine has an uncharged amide in place of the carboxylate, so it cannot hold a Mg²⁺ ion as well."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine is positive at pH 7; it would repel the Mg²⁺, not hold it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phe is nonpolar and uncharged, so the bond to Mg²⁺ is lost, as with the alanine mutant."}],"rule":"To keep a metal-binding or ion-pairing residue working, keep its charge: replace an acidic residue with another acidic one.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Keep the carboxylate to keep the Mg²⁺. Asp and Glu are the two acidic (–COO⁻) residues; Asn drops the charge, Lys reverses it, Phe removes all polarity."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says the aspartates hold Mg²⁺ through their **side-chain carboxylates**, and losing that group (alanine) abolishes activity. Glutamate keeps a –COO⁻, one CH₂ longer, so it is a **conservative substitution**. Asparagine is uncharged, phenylalanine nonpolar, and lysine positive, so none can hold the metal.","how":{"ask":"What feature of the aspartate side chain does the job, and which replacement keeps that feature?","know":"• **Conservative substitution**: replace a residue with one of similar chemistry (charge, polarity, size), usually keeping function.\n• At pH 7: Asp and Glu are −1 (carboxylates); Lys and Arg are +1; Asn is neutral; Phe is nonpolar.\n• Asp and Glu differ only by one CH₂ in the side chain.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the carboxylates hold the Mg²⁺, and removing them abolishes activity.","hl":"the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions","fig":"aspartate"},{"text":"Glutamate also ends in a carboxylate, so the metal stays bound.","fig":"glutamate"},{"text":"Asparagine and phenylalanine have no charge, so the metal is lost.","strike":["Asp → Asn","Asp → Phe"]},{"text":"Lysine is positive at pH 7 and would repel the Mg²⁺.","strike":["Asp → Lys"]},{"text":"Answer: Asp → Glu."}],"trap":"Choosing Asn because it looks almost the same: an amide is uncharged, and the carboxylate charge is what holds the metal."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"side-chain carboxylates","note":"negative groups"},{"t":"Mg²⁺ ions","note":"positive partner"},{"t":"replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity","note":"the charge is essential"}]},{"text":"**Keep the chemistry.** Glu is the only standard residue besides Asp with a side-chain carboxylate.","calc":["Asp: –CH₂–COO⁻","Glu: –CH₂–CH₂–COO⁻"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to keep an Asp's function, swap it for Glu, since both carry a carboxylate; Asn, Lys or Phe lose the negative charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-51a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:51#1","pe4-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:51"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"Counting every base pair, how many hydrogen bonds does the template duplex contain?","choices":["366","384","450","768"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That gives A·T three bonds and G·C two; it is the other way round."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That counts each bond twice, once per base; each hydrogen bond joins one pair."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"That gives every pair three bonds; A·T pairs hold only two."}],"rule":"Use Chargaff: %A = %T and %G = %C. Convert the % of bases to numbers of pairs, then A·T × 2 + G·C × 3.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"150 pairs = 300 bases. A = 22% → 66 A·T pairs; G = 28% → 84 G·C pairs. 66 × 2 + 84 × 3 = 132 + 252."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives a 150-bp duplex (300 bases) with A = 22%. Chargaff: T = 22%, so G = C = 28%. That is **66 A·T pairs** and **84 G·C pairs**. Each A·T pair has 2 hydrogen bonds and each G·C pair 3: 66 × 2 + 84 × 3 = 132 + 252 = **384**.","how":{"ask":"Convert the base composition into numbers of A·T and G·C pairs, then add their hydrogen bonds.","know":"• **Chargaff**: A = T and G = C in a duplex.\n• **A·T: 2 H-bonds; G·C: 3 H-bonds.**\n• A duplex of n base pairs has 2n bases.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: the length in base pairs and the share of adenine.","hl":"150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases","calc":["bases = 2 × 150 = 300","A = T = 22% → 66 each","G = C = 28% → 84 each"]},{"text":"Each A pairs with a T, each G with a C.","calc":["66 A·T pairs, 84 G·C pairs"]},{"text":"Add the hydrogen bonds.","calc":["66 × 2 + 84 × 3 = 132 + 252"]},{"text":"Swapping 2 and 3, doubling the count, or giving every pair 3 bonds all miss.","strike":["366","768","450"]},{"text":"Answer: 384."}],"trap":"Applying the percentage to base pairs instead of bases: 22% of all bases is 66 adenines, so 66 A·T pairs."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"150 base pairs","note":"300 bases"},{"t":"22% of all bases","note":"A, so T too"},{"t":"every base in a Watson–Crick pair","note":"Chargaff holds"}]},{"text":"**Chargaff.**","calc":["A + T = 44% → G + C = 56% → G = 28%"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** H-bonds = 2 × (A·T pairs) + 3 × (G·C pairs), with pairs counted from the bases by Chargaff's rule."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-52a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:52"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"Which metal could NOT readily replace Mg²⁺ in the polymerase, and why?","choices":["Sr, ion too large","Ni, d⁸ unstable","Cd, full 4d","Rb, 5s¹ only"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing its two 5s electrons gives a stable 2+ ion with a noble-gas core; size does not stop it from forming."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"It loses its two 4s electrons easily to form a common, stable 2+ ion; partly filled d subshells are normal."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A full d subshell is stable; the two 5s electrons come off to give a common 2+ ion."}],"rule":"Count the valence electrons that come off easily: s² metals and most transition metals form 2+; an s¹ metal forms only 1+.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look at the outer s subshell: s² → 2+ is easy; s¹ (an alkali metal) gives only 1+."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives the configurations. Ni, Sr and Cd each have **two outer s electrons** that come off easily, giving common **2+ ions** like Mg²⁺. Rb has a single 5s electron: removing it leaves a noble-gas core, and a second electron would have to come from that core at a huge energy cost. So **Rb forms only Rb⁺** and cannot stand in for Mg²⁺.","how":{"ask":"For each metal, decide whether it readily forms a 2+ ion, from its valence configuration.","know":"• Group 1 (s¹): forms only **1+**.\n• Group 2 (s²): forms **2+**.\n• Transition metals lose their outer s electrons first, so 2+ is common (Ni²⁺, Cd²⁺, Zn²⁺, Fe²⁺).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: each metal's outer subshell.","hl":"Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹","calc":["Ni: 4s² → Ni²⁺","Sr: 5s² → Sr²⁺","Cd: 5s² → Cd²⁺","Rb: 5s¹ → Rb⁺ only"]},{"text":"These three each lose two s electrons easily; size, d⁸ and a full d subshell do not block a 2+ ion.","strike":["Sr, ion too large","Ni, d⁸ unstable","Cd, full 4d"]},{"text":"Answer: Rb, 5s¹ only."}],"trap":"Rejecting a transition metal because of its d electrons: the d count does not stop a metal from losing its two outer s electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"Mg²⁺ ions","note":"need a 2+ ion"},{"t":"Rb, [Kr]5s¹","note":"one valence electron"}]},{"text":"**Why not Rb²⁺:** the second ionization energy of an alkali metal is enormous, because it would break into a filled shell."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a metal can stand in for a divalent ion only if it readily forms 2+; alkali metals (s¹, like Na, K, Rb) form only 1+."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-53a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:53#1","pe4-cp:53#2","pe4-cp:53#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:53"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P10","passage":"A portable spot welder joins thin steel sheets with a short, intense pulse of current. Its 14.4 V lithium-ion pack, rated 3.5 A·h, charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV through a boost circuit. When the operator presses the trigger, a spring-loaded copper electrode arm with a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s under a nearly constant net force, and the bank then discharges through the joint. To give trainees a sense of scale, the manual compares the energy stored in the charged bank with the energy needed to lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²). The control board sits inside a closed steel enclosure, which, near the heavy welding cables, is exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C. Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal or the cavity.","q":"How high must the toolbox be lifted to store as much energy as the charged capacitor bank?","choices":["2.0 m","4.1 m","2.0 × 10³ m","2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Reads μF as mF: a small capacitor bank cannot lift a toolbox 2 km; C must be in farads (× 10⁻⁶)."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Reads kV as V: using 3.0 V instead of 3000 V shrinks V² by 10⁶."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Dropped the ½: CV² alone is twice the stored energy, so the height comes out doubled."}],"rule":"U = ½CV² with C in F and V in V, then h = U ÷ (mg). Handle the powers of ten separately from the digits.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"U = ½ × 80 × 10⁻⁶ × (3.0 × 10³)² = 40 × 10⁻⁶ × 9 × 10⁶ = 360 J. h = 360 ÷ (18 × 9.8) ≈ 360 ÷ 176 ≈ 2 m."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives C = 80 μF = 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ F and V = 3.0 kV = 3.0 × 10³ V, so **U = ½CV²** = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 9.0 × 10⁶ = **360 J**. Sentence 4 sets this equal to **mgh** for 18 kg: h = 360 ÷ (18 × 9.8) ≈ **2.0 m**.","how":{"ask":"Compute the capacitor's stored energy, then the height that gives the toolbox the same potential energy.","know":"• **Capacitor energy: U = ½CV²** (C in farads, V in volts).\n• **Gravitational potential energy: U = mgh**, so h = U/(mg).\n• Prefixes: **μF = 10⁻⁶ F**, **kV = 10³ V**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the bank's capacitance and voltage.","hl":"charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV","calc":["C = 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ F","V = 3.0 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"Compute U = ½CV², squaring the exponent too.","calc":["V² = 9.0 × 10⁶ V²","U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 9.0 × 10⁶ = **360 J**"]},{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the mass and g; set U = mgh.","hl":"lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²)","calc":["h = 360 ÷ (18 × 9.8) = 360 ÷ 176.4 ≈ 2.04 m"]},{"text":"Prefix slips put the height off by 10³ or 10⁶ the wrong way.","strike":["2.0 × 10³ m","2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"Leaving out the ½ doubles the energy and the height.","strike":["4.1 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.0 m."}],"trap":"Forgetting to square the power of ten in V²: (3.0 × 10³)² is 9.0 × 10⁶, not 9.0 × 10³."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"80 μF","note":"C, × 10⁻⁶ F"},{"t":"3.0 kV","note":"V, × 10³ V"},{"t":"18 kg","note":"mass to lift"},{"t":"g = 9.8 m/s²","note":"gravity"}]},{"text":"**Convert the prefixes.** Micro is 10⁻⁶ and kilo is 10³."},{"text":"**Capacitor energy:** U = ½CV². Square the voltage, exponent included.","calc":["U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 9.0 × 10⁶ = 360 J"]},{"text":"**Equal energies:** set ½CV² = mgh and solve for h.","calc":["h = 360 J ÷ 176.4 N ≈ 2.0 m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to match a capacitor's energy with a lifted mass, convert to F and V, compute U = ½CV², then h = U/(mg)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-54a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:54#1","pe4-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:54#3","pe6-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:54"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P10","passage":"A portable spot welder joins thin steel sheets with a short, intense pulse of current. Its 14.4 V lithium-ion pack, rated 3.5 A·h, charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV through a boost circuit. When the operator presses the trigger, a spring-loaded copper electrode arm with a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s under a nearly constant net force, and the bank then discharges through the joint. To give trainees a sense of scale, the manual compares the energy stored in the charged bank with the energy needed to lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²). The control board sits inside a closed steel enclosure, which, near the heavy welding cables, is exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C. Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal or the cavity.","q":"Approximately how much charge can the fully charged pack deliver on a full discharge?","choices":["3.5 C","210 C","1.3 × 10⁴ C","1.8 × 10⁵ C"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"3.5 is in A·h, not C; an hour is thousands of seconds, so Q must be thousands of coulombs."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Uses 60 s per hour; an hour is 3600 s, sixty times more."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Multiplying by the voltage gives an energy in joules, not a charge; the voltage is not needed."}],"rule":"Q = I × t with t in seconds: 1 A·h = 3600 C. Ignore the voltage when the question asks for charge.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"1 A·h = 3600 C. 3.5 × 3600 = 12,600 C ≈ 1.3 × 10⁴ C."},"exp":"Sentence 2 rates the pack at **3.5 A·h**, a current × time, which is a charge. Since 1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C, Q = 3.5 × 3600 = 12,600 C ≈ **1.3 × 10⁴ C**. The 14.4 V would turn this into an energy (QV), not a charge.","how":{"ask":"Convert the amp-hour rating into coulombs.","know":"• **Q = I·t** (C = A × s).\n• **1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C**.\n• Voltage × charge = energy (J), not charge.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the pack's amp-hour rating.","hl":"rated 3.5 A·h"},{"text":"Convert hours to seconds.","calc":["Q = 3.5 A × 3600 s = 12,600 C"]},{"text":"Leaving A·h unconverted, or using 60 s per hour, undercounts the seconds.","strike":["3.5 C","210 C"]},{"text":"Multiplying by 14.4 V gives joules, not coulombs.","hl":"14.4 V","strike":["1.8 × 10⁵ C"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.3 × 10⁴ C."}],"trap":"Bringing in the voltage: the charge rating does not depend on it; volts only matter for energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"3.5 A·h","note":"a charge rating"},{"t":"14.4 V","note":"not needed for charge"}]},{"text":"**Charge = current × time.** An amp-hour already is a charge.","calc":["1 A·h = 3600 C"]},{"text":"**Also:** the voltage matters only for energy (E = QV): 12,600 C × 14.4 V ≈ 1.8 × 10⁵ J."},{"text":"**Also:** when an electrode material is rated per gram, multiply by its mass in grams to get the capacity, then convert: 1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = I·t, and 1 A·h = 3600 C; ignore the voltage when the question asks for charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-55a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:55#1","pe4-cp:55#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:55"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P10","passage":"A portable spot welder joins thin steel sheets with a short, intense pulse of current. Its 14.4 V lithium-ion pack, rated 3.5 A·h, charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV through a boost circuit. When the operator presses the trigger, a spring-loaded copper electrode arm with a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s under a nearly constant net force, and the bank then discharges through the joint. To give trainees a sense of scale, the manual compares the energy stored in the charged bank with the energy needed to lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²). The control board sits inside a closed steel enclosure, which, near the heavy welding cables, is exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C. Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal or the cavity.","q":"While the electrode arm speeds up, what is the average net force on it?","choices":["8.4 N","9.8 N","840 N","8.4 × 10³ N"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Leaves the mass in grams; 350 g is 0.350 kg, a factor of 1000 smaller."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Leaves the speeds in cm/s; convert to m/s (÷ 100) first."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses the final speed instead of the change; subtract the starting speed first."}],"rule":"F = m·Δv ÷ Δt with m in kg and v in m/s. Convert grams and cm/s first.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Δv = 140 − 20 = 120 cm/s = 1.2 m/s. F = 0.35 kg × 1.2 m/s ÷ 0.050 s = 0.42 ÷ 0.05."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives m = 350 g = **0.350 kg** and speeds from 20 to 140 cm/s, so Δv = 120 cm/s = **1.2 m/s**, over Δt = 0.050 s. F = mΔv/Δt = 0.350 × 1.2 ÷ 0.050 = **8.4 N**.","how":{"ask":"Convert to SI, find the change in velocity, then divide momentum change by time.","know":"• **F = ma = mΔv/Δt** (N = kg·m/s²).\n• **1 g = 10⁻³ kg; 1 cm/s = 10⁻² m/s**: convert before multiplying.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the arm's mass, its two speeds and the time.","hl":"a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s","calc":["m = 0.350 kg","Δv = 1.40 − 0.20 = 1.2 m/s"]},{"text":"Divide the momentum change by the time.","calc":["F = 0.350 × 1.2 ÷ 0.050 = **8.4 N**"]},{"text":"Grams or cm/s left unconverted inflate F by 10³ or 10².","strike":["8.4 × 10³ N","840 N"]},{"text":"Using 1.4 m/s, the final speed, skips the subtraction.","strike":["9.8 N"]},{"text":"Answer: 8.4 N."}],"trap":"Using the final speed as Δv: the arm was already moving at 20 cm/s, so subtract it first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"350 g","note":"m, → kg"},{"t":"from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s","note":"Δv, → m/s"},{"t":"in 0.050 s","note":"Δt"},{"t":"nearly constant net force","note":"average = constant"}]},{"text":"**Average force = mass × velocity change ÷ time.**","calc":["F = mΔv/Δt"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert to SI first: g → kg (÷ 1000) and cm/s → m/s (÷ 100)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** F = mΔv/Δt with mass in kg and speeds in m/s."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:56"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P10","passage":"A portable spot welder joins thin steel sheets with a short, intense pulse of current. Its 14.4 V lithium-ion pack, rated 3.5 A·h, charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV through a boost circuit. When the operator presses the trigger, a spring-loaded copper electrode arm with a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s under a nearly constant net force, and the bank then discharges through the joint. To give trainees a sense of scale, the manual compares the energy stored in the charged bank with the energy needed to lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²). The control board sits inside a closed steel enclosure, which, near the heavy welding cables, is exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C. Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal or the cavity.","q":"At equilibrium, the field inside the steel enclosure is zero because:","choices":["free electrons redistribute.","the steel absorbs it.","its electrons are all bound.","the box has no net charge."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Metals do not absorb static fields; the field inside is cancelled, not soaked up."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Steel is a metal, so it has free electrons; the passage even says they move before settling."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A neutral box still has free charges that separate; zero net charge does not cancel an outside field by itself."}],"rule":"Inside a conductor at equilibrium E = 0: free charges shift to its surfaces until their own field cancels the applied one.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Any field left inside would push free electrons, so equilibrium (charges at rest) means their surface charge has cancelled it."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives the clue: the free electrons **move, then stop**. In the external field (sentence 5) they drift to the enclosure's surfaces, leaving one side negative and the other positive, and that surface charge makes a field that **cancels** the applied one inside. Once the charges are at rest (electrostatic equilibrium), E inside must be zero; otherwise they would still be moving.","how":{"ask":"Explain why charges at rest in a conductor imply no field inside it.","know":"• A conductor has **free electrons** that move whenever a field acts on them.\n• They pile up on the **surface**, and the field they make opposes the applied one.\n• **Electrostatic equilibrium**: charges stopped, so E inside = 0 and any excess charge sits on the surface.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: the free electrons move and then stop.","hl":"Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving"},{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the applied field that set them moving.","hl":"exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C","calc":["electrons drift → surface charges → opposing field"]},{"text":"Static fields are cancelled, not absorbed, and a metal has free electrons.","strike":["the steel absorbs it.","its electrons are all bound."]},{"text":"Zero net charge alone does not cancel an outside field; the charges must separate.","strike":["the box has no net charge."]},{"text":"Answer: free electrons redistribute."}],"trap":"Thinking the metal blocks or absorbs the field: the field inside is cancelled by the conductor's own rearranged charges."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"free electrons stop moving","note":"electrostatic equilibrium"},{"t":"external field","note":"what the charges respond to"},{"t":"no electric field anywhere inside","note":"the result to explain"}]},{"text":"**Free electrons move in a field.** They drift until they reach the surface and pile up there."},{"text":"**Their field opposes the outside field.** If any field were left inside, electrons would keep moving."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium, free electrons move to the surface until their field cancels any field inside, so E = 0 there."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-57a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:57#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:57"],"q":"Blood pressure in a clinic patient reads 144/96 mmHg, with a heart rate of 64 beats/min. Using MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d), with P_s systolic and P_d diastolic pressure, what is the mean arterial pressure?","choices":["96 mmHg","112 mmHg","120 mmHg","128 mmHg"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That starts from systolic and subtracts a third; the formula starts from the lower, diastolic value and adds."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"A plain average of the two pressures skips the given formula, which weights diastole more heavily."},{"c":0,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the diastolic pressure and leaves out the added third of the pulse pressure."}],"rule":"Plug the numbers into the formula given: diastolic is the lower number. Heart rate is a distractor unless the formula uses it.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Pulse pressure 144 − 96 = 48; a third of it is 16; MAP = 96 + 16. The heart rate is not needed."},"exp":"Systolic P_s = 144 mmHg and diastolic P_d = 96 mmHg (the lower number). Pulse pressure = 144 − 96 = 48 mmHg; a third of it is 16 mmHg. MAP ≈ 96 + 16 = **112 mmHg**. The heart rate does not enter the formula.","how":{"ask":"Identify systolic and diastolic, then substitute into the given formula.","know":"• A blood pressure is written systolic/diastolic: the first (higher) number is systolic.\n• MAP sits closer to diastolic because the heart spends longer in diastole.\n• Use only the quantities the formula asks for.","steps":[{"text":"Read the two pressures.","hl":"144/96 mmHg","calc":["P_s = 144, P_d = 96"]},{"text":"Apply the formula.","hl":"MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d)","calc":["⅓(144 − 96) = 16","MAP ≈ 96 + 16 = **112 mmHg**"]},{"text":"Starting from systolic reverses the formula, and the plain average or the diastolic value alone skip it.","strike":["128 mmHg","120 mmHg","96 mmHg"]},{"text":"Answer: 112 mmHg."}],"trap":"Using the heart rate or a simple average: the question supplies the formula, so plug in exactly what it asks for."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"144/96 mmHg","note":"systolic/diastolic"},{"t":"64 beats/min","note":"not in the formula"},{"t":"MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d)","note":"the formula given"}]},{"text":"**Same formula, other form:** P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d) = (P_s + 2P_d)/3.","calc":["(144 + 2 × 96)/3 = 336/3 = 112"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with a formula given, identify each symbol (diastolic = the lower number), substitute, and ignore data the formula does not use."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-58a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"biology","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:58"],"q":"A neuron's voltage at its axon hillock is graphed over a single spike. What chiefly drives the voltage change at 1.8 ms?","choices":["Na⁺ flows in","K⁺ flows out","Sodium pump action","Ca²⁺ flows in"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"K⁺ leaving drives the falling phase after the peak; at this time the voltage is still climbing."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The pump is slow and keeps the resting gradients; it does not cause the rapid swing of a spike."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ca²⁺ enters at the axon terminal, later, to trigger transmitter release, not at the hillock during the upstroke."}],"rule":"Locate the time on the trace: rising toward the peak = Na⁺ in; falling after the peak = K⁺ out; dip below rest = K⁺ channels still open.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Find 1.8 ms on the trace: between threshold (about 1.6 ms) and the peak (about 2.1 ms), the steep upstroke, which is Na⁺ rushing in."},"exp":"At **1.8 ms** the trace is in its steep **rising phase**, between threshold (≈ −55 mV at 1.6 ms) and the peak (≈ +35 mV at 2.1 ms). Depolarization opens **voltage-gated Na⁺ channels**, and Na⁺ flows in down its gradient, driving the potential up. K⁺ efflux causes the later fall, the pump only restores gradients slowly, and Ca²⁺ entry happens at the terminal.","how":{"ask":"Find where 1.8 ms falls on the trace (rising, falling or undershoot) and match that phase to its ion movement.","know":"• **Threshold** (≈ −55 mV) at the hillock → voltage-gated **Na⁺ channels open: Na⁺ in** (depolarization).\n• Then voltage-gated **K⁺ channels: K⁺ out** (repolarization) and an undershoot (hyperpolarization).\n• Ca²⁺ influx and transmitter release come later, at the terminal.","steps":[{"text":"Place 1.8 ms on the trace.","hl":"at 1.8 ms","calc":["1.6 ms ≈ −55 mV (threshold) → 2.1 ms ≈ +35 mV (peak)","1.8 ms: steep rise"]},{"text":"A rising potential means positive charge entering: Na⁺."},{"text":"K⁺ out is the falling phase, the pump is too slow, and Ca²⁺ enters at the terminal.","strike":["K⁺ flows out","Sodium pump action","Ca²⁺ flows in"]},{"text":"Answer: Na⁺ flows in."}],"trap":"Reading the time as after the peak: check whether the trace is still climbing at that time before choosing K⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"axon hillock","note":"where the spike starts"},{"t":"single spike","note":"one action potential"},{"t":"at 1.8 ms","note":"read the phase here"}]},{"text":"**Order of events:** graded potentials reach threshold at the hillock → Na⁺ channels open, Na⁺ in → K⁺ channels open, K⁺ out → hyperpolarization → rest."},{"text":"**Also:** Ca²⁺ influx and neurotransmitter release come last, at the axon terminal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an action-potential trace, upstroke = Na⁺ in, downstroke = K⁺ out, undershoot = K⁺ channels slow to close."}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Membrane potential at the axon hillock (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Membrane potential (mV)","min":-100,"max":40,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Vm","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[1,-70],[1.6,-55],[2.1,35],[2.7,-40],[3.2,-82],[4.5,-73],[6,-70]]}]}},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-59a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:59#1","pe4-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:59"],"q":"A 300 W heater warms 0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C. If no heat is lost, how long does this take?","choices":["0.20 s","72 s","200 s","272 s"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Divides kJ by W without converting to J; warming half a kilogram in a fraction of a second is absurd."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses 68 °C as ΔT; subtract the starting temperature to get the change."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the starting reading, 18 °C, as if it were ΔT; neither reading alone is the change."}],"rule":"t = m·c·ΔT ÷ P, with c in J (kJ × 1000) and ΔT = final − start. Round each factor to 1 digit before multiplying.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"t = mcΔT ÷ P. ΔT = 50; 0.50 × 50 = 25; 25 × 2400 J = 60,000 J; 60,000 ÷ 300 = 200 s."},"exp":"**q = mcΔT** = 0.50 kg × 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C) × 50 °C = 60 kJ = 6.0 × 10⁴ J. **t = q/P** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J ÷ 300 J/s = **200 s**.","how":{"ask":"Find the heat the ethanol absorbs, convert it to joules, then divide by the heater's power.","know":"**q = mcΔT**, with m in the unit c uses (kJ/(kg·°C) takes kg; J/(g·°C) takes g) and **ΔT = T_final − T_initial**. **P = q/t**, so **t = q/P**; 1 W = 1 J/s and 1 kJ = 1000 J.","steps":[{"text":"Note the values; c is per kilogram, so the mass stays in kg.","hl":"0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C"},{"text":"Use the temperature change, not either reading: ΔT = 18 gives 72 s and ΔT = 68 gives 272 s.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = **50 °C**"],"strike":["72 s","272 s"]},{"text":"Compute the heat.","calc":["q = m c ΔT","q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","q = **60 kJ** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J"]},{"text":"Divide by the power, in joules per second.","hl":"300 W","calc":["t = q ÷ P","t = 60 000 J ÷ 300 J/s","t = **200 s**"]},{"text":"Dividing 60 kJ by 300 W without changing kJ to J gives a time 1000 times too short.","strike":["0.20 s"]},{"text":"Answer: 200 s."}],"trap":"Mixing kJ with W (J/s): 60 ÷ 300 = 0.20 s. Convert kJ to J first. Using 18 or 68 as ΔT gives 72 s or 272 s."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"300 W","note":"power: 300 J each second"},{"t":"0.50 kg","note":"mass"},{"t":"c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)","note":"per kg, so keep kg"},{"t":"from 18 °C to 68 °C","note":"start → end"}]},{"text":"**Match the units:** c is in kJ per kg per °C, so the mass goes in as 0.50 kg; no gram conversion.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = 50 °C"]},{"text":"**Heat needed: q = mcΔT.**","calc":["q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","0.50 × 2.4 = 1.2","1.2 × 50 = **60 kJ**"]},{"text":"**Time from power: t = q/P.** Power is in W = J/s, so change kJ to J first.","calc":["60 kJ = 60 000 J","t = 60 000 ÷ 300 = **200 s**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** q = mcΔT with the mass in the unit c uses, then t = q ÷ P with q in joules (1 W = 1 J/s)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-01a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:1#1","pe5-cp:1#2","pe5-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:1"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P1","passage":"Compound F, 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), blocks a yeast hydrolase by binding only the free enzyme in its active site: E + F ⇌ EF. At 30 °C and pH 7.0, F has Ki = 5.0 μM, and the enzyme's intrinsic kcat/KM is 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹. For each run, the team dissolves F fully in assay buffer (density 1.02 g/mL) to make 250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock. In Trial 1, the assay [F] was raised from 15 μM to 45 μM with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged, and the initial rate fell further. In Trial 2, a cyclodextrin that traps free F as an inert complex was added to an enzyme–F mixture at equilibrium. The share of enzyme bound as EF then dropped, while the Kd measured before and after the addition at 30 °C was the same. F's –OH is its only polar group, so F is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Four analogs each differ from F by a single change: P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, Q replaces the –OH with –H, R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and S adds a –Br at that same ring position.","q":"About what mass of F must be weighed out for one stock?","choices":["1.2 mg","12 mg","0.12 mg","1.2 g"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Reads μM as mM: the mass comes out a thousand times too large."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Turns 250.0 mL into 2.5 L, a factor-of-ten slip in the volume."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Turns 250.0 mL into 0.025 L, a factor-of-ten slip the other way."}],"rule":"Mass to weigh = M × V(L) × molar mass. Convert μM to M (× 10⁻⁶) and mL to L (× 10⁻³) first; the density is a distractor.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Moles first: 40 μM × 0.25 L = 10 μmol. Then 10 μmol × 122 g/mol ≈ 1200 μg, a little over 1 mg."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the stock: moles = M × V = (40 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L)(0.2500 L) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol. Sentence 1 gives the molar mass: 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol × 122 g/mol ≈ 1.2 × 10⁻³ g, so **about 1.2 mg**. The buffer density is not needed.","how":{"ask":"How many moles of F does the 250.0 mL stock hold, and what mass is that?","know":"• **mol = M × V**, with V in litres.\n• **μM = 10⁻⁶ M**, **mL = 10⁻³ L**.\n• **mass = mol × molar mass**; 1 g = 1000 mg.\n• A solvent density matters only when a mass of solution must be turned into a volume.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the stock's volume and concentration; sentence 1 gives the molar mass of F.","hl":"250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock","calc":["M = 40 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L","V = 0.2500 L","molar mass = 122 g/mol"]},{"text":"Concept: moles = M × V (litres), then mass = moles × molar mass, then g → mg.","calc":["n = (40 × 10⁻⁶)(0.2500) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol","m = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 122 ≈ 1.2 × 10⁻³ g"]},{"text":"Reading μM as mM gives a mass a thousand times too big.","strike":["1.2 g"]},{"text":"A slip in the mL → L step moves the answer by ten either way.","strike":["12 mg","0.12 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: about 1.2 mg."}],"trap":"Using the 1.02 g/mL density from sentence 3: it describes the buffer, not F, and plays no part in mol = M × V."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"122 g/mol","note":"grams per mole of F"},{"t":"density 1.02 g/mL","note":"not needed"},{"t":"250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock","note":"V and M"}]},{"text":"**Units first.** Put every quantity in mol, L and g before multiplying.","calc":["40 μM = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ M","250.0 mL = 0.2500 L"]},{"text":"**Chain it.** mol = M × V; mass = mol × molar mass; then g → mg (× 1000)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass of solute = molarity (M) × volume (L) × molar mass; convert μM and mL first, and ignore a solvent density that the math does not use."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-02a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:2#1","pe5-cp:2#2","pe5-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:2"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P1","passage":"Compound F, 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), blocks a yeast hydrolase by binding only the free enzyme in its active site: E + F ⇌ EF. At 30 °C and pH 7.0, F has Ki = 5.0 μM, and the enzyme's intrinsic kcat/KM is 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹. For each run, the team dissolves F fully in assay buffer (density 1.02 g/mL) to make 250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock. In Trial 1, the assay [F] was raised from 15 μM to 45 μM with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged, and the initial rate fell further. In Trial 2, a cyclodextrin that traps free F as an inert complex was added to an enzyme–F mixture at equilibrium. The share of enzyme bound as EF then dropped, while the Kd measured before and after the addition at 30 °C was the same. F's –OH is its only polar group, so F is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Four analogs each differ from F by a single change: P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, Q replaces the –OH with –H, R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and S adds a –Br at that same ring position.","q":"In Trial 2, what best accounts for the change in bound enzyme?","choices":["Less EF; Kd unchanged","More EF; Kd unchanged","Less EF; Kd larger","Same EF; Kd fixed"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: trapping F removes a reactant, so the equilibrium shifts left; more EF would need added F."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Kd changes only with temperature, and the passage says it stayed the same at 30 °C."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Kd stays fixed, but Q does not: losing free F moves Q off K, so the mixture must shift."}],"rule":"A concentration stress shifts the position of an equilibrium (Le Châtelier) while K stays put; only temperature changes K.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Le Châtelier: pulling out a reactant (free F) pushes E + F ⇌ EF backward, so EF falls apart; the temperature never changed, so Kd did not either."},"exp":"The cyclodextrin removes free F, a reactant of E + F ⇌ EF. **Removing a reactant shifts the equilibrium toward reactants** (Le Châtelier's principle), so EF dissociates and the bound share drops. The temperature is unchanged, so **Kd is unchanged**: only Q moved.","how":{"ask":"In Trial 2, which way did E + F ⇌ EF shift, and did Kd change?","know":"• **Le Châtelier's principle**: a system at equilibrium shifts to offset a stress.\n• **Removing a reactant** (or adding a product) → shift toward reactants.\n• **Adding a reactant** (or removing a product) → shift toward products.\n• A trapping agent acts by lowering the free concentration of what it grabs.\n• **K (or Kd) changes only with temperature.**","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the cyclodextrin traps free F: a reactant of E + F ⇌ EF is being pulled out.","hl":"traps free F","calc":["E + F ⇌ EF","cyclodextrin + F → trapped F: free [F] drops"]},{"text":"Concept, Le Châtelier: removing a reactant makes Q = [EF]/([E][F]) rise above K, so the reverse reaction runs and EF falls apart.","calc":["[F] ↓ → Q > K → shift left"],"strike":["More EF; Kd unchanged"]},{"text":"Sentence 6 says Kd was the same at 30 °C, and the bound share did change, so the mixture shifted.","hl":"was the same","strike":["Less EF; Kd larger","Same EF; Kd fixed"]},{"text":"Answer: less EF, with Kd unchanged."}],"trap":"Thinking Kd must change because less complex is present: a stress moves the concentrations, not the constant."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"at equilibrium","note":"Q = K before the stress"},{"t":"traps free F","note":"a reactant is removed"},{"t":"was the same","note":"Kd did not change"}]},{"text":"**Stress and response.** Removing a reactant makes Q > K, so the reverse reaction runs until Q = K again; the constant itself never moves at fixed temperature.","calc":["free F removed → Q > K → shift left","EF ↓ → bound share drops"]},{"text":"**Name the principle.** This is Le Châtelier's principle. Other named principles describe electrons, not equilibria: the Pauli exclusion principle (no two electrons in an atom share all four quantum numbers) and the uncertainty principle (position and momentum cannot both be known exactly)."},{"text":"**Also:** removing a product shifts right. In an esterification, RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O, pulling water out makes Q < K and **raises the ester yield**; adding a reactant does the same.","calc":["remove H₂O → Q falls below K","→ shift right → more ester"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding or removing a reactant or product shifts an equilibrium to offset the change (Le Châtelier's principle), while K stays constant unless the temperature changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-03a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:3#1","pe5-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:3"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P1","passage":"Compound F, 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), blocks a yeast hydrolase by binding only the free enzyme in its active site: E + F ⇌ EF. At 30 °C and pH 7.0, F has Ki = 5.0 μM, and the enzyme's intrinsic kcat/KM is 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹. For each run, the team dissolves F fully in assay buffer (density 1.02 g/mL) to make 250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock. In Trial 1, the assay [F] was raised from 15 μM to 45 μM with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged, and the initial rate fell further. In Trial 2, a cyclodextrin that traps free F as an inert complex was added to an enzyme–F mixture at equilibrium. The share of enzyme bound as EF then dropped, while the Kd measured before and after the addition at 30 °C was the same. F's –OH is its only polar group, so F is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Four analogs each differ from F by a single change: P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, Q replaces the –OH with –H, R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and S adds a –Br at that same ring position.","q":"Which analog is most likely more soluble in water than F, shown?","fig":"1-phenylethanol","choices":["Analog P","Analog Q","Analog R","Analog S"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"An extra CH₂ adds nonpolar surface and no polar group, so water solubility drops."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Swapping –OH for –H removes the only group that H-bonds to water, so solubility falls."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A ring Br adds bulk and no H-bond donor or acceptor, so the molecule gets greasier."}],"rule":"For water solubility, add a group that H-bonds to water (–OH, ring N, –NH₂, an ionizable acid); adding C–H, CH₃ or halogens, or removing a polar group, lowers it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan each change for a new H-bonding group. Only one change adds an O–H that water can bond to; every other change adds grease or removes the only polar group."},"exp":"Water dissolves molecules it can **hydrogen bond** to. Analog R adds a phenol **–OH**, a new H-bond donor and acceptor, so it should be more soluble. Analog P adds a CH₂, Analog S a Br, and Analog Q loses the only –OH: all are **less** soluble.","how":{"ask":"Which single change gives water a new place to hydrogen bond, without adding nonpolar bulk?","know":"• **Raise solubility**: add –OH, –NH₂, a ring N (CH → N), or a group that ionizes at the pH.\n• **Lower solubility**: add CH₂/CH₃ or halogens, or swap a polar group for C–H or an ether.\n• Compare each analog with the lead, one change at a time.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says F's –OH is its only polar group; the rest is a benzene ring and C–H, so F barely dissolves.","hl":"F's –OH is its only polar group"},{"text":"Concept: water dissolves what it can hydrogen bond to. Sort each change in sentence 8: polar group added, or grease added and polar group lost.","calc":["P: + CH₂ → less polar","Q: –OH → –H → loses H-bonding","R: + ring –OH → more H-bonding","S: + ring Br → less polar"]},{"text":"Adding a CH₂ or a Br adds grease with no new H-bonding.","strike":["Analog P","Analog S"]},{"text":"Losing the –OH removes the one group water binds.","strike":["Analog Q"]},{"text":"Answer: Analog R, the one that adds an –OH."}],"trap":"Picking the bromo analog because a C–Br bond is polar: a halogen adds a dipole but cannot hydrogen bond, so it does not help water dissolve the molecule."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"only slightly soluble in water","note":"goal: raise solubility"},{"t":"a single change","note":"compare each analog with F"},{"t":"adds an –OH to the ring","note":"new H-bond donor and acceptor"}]},{"text":"**Like dissolves like.** Water dissolves what it can hydrogen bond to; nonpolar C–H surface pushes a molecule out of water."},{"text":"**Also:** the same logic runs other changes: replacing a ring CH with N (benzene → pyridine) adds an H-bond acceptor and raises solubility, while turning –OH into –OCH₃ or adding a methyl lowers it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an analog is more water-soluble when its change adds a group that hydrogen bonds to water (–OH, N, NH, an ion); added carbons, methyls or halogens lower solubility."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-04a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:4#1","pe5-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:4"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P1","passage":"Compound F, 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), blocks a yeast hydrolase by binding only the free enzyme in its active site: E + F ⇌ EF. At 30 °C and pH 7.0, F has Ki = 5.0 μM, and the enzyme's intrinsic kcat/KM is 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹. For each run, the team dissolves F fully in assay buffer (density 1.02 g/mL) to make 250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock. In Trial 1, the assay [F] was raised from 15 μM to 45 μM with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged, and the initial rate fell further. In Trial 2, a cyclodextrin that traps free F as an inert complex was added to an enzyme–F mixture at equilibrium. The share of enzyme bound as EF then dropped, while the Kd measured before and after the addition at 30 °C was the same. F's –OH is its only polar group, so F is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Four analogs each differ from F by a single change: P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, Q replaces the –OH with –H, R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and S adds a –Br at that same ring position.","q":"In Trial 1, what are Ki and the intrinsic kcat/KM at the higher [F]?","choices":["5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","15 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","5.0 μM; 1.3 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","1.7 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"15 μM is the starting [F] in Trial 1, not Ki; a binding constant doesn't take on the assay concentration."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ki doesn't scale down with [I] either; 1.7 μM is just 5.0 ÷ 3."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Intrinsic kcat/KM belongs to the enzyme at fixed T and pH; more inhibitor slows the observed rate, not this constant."}],"rule":"Ki and intrinsic kcat/KM are constants at fixed T and pH; changing [I] changes the observed rate, never the constants.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Ki and intrinsic kcat/KM are constants of the molecules at set T and pH; they'd be the same at [I] = 0 or 1 M. Changing [I] changes only the observed rate: 5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶."},"exp":"**Ki** (the EI dissociation constant) and **kcat/KM** (the enzyme's catalytic efficiency) are **constants** of the enzyme–inhibitor pair at a fixed temperature and pH. Tripling [I] inhibits more enzyme and slows the observed rate, but both values stay **5.0 μM and 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹**, read straight from the data.","how":{"ask":"Which quantities change when [F] changes, and which are fixed by the enzyme, the inhibitor, T and pH?","know":"• **Ki** = [E][I]/[EI], an equilibrium constant: set by the enzyme, inhibitor, T and pH, not by how much inhibitor is added.\n• **KM, kcat and kcat/KM** are properties of the enzyme with its substrate under fixed conditions.\n• Raising [I] changes the **observed** rate (and the apparent KM or Vmax), not these constants.\n• The apparent kcat/KM measured with a competitive inhibitor present falls as [I] rises; the intrinsic constant does not.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says only [F] changed in Trial 1: substrate, temperature and pH were held.","hl":"with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged","calc":["[F]: 15 μM → 45 μM (× 3)"]},{"text":"Concept: Ki is a dissociation constant, so it does not scale with [F]; tripling it or dividing it by 3 confuses the constant with the concentration.","strike":["15 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","1.7 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"]},{"text":"kcat/KM belongs to the enzyme itself; dividing it by 3 treats the slower observed rate as a new constant.","strike":["5.0 μM; 1.3 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Read both constants straight from sentence 2.","hl":"F has Ki = 5.0 μM"},{"text":"Answer: 5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹."}],"trap":"Scaling a constant with [F] (Ki × 3 = 15 μM): more inhibitor means more inhibition, not a new Ki."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"At 30 °C and pH 7.0","note":"conditions that set the constants"},{"t":"Ki = 5.0 μM","note":"a constant"},{"t":"intrinsic kcat/KM","note":"the enzyme's own constant"},{"t":"raised from 15 μM to 45 μM","note":"3× more inhibitor"}]},{"text":"**Constants versus conditions.** A constant is fixed by the identity of the molecules and by T and pH; a concentration is something the experimenter sets.","calc":["Ki = [E][F]/[EF]: more F → more EF, same ratio"]},{"text":"**Read, don't recompute.** When a question asks for a constant under a new [F], copy the value from the passage unchanged."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ki, KM, kcat and kcat/KM are constants of the enzyme–inhibitor pair at fixed T and pH, so raising [I] changes the observed rate but none of these values."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-05a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:5#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:5"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"Which product does the dehydrogenase form from butanal?","fig":"butanal","choices":["2-Butanol","1-Butanol","Butane","Butanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-2-butanol","1-butanol","cpt-pe5-butane","cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: NADH reduces, but an acid is an oxidation product."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Carbon 2 never held the oxygen; the hydride adds to the existing C=O carbon, at the chain end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Removing the O entirely takes 4 e⁻; one hydride plus one H⁺ adds only 2 e⁻."}],"rule":"Hydride + H⁺ on a C=O gives an alcohol on that same carbon: an aldehyde gives a primary alcohol, a ketone a secondary one; the chain and the O position don't move.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"H⁻ + H⁺ added across C=O gives an OH on that same carbon. The aldehyde carbon is the chain end, so the OH stays on C1."},"exp":"The hydride and the proton add **H₂ across the C=O**, so the CHO carbon (C1) becomes CH₂OH: **1-butanol, a primary alcohol**. 2-Butanol would put the O on C2, butane would need 4 e⁻, and butanoic acid is an oxidation.","how":{"ask":"A hydride plus a proton add H₂ across the C=O. Which product keeps the oxygen, now as OH, on the carbon that held it?","know":"• **Reduction** of a carbonyl adds 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ (here H⁻ from NADH and H⁺ from water) across C=O; no C–C bond is made or broken.\n• An **aldehyde** carbon has one C neighbor, so it becomes a **primary** alcohol; a **ketone** carbon has two, so it becomes a **secondary** alcohol.\n• Removing the O altogether (to an alkane) takes 2 more electrons; a carboxylic acid is an oxidation.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says a hydride and a proton add to butanal's C=O, and no C–C bond is made or broken.","hl":"a hydride from NADH and a proton from water"},{"text":"Concept: H⁻ + H⁺ add H₂ across the C=O. Butanal's C=O sits on carbon 1 with an H (an aldehyde), so C1 ends up holding OH, two H and the propyl chain.","calc":["CH₃CH₂CH₂–CHO + H⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃CH₂CH₂–CH₂OH"]},{"text":"2-Butanol has its OH on carbon 2, which never held the oxygen.","strike":["2-Butanol"]},{"text":"Butane has lost the O (4 e⁻ gained, not 2). Butanoic acid has more bonds to O: an oxidation, not a reduction.","strike":["Butane","Butanoic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-butanol, a primary alcohol."}],"trap":"Thinking any carbonyl reduction gives a secondary alcohol: that holds for ketones; an aldehyde gives a primary alcohol."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"reduce butanal","note":"aldehyde, C=O on C1"},{"t":"a hydride from NADH and a proton from water","note":"2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺"},{"t":"making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond","note":"same skeleton"}]},{"text":"**Count what is added.** A hydride is H with 2 electrons; a proton is H⁺ with none. Together they make H₂: 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺."},{"text":"**Open one bond of the C=O.** The H⁻ bonds to the carbon and the H⁺ to the oxygen, leaving C–OH on the same carbon."},{"text":"**Also:** a ketone gives a **secondary** alcohol the same way: 2-butanone (C=O on C2, between CH₃ and CH₂CH₃) is reduced to 2-butanol, R–CH(OH)–R.","fig":"cpt-pe5-2-butanone"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ on a C=O gives H–C–OH on the same carbon: an aldehyde becomes a primary alcohol and a ketone a secondary alcohol, with no change in the carbon skeleton."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-06a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:6#1","pe5-cp:6#2","pe5-cp:6#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:6"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"Which labeled atom of FAD lies in the ring system all three flavins share?","fig":"cpt-pe5-fad-parts","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"X is in adenine, part of the AMP half that only FAD has."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Y is the ribose ring O of the AMP half, another FAD-only piece."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Z is a phosphorus outside any ring, and riboflavin has no phosphate at all."}],"rule":"Riboflavin, FMN and FAD share only the flavin ring (three fused six-rings, four N); phosphates and the AMP part are added on.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The common core of riboflavin, FMN and FAD is the flavin: the three fused rings holding four N. Pick the atom in that tricyclic part, W; phosphate, ribose and adenine are add-ons."},"exp":"All three flavin cofactors are built on the **isoalloxazine ring**: three fused six-membered rings with four N, where W sits. FMN adds a phosphate (Z) to riboflavin, and only FAD adds the AMP part, with its ribose (Y) and **adenine (X)**.","how":{"ask":"Which part of FAD is the flavin, the piece common to all three cofactors, and which labeled atom sits in it?","know":"• **Riboflavin** (vitamin B₂) = isoalloxazine ring + ribityl chain. **FMN** = riboflavin + one phosphate. **FAD** = FMN + AMP (a second phosphate, ribose and adenine).\n• **Isoalloxazine**: three fused six-membered rings in a row holding four N and two C=O; it carries the electrons.\n• Adenine is a purine (a six- fused to a five-membered ring); among the three cofactors only FAD has it.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says only the part that all three flavins contain takes the electrons, so look for that shared part in FAD.","hl":"riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain","fig":"cpt-pe5-fad-flavin"},{"text":"Concept: the shared part is the isoalloxazine ring, three fused rings with four ring N and two C=O. W, highlighted, is one of its nitrogens."},{"text":"Build the three cofactors by adding pieces, as sentence 2 does.","calc":["riboflavin = isoalloxazine + ribityl chain","FMN = riboflavin + phosphate","FAD = FMN + AMP (phosphate + ribose + adenine)"]},{"text":"X is in adenine and Y is the ring O of ribose: both belong to the AMP half, which only FAD has.","strike":["X","Y"]},{"text":"Z is a phosphorus outside any ring, and riboflavin has no phosphate at all.","strike":["Z"]},{"text":"Answer: W, on the isoalloxazine ring."}],"trap":"Choosing the adenine because it is also a fused, nitrogen-rich ring system; it has only two rings and comes with FAD's AMP part alone."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"FAD","note":"flavin adenine dinucleotide"},{"t":"atoms W–Z mark four regions","note":"one label per part"},{"t":"riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain","note":"the shared core"}]},{"text":"**Recognize the flavin.** Three six-membered rings fused in a row, four ring N and two C=O: the isoalloxazine ring, the redox part of every flavin cofactor."},{"text":"**Also:** read the name, flavin **adenine** dinucleotide. Adenine (X) and its ribose (Y) arrive only with the AMP that FAD adds to FMN; riboflavin and FMN have no adenine."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** riboflavin, FMN and FAD share the isoalloxazine ring (three fused rings, four N); FMN adds a phosphate, and only FAD adds AMP with its adenine."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-06b","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:6#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:6"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"Which fragment shown is the hydride-accepting part all three carriers share?","choices":["Flavin ring system","Nicotinamide ring","Adenine","Ubiquinone"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-lumiflavin","cpt-pe5-nicotinamide-ring","adenine","cpt-pe5-ubiquinone"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Adenine is in NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ only; NMN has no AMP, and adenine carries no electrons anyway."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Three fused rings with four N belong to FMN and FAD, not to any of these three carriers."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A quinone ring on a long isoprenoid tail is the membrane carrier CoQ, not part of a nucleotide."}],"rule":"For the part several cofactors share, list what each one contains and keep only the piece found in all of them; that is usually the redox-active ring.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NMN is the smallest of the three, and the other two are built on it. Its only ring is the pyridinium with –CONH₂, so that ring is the shared part."},"exp":"NMN is ribose phosphate + **nicotinamide**; NAD⁺ adds AMP (adenine), and NADP⁺ adds a phosphate. Only the **nicotinamide ring** is in all three, and it accepts the hydride (NAD⁺ → NADH). Adenine is missing from NMN; the flavin ring belongs to FMN/FAD; ubiquinone is a separate membrane carrier.","how":{"ask":"Which piece appears in NMN, NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ alike?","know":"• **Nicotinamide**: a pyridinium ring with –CONH₂; accepts H⁻ (NAD⁺ → NADH).\n• **Flavin** (isoalloxazine): three fused rings, four N; the redox part of riboflavin, FMN and FAD.\n• **Adenine**: two fused rings, five N; in AMP-containing cofactors, carries no electrons.\n• **Ubiquinone (CoQ)**: a quinone ring on a long isoprenoid tail; lipid-soluble, it shuttles electrons in the inner mitochondrial membrane.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 8 describes NMN, the smallest carrier: a ribose phosphate with one pyridinium ring bearing –CONH₂. NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ only add AMP pieces to it.","hl":"one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group"},{"text":"Concept: the part shared by a cofactor family is the core ring of its smallest member. Adenine comes with the AMP, which NMN lacks.","strike":["Adenine"]},{"text":"The flavin ring and the quinone are in other carriers entirely.","strike":["Flavin ring system","Ubiquinone"]},{"text":"Answer: the nicotinamide ring."}],"trap":"Picking adenine because NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ both have it: \"shared by all\" must include NMN."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"NADPH or reduced NMN","note":"three carriers"},{"t":"the ring part that all three share","note":"must be in NMN too"},{"t":"one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group","note":"nicotinamide"}]},{"text":"**Build-up cofactors.** Many cofactors are a small core plus handles: NMN → NAD⁺ → NADP⁺, and likewise riboflavin → FMN → FAD. The core ring does the chemistry."},{"text":"**Also:** ubiquinone is the odd one out among carriers: a quinone ring with two C=O across from each other, on a long nonpolar tail that lets it move within the inner mitochondrial membrane from complexes I and II to complex III."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the part shared by a family of cofactors is the redox ring of the smallest member (nicotinamide for NMN/NAD⁺/NADP⁺, flavin for riboflavin/FMN/FAD); handles such as adenine are not in every member."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-07a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:7#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:7"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"About how many Cl⁻ ions are in the crystallization mix per complex?","choices":["9000","4500","9","4.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Way too small: skips the 1000-fold mM → μM factor and also counts one Cl⁻ per formula."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Divides mM by μM without converting; 1 mM = 1000 μM."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Counts one Cl⁻ per MgCl₂; each formula unit releases two, so multiply by 2."}],"rule":"Ions per molecule = (salt conc × ions per formula) ÷ protein conc, in the same units (1 mM = 1000 μM). Round to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"MgCl₂ gives 2 Cl⁻: 2 × 18 = 36 mM = 36,000 μM. Per complex: 36,000 ÷ 4 = 9000. HEPES is irrelevant."},"exp":"Only MgCl₂ supplies Cl⁻, two per formula unit: [Cl⁻] = 2 × 18 mM = 36 mM = 36,000 μM. **Divide in the same units:** 36,000 μM ÷ 4.0 μM = **9000** Cl⁻ per complex.","how":{"ask":"Find the chloride concentration, put both in the same unit, then divide.","know":"• In one solution, a **particle ratio = concentration ratio**, because the volume cancels.\n• Count only the salt that supplies the ion, times the ions per formula unit (MgCl₂ → 2 Cl⁻).\n• Put both in one unit first: 1 mM = 1000 μM.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the salt and the protein: only MgCl₂ holds chloride, and the HEPES buffer is a distractor.","hl":"18 mM MgCl₂","calc":["[Cl⁻] = 2 × 18 mM = 36 mM"]},{"text":"Concept: each MgCl₂ gives two Cl⁻; a particle ratio is a concentration ratio in one unit, so convert to the protein's unit.","calc":["36 mM × 1000 μM/mM = 36,000 μM"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["36,000 μM ÷ 4.0 μM = **9000**"]},{"text":"4500 counts one Cl⁻ per MgCl₂, 9 divides mM by μM without converting, and 4.5 makes both mistakes.","strike":["4500","9","4.5"]},{"text":"Answer: about 9000 Cl⁻ ions per complex."}],"trap":"Dividing 36 by 4.0 directly: the two numbers are in different units (mM and μM)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"18 mM MgCl₂","note":"two Cl⁻ per formula unit"},{"t":"20 mM HEPES","note":"no chloride"},{"t":"4.0 μM, a monomer","note":"one protein per complex"}]},{"text":"**Count the ion.** Each MgCl₂ gives two Cl⁻.","calc":["2 × 18 mM = 36 mM Cl⁻"]},{"text":"**Match the units, then divide.**","calc":["36 mM = 36,000 μM","36,000 ÷ 4.0 = 9000 per complex"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a ratio of particles in one solution is the ratio of their concentrations in the same unit, counting ions per formula unit."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-08a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:8"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"Where does most of the transfer's energy go in intact membranes?","choices":["Pumping Na⁺ outward","Re-forming NADH","Speeding the electrons","Warming the cytoplasm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: re-forming NADH reverses an exergonic step; it costs energy rather than storing it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Electrons hop between carriers; their speed isn't where redox energy is stored."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The step releases little heat, so the energy can't be warming the cytoplasm."}],"rule":"Energy from a membrane redox step that isn't lost as heat is stored, usually as an ion gradient pumped across the membrane.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Membrane redox energy not lost as heat is banked as an ion gradient; marine bacteria often pump Na⁺. Pumping Na⁺ outward."},"exp":"ΔG for the transfer is fixed by its start and end states, so the energy cannot simply vanish. The enzyme **couples** the electron transfer to **pumping Na⁺ out against its gradient**, storing the free energy as an ion gradient instead of releasing it as heat.","how":{"ask":"Decide what work an exergonic membrane redox step can do, so that its energy does not come out as heat.","know":"• An exergonic step (ΔG < 0) can drive an uphill one if one enzyme **couples** them.\n• Membrane redox complexes couple electron flow to **moving ions (H⁺ or Na⁺) up their gradient**, storing energy as an electrochemical gradient.\n• Energy that does work is not released as heat.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the transfer is exergonic but gives off little heat while the membrane is intact.","hl":"releases little heat in intact membranes"},{"text":"Concept: little heat means the free energy did work instead; the work a membrane enzyme can do is moving ions across the sealed membrane."},{"text":"Sentence 5 names the ion at hand: pumping Na⁺ out builds a gradient that later drives ATP synthesis or the flagellum."},{"text":"Re-forming NADH would run the step backward and cost energy; warming the cytoplasm is exactly the heat that is missing.","strike":["Re-forming NADH","Warming the cytoplasm"]},{"text":"The electrons end up bound to the quinone; their speed is not a store of energy.","strike":["Speeding the electrons"]},{"text":"Answer: pumping Na⁺ outward."}],"trap":"Looking for an enzyme trick that makes the reaction release less energy; catalysts never change ΔG, so the energy must go into work."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"membrane complex","note":"can move ions across"},{"t":"strongly exergonic","note":"ΔG < 0"},{"t":"releases little heat","note":"energy did work"},{"t":"no sealed membrane","note":"no gradient → heat"}]},{"text":"**Energy coupling:** an exergonic reaction can drive an uphill process when one enzyme links them; the energy is then stored, not lost as heat."},{"text":"**In membranes, the uphill process is ion pumping.** Mitochondria pump H⁺; many marine bacteria pump Na⁺. Either way the gradient stores the energy for later work."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a membrane redox step releases far less heat than its ΔG, the energy was coupled to pumping ions (H⁺ or Na⁺) against their gradient."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-09a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:9#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:9"],"q":"Open tank A, 0.40 m wide, holds water (1.0 g/cm³); open tank B, 4.0 m wide, holds oil (0.80 g/cm³). Both liquids are 1.5 m deep. How does the absolute pressure at the bottom of A compare with that in B?","choices":["Greater in A; denser liquid","Equal; h is the same","Greater in B; more liquid","Greater in B; larger base area"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Same depth is not enough: P = P_atm + ρgh also depends on the liquid's density."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The total volume of liquid does not set the pressure at a point; only ρ, g and depth do."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A wider base spreads more weight over more area; the ratio, the pressure, depends only on ρgh."}],"rule":"Pressure at a depth in an open container: P = P_atm + ρgh. Compare density and depth only; width, shape and volume never matter.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same depth and same atmosphere, so only ρ differs. Denser liquid gives more pressure at that depth."},"exp":"P = P_atm + ρgh. Both bottoms are 1.5 m down and both tanks are open, so the pressures differ only through ρ: water (1000 kg/m³) beats oil (800 kg/m³), so **P_A > P_B**. Tank width and the amount of liquid do not matter.","how":{"ask":"In P = P_atm + ρgh, which factor differs between the two bottoms?","know":"• **P = P_atm + ρgh** for an open container.\n• Pressure at a depth depends only on ρ, g and h, not on the container's width, shape or volume.\n• Same liquid, same depth → same pressure; same depth, denser liquid → more pressure.","steps":[{"text":"Write P for each bottom.","calc":["P_A = P_atm + (1000)(9.8)(1.5) ≈ P_atm + 14.7 kPa","P_B = P_atm + (800)(9.8)(1.5) ≈ P_atm + 11.8 kPa"]},{"text":"The depths match, but the densities don't, so the pressures can't be equal.","hl":"Both liquids are 1.5 m deep","strike":["Equal; h is the same"]},{"text":"Width and the amount of liquid never enter ρgh.","strike":["Greater in B; more liquid","Greater in B; larger base area"]},{"text":"Answer: greater in A, because water is denser."}],"trap":"Thinking the big tank, with far more liquid, must press harder on its bottom: pressure at a depth ignores the container size."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"water (1.0 g/cm³)","note":"ρ_A"},{"t":"oil (0.80 g/cm³)","note":"ρ_B"},{"t":"Both liquids are 1.5 m deep","note":"same h"}]},{"text":"**Only three things count.** At a point below an open surface, the pressure is P_atm plus ρgh: density, gravity and depth."},{"text":"**Also:** with the same liquid in both tanks, the pressures at equal depth would be exactly equal, however different the widths or volumes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to compare pressures at points in open containers, compare ρ × depth; ignore the container's width, shape and volume."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-09b","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:9#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:9"],"q":"A wide basin and a thin tube, both open, hold ethanol. How does the pressure 20 cm below the basin's surface compare with that 35 cm below the tube's surface?","choices":["Greater in the tube","Greater in the basin","Equal, since ρ is the same","Equal, since air presses on each"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Same liquid is not enough: the depths differ, and P = P_atm + ρgh grows with depth."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Both surfaces share P_atm, but that skips the ρgh term, which differs with depth."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The basin's extra width and volume aren't in ρgh; its extra weight rests on extra floor area."}],"rule":"Pressure in a liquid depends only on its density, g and depth (P = P_atm + ρgh), never on the container's shape, width or volume.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same liquid and same air on top, so only depth matters. The deeper point has the higher pressure, whatever the container."},"exp":"**P = P_atm + ρgh** contains only the liquid's density, g and the depth below an open surface. Same ethanol, same air above, but the point in the tube is **deeper** (35 cm vs 20 cm), so the pressure there is **greater**, however thin the tube is.","how":{"ask":"Which quantities in the depth-pressure formula differ between the two points?","know":"• **P = P_atm + ρgh**: only ρ, g, h and the surface pressure appear.\n• Width, shape and volume are not in the formula (the hydrostatic paradox).\n• Same liquid + same surface pressure → the deeper point has the higher pressure.","steps":[{"text":"List what the two points share and what differs.","hl":"hold ethanol","calc":["ρ: same (ethanol)","surface: open to the air in both","h: 20 cm (basin) vs 35 cm (tube)"]},{"text":"Compute ρgh for each.","calc":["basin: 789 × 9.8 × 0.20 ≈ 1.5 kPa","tube: 789 × 9.8 × 0.35 ≈ 2.7 kPa"]},{"text":"Equal ρ and equal air pressure don't make the pressures equal when the depths differ.","strike":["Equal, since ρ is the same","Equal, since air presses on each"]},{"text":"The basin's width and volume never enter ρgh.","strike":["Greater in the basin"]},{"text":"Answer: greater in the tube, the deeper point."}],"trap":"Thinking the basin, with far more liquid, must have the greater pressure: only depth below the surface counts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"both open","note":"same P_atm on each surface"},{"t":"hold ethanol","note":"same ρ"},{"t":"20 cm below the basin's surface","note":"h₁"},{"t":"35 cm below the tube's surface","note":"h₂"}]},{"text":"**Pressure at depth: P = P_atm + ρgh.** Check which letters change between the points: only h."},{"text":"**Why shape drops out:** in a wide vessel the extra liquid presses on extra floor; in a flared one the slanted walls hold up the overhanging liquid. At equal depths in the same liquid, the pressures would be equal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to compare pressures in open containers of the same liquid, compare depths only; the container's width, shape and volume never matter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-10a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:10#1","pe5-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:10"],"q":"Every nucleus in a sample has a mass number of 32 and contains 17 neutrons. A student identifies the sample as sulfur, since ³²S is sulfur's most common isotope. Is the student's identification correct?","choices":["Yes; mass number 32 fits sulfur","No; Z = 17, chlorine","No; Z = 15, phosphorus","No; Z = 32, germanium"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the neutron count as the atomic number; Z counts protons, not neutrons."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the mass number as the atomic number; A counts protons plus neutrons."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Many elements have isotopes of the same mass number; only the proton count identifies an element."}],"rule":"Element = proton count. Protons = mass number − neutrons; a matching mass number never identifies an element.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Z = A − N = 32 − 17. Sulfur is Z = 16, so one proton fewer is the element just before it."},"exp":"Z = A − N = 32 − 17 = **15**, which is phosphorus (³²P, a radioactive isotope). Sulfur has Z = 16, so ³²S has 16 neutrons. The student matched the mass number, which does not identify an element.","how":{"ask":"How many protons does each nucleus have, and which element has that atomic number?","know":"• **Atomic number Z** = protons; it alone identifies the element.\n• **Mass number A** = protons + neutrons.\n• So **Z = A − N**. Isotopes of different elements can share A (isobars).","steps":[{"text":"Find the protons.","hl":"contains 17 neutrons","calc":["Z = A − N = 32 − 17 = 15"]},{"text":"Z = 15 is phosphorus; sulfur is Z = 16."},{"text":"Neither N nor A is the atomic number.","strike":["No; Z = 17, chlorine","No; Z = 32, germanium"]},{"text":"A shared mass number does not make the same element.","strike":["Yes; mass number 32 fits sulfur"]},{"text":"Answer: no; Z = 15, phosphorus."}],"trap":"Accepting the student's match on mass number: ³²S and ³²P have the same A but different Z."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"mass number of 32","note":"A"},{"t":"contains 17 neutrons","note":"N"},{"t":"Is the student's identification correct?","note":"check the student's logic"}]},{"text":"**Protons decide.** Subtract the neutrons from the mass number; the result is the atomic number, the element's ID.","calc":["Z = A − N"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to identify an element from a nucleus, compute protons = mass number − neutrons and look up that atomic number; never identify it by mass number."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-11a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:11#1","pe5-cp:11#2","pe5-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:11"],"q":"Each answer choice shows a molecule's bonds, without the lone pairs. Which molecule is polar?","choices":["Beryllium chloride","Sulfur trioxide","Carbon tetrafluoride","Nitrogen trichloride"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-becl2","cpt-pe5-so3","cpt-pe5-cf4","cpt-pe5-ncl3"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"BeCl₂ is linear with two identical bonds, so the dipoles cancel."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"SO₃ is trigonal planar with three equivalent S–O bonds and no lone pair on S, so its dipoles cancel."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"CF₄ is a symmetric tetrahedron with four identical bonds: nonpolar."}],"rule":"Symmetric shapes with identical bonds (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral) are nonpolar; a lone pair on the center (pyramidal, bent) makes it polar.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Symmetric shapes cancel bond dipoles: BeCl₂ linear, SO₃ trigonal planar, CF₄ tetrahedral are nonpolar. NCl₃'s lone pair makes it pyramidal, so its dipoles don't cancel: polar."},"exp":"In **NCl₃**, nitrogen holds three N–Cl bonds plus **a lone pair** (4 domains), which makes a pyramid whose bond dipoles cannot cancel. BeCl₂ is linear, SO₃ trigonal planar and CF₄ tetrahedral, each with identical bonds and no lone pair on the middle atom, so their dipoles cancel.","how":{"ask":"Find each shape by VSEPR, then ask whether the bond dipoles cancel.","know":"• **VSEPR**: count bonding domains and lone pairs around the middle atom.\n• Identical bonds in a **symmetric shape** (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral) cancel: nonpolar.\n• A **central-atom lone pair** (trigonal pyramidal, bent) leaves a net dipole: polar.","steps":[{"text":"Assign each shape.","hl":"Which molecule is polar?","calc":["BeCl₂: 2 domains, 0 lone pairs → linear","SO₃: 3 domains, 0 lone pairs → trigonal planar","CF₄: 4 domains, 0 lone pairs → tetrahedral","NCl₃: 3 bonds + 1 lone pair → trigonal pyramidal"]},{"text":"The three symmetric shapes with identical bonds cancel their dipoles.","strike":["Beryllium chloride","Sulfur trioxide","Carbon tetrafluoride"]},{"text":"Answer: nitrogen trichloride."}],"trap":"Judging from the drawing alone: the drawings omit lone pairs, so count N's five valence electrons to find the hidden pair."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"without the lone pairs","note":"count them yourself"},{"t":"polar","note":"net dipole ≠ 0"}]},{"text":"**VSEPR first:** count the bonding domains plus lone pairs around the middle atom; that sets the shape."},{"text":"**Count electrons on the central atom.** N has 5 valence electrons: 3 go to bonds, 2 stay as a lone pair.","calc":["N: 5 − 3 = 2 → one lone pair"]},{"text":"**Shape decides cancellation.** A lone pair pushes the three N–Cl bonds into a pyramid, so their dipoles add up instead of canceling."},{"text":"**Also:** symmetric shapes with identical bonds, such as linear BeCl₂, trigonal planar SO₃ and tetrahedral CF₄, are nonpolar even though each bond is polar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** symmetric shapes with identical bonds cancel their dipoles; a central-atom lone pair leaves a net dipole."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-12a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:12#1","pe5-cp:12#2","pe5-cp:12#3","pe6-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:12"],"q":"A gas under a free piston is at 50 °C, 2.4 atm and 0.80 mol. All else constant, which change would NOT double its volume?","choices":["Heating it to 100 °C","Doubling the moles","Dropping P to 1.2 atm","Heating it to 646 K"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"V ∝ n, so doubling the moles of gas doubles V."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"V ∝ 1/P, and 2.4 → 1.2 atm halves P, so V doubles."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"50 °C is 323 K, and 646 K is twice that: T doubles in kelvin, so V doubles."}],"rule":"Gas laws use kelvin: add 273 before comparing temperatures. V ∝ nT/P, so only a doubled n, doubled K or halved P doubles V.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Gas laws run in kelvin: 50 °C → 100 °C is 323 → 373 K, only about 15% more, not double. 646 K is 2 × 323, so that one does double V. Heating to 100 °C."},"exp":"From PV = nRT, **V ∝ nT/P** with T in **kelvin**. 50 °C is 323 K and 100 °C is 373 K, so V grows only by 373/323 ≈ 1.15. Doubling n (0.80 → 1.60 mol), halving P (2.4 → 1.2 atm) or doubling the kelvin temperature (323 → 646 K) each doubles V.","how":{"ask":"Turn each change into a factor on n, T (in kelvin) or P, and find the one that does not give ×2.","know":"• **Ideal gas: V = nRT/P**, so **V ∝ nT/P**.\n• T must be in **kelvin**: T(K) = T(°C) + 273.\n• ×2 n or ×2 T(K) doubles V; ×2 P halves V; ×½ P doubles V.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the starting temperature to kelvin.","hl":"50 °C","calc":["T = 50 + 273 = **323 K**"]},{"text":"Heating to 100 °C: find the kelvin factor.","hl":"Heating it to 100 °C","calc":["100 °C = 373 K","factor = 373 ÷ 323 ≈ **1.15**","V rises about 15%, not ×2"]},{"text":"Heating to 646 K is 2 × 323 K: T doubles in kelvin, so V doubles.","calc":["646 ÷ 323 = 2"],"strike":["Heating it to 646 K"]},{"text":"Doubling the amount takes n from 0.80 to 1.60 mol, and V ∝ n.","strike":["Doubling the moles"]},{"text":"Dropping P to 1.2 atm halves P; V ∝ 1/P, so V doubles. (Raising P to 4.8 atm would halve V instead.)","strike":["Dropping P to 1.2 atm"]},{"text":"Answer: heating it to 100 °C."}],"trap":"Doubling the Celsius number: 50 → 100 °C is only 323 → 373 K, a 15% change."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"free piston","note":"V is free to change"},{"t":"50 °C","note":"convert to K"},{"t":"NOT double","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Write V in terms of the others:** V = nRT/P, so V ∝ nT/P.","calc":["V₂/V₁ = (n₂/n₁)(T₂/T₁)(P₁/P₂)"]},{"text":"**Also: pressure sits in the denominator.** Doubling P halves V; halving P doubles V.","calc":["P ×2 → V ×½","P ×½ → V ×2"]},{"text":"**Also: always use kelvin.** Doubling a Celsius temperature does not double the kelvin temperature.","calc":["50 °C → 323 K","100 °C → 373 K","373/323 ≈ 1.15"]},{"text":"**Also:** with P and n both fixed, V ∝ T in kelvin (Charles's law). Cooling shrinks a gas sample even when none is lost: the same moles simply fill less space.","calc":["60 °C → 0 °C: 333 K → 273 K","V₂ = V₁ × 273/333 ≈ 0.82 V₁, about 18% smaller","the °C ratio (0/60) would wrongly give zero"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V ∝ nT/P with T in kelvin: ×2 n or ×2 T(K) doubles V, ×2 P halves it, and doubling °C does not double V."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-13a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:20#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:13"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P3","passage":"Silver dressings fight wound infection by slowly releasing Ag⁺, and a lab tracks that release with a fluorescent sensor. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, in nearly equal amounts; the dressings are made with silver enriched in the heavier isotope, so mass spectrometry can follow the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal. The sensor grips Ag⁺ through 4-methylimidazole, a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain whose five-membered ring keeps both ring nitrogens, one bearing H. Bound sensor fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm. To save money, a student tried a 590 nm laser as the light source; even at its highest power, no 520 nm emission was detected, and the buffer absorbs no visible light. Because wound fluid is rich in CO₂, each test chamber is dosed by breaking a sealed glass ampoule of internal volume 33.6 cm³ filled with pure CO₂ at 273 K and 1.00 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.","q":"How many neutrons does each tracked silver nucleus contain?","choices":["62","60","61","47"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the other isotope's mass number, 107; take A from this nucleus's own superscript."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the rounded average atomic mass, 108; one nucleus has its own whole-number A."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"47 is Z, the proton count, not the neutron count."}],"rule":"N = A − Z, with A from the isotope's own superscript and Z from the periodic table; the average atomic mass is never a nucleus's A.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ag is element 47. Use the isotope's own mass number: N = 109 − 47 = 62. The 107.87 average is a distractor."},"exp":"Silver is Z = 47 on the periodic table. **N = A − Z** = 109 − 47 = **62**. The ¹⁰⁷Ag isotope has 60, two fewer, matching its mass number being 2 lower; the average mass 107.87 u describes the mixture, not one nucleus.","how":{"ask":"Read Z from the periodic table, then subtract it from the isotope's mass number.","know":"• **N = A − Z**, with A the isotope's mass number (the superscript) and Z from the periodic table.\n• Isotopes of one element share Z, so their neutron counts differ by exactly their difference in A.\n• The **average atomic mass** is a weighted mean of the mixture, never a mass number.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the lab follows the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus: use that isotope's own mass number, not the average mass.","hl":"¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus","calc":["A = 109; Z(Ag) = 47"]},{"text":"Concept: neutrons = mass number − atomic number, with Z = 47 for silver from the periodic table.","calc":["N = 109 − 47 = **62**"]},{"text":"60 belongs to ¹⁰⁷Ag (107 − 47), 61 uses the rounded average mass (108 − 47), and 47 is the proton count.","strike":["60","61","47"]},{"text":"Answer: 62 neutrons."}],"trap":"Using the average atomic mass as if it were a mass number: 107.87 u describes the natural mix of isotopes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"107.87 u","note":"average, not A"},{"t":"¹⁰⁹Ag","note":"A = 109"}]},{"text":"**Get Z.** Silver is element 47, so every Ag nucleus has 47 protons."},{"text":"**Subtract.**","calc":["N = A − Z = 109 − 47 = 62"]},{"text":"**Check with the other isotope.** Same Z, so A differs only by neutrons.","calc":["¹⁰⁷Ag: 107 − 47 = 60","109 − 107 = 2 = 62 − 60 ✓"]},{"text":"**Also:** two isotopes of one element share Z, so a mass number one lower means exactly one neutron fewer, and a lower neutron-to-proton ratio. Carbon-11, a PET tracer, against stable carbon-12:","calc":["Z(C) = 6 for both","¹¹C: N = 11 − 6 = 5; ¹²C: N = 12 − 6 = 6","N/Z: 5/6 ≈ 0.83 versus 6/6 = 1.00"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** neutrons = mass number − atomic number, with Z from the periodic table; the average atomic mass is never a mass number."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-14a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:14#1","pe5-cp:14#4","pe5-cp:14#2","pe5-cp:14#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:14"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P3","passage":"Silver dressings fight wound infection by slowly releasing Ag⁺, and a lab tracks that release with a fluorescent sensor. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, in nearly equal amounts; the dressings are made with silver enriched in the heavier isotope, so mass spectrometry can follow the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal. The sensor grips Ag⁺ through 4-methylimidazole, a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain whose five-membered ring keeps both ring nitrogens, one bearing H. Bound sensor fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm. To save money, a student tried a 590 nm laser as the light source; even at its highest power, no 520 nm emission was detected, and the buffer absorbs no visible light. Because wound fluid is rich in CO₂, each test chamber is dosed by breaking a sealed glass ampoule of internal volume 33.6 cm³ filled with pure CO₂ at 273 K and 1.00 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.","q":"What is the molecular formula of the sensor's model compound, shown?","fig":"cpt-pe5-4-methylimidazole","choices":["C₄H₅N₂","C₄H₆N₂","C₄H₇N₂","C₄H₁₀N₂"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Odd H with two N: a neutral molecule of C, H and an even number of N must have an even H count; this one forgets the N–H."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Odd H again, impossible for a neutral C, H, N₂ molecule; it adds an H to the N that already has three bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too many H: that is the saturated ring, but this aromatic ring keeps its two double bonds."}],"rule":"Count H atom by atom (C makes 4 bonds, N makes 3). With an even number of N, a neutral molecule has an even number of H.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two N → even H count, so H₅ and H₇ are out. H₁₀ would be a ring with no double bonds; aromatic imidazole has two, 4 H fewer: C₄H₆N₂."},"exp":"The ring has five atoms, **3 C and 2 N**, plus a CH₃ carbon: **4 C**. Two ring carbons each hide **one H**, the ring carbon holding the methyl has none, the CH₃ has **3 H**, and the N drawn with H adds **one more**: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6, so **C₄H₆N₂**. Missing the N–H gives C₄H₅N₂, an H on both N gives C₄H₇N₂, and saturating the ring gives C₄H₁₀N₂.","how":{"ask":"How many carbons and nitrogens are there, and how many hidden H does each atom need to reach its full set of bonds?","know":"• Unlabeled corners and line ends are C; each C makes **4 bonds** and each neutral N makes **3**; missing bonds are hidden H.\n• An aromatic ring keeps its double bonds, so count them when filling each atom.\n• An H drawn on a heteroatom (N–H) counts too; an N with a single and a double ring bond needs no H.\n• With an even number of N, a neutral molecule has an even number of H.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the five-membered ring keeps both nitrogens but only one bears H; the line off the ring is a CH₃.","hl":"one bearing H","calc":["C: 3 ring + 1 methyl = **4 C**","N: **2**"]},{"text":"Concept: give every C four bonds and every N three; the missing bonds are H.","calc":["2 ring C (2 ring bonds + 1 double-bond extra = 3) → **1 H each** = 2 H","ring C with CH₃: 4 bonds already → **0 H**","CH₃ → **3 H**","N–H → **1 H**; other N (single + double) → **0 H**","H total: 2 + 3 + 1 = **6** → **C₄H₆N₂**"]},{"text":"C₄H₅N₂ forgets the N–H, and C₄H₇N₂ adds an H to the N that already has three bonds.","strike":["C₄H₅N₂","C₄H₇N₂"]},{"text":"C₄H₁₀N₂ fills the ring as if both double bonds were gone (the saturated ring).","strike":["C₄H₁₀N₂"]},{"text":"Answer: C₄H₆N₂."}],"trap":"Giving both nitrogens an H: the N with a double bond already has three bonds, so only the drawn N–H counts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"4-methylimidazole","note":"imidazole ring + CH₃"},{"t":"aromatic side chain","note":"ring keeps its double bonds"},{"t":"one bearing H","note":"only one N–H"}]},{"text":"**Worked example: pyridine.** Its six-membered ring has 5 C corners and one N. Each C shows one single and one double ring bond (3 bonds), so it hides 1 H; the N has a single and a double ring bond (3 bonds), so it has none.","fig":"pyridine","calc":["5 C × 1 H = 5 H","N: 0 H","→ **C₅H₅N**"]},{"text":"**Same count here.** Two ring C hide 1 H each, the ring C bearing the methyl hides none, the CH₃ has 3, one N carries its drawn H, and the other N, like pyridine's, has three bonds and no H.","calc":["2 + 0 + 3 + 1 = 6 H","→ **C₄H₆N₂**"]},{"text":"**Also:** the parent rings to know by formula: imidazole (five-membered, two N, one N–H) is C₃H₄N₂; pyrrole (five-membered, one N–H) is C₄H₅N; pyridine (six-membered, one N, no N–H) is C₅H₅N. Methyl on a ring carbon adds CH₂ to the formula."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count every C and N, then give every C four bonds and every N three; the missing bonds are H, and only an N drawn with H carries one. A substituted ring carbon loses the H it would otherwise hide."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-15a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:15#1","pe5-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:15"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P3","passage":"Silver dressings fight wound infection by slowly releasing Ag⁺, and a lab tracks that release with a fluorescent sensor. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, in nearly equal amounts; the dressings are made with silver enriched in the heavier isotope, so mass spectrometry can follow the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal. The sensor grips Ag⁺ through 4-methylimidazole, a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain whose five-membered ring keeps both ring nitrogens, one bearing H. Bound sensor fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm. To save money, a student tried a 590 nm laser as the light source; even at its highest power, no 520 nm emission was detected, and the buffer absorbs no visible light. Because wound fluid is rich in CO₂, each test chamber is dosed by breaking a sealed glass ampoule of internal volume 33.6 cm³ filled with pure CO₂ at 273 K and 1.00 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.","q":"About how many moles of CO₂ does one ampoule hold?","choices":["1.5 × 10⁻³ mol","1.5 mol","1.5 × 10⁻⁶ mol","6.7 × 10² mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Treats cm³ as litres, so the volume is a thousand times too big."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Converts cm³ to L twice, shrinking the answer by another factor of a thousand."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides the molar volume by the gas volume, inverting the ratio."}],"rule":"At STP, n = V(L) ÷ 22.4 L/mol; 1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L. A few tens of cm³ of gas is a few thousandths of a mole.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"22.4 L is 22,400 cm³ per mole. The ampoule is about 1/670 of that, so n is a bit over one-thousandth of a mole."},"exp":"V = 33.6 cm³ = 0.0336 L. At 273 K and 1 atm, n = V/22.4 L·mol⁻¹ = 0.0336/22.4 = **1.5 × 10⁻³ mol**. Using n = PV/RT at 273 K gives the same result.","how":{"ask":"What is the ampoule volume in litres, and what fraction of a mole is that at STP?","know":"• **STP**: 273 K, 1 atm; 1 mol of ideal gas = 22.4 L.\n• **1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L**.\n• Or n = PV/RT with V in L and T in K.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the ampoule's volume in cm³; convert it to litres.","hl":"internal volume 33.6 cm³","calc":["33.6 cm³ = 33.6 mL = 0.0336 L"]},{"text":"Concept: sentence 7 says a mole fills 22.4 L at these conditions, so divide the volume by 22.4 L/mol.","calc":["n = 0.0336 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 1.5 × 10⁻³ mol"]},{"text":"Skipping the cm³ → L step, or doing it twice, misses by a thousand.","strike":["1.5 mol","1.5 × 10⁻⁶ mol"]},{"text":"22.4 ÷ 0.0336 is litres per litre, not moles: the ratio is upside down.","strike":["6.7 × 10² mol"]},{"text":"Answer: about 1.5 × 10⁻³ mol."}],"trap":"Dividing 33.6 by 22.4 without converting cm³ to L, which gives a full mole of gas in a small ampoule."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"33.6 cm³","note":"convert to L"},{"t":"273 K and 1.00 atm","note":"STP"},{"t":"one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L","note":"molar volume"}]},{"text":"**Sense check.** A mole of gas fills 22.4 L, about a large bucket. A small ampoule holds only thousandths of a mole."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moles of gas at STP = volume in litres ÷ 22.4 L/mol (or PV/RT); convert cm³ or mL to litres first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:16#1","pe5-cp:16#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:16"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P3","passage":"Silver dressings fight wound infection by slowly releasing Ag⁺, and a lab tracks that release with a fluorescent sensor. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, in nearly equal amounts; the dressings are made with silver enriched in the heavier isotope, so mass spectrometry can follow the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal. The sensor grips Ag⁺ through 4-methylimidazole, a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain whose five-membered ring keeps both ring nitrogens, one bearing H. Bound sensor fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm. To save money, a student tried a 590 nm laser as the light source; even at its highest power, no 520 nm emission was detected, and the buffer absorbs no visible light. Because wound fluid is rich in CO₂, each test chamber is dosed by breaking a sealed glass ampoule of internal volume 33.6 cm³ filled with pure CO₂ at 273 K and 1.00 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.","q":"Which statement best explains the student's result?","choices":["Photons at 590 nm are too low in energy","Photons at 590 nm hold excess energy as heat","The laser at 590 nm is too dim","The buffer absorbs light at 590 nm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A longer wavelength carries less energy per photon, not more, so there is no excess to lose."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Brightness adds photons, not energy per photon; the passage used the highest power anyway."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The passage says the buffer absorbs no visible light, so nothing blocks the laser."}],"rule":"Fluorescence emits less energy than it absorbs, so the exciting photon must have a shorter wavelength (more energy) than the emitted one; intensity can't make up the gap.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Compare wavelengths: excitation must be shorter than emission. 590 nm is longer than 520 nm, so each photon falls short."},"exp":"E = hc/λ, so a 590 nm photon (≈ 2.1 eV) has **less** energy than a 520 nm photon (≈ 2.4 eV). Fluorescence emits less energy than was absorbed, never more, so **no photon can be emitted at 520 nm**. More laser power only adds photons.","how":{"ask":"Does one 590 nm photon have enough energy to produce one 520 nm photon?","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: shorter λ means more energy per photon (E in eV ≈ 1240 ÷ λ in nm).\n• **Fluorescence** emits less energy than it absorbs; the difference is lost as heat.\n• So the exciting λ must be **shorter** than the emitted λ. Intensity changes how many photons arrive, not the energy of each.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the emission and the working excitation; compare the energy per photon of the 590 nm laser with that of the 520 nm emission.","hl":"fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm","calc":["E(590) ≈ 1240/590 ≈ 2.1 eV","E(520) ≈ 1240/520 ≈ 2.4 eV"]},{"text":"Concept: fluorescence emits less energy than it absorbs. The 590 nm photon has less energy than the emission needs, so it has no excess to shed.","strike":["Photons at 590 nm hold excess energy as heat"]},{"text":"Sentence 5 rules out power and the buffer.","hl":"even at its highest power","strike":["The laser at 590 nm is too dim","The buffer absorbs light at 590 nm"]},{"text":"Answer: 590 nm photons are too low in energy."}],"trap":"Thinking a brighter beam can make up the gap: each photon still carries too little energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"fluoresces green at 520 nm","note":"emitted photon"},{"t":"excited at 470 nm","note":"shorter λ, more energy"},{"t":"highest power","note":"more photons, same energy each"}]},{"text":"**Energy runs downhill in fluorescence.** The molecule absorbs a photon, loses a little energy as heat, then emits a photon of lower energy (longer λ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an excitation wavelength longer than the emission wavelength can never produce that fluorescence, because each absorbed photon must carry more energy than the photon emitted."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-17a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:17#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:17"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P4","passage":"A lab studies a 40-kDa bacterial glucokinase that traps glucose by moving a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate; no water or other molecule takes part. The enzyme is followed by circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers, since only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most residues have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but L-threonine (atoms W–Z labeled in the structure given with a question) has a second one at a labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups. To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes the enzyme, a glutamate was mutated to glutamine, which carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. In cells, an E3 ligase attaches 8.5-kDa ubiquitin to the enzyme one at a time, so chains of several build up, and the ligase cuts no protein; samples taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min were heated in SDS loading buffer and run side by side on SDS-PAGE. Left open to air, the enzyme's one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers; samples of that stock taken at 3, 20 and 120 min were mixed with SDS buffer containing no reducing agent, so S–S bonds stay intact, and run on a second gel.","q":"Which labeled atom is threonine's second stereocenter?","fig":"cpt-pe5-threonine-sites","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The carboxyl carbon is trigonal planar with only three groups, so it can't be a stereocenter."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A CH₃ carbon has three identical H, so it can't be a stereocenter."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The amine N carries two identical H and inverts freely, so it isn't a stereocenter."}],"rule":"A stereocenter is an sp³ atom with four different groups; any atom with two identical groups (H, H) or a double bond is out.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stereocenter = sp³ with four different groups. The β-carbon holds H, OH, CH₃ and Cα, so X. The carboxyl C is planar, CH₃ has three H, NH₂ has two H."},"exp":"Besides the α-carbon, only the **β-carbon (X)** holds four different groups: H, OH, CH₃ and the α-carbon. The carboxyl carbon (W) is planar with three groups, the methyl carbon (Y) has three identical H, and the amine N (Z) has two identical H. Threonine's two stereocenters both make it chiral, which is what a CD signal needs.","how":{"ask":"Apart from the α-carbon, which labeled atom carries four different groups?","know":"• A **stereocenter** (chiral carbon) bears four different groups, so the molecule is not superimposable on its mirror image.\n• **CD** works because a chiral molecule absorbs the two circular polarizations of light by different amounts; in proteins the chiral α-carbons start it.\n• Every standard amino acid except **glycine** has a chiral α-carbon; **threonine** and **isoleucine** also have a chiral β-carbon.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 defines the target: a carbon with four different groups. List the groups on each labeled atom; X is highlighted.","fig":"cpt-pe5-threonine-beta","calc":["W, carboxyl C: =O, OH, Cα → **3 groups, planar**","X, Cβ: H, OH, CH₃, Cα → **4 different**","Y, CH₃: three identical H","Z, N: H, H, Cα → **not a stereocenter**"],"hl":"A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups"},{"text":"The methyl carbon carries three identical H, and the amine N carries two identical H and inverts freely.","strike":["Y","Z"]},{"text":"The carboxyl carbon is trigonal planar with only three groups.","strike":["W"]},{"text":"Answer: X, the β-carbon."}],"trap":"Picking the carboxyl carbon because its neighbors look varied; a planar carbon with only three groups cannot be a stereocenter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers","note":"chirality gives the signal"},{"t":"L-threonine","note":"the residue to test"},{"t":"one stereocenter, the α-carbon","note":"not labeled"},{"t":"has a second one at a labeled atom","note":"find it"}]},{"text":"**Test each atom.** Count its groups, hidden H included; all four must differ."},{"text":"**Also:** the α-carbon (H, NH₂, COOH, side chain) is the stereocenter in every amino acid but glycine, whose α-carbon carries two H. Isoleucine, like threonine, adds a second one at Cβ."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a stereocenter is an sp³ carbon with four different groups; besides the α-carbon of every amino acid but glycine, threonine's β-carbon (H, OH, CH₃, Cα) is one too."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-18a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:18#1","pe5-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:18"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P4","passage":"A lab studies a 40-kDa bacterial glucokinase that traps glucose by moving a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate; no water or other molecule takes part. The enzyme is followed by circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers, since only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most residues have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but L-threonine (atoms W–Z labeled in the structure given with a question) has a second one at a labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups. To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes the enzyme, a glutamate was mutated to glutamine, which carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. In cells, an E3 ligase attaches 8.5-kDa ubiquitin to the enzyme one at a time, so chains of several build up, and the ligase cuts no protein; samples taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min were heated in SDS loading buffer and run side by side on SDS-PAGE. Left open to air, the enzyme's one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers; samples of that stock taken at 3, 20 and 120 min were mixed with SDS buffer containing no reducing agent, so S–S bonds stay intact, and run on a second gel.","q":"Which property of the side chains shown is unchanged by the mutation?","fig":["glutamate-sc","glutamine-sc"],"choices":["Net charge","Carbon count","H-bond donors","Salt-bridge ability"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The carboxylate is −1 at pH 7.0, and the amide is neutral, so the net charge changes."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An ionized –COO⁻ has no N–H or O–H to donate; the amide –NH₂ adds two H-bond donors."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A salt bridge needs a charge; the neutral amide cannot form one, which is the point of the test."}],"rule":"For a substitution, compare the two side chains one property at a time (charge at the pH, H-bonding, size, atoms); keep only the property both share.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Only O⁻ swaps for NH₂; the CH₂–CH₂–C skeleton is untouched. Anything tied to the charge or the N–H changes; the skeleton does not."},"exp":"Glutamate (–CH₂CH₂COO⁻) and glutamine (–CH₂CH₂CONH₂) have the **same three-carbon side chain**; only O⁻ becomes NH₂. That swap removes the −1 charge, so salt bridges are lost, and adds two N–H donors. The **carbon count** is unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Line up the two side chains: what stays the same once O⁻ is swapped for NH₂?","know":"• Glu: –CH₂CH₂COO⁻ (pKa ≈ 4, charged at pH 7). Gln: –CH₂CH₂CONH₂ (never ionizes).\n• A **salt bridge** needs opposite charges.\n• H-bond donors are N–H and O–H; a carboxylate has none, an amide has two.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says glutamine carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻: only O⁻ becomes NH₂ on the same carbon chain.","hl":"carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻"},{"text":"Concept: compare charge at the pH. Sentence 6 makes the charge go from −1 to 0, so the salt bridge goes with it.","strike":["Net charge","Salt-bridge ability"]},{"text":"The amide brings N–H donors the carboxylate lacked.","strike":["H-bond donors"]},{"text":"Answer: the carbon count, three side-chain carbons in both."}],"trap":"Assuming a \"conservative\" swap keeps the charge: Glu → Gln looks small but removes a full negative charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"surface salt bridge","note":"needs a charge"},{"t":"is ionized","note":"Glu is −1"},{"t":"does not ionize","note":"Gln is 0"}]},{"text":"**Property by property.** Charge, H-bonding, size and skeleton each get a yes or no; the unchanged property is the one with the same answer for both."},{"text":"**Also:** when both side chains are uncharged at the pH, for example Phe → Tyr at pH 7 (Tyr –OH pKa ≈ 10), the net charge is what stays the same, while mass and H-bonding change."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find what a mutation leaves unchanged, write both side chains at the stated pH and compare charge, H-bonding, size and carbon skeleton one at a time."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-19a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:19#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:19"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P4","passage":"A lab studies a 40-kDa bacterial glucokinase that traps glucose by moving a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate; no water or other molecule takes part. The enzyme is followed by circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers, since only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most residues have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but L-threonine (atoms W–Z labeled in the structure given with a question) has a second one at a labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups. To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes the enzyme, a glutamate was mutated to glutamine, which carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. In cells, an E3 ligase attaches 8.5-kDa ubiquitin to the enzyme one at a time, so chains of several build up, and the ligase cuts no protein; samples taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min were heated in SDS loading buffer and run side by side on SDS-PAGE. Left open to air, the enzyme's one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers; samples of that stock taken at 3, 20 and 120 min were mixed with SDS buffer containing no reducing agent, so S–S bonds stay intact, and run on a second gel.","q":"As sampling time increases in the ligase reaction, what change in the lanes is expected?","choices":["40-kDa band weakens; heavier ladder forms","40-kDa band weakens; smaller fragments form","No band changes; lanes match","Each band moves down; none fades"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Adding ubiquitin makes the substrate heavier, and heavier chains run a shorter distance, not farther."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Boiling in SDS stops each sample at its own time point, so later lanes hold more modified substrate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"SDS gives every chain the same charge per mass, so heavier products move less, not more."}],"rule":"SDS-PAGE sorts by size: added mass shifts bands up (shorter runs), removed mass shifts them down; over a time course the starting band fades as new bands grow.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Is mass being added or removed? Ubiquitin is added, so new bands sit above the starting one, in steps of about one ubiquitin each."},"exp":"Each ubiquitin adds about 8.5 kDa, so the substrate becomes **heavier species** (≈ 48.5, 57, 65.5 kDa …). On SDS-PAGE, heavier chains run **less far**, so over time the 40-kDa band **fades** and a **ladder of higher bands** grows. Boiling in SDS stops each sample at its time point.","how":{"ask":"Does the reaction add or remove mass, and where do heavier chains run on the gel?","know":"• **SDS-PAGE** coats chains with SDS, giving the same charge per mass, so they separate by **size** alone.\n• **Smaller** chains run **farther** from the well; larger chains stay nearer.\n• Boiling in SDS buffer denatures the enzymes and stops the reaction in each sample.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says ubiquitin is only added and nothing is cut: the enzyme gains mass.","hl":"the ligase cuts no protein"},{"text":"Concept: on SDS-PAGE heavier species run a shorter distance, so new bands appear above the 40-kDa band.","strike":["40-kDa band weakens; smaller fragments form","Each band moves down; none fades"]},{"text":"Each lane captures a later moment of the reaction, so the lanes differ.","strike":["No band changes; lanes match"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Band strength in each lane (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Sampling time (min)","min":0,"max":90,"step":15},"y":{"label":"Share of substrate (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"40-kDa band","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[2,95],[10,78],[30,45],[90,12]]},{"name":"Ubiquitinated bands","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[2,5],[10,22],[30,55],[90,88]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: the 40-kDa band weakens while a ladder of heavier bands forms."}],"trap":"Reading every time course as cleavage: here mass is added, so new bands appear above the start, not below."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"8.5-kDa ubiquitin","note":"mass added each time"},{"t":"the ligase cuts no protein","note":"no smaller fragments"},{"t":"heated in SDS loading buffer","note":"each sample frozen in time"}]},{"text":"**Gel geography.** On SDS-PAGE, the well is at the top; light chains travel farthest, heavy chains stay near the top."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a time course on SDS-PAGE, decide whether the reaction adds or removes mass; the starting band fades and new bands grow above it (mass added) or below it (mass removed)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-19b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:19#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:19"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P4","passage":"A lab studies a 40-kDa bacterial glucokinase that traps glucose by moving a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate; no water or other molecule takes part. The enzyme is followed by circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers, since only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most residues have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but L-threonine (atoms W–Z labeled in the structure given with a question) has a second one at a labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups. To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes the enzyme, a glutamate was mutated to glutamine, which carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. In cells, an E3 ligase attaches 8.5-kDa ubiquitin to the enzyme one at a time, so chains of several build up, and the ligase cuts no protein; samples taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min were heated in SDS loading buffer and run side by side on SDS-PAGE. Left open to air, the enzyme's one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers; samples of that stock taken at 3, 20 and 120 min were mixed with SDS buffer containing no reducing agent, so S–S bonds stay intact, and run on a second gel.","q":"On the second gel, how do later lanes differ from earlier ones?","choices":["40-kDa band weaker; 80-kDa band stronger","40-kDa band stronger; 80-kDa band weaker","40-kDa band weaker; 20-kDa band stronger","All lanes alike; SDS breaks the dimers"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards in time: dimers accumulate as the sample sits in air, so the monomer band shrinks."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Halving the mass would need cleavage; joining two chains doubles it instead."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"SDS breaks noncovalent contacts but not a covalent S–S bond, and no reducing agent was added."}],"rule":"On SDS-PAGE over time, track mass: the starting band fades and the product band grows at the product's mass; covalent links survive SDS unless a reducing agent cuts S–S.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Two 40-kDa chains joined by S–S run as one chain of double mass. Over time, more monomer turns into dimer."},"exp":"Each disulfide-linked dimer weighs 2 × 40 = **80 kDa**. Without a reducing agent the S–S bond survives SDS, so over time the **40-kDa band fades** and the **80-kDa band grows**.","how":{"ask":"What mass does the product have, and does SDS without a reducing agent keep it intact?","know":"• **SDS** unfolds proteins and breaks noncovalent contacts; **reducing agents** (DTT, β-mercaptoethanol) are needed to break S–S bonds.\n• Smaller chains run farther; a dimer runs at twice the monomer mass.\n• In a time course, the starting band weakens as the product band strengthens.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 8 says two chains are joined covalently by an S–S bond: the product is a dimer, twice the mass.","hl":"disulfide-linked dimers","calc":["2 × 40 kDa = 80 kDa"]},{"text":"Concept: SDS breaks noncovalent contacts, not S–S bonds, and there is no reducing agent, so the dimer stays intact on the gel.","hl":"containing no reducing agent","strike":["All lanes alike; SDS breaks the dimers"]},{"text":"Dimers build up with time, so the monomer band shrinks, and no half-size piece forms.","strike":["40-kDa band stronger; 80-kDa band weaker","40-kDa band weaker; 20-kDa band stronger"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Band strength in each lane (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Sampling time (min)","min":0,"max":120,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Share of total protein (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"40-kDa band","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,96],[20,74],[120,30]]},{"name":"80-kDa band","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[3,4],[20,26],[120,70]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: the 40-kDa band weakens and the 80-kDa band strengthens."}],"trap":"Assuming SDS takes every complex apart: it does not break covalent disulfide bonds."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"one exposed cysteine","note":"one S–S per dimer"},{"t":"disulfide-linked dimers","note":"two chains, double mass"},{"t":"no reducing agent","note":"S–S stays intact"}]},{"text":"**Product mass decides where the new band sits.** Joining chains moves material up the gel; cutting moves it down."},{"text":"**Also:** a protease does the opposite. If a 90-kDa chain is cut into 54- and 36-kDa pieces, later lanes show a fainter 90-kDa band and stronger bands below it, which run farther."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** over a time course on SDS-PAGE, the starting band fades while bands at the product masses grow: above it for joined chains, below it for cleaved pieces."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-20a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:20#1","pe5-cp:20#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:20"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P4","passage":"A lab studies a 40-kDa bacterial glucokinase that traps glucose by moving a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate; no water or other molecule takes part. The enzyme is followed by circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers, since only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most residues have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but L-threonine (atoms W–Z labeled in the structure given with a question) has a second one at a labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups. To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes the enzyme, a glutamate was mutated to glutamine, which carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. In cells, an E3 ligase attaches 8.5-kDa ubiquitin to the enzyme one at a time, so chains of several build up, and the ligase cuts no protein; samples taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min were heated in SDS loading buffer and run side by side on SDS-PAGE. Left open to air, the enzyme's one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers; samples of that stock taken at 3, 20 and 120 min were mixed with SDS buffer containing no reducing agent, so S–S bonds stay intact, and run on a second gel.","q":"Glucose 6-phosphate has what molar mass, and by how much does the combined mass of glucose plus ATP change?","choices":["260 g/mol; no net change","260 g/mol; +80 g/mol gain","80 g/mol; no net change","687 g/mol; no net change"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Counts the group gained by glucose but skips the same mass lost when ATP becomes ADP."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Adds ATP and glucose together and skips subtracting the ADP left behind."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the mass of the transferred group itself, not of the product that carries it."}],"rule":"In a group transfer, mass is conserved: product = substrate + cofactor − spent cofactor, and the combined mass of everything does not change.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"The group that leaves ATP is ATP − ADP = 80 g/mol, and it all lands on glucose. Add it to 180; total mass is unchanged."},"exp":"The group moved is ATP − ADP = 507 − 427 = 80 g/mol, so glucose 6-phosphate = 180 + 80 = **260 g/mol**. Mass is conserved: 507 + 180 = 427 + 260 = 687 g/mol on each side, a **net change of zero**.","how":{"ask":"What mass does the cofactor lose, where does it go, and does the total change?","know":"• In a **group transfer**, one molecule's loss is the other's gain.\n• **Mass is conserved**: (substrate + cofactor) = (product + spent cofactor).\n• Group mass = cofactor − spent cofactor.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the ATP, glucose and ADP masses and says no other molecule takes part; the group that moves is ATP − ADP.","hl":"ATP (507 g/mol)","calc":["507 − 427 = 80 g/mol"]},{"text":"Concept: what leaves the donor lands on the acceptor. Add the group to glucose.","calc":["180 + 80 = 260 g/mol"]},{"text":"Check the total: both sides weigh the same.","calc":["507 + 180 = 687 = 427 + 260"],"strike":["260 g/mol; +80 g/mol gain","687 g/mol; no net change"]},{"text":"The 80 g/mol is the group, not the product.","strike":["80 g/mol; no net change"]},{"text":"Answer: 260 g/mol, with no net change."}],"trap":"Treating the phosphoryl group as new mass gained: it came off ATP, so the total is unchanged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"moving a phosphoryl group","note":"group transfer"},{"t":"ADP (427 g/mol)","note":"spent donor"},{"t":"no water or other molecule takes part","note":"mass is conserved"}]},{"text":"**Bookkeeping.** What leaves the donor is exactly what lands on the acceptor.","calc":["product = substrate + (cofactor − spent cofactor)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in any group-transfer reaction, product mass = substrate + (donor − spent donor), and the total mass of the two reactants is unchanged."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-21a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:21#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:21"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P5","passage":"Aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step from a 19-carbon steroid substrate that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group of estradiol. It removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. To purify this membrane enzyme (pI 4.9) from bacteria, a lab broke the cells in 400 mL of lysis buffer holding 50 mM sodium phosphate (pH 7.5) and 150 mM NaCl (58.5 g/mol), and the extract was desalted. It was then loaded at pH 8.5 onto DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge; aromatase bound while most other proteins washed through, and a NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M released it. After three column steps in all, mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene, the GST tag was retained, and the enzyme eluted from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta. Separately, for a denaturing gel of every membrane protein, where unfolding is acceptable, the proteins had to be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water by one of four additives drawn with a question.","q":"Which structure is the substrate of aromatase?","choices":["Testosterone","Oleic acid","Phosphatidylcholine","11-cis-Retinal"],"choiceFigs":["testosterone","oleic-acid","phosphatidylcholine","11-cis-retinal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A fatty acid is one unbranched chain with no rings, so it cannot supply four fused rings."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A glycerophospholipid is glycerol, two fatty acids and a phosphate head: no ring system at all."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Retinal has a single six-membered ring on a polyene chain, not a four-ring steroid core."}],"rule":"A steroid is made from a lipid that already has the four fused rings (6-6-6-5); a precursor with no rings or one ring is out.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count fused rings in each drawing. Only one has the 6-6-6-5 steroid core; the other three have zero or one ring."},"exp":"Steroids keep the four fused rings of **cholesterol**, their parent. Aromatase acts on the 19-carbon androgen **testosterone**: it removes C19 and aromatizes ring A to give estradiol. Oleic acid and phosphatidylcholine have no rings, and retinal has only one.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing already has the four fused rings and the 19 carbons the passage describes?","know":"• **Steroids** share a four-ring core, three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring.\n• **Cholesterol** (C27) is the parent: side-chain cleavage gives pregnenolone (C21), then progesterone and cortisol (C21), androgens such as testosterone (C19), and estrogens (C18).\n• Fatty acids, phospholipids and retinoids are not steroids.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the substrate's skeleton: four fused rings, 19 carbons and a 17-OH; sentence 2 adds a ring-A ketone.","hl":"already has all four fused rings"},{"text":"Concept: steroid hormones come from precursors that keep the four fused rings. Check each drawing for the steroid core.","calc":["testosterone: 4 fused rings, C19, 17-OH, ring-A ketone","oleic acid: 0 rings","phosphatidylcholine: 0 rings","retinal: 1 ring"]},{"text":"No rings means no steroid skeleton to start from.","strike":["Oleic acid","Phosphatidylcholine"]},{"text":"One ring on a long polyene chain is a retinoid, not a steroid.","strike":["11-cis-Retinal"]},{"text":"Answer: testosterone, the C19 androgen."}],"trap":"Choosing a lipid just because it is a lipid: only a molecule with the four fused rings can become a steroid hormone in one step."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"in a single step","note":"the substrate is one step back"},{"t":"19-carbon steroid substrate","note":"C19 androgen"},{"t":"all four fused rings","note":"steroid core already there"}]},{"text":"**One family tree.** Cholesterol → pregnenolone → progesterone → androgens → estrogens; each step trims carbons but keeps the four rings."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** steroid hormones come from cholesterol through precursors that keep the four fused rings; pick the precursor whose ring core and carbon count fit the step described."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-22a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:22#1","pe5-cp:22#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:22"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P5","passage":"Aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step from a 19-carbon steroid substrate that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group of estradiol. It removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. To purify this membrane enzyme (pI 4.9) from bacteria, a lab broke the cells in 400 mL of lysis buffer holding 50 mM sodium phosphate (pH 7.5) and 150 mM NaCl (58.5 g/mol), and the extract was desalted. It was then loaded at pH 8.5 onto DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge; aromatase bound while most other proteins washed through, and a NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M released it. After three column steps in all, mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene, the GST tag was retained, and the enzyme eluted from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta. Separately, for a denaturing gel of every membrane protein, where unfolding is acceptable, the proteins had to be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water by one of four additives drawn with a question.","q":"The DEAE step is a purification by:","choices":["anion exchange","cation exchange","affinity","size exclusion"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A cation exchanger has a negative resin that binds positive proteins; here the resin is positive."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No specific ligand or tag is involved; salt releases the protein by screening charge, not by competing."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Size exclusion never binds proteins; it only delays small ones in the bead pores."}],"rule":"Name a column by how the protein binds and is released: by charge (salt elutes) → ion exchange named for the ion the resin binds; by a specific tag or ligand (competitor elutes) → affinity.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Positive resin + negative protein + salt elution = an exchanger for negative ions. A tag and a competitor would make it affinity."},"exp":"Below pH 8.5 sits its pI (4.9), so aromatase is **negative** when loaded and binds the **positive** DEAE groups. Rising NaCl displaces it by charge screening. A positive resin that exchanges negative ions is **anion exchange**.","how":{"ask":"What charge do the protein and the resin carry, and what releases the protein?","know":"• Above its **pI**, a protein is negative; below it, positive.\n• **Anion exchange**: + resin (e.g., DEAE) binds − proteins. **Cation exchange**: − resin (e.g., CM) binds + proteins. Salt elutes both.\n• **Affinity**: a specific tag or ligand binds; a competitor elutes (His-tag on Ni²⁺, eluted by imidazole).\n• **Size exclusion**: no binding; large proteins elute first.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the enzyme's pI; it is loaded at pH 8.5, above the pI, so aromatase is negative.","hl":"pI 4.9","calc":["pH 8.5 > pI 4.9 → net charge −"]},{"text":"Concept: an exchanger is named for the ion it binds. Sentence 4 says the resin is positive, so it holds negative ions: not a cation exchanger.","hl":"carry a positive charge","strike":["cation exchange"]},{"text":"Salt, not a competing ligand, releases it, and a binding step rules out size exclusion.","strike":["affinity","size exclusion"]},{"text":"Answer: anion exchange."}],"trap":"Naming the column after the resin's own charge: a positive resin binds anions, so it is an anion exchanger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"pI 4.9","note":"below pH 8.5 → protein −"},{"t":"carry a positive charge","note":"resin binds anions"},{"t":"NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M","note":"salt elutes → ion exchange"}]},{"text":"**Also:** a step where a His₆-tagged protein binds immobilized Ni²⁺ and is eluted by imidazole is **affinity**: the tag binds a specific metal site, and imidazole, the His side-chain ring, competes for the Ni²⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to categorize a column step, ask what binds the protein and what releases it: charge and salt → ion exchange (named for the ion the resin binds), a tag and a competitor → affinity, no binding → size exclusion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-23a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:23#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:23"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P5","passage":"Aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step from a 19-carbon steroid substrate that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group of estradiol. It removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. To purify this membrane enzyme (pI 4.9) from bacteria, a lab broke the cells in 400 mL of lysis buffer holding 50 mM sodium phosphate (pH 7.5) and 150 mM NaCl (58.5 g/mol), and the extract was desalted. It was then loaded at pH 8.5 onto DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge; aromatase bound while most other proteins washed through, and a NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M released it. After three column steps in all, mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene, the GST tag was retained, and the enzyme eluted from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta. Separately, for a denaturing gel of every membrane protein, where unfolding is acceptable, the proteins had to be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water by one of four additives drawn with a question.","q":"Which additive would do both jobs for the denaturing gel?","choices":["Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)","Hexadecane","Glycerol","Sodium dodecyl sulfate"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-dmso","cpt-pe5-hexadecane","p1-glycerol","sodium-dodecyl-sulfate"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"No oily tail: DMSO is a small polar solvent, not a detergent, so it can't coat a membrane protein."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No polar head: a pure alkane won't dissolve in water, so it can't carry a protein into it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycerol is all polar head with no oily tail; it mixes with water but can't grip the protein's buried surface."}],"rule":"To pull a protein out of a membrane and keep it in water, pick the amphipathic molecule: a long hydrophobic tail and a polar or charged head on one molecule.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for one molecule with both a long C–H chain and a polar head. DMSO and glycerol lack a tail; hexadecane lacks a head."},"exp":"A detergent must be **amphipathic**. SDS has a **12-carbon tail** that coats the proteins' oily surfaces and a charged **sulfate head** that faces water; it also unfolds them, which is fine for a denaturing gel. DMSO and glycerol have no tail; hexadecane has no head.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing has, on one molecule, a long C–H chain and a polar or charged head?","know":"• A **detergent** is amphipathic: a long hydrophobic tail plus a polar head. Tails coat the protein's lipid-facing surface; heads face water, forming micelles.\n• Ionic detergents (SDS) also denature; nonionic sugar-headed ones (octyl glucoside, dodecyl maltoside) keep the fold.\n• A molecule that is only polar or only nonpolar cannot bridge the two environments.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 sets two jobs, pull the proteins out of the bilayer and keep them in water, and it allows unfolding.","hl":"kept dissolved in water"},{"text":"Concept: both jobs take an amphipathic detergent, a long C–H tail and a polar head on one molecule. Sort each drawing into tail and head.","calc":["SDS: tail **C₁₂**, head **–OSO₃⁻ Na⁺**","DMSO: tail **none**, small polar S=O","hexadecane: tail **C₁₆**, head **none**","glycerol: tail **none**, **3 O–H**"]},{"text":"DMSO and glycerol are all head.","strike":["Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)","Glycerol"]},{"text":"Hexadecane is all tail.","strike":["Hexadecane"]},{"text":"Answer: sodium dodecyl sulfate."}],"trap":"Choosing glycerol because it is very water-soluble; with no tail it cannot cover a membrane protein's hydrophobic surface."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"pulled out of the lipid bilayer","note":"needs a tail"},{"t":"kept dissolved in water","note":"needs a head"},{"t":"unfolding is acceptable","note":"a harsh, ionic detergent is fine"}]},{"text":"**Read each drawing.** A chain of only C and H is oily (the tail); O–H groups or charges are polar (the head). A detergent has both on one molecule."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to move a membrane protein into water, use an amphipathic detergent (long hydrophobic tail + polar head); choose a nonionic one if the fold must survive."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-24a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:24#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:24"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P5","passage":"Aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step from a 19-carbon steroid substrate that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group of estradiol. It removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. To purify this membrane enzyme (pI 4.9) from bacteria, a lab broke the cells in 400 mL of lysis buffer holding 50 mM sodium phosphate (pH 7.5) and 150 mM NaCl (58.5 g/mol), and the extract was desalted. It was then loaded at pH 8.5 onto DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge; aromatase bound while most other proteins washed through, and a NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M released it. After three column steps in all, mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene, the GST tag was retained, and the enzyme eluted from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta. Separately, for a denaturing gel of every membrane protein, where unfolding is acceptable, the proteins had to be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water by one of four additives drawn with a question.","q":"How many moles and grams of NaCl does one batch of lysis buffer need?","choices":["0.060 mol; 3.5 g","0.60 mol; 35 g","0.0060 mol; 0.35 g","2.7 mol; 160 g"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Turns 400 mL into 4.0 L, a factor-of-ten slip in the volume."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Turns 400 mL into 0.040 L, a factor-of-ten slip the other way."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides the volume by the concentration instead of multiplying them."}],"rule":"mol = M × V(L), with mM → M and mL → L; then grams = mol × molar mass. The other buffer components are distractors.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mM × mL gives μmol: 150 × 400 = 60,000 μmol = 0.06 mol. Times about 60 g/mol is about 3.5 g."},"exp":"n = (0.150 mol/L)(0.400 L) = **0.060 mol**. Mass = 0.060 mol × 58.5 g/mol ≈ **3.5 g**. The phosphate is a separate component and does not change the NaCl amount.","how":{"ask":"What are the NaCl concentration and the batch volume in M and L, and what is their product?","know":"• **mol = M × V**, V in litres; **mM = 10⁻³ M**.\n• **grams = mol × molar mass**.\n• Each solute in a buffer is weighed separately.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the NaCl concentration and the batch volume; the 50 mM phosphate is a distractor.","hl":"150 mM NaCl","calc":["M = 0.150 mol/L","V = 400 mL = 0.400 L"]},{"text":"Concept: moles = M × V(L), then grams = moles × molar mass.","calc":["n = 0.150 × 0.400 = 0.060 mol","m = 0.060 × 58.5 ≈ 3.5 g"]},{"text":"A slip in the mL → L step moves both numbers by ten.","strike":["0.60 mol; 35 g","0.0060 mol; 0.35 g"]},{"text":"V ÷ M is not moles: the operation is backwards.","strike":["2.7 mol; 160 g"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.060 mol, about 3.5 g."}],"trap":"Using 50 mM, the phosphate concentration, for the salt: each component has its own concentration."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"150 mM NaCl","note":"the salt asked about"},{"t":"58.5 g/mol","note":"for grams"},{"t":"400 mL","note":"convert to L"}]},{"text":"**Two lines.** mol = M × V(L); g = mol × molar mass. Keep the zeros straight by writing each quantity in base units."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to make a buffer, moles of each solute = its molarity × the volume in litres; multiply by its molar mass for the grams to weigh."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-25a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:25#1","pe5-cp:25#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:25"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P5","passage":"Aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step from a 19-carbon steroid substrate that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group of estradiol. It removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. To purify this membrane enzyme (pI 4.9) from bacteria, a lab broke the cells in 400 mL of lysis buffer holding 50 mM sodium phosphate (pH 7.5) and 150 mM NaCl (58.5 g/mol), and the extract was desalted. It was then loaded at pH 8.5 onto DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge; aromatase bound while most other proteins washed through, and a NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M released it. After three column steps in all, mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene, the GST tag was retained, and the enzyme eluted from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta. Separately, for a denaturing gel of every membrane protein, where unfolding is acceptable, the proteins had to be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water by one of four additives drawn with a question.","q":"Which result best shows the purified aromatase is natively folded?","choices":["Its kcat equals the placental one","Its weight fits the gene sequence","Its GST tag survived purification","It went through three column steps"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mass spectrometry weighs the chain, and an unfolded aromatase has exactly the same mass."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A tag stays attached whether the chain is folded or not, so it says nothing about the fold."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Columns show purity, not structure; unfolded proteins pass through columns too."}],"rule":"Only a functional test, matching the native protein's activity, proves the fold; an unfolded chain keeps its mass, its tag and its purity.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only activity proves the fold: a denatured chain keeps its mass, its tag and its purification history. Matching the placenta enzyme's kcat."},"exp":"Turnover needs a properly built **active site**, so a kcat equal to the placenta aromatase's shows a **native-like fold**. Mass, the retained tag and the column steps would look the same for an unfolded chain.","how":{"ask":"Which of these results would come out differently if the aromatase were unfolded?","know":"• The **native fold** (tertiary structure) builds the active site; an unfolded chain keeps the same sequence, mass and tags.\n• **kcat** (turnover number) counts catalytic events per active site per second, so it needs a correctly formed site.\n• Mass, a retained tag and the number of purification steps report identity and purity, not structure.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 lists mass, tag and peak shape, and sentence 6 adds activity. Look for the one result that depends on 3-D shape.","hl":"mass predicted from the gene"},{"text":"Concept: ask whether an unfolded chain would give the same result. Mass spectrometry weighs the chain, and an unfolded aromatase has exactly the same mass.","hl":"mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene","strike":["Its weight fits the gene sequence"]},{"text":"A tag stays attached, and a protein passes through columns, whether or not it is folded.","strike":["Its GST tag survived purification","It went through three column steps"]},{"text":"Catalysis needs a correctly built active site, so matching the natural enzyme's kcat shows a native-like fold. Answer: its kcat equals the placental one."}],"trap":"Trusting the exact mass: it proves the right chain was made, but a denatured chain weighs the same."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"mass predicted from the gene","note":"same if unfolded"},{"t":"the GST tag was retained","note":"same if unfolded"},{"t":"its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta","note":"needs a working active site"},{"t":"natively folded","note":"the goal"}]},{"text":"**Ask of each result: would an unfolded protein give the same answer?** If yes, it says nothing about the fold."},{"text":"**Also:** mass, a retained tag and the number of purification steps are identical for folded and unfolded protein, so none of them can show the fold."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only a native-like function (the same kcat, the same ligand Kd) shows a purified protein is folded; mass, tags and purity do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-26a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:26#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:26"],"q":"In massive stars, fusing two ¹²C nuclei requires a far hotter core than fusing two ¹H nuclei does. Which statement best explains why?","choices":["Carbon nuclei repel far more strongly","Carbon nuclei feel a weaker strong force","Heavier nuclei move faster when heated","Carbon must first shed its electrons"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"At a given temperature, heavier nuclei move more slowly, which makes fusing them harder, not easier."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The strong force acts only at short range and is not what keeps nuclei apart before they touch."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"In a stellar core both gases are fully ionized plasmas; bare nuclei are present either way."}],"rule":"Fusion needs nuclei fast enough to beat their electric repulsion, which grows with the product of their charges (Z₁Z₂); bigger Z means a hotter plasma is needed.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Coulomb barrier ∝ Z₁Z₂: 1 × 1 for hydrogen versus 6 × 6 for carbon, a 36-fold larger barrier to climb."},"exp":"Nuclei must get within about a femtometre before the short-range strong force can bind them, and their **Coulomb repulsion** grows as Z₁Z₂. Two carbon nuclei (Z = 6) repel 36 times as strongly as two protons, so only much faster nuclei get close enough: a hotter core.","how":{"ask":"What keeps two nuclei apart, and how does it scale with their charge?","know":"• Nuclei are positive and repel (Coulomb), with a barrier ∝ Z₁Z₂.\n• The **strong force** binds nucleons but only within about 10⁻¹⁵ m.\n• Temperature sets the average kinetic energy; at the same T, heavier particles move more slowly.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the charge products.","hl":"two ¹²C nuclei","calc":["H + H: Z₁Z₂ = 1","C + C: Z₁Z₂ = 36"]},{"text":"At equal temperature, heavier nuclei are slower, not faster.","strike":["Heavier nuclei move faster when heated"]},{"text":"The strong force only matters once they are close; and stellar cores are fully ionized anyway.","strike":["Carbon nuclei feel a weaker strong force","Carbon must first shed its electrons"]},{"text":"Answer: carbon nuclei repel far more strongly."}],"trap":"Blaming the strong force: it is the electric repulsion, before contact, that sets the needed temperature."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two ¹²C nuclei","note":"Z = 6 each"},{"t":"two ¹H nuclei","note":"Z = 1 each"},{"t":"far hotter core","note":"more kinetic energy needed"}]},{"text":"**Two forces.** Electric repulsion acts at long range and pushes nuclei apart; the strong force pulls only once they nearly touch. High temperature gives nuclei the speed to get through the repulsion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fusion needs very high temperatures because only very fast nuclei can push through their electric repulsion, and that barrier grows with the product of the charges."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-26b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:26"],"q":"The strong nuclear force binds protons inside a nucleus, yet two free protons 10 nm apart only repel each other. Which property of the strong force explains this?","choices":["Its short range","Its weak strength","Its repulsive sign","Its charge dependence"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"False premise: the strong force is the strongest of the four forces, so weakness can't be it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong sign: between nucleons it attracts; that is why nuclei hold together."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The strong force acts the same on protons and neutrons; it doesn't depend on charge."}],"rule":"The strong force is the strongest and attractive, but it reaches only about a nucleus's width (~1 fm); farther apart, only Coulomb repulsion acts.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Strong force: attractive, strongest, but reaches only ~1 fm. 10 nm is about 10⁷ fm away, far outside its reach, so only Coulomb repulsion acts. Short range."},"exp":"The strong force attracts nucleons and overpowers Coulomb repulsion, but it acts **only over about a nucleon's width, a few femtometres (~10⁻¹⁵ m)**. At 10 nm, ten million times farther, it is effectively zero, so only the electric repulsion remains.","how":{"ask":"Decide why a force that wins inside a nucleus does nothing at a larger distance.","know":"• The **strong force** attracts protons and neutrons alike and is stronger than the electric force at nuclear distances.\n• It is **short-ranged**: it falls to nearly zero beyond a few × 10⁻¹⁵ m.\n• The electric force falls only as 1/r², so it dominates at larger distances.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the separation with the strong force's reach.","hl":"10 nm apart","calc":["10 nm = 10⁻⁸ m","10⁻⁸ m ÷ 10⁻¹⁵ m = 10⁷ times too far"]},{"text":"The strong force is the strongest force, is attractive, and acts the same on protons and neutrons.","strike":["Its weak strength","Its repulsive sign","Its charge dependence"]},{"text":"Answer: its short range."}],"trap":"Calling the strong force weak because it vanishes at distance: it is the strongest force, but only within about one nucleus."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"binds protons inside a nucleus","note":"wins at ~10⁻¹⁵ m"},{"t":"10 nm apart","note":"far beyond its reach"}]},{"text":"**Two forces, two ranges.** Coulomb repulsion reaches far; the strong attraction reaches only about a nucleon's width."},{"text":"**Where each wins.** Inside a nucleus the strong force wins; beyond about 10⁻¹⁵ m, repulsion wins.","calc":["r ≲ 10⁻¹⁵ m → strong force dominates","r ≫ 10⁻¹⁵ m → electric repulsion only"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the strong force beats Coulomb repulsion only within about 10⁻¹⁵ m; beyond that range, like charges simply repel."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-27a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:27#1","pe5-cp:27#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:27"],"q":"(R)-Carvone, shown with its mirror image (S)-carvone, smells of spearmint, while the S form smells of caraway. Which property differs between them?","fig":["cpt-pe5-r-carvone","cpt-pe5-s-carvone"],"choices":["Sign of rotation","Boiling temperature","Infrared spectrum","Solubility in ethanol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mirror-image molecules have the same intermolecular forces, so they boil at the same temperature."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"IR sees bonds and functional groups, and mirror images have identical bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ethanol is achiral, so it interacts with both mirror images identically; their solubility is the same."}],"rule":"Enantiomers match in every achiral property (bp, IR, solubility in achiral solvents); they differ only toward polarized light or other chiral things.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mirror-image pair = identical in every achiral test (bp, IR, solubility in ethanol). Only plane-polarized light (or a chiral receptor, like smell) tells them apart. Sign of rotation."},"exp":"The two forms are **enantiomers** (mirror images at one stereocenter). In an achiral setting they share boiling point, density, solubility and IR spectrum; they differ only in the **sign of optical rotation** and in how they bind chiral partners, such as the protein odor receptors that tell them apart.","how":{"ask":"Decide which properties can tell two mirror-image molecules apart.","know":"• **Enantiomers** have identical physical properties in an achiral environment: bp, mp, density, solubility in achiral solvents, IR and NMR.\n• They rotate plane-polarized light by **equal amounts in opposite directions**.\n• They interact differently with **chiral** things: enzymes, receptors, chiral columns.","steps":[{"text":"R and S at the same single stereocenter, with the same connectivity, make the two forms enantiomers.","hl":"shown with its mirror image (S)-carvone"},{"text":"Boiling point, IR and solubility in an achiral solvent such as ethanol are the same for both.","strike":["Boiling temperature","Infrared spectrum","Solubility in ethanol"]},{"text":"Answer: the sign of optical rotation."}],"trap":"Concluding from the different smells that the molecules differ in ordinary physical properties: the odor difference comes from chiral receptors."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"mirror image","note":"enantiomers"},{"t":"smells of spearmint","note":"chiral receptor response"}]},{"text":"**Classify the pair.** Same connectivity, opposite configuration at the one stereocenter: enantiomers."},{"text":"**Achiral tests can't tell them apart.** Heat, solvents like ethanol and IR light treat both the same."},{"text":"**Also:** enantiomers smell or act differently because odor receptors and enzymes are made of L-amino acids and are therefore chiral, binding each form differently."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** enantiomers share bp, density, solubility and IR; they differ only in the sign of optical rotation and in interactions with chiral partners."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-28a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:28#1","pe5-cp:28#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:28"],"q":"Water flows at 0.40 m/s through a hose of 2.4 cm inner diameter. What is its speed where the hose narrows to 0.80 cm?","choices":["3.6 m/s","1.2 m/s","0.044 m/s","0.40 m/s"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides by the area ratio, making the water slow down where the pipe narrows."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Scales speed by the diameter ratio alone and skips squaring it; flow depends on area."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Speed can't stay the same: the same volume per second must pass through a smaller area."}],"rule":"Continuity: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ and A ∝ d², so v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)². Narrower means faster, by the square of the diameter ratio.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Diameter ratio 2.4/0.80 = 3, so the area falls 9-fold and the speed rises 9-fold from 0.40 m/s."},"exp":"For an incompressible fluid, A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. Since A = πd²/4, v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)² = 0.40 × (2.4/0.80)² = 0.40 × 9 = **3.6 m/s**.","how":{"ask":"By what factor does the cross-sectional area shrink, and how does that change the speed?","know":"• **Continuity**: volume flow rate Q = Av is the same everywhere in a pipe.\n• **A = πd²/4**, so area scales with diameter squared.\n• Halving the diameter quarters the area and makes the fluid 4 times faster.","steps":[{"text":"Find the diameter ratio.","hl":"2.4 cm inner diameter","calc":["d₁/d₂ = 2.4/0.80 = 3"]},{"text":"Square it for the area ratio, and scale the speed up by that factor.","calc":["A₁/A₂ = 3² = 9","v₂ = 0.40 × 9 = 3.6 m/s"]},{"text":"Using the diameter ratio unsquared, or dividing by the area ratio, gets the size or the direction wrong.","strike":["1.2 m/s","0.044 m/s"]},{"text":"The same flow through less area can't keep the same speed.","strike":["0.40 m/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.6 m/s."}],"trap":"Scaling by the diameter ratio instead of its square: flow depends on area."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"0.40 m/s","note":"v₁"},{"t":"2.4 cm inner diameter","note":"d₁"},{"t":"narrows to 0.80 cm","note":"d₂"}]},{"text":"**Continuity.** What enters each second must leave each second: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂.","calc":["v₂ = v₁ × (d₁/d₂)²"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a narrowing pipe, speed rises by the square of the diameter ratio (halve d → 4× v)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-29a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:29"],"q":"Which of the following ions has the same ground-state electron configuration as a noble gas?","choices":["Se²⁻","Fe³⁺","Cu⁺","Sn²⁺"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Odd count: 26 − 3 = 23 electrons, and every noble-gas count (2, 10, 18, 36, 54) is even."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"29 − 1 = 28 electrons falls between Ar (18) and Kr (36), not on a noble-gas count."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"50 − 2 = 48 electrons ([Kr]4d¹⁰5s²) sits between Kr (36) and Xe (54)."}],"rule":"Count electrons as Z − charge (add for anions) and compare with the noble-gas counts 2, 10, 18, 36, 54, 86.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Electrons = Z − charge; noble counts 2, 10, 18, 36, 54, 86. Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = 36 (Kr) ✓. Fe³⁺ 23, Cu⁺ 28, Sn²⁺ 48: none."},"exp":"**Electrons = Z − charge.** Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = **36**, the count of krypton. Fe³⁺ has 23, Cu⁺ 28 and Sn²⁺ 48, none a noble-gas count (2, 10, 18, 36, 54); a half-filled or full d subshell is not a noble-gas configuration.","how":{"ask":"Count each ion's electrons and compare with the noble-gas counts.","know":"• **Electrons = Z − charge** (a 2− ion has Z + 2).\n• Noble-gas electron counts: **2 (He), 10 (Ne), 18 (Ar), 36 (Kr), 54 (Xe)**.\n• Transition-metal ions lose s electrons first but keep their d electrons, so they rarely match a noble gas.","steps":[{"text":"Count electrons in each ion.","hl":"same ground-state electron configuration as a noble gas","calc":["Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = **36** = Kr ✓","Fe³⁺: 26 − 3 = 23","Cu⁺: 29 − 1 = 28","Sn²⁺: 50 − 2 = 48"]},{"text":"23, 28 and 48 are not noble-gas counts: Fe³⁺ is [Ar]3d⁵, Cu⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰, and Sn²⁺ is [Kr]4d¹⁰5s².","strike":["Fe³⁺","Cu⁺","Sn²⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: Se²⁻."}],"trap":"Choosing an ion with a half-filled or full d subshell: those are stable, but not noble-gas configurations."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"noble gas","note":"2, 10, 18, 36, 54 electrons"},{"t":"ground-state","note":"count electrons"}]},{"text":"**Count electrons.** Subtract a positive charge from Z; add a negative charge to it.","calc":["Se: Z = 34, charge 2− → 36"]},{"text":"**Match a noble gas.** 36 electrons is krypton's count, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** electrons = Z − charge, and an ion is noble-gas-like only if that count is 2, 10, 18, 36 or 54."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-30a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:30"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which tyrosine can be tagged selectively, and in which buffer?","choices":["Y21, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer B","Y118, in buffer B"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"In buffer A another tyrosine is tagged too (30%), so Y21 is never tagged alone there."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"In buffer A that residue is tagged alongside Y21 (70%), so it isn't tagged alone there."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Y118 reads none in both buffers; a residue never tagged can't be tagged selectively."}],"rule":"Selective means one residue tagged while every other reads none: scan each buffer for a column with a single nonzero value.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Selective = exactly one nonzero in a buffer's column. Buffer A tags Y21 and Y64 together; buffer B tags only Y64. Y64, in buffer B."},"exp":"**Selective** means one residue reacts while every other residue reads zero. In buffer B only **Y64** is tagged (50%); in buffer A, Y21 and Y64 are tagged together, and Y118 is never tagged.","how":{"ask":"Under which buffer does exactly one tyrosine react while the others stay at zero?","know":"• **Selective modification**: under one condition, the target is labeled and **every other residue reads 0%**.\n• Read the data **one condition at a time**; a high percentage alone is not enough if another residue is also labeled.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives buffer A as one row: two tyrosines are tagged.","hl":"Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none","calc":["buffer A: Y21 and Y64 both > 0 → **not selective**"]},{"text":"Concept: selective means only that residue reacts. Nothing tagged in buffer A is tagged alone: Y21 brings Y64 with it, and Y64 brings Y21.","strike":["Y21, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer A"]},{"text":"The same sentence gives buffer B as one row: only one tyrosine reacts.","hl":"Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none","calc":["buffer B: Y64 > 0, Y21 = 0, Y118 = 0 → **Y64 alone**"]},{"text":"Y118 reads none in both buffers, so it is never tagged at all, selectively or otherwise.","strike":["Y118, in buffer B"]},{"text":"Answer: Y64, in buffer B."}],"trap":"Picking the largest percentage (Y21 at 70%): it is tagged alongside Y64, so it is not selective."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what each number means** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"buffer A","note":"condition 1"},{"t":"Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none","note":"two residues react"},{"t":"buffer B","note":"condition 2"},{"t":"Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none","note":"one residue reacts"}]},{"text":"**Define the target.** A selective condition labels the residue of interest while all the others read zero. Size of the percentage doesn't matter; exclusivity does."},{"text":"**Scan each condition across.** Count the nonzero entries in each row: one nonzero entry is a selective condition for that residue.","calc":["buffer A: 2 nonzero → none selective","buffer B: 1 nonzero (Y64) → Y64 selective"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find a selective condition, read the table one condition at a time and pick the condition where only the target residue reacts and every other residue reads 0%."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-31a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:31#1","pe5-cp:31#2","pe5-cp:31#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:31"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"At pH 7.0, the side chain of the new residue (models shown) is best categorized as:","fig":["phenylalanine-sc","p1-benzoic-acid"],"choices":["Acidic","Basic","Hydrophobic","Polar neutral"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A –COOH donates its proton and turns negative; basic groups accept a proton and turn positive."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips the pKa check: nearly three units above its pKa, the –COOH is almost fully ionized, so it is charged."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The ring alone is nonpolar, but an ionized carboxylate makes the whole side chain charged and water-loving."}],"rule":"Classify a modified side chain by its new group's pKa versus the pH: an acid with pKa < pH is ionized (acidic, −); a base with pKa > pH is protonated (basic, +).","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"pKa 4.2 vs pH 7.0: the pH is higher, so the –COOH has lost its proton. A side chain carrying –COO⁻ is acidic."},"exp":"With pH 7.0 well above the pKa of 4.2, the ring –COOH is about 99.8% ionized to **–COO⁻**, so the side chain carries a negative charge, like Asp and Glu: it is **acidic**.","how":{"ask":"Is the added group an acid or a base, and is it ionized at pH 7.0?","know":"• **pH > pKa**: the group is mostly deprotonated; **pH < pKa**: mostly protonated. Each unit is a factor of 10.\n• An ionized –COOH (–COO⁻) makes a side chain **acidic** (Asp, Glu).\n• A protonated amine (–NH₃⁺) makes it **basic** (Lys, Arg).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the new ring –COOH a pKa of about 4.2, and binding is measured at pH 7.0.","hl":"pKa about 4.2","calc":["7.0 − 4.2 = 2.8 units above","COO⁻ : COOH ≈ 10^2.8 ≈ 600 : 1"]},{"text":"Concept: above its pKa an acid has given up its proton, so it is negative, not positive.","strike":["Basic"]},{"text":"It is charged, so it is neither neutral nor hydrophobic.","strike":["Polar neutral","Hydrophobic"]},{"text":"Answer: acidic."}],"trap":"Calling it hydrophobic because the side chain is mostly a benzene ring: one ionized group outweighs the ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"4-carboxyphenylalanine","note":"an acid added to the ring"},{"t":"pKa about 4.2","note":"compare with the pH"},{"t":"pH 7.0","note":"well above the pKa"}]},{"text":"**Compare pKa with pH.** Above the pKa, the group has lost its proton; below it, it keeps it."},{"text":"**Also:** an added amine works the other way. A ring –CH₂NH₂ whose –NH₃⁺ form has pKa ≈ 9.5 stays protonated at pH 7.0, so that side chain is basic (+)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to classify a modified residue, compare the new group's pKa with the pH: an ionized acid makes it acidic, a protonated base makes it basic, and an un-ionized polar group makes it polar neutral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-32a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:32#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:32"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"The catalytic acid is most likely which residue?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Activity of the enzyme versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":11,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Relative activity (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,99],[6,98],[6.5,97],[7,95],[7.5,86],[8.3,50],[8.8,22],[9.3,8],[9.8,3],[10.3,1],[11,0]]}]},"choices":["Cysteine","Tyrosine","Aspartate","Arginine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Tyrosine (pKa ≈ 10.1) would stay protonated, and active, until about pH 10, above where this curve drops."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9) loses its H⁺ near pH 4, far below where this activity drops."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Arginine (pKa ≈ 12.5) would stay protonated, and active, across the whole pH range plotted."}],"rule":"Activity that needs a protonated residue drops near that residue's pKa: read the pH of the drop, then match it (Asp ≈ 4, His ≈ 6, Tyr ≈ 10, Arg ≈ 12.5).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Activity drops where the needed H⁺ leaves, at pH ≈ pKa. The drop here sits near pH 8, matching Cys –SH (~8.3); Asp ~4, Tyr ~10, Arg ~12.5 don't fit."},"exp":"Activity tracks how much of the residue is still protonated, so the **pH at 50% activity equals its pKa**. On this plot that is **pH ≈ 8.3**, which matches cysteine's thiol (–SH, pKa ≈ 8.3). Asp (≈ 3.9), Tyr (≈ 10.1) and Arg (≈ 12.5) would put the midpoint elsewhere.","how":{"ask":"Read the pH at half activity; that pH is the pKa of the residue. Which side chain has that pKa?","know":"• When activity depends on one ionizable group, the **pH at 50% activity = that group's pKa** (Henderson–Hasselbalch: half protonated at pH = pKa).\n• Side-chain pKa values: **Asp ≈ 3.9, Glu ≈ 4.1, His ≈ 6.0, Cys ≈ 8.3, Tyr ≈ 10.1, Lys ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5**.\n• A falling curve: the group must be **protonated** (an acid); a rising curve: it must be deprotonated (a base).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says the residue must be protonated to act, so activity falls as it loses its proton with rising pH.","hl":"must be protonated to act"},{"text":"Concept: the pH at half activity is the residue's pKa. Read the midpoint: drop from 50% activity to the x-axis.","calc":["50% activity at **pH ≈ 8.3**","pKa ≈ 8.3"]},{"text":"Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9) would lose its proton at much lower pH; its midpoint would sit near 4.","strike":["Aspartate"]},{"text":"Tyrosine (≈ 10.1) and arginine (≈ 12.5) stay protonated past pH 9, so their activity would not drop until pH 10 or higher.","strike":["Tyrosine","Arginine"]},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Thinking a residue that must be protonated has to be basic, like arginine: the midpoint, not the direction, identifies the residue."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"loses activity as the pH rises","note":"the group loses H⁺"},{"t":"must be protonated to act","note":"active form is HA"},{"t":"catalytic acid","note":"match the pKa"}]},{"text":"**Midpoint = pKa.** At pH = pKa a group is half protonated, so an enzyme that needs it protonated runs at half speed there.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","at pH = pKa: [A⁻] = [HA] → 50%"]},{"text":"**Match the pKa.** A midpoint near 8.3 fits cysteine's –SH; Asp/Glu sit near 4, His near 6, Lys near 10.5."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the pH at half activity is the catalytic group's pKa; match it to a side chain: Asp/Glu ≈ 4, His ≈ 6, Cys ≈ 8.3, Lys ≈ 10.5."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-32b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:32#1","pe5-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:32"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which pair names the rings of the pocket's tryptophan and proline, in that order?","choices":["Indole; pyrrolidine","Imidazole; pyrrolidine","Indole; pyrimidine","Imidazole; pyrimidine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Imidazole is histidine's ring (two N atoms in a five-membered ring), not tryptophan's."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Pyrimidine is a nucleobase ring (C, T, U); no amino-acid side chain carries one."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong twice: imidazole is histidine's ring, and pyrimidines belong to nucleobases."}],"rule":"Trp: a benzene fused to a five-ring with one N. Pro: a saturated five-ring with one N. His: a five-ring with two N (imidazole).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Trp = indole (benzene fused to a one-N five-ring); Pro = pyrrolidine (saturated one-N five-ring). Imidazole is His; pyrimidine is a nucleobase ring. Indole; pyrrolidine."},"exp":"Tryptophan's side chain is an **indole** (a benzene ring fused to a five-membered N–H ring). Proline's ring is a **pyrrolidine**, a saturated five-membered ring that includes the backbone nitrogen. Imidazole belongs to histidine, and pyrimidines are nucleobases (C, T, U), never side chains.","how":{"ask":"Name the ring on tryptophan, then the ring on proline.","know":"• **His → imidazole** (5-membered, 2 N, aromatic).\n• **Trp → indole** (benzene fused to a 5-membered N–H ring; 9 ring atoms).\n• **Pro → pyrrolidine** (5-membered, saturated, the backbone N is in the ring).\n• **Phe, Tyr → benzene** (phenyl).\n• **Pyrimidines** (cytosine, thymine, uracil) and **purines** (A, G) are nucleobases, not amino-acid side chains.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 names the two pocket residues. Tryptophan carries a fused two-ring system with one N–H, the indole.","hl":"a tryptophan and a proline","fig":"tryptophan"},{"text":"Imidazole is histidine's ring (two N atoms in a five-membered ring), not tryptophan's.","strike":["Imidazole; pyrrolidine","Imidazole; pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Proline: its side chain loops back onto its own α-amino N, closing a saturated five-membered ring, the pyrrolidine.","hl":"proline","fig":"proline"},{"text":"A pyrimidine is a six-membered ring with two N atoms found in nucleic-acid bases; no amino acid carries one.","strike":["Indole; pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Answer: indole; pyrrolidine."}],"trap":"Picking pyrimidine because it sounds like pyrrolidine: pyrimidines are DNA/RNA bases, never side chains."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage and stem.","marks":[{"t":"a tryptophan and a proline","note":"two ring residues"},{"t":"neither ring is a nucleotide base","note":"no pyrimidine"},{"t":"in that order","note":"Trp first"}]},{"text":"**Ring residues and their rings.** Count the ring atoms and nitrogens to tell them apart.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Side-chain rings of the ring-bearing amino acids","x":{"label":"Residue (ring)"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["His (imidazole)","Trp (indole)","Pro (pyrrolidine)","Phe (benzene)"],"series":[{"name":"Ring atoms","values":[5,9,5,6]},{"name":"Ring nitrogens","values":[2,1,1,0]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine, uracil) and purines (adenine, guanine) are nucleobases in DNA and RNA. A choice that names one as a side-chain ring is always wrong."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** His = imidazole, Trp = indole, Pro = pyrrolidine, Phe/Tyr = benzene; pyrimidines and purines are nucleobases, never amino-acid side chains."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-33a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:33#1","pe6-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:33"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which best explains why the buried tryptophan reacts after heating?","choices":["Noncovalent contacts broke","The backbone was cut","A disulfide was reduced","Heat made the reagent more reactive"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Hydrolysis adds water and splits the chain, so the mass would change; it stayed the same, with no protease."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No reducing agent was present, and breaking an S–S bond by reduction adds two H, changing the mass."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The control shows the reagent reacts equally fast at both temperatures, so its reactivity did not change."}],"rule":"Heat denatures a protein by breaking weak noncovalent interactions; if the mass is unchanged and nothing was added to cut bonds, no covalent bond broke.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same mass + no reductant + no protease = no covalent change. The only thing left that heat can break is the network of weak interactions holding the fold."},"exp":"Heating to 70 °C **denatures** the enzyme: it breaks the **H-bonds, hydrophobic contacts and salt bridges** that hold the fold, so the buried tryptophan becomes exposed. No covalent bond broke, which is why the mass is unchanged; the control rules out a change in the reagent.","how":{"ask":"Which explanation fits all three facts: reaction after heating, unchanged mass, and an unchanged reagent?","know":"• **Denaturation** (heat, urea, pH extremes) breaks noncovalent interactions and unfolds a protein; the covalent chain stays intact.\n• **Hydrolysis** of peptide bonds or **reduction** of disulfides changes the chain chemically (and the mass).\n• Buried residues are shielded by the fold; unfolding exposes them.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the heated chain keeps exactly its original mass, and no protease was present: the backbone is intact.","hl":"exactly its original mass","strike":["The backbone was cut"]},{"text":"No reducing agent, and a reduction would change the mass too.","hl":"no reducing agent","strike":["A disulfide was reduced"]},{"text":"The control removes the reagent as the cause.","hl":"equally fast at both temperatures","strike":["Heat made the reagent more reactive"]},{"text":"Concept: a fold is held by many noncovalent contacts; heat breaks them, the chain unfolds, and the buried residue is exposed with no covalent change."},{"text":"Answer: noncovalent contacts broke, unfolding the enzyme and exposing the tryptophan."}],"trap":"Assuming heat must break covalent bonds to expose a residue: unfolding alone does it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"buried in the core","note":"hidden by the fold"},{"t":"exactly its original mass","note":"no covalent change"},{"t":"free tryptophan reacts equally fast","note":"reagent unchanged by heat"}]},{"text":"**Weak bonds hold the fold.** Tertiary structure rests on many weak interactions; heat breaks them and the chain unfolds, while peptide and disulfide bonds survive."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when heating exposes a buried residue with no change in mass, the protein has unfolded (denatured) through broken noncovalent interactions, not through any covalent cleavage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:34#1","pe6-cp:50#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:34"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which salt is in the mass-spectrometry buffer?","choices":["Methylammonium formate","Methylammonium acetate","Dimethylammonium formate","Methyl formate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Formic acid gives formate (HCOO⁻); acetate would need acetic acid."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Dimethylammonium needs a base with two methyls, (CH₃)₂NH; methylamine has only one."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An ester, not a salt: an acid plus an amine swap a proton and make ions, not an ester."}],"rule":"Acid + amine = proton transfer: name the protonated amine (-ammonium) plus the acid's anion (-ate), each keeping its own carbons.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Amine + acid = H⁺ hand-off: CH₃NH₂ → methylammonium, HCOOH → formate (formic ↔ formate, acetic ↔ acetate). Methylammonium formate; methyl formate would be an ester."},"exp":"The base takes H⁺ from the acid: **CH₃NH₂ + HCOOH → CH₃NH₃⁺ + HCOO⁻**. The salt is named cation first: **methylammonium formate**.","how":{"ask":"Move one H⁺ from the acid to the base, then name the cation and the anion.","know":"• **Acid + base → salt** of the base's conjugate acid (cation) and the acid's conjugate base (anion).\n• An amine RNH₂ becomes RNH₃⁺ (ammonium-type cation); HCOOH becomes HCOO⁻ (formate).\n• Salts are named **cation, then anion**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 8 says methylamine is neutralized by formic acid: move one H⁺ from the acid to the amine N.","hl":"neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH","calc":["CH₃NH₂ + HCOOH → CH₃NH₃⁺ + HCOO⁻"]},{"text":"Concept: acid + base gives a salt, named cation then anion: CH₃NH₃⁺ is methylammonium; HCOO⁻ is formate."},{"text":"Acetate needs acetic acid, dimethylammonium needs a base with two methyls, (CH₃)₂NH, and methyl formate is an ester, not a salt.","strike":["Methylammonium acetate","Dimethylammonium formate","Methyl formate"]},{"text":"Answer: methylammonium formate."}],"trap":"Picking the ester: neutralization moves a proton and makes ions; an ester needs an alcohol and a condensation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"methylamine","note":"the base"},{"t":"formic acid, HCOOH","note":"the acid"},{"t":"volatile buffer","note":"a salt of two volatile partners"}]},{"text":"**Move one proton.** The acid gives H⁺; the base accepts it.","calc":["CH₃NH₂ + H⁺ → CH₃NH₃⁺","HCOOH − H⁺ → HCOO⁻"]},{"text":"**Name cation, then anion.** methylammonium + formate."},{"text":"**Also:** a weak base plus a weak acid gives the salt of their conjugates: NH₃ + HCN → NH₄⁺ + CN⁻; name the cation first, then the anion: ammonium cyanide."},{"text":"**Also:** an ionic compound is named cation, then anion, with no Greek prefixes (those belong to molecular compounds such as N₂O₄, dinitrogen tetroxide). NH₄⁺ is ammonium, never \"ammonia\" (ammonia is the neutral base NH₃), and a one-atom anion ends in -ide.","calc":["NH₃ + HBr → NH₄⁺ + Br⁻: ammonium bromide","NH₄⁺ with NO₃⁻: ammonium nitrate"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** acid + base → a salt of the base's conjugate acid and the acid's conjugate base, named cation then anion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-34b","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:34#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:34"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which formula and molar mass fit the column's eluting salt?","choices":["HCOONa, 68 g/mol","CH₃COONa, 82 g/mol","NaHCO₃, 84 g/mol","Na₂CO₃, 106 g/mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"CH₃COO⁻ is acetate, from acetic acid; formic acid has an H, not a CH₃, on its carboxyl carbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"HCO₃⁻ is bicarbonate, with three O; formate has two O and its H bonded to carbon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"CO₃²⁻ is carbonate, a 2− ion with three O; a monoprotic acid gives a 1− anion and one Na⁺."}],"rule":"Acid + base → salt: keep the acid's anion (formate HCOO⁻, acetate CH₃COO⁻) and the base's cation; then add atomic masses.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Formic acid minus its H⁺ is formate, HCOO⁻; plus Na⁺: HCOONa. Mass ≈ 1 + 12 + 32 + 23."},"exp":"Removing the acidic H from HCOOH gives **formate, HCOO⁻**; pairing it with Na⁺ gives **sodium formate, HCOONa**. Molar mass = 1 + 12 + 2(16) + 23 = **68 g/mol**. Acetate, bicarbonate and carbonate are different anions.","how":{"ask":"Which anion does formic acid give, and what does the sodium salt weigh?","know":"• **Formate** HCOO⁻ (from HCOOH); **acetate** CH₃COO⁻ (from CH₃COOH).\n• **Bicarbonate** HCO₃⁻ and **carbonate** CO₃²⁻ come from carbonic acid; both have three O.\n• Neutralization: HA + NaOH → NaA + H₂O.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says the eluting salt comes from formic acid and NaOH: write the neutralization.","hl":"neutralizing formic acid with NaOH","calc":["HCOOH + NaOH → HCOONa + H₂O"]},{"text":"Concept: the salt is the acid's conjugate base with the base's cation. The anion is formate, not acetate, bicarbonate or carbonate.","strike":["CH₃COONa, 82 g/mol","NaHCO₃, 84 g/mol","Na₂CO₃, 106 g/mol"]},{"text":"Add the atomic masses.","calc":["H 1 + C 12 + O₂ 32 + Na 23 = 68 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: HCOONa, 68 g/mol."}],"trap":"Writing formate as HCO₃⁻: the H of formate is on carbon, and there are only two oxygens."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"formic acid with NaOH","note":"acid + base → salt + water"},{"t":"HCOOH","note":"H on the carboxyl carbon"},{"t":"Na = 23 g/mol","note":"for the molar mass"}]},{"text":"**Know the look-alikes.** HCOO⁻ (formate, 2 O), CH₃COO⁻ (acetate, 2 O), HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate, 3 O), CO₃²⁻ (carbonate, 3 O, 2−)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the salt of a neutralization is the acid's conjugate base paired with the base's cation (ammonium acetate from acetic acid + ammonia, sodium formate from formic acid + NaOH)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-35a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:35#1","pe5-cp:35#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:35"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which column separates N from S, and which form leaves the column first?","choices":["Cation exchange; S elutes first","Cation exchange; N elutes first","Anion exchange; S elutes first","Size exclusion; N elutes first"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The more positive form binds a negative resin more tightly, so it needs more salt and comes off later."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Both forms are positive at this pH, so neither sticks to the + resin of an anion exchanger."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Masses within half a percent run together on a size column; the forms differ in charge, not size."}],"rule":"Forms that differ in charge but not size: use ion exchange matched to their sign; the form with less charge of that sign elutes first as salt rises.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Both forms are positive, so use a cation exchanger. The form with fewer positive charges holds on less tightly and is displaced first by the rising salt."},"exp":"Both forms are **positive** at pH 5.5, so they bind a **cation exchanger** (negative resin). Salt displaces the weaker binder first: the **succinylated form (+7)** elutes before the native form (+9). Their masses are nearly equal, so size exclusion cannot separate them.","how":{"ask":"What sign of charge do both forms carry, which resin binds that sign, and which form binds more weakly?","know":"• **Cation exchanger**: negative resin, binds positive proteins. **Anion exchanger**: positive resin, binds negative proteins.\n• A rising salt gradient displaces the **least** charged binder first.\n• **Size exclusion** separates by size only.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives both forms' charges at pH 5.5 and their near-equal masses.","hl":"+9 and +7 at pH 5.5","calc":["native +9, succinylated +7 → both positive","mass difference < 0.5% → not a size separation"]},{"text":"Concept: a protein sticks to a resin of opposite charge. Positive proteins bind a negative resin: a cation exchanger.","strike":["Anion exchange; S elutes first"]},{"text":"Near-identical masses rule out size exclusion.","strike":["Size exclusion; N elutes first"]},{"text":"The +7 form binds less tightly, so it leaves at lower salt; the +9 form comes off later.","strike":["Cation exchange; N elutes first"]},{"text":"Answer: cation exchange, with S eluting first."}],"trap":"Reasoning that the added carboxylate makes the enzyme \"negative\" and choosing an anion exchanger: the net charge is still positive."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"+9 and +7 at pH 5.5","note":"both positive; N more so"},{"t":"differ in mass by under 0.5%","note":"not a size separation"},{"t":"rising gradient","note":"weaker binder elutes first"}]},{"text":"**Match the resin to the sign.** A protein sticks to a resin of opposite charge; the more charge it carries, the more salt it takes to remove it."},{"text":"**Also:** at a pH where both forms are negative (say pH 8, net −3 and −5), the same pair would go on an anion exchanger, and the more negative modified form would bind more tightly and elute later."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to separate a protein from a form that differs only in charge, use ion exchange of the sign both carry; the form with less net charge of that sign elutes first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-36a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:36#1","pe5-cp:36#2","pe5-cp:36#3","pe5-cp:36#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:36"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"Based on the passage's data, which compound is the starting liquid?","choices":["Compound W","Compound X","Compound Y","Compound Z"],"choiceFigs":["butanal","cpt-pe5-2-butanone","cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid","cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Compound W, an aldehyde, has only one O (C₄H₈O), so it can't match the formula."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Compound X, a ketone, also has one O, and its C=O absorbs lower, near 1715 cm⁻¹."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Compound Y fits the formula, but a carboxylic acid shows a very broad O–H band, which is missing."}],"rule":"Name the carbonyl by what the C=O carbon carries: H (aldehyde), C (ketone), OH (acid), OR (ester). Then check formula and IR: an acid needs a broad O–H; an ester a high C=O and a C–O band.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Two O in the formula leaves the acid and the ester. No broad O–H band rules out the acid."},"exp":"C₄H₈O₂ has two O, so only the acid (Y) and the ester (Z) fit. A carboxylic acid would show a **very broad O–H band** (about 2500–3300 cm⁻¹); none is present. A C=O near 1740 cm⁻¹ plus a strong C–O band near 1200 cm⁻¹ marks an **ester**: Z, methyl propanoate.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing has two O, a C=O, and no O–H?","know":"• **Ketone**: C=O with C on both sides (≈ 1715 cm⁻¹).\n• **Aldehyde**: C=O with an H (≈ 1725; weak C–H pair just below 2900).\n• **Carboxylic acid**: C=O with OH (≈ 1710; very broad O–H 2500–3300).\n• **Ester**: C=O with OR (≈ 1740; strong C–O 1150–1250).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the formula first: C₄H₈O₂ has two O, so a one-O aldehyde or ketone is out.","hl":"C₄H₈O₂","strike":["Compound W","Compound X"]},{"text":"The same sentence reports no broad O–H band, so no carboxylic acid.","hl":"no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹","strike":["Compound Y"]},{"text":"Concept: name the carbonyl group from its neighbors and confirm with IR. A high C=O (1742 cm⁻¹) plus a strong C–O band fits an ester.","hl":"strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹","fig":"cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"},{"text":"Answer: compound Z, the ester methyl propanoate."}],"trap":"Picking the acid because it matches the formula: a carboxylic acid always shows its broad O–H band."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"C₄H₈O₂","note":"two O"},{"t":"1742 cm⁻¹","note":"ester C=O"},{"t":"no broad band","note":"no O–H"}]},{"text":"**Look past the O.** The carbonyl carbon has two other bonds; name the group from those two neighbors. Here, a carbon and an –OCH₃: an ester.","fig":"cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"},{"text":"**Also:** carbon on both sides of the carbonyl carbon makes a **ketone**, as in 2-butanone (CH₃COCH₂CH₃).","fig":"cpt-pe5-2-butanone"},{"text":"**Also:** an H on the carbonyl carbon makes an **aldehyde** (butanal), and an –OH makes a **carboxylic acid** (butanoic acid).","fig":"butanal"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** identify a carbonyl compound from what is bonded to the C=O carbon (H, C, OH or OR), and confirm with the formula and IR: broad O–H for an acid, about 1740 plus C–O for an ester."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-37a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:37#1","pe5-cp:37#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:37"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"What pressure remains in the flask once it is back at 298 K?","choices":["2.4 atm","6.0 atm","5.4 atm","12 atm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio flipped: gas moles drop from 9 to 4, so the final pressure must be below 5.4 atm, not above it."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That's the starting pressure copied; gas moles change (9 → 4), so in a rigid flask at fixed T it can't stay the same."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Counts the liquid water as gas; a liquid's volume is tiny, so only NO is left pushing on the walls."}],"rule":"Only gases count: P_final = P_start × (gas moles after ÷ gas moles before). Round the pressure and the ratio to 1 digit.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count gas only: 4 + 5 = 9 mol → 4 NO (water is liquid). Pre-divide: 5.4 ÷ 9 = 0.6, × 4 = 2.4 atm."},"exp":"In a rigid flask at fixed T, **P ∝ moles of gas**. Gas moles go from 4 + 5 = 9 to 4 (the water is liquid), so P = 5.4 atm × 4/9 = **2.4 atm**.","how":{"ask":"Count gas moles before and after from the coefficients, then scale the pressure by that ratio.","know":"• At constant V and T, PV = nRT gives **P ∝ n(gas)**: P_final = P_initial × n_after/n_before.\n• Take the moles from the **coefficients** when the reactants are in the stoichiometric ratio and react completely.\n• Count **only gases**; (l) and (s) species add no pressure (water's small vapor pressure is ignored).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the flask is rigid at 298 K, and sentence 3 returns it to 298 K: only the gas moles change.","hl":"a rigid flask at 298 K"},{"text":"Concept: at fixed V and T, P is proportional to gas moles. Gas moles before: both reactants are gases.","hl":"in a 4:5 mole ratio","calc":["n_before = 4 + 5 = **9**"]},{"text":"Gas moles after: the mix is exactly stoichiometric, so nothing is left over; NO is a gas, and sentence 3 keeps the water liquid.","calc":["n_after = 4 (NO) + 0 (H₂O, liquid) = **4**"],"hl":"the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure"},{"text":"Scale the pressure.","calc":["P_final = P_initial × n_after/n_before","P_final = 5.4 atm × 4/9","P_final = **2.4 atm**"]},{"text":"6.0 atm counts the liquid water as gas (5.4 × 10/9); 12 atm flips the ratio (5.4 × 9/4); 5.4 atm assumes the pressure does not change.","strike":["6.0 atm","12 atm","5.4 atm"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.4 atm."}],"trap":"Counting every product coefficient: a liquid or solid product takes up almost no volume and adds no pressure."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"rigid flask","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"returned to 298 K","note":"T fixed"},{"t":"4:5 mole ratio","note":"stoichiometric"},{"t":"6 H₂O(l)","note":"liquid: no pressure"}]},{"text":"**P tracks gas moles.** With V and T fixed, PV = nRT makes P directly proportional to n.","calc":["P₂/P₁ = n₂/n₁"]},{"text":"**Also: read the gas moles from the coefficients.** Add the coefficients of the (g) species on each side.","calc":["left: 4 + 5 = 9 mol gas","right: 4 mol gas (the 6 mol of water is liquid)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant V and T, P_final = P_initial × (gas moles after ÷ gas moles before), counting only (g) species from the balanced equation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-38a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:38#1","pe5-cp:38#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:38"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"Which layers hold naphthalene and the benzoate ion, respectively?","choices":["Organic; aqueous","Aqueous; organic","Organic; organic","Aqueous; aqueous"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both swapped: the neutral hydrocarbon goes to the organic layer and the ion to the water, not the other way."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Benzoate is an ion, and ions stay in water, not in nonpolar CH₂Cl₂."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Naphthalene is a neutral hydrocarbon; it won't go into water."}],"rule":"Like dissolves like: neutral, nonpolar compounds go to the organic layer; ions (salts, charged acids or bases) stay in water.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Neutral and nonpolar → organic layer (naphthalene). Any ion → water (benzoate⁻ from the sodium salt). Organic; aqueous."},"exp":"**Like dissolves like.** Naphthalene is a neutral hydrocarbon, so it moves into **dichloromethane**; benzoate is an **anion**, held in **water** by ion–dipole attraction. Layer density decides only which phase sits on top.","how":{"ask":"Which compound is neutral and nonpolar, which is an ion, and which solvent does each resemble?","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: neutral, nonpolar molecules go to the organic layer; ions are surrounded by water dipoles and stay in the aqueous layer.\n• Dichloromethane is an organic solvent that does not mix with water.\n• Density only decides which layer is on top (dichloromethane sinks), not where a compound goes.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 labels the two solids: naphthalene is a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate is a salt.","hl":"naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon"},{"text":"Concept: like dissolves like. Naphthalene goes to the organic solvent; in water the salt is Na⁺ plus the benzoate anion, held by water's dipoles.","hl":"sodium benzoate"},{"text":"Aqueous; organic swaps both; aqueous; aqueous puts a hydrocarbon in water; organic; organic puts an ion in a nonpolar solvent.","strike":["Aqueous; organic","Aqueous; aqueous","Organic; organic"]},{"text":"Answer: organic; aqueous."}],"trap":"Placing compounds by layer position: dichloromethane is denser and lies under the water, but which layer is on top says nothing about where a compound dissolves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon","note":"organic layer"},{"t":"sodium benzoate, a salt","note":"ions stay in water"},{"t":"water and dichloromethane","note":"two immiscible layers"}]},{"text":"**Sort by charge first.** An ion, cation or anion, stays in water: it cannot leave its shell of water dipoles for a nonpolar solvent."},{"text":"**Then by polarity.** A neutral molecule with no polar groups, such as a hydrocarbon, moves into the organic layer."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an extraction, like dissolves like: a neutral nonpolar compound goes to the organic layer, and an ionized species stays in water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-39a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:39#1","pe5-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:39"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"What is the inhibition constant of K, approximately?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Enzyme activity versus inhibitor concentration","x":{"label":"log₁₀([I] / M)","min":-8,"max":-3,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Activity (% of uninhibited)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-8,99],[-7.5,97],[-7,91],[-6.5,76],[-6,50],[-5.5,24],[-5,9],[-4.5,3],[-4,1],[-3,0]]}]},"choices":["0.1 μM","1 μM","6 μM","50 μM"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"One decade off: that is ten times too little inhibitor, where activity is still about 90%."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"6 is the x-axis log value; [I] = 10^(log value) M, never the log number itself."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"50 is the activity percentage on the y-axis, not a concentration; read the x-axis."}],"rule":"Find 50% activity on the y-axis, read its x value (log [I]), then [I] = 10^x M; each x unit is a factor of 10.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At 50% activity read x: log[I] = −6. Undo the log: 10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM."},"exp":"Read across from **50% activity** to the curve and down: **log[I] = −6**. The x-axis is the log of the molar concentration, so [I] = 10⁻⁶ M, and 10⁻⁶ M × (10⁶ μM per M) = **1 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Find 50% activity, read the x-value below it, then undo the log and convert M to μM.","know":"• On a log-scale plot, the x-value is **log₁₀[I]**: [I] = 10^x in the axis unit.\n• **Check the unit** on the axis label (here M) before converting: 1 μM = 10⁻⁶ M.\n• The half-maximal point (IC₅₀) is read straight down from 50% activity.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the inhibition constant is the concentration at half-maximal activity: find 50% on the y-axis and drop to the x-axis.","hl":"half-maximal activity","calc":["50% activity → log₁₀([I]/M) = **−6**"]},{"text":"Concept: the x-axis is log₁₀ of molar [K]; undo the log, then convert M to μM.","calc":["[I] = 10⁻⁶ M","10⁻⁶ M × 10⁶ μM/M = **1 μM**"]},{"text":"0.1 μM is 10⁻⁷ M, one decade off: it is the point where activity is still about 90%.","strike":["0.1 μM"]},{"text":"6 μM reads the log value as if it were a concentration, and 50 μM reads the percentage instead of the x-axis.","strike":["6 μM","50 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 1 μM."}],"trap":"Reporting the axis number itself (6) as a concentration: the x-axis is a log, so the concentration is 10 raised to it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K","note":"x is a log, in M"},{"t":"half-maximal activity","note":"read at 50%"}]},{"text":"**Read across, then down.** Go to 50% on the y-axis, across to the curve, then straight down to the x-axis."},{"text":"**Undo the log and mind the units.** The axis gives log₁₀([I]/M); raise 10 to it to get molar, then convert.","calc":["x = −6 → 10⁻⁶ M","10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM","x = −5 would be 10⁻⁵ M = 10 μM"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an activity-versus-log[I] curve, find 50% activity, drop to the x-axis, raise 10 to that value in the axis unit, then convert to the asked unit (10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-40a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:40#1","pe5-cp:40#2","pe5-cp:40#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:40"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"What does the kinase's Hill coefficient indicate about substrate binding?","choices":["Positive cooperativity","Negative cooperativity","No cooperativity","Exactly two binding sites"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Positive cooperativity needs n > 1; 0.6 is below 1."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No cooperativity means n = 1 exactly, and 0.6 isn't 1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The Hill coefficient isn't a site count; it measures how binding at one site shifts the others."}],"rule":"Hill n above 1: one binding helps the next. n = 1: independent sites. n below 1: one binding hinders the next. n isn't a site count.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hill n: > 1 positive, = 1 none, < 1 negative cooperativity; n is never a site count. 0.6 → negative cooperativity."},"exp":"A Hill coefficient **n < 1** means **negative cooperativity**: binding at one site lowers the affinity of the others, so the curve rises quickly and then flattens out early. n > 1 would be positive cooperativity (sigmoidal), and n = 1 independent sites (hyperbolic). n is not a count of sites; it can only reach the number of sites in the limit of perfect positive cooperativity.","how":{"ask":"Compare the Hill coefficient with 1.","know":"• **n < 1**: negative cooperativity (one binding makes the next harder).\n• **n > 1**: positive cooperativity (one binding makes the next easier; sigmoidal curve).\n• **n = 1**: no cooperativity (independent sites; hyperbolic curve).\n• n measures interaction between sites; it is at most the number of sites, never a direct count.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the kinase's Hill coefficient, 0.6, with a curve that is not S-shaped.","hl":"Hill coefficient of 0.6","calc":["n = 0.6 < 1 → **negative cooperativity**"]},{"text":"Concept: compare n with 1. Positive cooperativity needs n > 1, and no cooperativity needs n = 1 exactly.","strike":["Positive cooperativity","No cooperativity"]},{"text":"n is not the number of sites: a dimer has two sites, but n = 0.6 only says each binding hinders the next, and a tetramer could show n = 0.6 too.","strike":["Exactly two binding sites"]},{"text":"Answer: negative cooperativity."}],"trap":"Reading n as the number of binding sites, or calling any n other than 1 positive: n below 1 means the sites hinder each other."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"a dimer with two identical substrate sites","note":"two sites, not n"},{"t":"Hill coefficient of 0.6","note":"below 1"}]},{"text":"**n < 1: negative cooperativity.** Binding at one site makes binding at the next harder, so the curve rises fast and then flattens out early (the n = 0.6 curve).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fractional saturation for three Hill coefficients (same half-saturation, 4 μM)","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fractional saturation","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"n = 2.5","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.03],[2,0.15],[3,0.33],[4,0.5],[6,0.73],[8,0.85],[12,0.94],[16,0.97],[20,0.98]]},{"name":"n = 1","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.2],[2,0.33],[3,0.43],[4,0.5],[6,0.6],[8,0.67],[12,0.75],[16,0.8],[20,0.83]]},{"name":"n = 0.6","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.3],[2,0.4],[3,0.46],[4,0.5],[6,0.56],[8,0.6],[12,0.66],[16,0.7],[20,0.72]]}]}},{"text":"**n > 1: positive cooperativity.** Binding at one site makes binding at the next easier, so the curve is sigmoidal (the n = 2.5 curve)."},{"text":"**n = 1: no cooperativity.** The sites bind independently, and the curve is a plain hyperbola, like a monomer's."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read the Hill coefficient against 1: n > 1 is positive cooperativity, n < 1 negative cooperativity, n = 1 none (independent sites)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-40b","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:40#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:40"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"Which related kinase binds with no cooperativity, and what Hill coefficient does its curve imply?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fractional saturation versus ligand concentration","x":{"label":"[L] (μM)","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fractional saturation","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"X","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.33],[2,0.5],[3,0.6],[4,0.67],[5,0.71],[6,0.75],[8,0.8],[10,0.83],[15,0.88],[20,0.91]]},{"name":"Y","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.01],[2,0.07],[3,0.19],[4,0.35],[5,0.5],[6,0.62],[8,0.79],[10,0.87],[15,0.96],[20,0.98]]}]},"choices":["Y; equal to 1","X; greater than 1","X; equal to 1","Y; greater than 1"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Y's curve is S-shaped, the mark of cooperativity, so it isn't the noncooperative kinase."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No cooperativity means n = 1 exactly; n > 1 is positive cooperativity, so this pairing contradicts itself."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Y's S-curve does mean n > 1, but that is cooperative, which the question rules out."}],"rule":"Hyperbolic curve (steep from the start, no lag): no cooperativity, n = 1. S-shaped curve: positive cooperativity, n > 1.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hyperbola (steep from zero, no lag) = independent sites, n = 1; sigmoid = n > 1. X is the hyperbola: X; equal to 1."},"exp":"X traces a **hyperbola**: it rises steeply from the start and bends over smoothly toward full saturation, with no lag. That shape means every site binds independently, **no cooperativity, n = 1**. Y's curve is **S-shaped (sigmoidal)**, the mark of positive cooperativity (n > 1), even though Y reaches half saturation later (about 5 μM vs about 2 μM for X).","how":{"ask":"Look at the shape of each curve, not where it reaches half saturation.","know":"• **Sigmoidal** (S-shaped) binding curve → positive cooperativity, **n > 1**, typical of multi-subunit proteins such as hemoglobin.\n• **Hyperbolic** curve → no cooperativity, **n = 1** (like myoglobin).\n• The half-saturation point measures **affinity**, not cooperativity: a kinase can bind tightly and still be noncooperative.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 points to the plotted curves of monomeric X and tetrameric Y; read the shape of each, not its half-saturation point.","hl":"monomeric X and tetrameric Y"},{"text":"Concept: a hyperbola means independent sites (n = 1); a sigmoid means positive cooperativity (n > 1). X rises steeply from the start and bends over, with no flat lag: a hyperbola.","calc":["X: 0.33 at 1 μM → 0.50 at 2 μM → 0.83 at 10 μM"]},{"text":"A hyperbola means independent sites, so n = 1 exactly, not n > 1.","strike":["X; greater than 1"]},{"text":"Y starts flat, then rises steeply: an S shape, so Y is the cooperative one (n > 1) and is not the kinase asked about.","calc":["Y: 0.07 at 2 μM → 0.50 at 5 μM → 0.87 at 10 μM"],"strike":["Y; equal to 1","Y; greater than 1"]},{"text":"Answer: X; equal to 1."}],"trap":"Picking Y because it is half-saturated at a higher [L], as if weaker binding meant no cooperativity: half saturation is affinity; only the shape shows cooperativity."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage and stem.","marks":[{"t":"no cooperativity","note":"n = 1, a hyperbola"},{"t":"Hill coefficient","note":"read from the shape"},{"t":"both reach full saturation","note":"saturation says nothing about n"}]},{"text":"**Shape, not position.** A sigmoid means binding gets easier as sites fill; a hyperbola means every site binds the same way."},{"text":"**Half saturation is affinity.** X reaches 0.5 at about 2 μM and Y at about 5 μM, so X binds more tightly, but tight binding says nothing about cooperativity: X is noncooperative and Y cooperative."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a sigmoidal binding curve means positive cooperativity (n > 1) and a hyperbolic curve means none (n = 1), whatever their half-saturation points."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:41#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:41"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P8","passage":"Gut bacteria turn a colorless precursor into a dye in three steps, W → X → Y → Z, catalyzed in order by enzymes K, L and M. X is the bright pigment that is wanted, but both Y and Z are toxic to the host, and any one or two of the enzymes can be knocked out. A crystal structure of enzyme K with a nucleotide analog shows the analog's doubly charged phosphate within 2.9 Å of one residue, making a salt bridge, and its flat purine ring lying parallel to a second side chain's ring at 3.6 Å, a π-stacking contact; no metal ion is in the site. The same bacteria release a neutral, rotten-egg-smelling gas through cysteine desulfhydrase, a pyridoxal phosphate enzyme: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___, where the blank is the gas. They also free nitrogen with urease, which turns urea, (NH₂)₂CO, plus one H⁺ into two NH₄⁺ ions and one HCO₃⁻ ion; the only other reactant is the solvent, and both sides carry the same atoms and charge. To label proteins with X, the lab uses an activated ester of X; the free-energy profile for its hydrolysis is plotted with a question, reactants at the start of the curve and products at its end, and steps with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds at room temperature.","q":"Which knockout builds up pigment X but stops both toxins?","choices":["Enzyme K","Enzyme L","Enzyme M","Enzymes K and M"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Knocking out K stops W → X, upstream of everything, so no X pigment builds up."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"M only makes Z; with M gone, toxic Y still forms from X and piles up."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The K knockout shuts the pathway off upstream of X, so no pigment is made."}],"rule":"A knockout piles up the substrate of the blocked step: to build up an intermediate and stop everything after it, block the step right after it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A dead enzyme piles up its own substrate. X is the substrate of the step X → Y, so knock out that step's enzyme: X builds, and neither Y nor Z can form."},"exp":"To keep an intermediate but block everything it becomes, **inhibit the enzyme that consumes it**. Enzyme L converts X → Y, so knocking it out piles up X and stops both Y and Z. Knocking out K stops X itself; knocking out M still lets toxic Y form.","how":{"ask":"Which enzyme uses up X?","know":"• In a linear pathway, an intermediate **accumulates** when the enzyme that **consumes** it is blocked.\n• Blocking any step **before** the intermediate stops its own production.\n• Blocking a step **further downstream** still lets the products in between form.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the pathway order: map each enzyme to its step.","hl":"W → X → Y → Z","calc":["K: W → X","L: X → Y","M: Y → Z"]},{"text":"Concept: an intermediate piles up when the step that uses it is blocked. Sentence 2 makes Y and Z the toxins; X is made by K and used by L, so block L and X accumulates while Y and Z cannot form.","hl":"both Y and Z are toxic"},{"text":"Knocking out K shuts the pathway off before X, alone or with M.","strike":["Enzyme K","Enzymes K and M"]},{"text":"Knocking out M stops only Z; toxic Y still forms.","strike":["Enzyme M"]},{"text":"Answer: Enzyme L."}],"trap":"Blocking the last enzyme out of habit: that stops only the final product and leaves the toxic intermediate Y."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"W → X → Y → Z","note":"linear pathway"},{"t":"K, L and M","note":"one enzyme per step"},{"t":"both Y and Z are toxic","note":"stop everything after X"}]},{"text":"**Block the exit, not the entrance.** An intermediate piles up when its consuming step is blocked and production continues."},{"text":"**Upstream blocks lose everything downstream.** If K is missing, X is never made, and neither are Y and Z."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to accumulate an intermediate of a linear pathway and stop all later products, inhibit the enzyme that consumes it (the step right after it)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-42a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:42#1","pe5-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:42#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:42"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P8","passage":"Gut bacteria turn a colorless precursor into a dye in three steps, W → X → Y → Z, catalyzed in order by enzymes K, L and M. X is the bright pigment that is wanted, but both Y and Z are toxic to the host, and any one or two of the enzymes can be knocked out. A crystal structure of enzyme K with a nucleotide analog shows the analog's doubly charged phosphate within 2.9 Å of one residue, making a salt bridge, and its flat purine ring lying parallel to a second side chain's ring at 3.6 Å, a π-stacking contact; no metal ion is in the site. The same bacteria release a neutral, rotten-egg-smelling gas through cysteine desulfhydrase, a pyridoxal phosphate enzyme: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___, where the blank is the gas. They also free nitrogen with urease, which turns urea, (NH₂)₂CO, plus one H⁺ into two NH₄⁺ ions and one HCO₃⁻ ion; the only other reactant is the solvent, and both sides carry the same atoms and charge. To label proteins with X, the lab uses an activated ester of X; the free-energy profile for its hydrolysis is plotted with a question, reactants at the start of the curve and products at its end, and steps with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds at room temperature.","q":"Which pair of residues in enzyme K best accounts for these two contacts?","choices":["Lysine and tryptophan","Glutamate and tryptophan","Lysine and threonine","Aspartate and leucine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A carboxylate is negative, so it would repel the phosphate instead of forming a salt bridge with it."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Aspartate repels a phosphate, and leucine's branched alkyl chain has no π system."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Gets the salt bridge but skips the stacking contact: threonine has no ring to stack on."}],"rule":"Salt bridge to a phosphate (−) needs a + side chain (Lys, Arg); π-stacking requires a flat aromatic side chain (Phe, Tyr, Trp). The pair must pass both tests.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two filters: + charge for the phosphate, aromatic ring for the stacking. Only one choice has a positive residue paired with an aromatic one."},"exp":"A phosphate is negative, so the salt bridge needs a **positive** side chain: **lysine** (–NH₃⁺). Stacking with a flat purine needs an **aromatic** ring: **tryptophan**'s indole. Glu and Asp are negative; Thr and Leu have no aromatic ring.","how":{"ask":"Which residue is positive at physiological pH, and which has a flat aromatic ring?","know":"• **Salt bridge**: opposite charges; phosphate (−) pairs with Lys or Arg (+).\n• **π-stacking**: two flat aromatic rings face to face; Phe, Tyr, Trp (and His).\n• Asp and Glu are negative; Ser, Thr, Leu and Val have no ring.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the phosphate makes a salt bridge. Concept: a phosphate is negative, so its partner must be positive.","hl":"making a salt bridge","calc":["phosphate (−) + Lys –NH₃⁺ (+)"]},{"text":"Negative side chains repel the phosphate.","strike":["Glutamate and tryptophan","Aspartate and leucine"]},{"text":"The same sentence names a π-stacking contact: stacking requires a flat aromatic side chain; threonine has none.","hl":"a π-stacking contact","strike":["Lysine and threonine"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and tryptophan."}],"trap":"Checking only one contact: a pair can pass the charge test and still fail the ring test."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"doubly charged phosphate","note":"negative"},{"t":"making a salt bridge","note":"needs + residue"},{"t":"a π-stacking contact","note":"needs aromatic ring"}]},{"text":"**Two filters in a row.** Strike every pair whose first residue is not positive, then every pair whose second residue has no aromatic ring."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a phosphate salt bridge needs Lys or Arg, and π-stacking needs Phe, Tyr or Trp; pick the pair that meets both."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-43a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:43#1","pe5-cp:43#3","pe5-cp:43#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:43"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P8","passage":"Gut bacteria turn a colorless precursor into a dye in three steps, W → X → Y → Z, catalyzed in order by enzymes K, L and M. X is the bright pigment that is wanted, but both Y and Z are toxic to the host, and any one or two of the enzymes can be knocked out. A crystal structure of enzyme K with a nucleotide analog shows the analog's doubly charged phosphate within 2.9 Å of one residue, making a salt bridge, and its flat purine ring lying parallel to a second side chain's ring at 3.6 Å, a π-stacking contact; no metal ion is in the site. The same bacteria release a neutral, rotten-egg-smelling gas through cysteine desulfhydrase, a pyridoxal phosphate enzyme: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___, where the blank is the gas. They also free nitrogen with urease, which turns urea, (NH₂)₂CO, plus one H⁺ into two NH₄⁺ ions and one HCO₃⁻ ion; the only other reactant is the solvent, and both sides carry the same atoms and charge. To label proteins with X, the lab uses an activated ester of X; the free-energy profile for its hydrolysis is plotted with a question, reactants at the start of the curve and products at its end, and steps with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds at room temperature.","q":"In the desulfhydrase equation, what is the missing product?","choices":["H₂S","HS⁻","H₂S + H⁺","SO₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: the sides are already neutral, and HS⁻ adds a − charge and is one H short."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: both sides are already neutral, so adding H⁺ leaves the right side +1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Atom check: the 3 O on the left all end up in pyruvate, so no O atoms remain for SO₂."}],"rule":"Balance atoms, then charge: count each element and the net charge on both sides before picking the missing species.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Charge: left 0; pyruvate⁻ + NH₄⁺ = 0, so the missing piece is neutral. Leftover atoms are S + 2 H → H₂S."},"exp":"Count atoms and charge. Left: C₃H₉NO₃S, charge 0. Right so far: pyruvate C₃H₃O₃⁻ plus NH₄⁺ = C₃H₇NO₃, charge 0. The leftover is **S + 2 H with no charge = H₂S**.","how":{"ask":"Subtract the known products' atoms and charge from the reactants'; whatever is left is the missing product.","know":"• Atoms and charge are both conserved.\n• **Missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms**, with the leftover charge.\n• Then name the leftover (S + 2 H, neutral = H₂S; N + 4 H, +1 = NH₄⁺) and recheck the total charge.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the full equation and says the gas is neutral. Total the reactants.","hl":"HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O","calc":["cysteine C₃H₇NO₂S (charge 0) + H₂O","= **C₃H₉NO₃S**, charge 0"]},{"text":"Concept: missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms, with the charge checked. Total the known products.","calc":["pyruvate C₃H₃O₃⁻ + NH₄⁺","= **C₃H₇NO₃**, charge 0"]},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["C₃H₉NO₃S − C₃H₇NO₃ = **H₂S**, charge 0 − 0 = 0"]},{"text":"HS⁻ has one H too few and a − charge the sides do not need; adding H⁺ leaves a + charge unbalanced; SO₂ adds O atoms that are not available.","strike":["HS⁻","H₂S + H⁺","SO₂"]},{"text":"Answer: H₂S."}],"trap":"Balancing only the atoms: a choice can carry the right sulfur yet break the charge balance."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻","note":"zwitterion, net 0"},{"t":"NH₄⁺","note":"known product, +1"},{"t":"missing product","note":"atom bookkeeping"}]},{"text":"**Count the left side.** Cysteine C₃H₇NO₂S plus water.","calc":["C₃H₉NO₃S, charge 0"]},{"text":"**Count the right side.** Pyruvate (−1) and ammonium (+1).","calc":["C₃H₇NO₃, charge 0"]},{"text":"**Name the leftover.** S and 2 H, no charge: H₂S."},{"text":"**Also:** the same subtraction names an ammonium ion: a leftover of one N, four H and a +1 charge is NH₄⁺, as when an amino acid loses its amino group."},{"text":"**Also:** finish by checking charge: here 0 on the left and −1 + 1 + 0 = 0 on the right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms; name what is left, then confirm the total charge matches on both sides."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-43b","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:43#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:43"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P8","passage":"Gut bacteria turn a colorless precursor into a dye in three steps, W → X → Y → Z, catalyzed in order by enzymes K, L and M. X is the bright pigment that is wanted, but both Y and Z are toxic to the host, and any one or two of the enzymes can be knocked out. A crystal structure of enzyme K with a nucleotide analog shows the analog's doubly charged phosphate within 2.9 Å of one residue, making a salt bridge, and its flat purine ring lying parallel to a second side chain's ring at 3.6 Å, a π-stacking contact; no metal ion is in the site. The same bacteria release a neutral, rotten-egg-smelling gas through cysteine desulfhydrase, a pyridoxal phosphate enzyme: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___, where the blank is the gas. They also free nitrogen with urease, which turns urea, (NH₂)₂CO, plus one H⁺ into two NH₄⁺ ions and one HCO₃⁻ ion; the only other reactant is the solvent, and both sides carry the same atoms and charge. To label proteins with X, the lab uses an activated ester of X; the free-energy profile for its hydrolysis is plotted with a question, reactants at the start of the curve and products at its end, and steps with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds at room temperature.","q":"Which reactant completes the balanced urease equation (NH₂)₂CO + ___ + H⁺ → 2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻?","choices":["2 H₂O","3 H₂O","1 O₂","2 OH⁻"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Atom check: 3 H₂O brings one O and two H more than the right side holds."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No H: the right side has 9 H vs 5 on the left, and O₂ adds none."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: the right side is +1 (2+ and 1−), and 2 OH⁻ would make the left −1."}],"rule":"Balance O first, then H, then check charge: both sides must match atom for atom and in total charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"O first: right has 3 O, urea has 1, so add 2 O as neutral water (charge already +1 both sides). 2 H₂O also brings the 4 missing H."},"exp":"The right side has 3 O and 9 H; urea and H⁺ supply only 1 O and 5 H. **Water supplies the missing O and H**: 2 H₂O adds 2 O and 4 H, giving CH₉N₂O₃ with charge +1 on both sides.","how":{"ask":"Find which side is short of O and H, and add water to fix both.","know":"• In aqueous (biochemical) reactions, **H₂O supplies missing O and H** (hydrolysis).\n• Add water to the side short of O, then recheck H and charge.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the products and says the only other reactant is the solvent. Count the products.","hl":"two NH₄⁺ ions and one HCO₃⁻ ion","calc":["2 NH₄⁺: N₂H₈ (+2); HCO₃⁻: HCO₃ (−1)","total C H₉ N₂ O₃, charge +1"]},{"text":"Concept: in water, an O and H shortfall is filled with H₂O. Count the known reactants.","calc":["(NH₂)₂CO: CH₄N₂O; H⁺: H (+1)","total C H₅ N₂ O, charge +1"]},{"text":"The left is short 2 O and 4 H, which is exactly 2 H₂O.","calc":["missing: H₄O₂ = 2 H₂O"]},{"text":"3 H₂O adds too many atoms, O₂ brings no H, and 2 OH⁻ brings only 2 H and a 2− charge.","strike":["3 H₂O","1 O₂","2 OH⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: 2 H₂O."}],"trap":"Adding O₂ to fix the oxygen count: in a hydrolysis the extra O arrives with H, as water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"urease","note":"a hydrolysis"},{"t":"2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻","note":"count these atoms"},{"t":"the only other reactant is the solvent","note":"water"},{"t":"both sides carry the same atoms and charge","note":"check both"}]},{"text":"**Find the shortfall.** Compare O and H on both sides.","calc":["O: 3 right vs 1 left → need 2","H: 9 right vs 5 left → need 4"]},{"text":"**Fill it with water.** 2 O and 4 H together are 2 H₂O; the charge stays +1 on each side."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an aqueous reaction, add H₂O to the side short of O, then recheck H and charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-44a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:44#1","pe5-cp:44#2","pe5-cp:44#3","pe6-cp:55#1","pe6-cp:55#2","pe6-cp:55#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:44"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P8","passage":"Gut bacteria turn a colorless precursor into a dye in three steps, W → X → Y → Z, catalyzed in order by enzymes K, L and M. X is the bright pigment that is wanted, but both Y and Z are toxic to the host, and any one or two of the enzymes can be knocked out. A crystal structure of enzyme K with a nucleotide analog shows the analog's doubly charged phosphate within 2.9 Å of one residue, making a salt bridge, and its flat purine ring lying parallel to a second side chain's ring at 3.6 Å, a π-stacking contact; no metal ion is in the site. The same bacteria release a neutral, rotten-egg-smelling gas through cysteine desulfhydrase, a pyridoxal phosphate enzyme: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___, where the blank is the gas. They also free nitrogen with urease, which turns urea, (NH₂)₂CO, plus one H⁺ into two NH₄⁺ ions and one HCO₃⁻ ion; the only other reactant is the solvent, and both sides carry the same atoms and charge. To label proteins with X, the lab uses an activated ester of X; the free-energy profile for its hydrolysis is plotted with a question, reactants at the start of the curve and products at its end, and steps with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds at room temperature.","q":"Based on the free-energy profile, the ester's hydrolysis is:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Free energy along the reaction coordinate","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":90,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Reaction","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,50],[1,50],[2,53],[3,61],[4,71],[5,75],[6,68],[7,48],[8,30],[9,21],[10,20]]}]},"choices":["Fast and favorable","Slow and favorable","Fast and unfavorable","Slow and unfavorable"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: products (20) end below reactants (50), so ΔG < 0 and the reaction is spontaneous."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Products sit below reactants: downhill means spontaneous, so nonspontaneous fails here too."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The barrier is peak minus reactants (75 − 50), under the 35 kJ/mol cutoff, so it isn't slow."}],"rule":"Speed from the barrier (peak − reactants vs the cutoff); spontaneity from the ends (products − reactants < 0). Read each separately.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two separate reads: peak − start = 75 − 50 = 25 < 35 → fast; end − start = 20 − 50 = −30 < 0 → spontaneous. Fast and spontaneous."},"exp":"Read three heights: reactants **50**, peak **75**, products **20 kJ/mol**. **Ea = 75 − 50 = 25 kJ/mol**, under 35, so it is fast. **ΔG = 20 − 50 = −30 kJ/mol**, below zero, so it is spontaneous.","how":{"ask":"Read Ea (peak − reactants) for the speed and ΔG (products − reactants) for spontaneity, separately.","know":"• **Ea = transition state − reactants**: a low Ea means a fast reaction.\n• **ΔG = products − reactants**: ΔG < 0 means spontaneous.\n• Speed (kinetics) and spontaneity (thermodynamics) are independent: read each from its own pair of heights.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 sets the speed cutoff: a barrier under 35 kJ/mol means fast. It also places reactants at the start of the curve and products at its end.","hl":"barriers under 35 kJ/mol"},{"text":"Concept: speed comes from Ea (peak − reactants), spontaneity from ΔG (products − reactants). Read the three heights from the plot.","calc":["reactants = **50 kJ/mol**","peak = **75 kJ/mol**","products = **20 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Speed from the barrier.","calc":["Ea = 75 − 50 = **25 kJ/mol** < 35 → **fast**"]},{"text":"A low barrier rules out both slow choices.","strike":["Slow and favorable","Slow and unfavorable"]},{"text":"Spontaneity from the ends.","calc":["ΔG = 20 − 50 = **−30 kJ/mol** < 0 → **spontaneous**"],"strike":["Fast and unfavorable"]},{"text":"Answer: fast and favorable (ΔG < 0, spontaneous)."}],"trap":"Measuring the barrier from the products (75 − 20 = 55 kJ/mol): Ea for the forward reaction starts at the reactants."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"free-energy profile","note":"read heights"},{"t":"barriers under 35 kJ/mol","note":"the speed cutoff"},{"t":"reactants at the start of the curve","note":"Ea and ΔG both start here"}]},{"text":"**Fast means low Ea.** The barrier height above the reactants sets the rate.","calc":["Ea = peak − reactants"]},{"text":"**Spontaneous means ΔG < 0.** Products lower than reactants is downhill.","calc":["ΔG = products − reactants"]},{"text":"**Also:** if no profile is given, infer the same two facts from words: \"occurs within seconds\" or \"rapid\" means a low Ea; \"favored\", \"releases free energy\" or \"proceeds to completion\" means ΔG < 0."},{"text":"**Also:** a solid that sits unchanged until a spark or shock sets it off is two things at once. Thermodynamically it is favorable: reacting releases heat, so products lie below reactants (exothermic). Kinetically it is stuck behind a tall barrier (a large Ea), so nothing happens until a trigger supplies that energy. Read any energy diagram with the same two subtractions: end heights give ΔH on an enthalpy diagram (ΔG on a free-energy diagram), peak minus reactants for Ea.","calc":["explosive: reactants 30, peak 200, products −250 kJ/mol","ΔH = −250 − 30 = −280 kJ/mol → exothermic","Ea = 200 − 30 = 170 kJ/mol → high: needs a trigger"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an energy profile, the rate is set by Ea (peak − reactants; low = fast) and spontaneity by ΔG (products − reactants; < 0 = spontaneous); read each separately."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-45a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:45#1","pe5-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:45"],"q":"Fully protonated glutamic acid, shown with its acidic groups labeled A–C, is titrated with NaOH. The first proton removed comes from which group?","fig":"cpt-pe5-glu-sites","choices":["Group B","Group A","Group C","All at once"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"B is the side-chain COOH (pKa ≈ 4.3); it is the second group to lose its H⁺, after a stronger acid."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A is the α-NH₃⁺ (pKa ≈ 9.7), the weakest acid, so it gives up its H⁺ last."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Separate pKa values (≈ 2, 4, 10) mean the groups lose H⁺ one at a time, not together."}],"rule":"Protons leave in pKa order, lowest first: α-COOH (≈ 2), side-chain COOH (≈ 4), then α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 10).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Glu loses H⁺ by pKa: α-COOH ~2, side-chain COOH ~4, α-NH₃⁺ ~10. First off = the α-COOH, group C."},"exp":"Groups lose H⁺ in order of **increasing pKa**: the **α-COOH (C, ≈ 2.2)** first, then the side-chain COOH (B, ≈ 4.3), then the α-NH₃⁺ (A, ≈ 9.7). The pKa values are far apart, so the groups titrate one after another.","how":{"ask":"Rank the labeled groups by pKa; the most acidic group (smallest pKa) gives up H⁺ first.","know":"• As base is added, groups deprotonate in order of **increasing pKa**.\n• Typical pKa: α-COOH ≈ 2, side-chain COOH ≈ 4, α-NH₃⁺ ≈ 9–10, basic side chains (Lys ε-NH₃⁺ ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5) last.","steps":[{"text":"Identify each label.","hl":"labeled A–C","calc":["A = α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.7)","B = side-chain COOH (≈ 4.3)","C = α-COOH (≈ 2.2)"]},{"text":"Order by pKa.","calc":["C (2.2) < B (4.3) < A (9.7)","**first C**, second B, third A"]},{"text":"B goes second, A goes last, and the well-separated pKa values rule out a single step.","strike":["Group B","Group A","All at once"]},{"text":"Answer: group C, the α-carboxyl."}],"trap":"Picking the side-chain group first because it is farther out: the α-COOH is the most acidic, pulled by the nearby NH₃⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Fully protonated","note":"every group holds its H⁺"},{"t":"titrated with NaOH","note":"rising pH"},{"t":"first","note":"lowest pKa"}]},{"text":"**Lowest pKa goes first.** The α-COOH (≈ 2) is made extra acidic by the neighboring α-NH₃⁺."},{"text":"**Then the side-chain acid.** Glutamate's γ-COOH has pKa ≈ 4.3.","calc":["order: α-COOH → side-chain COOH → α-NH₃⁺"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a basic amino acid the order ends with the side chain: lysine goes α-COOH (≈ 2.2), α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.0), then ε-NH₃⁺ (≈ 10.5)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a fully protonated amino acid loses H⁺ in order of increasing pKa: α-COOH, acidic side chain, α-NH₃⁺, basic side chain."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-46a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:46#1","pe5-cp:46#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:46"],"q":"The enthalpy profile shown compares a reaction uncatalyzed and with an enzyme. What are the catalyzed forward activation energy and ΔH?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Enthalpy along the reaction coordinate, uncatalyzed and catalyzed","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Enthalpy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":160,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Uncatalyzed","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,40],[3,72],[4,118],[5,140],[6,128],[7,100],[8,75],[9,62],[10,60]]},{"name":"Catalyzed","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,36],[3,55],[4,80],[5,90],[6,85],[7,74],[8,65],[9,61],[10,60]]}]},"choices":["60 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","110 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","50 kJ/mol; −30 kJ/mol","90 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: the products sit above the reactants on the profile, so ΔH is positive, not negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's the catalyzed peak's height above zero; subtract the reactants' energy to get the barrier."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That barrier uses the uncatalyzed peak (140); the enzyme's path is the lower peak."}],"rule":"Ea = peak − reactants on the curve asked about; ΔH = products − reactants, and a catalyst lowers the peak but never changes ΔH.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A catalyst can't move ΔH, so it's +30 for both paths (60 − 30). Catalyzed Ea = lower peak − reactants = 90 − 30 = 60 kJ/mol."},"exp":"Catalyzed **Ea = 90 − 30 = 60 kJ/mol**: the climb from the reactant level to the enzyme's lower peak. **ΔH = 60 − 30 = +30 kJ/mol** (endothermic) on both paths: an enzyme lowers the barrier but leaves the reactant and product levels where they were.","how":{"ask":"Measure Ea from the reactants to the catalyzed peak; measure ΔH from the reactants to the products.","know":"• **Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy**, using the peak of the path asked about.\n• **ΔH = products − reactants**; a catalyst lowers the peak but leaves both ends, so ΔH is the same on either path.","steps":[{"text":"Read the levels.","calc":["reactants = **30**","uncatalyzed peak = **140**","catalyzed peak = **90**","products = **60** kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Catalyzed activation energy.","hl":"catalyzed forward activation energy","calc":["Ea(cat) = 90 − 30 = **60 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"110 kJ/mol uses the uncatalyzed peak (140 − 30), and 90 kJ/mol is the peak's height above zero, not above the reactants.","strike":["110 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","90 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Enthalpy change, the same on both paths.","calc":["ΔH = 60 − 30 = **+30 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"50 kJ/mol is only how far the enzyme lowers the peak (140 − 90), and −30 flips the sign of ΔH by subtracting products from reactants.","strike":["50 kJ/mol; −30 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 60 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol."}],"trap":"Reading the peak's height straight off the axis (90): Ea is measured from the reactant level, not from zero."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"uncatalyzed and with an enzyme","note":"two paths"},{"t":"forward activation energy","note":"from reactants up"},{"t":"ΔH","note":"products − reactants"}]},{"text":"**Ea: reactants to the peak of the path asked.** The enzyme's curve has the lower peak.","calc":["Ea = TS − reactants = 90 − 30 = 60 kJ/mol","uncatalyzed: 140 − 30 = 110 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** ΔH = products − reactants, and both curves start and end at the same levels, so ΔH is the same with or without a catalyst.","calc":["ΔH = 60 − 30 = +30 kJ/mol (endothermic)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy (using the catalyzed peak for the catalyzed path) and ΔH = products − reactants, which a catalyst never changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-47a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:47#1","pe5-cp:47#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:47"],"q":"Crates of 6.0 kg and 10 kg hang on a rope over a frictionless pulley. Using g = 10 m/s², what is their acceleration?","choices":["2.5 m/s²","6.7 m/s²","40 m/s²","0.40 m/s²"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That is the net force in newtons reported as an acceleration; it is also four times g."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides total mass by net force, flipping the ratio."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Divides the net force by the lighter crate only, skipping the heavier one that must also accelerate."}],"rule":"Atwood machine: a = (m₂ − m₁)g / (m₁ + m₂): net force over total mass. The answer must be less than g.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Net force = (10 − 6.0)(10) = 40 N; total mass = 16 kg. a = 40/16."},"exp":"Net force = (m₂ − m₁)g = (10 − 6.0)(10) = 40 N, and it accelerates both crates, 16 kg in all: a = 40/16 = **2.5 m/s²**.","how":{"ask":"What is the net force on the system, and what total mass does it move?","know":"• Treat both masses and the rope as one system.\n• **Net force** = weight difference = (m₂ − m₁)g.\n• **a = F_net / (m₁ + m₂)**; always less than g.","steps":[{"text":"Find the net force.","hl":"6.0 kg and 10 kg","calc":["F_net = (10 − 6.0)(10) = 40 N"]},{"text":"Divide by the total mass.","calc":["a = 40 / 16 = 2.5 m/s²"]},{"text":"Net force is in newtons, not m/s², and the inverted ratio has the wrong units too.","strike":["40 m/s²","0.40 m/s²"]},{"text":"Both crates accelerate, so divide by both masses.","strike":["6.7 m/s²"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.5 m/s²."}],"trap":"Dividing by one crate's mass: the net force must accelerate everything that moves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"6.0 kg and 10 kg","note":"m₁ and m₂"},{"t":"frictionless pulley","note":"no friction, no mass"},{"t":"g = 10 m/s²","note":"given g"}]},{"text":"**One system.** The rope makes the two crates move together, so the weight difference drives the combined mass.","calc":["a = (m₂ − m₁)g / (m₁ + m₂)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for two masses over a pulley, acceleration = (weight difference) ÷ (total mass), which is always less than g."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:48#1","pe5-cp:48#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:48"],"q":"The heating curve shown follows 2.0 g of ice warmed from −40 °C to liquid water at 90 °C. Which step takes the most heat?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Heating curve for 2.0 g of water","x":{"label":"Heat added (kJ)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-40],[0.17,0],[0.84,0],[1.59,90]]}]},"choices":["Warming the ice","Melting the ice","Warming the water","Boiling the water"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"The curve stops at 90 °C, below the boiling point, so no water vaporizes at all."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Ice has a small specific heat and rises only 40 degrees: the shortest stretch of the heat axis."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Melting takes 334 J/g, but heating liquid water through 90 degrees takes about 4.18 × 90 ≈ 376 J/g."}],"rule":"Each step's heat equals its width along the heat-added axis; compare mL (plateaus) with mcΔT (slopes) instead of assuming phase changes win.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read widths on the heat axis: ice slope ≈ 0.17 kJ, plateau ≈ 0.67 kJ, liquid slope ≈ 0.75 kJ. The widest segment wins."},"exp":"Per gram: warming ice 2.1 × 40 ≈ 84 J; melting 334 J; warming water 4.18 × 90 ≈ 376 J. For 2.0 g, that is about 0.17, 0.67 and **0.75 kJ**: **warming the liquid** takes the most heat here. No boiling occurs.","how":{"ask":"How wide is each segment of the curve along the heat axis?","know":"• **Plateaus** (phase changes): q = mL; L_fus(water) = 334 J/g, L_vap = 2260 J/g.\n• **Slopes** (one phase warming): q = mcΔT; c(ice) ≈ 2.1, c(water) = 4.18 J/(g·°C).\n• Heat for a step = its horizontal width on a heat-added axis.","steps":[{"text":"Check which steps occur.","hl":"liquid water at 90 °C","strike":["Boiling the water"]},{"text":"Compute each step for 2.0 g.","calc":["ice: 2.0 × 2.1 × 40 ≈ 0.17 kJ","melt: 2.0 × 334 ≈ 0.67 kJ","water: 2.0 × 4.18 × 90 ≈ 0.75 kJ"]},{"text":"The ice slope is narrowest, and the melting plateau is narrower than the liquid slope.","strike":["Warming the ice","Melting the ice"]},{"text":"Answer: warming the water."}],"trap":"Assuming the phase change always takes the most heat: here 90 degrees of liquid warming outweighs melting."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"−40 °C","note":"start: ice"},{"t":"liquid water at 90 °C","note":"end: no boiling"},{"t":"most heat","note":"widest segment"}]},{"text":"**Read widths, not heights.** On a heat-added axis, each segment's horizontal width is the heat it took."},{"text":"**Also:** if the curve had gone on to steam, vaporizing would dwarf every other step: L_vap = 2260 J/g, almost seven times L_fus and more than five times the heat to warm water from 0 to 100 °C."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find the step of a heating curve that takes the most heat, compare mL for each plateau and mcΔT for each slope (or read widths); vaporization wins only if boiling is part of the curve."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-49a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:49#1","pe5-cp:49#2","pe5-cp:49#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:49"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P9","passage":"A rescue robot is tested on soft flooring that dents when the pressure under a foot exceeds a set limit. Each square foot pad, 6.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N when the robot's other foot is lifted. Under each pad, a touch sensor stores charge on a parallel-plate capacitor with air between its plates, which stays wired to a 12 V battery while the robot is on; designers want more stored charge so the signal is larger. To keep its joints warm, the robot fires brief pulses through a heating coil of constant resistance: a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J and empties fully through the coil in 5.0 ms. A planned upgrade replaces that capacitor with a 120 μF one that must deliver the same charge per pulse.","q":"What pressure does one pad exert on the floor?","choices":["20 kPa","200 kPa","2.0 kPa","200 Pa"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Too big: that is 72 N on 3.6 cm², ten times too little area; the pad is 36 cm²."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That is 72 ÷ 36, a value in N/cm² mislabeled as kPa; convert cm² to m² first."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Area slip: that uses 1 cm² = 10⁻² m²; one cm² is really 10⁻⁴ m²."}],"rule":"P = F ÷ A with A in m², and 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m². Divide in N/cm² with rounded numbers, then × 10⁴ for Pa.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Divide in cm² first: 72 N ÷ 36 cm² = 2 N/cm². Each m² is 10⁴ cm², so × 10⁴ → 20,000 Pa."},"exp":"Pressure is force over area in square metres, **P = F/A**. Sentence 2 gives a pad 0.060 m × 0.060 m = 3.6 × 10⁻³ m² and a force of 72 N, so P = 72 N ÷ 0.0036 m² = 20,000 Pa = **20 kPa**. One cm² is 10⁻⁴ m², not 10⁻² m².","how":{"ask":"Take the force from the passage, find the pad's area in square metres, then divide.","know":"• **Pressure: P = F/A**, in Pa = N/m².\n• **1 cm = 10⁻² m, so 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m²**: multiply the side lengths in cm, then × 10⁻⁴.\n• 1 kPa = 10³ Pa.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the force: the push straight down on the floor.","hl":"presses straight down on the floor with 72 N"},{"text":"Concept: P = F/A with A in m². The same sentence gives the side; find the area in cm², then in m².","hl":"6.0 cm on a side","calc":["A = 6.0 × 6.0 = 36 cm²","36 cm² × 10⁻⁴ = 3.6 × 10⁻³ m²"]},{"text":"Divide the force by the area.","calc":["P = 72 N ÷ 3.6 × 10⁻³ m² = 2.0 × 10⁴ Pa = 20 kPa"]},{"text":"200 Pa converts the area with 10⁻² instead of 10⁻⁴; 2.0 kPa is 72 ÷ 36, a value in N/cm² mislabeled as kPa; 200 kPa slips one extra power of ten into the area.","strike":["200 Pa","2.0 kPa","200 kPa"]},{"text":"Answer: 20 kPa."}],"trap":"Squaring the length but not the conversion: 1 cm² is (10⁻² m)² = 10⁻⁴ m²."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"square foot pad","note":"area = side²"},{"t":"6.0 cm on a side","note":"convert to m"},{"t":"72 N","note":"the force"},{"t":"What pressure","note":"P = F/A"}]},{"text":"**P = F/A.** Pressure is the force spread over each square metre of contact."},{"text":"**Also: convert cm² to m².** Multiply the sides in cm, then × 10⁻⁴.","calc":["6.0 × 6.0 = 36 cm²","36 cm² × 10⁻⁴ = 0.0036 m²","72 ÷ 0.0036 = 20,000 Pa"]},{"text":"**Also: take F from the passage.** A passage states the force in a sentence; pick the number in newtons that describes the push on the surface, here the 72 N pressing straight down."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** P = F/A with A in m²; for sides in cm, multiply them and then × 10⁻⁴ before dividing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-50a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:50#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:50"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P9","passage":"A rescue robot is tested on soft flooring that dents when the pressure under a foot exceeds a set limit. Each square foot pad, 6.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N when the robot's other foot is lifted. Under each pad, a touch sensor stores charge on a parallel-plate capacitor with air between its plates, which stays wired to a 12 V battery while the robot is on; designers want more stored charge so the signal is larger. To keep its joints warm, the robot fires brief pulses through a heating coil of constant resistance: a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J and empties fully through the coil in 5.0 ms. A planned upgrade replaces that capacitor with a 120 μF one that must deliver the same charge per pulse.","q":"What average current flows through the coil during a pulse?","choices":["4.8 A","4.8 mA","1.9 kA","24 mA"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"The time was left in milliseconds; 5.0 ms is 0.0050 s, so the current is 1000 times off."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"J ÷ s gives watts, a power, not amperes: that divides the stored energy, not the charge, by the time."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the charge, Q = 0.024 C, relabeled; a current needs Q ÷ t."}],"rule":"Current is charge per second: Q = CV, then divide by t in seconds; the joules are a distractor. Round C and V to 1 digit, keep the powers of 10.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Amps = coulombs per second, so ignore the joules. Q = 30 μ × 800 = 24,000 μC = 0.024 C; ÷ 0.005 s: 24 ÷ 5 ≈ 5, powers cancel → about 5 A."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives the pulse capacitor. **Q = CV** = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F × 800 V = 0.024 C. **I = Q/t** = 0.024 C ÷ (5.0 × 10⁻³ s) = **4.8 A**. The 9.6 J of stored energy is not needed.","how":{"ask":"Find the charge that leaves the plates, then divide it by the discharge time in seconds.","know":"• **Q = CV** (C in farads, V in volts, Q in coulombs).\n• **Average current: I = Q/t** (A = C/s).\n• **μ = 10⁻⁶, m = 10⁻³**: convert μF and ms first.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the pulse capacitor, its voltage and the discharge time; the 9.6 J is a distractor.","hl":"a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V","calc":["C = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F","V = 800 V","t = 5.0 ms = 5.0 × 10⁻³ s"]},{"text":"Concept: current is charge per time. Find the charge on the plates with Q = CV.","calc":["Q = (30 × 10⁻⁶)(800) = 0.024 C"]},{"text":"All of that charge flows out in the stated time; divide.","hl":"in 5.0 ms","calc":["I = 0.024 C ÷ 0.0050 s = 4.8 A"]},{"text":"4.8 mA leaves the time in milliseconds; 24 mA is the charge, 0.024 C, read as a current without dividing by t.","strike":["4.8 mA","24 mA"]},{"text":"1.9 kA divides the energy by the time, 9.6 J ÷ 0.0050 s, which is a power in watts, not a current.","strike":["1.9 kA"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.8 A."}],"trap":"Reaching for the stored energy: current is charge per time, so use Q = CV and skip the joules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"30 μF","note":"C"},{"t":"800 V","note":"V"},{"t":"9.6 J","note":"not needed"},{"t":"5.0 ms","note":"t"},{"t":"average current","note":"I = Q/t"}]},{"text":"**Q = CV.** The stored charge is capacitance × voltage, in farads × volts.","calc":["Q = 30 × 10⁻⁶ × 800 = 0.024 C"]},{"text":"**Also: average current = charge ÷ time**, with μF → 10⁻⁶ F and ms → 10⁻³ s.","calc":["5.0 ms = 0.0050 s","I = 0.024 ÷ 0.0050 = 4.8 A"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a capacitor discharge, I_avg = Q/t = CV/t, with μF → 10⁻⁶ F and ms → 10⁻³ s; the stored energy is a distractor."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-51a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:51#1","pe5-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:51"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P9","passage":"A rescue robot is tested on soft flooring that dents when the pressure under a foot exceeds a set limit. Each square foot pad, 6.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N when the robot's other foot is lifted. Under each pad, a touch sensor stores charge on a parallel-plate capacitor with air between its plates, which stays wired to a 12 V battery while the robot is on; designers want more stored charge so the signal is larger. To keep its joints warm, the robot fires brief pulses through a heating coil of constant resistance: a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J and empties fully through the coil in 5.0 ms. A planned upgrade replaces that capacitor with a 120 μF one that must deliver the same charge per pulse.","q":"What change raises the charge the touch sensor's plates hold?","choices":["Inserting a glass sheet","Moving the plates apart","Cutting the plates smaller","Swapping to a 6 V battery"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"C = κε₀A/d: a bigger d lowers C, so Q = CV falls."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Smaller plates shrink A, which lowers C, so the charge drops instead of rising."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Q = CV: halving V halves Q, so a smaller battery can't raise the charge."}],"rule":"With the battery attached, Q = CV: only a change that raises C (bigger A, smaller d, a dielectric) or raises V adds charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Battery attached means V is locked, so Q rises only if C rises. C = κε₀A/d: only a dielectric (bigger κ) helps; apart, smaller plates or lower V all cut Q."},"exp":"The battery holds V at 12 V, so the charge **Q = CV** can grow only through a larger C. Since **C = κε₀A/d**, a glass sheet (κ ≈ 5) raises C about fivefold, and the battery moves about five times the charge onto the plates.","how":{"ask":"Hold V fixed and decide which change raises C = κε₀A/d.","know":"• **Q = CV**; with the battery connected, **V is fixed**.\n• **C = κε₀A/d**: C rises with larger area A, smaller separation d, or a dielectric (κ > 1).\n• At fixed C, a higher V also raises Q.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the sensor stays wired to the battery: V is fixed, so Q follows C.","hl":"stays wired to a 12 V battery","calc":["Q = C × 12 V"]},{"text":"Concept: C = κε₀A/d. A glass sheet is a dielectric (κ ≈ 5) replacing the air: C rises about fivefold, and so does Q.","hl":"Inserting a glass sheet","calc":["C' = κC ≈ 5C","Q' = 5C × 12 V = 5Q"]},{"text":"Moving the plates apart raises d, and cutting the plates shrinks A; both lower C and so lower Q.","strike":["Moving the plates apart","Cutting the plates smaller"]},{"text":"A 6 V battery halves V, which halves Q at the same C.","strike":["Swapping to a 6 V battery"]},{"text":"Answer: inserting a glass sheet."}],"trap":"Thinking a dielectric lowers the charge because it weakens the field: that happens only in an isolated capacitor; with the battery connected, V and E stay fixed and the plates take on κ times the charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"parallel-plate capacitor","note":"C = κε₀A/d"},{"t":"air between its plates","note":"κ ≈ 1 now"},{"t":"stays wired to a 12 V battery","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"more stored charge","note":"raise Q = CV"}]},{"text":"**Q = CV and C = κε₀A/d.** At fixed V, anything that raises C raises Q."},{"text":"**Also: the four ways up.** More plate area, a smaller gap, a dielectric, or a higher voltage each increase Q.","calc":["A ×2 → Q ×2","d ×½ → Q ×2","κ = 5 → Q ×5","V ×2 → Q ×2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with Q = CV and C = κε₀A/d, charge rises with larger area, smaller separation, an inserted dielectric, or a higher voltage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:52"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P9","passage":"A rescue robot is tested on soft flooring that dents when the pressure under a foot exceeds a set limit. Each square foot pad, 6.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N when the robot's other foot is lifted. Under each pad, a touch sensor stores charge on a parallel-plate capacitor with air between its plates, which stays wired to a 12 V battery while the robot is on; designers want more stored charge so the signal is larger. To keep its joints warm, the robot fires brief pulses through a heating coil of constant resistance: a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J and empties fully through the coil in 5.0 ms. A planned upgrade replaces that capacitor with a 120 μF one that must deliver the same charge per pulse.","q":"What voltage would give the upgraded capacitor the same charge?","choices":["200 V","3200 V","800 V","400 V"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio upside-down: a bigger capacitor needs less voltage for the same Q, so V must drop below 800 V."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That keeps the old voltage; the same V on a bigger C puts more charge on it (Q = CV)."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That matches the stored energy (½CV²), a square-root ratio; equal charge needs Q = CV."}],"rule":"Same charge means C₁V₁ = C₂V₂: a bigger capacitor needs a smaller voltage. Multiply V by old C ÷ new C, rounded to 1 digit.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Same Q means V ∝ 1/C. C grows 30 → 120, a factor of 4, so V = 800 ÷ 4."},"exp":"**Same Q: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.** Sentence 4 gives Q = 30 μF × 800 V = 0.024 C, and sentence 5 the new 120 μF, so V₂ = 0.024 C ÷ 120 μF = **200 V**. A larger capacitor needs proportionally less voltage to hold the same charge.","how":{"ask":"Write the charge on the present capacitor and solve for the voltage that puts that charge on the upgrade.","know":"• **Q = CV**; equal charge means **C₁V₁ = C₂V₂**.\n• So **V₂ = C₁V₁/C₂**: a bigger C needs less V.\n• The μF units cancel in the ratio C₁/C₂.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the present capacitor; find its charge.","hl":"a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V","calc":["Q = 30 μF × 800 V = 24,000 μC"]},{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the upgrade. Concept: equal charge means C₁V₁ = C₂V₂; solve for V₂.","hl":"a 120 μF one that must deliver the same charge","calc":["V₂ = C₁V₁/C₂ = 30 × 800 ÷ 120 = 200 V"]},{"text":"3200 V multiplies by C₂/C₁ instead of C₁/C₂; 800 V keeps the old voltage, which would put more charge on the larger capacitor.","strike":["3200 V","800 V"]},{"text":"400 V matches the stored energy (½CV²) instead of the charge.","strike":["400 V"]},{"text":"Answer: 200 V."}],"trap":"Flipping the ratio: a larger capacitor holds the same charge at a lower voltage, so V₂ must come out below 800 V."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"30 μF","note":"C₁"},{"t":"800 V","note":"V₁"},{"t":"120 μF","note":"C₂"},{"t":"same charge","note":"C₁V₁ = C₂V₂"}]},{"text":"**Equal charge: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.** Since Q = CV, set the two charges equal."},{"text":"**Solve as a ratio.** The μF cancel.","calc":["V₂ = 800 V × 30/120","V₂ = 800 V × 0.25"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for equal charge on two capacitors, V₂ = C₁V₁/C₂; the larger capacitor needs proportionally less voltage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-53a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:53"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P10","passage":"A wearable health monitor shines light into skin from several LEDs and a tunable laser, all in air, where the speed of light does not depend on wavelength. Its blue-green LED emits at 480 nm, and that light's frequency sets the energy each photon delivers to a dye in the sensor. A near-infrared LED, chosen because its light passes through skin far better than visible light, emits photons that each carry 1.55 eV; the product of Planck's constant and the speed of light is about 1240 eV·nm. For deeper imaging, the tunable laser is retuned from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm at the same power. A bench test compares two other sources: each ultraviolet photon from one carries three times the energy of an infrared photon from the other, and the detector counts single photons.","q":"After the laser is retuned, its frequency is:","choices":["one-third as high.","three times as high.","unchanged.","one-ninth as high."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Frequency and wavelength are inversely related, so a longer wavelength cannot raise the frequency."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"With c fixed, changing λ must change f; only the power was kept the same."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Squares the ratio: c = fλ is a first-power relation, so f scales by the ratio itself."}],"rule":"c = fλ with c fixed: f ∝ 1/λ, so scaling λ by n scales f by 1/n (not 1/n²).","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The wavelength triples (500 → 1500 nm). Since fλ is constant, the frequency falls by the same factor."},"exp":"In air, c = fλ is constant. The wavelength goes from 500 nm to 1500 nm, three times longer, so the frequency falls to **one-third** of its old value. Power does not affect frequency.","how":{"ask":"By what factor did λ change, and how must f change to keep fλ constant?","know":"• **c = fλ**; in one medium c is fixed.\n• So **f ∝ 1/λ**: doubling λ halves f, tripling λ cuts f to one-third.\n• Photon energy E = hf also falls by that factor.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the change in wavelength: it triples.","hl":"from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm","calc":["1500 / 500 = 3"]},{"text":"Concept: sentence 1 keeps c = fλ fixed, so f scales as 1/λ.","calc":["f₂ = f₁ × (500/1500) = f₁/3"]},{"text":"Longer λ cannot mean higher f.","strike":["three times as high."]},{"text":"f must change, and by the first power of the ratio, not its square.","strike":["unchanged.","one-ninth as high."]},{"text":"Answer: one-third as high."}],"trap":"Squaring the ratio as if f ∝ 1/λ²: the relation is first power."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"500 nm to 1500 nm","note":"λ × 3"},{"t":"does not depend on wavelength","note":"c fixed"}]},{"text":"**Inverse scaling.** With c = fλ fixed, any factor on λ appears as its reciprocal on f."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in one medium, f = c/λ, so multiplying the wavelength by n divides the frequency by n."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-54a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:54#1","pe5-cp:54#2","pe5-cp:54#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:54"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P10","passage":"A wearable health monitor shines light into skin from several LEDs and a tunable laser, all in air, where the speed of light does not depend on wavelength. Its blue-green LED emits at 480 nm, and that light's frequency sets the energy each photon delivers to a dye in the sensor. A near-infrared LED, chosen because its light passes through skin far better than visible light, emits photons that each carry 1.55 eV; the product of Planck's constant and the speed of light is about 1240 eV·nm. For deeper imaging, the tunable laser is retuned from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm at the same power. A bench test compares two other sources: each ultraviolet photon from one carries three times the energy of an infrared photon from the other, and the detector counts single photons.","q":"Compared with the infrared photon, the ultraviolet photon has:","choices":["3× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength.","3× the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","⅓ the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","9× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"λ = c/f, so the wavelength must fall when the frequency rises; it can't triple too."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"E = hf: more energy means more frequency, never ⅓ of it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"E = hf is linear, with nothing squared: tripling the energy triples the frequency, it doesn't make it 9×."}],"rule":"E = hf and λ = c/f: energy and frequency change by the same factor, and wavelength changes by the inverse factor.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"E ∝ f and E ∝ 1/λ. Triple the energy: f triples, λ drops to a third. Only one choice pairs 3× with ⅓."},"exp":"**E = hf**: energy is proportional to frequency, so 3× the energy means **3× the frequency**. **E = hc/λ**: energy is inversely proportional to wavelength, so 3× the energy means **⅓ the wavelength**.","how":{"ask":"Scale frequency and wavelength by the energy factor.","know":"• **E = hf** (h is a constant): f ∝ E.\n• **E = hc/λ** (c fixed): λ ∝ 1/E.\n• A photon with k times the energy has k times the frequency and 1/k the wavelength.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the energy factor between the two photons: k = 3.","hl":"three times the energy"},{"text":"Concept: E = hf, so frequency scales directly with energy.","calc":["f = E/h → f(UV) = 3 × f(IR)"]},{"text":"Wavelength scales inversely with energy.","calc":["λ = hc/E → λ(UV) = λ(IR) ÷ 3"]},{"text":"Wavelength cannot rise with energy, frequency cannot fall, and nothing squares here.","strike":["3× the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","⅓ the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","9× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength."]},{"text":"Answer: 3× the frequency and ⅓ the wavelength."}],"trap":"Scaling wavelength the same way as frequency: since c = fλ is fixed, they always move in opposite directions."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"three times the energy","note":"k = 3"},{"t":"ultraviolet photon","note":"higher-energy one"}]},{"text":"**Frequency follows energy.** E = hf, so f is proportional to E.","calc":["E × 3 → f × 3"]},{"text":"**Wavelength opposes energy.** E = hc/λ, so λ is inversely proportional to E.","calc":["E × 3 → λ ÷ 3"]},{"text":"**Also:** a photon with k times the energy has 1/k the wavelength, because f and λ multiply to the fixed speed c."},{"text":"**Also:** amplitude and intensity describe a beam, not one photon. A brighter beam has more photons per second, but each photon's energy, frequency and wavelength stay the same."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf = hc/λ, so k times the energy means k times the frequency and 1/k the wavelength."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-55a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:55#1","pe5-cp:55#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:55"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P10","passage":"A wearable health monitor shines light into skin from several LEDs and a tunable laser, all in air, where the speed of light does not depend on wavelength. Its blue-green LED emits at 480 nm, and that light's frequency sets the energy each photon delivers to a dye in the sensor. A near-infrared LED, chosen because its light passes through skin far better than visible light, emits photons that each carry 1.55 eV; the product of Planck's constant and the speed of light is about 1240 eV·nm. For deeper imaging, the tunable laser is retuned from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm at the same power. A bench test compares two other sources: each ultraviolet photon from one carries three times the energy of an infrared photon from the other, and the detector counts single photons.","q":"Approximately what is the frequency of the blue-green LED's light?","choices":["6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz","6.3 × 10⁵ Hz","6.3 × 10¹⁷ Hz","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Skips the nm → m step (10⁻⁹); 10⁵ Hz is radio, far below visible light."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Uses picometres instead of nanometres, 1000 times too high: 10¹⁷ Hz is X-ray, not visible light."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Flipped: that's λ ÷ c, a time in seconds; frequency is c ÷ λ."}],"rule":"f = c/λ with λ in metres (1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m). Divide the leading digits and the powers of 10 separately.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"f = c/λ: 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 4.8 × 10⁻⁷. Digits 3 ÷ 4.8 ≈ 0.63; powers 10⁸⁺⁷ = 10¹⁵ → 0.63 × 10¹⁵ = 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Visible light always lands near 10¹⁴."},"exp":"**f = c/λ** with λ in metres: 480 nm = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m, so f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m) ≈ **6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz**, inside the visible band of about 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz.","how":{"ask":"Convert the wavelength to metres, then divide the speed of light by it.","know":"• **c = fλ**, so **f = c/λ**, with c ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m/s and λ in **metres**.\n• 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.\n• Visible light (about 400–750 nm) has f ≈ 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, so any visible answer must land near 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the wavelength in nanometres; convert it to metres.","hl":"480 nm","calc":["480 nm × 10⁻⁹ m/nm = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"Concept: f = c/λ, with λ in metres. Divide c by λ.","calc":["f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m)","= (3 ÷ 4.8) × 10¹⁵ Hz","= 0.625 × 10¹⁵ ≈ **6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"Check the band: blue-green light must sit between 4 and 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz. 10⁵ Hz skips the nm conversion (radio range), 10¹⁷ Hz uses picometres (X-ray range), and 10⁻¹⁵ divides λ by c.","strike":["6.3 × 10⁵ Hz","6.3 × 10¹⁷ Hz","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz."}],"trap":"Dividing by 480 instead of 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m: keep λ in metres, and use the visible band as a sanity check."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"480 nm","note":"convert to m"},{"t":"blue-green LED","note":"visible: expect ~10¹⁴ Hz"}]},{"text":"**Fix the unit.** Speed of light is in m/s, so λ must be in m.","calc":["480 × 10⁻⁹ m = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"**Divide.**","calc":["f = c/λ = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"**Also:** visible light spans about 400–750 nm, which is about 7.5 × 10¹⁴ down to 4 × 10¹⁴ Hz; a visible-light frequency far outside 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz signals a unit error."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** f = c/λ with λ in metres (nm × 10⁻⁹), then check that visible light lands near 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-56a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:56"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P10","passage":"A wearable health monitor shines light into skin from several LEDs and a tunable laser, all in air, where the speed of light does not depend on wavelength. Its blue-green LED emits at 480 nm, and that light's frequency sets the energy each photon delivers to a dye in the sensor. A near-infrared LED, chosen because its light passes through skin far better than visible light, emits photons that each carry 1.55 eV; the product of Planck's constant and the speed of light is about 1240 eV·nm. For deeper imaging, the tunable laser is retuned from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm at the same power. A bench test compares two other sources: each ultraviolet photon from one carries three times the energy of an infrared photon from the other, and the detector counts single photons.","q":"The wavelength of the near-infrared LED's light is approximately:","choices":["800 nm","80 nm","8000 nm","1900 nm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a factor of ten: a decimal slip in the division puts the light in the far ultraviolet."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a factor of ten the other way, far into the infrared."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplies hc by E instead of dividing; λ = hc/E falls as energy rises."}],"rule":"λ(nm) ≈ 1240 / E(eV). Visible light is about 1.8–3.1 eV (700–400 nm); lower energy means longer wavelength.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"1240 ÷ 1.55: 1.55 × 800 = 1240. A bit below 1.8 eV puts it just past red, in the near infrared."},"exp":"E = hc/λ, so λ = hc/E = (1240 eV·nm)/(1.55 eV) = **800 nm**, near-infrared light, just past red.","how":{"ask":"What is hc/E in nanometres?","know":"• **E = hf = hc/λ**, so λ = hc/E.\n• **hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm**.\n• Visible: 400 nm (≈ 3.1 eV) to 700 nm (≈ 1.8 eV).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the photon energy and hc for the near-infrared LED.","hl":"each carry 1.55 eV","calc":["λ = hc/E"]},{"text":"Concept: λ = hc/E, so divide 1240 eV·nm by the energy in eV.","calc":["λ = 1240 / 1.55 = 800 nm"]},{"text":"A factor-of-ten slip either way leaves the near infrared.","strike":["80 nm","8000 nm"]},{"text":"hc × E has the wrong units and the wrong trend.","strike":["1900 nm"]},{"text":"Answer: about 800 nm."}],"trap":"Multiplying instead of dividing: higher photon energy must give a shorter wavelength."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"1.55 eV","note":"E per photon"},{"t":"1240 eV·nm","note":"hc"},{"t":"near-infrared","note":"expect λ above 700 nm"}]},{"text":"**Shortcut constant.** With hc = 1240 eV·nm, λ in nm is 1240 divided by E in eV."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ(nm) = 1240/E(eV); check the answer against the visible range, 400–700 nm."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-57a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:57#1","pe5-cp:57#2","pe5-cp:57#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:57"],"q":"Hypochlorous acid, HOCl, has a pKa of 7.5 in water. What is Kb of its conjugate base, the hypochlorite ion?","choices":["3.2 × 10⁻⁷","3.2 × 10⁻⁸","3.2 × 10⁻²²","6.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Kb = Kw/Ka; multiplying Ka by Kw instead gives 10⁻²², far too small."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"6.5 is pKb (14 − 7.5); the last step, Kb = 10^−pKb, was skipped. Kb for a weak base is a small power of ten."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That's Ka of the acid itself, 10^−7.5, taken straight from the given pKa; the base's Kb is different."}],"rule":"Conjugate pairs: pKa + pKb = 14 (Ka·Kb = 10⁻¹⁴), so the weaker the acid, the larger its conjugate base's Kb.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"pKa + pKb = 14 → pKb 6.5. 10^−6.5 = 10^0.5 × 10⁻⁷, and 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2, so Kb ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷."},"exp":"**pKa + pKb = 14**, so pKb = 14 − 7.5 = 6.5, and **Kb = 10^−pKb** = 10⁻⁶·⁵ ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻⁷**. Check: Ka·Kb = (3.2 × 10⁻⁸)(3.2 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ = Kw.","how":{"ask":"Get pKb from the pKa, then turn pKb into Kb.","know":"• For a conjugate pair: **pKa + pKb = 14** and **Ka·Kb = Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴** (at 25 °C).\n• **Kb = 10^−pKb**.\n• A weaker acid (higher pKa) has a stronger conjugate base (larger Kb).","steps":[{"text":"Start from the acid's pKa.","hl":"pKa of 7.5","calc":["pKb = 14 − 7.5 = **6.5**"]},{"text":"Convert pKb to Kb.","calc":["Kb = 10⁻⁶·⁵ = 10⁰·⁵ × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 3.16 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻⁷**"]},{"text":"Check with Ka·Kb = Kw.","calc":["Ka = 10⁻⁷·⁵ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁸","Kb = Kw/Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 3.2 × 10⁻⁸ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷ ✓"]},{"text":"3.2 × 10⁻⁸ is Ka itself, 3.2 × 10⁻²² multiplies Ka by Kw instead of dividing, and 6.5 is pKb, not Kb.","strike":["3.2 × 10⁻⁸","3.2 × 10⁻²²","6.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.2 × 10⁻⁷."}],"trap":"Stopping at pKb = 6.5: the question asks for Kb, so finish with Kb = 10^−pKb."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa of 7.5","note":"the acid's strength"},{"t":"Kb of its conjugate base","note":"base strength"}]},{"text":"**Use the pair rule.**","calc":["pKa + pKb = 14 → pKb = 6.5"]},{"text":"**Undo the log.**","calc":["Kb = 10⁻⁶·⁵ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷"]},{"text":"**Also:** Kb = 10^−pKb; a half-unit pKb gives 10⁰·⁵ ≈ 3.16 in front of the power of ten."},{"text":"**Also:** Ka·Kb = Kw, so Kb = Kw/Ka; the weaker the acid (smaller Ka), the stronger its conjugate base."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pKb = 14 − pKa and Kb = 10^−pKb (equivalently Kb = Kw/Ka)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-58a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:58#1","pe5-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:58"],"q":"Acetic acid and ethanol form ethyl acetate and water over an acidic resin catalyst at 70 °C. Which change would NOT raise the equilibrium amount of ester?","choices":["Adding more ethanol","Adding a drying agent","Adding extra glacial acetic acid","Grinding the resin finer"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Adding a reactant lowers Q below K, so the reaction shifts toward more ester."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A drying agent removes water, a product, so the equilibrium shifts right and makes more ester."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"More of the other reactant also lowers Q below K and pushes the system toward ester."}],"rule":"In a NOT question, three choices shift the equilibrium (add reactant, remove product); the odd one changes only the rate, such as anything done to the catalyst.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Sort the choices: reactant added, product removed, reactant added... and one that only changes how fast equilibrium is reached. Rate is not yield."},"exp":"Adding ethanol or acetic acid (reactants) or removing water (product) shifts the equilibrium toward ethyl acetate (Le Châtelier). A catalyst, in any amount or particle size, speeds both directions equally: it reaches equilibrium sooner but **does not change K or the yield**.","how":{"ask":"Which change only alters the rate, leaving Q and K untouched?","know":"• **Le Châtelier**: adding a reactant or removing a product shifts the reaction toward products.\n• A **catalyst** lowers Ea for the forward and reverse steps equally: faster equilibrium, same position.\n• Only temperature changes K.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium.","calc":["CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O"]},{"text":"Adding either reactant lowers Q below K, so more ester forms.","strike":["Adding more ethanol","Adding extra glacial acetic acid"]},{"text":"Removing water, a product, also lowers Q.","strike":["Adding a drying agent"]},{"text":"Finer resin only exposes more catalyst surface: faster, not further.","hl":"acidic resin catalyst"},{"text":"Answer: grinding the resin finer."}],"trap":"Thinking more catalyst means more product: a catalyst never moves the equilibrium position."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"acidic resin catalyst","note":"rate only"},{"t":"NOT","note":"find the one that fails"},{"t":"equilibrium amount","note":"yield, not speed"}]},{"text":"**Two kinds of change.** Concentration changes move Q away from K and shift the reaction; catalyst changes leave Q and K alone and only speed things up."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to raise an equilibrium yield, add a reactant or remove a product; changing the catalyst (amount, surface area or type) changes only the rate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-59a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:59#1","pe5-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:59"],"q":"A robot drags a sled 12 m on a level track. The graph shows its parallel pull and the friction on the sled. How much work does the pull do?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Forces on the sled versus position","x":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Force (N)","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Pull","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2,20],[4,40],[6,40],[8,40],[10,40],[11,20],[12,0]]},{"name":"Friction","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,5],[12,5]]}]},"choices":["360 J","480 J","300 J","240 J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"40 N × 12 m treats the pull as 40 N all the way, but the graph ramps up and down."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"6 m × 40 N counts only the flat top and drops both ramp triangles."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"That subtracts friction's 5 N × 12 m: it is the net work, but the question asks for the pull's work alone."}],"rule":"Work by one force = area under that force's line on a force-vs-distance graph; count ramps as triangles, ½ × base × height.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Area under the pull line = trapezoid: average of the two bases × height. Top 6 m, bottom 12 m → 9 × 40 = 360 J. Friction line is irrelevant."},"exp":"Work done by a changing force is the **area under its F–x line**. Split the pull into a triangle, a rectangle and a triangle: ½(4)(40) + (6)(40) + ½(2)(40) = 80 + 240 + 40 = **360 J**. Friction is not part of the pull's work.","how":{"ask":"Find the area under the pull line only, from 0 to 12 m.","know":"• **W = area under the F–x graph** (N × m = J) for a force along the motion.\n• Split the area into **rectangles (base × height)** and **triangles (½ × base × height)**.\n• Use only the force the question names.","steps":[{"text":"Read the corners of the pull line off the graph.","hl":"How much work does the pull do","calc":["0 → 4 m: rises 0 → 40 N","4 → 10 m: flat at 40 N","10 → 12 m: falls 40 → 0 N"],"graph":{"focus":["Pull"]}},{"text":"Find the area of each piece.","calc":["ramp up: ½ × 4 m × 40 N = 80 J","plateau: 6 m × 40 N = 240 J","ramp down: ½ × 2 m × 40 N = 40 J"],"graph":{"focus":["Pull"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":12,"text":"area = work by the pull"}]}},{"text":"Add the pieces.","calc":["W = 80 J + 240 J + 40 J = **360 J**"]},{"text":"480 J is 40 N × 12 m, treating the pull as 40 N the whole way; 240 J keeps only the flat part.","strike":["480 J","240 J"]},{"text":"300 J subtracts friction's 5 N × 12 m = 60 J, which gives the net work, not the pull's work.","strike":["300 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 360 J."}],"trap":"Multiplying the peak force by the whole distance; the ramps are triangles, worth only half of their rectangles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"12 m","note":"total distance"},{"t":"parallel pull","note":"all of the pull does work"},{"t":"work does the pull do","note":"pull only, not friction"}]},{"text":"**Work is force × distance**, but this pull changes with position, so no single force works. Instead, the **work equals the area under the pull line**.","graph":{"focus":["Pull"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":12,"text":"area = work"}]}},{"text":"**Also: split the shape into rectangles and triangles.** Here: a rising ramp, a flat top and a falling ramp.","calc":["triangle: ½ × base × height","rectangle: base × height"]},{"text":"**Ignore the friction line.** It shares the axes, but the question asks only about the pull.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Friction","x":6,"text":"friction: not asked","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the work by a varying force is the area under the line of that force on an F–x plot; cut it into rectangles and triangles, add the pieces, and use only the force asked about."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-01a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:1#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:1"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"Based on its IR spectrum, which compound is the Route A liquid?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"IR spectrum of the unknown liquid","x":{"label":"Wavenumber (cm⁻¹)","min":500,"max":4000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Transmittance (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Unknown","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[500,88],[600,80],[800,72],[1000,45],[1070,30],[1150,70],[1250,78],[1350,62],[1450,55],[1550,88],[1650,92],[1700,92],[1800,92],[2000,94],[2500,92],[2800,85],[2860,45],[2930,30],[3000,65],[3100,50],[3200,30],[3350,15],[3450,22],[3550,45],[3650,82],[3800,92],[4000,94]]}]},"choices":["Cyclohexanol","Cyclohexanone","Cyclohexene","Hexanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cyclohexanol","cyclohexanone","cpt-pe6-cyclohexene","cpt-pe6-hexanoic-acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone's C=O makes a deep, sharp dip near 1700 cm⁻¹; this trace stays above 90% there."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An alkene has no O–H, so it can't make the broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylic acid has a C=O too, so it would dip hard near 1700 cm⁻¹; the trace stays flat there."}],"rule":"Check two IR windows: a broad deep dip near 3300 cm⁻¹ means O–H; a sharp strong dip near 1700 means C=O. Keep the compound matching both.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"IR pair to memorize: broad ~3300 = O–H, sharp ~1700 = C=O. Broad O–H and no 1700 dip → alcohol, so cyclohexanol (acid would also show C=O)."},"exp":"The trace has a **broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹ (O–H)** and **nothing near 1700 cm⁻¹ (no C=O)**, plus C–H dips just under 3000 and a C–O dip near 1070. An O–H with no C=O is an alcohol: cyclohexanol. A carboxylic acid would also show a strong C=O near 1710.","how":{"ask":"Is there a broad dip between 3200 and 3600 cm⁻¹, and is there a sharp dip near 1700 cm⁻¹?","know":"• IR windows: **O–H** broad, 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; **N–H** 3300–3500, narrower; **C–H** 2850–3100; **C=O** sharp and strong, ~1700; **C=C** weaker, ~1650.\n• Carboxylic acid = C=O plus a very broad O–H that spreads down toward 2500.\n• Alcohol = O–H with no C=O; ketone = C=O with no O–H.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the clue: the ketone was reduced with NaBH₄ and quenched with acid. Reduction of C=O should leave an O–H, so look for a new O–H band and a missing C=O band.","hl":"reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol"},{"text":"Check the O–H window first: a broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unknown","x":3350}],"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3600,"text":"O–H window"}]},"calc":["3350 cm⁻¹: about **15 %** transmittance → strong, broad O–H"]},{"text":"Now the C=O window: the trace stays near the top at 1700 cm⁻¹, so there is no carbonyl. That removes the ketone and the acid.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unknown","x":1700}],"shade":[{"x1":1650,"x2":1780,"text":"C=O window"}]},"calc":["1700 cm⁻¹: about **92 %** → no C=O"],"strike":["Cyclohexanone","Hexanoic acid"]},{"text":"An alkene has no O–H; its weak C=C band would sit near 1650 and could not make the deep dip at 3350.","strike":["Cyclohexene"]},{"text":"Also: the other windows. N–H (3300–3500) needs nitrogen, which no choice has; the C–H dips at 2850–2930 appear for all four compounds, so they never decide."},{"text":"Answer: cyclohexanol.","fig":"cyclohexanol"}],"trap":"Seeing a broad O–H and picking the acid: an acid always shows a strong C=O near 1700 too, and this spectrum has none."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"reduced with NaBH₄","note":"C=O → CH–OH expected"},{"t":"six-carbon liquid","note":"all choices are C6"},{"t":"IR spectrum","note":"which bonds are present"}]},{"text":"**Read dips, not peaks.** Low transmittance means a bond absorbed that light."},{"text":"**Know five windows.**","calc":["O–H: 3200–3600, broad","N–H: 3300–3500","C–H: 2850–3100","C=O: ~1700, sharp","C=C: ~1650, weak"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** check 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ for a broad O–H and ~1700 for a sharp C=O: O–H alone = alcohol, C=O alone = ketone or aldehyde, both (with a very broad O–H) = carboxylic acid."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-01b","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:1#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:1"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"Based on the IR spectra, which compound distilled out of the H₃PO₄ step?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"IR spectra of the starting alcohol (Before) and the distillate (After)","x":{"label":"Wavenumber (cm⁻¹)","min":500,"max":4000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Transmittance (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Before","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[500,86],[600,82],[800,78],[1000,60],[1100,30],[1200,70],[1380,58],[1460,55],[1600,90],[1720,90],[1800,92],[2000,94],[2500,92],[2800,85],[2880,45],[2970,30],[3050,60],[3150,45],[3250,25],[3350,15],[3450,22],[3550,48],[3650,85],[4000,94]]},{"name":"After","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[500,88],[600,85],[800,80],[1000,82],[1170,75],[1300,80],[1370,60],[1460,58],[1600,90],[1650,68],[1720,90],[1780,88],[2000,94],[2500,93],[2800,88],[2880,50],[2980,35],[3025,62],[3150,90],[3300,93],[3600,94],[4000,95]]}]},"choices":["Cyclohexene","Cyclohexanol","Cyclohexanone","Hexanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-cyclohexene","cyclohexanol","cyclohexanone","cpt-pe6-hexanoic-acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips the change: the starting alcohol’s broad dip near 3350 cm⁻¹ is gone from the After trace."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone shows a deep, sharp C=O dip near 1715 cm⁻¹; the After trace stays near the top there."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An acid would show both a very broad O–H and a strong C=O near 1710 cm⁻¹; the After trace has neither."}],"rule":"Name an IR product by the bands it lost and gained: no broad 3200–3600 dip means no O–H; no sharp ~1700 dip means no C=O.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two windows: broad ~3300 = O–H, sharp ~1700 = C=O. The After trace lacks both, so the product has no O at all: the alkene from losing water."},"exp":"Hot H₃PO₄ removes water from an alcohol: **C–OH plus a neighbor H becomes C=C**. The After trace has **lost the broad O–H dip near 3350 cm⁻¹** and shows **no C=O dip near 1700 cm⁻¹**, so the distillate is neither the alcohol, the ketone nor an acid. Only cyclohexene, with C–H dips near 2950 and a weak C=C near 1650, fits.","how":{"ask":"Which bands are missing from the After trace, and which choice lacks the groups that make them?","know":"• IR windows: **O–H** broad, 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; **C=O** sharp and strong, ~1700; **C=C** weak, ~1650; **C–H** 2850–3100.\n• A carboxylic acid shows C=O plus a very broad O–H; an alcohol shows O–H only; a ketone shows C=O only.\n• A product that gains an –OH shows a new broad band above 3000; one that loses it shows that band vanish.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the Route A liquid, an alcohol by its broad O–H band (Before trace), was warmed with H₃PO₄ and the product distilled out. Hot strong acid pulls water out of an alcohol: the –OH and an H on the next carbon leave, and a C=C forms.","hl":"warmed with H₃PO₄","fig":["cyclohexanol","cpt-pe6-cyclohexene"]},{"text":"Compare at 3350 cm⁻¹: a broad deep dip Before, flat After. The distillate has no O–H, so it is not the starting alcohol.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Before","x":3350},{"series":"After","x":3350}],"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3600,"text":"O–H window"}]},"calc":["Before at 3350: ~15 % → **O–H**","After at 3350: ~93 % → no O–H"],"strike":["Cyclohexanol"]},{"text":"Compare at 1720 cm⁻¹: flat in both traces. No C=O formed, which rules out a ketone and an acid.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Before","x":1720},{"series":"After","x":1720}]},"calc":["After at 1720: ~90 % → no C=O"],"strike":["Cyclohexanone","Hexanoic acid"]},{"text":"Also: the C–H dips near 2950 stay in both traces, so they never decide; a weak dip near 1650 fits a C=C."},{"text":"Answer: cyclohexene.","fig":"cpt-pe6-cyclohexene"}],"trap":"Picking the unchanged alcohol because the C–H dips look the same: the decisive change is the broad O–H dip near 3350 that vanished."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"warmed with H₃PO₄","note":"strong acid, heat: loses water"},{"t":"volatile material that distilled out","note":"the product leaves as it forms"},{"t":"compared with it by IR","note":"compare the two traces"}]},{"text":"**Map each choice to its bands.**","calc":["alcohol: broad O–H only","ketone: sharp C=O only","acid: C=O + very broad O–H","alkene: neither (weak C=C ~1650)"]},{"text":"**Read the After trace in two windows.** No dip at 3200–3600 and none at ~1700 leaves only the compound with no O–H and no C=O."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to name a product from IR, check the broad O–H window (3200–3600 cm⁻¹) and the sharp C=O window (~1700): the band that appears or vanishes tells you which group was gained or lost."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-02a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:2#1","pe6-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:2"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"Which method would best measure the R : S ratio of the Route B alcohol?","choices":["Polarimetry","NMR integration","Infrared spectrum","Mass spectrometry"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mirror-image isomers give identical ¹H NMR spectra in an ordinary solvent, so their peaks overlap and cannot be split into a ratio."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"IR shows which functional groups are present; both mirror images have the same groups and the same bands."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both mirror images have the same mass and break apart the same way, so their mass spectra match."}],"rule":"To measure an isomer ratio, pick the method whose signal differs between the isomers: NMR areas for constitutional isomers, optical rotation for mirror images.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mirror images differ only in how they rotate plane-polarized light. Observed rotation ÷ pure rotation gives the excess, and from it the ratio."},"exp":"The two products are **enantiomers**: same groups, same connectivity, same mass. NMR, IR and MS all read them the same. Only a chiral measurement tells them apart: **polarimetry**. The observed rotation divided by the pure R rotation gives the enantiomeric excess, and from that the R : S ratio. (For constitutional isomers, ¹H NMR integration would be the tool, since their peaks differ.)","how":{"ask":"What kind of isomers are these, and which measurement gives a different signal for each of them?","know":"• **Constitutional isomers** sharing functional groups: IR looks alike, but ¹H NMR peaks differ, and each integral ∝ number of H, so integrals give the ratio.\n• **Enantiomers**: identical NMR, IR and MS in an achiral setting; they differ only in the sign of optical rotation.\n• % ee = observed rotation ÷ rotation of the pure enantiomer × 100.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says every molecule has the same groups and the same connectivity, and the two forms are mirror images: the two products are enantiomers.","hl":"mirror images"},{"text":"IR only identifies functional groups, which both share.","strike":["Infrared spectrum"]},{"text":"NMR integration works for constitutional isomers, whose peaks differ, but mirror images give the same peaks; their masses also match.","strike":["NMR integration","Mass spectrometry"]},{"text":"The passage gives the rotation of the pure R form; the measured rotation of the mixture gives the excess of one form.","hl":"rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle","calc":["ee = observed ÷ pure","e.g. ee 60% → 80 : 20"]},{"text":"Answer: polarimetry."}],"trap":"Reaching for NMR integration because it measures ratios of constitutional isomers: enantiomers give one identical spectrum."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"same functional groups","note":"IR can’t separate them"},{"t":"same connectivity","note":"not constitutional isomers"},{"t":"mirror images","note":"enantiomers"}]},{"text":"**Match the method to the difference.**","calc":["different groups → IR","different H environments → ¹H NMR areas","mirror images → optical rotation"]},{"text":"**Integration counts H.** For two constitutional isomers, divide each unique peak’s area by its H count to get the mole ratio; enantiomers never give two separate peaks to compare."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to measure how much of each isomer is present, choose the technique whose signal differs between them: ¹H NMR integrals for constitutional isomers, polarimetry for enantiomers; IR only names functional groups."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:3#1","pe6-cp:3#2","pe6-cp:3#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:3"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"What compound does the Route B Grignard step produce, after the acid is added?","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane","choices":["2-Methylpentan-3-ol","3-Methylpentan-3-ol","2-Methylpentan-3-one","2-Methylpropanoic acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops halfway: a Grignard adds to an aldehyde and acid workup leaves an alcohol, not a new ketone."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That comes from butan-2-one, the methyl-shift ketone; the passage says a hydride shifted and an aldehyde formed."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"No oxidant is present: a Grignard adds carbon to C=O, it never makes a carboxylic acid."}],"rule":"Epoxide + Lewis acid: open to the most stable cation, shift H or alkyl to make C=O. Then RMgBr adds R to the carbonyl carbon; acid gives the alcohol.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tertiary cation on C2, H shifts from C1 → (CH₃)₂CH–CHO. EtMgBr adds Et to the CHO carbon → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–Et, a secondary alcohol."},"exp":"BF₃ binds the epoxide O and the ring opens toward the **tertiary cation on C2**. A **1,2-hydride shift** from C1 moves the + to C1, where O forms C=O: **2-methylpropanal, (CH₃)₂CH–CHO**. In the Grignard step the ethyl Grignard adds to that carbonyl carbon, and aqueous acid protonates the alkoxide: **(CH₃)₂CHCH(OH)CH₂CH₃**, a secondary alcohol.","how":{"ask":"Which carbonyl compound does the BF₃ step make, and what does an ethyl Grignard plus acid do to it?","know":"• A Lewis acid (BF₃) takes a lone pair of the epoxide O; the ring opens to the **more stable carbocation** (3° > 2° > 1°).\n• A **1,2-hydride or alkyl shift** from the C–O carbon then builds C=O there: an H left on it → aldehyde; only carbons → ketone.\n• RMgBr + aldehyde → (acid workup) secondary alcohol; RMgBr + ketone → tertiary alcohol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the start and the conditions: 2,2-dimethyloxirane with BF₃, a Lewis acid, opening toward the more stable cation. That cation is the tertiary one on C2 (two methyls), not the primary CH₂.","hl":"opened toward the more stable carbocation","fig":"cpt-pe6-oxirane-cation","calc":["C2⁺: 3 carbon neighbors → 3°","C1⁺: 1 carbon neighbor → 1°"]},{"text":"An H shifts from C1 to C2, and the O on C1 forms C=O. C1 keeps one H, so it is an aldehyde, as the passage says. A methyl shift would instead give butan-2-one.","hl":"1,2-hydride shift","calc":["(CH₃)₂C⁺–CH₂–O–BF₃⁻ → (CH₃)₂CH–CHO"],"strike":["3-Methylpentan-3-ol"]},{"text":"Grignard step: the ethyl group attacks the C=O carbon; acid then protonates the O⁻. The product is an alcohol, not a ketone, and nothing oxidizes it.","hl":"ethylmagnesium bromide","calc":["(CH₃)₂CH–CHO + CH₃CH₂MgBr → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(O⁻)–CH₂CH₃","H₃O⁺ → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃"],"strike":["2-Methylpentan-3-one","2-Methylpropanoic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-methylpentan-3-ol, (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃, a secondary alcohol."}],"trap":"Opening the epoxide at the less hindered CH₂ as in a basic opening, or shifting a methyl instead of the hydride the passage names: either leads to the wrong carbonyl and the wrong alcohol."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"BF₃","note":"Lewis acid"},{"t":"2,2-dimethyloxirane","note":"C2 has two CH₃"},{"t":"aldehyde C–H stretch","note":"the first step gives an aldehyde"},{"t":"aqueous acid was added last","note":"O⁻ → OH"}]},{"text":"**Lewis acid on O, open toward the stable cation.** BF₃ accepts a lone pair from the epoxide O, and the C–O bond that breaks leaves the tertiary cation.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane"},{"text":"**Pick the intermediate from the product.** The shift comes from the C–O carbon: if it keeps an H the carbonyl is an aldehyde, if only carbons remain it is a ketone.","calc":["C2⁺ then H shift from C1 → aldehyde"]},{"text":"**Grignard step.** R⁻ adds to the carbonyl carbon; the acid workup gives OH.","calc":["aldehyde → 2° alcohol","ketone → 3° alcohol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with a Lewis acid, an epoxide opens to its most stable carbocation and a 1,2-shift builds the C=O; then a Grignard adds its R group to that carbonyl carbon, and acid workup gives the alcohol."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-04a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:4#1","pe6-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:4"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"In the natural floral alcohol, which marked carbon is a chirality center?","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The CH₂OH carbon has two H atoms: two identical groups rule it out."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A CH₃ carbon carries three identical hydrogens, so it can never be a chirality center."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Z is a CH₂ in the chain; its two hydrogens are identical groups, so it fails the four-groups test."}],"rule":"A chirality center is an sp³ carbon with four different groups: any CH₂ or CH₃ (repeated H) is out at a glance.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stereocenter needs four different groups, so skip every CH₂ (two H) and CH₃ (three H). Only the CH branch carbon, X (H, CH₃, propyl, CH₂CH₂OH), is left."},"exp":"To be a **chirality center**, a tetrahedral (sp³) carbon must hold **four different groups**. **X (C3)** carries H, CH₃, CH₂CH₂OH and CH₂CH₂CH₃. W and Z are CH₂ carbons (two H each) and Y is a CH₃. With exactly one such center and no mirror plane, the molecule is **chiral**.","how":{"ask":"Check each marked carbon for four different attached groups.","know":"• **Chirality center**: sp³ carbon with **four different groups**.\n• Instant rejects: **CH₃, CH₂** (repeated H), and any **C=C, C=O or aromatic** carbon.\n• One chirality center and no internal mirror plane → the **molecule is chiral**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 names the compound, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, and says it exists as one enantiomer, so it must hold a chirality center. List the groups on each marked carbon of the drawn structure.","hl":"occurs as one enantiomer only","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"Reject carbons with repeated hydrogens.","calc":["W: CH₂OH → H, H ✗","Y: CH₃ → H, H, H ✗","Z: CH₂ → H, H ✗"]},{"text":"W, Y and Z each carry at least two hydrogens.","strike":["W","Y","Z"]},{"text":"Check the remaining carbon.","calc":["X: H, CH₃, CH₂CH₂OH, CH₂CH₂CH₃ → all different ✓"]},{"text":"Answer: X."}],"trap":"Picking the carbon that holds the OH: being next to a heteroatom doesn't help if it still has two H atoms."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3-methylhexan-1-ol","note":"six-carbon chain, CH₃ on C3"},{"t":"one enantiomer only","note":"the molecule is chiral"},{"t":"chirality center","note":"four different groups"}]},{"text":"**The four-groups test.** An sp³ carbon is a chirality center only if all four substituents differ; two hydrogens on one carbon fail at once.","calc":["CH₃, CH₂: never","CH with three different other groups: yes"]},{"text":"**Compare the chains, not just the first atom.** On C3, both CH₂ neighbors start alike, but one leads to OH and the other to a CH₃ end, so they differ.","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"**Also:** a molecule with exactly one chirality center has no internal mirror plane, so it is chiral: its mirror image cannot be superimposed and the two are enantiomers."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only a tetrahedral carbon holding four different groups can be a chirality center (skip every CH₂, CH₃ and double-bonded carbon); one such center and no mirror plane makes the molecule chiral."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-05a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:5#1","pe6-cp:5#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:5"],"passage":"In a forensic DNA lab, a restriction digest and a size ladder were loaded into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel, and the field pulled the DNA through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. Digest fragment P traveled 2.9 cm and fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, and the ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their lengths. The running buffer is normally slightly basic, but one bottle that had stood open for weeks absorbed CO₂ and read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where pure water is neutral. After each run, a camera sled on a straight, level track photographs the gel. A motor starts the sled from rest, holds it at a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a halt over the gel, while a sensor logs its position. The gel is then fixed in an acidic bath buffered at pH 3.4, made by mixing a weak acid HA with its sodium salt NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, 30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration.","q":"In which time window does no net force act on the camera sled?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Sled position over time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":1,"max":10,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":21,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"Sled","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1,0],[1.5,0.13],[2,0.5],[2.5,1.13],[3,2],[3.5,3.13],[4,4.5],[5,7.5],[6,10.5],[7,13.5],[8,16.5],[8.5,17.81],[9,18.75],[9.5,19.31],[10,19.5]]}]},"choices":["1 to 4 s","4 to 8 s","8 to 10 s","1 to 10 s"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"The whole trip includes the speed-up and the braking, where the velocity changes, so a net force acts for part of it."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Here the curve gets steeper: the sled speeds up from rest, so a net force points along its motion."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Here the curve flattens out: the sled slows to a stop, so a net force points against its motion."}],"rule":"Zero net force means constant velocity: on a position–time graph, pick the straight sloped stretch; any bending stretch means acceleration.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"F_net = 0 ⇔ a = 0 ⇔ constant v ⇔ straight x–t line. Skip the curving start and stop; the straight middle stretch is the answer."},"exp":"On a position–time graph the **slope is the velocity**. A net force changes the velocity, so it shows as a **curve**. Between 1 and 4 s the slope grows as the sled speeds up, and between 8 and 10 s it shrinks as the sled brakes. From **4 to 8 s** the line is straight: 3 m every second, a constant 3 m/s, so **a = 0 and F_net = 0**.","how":{"ask":"Where is the position graph a straight sloped line, so the velocity is not changing?","know":"• Slope of an x–t graph = **velocity**; slope of a v–t graph = **acceleration**.\n• Newton’s 2nd law: F_net = ma, so F_net = 0 exactly when v is constant.\n• Straight x–t → constant v; steepening curve → speeding up; flattening curve → slowing.\n• Area under a v–t graph = **displacement**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 describes three stages: speed up from rest, steady cruising speed, slow to a stop. No net force means no acceleration, so look for the steady stage: the stretch of the graph whose slope stays the same.","hl":"holds it at a steady cruising speed"},{"text":"From 1 to 4 s the gains grow each half-second: the sled speeds up from rest. Force along the motion.","calc":["1 → 2 s: +0.5 m","3 → 4 s: +2.5 m"],"strike":["1 to 4 s"]},{"text":"From 4 to 8 s the sled gains 3 m every second: a straight line.","calc":["slope = (16.5 − 4.5) m ÷ (8 − 4) s = **3 m/s**, constant","a = 0 → F_net = 0"]},{"text":"From 8 to 10 s the gains shrink to zero: braking, force against the motion. So the whole trip also fails.","calc":["8 → 9 s: +2.25 m","9 → 10 s: +0.75 m"],"strike":["8 to 10 s","1 to 10 s"]},{"text":"Also: the slopes give the v–t graph, and its area returns the distance on the x–t graph.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"The same sled: velocity versus time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":1,"max":10,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Velocity (m/s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Sled","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[1,0],[4,3],[8,3],[10,0]]}]},"calc":["1–4 s: triangle ½ × 3 s × 3 m/s = 4.5 m","4–8 s: rectangle 3 m/s × 4 s = 12 m","8–10 s: triangle ½ × 2 s × 3 m/s = 3 m","total **19.5 m**, the final position"]},{"text":"Answer: 4 to 8 s."}],"trap":"Thinking the net force is largest where the sled moves fastest: a steady velocity, however large, needs no net force; only a change in velocity does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"starts the sled from rest","note":"it must speed up first"},{"t":"steady cruising speed","note":"constant v: zero net force"},{"t":"slows it to a halt","note":"slowing: force against v"},{"t":"no net force","note":"a = 0"}]},{"text":"**Slope of x–t = velocity.** Steep = fast, flat = stopped, a straight line = steady speed."},{"text":"**Slope of v–t = acceleration; area under v–t = displacement.** By F_net = ma, a straight x–t stretch (flat v–t) has zero net force.","calc":["speeding up: F along v","slowing: F against v","steady v: F_net = 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on x–t, slope = velocity, so a straight sloped stretch means constant v and zero net force; on v–t, slope = acceleration and area = displacement."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P2"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-06a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:6#1","pe6-cp:6#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:6"],"passage":"In a forensic DNA lab, a restriction digest and a size ladder were loaded into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel, and the field pulled the DNA through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. Digest fragment P traveled 2.9 cm and fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, and the ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their lengths. The running buffer is normally slightly basic, but one bottle that had stood open for weeks absorbed CO₂ and read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where pure water is neutral. After each run, a camera sled on a straight, level track photographs the gel. A motor starts the sled from rest, holds it at a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a halt over the gel, while a sensor logs its position. The gel is then fixed in an acidic bath buffered at pH 3.4, made by mixing a weak acid HA with its sodium salt NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, 30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration.","q":"What is the approximate length of fragment P?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"DNA ladder: distance traveled versus length","x":{"label":"Fragment length (bp)","min":0,"max":9000,"step":1000},"y":{"label":"Distance from well (cm)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Ladder","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[250,6.4],[500,5.4],[1000,4.4],[2000,3.4],[4000,2.4],[8000,1.4]]}]},"choices":["About 290 bp","About 1200 bp","About 2800 bp","About 6000 bp"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"copied","why":"That turns the 2.9 cm distance into a size; a fragment that short would run past the 250 bp band, near the bottom."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Anything under 2000 bp runs farther than 3.4 cm, but fragment P stopped short of that band."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A fragment over 4000 bp would stop before 2.4 cm, yet fragment P ran past that band."}],"rule":"Short DNA runs farther: find the two ladder bands that bracket the distance and pick a size between their sizes.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Bracket it: 2.9 cm lies between the 2000 bp (3.4 cm) and 4000 bp (2.4 cm) bands, so the size is between them; halfway on a log scale is about 2800."},"exp":"DNA is negative (one phosphate per nucleotide), so it runs from the − wells toward the + electrode, and **shorter fragments move farther** through the gel mesh. 2.9 cm lies **between the 2000 bp band (3.4 cm) and the 4000 bp band (2.4 cm)**, halfway in distance. Distance tracks log(size), so halfway between 2000 and 4000 is 2000 × √2 ≈ **2800 bp**.","how":{"ask":"Which two ladder bands bracket 2.9 cm, and where between them does fragment P fall?","know":"• DNA’s phosphate backbone is negative, so it moves toward the + electrode (the anode).\n• In a gel, shorter fragments move farther; a ladder gives known sizes at known distances.\n• Distance falls about linearly with log(size): here each doubling costs 1.0 cm.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the clue: fragment P traveled 2.9 cm. Find 2.9 cm on the distance axis of the ladder graph.","hl":"fragment P traveled 2.9 cm"},{"text":"Bracket it with ladder bands.","calc":["2000 bp → 3.4 cm (ran farther)","4000 bp → 2.4 cm (ran less far)","2.9 cm lies between → **2000–4000 bp**"],"strike":["About 1200 bp","About 6000 bp"]},{"text":"Place it: 2.9 is halfway between 3.4 and 2.4, so it is half a doubling above 2000.","calc":["2000 × 2^0.5 ≈ 2000 × 1.41 ≈ **2800 bp**"]},{"text":"290 bp reuses the distance digits as a size; a fragment that short would run past the 250 bp band.","strike":["About 290 bp"]},{"text":"Answer: about 2800 bp."}],"trap":"Thinking longer DNA runs farther, which flips the bracket: the gel mesh holds long fragments back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"negative electrode","note":"wells at −; DNA heads to +"},{"t":"phosphate backbone carries negative charge","note":"why DNA moves in a field"},{"t":"fragment P traveled 2.9 cm","note":"read across the ladder curve"}]},{"text":"**Why DNA moves.** One negative phosphate per nucleotide gives DNA a net − charge, so the field pulls it toward the + electrode, the anode."},{"text":"**Short runs far.** The gel is a mesh: small fragments slip through, long ones are held back. Bracket the unknown between two ladder bands, then place it on the curve."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** DNA is negative and runs toward +; shorter DNA migrates farther, so size an unknown by the two ladder bands whose distances bracket it, reading the curve, not a straight line."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P2"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-06b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:6#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:6"],"passage":"In a forensic DNA lab, a restriction digest and a size ladder were loaded into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel, and the field pulled the DNA through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. Digest fragment P traveled 2.9 cm and fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, and the ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their lengths. The running buffer is normally slightly basic, but one bottle that had stood open for weeks absorbed CO₂ and read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where pure water is neutral. After each run, a camera sled on a straight, level track photographs the gel. A motor starts the sled from rest, holds it at a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a halt over the gel, while a sensor logs its position. The gel is then fixed in an acidic bath buffered at pH 3.4, made by mixing a weak acid HA with its sodium salt NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, 30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration.","q":"What is the approximate length of fragment Q?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"DNA ladder: distance traveled versus length","x":{"label":"Fragment length (bp)","min":0,"max":9000,"step":1000},"y":{"label":"Distance from well (cm)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Ladder","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[250,6.4],[500,5.4],[1000,4.4],[2000,3.4],[4000,2.4],[8000,1.4]]}]},"choices":["About 350 bp","About 700 bp","About 1400 bp","About 4900 bp"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Read backwards: Q ran past the 1000 bp band (4.4 cm), and farther means shorter, so Q is under 1000 bp."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the 4.9 cm distance as a size; a fragment that long would stop short of the 4000 bp band at 2.4 cm."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A 350 bp fragment would run past the 500 bp band, beyond 5.4 cm, but Q stopped short of that band."}],"rule":"Small fragments run farther: find the two ladder bands whose distances bracket the unknown and pick a size between their sizes.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Bracket it: 4.9 cm sits between the 1000 bp (4.4 cm) and 500 bp (5.4 cm) bands, so the size is between 500 and 1000. Only one choice fits."},"exp":"DNA is negative, so it runs from the − wells toward +, and **shorter fragments slip through the gel mesh faster and travel farther**. 4.9 cm lies **between the 1000 bp band (4.4 cm) and the 500 bp band (5.4 cm)**, halfway in distance. Distance tracks log(size), so halfway between 500 and 1000 is 500 × √2 ≈ **700 bp**.","how":{"ask":"Which two ladder bands bracket 4.9 cm, and where between them does fragment Q fall?","know":"• DNA is negative, so it runs toward the + electrode.\n• In a gel, shorter fragments move farther; the ladder gives known sizes at known distances.\n• Distance falls roughly with log(size): on this gel each doubling of size costs 1.0 cm.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the clue: fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, farther than P. Farther means shorter, so Q is the smaller fragment. Find 4.9 cm on the distance axis.","hl":"fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm"},{"text":"Bracket it with ladder bands.","calc":["1000 bp → 4.4 cm (ran less far)","500 bp → 5.4 cm (ran farther)","4.9 cm lies between → **500–1000 bp**"]},{"text":"Place it: 4.9 is halfway between 4.4 and 5.4, so it is half a doubling above 500.","calc":["500 × 2^0.5 ≈ 500 × 1.41 ≈ **700 bp**"]},{"text":"A 350 bp fragment would run past the 500 bp band (beyond 5.4 cm), and a 1400 bp one would stop short of the 1000 bp band (before 4.4 cm).","strike":["About 350 bp","About 1400 bp"]},{"text":"4900 bp copies the distance number as a size; a band that long would sit near 2.2 cm.","strike":["About 4900 bp"]},{"text":"Answer: about 700 bp."}],"trap":"Thinking longer DNA runs farther, which flips the bracket: the gel mesh holds long fragments back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm","note":"read across the ladder curve"},{"t":"ladder bands’ distances","note":"known sizes at known distances"},{"t":"negative electrode","note":"DNA starts at − and runs to +"}]},{"text":"**Short runs far.** The gel is a mesh: small fragments slip through, large ones are held back, so Q (4.9 cm) is shorter than P (2.9 cm)."},{"text":"**Bracket, then place.** Find the ladder bands just above and below the unknown’s distance; the size lies between theirs, placed on a log scale."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** shorter DNA migrates farther; size an unknown by the two ladder bands whose distances bracket it, reading the curve, not a straight line."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P2"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-07a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:7#1","pe6-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:7"],"passage":"In a forensic DNA lab, a restriction digest and a size ladder were loaded into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel, and the field pulled the DNA through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. Digest fragment P traveled 2.9 cm and fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, and the ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their lengths. The running buffer is normally slightly basic, but one bottle that had stood open for weeks absorbed CO₂ and read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where pure water is neutral. After each run, a camera sled on a straight, level track photographs the gel. A motor starts the sled from rest, holds it at a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a halt over the gel, while a sensor logs its position. The gel is then fixed in an acidic bath buffered at pH 3.4, made by mixing a weak acid HA with its sodium salt NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, 30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration.","q":"Relative to pure water, the buffer in the open bottle has:","choices":["[H⁺] up 4-fold; [OH⁻] down 4-fold.","[H⁺] down 4-fold; [OH⁻] up 4-fold.","[H⁺] up 4-fold; [OH⁻] fixed.","[H⁺] up 1.1-fold; [OH⁻] down 1.1-fold."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: a pH below 7 means more H⁺ than pure water, not less."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips Kw: [H⁺][OH⁻] stays 10⁻¹⁴, so when H⁺ rises, OH⁻ must fall by the same factor."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That divides the two pH values; pH is a log scale, so the gap must be undone with 10^Δ."}],"rule":"Each pH unit is a factor of 10 in [H⁺]; a gap of Δ units is 10^Δ. Below 7, H⁺ rises and OH⁻ falls by the same factor (Kw).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Log anchors: 10^0.3 ≈ 2, 10^0.6 ≈ 4. pH 6.4 is 0.6 below 7, so H⁺ is about 4× higher and OH⁻ 4× lower."},"exp":"Pure water at room temperature has **[H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻⁷ M**. pH 6.4 is **0.6 units below 7**, so [H⁺] = 10^0.6 ≈ **4 times** higher (≈ 4 × 10⁻⁷ M): weakly acidic. Because **[H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴**, [OH⁻] falls by the same factor to ≈ 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ M, **4 times lower**.","how":{"ask":"How far is the buffer’s pH from 7, and what factor does that gap make for each ion?","know":"• At room temperature, pure water: **[H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻⁷ M, pH 7**.\n• pH just below 7 → weakly acidic; just above → weakly basic.\n• A gap of Δ pH units is a factor of **10^Δ** in [H⁺]; Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ moves [OH⁻] the opposite way by the same factor.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the open bottle read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where water is neutral: below 7, so it has more H⁺ than water (the absorbed CO₂ made it acidic).","hl":"read pH 6.4 at room temperature","strike":["[H⁺] down 4-fold; [OH⁻] up 4-fold."]},{"text":"Turn the gap into a factor with 10^Δ, not by dividing the pH values.","calc":["Δ = 7.0 − 6.4 = 0.6","10^0.6 ≈ 4"],"strike":["[H⁺] up 1.1-fold; [OH⁻] down 1.1-fold."]},{"text":"Apply Kw: the two ions move in opposite directions by the same factor.","calc":["[H⁺] ≈ 4 × 10⁻⁷ M","[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ (4 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ M"],"strike":["[H⁺] up 4-fold; [OH⁻] fixed."]},{"text":"Answer: H⁺ up 4-fold and OH⁻ down 4-fold."}],"trap":"Reading a 0.6 pH drop as a small change: pH is a log scale, so 0.6 units is a factor of about 4."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"read pH 6.4","note":"just below neutral"},{"t":"absorbed CO₂","note":"CO₂ + water → carbonic acid"},{"t":"pure water is neutral","note":"pH 7 reference, Kw = 10⁻¹⁴"}]},{"text":"**Neutral is the reference.** Below 7, [H⁺] exceeds [OH⁻]; above 7, the reverse. Close to 7 means weakly acidic or weakly basic."},{"text":"**Log factors to remember.**","calc":["10^0.3 ≈ 2","10^0.5 ≈ 3","10^0.6 ≈ 4","10^0.7 ≈ 5","10^1 = 10"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare a solution with water by the pH gap Δ from 7: [H⁺] changes by 10^Δ, and [OH⁻] changes by the same factor the other way."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P2"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-08a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:8#2","pe6-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:8"],"passage":"In a forensic DNA lab, a restriction digest and a size ladder were loaded into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel, and the field pulled the DNA through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. Digest fragment P traveled 2.9 cm and fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, and the ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their lengths. The running buffer is normally slightly basic, but one bottle that had stood open for weeks absorbed CO₂ and read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where pure water is neutral. After each run, a camera sled on a straight, level track photographs the gel. A motor starts the sled from rest, holds it at a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a halt over the gel, while a sensor logs its position. The gel is then fixed in an acidic bath buffered at pH 3.4, made by mixing a weak acid HA with its sodium salt NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, 30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration.","q":"What [A⁻]/[HA] ratio does the fixing bath need?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30 mL of weak acid HA with NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"HA + NaOH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2.26],[3,2.75],[6,3.1],[9,3.33],[12,3.52],[15,3.7],[18,3.88],[21,4.07],[24,4.3],[27,4.65],[29,5.16],[30,8.29],[31,11.39],[33,11.85],[36,12.13],[40,12.33]]}]},"choices":["2.0","0.30","1.0","0.50"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"pH 3.4 is below the pKa, so acid must outnumber base: the ratio has to be under 1; 2.0 flips it."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"0.30 is the pKa − pH gap used as the ratio itself; the gap is a log, so undo it with 10^x."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A 1:1 mix sits exactly at the pKa, the half-equivalence pH (3.7 at 15 mL), not at 3.4."}],"rule":"pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]); read the pKa at half-equivalence. pH below pKa → ratio under 1; 0.3 log units is a factor of 2.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Half-equivalence (15 mL) pH = pKa ≈ 3.7. Target is 0.3 lower, and 0.3 log units = factor 2, so base/acid = ½ = 0.50."},"exp":"Equivalence is at **30 mL** (the steep jump), so half-equivalence is **15 mL**, where pH = **pKa ≈ 3.7**. Henderson–Hasselbalch: 3.4 − 3.7 = −0.3 is the log of the base:acid ratio, so **[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50**.","how":{"ask":"Read the pKa at half-equivalence, then raise 10 to (pH − pKa).","know":"• **pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])**, so **[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa)**.\n• At the **half-equivalence volume**, [A⁻] = [HA] and pH = pKa.\n• Equivalence = the midpoint of the steep jump.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the titration: 30 mL of HA with NaOH of equal concentration, so equivalence comes at 30 mL. Find the steep jump, then halve that volume.","hl":"30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration","calc":["equivalence ≈ 30 mL → half-equivalence = 15 mL"]},{"text":"Read the pH there: that is the pKa.","calc":["pH at 15 mL ≈ 3.7 = pKa"]},{"text":"Apply the ratio form of Henderson–Hasselbalch.","hl":"pH 3.4","calc":["pH − pKa = 3.4 − 3.7 = −0.3","ratio = 10^(−0.3) ≈ **0.50**"]},{"text":"2.0 inverts the ratio, 1.0 is the buffer at its pKa, and 0.30 uses the exponent as the ratio.","strike":["2.0","1.0","0.30"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.50."}],"trap":"Reading the pKa at the equivalence point (pH ≈ 8.3) instead of at half the equivalence volume."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"30 mL","note":"acid volume; equal molarity → equivalence at 30 mL"},{"t":"pH 3.4","note":"target buffer pH"},{"t":"[A⁻]/[HA]","note":"base over acid"}]},{"text":"**pKa from the curve.** Halfway to equivalence, half the acid has become A⁻, so the log term is zero and pH = pKa.","calc":["V(½ eq) = 15 mL → pKa ≈ 3.7"]},{"text":"**Ratio form.** Solve Henderson–Hasselbalch for the ratio.","calc":["[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa) = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50"]},{"text":"**Also:** a pH exactly 1 unit below the pKa gives a base:acid ratio of 0.1, and a pH equal to the pKa gives 1; each unit is a factor of 10."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** base:acid = 10^(pH − pKa), with the pKa read at the half-equivalence volume."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P2"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:9#1","pe6-cp:9#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:9"],"q":"Supercooled water at −10 °C freezes spontaneously once seeded, and its surroundings warm. Which set of signs describes this freezing?","choices":["ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG negative","ΔH negative, ΔS positive, ΔG negative","ΔH positive, ΔS negative, ΔG negative","ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG positive"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign flipped: liquid → crystal is more ordered, so ΔS is negative, not positive."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"ΔH > 0 means heat is absorbed; here the surroundings warm, so the water releases heat and ΔH is negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A process that happens on its own has ΔG < 0 by definition; a negative ΔS does not forbid it."}],"rule":"Read each sign from the story: heat given off → ΔH < 0, heat taken in → ΔH > 0; more order → ΔS < 0, less → ΔS > 0; happens on its own → ΔG < 0.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read signs from the stem: spontaneous → ΔG < 0; surroundings warm → heat released, ΔH < 0; liquid → solid is more ordered, ΔS < 0. All three negative."},"exp":"Spontaneous means **ΔG < 0**, the only requirement. The surroundings warm, so heat leaves the water: **ΔH < 0**. Freezing orders the molecules: **ΔS < 0**. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS stays negative because |ΔH| > T|ΔS| at this low temperature: an enthalpy-driven process.","how":{"ask":"Read each sign from an observation: spontaneity, heat flow and order.","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0, the only sign that is required.\n• Surroundings warm → the system releases heat → ΔH < 0; surroundings cool → ΔH > 0.\n• More order (gas → liquid → solid, fewer particles) → ΔS < 0.","steps":[{"text":"Spontaneous: ΔG is negative.","hl":"freezes spontaneously","strike":["ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG positive"]},{"text":"The surroundings warm, so heat flows out of the water: exothermic.","hl":"its surroundings warm","strike":["ΔH positive, ΔS negative, ΔG negative"]},{"text":"Liquid → crystal is more ordered, so ΔS is negative.","strike":["ΔH negative, ΔS positive, ΔG negative"]},{"text":"Check: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is negative only if the enthalpy term wins.","calc":["ΔH < 0, and −TΔS > 0 (it opposes)","ΔG < 0 → |ΔH| > T|ΔS| (enthalpy-driven)"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG negative."}],"trap":"Believing entropy must rise for anything spontaneous; only the total entropy (system + surroundings) must rise, and the released heat raises the surroundings' entropy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"freezes spontaneously","note":"ΔG < 0"},{"t":"its surroundings warm","note":"heat released: ΔH < 0"},{"t":"Supercooled water","note":"liquid → solid: ΔS < 0"}]},{"text":"**Only ΔG is required.** Spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0; nothing forces ΔH or ΔS to a particular sign."},{"text":"**Also: ΔH and ΔS can each have either sign**, as long as ΔG = ΔH − TΔS comes out negative:","calc":["ΔH −, ΔS + → spontaneous at every T","ΔH −, ΔS − → spontaneous at low T (enthalpy-driven)","ΔH +, ΔS + → spontaneous at high T (entropy-driven)","ΔH +, ΔS − → never spontaneous"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** spontaneity needs only ΔG < 0; read ΔH from heat flow and ΔS from order, then check that ΔH − TΔS is negative."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-10a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:10#1","pe6-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:10"],"q":"A protein kinase is given ATP whose terminal phosphate carries ³²P, plus four peptides that differ only in the side chain shown. Which peptide becomes radioactive?","choices":["Tyrosine","Phenylalanine","Leucine","Cysteine"],"choiceFigs":["tyrosine-sc","phenylalanine-sc","leucine-sc","cysteine-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Same aromatic ring but no –OH on it: a kinase needs a side-chain oxygen to attach the phosphate to."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An all-carbon side chain has no hydroxyl group, so there is nothing for the phosphate to bond to."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An –SH is not what Ser/Thr/Tyr kinases modify; they form a phosphate ester on an –OH oxygen."}],"rule":"Kinases attach phosphate to an –OH: only Ser, Thr and Tyr side chains qualify; a ring or chain without an OH cannot be phosphorylated.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphorylation = a phosphate ester on a side-chain OH. Scan the four drawings for –OH; only the phenol ring carries one."},"exp":"A kinase moves the terminal (γ) phosphate of ATP onto a **side-chain –OH**, forming a phosphate ester; here the ³²P label rides along. Of the four side chains, only **tyrosine** has an –OH (on its phenol ring). Phenylalanine has the same ring without the OH, leucine is all carbon and hydrogen, and cysteine has an –SH, which these kinases do not phosphorylate.","how":{"ask":"Which drawn side chain has an –OH for the kinase to put the labeled phosphate on?","know":"• Protein kinases transfer ATP’s γ-phosphate to an –OH of **Ser, Thr or Tyr**.\n• Phe = Tyr without the OH; Val, Leu, Ile, Ala, Met have no OH.\n• The radioactive label goes wherever the γ-phosphate goes.","steps":[{"text":"The label sits on the phosphate the kinase transfers, so the peptide that gets phosphorylated becomes radioactive.","hl":"terminal phosphate carries ³²P"},{"text":"Check each side chain for an –OH.","calc":["phenol ring with OH → yes","ring with no OH → no","isobutyl, all C–H → no","CH₂–SH → no"]},{"text":"Phenylalanine and leucine have no oxygen at all, and cysteine’s sulfur is not a kinase target.","strike":["Phenylalanine","Leucine","Cysteine"],"fig":"phenylalanine-sc"},{"text":"Answer: tyrosine.","fig":"tyrosine-sc"}],"trap":"Picking phenylalanine because it looks like tyrosine: without the –OH there is no oxygen for the phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"protein kinase","note":"adds phosphate to –OH"},{"t":"terminal phosphate carries ³²P","note":"label follows the phosphate"},{"t":"differ only in the side chain","note":"compare side chains only"}]},{"text":"**What a kinase needs.** An –OH oxygen attacks the γ-phosphorus of ATP; the product is a phosphate ester (phospho-Ser, -Thr or -Tyr)."},{"text":"**Spot the OH.**","calc":["Ser: CH₂–OH","Thr: CH(OH)–CH₃","Tyr: phenol OH","Phe, Leu, Val, Ala, Met: none"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only side chains with an –OH (Ser, Thr, Tyr) can be phosphorylated by a protein kinase; Phe is Tyr without the OH and cannot."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-11a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:11#1","pe6-cp:11#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:11"],"q":"The resistance R of a copper wire was measured for several lengths L, as plotted. Which relationship between R and L do the data support?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Resistance of a copper wire versus its length","x":{"label":"Length L (m)","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Resistance R (Ω)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Wire","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.6,1.5],[1.2,3],[1.8,4.5],[2.4,6]]}]},"choices":["R ∝ L","R ∝ L squared","R ∝ square root of L","R ∝ inverse of L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"An inverse law means a longer wire reads lower R; here every longer wire reads higher."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"With R ∝ L², doubling L (0.6 → 1.2 m) would make R 4 times larger; it only doubles (1.5 → 3.0 Ω)."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A square-root law would raise R only about 1.4× when L doubles; the data double it."}],"rule":"Doubling test: double x and see what y does: ×2 linear, ×4 square, ×1.4 square root, ×½ inverse.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Double L, R doubles (1.5 → 3.0); ×4 L gives ×4 R. Same factor in and out means direct proportion: R ∝ L."},"exp":"Doubling L from 0.6 to 1.2 m doubles R from 1.5 to 3.0 Ω, and quadrupling L (to 2.4 m) quadruples R (to 6.0 Ω). The ratio R/L stays **2.5 Ω/m**, and the points fall on a straight line through the origin: **R ∝ L**.","how":{"ask":"Divide each R by its L: is that ratio constant, or is R/L² constant instead?","know":"• y ∝ x: y/x is constant and the graph is a straight line through the origin; doubling x doubles y.\n• y ∝ x²: y/x² is constant; doubling x multiplies y by 4.\n• y ∝ √x: doubling x multiplies y by about 1.4. y ∝ 1/x: y × x is constant, and y falls as x grows.","steps":[{"text":"Test the ratios instead of eyeballing the line.","hl":"Which relationship between R and L","calc":["R/L: 1.5 ÷ 0.6 = **2.5**; 3.0 ÷ 1.2 = **2.5**","4.5 ÷ 1.8 = **2.5**; 6.0 ÷ 2.4 = **2.5** Ω/m"]},{"text":"R/L² changes from point to point, so the square law fails.","calc":["1.5 ÷ 0.6² = 1.5 ÷ 0.36 ≈ 4.2","6.0 ÷ 2.4² = 6.0 ÷ 5.76 ≈ 1.0"],"strike":["R ∝ L squared"]},{"text":"Doubling test: L × 2 (0.6 → 1.2 m) gives R × 2 (1.5 → 3.0 Ω), which is linear. A square-root law would give only × 1.4, and an inverse law would halve R.","calc":["√L: 1.5 × 1.41 ≈ 2.1 Ω (measured 3.0)","1/L: 1.5 ÷ 2 = 0.75 Ω (measured 3.0)"],"strike":["R ∝ square root of L","R ∝ inverse of L"]},{"text":"Answer: R ∝ L."}],"trap":"Calling any rising graph \"proportional\": a square law also rises, so test a ratio. Here only R/L stays fixed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"resistance R","note":"y axis: ohms"},{"t":"several lengths L","note":"x axis: meters"},{"t":"relationship between R and L","note":"which power law?"}]},{"text":"**Two ratio tests.** Divide y by x and by x²; whichever ratio stays fixed names the law.","calc":["y/x fixed → y ∝ x","y/x² fixed → y ∝ x²"]},{"text":"**Doubling shortcut.** Double x and watch y: × 2 → linear, × 4 → square, × 1.4 → square root, × ½ → inverse."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** test y/x and y/x² across the data (or double x): a constant y/x, or y doubling with x, means y ∝ x; a constant y/x², or y × 4, means y ∝ x²."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-12a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:12#1","pe6-cp:12#2","pe6-cp:12#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:12"],"q":"A chemist draws trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane, shown, in its lower-energy chair. Which substituent occupies an axial position in that chair?","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx","choices":["Only the methyl","Only the tert-butyl","Both substituents","Neither substituent"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Trans at C1 and C3 puts the groups on opposite faces, which on a chair is always one axial plus one equatorial; both axial can't happen."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Trans at C1 and C3 means opposite faces, and on a chair that is always one axial plus one equatorial bond; no diequatorial chair exists."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"tert-Butyl is the bulkiest common group: axial, it hits the two axial H's on its face (1,3-diaxial strain), so the favored chair keeps it equatorial."}],"rule":"Cis/trans fixes how many groups must be axial; when one must be, the favored chair puts the bulkier group equatorial.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Trans-1,3 on a chair = one up-axial, one equatorial. tert-Butyl always takes equatorial (it is huge), so the methyl is the axial one."},"exp":"In a trans-1,3 ring the groups sit on opposite faces, and a chair then gives C1 and C3 **one axial and one equatorial** group in either conformation. The ring flips so the bulky **tert-butyl is equatorial**, which leaves the **methyl axial**.","how":{"ask":"Can this substitution pattern put both groups equatorial? If not, which group takes the axial spot?","know":"• Around a chair the axial bonds alternate up, down, up, so C1 and C3 point their axial bonds to the same face.\n• **Diequatorial is possible only for cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4**; cis-1,2, cis-1,4 and trans-1,3 always keep one group axial.\n• When one group must be axial, the favored chair gives that spot to the **smaller** group (tert-butyl ≫ isopropyl > ethyl ≈ methyl).","steps":[{"text":"Read the pattern: trans means opposite faces, and the groups sit on C1 and C3.","hl":"trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx"},{"text":"At C1 and C3 same-face bonds are the same type, so opposite faces force one axial and one equatorial group in both chairs. Both axial and both equatorial are impossible.","calc":["trans-1,3 → one axial + one equatorial","both axial ✗; both equatorial ✗"],"strike":["Both substituents","Neither substituent"]},{"text":"Compare the two chairs by the cost of the axial group: tert-butyl axial is far worse than methyl axial.","calc":["CH₃ axial: ≈ 7 kJ/mol of strain","tert-butyl axial: > 20 kJ/mol","favored chair: tert-butyl equatorial"],"strike":["Only the tert-butyl"]},{"text":"Answer: only the methyl is axial."}],"trap":"Assuming the favored chair always has every group equatorial; that is true only for cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4 patterns."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","note":"opposite faces, C1 and C3"},{"t":"lower-energy chair","note":"best placement of groups"},{"t":"axial position","note":"straight up or down"}]},{"text":"**Big groups go equatorial.** An axial group bumps the two axial H atoms on its own face; the bigger the group, the bigger the penalty, so tert-butyl is effectively locked equatorial.","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx"},{"text":"**Check the pattern.** Axial bonds alternate up and down around the ring, so C1 and C3 share a direction.","calc":["same face at C1, C3 → same type (cis-1,3)","opposite faces at C1, C3 → one of each (trans-1,3)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the patterns that can be diequatorial are cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4; for those, the favored chair puts both groups equatorial."},{"text":"**Also:** in every other pattern (cis-1,2, cis-1,4, trans-1,3) one group must be axial, and the favored chair hands that spot to the smaller group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** place the biggest group equatorial first, then ask whether the pattern lets the second group be equatorial too; if not, the smaller group is axial."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-13a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:13#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:13"],"passage":"A clinical lab assays a bacterial phosphatase with p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV; the enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases yellow p-nitrophenolate. For a reusable sensor, the enzyme is fixed to an aldehyde-coated bead through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links formed with its lysines. Drug D was tested by measuring initial rates over many pNPP concentrations, with these fitted constants: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. Compound Q was tested the same way, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it, and those results are plotted in double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form. The phosphatase is also a regulatory enzyme: ATP binds separate regulatory subunits and shifts it toward its low-activity T state. In a last set of runs, the pNPP and ATP levels were varied while all other conditions stayed the same.","q":"Which labeled atom of pNPP has trigonal planar geometry?","fig":"pnpp-sites","choices":["Atom I","Atom II","Atom III","Atom IV"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A terminal O⁻ is bonded to one atom only, so it has no bond angle at all, let alone a planar set of three."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphorus here is bonded to four oxygens with no lone pair: four domains, tetrahedral, about 109.5°."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The bridging O has two bonds and two lone pairs: four domains, so it is bent, near 105°."}],"rule":"Count electron domains: an atom in a double or aromatic bond with three domains is sp², trigonal planar, ~120°; four domains means sp³, ~109.5°.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ring or double-bond carbon ⇒ sp² ⇒ planar, ~120°. P with four O and an O with two lone pairs are both four-domain atoms."},"exp":"Trigonal planar means **three electron domains: sp², about 120°**. Atom IV is a **benzene-ring carbon**, bonded to three atoms with no lone pair: sp² and planar. Atom II, phosphorus, has four bonded O (tetrahedral, sp³); atom III, the bridging O, has two bonds and two lone pairs (bent, sp³); atom I is a terminal O⁻ with only one bond. The same three-domain count makes the **C=N carbon of a Schiff base** sp², about 120°.","how":{"ask":"Which labeled atom has exactly three electron domains and no lone pair?","know":"• Domains = bonded atoms + lone pairs (a double bond counts once).\n• 4 domains → sp³, ~109.5° (bent or pyramidal with lone pairs); 3 → sp², 120°; 2 → sp, 180°.\n• Aromatic ring carbons and every C=O, C=C or C=N carbon are sp² and planar; a **Schiff base** is an imine, C=N.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the beads hold the enzyme through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links. The C=N carbon has three electron domains: sp², trigonal planar, about 120°. Look for a labeled atom with the same three-domain count.","hl":"imine (C=N)"},{"text":"Count domains on each labeled atom.","fig":"pnpp-sites","calc":["I: terminal O⁻, one bond → no angle","II: P with four O → 4 → tetrahedral","III: O with two bonds + two lone pairs → 4 → bent","IV: ring carbon, three bonds → 3 → **sp²**"]},{"text":"Atoms I, II and III are not three-domain centers.","strike":["Atom I","Atom II","Atom III"]},{"text":"Answer: atom IV, the aromatic ring carbon."}],"trap":"Calling the bridging O planar because it has only two atoms attached: its two lone pairs make four domains, so it is bent."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Schiff bases","note":"imines, C=N"},{"t":"trigonal planar","note":"3 domains, sp², 120°"},{"t":"four atoms labeled I to IV","note":"count each one"}]},{"text":"**Domains decide the shape.** Count each bonded atom once (double bonds too) and add lone pairs.","calc":["3 domains → sp², 120°","4 domains → sp³, 109.5°"]},{"text":"**Double and aromatic bonds mark sp² carbons.** Every ring carbon of benzene and every C=O, C=C or C=N carbon is planar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a carbon in a double or aromatic bond (C=O, C=N, C=C, benzene) has three electron domains, so it is sp² and trigonal planar (~120°); four domains, counting lone pairs, means sp³."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P3"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-14a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:14#1","pe6-cp:14#2","pe6-cp:14#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:14"],"passage":"A clinical lab assays a bacterial phosphatase with p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV; the enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases yellow p-nitrophenolate. For a reusable sensor, the enzyme is fixed to an aldehyde-coated bead through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links formed with its lysines. Drug D was tested by measuring initial rates over many pNPP concentrations, with these fitted constants: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. Compound Q was tested the same way, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it, and those results are plotted in double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form. The phosphatase is also a regulatory enzyme: ATP binds separate regulatory subunits and shifts it toward its low-activity T state. In a last set of runs, the pNPP and ATP levels were varied while all other conditions stayed the same.","q":"Compared with the no-drug curve, the curve with drug D has:","choices":["same plateau; higher KM.","same plateau; lower KM.","lower plateau; same KM.","lower plateau; higher KM."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: KM rose with the drug, so the curve reaches half its plateau at a larger [S], not a smaller one."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Swaps the two constants: the table changes KM, the half-plateau [S], and leaves Vmax, the plateau height, alone."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Vmax is identical with and without D, and Vmax alone sets how high the curve levels off."}],"rule":"On rate vs [S], Vmax is the plateau height and KM is the [S] at half that height. Read each constant’s change from the table onto its own feature.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Plateau = Vmax, half-plateau [S] = KM. Vmax same → curves level off together; KM tripled → the drug curve rises more slowly at low [S]."},"exp":"On a Michaelis–Menten plot the **plateau height is Vmax** and the **[S] at half the plateau is KM**. With drug D, Vmax stays 45 μmol/min, so both curves **level off at the same height**; KM triples from 2.0 to 6.0 mM, so the drug curve reaches half-height (22.5 μmol/min) only at **a higher [S]**. That pattern, same Vmax with larger KM, is what a competitive inhibitor gives.","how":{"ask":"Which table entry changed, and which feature of the curve does that entry control?","know":"• **Vmax** sets the height of the plateau.\n• **KM** is the [S] at which v = Vmax/2; a larger KM shifts the half-plateau point right.\n• Same KM → the half-max points coincide even if the plateaus differ; same Vmax → same plateau even if KM differs.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the table: with D, Vmax is still 45 μmol/min but KM rose from 2.0 to 6.0 mM. Vmax is the same in both rows, so both curves level off at the same height.","hl":"with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM","strike":["lower plateau; same KM.","lower plateau; higher KM."]},{"text":"KM rises with the drug, so half of that plateau is reached at a larger [S].","calc":["without D: half-height at 2.0 mM","with D: half-height at 6.0 mM"],"strike":["same plateau; lower KM."]},{"text":"Also: if instead Vmax changed and KM stayed fixed, the plateaus would differ while the half-plateau points lined up at one [S]."},{"text":"Answer: same plateau, higher KM."}],"trap":"Thinking any inhibitor lowers the plateau: only a change in Vmax moves the plateau; a KM change moves the half-plateau point."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM","note":"no drug"},{"t":"with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM","note":"only KM changed"},{"t":"initial rates over many pNPP concentrations","note":"rate versus [S] curve"}]},{"text":"**Two features, two constants.**","calc":["plateau height = Vmax","[S] at half-plateau = KM"]},{"text":"**Read the table row by row.** Same Vmax → same plateau. Bigger KM → the curve climbs more slowly and reaches half-height further right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Vmax sets how high a rate-versus-[S] curve levels off, and KM is the [S] where it reaches half that height; a table change in one constant moves only its own feature."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P3"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-15a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:15#1","pe6-cp:15#2","pe6-cp:15#3","pe6-cp:15#4"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:15"],"passage":"A clinical lab assays a bacterial phosphatase with p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV; the enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases yellow p-nitrophenolate. For a reusable sensor, the enzyme is fixed to an aldehyde-coated bead through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links formed with its lysines. Drug D was tested by measuring initial rates over many pNPP concentrations, with these fitted constants: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. Compound Q was tested the same way, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it, and those results are plotted in double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form. The phosphatase is also a regulatory enzyme: ATP binds separate regulatory subunits and shifts it toward its low-activity T state. In a last set of runs, the pNPP and ATP levels were varied while all other conditions stayed the same.","q":"What kind of inhibitor is compound Q?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Double-reciprocal plot with and without compound Q","x":{"label":"1/[S] (mM⁻¹)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"1/v (min/μmol)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"No Q","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,2],[1,4],[2,6],[3,8],[4,10]]},{"name":"With Q","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,2],[1,8],[2,14],[3,20],[4,26]]}]},"choices":["Competitive inhibition","Uncompetitive inhibition","Noncompetitive inhibition","Mixed inhibition"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An uncompetitive inhibitor gives parallel lines, shifting both intercepts; these two lines have different slopes."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Pure noncompetitive raises the y-intercept (lower Vmax) at a fixed x-intercept; here the y-intercept does not move."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mixed lines cross away from the y-axis because Vmax and KM both change; these lines meet on the axis."}],"rule":"Lineweaver–Burk: same y-intercept → Vmax unchanged; same x-intercept → KM unchanged; parallel lines → both fall; crossing elsewhere → mixed.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"y-intercept = 1/Vmax. Both lines start at 2 on the y-axis, so Vmax is unchanged and only the slope (KM/Vmax) grew: KM up, Vmax same."},"exp":"On a double-reciprocal plot the **y-intercept is 1/Vmax** and the **x-intercept is −1/KM**. Both lines meet the y-axis at 2 min/μmol, so **Vmax is unchanged** (0.5 μmol/min). The line with Q is three times steeper, so its x-intercept moves toward zero: **KM is tripled**. KM up with Vmax unchanged is **competitive inhibition**: Q competes with substrate, and enough substrate overcomes it.","how":{"ask":"Do the lines share a y-intercept, an x-intercept, a slope, or none of these?","know":"• y-intercept = 1/Vmax; x-intercept = −1/KM; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Competitive: KM up, Vmax same → lines meet on the y-axis.\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax down, KM same → meet on the x-axis.\n• Uncompetitive: both down by the same factor → parallel lines.\n• Mixed: Vmax down and KM changed → lines cross off both axes.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says Q was tested once without Q and once with a fixed amount, and the data are a double-reciprocal plot. On that plot the y-intercept is 1/Vmax and the x-intercept is −1/KM, so compare the two lines’ intercepts.","hl":"double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form"},{"text":"Read the y-intercepts: both lines start at 2.","calc":["1/Vmax = 2 min/μmol in both → Vmax same"],"strike":["Noncompetitive inhibition","Mixed inhibition"]},{"text":"Compare slopes: the Q line is steeper, so the lines are not parallel.","calc":["no Q: slope 2","with Q: slope 6 → KM ×3"],"strike":["Uncompetitive inhibition"]},{"text":"Same Vmax and higher KM means the inhibitor competes with substrate for the active site."},{"text":"Answer: competitive inhibition."}],"trap":"Reading the steeper line alone as \"less activity, so Vmax fell\": check the y-intercept, which holds steady."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it","note":"compare two lines"},{"t":"double-reciprocal","note":"1/v vs 1/[S]"}]},{"text":"**What each part of the line means.**","calc":["y-intercept = 1/Vmax","x-intercept = −1/KM","slope = KM/Vmax"]},{"text":"**Four patterns.**","calc":["competitive: KM ↑, Vmax same","noncompetitive: Vmax ↓, KM same","uncompetitive: both ↓, parallel","mixed: Vmax ↓, KM changed"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Lineweaver–Burk plot, lines meeting on the y-axis mean competitive, on the x-axis noncompetitive, parallel lines uncompetitive, and crossing elsewhere mixed; the same patterns read straight from a table of KM and Vmax."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P3"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-16a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:16"],"passage":"A clinical lab assays a bacterial phosphatase with p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV; the enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases yellow p-nitrophenolate. For a reusable sensor, the enzyme is fixed to an aldehyde-coated bead through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links formed with its lysines. Drug D was tested by measuring initial rates over many pNPP concentrations, with these fitted constants: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. Compound Q was tested the same way, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it, and those results are plotted in double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form. The phosphatase is also a regulatory enzyme: ATP binds separate regulatory subunits and shifts it toward its low-activity T state. In a last set of runs, the pNPP and ATP levels were varied while all other conditions stayed the same.","q":"Under which conditions would the phosphatase release p-nitrophenolate fastest?","choices":["High [pNPP] and high [ATP]","High [pNPP] and low [ATP]","Low [pNPP] and high [ATP]","Low [pNPP] and low [ATP]"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both settings flipped: this starves the enzyme of substrate and loads it with its allosteric inhibitor."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"ATP is an allosteric inhibitor: plenty of it holds the enzyme in the T state even when substrate is abundant."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"With little pNPP the active sites sit mostly empty, so the rate stays low even with no inhibitor."}],"rule":"For the fastest rate: substrate high, allosteric activators high, allosteric inhibitors low. Name each molecule’s role before choosing.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"T state = low activity, so whatever favors T is an inhibitor: keep ATP low. pNPP is the substrate: keep it high."},"exp":"Rate rises with **substrate** until saturation, so pNPP should be **high**. **ATP binds the regulatory subunits and favors the low-activity T state**: it is an **allosteric inhibitor**, so it should be **low**. High [pNPP] with low [ATP] gives the fastest release of p-nitrophenolate. (With an allosteric activator instead, you would want the activator high.)","how":{"ask":"Is each varied molecule a substrate, an activator or an inhibitor, and which level of each speeds the reaction?","know":"• More substrate → faster, up to Vmax.\n• Allosteric activator (favors the R state) → want it high; allosteric inhibitor (favors T) → want it low.\n• An allosteric regulator binds a site other than the active site, often on separate regulatory subunits.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says ATP binds the regulatory subunits and shifts the enzyme toward its low-activity T state: ATP is an allosteric inhibitor, so it must be low.","hl":"shifts it toward its low-activity T state","strike":["High [pNPP] and high [ATP]","Low [pNPP] and high [ATP]"]},{"text":"pNPP is the substrate the enzyme cuts, so more of it fills more active sites and the rate rises toward Vmax.","hl":"p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP)","strike":["Low [pNPP] and low [ATP]"]},{"text":"Also: if the regulator were an activator that favors the R state, the best choice would be high activator plus high substrate."},{"text":"Answer: high [pNPP] and low [ATP]."}],"trap":"Choosing \"high\" for everything: a high level of an allosteric inhibitor slows the enzyme, whatever the substrate level."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"low-activity T state","note":"inhibitor, keep low"},{"t":"separate regulatory subunits","note":"allosteric site, not the active site"},{"t":"pNPP and ATP levels were varied","note":"substrate and regulator"},{"t":"fastest","note":"maximize the rate"}]},{"text":"**Sort each molecule by role.**","calc":["substrate → high","activator → high","inhibitor → low"]},{"text":"**T versus R.** Allosteric enzymes flip between a low-activity T state and a high-activity R state; a molecule that stabilizes T slows the enzyme at any substrate level."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the fastest conditions combine high substrate, high allosteric activator and low allosteric inhibitor; identify each molecule’s role from the passage first."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P3"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-17a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:17#1","pe6-cp:17#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:17"],"passage":"To follow a chlorinated sweetener through the body, a lab made glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol), and tagged a small share of the molecules with carbon-14. Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while ordinary carbon-12, which is stable, does not; both isotopes have six protons. Each decay is registered by a counter whose pulses travel through copper leads to a meter. The meter’s reference electrode connects to the sample cup through an agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The counter sits inside lead shielding that the lab recasts itself. In one batch, a 240 g lead block was heated in a graphite crucible until it reached its melting point but was still entirely solid; for lead, L_fusion = 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 128 J/kg·K.","q":"Inside the KCl–agar gel, the charge is carried by:","choices":["Ions; electrolytic","Electrons; metallic","Electrons; electrolytic","Ions; metallic"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Free electrons carry current in metals such as the copper leads; a salt gel has no sea of mobile electrons."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Electrons cannot drift through a water-based gel; electrolytic conduction never uses free electrons."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Metallic conduction means electrons moving through a metal lattice; agar gel is not a metal."}],"rule":"Name the carrier by the material: metal wires → free electrons (metallic conduction); solutions, gels and salt bridges → dissolved ions (electrolytic).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Metal = electron sea; salt solution = K⁺ and Cl⁻ that move. The gel is a salt solution, so the carriers are charged particles of the salt."},"exp":"The gel is water saturated with **KCl**, so it holds **K⁺ and Cl⁻ ions** free to move: K⁺ drifts one way and Cl⁻ the other. Charge carried by moving ions is **electrolytic conduction**. In the **copper leads**, by contrast, current is carried by **free electrons** drifting through the metal lattice (metallic conduction).","how":{"ask":"Is the gel a metal or a salt solution, and what moves in each kind of conductor?","know":"• **Metallic conduction**: free (delocalized) electrons drift through a metal lattice; wires, leads.\n• **Electrolytic conduction**: dissolved ions migrate; solutions, gels, salt bridges, cell fluids.\n• A circuit can use both: electrons in the wire, ions in the solution.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the reference electrode connects through an agar gel saturated with KCl, so the gel holds dissolved K⁺ and Cl⁻ ions free to move.","hl":"agar gel saturated with KCl"},{"text":"A watery gel is not a metal, so the metallic labels are out.","strike":["Electrons; metallic","Ions; metallic"]},{"text":"Free electrons do not travel through an electrolyte; only ions move there.","strike":["Electrons; electrolytic"]},{"text":"Also: in the copper leads the carriers are free electrons, which is metallic conduction.","hl":"copper leads"},{"text":"Answer: ions, electrolytic conduction."}],"trap":"Assuming electrons carry current everywhere in a circuit: they do in the wires, but in a salt bridge or gel the ions move."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"copper leads","note":"metal: electrons"},{"t":"saturated with KCl","note":"dissolved ions"},{"t":"charge is carried by","note":"name the carrier"}]},{"text":"**Two kinds of conductor.**","calc":["metal → free electrons → metallic","electrolyte → ions → electrolytic"]},{"text":"**One circuit, two carriers.** Electrons leave an electrode through the wire; inside the solution or bridge, ions carry the charge the rest of the way around."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a metal the charge carriers are free electrons (metallic conduction); in a solution, gel or salt bridge they are ions (electrolytic conduction)."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P4"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-18a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:18#1","pe6-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:18"],"passage":"To follow a chlorinated sweetener through the body, a lab made glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol), and tagged a small share of the molecules with carbon-14. Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while ordinary carbon-12, which is stable, does not; both isotopes have six protons. Each decay is registered by a counter whose pulses travel through copper leads to a meter. The meter’s reference electrode connects to the sample cup through an agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The counter sits inside lead shielding that the lab recasts itself. In one batch, a 240 g lead block was heated in a graphite crucible until it reached its melting point but was still entirely solid; for lead, L_fusion = 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 128 J/kg·K.","q":"How much more heat is needed to melt the lead block completely?","choices":["5.5 × 10³ J","2.3 × 10⁴ J","9.6 × 10⁴ J","5.5 × 10⁶ J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Uses the mass in grams with L in J/kg, which inflates the heat a thousandfold; grams must become kilograms first."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides L by the mass instead of multiplying: heat for melting grows with mass, q = mL."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That is L for a whole kilogram, copied from the passage; the block is only a quarter of a kilogram, so the heat is smaller."}],"rule":"Melting at the melting point: q = mL with m in kg; no temperature change, so c is a distractor.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Already at the melting point: ΔT = 0, so the c value is a decoy. q = 0.24 × 2.3 × 10⁴; 0.24 × 2.3 ≈ 0.55, so about 0.55 × 10⁴ J."},"exp":"The lead is already at its melting point, so there is **no temperature change** and the specific heat is not needed. **q = mL** = 0.240 kg × 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg ≈ **5.5 × 10³ J**.","how":{"ask":"Which heat formula applies when a solid changes phase at constant temperature?","know":"• **Phase change:** q = m·L (no ΔT); L_fusion for melting, L_vap for boiling.\n• **Temperature change:** q = m·c·ΔT, only when T changes.\n• Match units: L in J/kg needs m in kg (g ÷ 1000).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the block reached its melting point but was still entirely solid: no ΔT is left, so q = mL only, and the specific heat is a distractor.","hl":"reached its melting point but was still entirely solid"},{"text":"Convert the mass to kilograms, since L is per kilogram.","hl":"240 g","strike":["5.5 × 10⁶ J"],"calc":["240 g ÷ 1000 = 0.240 kg"]},{"text":"Multiply mass by the heat of fusion; neither copying L nor dividing by the mass gives heat.","strike":["2.3 × 10⁴ J","9.6 × 10⁴ J"],"calc":["q = 0.240 kg × 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg","q ≈ 0.55 × 10⁴ J = 5.5 × 10³ J"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.5 × 10³ J."}],"trap":"Adding an m·c·ΔT term; the solid is already at its melting point, so there is no warming step and c goes unused."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"reached its melting point but was still entirely solid","note":"no ΔT"},{"t":"240 g","note":"→ 0.240 kg"},{"t":"2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg","note":"L_fusion"},{"t":"128 J/kg·K","note":"distractor"}]},{"text":"**Pick the formula.** Melting at a fixed temperature uses q = mL; m·c·ΔT is for warming or cooling."},{"text":"**Also: units first.** L is per kilogram, so grams must become kilograms before multiplying.","calc":["240 g = 0.240 kg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heat for a phase change at constant T is q = m·L with m in kg; use m·c·ΔT only when the temperature moves."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P4"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-19a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:19#1","pe6-cp:19#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:19"],"passage":"To follow a chlorinated sweetener through the body, a lab made glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol), and tagged a small share of the molecules with carbon-14. Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while ordinary carbon-12, which is stable, does not; both isotopes have six protons. Each decay is registered by a counter whose pulses travel through copper leads to a meter. The meter’s reference electrode connects to the sample cup through an agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The counter sits inside lead shielding that the lab recasts itself. In one batch, a 240 g lead block was heated in a graphite crucible until it reached its melting point but was still entirely solid; for lead, L_fusion = 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 128 J/kg·K.","q":"Ignoring the carbon-14 tag, what is the sweetener’s approximate molar mass?","choices":["163 g/mol","197.5 g/mol","198.5 g/mol","215.5 g/mol"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That removes the OH (17) and stops; the chlorine atom that takes its place still adds 35.5."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That adds the Cl but never takes the OH away: a swap is one subtraction plus one addition, not just an addition."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That subtracts 18, as if a whole water left; OH weighs 17 (16 + 1), one H less."}],"rule":"A swap is minus the old group, plus the new atom (OH = 17). Round the masses to whole numbers, then subtract and add.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Swap, don't rebuild: 180 − OH (17) + Cl (35.5) = 180 + 18.5 = 198.5 g/mol."},"exp":"Start from glucose, **180 g/mol**. Take off one **OH (16 + 1 = 17)** and put on one **Cl (35.5)**: 180 − 17 + 35.5 = **198.5 g/mol** (formula C₆H₁₁ClO₅: 72 + 11 + 35.5 + 80 = 198.5).","how":{"ask":"Adjust the parent sugar's molar mass for the group that leaves and the atom that enters.","know":"• A substitution changes the molar mass by **(mass added) − (mass removed)**.\n• OH = 16 + 1 = **17 g/mol** (not 18: that is a whole water).\n• Cl ≈ **35.5 g/mol**.\n• Net change for OH → Cl: **+18.5 g/mol**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the parent sugar and the swap: glucose, 180 g/mol, with one hydroxyl replaced by chlorine. Start from the parent molecule’s molar mass.","hl":"C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol","calc":["parent = 180 g/mol"]},{"text":"Subtract the hydroxyl group that leaves.","hl":"one hydroxyl group replaced","calc":["OH = 16 + 1 = 17","180 − 17 = 163 g/mol"]},{"text":"Add the chlorine atom that takes its place.","hl":"chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol)","calc":["163 + 35.5 = **198.5 g/mol**"]},{"text":"163 stops after the subtraction, and 215.5 adds the chlorine without removing the OH.","strike":["163 g/mol","215.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"197.5 subtracts 18, as if a whole water left, but only the OH (17) is replaced.","strike":["197.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 198.5 g/mol."}],"trap":"Subtracting 18 for the leaving group (197.5 g/mol): in a direct swap only OH (17) leaves, not H₂O."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"180 g/mol","note":"parent sugar"},{"t":"one hydroxyl group","note":"leaves: −17"},{"t":"chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol)","note":"enters: +35.5"}]},{"text":"**Start from the parent.** The new molecule is glucose with one change, so its molar mass is glucose's plus a correction.","calc":["M(new) = M(parent) − M(removed) + M(added)"]},{"text":"**Weigh the swapped pieces.** OH is one O (16) and one H (1); Cl is 35.5.","calc":["−17 + 35.5 = +18.5","180 + 18.5 = 198.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same method works for any swap. Glucose with one OH replaced by ¹⁸F (mass 18) changes by −17 + 18 = +1."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a substituted molecule, molar mass = parent − group removed + group added; OH (17) → Cl (35.5) is a net +18.5."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P4"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-20a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:20#2","pe6-cp:20#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:20"],"passage":"To follow a chlorinated sweetener through the body, a lab made glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol), and tagged a small share of the molecules with carbon-14. Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while ordinary carbon-12, which is stable, does not; both isotopes have six protons. Each decay is registered by a counter whose pulses travel through copper leads to a meter. The meter’s reference electrode connects to the sample cup through an agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The counter sits inside lead shielding that the lab recasts itself. In one batch, a 240 g lead block was heated in a graphite crucible until it reached its melting point but was still entirely solid; for lead, L_fusion = 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 128 J/kg·K.","q":"How does carbon-14’s n : p ratio compare with carbon-12’s, and what happens in its nucleus as it decays (n = neutron, p = proton)?","choices":["Higher; n → p","Higher; p → n","Lower; n → p","Lower; p → n"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: β⁻ emission ejects an electron, which turns a neutron into a proton, not the reverse."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio flipped: carbon-14 has eight neutrons for six protons, more than carbon-12’s six for six."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That pair describes proton-rich positron emitters such as carbon-11; β⁻ emitters carry extra neutrons."}],"rule":"Same Z, different A: extra neutrons → n:p too high → β⁻ (n → p); too few neutrons → n:p too low → β⁺ or electron capture (p → n).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Isotopes share Z; only N differs. Heavier isotope → more neutrons → n:p up. β⁻ fixes a neutron excess by converting one neutron into a proton."},"exp":"Isotopes of carbon all have **6 protons**; carbon-14 has **8 neutrons** and carbon-12 has 6, so carbon-14’s **n : p ratio is higher**. It sheds the excess by **β⁻ decay**: a **neutron becomes a proton** plus an emitted electron (and an antineutrino), giving nitrogen-14 (7 p, 7 n). A proton-poor-in-neutrons isotope does the reverse: carbon-11 (6 p, 5 n) decays by positron emission, turning a proton into a neutron and becoming boron-11.","how":{"ask":"How many neutrons does each isotope have, and which particle change does β⁻ emission cause?","know":"• Isotopes: same Z (protons), different N (neutrons); N = A − Z.\n• **β⁻ decay**: n → p + e⁻; Z up 1, A same; fixes too many neutrons.\n• **β⁺ (positron) decay** or electron capture: p → n; Z down 1, A same; fixes too few neutrons.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says both isotopes have six protons, so they differ only in neutrons. Count neutrons for each.","hl":"six protons","calc":["carbon-12: 12 − 6 = 6 n → n:p = 1.0","carbon-14: 14 − 6 = 8 n → n:p ≈ 1.33"],"strike":["Lower; n → p","Lower; p → n"]},{"text":"β⁻ emission ejects an electron from the nucleus: a neutron becomes a proton, so Z rises to 7 (nitrogen).","hl":"beta-minus emission","calc":["¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + e⁻"],"strike":["Higher; p → n"]},{"text":"Also: positron emission is the opposite fix, for neutron-poor nuclei: carbon-11 (5 n) turns a proton into a neutron and becomes boron-11."},{"text":"Answer: higher n:p, and a neutron becomes a proton."}],"trap":"Mixing up β⁻ and β⁺: a negative electron leaving means the nucleus gained positive charge, so a neutron became a proton."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Carbon-14 decays","note":"6 p, 8 n"},{"t":"carbon-12, which is stable","note":"6 p, 6 n"},{"t":"beta-minus emission","note":"n → p"}]},{"text":"**Isotopes differ only in neutrons.** Same Z, so the heavier isotope has the higher n : p ratio."},{"text":"**Decay fixes the ratio.**","calc":["too many n → β⁻: n → p","too few n → β⁺ or capture: p → n"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare isotopes by neutron count (N = A − Z); a neutron-rich nucleus decays by β⁻ (n → p, Z up 1), and a neutron-poor one by positron emission or capture (p → n, Z down 1)."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P4"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-21a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:21#1","pe6-cp:21#2","pe6-cp:21#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:21"],"passage":"A heat-stable enzyme from a hot-spring microbe binds the cofactor flavin mononucleotide (FMN), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV, so tightly that only denaturing the protein releases it. In the binding pocket, an arginine side chain points an N–H toward the flavin ring and forms a hydrogen bond to it. The fold is held together partly by a salt bridge between the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻, and mutating either residue lowers the enzyme’s melting temperature. The enzyme is switched on when an aspartate side chain, modeled by a small compound with four sites labeled I to IV, attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair, forming an acyl phosphate. After water later removes the phosphoryl group, that same atom takes a proton from a nearby water molecule. In the catalytic step, a glutamic acid residue that stays protonated in a nonpolar pocket hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group as a C–O bond breaks. It is then reprotonated by water from the solvent, not by the substrate.","q":"Which two amino acids form the stabilizing salt bridge?","choices":["Lysine and glutamate","Arginine and glutamate","Lysine and aspartate","Histidine and glutamine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Arginine ends in a guanidinium, –NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂, after three CH₂; a plain –NH₃⁺ after four CH₂ is a different residue."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Aspartate's side chain is –CH₂–COO⁻, one CH₂; the stem's carboxylate sits after two."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine's side chain is an imidazole ring and glutamine ends in an amide (–CONH₂); neither ends in NH₃⁺ or COO⁻."}],"rule":"Count the CH₂ groups and read the end group: Lys –(CH₂)₄NH₃⁺, Arg guanidinium, Asp –CH₂COO⁻, Glu –(CH₂)₂COO⁻.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count CH₂ links: 4 then NH₃⁺ = lysine; 2 then COO⁻ = glutamate (aspartate has 1, arginine ends in guanidinium). Lys + Glu."},"exp":"**Four CH₂ then –NH₃⁺** is lysine (arginine ends in a guanidinium after three CH₂). **Two CH₂ then –COO⁻** is glutamate (aspartate has one CH₂; glutamine ends in –CONH₂). Lys⁺ and Glu⁻ form the salt bridge.","how":{"ask":"Name each side chain by its end group, then count its CH₂ groups.","know":"• **Lys**: –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺. **Arg**: –(CH₂)₃–NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂ (guanidinium).\n• **Asp**: –CH₂–COO⁻. **Glu**: –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻.\n• **Asn**: –CH₂–CONH₂. **Gln**: –(CH₂)₂–CONH₂.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the first side chain, –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺: four CH₂ and a plain ammonium end. Arginine would end in a guanidinium; histidine is a ring.","hl":"–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺","calc":["(CH₂)₄ + NH₃⁺ → **lysine**","arginine: (CH₂)₃ + guanidinium","histidine: imidazole ring"],"strike":["Arginine and glutamate","Histidine and glutamine"]},{"text":"Second side chain: two CH₂ and a carboxylate. Aspartate has only one CH₂.","hl":"–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻","calc":["(CH₂)₂ + COO⁻ → **glutamate**","aspartate: one CH₂","glutamine: –CONH₂, no charge"],"strike":["Lysine and aspartate"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and glutamate."}],"trap":"Aspartate and glutamate share the carboxylate end; only the CH₂ count tells them apart."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺","note":"side chain 1"},{"t":"–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻","note":"side chain 2"},{"t":"salt bridge","note":"+ meets −"}]},{"text":"**Name the end group first.** A lone –NH₃⁺ is lysine's; a carbon bonded to three N with a + charge is arginine's guanidinium.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc"],"calc":["–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ → Lys","–(CH₂)₃–NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂ → Arg"]},{"text":"**Then count the CH₂ groups** to tell close relatives apart.","calc":["–CH₂–COO⁻ → Asp","–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻ → Glu"]},{"text":"**Also:** side-chain amides: –CH₂–C(=O)NH₂ is asparagine, and one more CH₂ makes glutamine. Neither is charged."},{"text":"**Also:** side-chain carboxylates: aspartate (one CH₂) and glutamate (two CH₂) are the negative residues at pH 7."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read the end group (ammonium, guanidinium, amide, carboxylate), then count CH₂ groups: Lys 4, Arg 3, Asp and Asn 1, Glu and Gln 2."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P5"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-22a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:22#1","pe6-cp:22#2","pe6-cp:22#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:22"],"passage":"A heat-stable enzyme from a hot-spring microbe binds the cofactor flavin mononucleotide (FMN), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV, so tightly that only denaturing the protein releases it. In the binding pocket, an arginine side chain points an N–H toward the flavin ring and forms a hydrogen bond to it. The fold is held together partly by a salt bridge between the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻, and mutating either residue lowers the enzyme’s melting temperature. The enzyme is switched on when an aspartate side chain, modeled by a small compound with four sites labeled I to IV, attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair, forming an acyl phosphate. After water later removes the phosphoryl group, that same atom takes a proton from a nearby water molecule. In the catalytic step, a glutamic acid residue that stays protonated in a nonpolar pocket hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group as a C–O bond breaks. It is then reprotonated by water from the solvent, not by the substrate.","q":"Which labeled site of the aspartate model performs both steps?","fig":"asp-ca-sites","choices":["Site I","Site II","Site III","Site IV"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A carbonyl carbon is electron-poor: it is attacked by nucleophiles rather than attacking, and it has no lone pair for H⁺."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide N’s lone pair is tied up in resonance with C=O, so it is a poor nucleophile and a poor base."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An sp³ C–H has no lone pair: it can neither attack phosphorus nor accept a proton."}],"rule":"Attacking with a lone pair (nucleophile) and later accepting H⁺ (general base) both need a free lone pair: look for O⁻ or a free N, never a C–H or amide N–H.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Both jobs use one lone pair. The only labeled atom with a negative charge and free lone pairs is the carboxylate oxygen."},"exp":"Attacking phosphorus with a lone pair makes the atom a **nucleophile**; taking a proton later makes it a **general base**. Both need an **available lone pair**. Site I is the **carboxylate O⁻**: negatively charged with three lone pairs. Site II is an amide N–H (lone pair delocalized), site III a C–H (no lone pair), and site IV the carbonyl carbon, an electrophile.","how":{"ask":"Which labeled atom has a free lone pair to attack phosphorus and later to take a proton?","know":"• **Nucleophile**: donates a lone pair to an electron-poor atom (P, C=O carbon).\n• **General base**: removes a proton; **general acid**: donates one.\n• Good lone-pair donors: O⁻ (carboxylate, alkoxide), neutral amine or imidazole N. Poor: amide N, C–H.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the aspartate attacks phosphorus with a lone pair (a nucleophile), and the next sentence says the same atom later takes a proton (a general base). Both steps need a free lone pair.","hl":"attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair"},{"text":"Check each site.","fig":"asp-ca-sites","calc":["I: carboxylate O⁻, three lone pairs → ✓","II: amide N–H, lone pair in resonance → poor","III: C–H, no lone pair","IV: C=O carbon, electron-poor"]},{"text":"The carbonyl carbon is the electrophile in such reactions, the opposite role.","strike":["Site IV"]},{"text":"The amide N and the C–H have no usable lone pair.","strike":["Site II","Site III"]},{"text":"Answer: site I, the carboxylate oxygen: nucleophile first, general base later."}],"trap":"Picking the carbonyl carbon because the reaction \"happens at the carboxyl group\": the carbon is attacked; the oxygen does the attacking."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"with a lone pair","note":"nucleophile"},{"t":"takes a proton","note":"general base"},{"t":"that same atom","note":"one site, both roles"}]},{"text":"**Three roles to name.**","calc":["gives a lone pair to an electrophile → nucleophile","removes H⁺ → general base","donates H⁺ → general acid"]},{"text":"**Who has a free lone pair?** O⁻ and neutral amine N do; amide N shares its pair with C=O; carbons in C–H or C=O have none to give."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an atom that attacks with a lone pair is a nucleophile, one that takes a proton is a general base, and one that gives a proton is a general acid; both of the first two need a free lone pair, as on O⁻ or a neutral N."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P5"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-23a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:23#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:23"],"passage":"A heat-stable enzyme from a hot-spring microbe binds the cofactor flavin mononucleotide (FMN), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV, so tightly that only denaturing the protein releases it. In the binding pocket, an arginine side chain points an N–H toward the flavin ring and forms a hydrogen bond to it. The fold is held together partly by a salt bridge between the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻, and mutating either residue lowers the enzyme’s melting temperature. The enzyme is switched on when an aspartate side chain, modeled by a small compound with four sites labeled I to IV, attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair, forming an acyl phosphate. After water later removes the phosphoryl group, that same atom takes a proton from a nearby water molecule. In the catalytic step, a glutamic acid residue that stays protonated in a nonpolar pocket hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group as a C–O bond breaks. It is then reprotonated by water from the solvent, not by the substrate.","q":"Which labeled FMN atom could take the arginine’s hydrogen bond yet has no H to give?","fig":"fmn-sites","choices":["Atom I","Atom II","Atom III","Atom IV"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"That ring N carries an H, so it can donate; its lone pair is spread into the two flanking C=O groups."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"This N bonds to three carbons and its lone pair is part of the ring’s π system, so it is a poor acceptor."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphorus here is bonded to four oxygens and has no lone pair, so it can neither accept nor donate."}],"rule":"H-bond acceptor = N, O or F with an available lone pair; donor = an H on N, O or F. Acceptor-only sites are lone-pair atoms with no H.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Acceptor-only = lone pair, no H. Skip any N–H or O–H (donors) and any atom whose lone pair is busy; the ring N with a free in-plane pair is left."},"exp":"To **accept** a hydrogen bond an atom must be N, O or F **with an available lone pair**; to **donate** it needs an **H bonded to N, O or F**. Atom II is a ring nitrogen with **no H** and a lone pair pointing out in the ring plane: an acceptor only. Atom I is an N–H (a donor), atom III is a ring N whose lone pair belongs to the π system, and atom IV is a phosphorus with no lone pair. The same logic makes the C=O and carboxylate oxygens of a ligand such as pyruvate acceptors only.","how":{"ask":"Which labeled atom has a free lone pair but no attached H?","know":"• **Donor**: H on N, O or F. **Acceptor**: an N, O or F atom bearing a lone pair.\n• C=O oxygens, carboxylate O⁻, ether O and pyridine-like ring N (no H) are acceptors only.\n• An N whose lone pair is delocalized (amide N, pyrrole-like N) is a poor acceptor.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the arginine points an N–H toward the flavin: the arginine supplies the H, so the flavin atom must accept. It needs a lone pair, and the question says it has no H.","hl":"points an N–H toward the flavin ring"},{"text":"Check each labeled atom.","fig":"fmn-sites","calc":["I: N–H → donor","II: ring N, no H, free lone pair → acceptor only ✓","III: N bonded to three C, pair in the π system","IV: P, no lone pair"]},{"text":"Atom I can donate, and atoms III and IV have no usable lone pair.","strike":["Atom I","Atom III","Atom IV"]},{"text":"Answer: atom II."}],"trap":"Picking the N–H because it \"looks like\" the binding site: an N–H is a donor, so it fails the \"no H to give\" condition."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"points an N–H toward the flavin ring","note":"arginine donates"},{"t":"take the arginine’s hydrogen bond","note":"needs a lone pair"},{"t":"no H to give","note":"not a donor"}]},{"text":"**Donor vs acceptor.**","calc":["donor: N–H, O–H, F–H","acceptor: N, O or F bearing a lone pair","acceptor only: lone pair, no H"]},{"text":"**Common acceptor-only groups.** Carbonyl O, carboxylate O⁻, ether O, and a ring N with no H (pyridine-like)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an atom can accept but not donate a hydrogen bond when it is N, O or F with a free lone pair and no H attached, such as a C=O oxygen, a carboxylate oxygen or a pyridine-like ring N."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P5"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-24a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:24#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:24"],"passage":"A heat-stable enzyme from a hot-spring microbe binds the cofactor flavin mononucleotide (FMN), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV, so tightly that only denaturing the protein releases it. In the binding pocket, an arginine side chain points an N–H toward the flavin ring and forms a hydrogen bond to it. The fold is held together partly by a salt bridge between the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻, and mutating either residue lowers the enzyme’s melting temperature. The enzyme is switched on when an aspartate side chain, modeled by a small compound with four sites labeled I to IV, attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair, forming an acyl phosphate. After water later removes the phosphoryl group, that same atom takes a proton from a nearby water molecule. In the catalytic step, a glutamic acid residue that stays protonated in a nonpolar pocket hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group as a C–O bond breaks. It is then reprotonated by water from the solvent, not by the substrate.","q":"The catalytic glutamic acid most likely functions to:","choices":["act as a general acid.","act as a general base.","relay protons.","act as a nucleophile."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: the residue gives its proton away to the substrate; a base would take one from it."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"A relay takes a proton from one reacting group and passes it to another; this residue never took one from the substrate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"It forms no bond to the substrate carbon; it only transfers H⁺ to the leaving oxygen."}],"rule":"Track the proton: gives H⁺ → general acid; takes H⁺ → general base; takes H⁺ from one group and hands it to another → proton shuttle; bonds to carbon → nucleophile.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask where the residue’s proton goes. It starts protonated and gives H⁺ to the leaving group, so it is a proton donor, a general acid."},"exp":"The glutamic acid starts **protonated** (a nonpolar pocket raises its pKa) and **donates its H⁺** to the oxygen of the departing group as the C–O bond breaks. A residue that gives a proton to a substrate is a **general acid catalyst**. A **proton shuttle** would first take a proton from one group of the substrate and then pass that same proton to another, as the threonine-like relays in some carboxylases do.","how":{"ask":"Does the residue give a proton, take one, or take one from one group and pass it to another?","know":"• **General acid**: donates H⁺ to the substrate (e.g. to a leaving group).\n• **General base**: removes H⁺ from the substrate.\n• **Proton shuttle**: accepts H⁺ from one group, then gives it to another group.\n• **Nucleophile**: forms a bond to an electrophilic atom.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the residue stays protonated and then hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group: it donates H⁺.","hl":"hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group","strike":["act as a general base."]},{"text":"It is reprotonated by solvent water, not by the substrate, so it never moved a proton from one reacting group to another.","hl":"not by the substrate","strike":["relay protons."]},{"text":"It makes no bond to the substrate carbon.","strike":["act as a nucleophile."]},{"text":"Answer: it acts as a general acid."}],"trap":"Calling any residue that gains and loses a proton a \"shuttle\": a shuttle takes the proton from one reacting group and gives it to another."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"stays protonated","note":"starts as HA"},{"t":"hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group","note":"donates H⁺"},{"t":"reprotonated by water from the solvent","note":"not a relay"}]},{"text":"**Follow the proton.**","calc":["gives H⁺ → general acid","takes H⁺ → general base","takes from A, gives to B → shuttle"]},{"text":"**pKa and environment.** A nonpolar pocket keeps a carboxylic acid protonated at a pH where it would usually be ionized, so it can donate H⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name a catalytic residue’s role by where its proton goes: donates H⁺ → general acid, removes H⁺ → general base, carries H⁺ from one reacting group to another → proton shuttle."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P5"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-25a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:25#1","pe6-cp:25#2","pe6-cp:25#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:25"],"q":"Complete hydrolysis of an unknown tripeptide releases twice as much Phe as Ser. Partial hydrolysis gives the dipeptides Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser. What is the tripeptide's sequence?","choices":["Phe–Phe–Ser","Ser–Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe","Phe–Ser–Ser"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dipeptides read N → C: this chain holds Ser–Phe, the reverse of the observed Phe–Ser, and no Phe–Ser at all."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Two Ser and one Phe reverses the count: complete hydrolysis showed twice as much Phe as Ser."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Its neighbor pairs are Phe–Ser and Ser–Phe; with no Phe next to Phe, it cannot release a Phe–Phe dipeptide."}],"rule":"Get the composition first, then overlap the fragments; dipeptides are read N → C, so each must appear in that order in the chain.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Chain the dipeptides on their shared Phe: Phe–Phe then Phe–Ser overlap into Phe–Phe–Ser, which also matches 2 Phe : 1 Ser."},"exp":"Counts: **2 Phe + 1 Ser**. Each dipeptide is a pair of neighbors written N → C. Overlap them on the shared Phe: Phe–**Phe** + **Phe**–Ser → **Phe–Phe–Ser**, which releases exactly Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser.","how":{"ask":"Which order of two Phe and one Ser contains both Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser as neighbor pairs?","know":"• Complete hydrolysis breaks every peptide bond: it gives the **composition**.\n• Partial hydrolysis leaves short pieces that **keep their order** (N → C).\n• Overlap the pieces on a shared residue to build the chain, then check the counts.","steps":[{"text":"Composition: twice as much Phe as Ser in three residues means two Phe and one Ser, which rules out two Ser.","hl":"twice as much Phe as Ser","calc":["2 Phe + 1 Ser"],"strike":["Phe–Ser–Ser"]},{"text":"List the neighbor pairs each remaining order would release.","calc":["Phe–Phe–Ser → **Phe–Phe**, **Phe–Ser**","Ser–Phe–Phe → Ser–Phe, Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe → Phe–Ser, Ser–Phe"]},{"text":"Only one order gives both observed pieces: the others give Ser–Phe or lack Phe–Phe.","hl":"Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser","strike":["Ser–Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe"]},{"text":"Answer: Phe–Phe–Ser."}],"trap":"Ser–Phe is not the same piece as Phe–Ser; the order is fixed N → C, so a reversed pair is a different dipeptide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Complete hydrolysis","note":"all bonds cut: counts"},{"t":"twice as much Phe as Ser","note":"2 Phe, 1 Ser"},{"t":"Partial hydrolysis","note":"some bonds cut: neighbors"},{"t":"Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser","note":"two neighbor pairs"}]},{"text":"**Each dipeptide shows two neighbors**, in N → C order.","calc":["Phe–Phe: a Phe right before a Phe","Phe–Ser: a Phe right before Ser"]},{"text":"**Assemble one chain that contains both**: overlap them on the shared Phe.","calc":["Phe–Phe","     Phe–Ser","→ Phe–Phe–Ser"]},{"text":"**Check against the counts.**","calc":["Phe–Phe–Ser: 2 Phe, 1 Ser ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** get the composition from complete hydrolysis, overlap the partial pieces on shared residues, and keep only the order that explains every piece and the counts."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-26a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:26#1","pe6-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:26"],"q":"How many liters of O₂ at STP does the complete combustion of 29 g of butane, C₄H₁₀, consume?","choices":["11.2 L","44.8 L","72.8 L","145.6 L"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses one O₂ per butane; the balanced equation needs 13 O₂ for every 2 butane, so the coefficient step was skipped."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the CO₂ volume (4 CO₂ per butane), copied in place of the O₂ coefficient."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the 13 from the doubled equation as O₂ per butane, forgetting that it serves 2 butane molecules."}],"rule":"Combustion volume: balance first, then mol fuel × (O₂ coefficient ÷ fuel coefficient) × 22.4 L at STP.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"C₄H₁₀ + 6.5 O₂ → 4 CO₂ + 5 H₂O. 29 g ÷ 58 g/mol = 0.50 mol; × 6.5 = 3.25 mol O₂; × 22.4 ≈ 73 L."},"exp":"Balance: **2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O**, so each butane needs **6.5 O₂**. Butane is 58 g/mol, so 29 g = **0.50 mol**. O₂ = 0.50 × 6.5 = **3.25 mol**, and at STP 3.25 × 22.4 L = **72.8 L**.","how":{"ask":"How many O₂ does one butane need once the equation is balanced, and how many moles of butane are there?","know":"• Balance CₓHᵧ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O: C first, then H, then O (halves allowed, then double).\n• Moles of gas × 22.4 L/mol = volume at STP.\n• Molar mass of C₄H₁₀ = 4(12) + 10(1) = 58 g/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Balance the combustion.","hl":"butane, C₄H₁₀","calc":["C: 4 CO₂; H: 5 H₂O","O needed: 8 + 5 = 13 O atoms = 6.5 O₂","×2 → 2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O"]},{"text":"Convert the mass of butane to moles.","hl":"29 g","calc":["29 g ÷ 58 g/mol = 0.50 mol"]},{"text":"Scale by the O₂ ratio and convert to liters.","calc":["0.50 × 13/2 = 3.25 mol O₂","3.25 × 22.4 L = **72.8 L**"]},{"text":"11.2 L skips the 6.5 ratio, 44.8 L is the CO₂, and 145.6 L uses 13 O₂ per butane.","strike":["11.2 L","44.8 L","145.6 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 72.8 L."}],"trap":"Reading the 13 in the doubled equation as O₂ per butane: it is O₂ per 2 butane."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"29 g","note":"0.50 mol butane"},{"t":"O₂ at STP","note":"22.4 L per mol"},{"t":"complete combustion","note":"CO₂ + H₂O"}]},{"text":"**Balance, then ratio.**","calc":["2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O","O₂ : butane = 13 : 2"]},{"text":"**Moles to liters.** At STP, every mole of an ideal gas fills 22.4 L."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for gas used in a combustion, balance CₓHᵧ + O₂ first, convert the fuel to moles, multiply by the O₂ : fuel ratio, then by 22.4 L/mol at STP."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-27a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:27#2","pe6-cp:27#1","pe6-cp:27#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:27"],"q":"Four small oxygen-containing compounds are shown. Which one gives a ¹H NMR signal that is a doublet integrating to 6H?","choices":["2-Methyl-1-propanol","1-Propanol","Ethanol","tert-Butyl methyl ether"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-isobutanol","1-propanol","cpt-ethanol","cpt-pe6-mtbe"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"1-Propanol's CH₃ sits next to a CH₂ (2 H), so it is a 3H triplet, not a 6H doublet."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Ethanol's CH₃ sits next to a CH₂ (2 H), so it is a 3H triplet, not a 6H doublet."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Its (CH₃)₃C– carbon bears no H, so those 9 H are a singlet, and the OCH₃ is a singlet too: no doublet at all."}],"rule":"A 6H doublet means two equal CH₃ groups on a carbon with one H: look for an isopropyl-type (CH₃)₂CH– group.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A 6H doublet = an isopropyl pattern: two CH₃ on one CH. Only 2-methyl-1-propanol has (CH₃)₂CH–."},"exp":"**n + 1 rule:** a signal is split into n + 1 peaks by the n H on adjacent carbons. A doublet means **one neighbor H**. In 2-methyl-1-propanol, (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂OH, the two equivalent CH₃ groups (6 H) border a single CH, so they appear as a **6H doublet**. 1-Propanol's and ethanol's CH₃ are triplets (next to CH₂), and the ether gives only singlets.","how":{"ask":"Which compound has six equivalent H on carbons that border a carbon carrying exactly one H?","know":"• n + 1 rule: n H on neighboring carbons split a signal into n + 1 peaks.\n• Doublet → 1 neighbor H; triplet → 2; quartet → 3; singlet → 0.\n• A 6H signal = two equivalent CH₃ groups. A (CH₃)₃C– group sits on a carbon with no H, so its 9 H give a singlet.","steps":[{"text":"6 H means two equivalent CH₃ groups; a doublet means exactly one H next door.","hl":"doublet integrating to 6H"},{"text":"2-Methyl-1-propanol is (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂OH: each CH₃ borders the CH, which carries 1 H.","calc":["n = 1 → 1 + 1 = **2 peaks** (doublet)","2 × CH₃ = **6 H**"]},{"text":"1-Propanol's CH₃ borders a CH₂, and so does ethanol's.","calc":["n = 2 → 2 + 1 = 3 peaks (triplet), only 3 H"],"strike":["1-Propanol","Ethanol"]},{"text":"Also: in the ether the central carbon of (CH₃)₃C– has no H, so its 9 H have n = 0 and give a singlet; the OCH₃ is a singlet as well.","calc":["n = 0 → 0 + 1 = 1 peak (singlet)"],"strike":["tert-Butyl methyl ether"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-methyl-1-propanol.","fig":"cpt-pe6-isobutanol"}],"trap":"Counting the H in the group itself instead of its neighbors: a CH₃ is split by the H next door, not by its own three."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"doublet","note":"1 neighbor H"},{"t":"6H","note":"two equivalent CH₃"}]},{"text":"**Also: the n + 1 rule.** Count the H on the adjacent carbons (not the O–H) and add 1.","calc":["0 neighbors → singlet","1 → doublet","2 → triplet","3 → quartet"]},{"text":"**Also:** a group whose neighbor carbon has no H is never split: the 9 H of a (CH₃)₃C– group show as one singlet."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** splitting = n + 1 from the H on neighboring carbons, so a 6H doublet points to (CH₃)₂CH– and an unsplit 9H signal to (CH₃)₃C–."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-27b","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:27#4","pe6-cp:27#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:27"],"q":"An alcohol with formula C₅H₁₂O gives four ¹H NMR signals: a 6H singlet, a 3H triplet, a 2H quartet and a 1H broad singlet. Which compound is it?","choices":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol","2-Methylbutan-2-ol"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pentan-1-ol","cpt-pentan-2-ol","cpt-3-methylbutan-2-ol","cpt-2-methylbutan-2-ol"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A straight chain has no carbon holding two CH₃ groups, so it cannot give a 6H singlet."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its lone branch CH₃ sits next to a CH and shows as a 3H doublet; nothing gives 6H."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Its two CH₃ groups sit on a CH, so they appear as a 6H doublet, not a singlet."}],"rule":"n + 1 rule: a signal splits into (neighboring H + 1) peaks. A 6H singlet means two CH₃ on a carbon with no H; an O–H usually shows as an unsplit singlet.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"6H singlet ⇒ (CH₃)₂C– on a carbon with no H ⇒ a tertiary carbinol. Triplet 3H + quartet 2H ⇒ an ethyl group on that carbon."},"exp":"A **6H singlet** means two equivalent CH₃ groups on a carbon bearing **no hydrogen**; the only such choice is the tertiary alcohol **(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂CH₃**. Its ethyl group gives the **3H triplet** (CH₃ next to CH₂) and the **2H quartet** (CH₂ next to CH₃), and the **O–H** is a broad, unsplit 1H singlet, since rapid exchange erases its coupling.","how":{"ask":"Which choice has two CH₃ groups on a carbon that carries no H, plus an ethyl group?","know":"• **n + 1 rule**: n equivalent H on neighboring carbons split a signal into n + 1 peaks.\n• Singlet = no neighboring H; doublet = one; triplet = two; quartet = three.\n• An O–H usually appears as a broad singlet (fast exchange removes splitting).\n• Integration gives the number of H in each signal.","steps":[{"text":"Read the 6H singlet: two CH₃ with zero neighboring H, so they sit on a carbon with no H, the C–OH carbon of a tertiary alcohol.","hl":"a 6H singlet","calc":["6H = 2 × CH₃","singlet → n = 0 neighbors"]},{"text":"Pentan-1-ol has no such pair of methyls; in pentan-2-ol and 3-methylbutan-2-ol the CH₃ groups sit next to a CH, so they are doublets.","strike":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol"]},{"text":"Check the rest: the triplet and quartet are an ethyl group, and the broad singlet is the OH.","hl":"a 3H triplet, a 2H quartet","calc":["CH₃ next to CH₂ → 2 + 1 = triplet","CH₂ next to CH₃ → 3 + 1 = quartet","O–H → broad singlet"],"fig":"cpt-2-methylbutan-2-ol"},{"text":"Answer: 2-methylbutan-2-ol."}],"trap":"Expecting the OH to split its neighbors or be split: in a routine spectrum it exchanges too fast and appears as a broad singlet."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"C₅H₁₂O","note":"all choices fit"},{"t":"6H singlet","note":"2 CH₃, no neighbor H"},{"t":"1H broad singlet","note":"the O–H"}]},{"text":"**Splitting counts neighbors.**","calc":["0 neighbors → singlet","1 → doublet","2 → triplet","3 → quartet"]},{"text":"**Classic patterns.** tert-Butyl = 9H singlet; isopropyl = 6H doublet + 1H septet; ethyl = 3H triplet + 2H quartet; O–H = broad singlet."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match each NMR signal to a group by its integration (how many H) and its n + 1 splitting (how many neighboring H); groups on a carbon with no H appear as singlets, and an O–H is usually an unsplit singlet."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-28a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:28#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:28"],"q":"A block floats in fresh water (1000 kg/m³) with 60% of its volume submerged, and in an oil with 80% submerged. What is the oil’s density?","choices":["600 kg/m³","750 kg/m³","800 kg/m³","1330 kg/m³"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio flipped: the block sinks deeper in the oil, so the oil must be less dense than water, not more."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the block’s own density; the second step, dividing it by the fraction under in oil, was skipped."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the 80% submerged fraction as a density; the fraction equals ρ_block ÷ ρ_oil, not ρ_oil."}],"rule":"Floating: fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid. Get ρ_object from the first fluid, then divide it by the fraction in the second.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = 600 kg/m³. In oil, ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = 750 kg/m³: sinking deeper means a thinner liquid."},"exp":"A floating object displaces its own weight, so **fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid**. In water: ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = **600 kg/m³**. In oil: 0.80 = 600 ÷ ρ_oil, so ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = **750 kg/m³**. The block rides lower in oil, which fits a liquid less dense than water.","how":{"ask":"What is the block’s density from the water data, and what oil density makes it 80% submerged?","know":"• Floating: buoyant force = weight, so ρ_liquid × V_sub = ρ_object × V.\n• Fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid; ρ_liquid = ρ_object ÷ fraction.\n• Deeper float → less dense fluid; higher float → denser fluid. Fully submerged and level with the surface → ρ_liquid = ρ_object.","steps":[{"text":"Find the block’s density from the water float.","hl":"60% of its volume submerged","calc":["ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = 600 kg/m³"],"strike":["600 kg/m³"]},{"text":"Use the oil float to find the oil’s density.","hl":"80% submerged","calc":["ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = **750 kg/m³**"]},{"text":"It floats lower in the oil, so the oil is less dense than water; 1330 flips the ratio and 800 copies the fraction.","strike":["1330 kg/m³","800 kg/m³"]},{"text":"Answer: 750 kg/m³."}],"trap":"Dividing the wrong way: sinking deeper means a thinner fluid, so the second density must come out below the first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1000 kg/m³","note":"reference fluid"},{"t":"60% of its volume submerged","note":"ρ_block ÷ 1000"},{"t":"80% submerged","note":"ρ_block ÷ ρ_oil"}]},{"text":"**Fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid.** The same block has one density, so use the first fluid to get it."},{"text":"**Edges of the range.** Fully submerged at the surface → fluid density equals the object’s; the smallest fraction a scale shows → the densest fluid it can read."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a floating object, fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid; find ρ_object from one fluid, then ρ_liquid = ρ_object ÷ fraction for any other."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-28b","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:28#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:28"],"q":"A hydrometer has an average density of 0.90 g/cm³. Its scale is marked from fully submerged down to 75% submerged. What is the highest liquid density it can read?","choices":["0.68 g/cm³","0.83 g/cm³","0.90 g/cm³","1.20 g/cm³"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"0.68 multiplies 0.90 × 0.75; floating gives ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction, so a fraction below 1 must raise the density."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio upside-down (0.75 ÷ 0.90); riding higher needs a denser liquid, so the answer must sit above 0.90."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"0.90 g/cm³ is the hydrometer's own density, the fully submerged reading: the lowest on the scale, not the highest."}],"rule":"Fluid density = object density ÷ fraction submerged: the less it sinks, the denser the liquid, so the top reading uses the smallest fraction.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"ρ fluid = ρ object ÷ fraction under: smallest fraction gives the densest liquid. 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = 0.9 × 4/3 = 1.20 g/cm³."},"exp":"From fraction = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid, **ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction**. Fully submerged (fraction 1) gives the **lowest** reading, 0.90 g/cm³. The smallest marked fraction gives the **highest**: 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = **1.20 g/cm³**.","how":{"ask":"At which mark does the hydrometer float highest, and what liquid density puts it there?","know":"• Floating: submerged fraction = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid, so ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction.\n• Denser liquid → floats higher → smaller fraction under.\n• Range: fraction 1 → lowest density (= ρ_object); smallest marked fraction → highest density.","steps":[{"text":"The highest density is where the hydrometer floats highest: the smallest marked fraction.","hl":"75% submerged"},{"text":"Solve the floating rule for the liquid's density.","calc":["ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction","= 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = **1.20 g/cm³**"]},{"text":"0.83 flips the ratio (0.75 ÷ 0.90) and 0.68 multiplies; both land below 0.90, yet riding higher needs a denser liquid.","strike":["0.83 g/cm³","0.68 g/cm³"]},{"text":"0.90 g/cm³ is the fully submerged reading: the bottom of the range.","strike":["0.90 g/cm³"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.20 g/cm³."}],"trap":"Thinking the fully submerged mark is the top of the scale: a hydrometer sinks deepest in the thinnest liquid, so full submersion is its lowest reading."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"average density of 0.90 g/cm³","note":"ρ_object"},{"t":"fully submerged","note":"fraction 1"},{"t":"75% submerged","note":"smallest fraction"},{"t":"highest liquid density","note":"floats highest"}]},{"text":"**Flip the floating rule.**","calc":["fraction = ρ_obj ÷ ρ_fluid → ρ_fluid = ρ_obj ÷ fraction"]},{"text":"**Both ends of the scale.**","calc":["fraction 1.00 → 0.90 ÷ 1.00 = 0.90 g/cm³ (lowest)","fraction 0.75 → 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = 1.20 g/cm³ (highest)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a hydrometer reads ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction submerged: fully under is its lowest reading, the smallest marked fraction its highest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-29a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:29"],"passage":"Protein X, a small basic enzyme with an isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5, was labeled with iodine-131 so that it could be tracked in tissue. The iodine-131 nucleus emits a beta particle, an electron created inside the nucleus, and becomes xenon-131 in an excited state, which then relaxes by emitting a gamma ray that a scanner detects. To choose a running buffer, protein X was loaded into the middle of three gel strips buffered at pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5, each with the positive electrode at one end and the negative electrode at the other. During weeks of storage the protein slowly formed soluble aggregates, which ran as covalent dimers and trimers on SDS-PAGE without a reducing agent but only as monomers when dithiothreitol (DTT), which breaks S–S bonds, was added. Mass spectrometry found no dityrosine or other radical cross-links. Finally, the share of molecules unfolded was estimated from the shift in tryptophan fluorescence after four treatments: heat at 70 °C, 92%; 3 M urea, 41%; ammonium sulfate, 5%; plain buffer at room temperature, 3%.","q":"Which prediction about protein X’s movement is correct?","choices":["To cathode at 5.5; none at 7.5","To anode at 5.5; none at 7.5","To cathode at 5.5; to anode at 7.5","To anode at 5.5; to cathode at 7.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Below its pI a protein carries net + charge, so it heads for the − electrode, not the + one."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both halves flipped: + charge below the pI heads to the cathode, and at the pI there is no net charge to pull on."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"At pH equal to the pI the net charge is zero, so the protein does not migrate in that strip."}],"rule":"pH below pI → net + → moves to the cathode (−); pH = pI → net 0 → stays put; pH above pI → net − → moves to the anode (+).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Compare pH with pI. Lower pH → extra H⁺ on the protein → positive → toward −. Equal → neutral → no movement."},"exp":"Below its pI a protein holds extra protons, so it carries a **net positive charge** and drifts toward the **negative electrode, the cathode**: at pH 5.5 (two units below the pI of 7.5) protein X heads to the cathode. At **pH 7.5 = pI** its net charge is **zero**, so it stays put. (At pH 9.5, above the pI, it would be net negative and move toward the anode.)","how":{"ask":"Is each buffer’s pH below, equal to or above the pI, and what net charge does that give?","know":"• At pH = pI, net charge = 0 → no migration.\n• pH < pI → net + → toward the cathode (−).\n• pH > pI → net − → toward the anode (+).\n• In electrophoresis the anode is +, the cathode −.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the pI, 7.5. pH 5.5 lies under that value, so protein X carries net positive charge and drifts toward the negative electrode.","hl":"isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5","calc":["5.5 < 7.5 → net + → cathode"],"strike":["To anode at 5.5; none at 7.5","To anode at 5.5; to cathode at 7.5"]},{"text":"pH 7.5 equals the pI: no net charge, no movement.","calc":["7.5 = pI → net 0 → stays"],"strike":["To cathode at 5.5; to anode at 7.5"]},{"text":"Also: at pH 9.5 the protein is net negative and would move toward the anode."},{"text":"Answer: toward the cathode at pH 5.5, no movement at 7.5."}],"trap":"Mixing up the electrode names: in a gel the cathode is the negative electrode, where positive proteins go."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5","note":"net charge 0 at pH 7.5"},{"t":"pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5","note":"below, at, above pI"},{"t":"positive electrode","note":"the anode"}]},{"text":"**pH vs pI sets the sign.**","calc":["pH < pI → +","pH = pI → 0","pH > pI → −"]},{"text":"**Opposites attract.** A positive protein moves toward the cathode (−); a negative one toward the anode (+); a neutral one stays."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a protein is net positive below its pI, neutral at its pI and net negative above it, so it moves toward the cathode, stays put, or moves toward the anode."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P6"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-30a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:30#1","pe6-cp:30#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:30"],"passage":"Protein X, a small basic enzyme with an isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5, was labeled with iodine-131 so that it could be tracked in tissue. The iodine-131 nucleus emits a beta particle, an electron created inside the nucleus, and becomes xenon-131 in an excited state, which then relaxes by emitting a gamma ray that a scanner detects. To choose a running buffer, protein X was loaded into the middle of three gel strips buffered at pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5, each with the positive electrode at one end and the negative electrode at the other. During weeks of storage the protein slowly formed soluble aggregates, which ran as covalent dimers and trimers on SDS-PAGE without a reducing agent but only as monomers when dithiothreitol (DTT), which breaks S–S bonds, was added. Mass spectrometry found no dityrosine or other radical cross-links. Finally, the share of molecules unfolded was estimated from the shift in tryptophan fluorescence after four treatments: heat at 70 °C, 92%; 3 M urea, 41%; ammonium sulfate, 5%; plain buffer at room temperature, 3%.","q":"Which side chain forms the cross-links in the stored protein?","choices":["Cysteine","Methionine","Threonine","Lysine"],"choiceFigs":["cysteine-sc","methionine-sc","threonine-sc","lysine-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its sulfur is a thioether with no S–H, so it cannot join another side chain through an S–S bond."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A hydroxyl does not form covalent links between chains that a disulfide reducer can break."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An amine does not form S–S bonds, so a link through it would survive the reducing agent."}],"rule":"Covalent links undone by DTT or β-mercaptoethanol are disulfides from two –SH groups; radical links such as dityrosine survive reducing agents.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Undone by a reducing agent ⇒ S–S bond ⇒ needs two thiols. Only one drawn side chain ends in –SH."},"exp":"The links are **covalent** (they survive SDS) but **break with DTT**, a reducing agent that cuts **disulfide (S–S) bonds**. A disulfide forms when two **cysteine thiols (–SH)** are oxidized together, so cysteine forms the cross-links. Methionine’s sulfur has no H to lose, and radical-made links such as dityrosine, which join two tyrosine rings by a C–C bond, would not be reversed by DTT and were not found.","how":{"ask":"What kind of covalent bond does DTT break, and which side chain can make it?","know":"• **Disulfide**: Cys–SH + HS–Cys → Cys–S–S–Cys (oxidation); DTT or β-mercaptoethanol reduces it back.\n• **Dityrosine**: radicals oxidize tyrosine phenol rings, which join by a C–C bond; reducing agents do not undo it.\n• Methionine is a thioether (C–S–C): no S–H.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the links survive SDS, so they are covalent, and DTT, which breaks S–S bonds, erases them: they are disulfides.","hl":"which breaks S–S bonds"},{"text":"An S–S bond needs a thiol on each partner: only cysteine has –SH.","fig":"cysteine-sc","calc":["2 Cys–SH → Cys–S–S–Cys + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻"]},{"text":"Methionine has a thioether sulfur with no H; threonine’s –OH and lysine’s –NH₂ make no S–S bonds.","strike":["Methionine","Threonine","Lysine"]},{"text":"Also: radical damage joins two tyrosines into dityrosine, a C–C link that DTT cannot break; the passage rules it out.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dityrosine"},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Picking methionine because it contains sulfur: without an S–H it cannot form a disulfide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"covalent dimers and trimers","note":"bonds, not sticking"},{"t":"which breaks S–S bonds","note":"disulfides"},{"t":"no dityrosine","note":"radical link ruled out"}]},{"text":"**Two covalent cross-links.**","calc":["Cys–S–S–Cys: oxidation; DTT reverses","dityrosine (Tyr–Tyr C–C): radicals; not reversible"]},{"text":"**Sulfur alone is not enough.** A disulfide needs an –SH; methionine’s S is already bonded to two carbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a covalent protein cross-link that a reducing agent such as DTT breaks is a disulfide between two cysteine thiols; a radical-made dityrosine link between two tyrosine rings is not reversed."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P6"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-31a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:31"],"passage":"Protein X, a small basic enzyme with an isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5, was labeled with iodine-131 so that it could be tracked in tissue. The iodine-131 nucleus emits a beta particle, an electron created inside the nucleus, and becomes xenon-131 in an excited state, which then relaxes by emitting a gamma ray that a scanner detects. To choose a running buffer, protein X was loaded into the middle of three gel strips buffered at pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5, each with the positive electrode at one end and the negative electrode at the other. During weeks of storage the protein slowly formed soluble aggregates, which ran as covalent dimers and trimers on SDS-PAGE without a reducing agent but only as monomers when dithiothreitol (DTT), which breaks S–S bonds, was added. Mass spectrometry found no dityrosine or other radical cross-links. Finally, the share of molecules unfolded was estimated from the shift in tryptophan fluorescence after four treatments: heat at 70 °C, 92%; 3 M urea, 41%; ammonium sulfate, 5%; plain buffer at room temperature, 3%.","q":"Which treatment leaves the most nonpolar side chains of protein X in contact with water?","choices":["Heat, 70 °C","Urea at 3 M","Ammonium sulfate","Plain buffer"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Picks the least-unfolded sample: a folded protein keeps its nonpolar side chains buried in the core."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Urea does denature proteins, but at this dose fewer than half of the molecules unfolded."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"This salt stabilizes the fold (it is used for salting out); almost none of the enzyme unfolded."}],"rule":"The most exposed core belongs to the most unfolded sample: rank the data, whatever the denaturant’s name.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Scan for the largest % unfolded. Unfolding brings buried nonpolar side chains to the surface, so the top value wins."},"exp":"In a folded protein the **nonpolar side chains sit hidden** inside, away from water. Unfolding exposes them, so the condition that unfolds the **largest share** exposes the most: **heat at 70 °C (92%)**. 3 M urea unfolded only 41%, ammonium sulfate (a stabilizing salt) 5%, and plain buffer 3%.","how":{"ask":"Which treatment unfolded the largest share of the enzyme?","know":"• Folding buries nonpolar (hydrophobic) side chains in the core; denaturing exposes them to water.\n• Denaturants: heat, urea, guanidinium, detergents, extreme pH. Stabilizing salts (ammonium sulfate) do the opposite.\n• The amount of a denaturant matters: read the data, not just the name.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the share of molecules unfolded after each treatment. Exposure of the nonpolar core tracks unfolding, so rank the treatments by percent unfolded.","hl":"share of molecules unfolded","calc":["heat 92% > urea 41% > salt 5% > buffer 3%"]},{"text":"Buffer and the salt leave the fold intact, and the urea dose unfolded under half.","strike":["Plain buffer","Ammonium sulfate","Urea at 3 M"]},{"text":"Answer: heat at 70 °C."}],"trap":"Choosing urea because it is the textbook denaturant: here the data show heat unfolded far more of this enzyme."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"heat at 70 °C, 92%","note":"most unfolded"},{"t":"3 M urea, 41%","note":"partial"},{"t":"nonpolar side chains","note":"buried when folded"}]},{"text":"**Unfolded = core exposed.** The hydrophobic effect buries nonpolar side chains; denaturing undoes that."},{"text":"**Let the data decide.** Rank by % unfolded; the denaturant’s reputation does not matter."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** hydrophobic side chains are most exposed to water in whichever condition denatures the protein most, so pick the highest percent unfolded in the data."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P6"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-32a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:32#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:32"],"passage":"Protein X, a small basic enzyme with an isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5, was labeled with iodine-131 so that it could be tracked in tissue. The iodine-131 nucleus emits a beta particle, an electron created inside the nucleus, and becomes xenon-131 in an excited state, which then relaxes by emitting a gamma ray that a scanner detects. To choose a running buffer, protein X was loaded into the middle of three gel strips buffered at pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5, each with the positive electrode at one end and the negative electrode at the other. During weeks of storage the protein slowly formed soluble aggregates, which ran as covalent dimers and trimers on SDS-PAGE without a reducing agent but only as monomers when dithiothreitol (DTT), which breaks S–S bonds, was added. Mass spectrometry found no dityrosine or other radical cross-links. Finally, the share of molecules unfolded was estimated from the shift in tryptophan fluorescence after four treatments: heat at 70 °C, 92%; 3 M urea, 41%; ammonium sulfate, 5%; plain buffer at room temperature, 3%.","q":"In the beta step of the label’s decay, what happens to Z and to A?","choices":["Z rises by one; A same","Z falls by one; A same","Z same; A same","Z rises by one; A falls by one"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: an electron leaving means a neutron became a proton, so the nuclear charge goes up."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"That describes the gamma step that follows; the beta step changes the element from iodine to xenon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An electron carries almost no mass and the nucleon count is unchanged: a neutron simply became a proton."}],"rule":"α: Z −2, A −4. β⁻: Z +1, A same. β⁺ or capture: Z −1, A same. γ: neither changes; the nucleus only sheds energy.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"β⁻: n → p + e⁻. One more proton, same total nucleons: Z up one, A unchanged (iodine 53 → xenon 54)."},"exp":"In **β⁻ decay** a neutron turns into a proton and an electron is ejected: **Z rises by one** (iodine, Z = 53, becomes xenon, Z = 54) and **A is unchanged** (131 nucleons before and after). The **gamma ray** that follows comes from the excited xenon nucleus dropping to its ground state: a photon with **no change in Z or A**.","how":{"ask":"What particle leaves in the beta step, and how does that change the proton and nucleon counts?","know":"• **α**: ⁴₂He leaves → Z −2, A −4.\n• **β⁻**: n → p + e⁻ → Z +1, A same.\n• **β⁺ / electron capture**: p → n → Z −1, A same.\n• **γ**: an excited nucleus emits a photon → Z and A unchanged.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the beta particle is an electron created inside the nucleus: a neutron became a proton.","hl":"an electron created inside the nucleus","calc":["n → p + e⁻"],"strike":["Z falls by one; A same"]},{"text":"One more proton, same number of nucleons.","calc":["¹³¹₅₃I → ¹³¹₅₄Xe* + e⁻","Z: 53 → 54; A: 131 → 131"],"strike":["Z rises by one; A falls by one"]},{"text":"No change in either belongs to the next step: the excited nucleus emits a gamma ray.","hl":"emitting a gamma ray","strike":["Z same; A same"]},{"text":"Answer: Z rises by one; A stays the same."}],"trap":"Mixing the two steps: the gamma ray changes nothing, but the beta emission before it makes a new element."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"beta particle","note":"β⁻: Z +1"},{"t":"excited state","note":"will emit γ"},{"t":"gamma ray","note":"no change in Z or A"}]},{"text":"**Four emissions to know.**","calc":["α: Z −2, A −4","β⁻: Z +1, A 0","β⁺: Z −1, A 0","γ: Z 0, A 0"]},{"text":"**An excited nucleus relaxes by gamma emission.** Like an excited atom giving off light, it only loses energy, so the element and the mass number stay the same."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match the emission to its change: α lowers Z by 2 and A by 4, β⁻ raises Z by 1, β⁺ or capture lowers Z by 1, and a γ photon from an excited nucleus changes neither."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P6"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-33a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:33#1","pe6-cp:33#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:33"],"passage":"To follow an 80 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"After the first trial, roughly what percent of the kinase stayed unlabeled?","choices":["About 25%","About 75%","About 100%","About 33%"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"25% is the labeled share: the faint glowing band is the dye-bearing protein. Assigning it to unlabeled protein flips the answer."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"33% copies the stem's 'one-third' as is; that is a band-to-band ratio, not a share of all the protein."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The shifted band takes the protein stain AND fluoresces, so some protein carries dye; not all of it can be unlabeled."}],"rule":"Turn a darkness ratio into shares of the total (a : b means a ÷ (a + b)), and decide which band is which first.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Unshifted, non-glowing band = unlabeled; shifted, glowing = labeled. Darkness 3 : 1 → unlabeled share 3 of 4 = 75%."},"exp":"Coomassie shows all protein; fluorescence shows dye. The band level with untreated kinase **stains but does not glow**: unlabeled. The shifted band that fluoresces is **labeled** protein. Darkness 3 : 1, so unlabeled = 3 ÷ (3 + 1) ≈ **75%**.","how":{"ask":"Which stained band is the unlabeled protein, and what share of the total stain does it hold?","know":"• **Protein stain** (Coomassie) shows every protein band; **fluorescence** shows only dye.\n• A lane of untreated kinase marks where unlabeled protein runs.\n• Band darkness is proportional to amount, so a fraction = that band ÷ the sum of all protein bands.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 describes the first-trial gel: one stained band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. Assign each band by the two tests.","hl":"one band level with the untreated kinase","calc":["level with untreated: stain ✓, glow ✗ → **unlabeled**","shifted band: stain ✓, glow ✓ → **labeled**"]},{"text":"The labeled band both stains and glows, so some of the protein carries dye.","hl":"shifted band that fluoresces","strike":["About 100%"]},{"text":"Turn darkness into a share of the total. 25% is the labeled share, and 33% is the 1 : 3 ratio, not a share of all the protein.","hl":"one-third as dark","calc":["unlabeled : labeled = 3 : 1","unlabeled = 3 ÷ (3 + 1) = **0.75 = 75%**","labeled = 1 ÷ 4 = 25%"],"strike":["About 25%","About 33%"]},{"text":"Answer: about 75% stayed unlabeled."}],"trap":"Reading 1 : 3 as one-third; a fraction divides by the total of both bands (4 parts), not by the other band."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Coomassie-stained","note":"shows all protein"},{"t":"level with the untreated kinase","note":"where unlabeled runs"},{"t":"shifted band that fluoresces","note":"carries dye"},{"t":"one-third as dark","note":"1 part vs 3 parts"}]},{"text":"**Compare the lanes.** The untreated-protein lane is the ruler: a sample band at the same height is protein with no dye on it."},{"text":"**Two tests per band.**","calc":["stain ✓ glow ✓ → labeled protein","stain ✓ glow ✗ → unlabeled protein","stain ✗ glow ✓ → free dye"]},{"text":"**Turn darkness into a share.**","calc":["unlabeled 3 parts + labeled 1 part = 4 parts","unlabeled = 3/4 = 75%"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the unlabeled band by its match to the untreated lane (stains, no glow), then divide its darkness by the total of all protein bands."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-33b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:33#3","pe6-cp:33#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:33"],"passage":"To follow an 80 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"After the second trial, about what fraction of the kinase was left unlabeled?","choices":["About 20%","About 80%","Almost none","About 33%"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Flipped: the dark shifted band is the labeled kinase, so it is the larger share, not the unlabeled one."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Reads only the UV image: unlabeled kinase cannot glow, so it shows up only in the stained lane."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Counts the glowing bottom band as protein; it never stains, so it is free dye, not enzyme."}],"rule":"Unlabeled share = the stained band at the unlabeled position ÷ all stained protein bands; a band that glows but never stains is free dye.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Stained protein only: labeled 4 parts, unlabeled 1 part. Unlabeled = 1 ÷ (4 + 1) = 1/5."},"exp":"Only the **Coomassie stain** shows all protein. It shows a dark **shifted (labeled)** band and a faint band **level with untreated kinase** at one-quarter the intensity: labeled : unlabeled = 4 : 1, so the unlabeled share is 1 ÷ 5 ≈ **20%**. The bottom band glows under UV but **never stains**, so it is **free dye**, not protein, and is left out of the count.","how":{"ask":"Which bands are protein, and what share of the stained protein sits at the unlabeled position?","know":"• A protein stain (Coomassie) shows every protein band; UV shows only dye.\n• Labeled protein = stains and glows; unlabeled protein = stains only; free dye = glows only, runs near the bottom.\n• Fraction unlabeled = unlabeled band ÷ all protein bands, compared by intensity.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the bottom band glows but stayed blank after staining: it is free dye, not protein. Sort the rest: shifted = labeled (stains and glows); level with untreated = unlabeled (stains only).","hl":"the bottom band stayed blank","strike":["About 33%"]},{"text":"Unlabeled kinase is invisible under UV, so the UV image alone says nothing about it.","strike":["Almost none"]},{"text":"Compare stained intensities.","hl":"had one-quarter of its intensity","calc":["labeled : unlabeled = 4 : 1","unlabeled = 1 ÷ 5 = **20%**"],"strike":["About 80%"]},{"text":"Answer: about 20%."}],"trap":"Counting the bottom band as protein: free dye glows but never takes up a protein stain."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"imaged under UV","note":"shows dye only"},{"t":"Coomassie-stained","note":"shows all protein"},{"t":"faint band level with the untreated kinase","note":"unlabeled protein"}]},{"text":"**Two readouts, two questions.** UV asks \"is dye here?\"; the stain asks \"is protein here?\". Labeled protein answers yes to both."},{"text":"**Compare lanes.** The untreated lane marks where unlabeled protein runs; a band there in the sample lane is unlabeled kinase."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** estimate the unlabeled share from the protein-stained gel, as the band at the unlabeled position over all protein bands; a band that glows but does not stain is free dye, not protein."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-34a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:34#1","pe6-cp:34#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:34"],"passage":"To follow an 80 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"Of the side chains shown, which is the strongest nucleophile toward the dye?","choices":["Lys","Thr","Gln","Glu"],"choiceFigs":["lysine-sc","threonine-sc","glutamine-sc","glutamate-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An alcohol O–H is a weak nucleophile in water; that is why amine- and thiol-reactive dyes leave Ser and Thr alone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide N's lone pair is delocalized into the C=O, so Gln (like Asn) barely reacts as a nucleophile."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylate spreads its charge over two O atoms by resonance, so Glu is a weak nucleophile."}],"rule":"In protein labeling the strong nucleophiles are a free amine or thiol lone pair; O–H, amides and carboxylates are weak.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At pH 7 the strong side-chain nucleophiles are amine (Lys) and thiol (Cys). No Cys listed, so Lys; OH, amide and carboxylate are weak."},"exp":"Lysine's ε-amine (pKa ≈ 10.5) is mostly –NH₃⁺ at pH 7, but its small **neutral –NH₂ share** has a free lone pair and reacts fast. Thr's –OH, Gln's amide and Glu's resonance-stabilized carboxylate are **poor nucleophiles**. Cysteine's –SH/–S⁻ is the other strong nucleophile at pH 7.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain has a lone pair that is free (not delocalized, not tightly held) at pH 7?","know":"• Good nucleophiles at pH 7: **Lys –NH₂** (the unprotonated share) and **Cys –SH/–S⁻**.\n• Poor: alcohol O (Ser, Thr), amide N (Asn, Gln), carboxylate O (Asp, Glu).\n• Amide and carboxylate lone pairs are delocalized by resonance.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says the dye is an electrophile and the kinase has no cysteine: the dye needs an electron-pair donor near neutral pH, and the thiol option is gone.","hl":"an electrophile that acylates","fig":"lysine-sc"},{"text":"Rule out the weak donors: Thr holds its O lone pairs tightly, and Gln and Glu spread theirs by resonance.","calc":["Thr: R–OH, weak O nucleophile","Gln: R–C(=O)NH₂, N lone pair in resonance","Glu: R–COO⁻, charge spread over two O"],"strike":["Thr","Gln","Glu"]},{"text":"Lys: only a tiny share is neutral –NH₂ at pH 7, but that free amine reacts, and more deprotonates as it is used up.","calc":["share as –NH₂ = 1 ÷ (1 + 10^(10.5 − 7.4))","= 1 ÷ (1 + 1259) ≈ 0.08%"]},{"text":"Answer: Lys."}],"trap":"Picking Glu because it carries a full negative charge; charge alone doesn't make a good nucleophile when resonance spreads it over two oxygens."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"an electrophile","note":"needs a lone-pair donor"},{"t":"pH 7.4","note":"near neutral: check protonation"},{"t":"strongest nucleophile","note":"most available lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Two strong nucleophiles at pH 7:** lysine's –NH₂ (amine) and cysteine's –SH (thiol, partly S⁻).","fig":["lysine-sc","cysteine-sc"]},{"text":"**Also:** Asp, Glu, Ser, Thr, Asn and Gln are poor nucleophiles at pH 7: alcohol oxygens hold their lone pairs tightly, and amides and carboxylates delocalize theirs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find a labeling site, look for an available lone pair: the Lys amine and the Cys thiol react; O–H, amide and carboxylate side chains do not."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-34b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:34#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:34"],"passage":"To follow an 80 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"Which side chain carries the NHS-ester dye?","choices":["–(CH₂)₄NH₂","–CH₂OH","–CH₂COO⁻","–(CH₂)₂CONH₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A hydroxyl is a weak nucleophile in water and is not the group NHS esters are built to acylate."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylate is not an amine; NHS esters react with –NH₂, and this group has none."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide NH₂ has its lone pair delocalized into C=O, so it is not nucleophilic toward the dye."}],"rule":"Match the dye to its group: NHS esters and isothiocyanates → amines (Lys, N-terminus); maleimides and iodoacetamides → thiols (Cys).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NHS ester = amine-reactive. The only side chain ending in a free –NH₂ (not an amide) is lysine’s."},"exp":"An **NHS ester** is an activated ester that acylates **free amines**, forming an amide; at pH 8.3 enough lysine ε-amino groups are unprotonated to react. The labeled side chain is **lysine, –(CH₂)₄NH₂**. Serine’s –OH and aspartate’s –COO⁻ are not amines, and glutamine’s –CONH₂ is an amide whose N lone pair is delocalized. The maleimide failed because a maleimide needs a cysteine –SH, which this protein lacks.","how":{"ask":"Which group does an NHS ester react with, and which drawn side chain has it?","know":"• **Amine-reactive dyes** (NHS esters, isothiocyanates) label Lys –NH₂ and the N-terminus.\n• **Thiol-reactive dyes** (maleimides, iodoacetamides) label Cys –SH.\n• Asp, Glu, Ser, Thr, Asn and Gln are poor nucleophiles at pH 7–8.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says the NHS ester acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups, and the maleimide labeled nothing, so there is no –SH.","hl":"acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups"},{"text":"Only one drawn group is a free amine: the lysine side chain.","fig":"lysine-sc","calc":["–(CH₂)₄NH₂ → Lys"]},{"text":"Serine’s –OH and aspartate’s carboxylate are not amines, and glutamine’s amide N is not nucleophilic.","strike":["–CH₂OH","–CH₂COO⁻","–(CH₂)₂CONH₂"]},{"text":"Answer: the lysine side chain."}],"trap":"Picking glutamine because it ends in NH₂: an amide NH₂ shares its lone pair with C=O and does not react."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NHS-ester dye","note":"amine-reactive"},{"t":"no cysteine","note":"no –SH"},{"t":"pH 8.3","note":"more Lys –NH₂ free"}]},{"text":"**Two dye families.**","calc":["NHS ester → –NH₂ (Lys)","maleimide → –SH (Cys)"]},{"text":"**Nucleophile check.** Free amines and thiols react; hydroxyls, carboxylates and amides do not, under labeling conditions."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** amine-reactive dyes such as NHS esters label lysine (and the N-terminus); thiol-reactive dyes such as maleimides label cysteine."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-35a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:35#1","pe6-cp:35#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:35"],"passage":"To follow an 80 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"What mass of NaCl goes into the 200 mL of labeling buffer?","choices":["0.10 g","2.3 g","6.0 g","6000 g"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Uses 200 L instead of 0.200 L: convert mL to L before multiplying by the molarity."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops at moles of NaCl; multiply by the molar mass to get grams."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses only sodium’s 23.0 g/mol; NaCl’s molar mass also includes chlorine’s 35.5 g/mol."}],"rule":"Grams = M × V(L) × molar mass. Convert mL to L first, and build the molar mass from every atom in the formula.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mol = 0.51 × 0.200 ≈ 0.10; × 58.5 g/mol ≈ 6 g. Check: 3% w/v = 3 g per 100 mL → 6 g in 200 mL."},"exp":"Moles = M × V = 0.51 mol/L × 0.200 L ≈ **0.102 mol**. NaCl’s molar mass is 23.0 + 35.5 = **58.5 g/mol**, so mass ≈ 0.102 × 58.5 ≈ **6.0 g**. The % (w/v) gives the same answer: 3 g per 100 mL × 200 mL = 6 g. (Normal saline, 0.9%, is 0.15 M.)","how":{"ask":"How many moles are in 200 mL at 0.51 M, and what do they weigh?","know":"• **mol = M × V(L)**; **mass = mol × molar mass**.\n• Normal saline = 0.9% (w/v) NaCl ≈ 0.15 M; % (w/v) = grams per 100 mL.\n• NaCl = 23.0 + 35.5 = 58.5 g/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the recipe: 0.51 M NaCl, 200 mL. Convert the volume and find moles.","hl":"200 mL of that buffer","calc":["200 mL = 0.200 L","0.51 × 0.200 ≈ 0.102 mol"],"strike":["6000 g"]},{"text":"Build the molar mass from both atoms, then convert to grams.","hl":"Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol","calc":["58.5 g/mol","0.102 × 58.5 ≈ **6.0 g**"],"strike":["0.10 g","2.3 g"]},{"text":"Check with % (w/v): 3 g per 100 mL → 6 g per 200 mL.","hl":"3% (w/v)"},{"text":"Answer: about 6.0 g."}],"trap":"Multiplying molarity by milliliters: M × mL gives millimoles, so convert or the answer is off by 1000."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.51 M","note":"mol per liter"},{"t":"200 mL","note":"0.200 L"},{"t":"Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol","note":"sum = 58.5"}]},{"text":"**Two steps.**","calc":["mol = M × V(L)","g = mol × molar mass"]},{"text":"**Saline anchors.** Normal saline is 0.9% (w/v), about 0.15 M NaCl, and 9 g per liter; % (w/v) means grams per 100 mL."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass of solute = molarity × volume in liters × molar mass; for normal saline, 0.15 M NaCl is 0.9 g per 100 mL."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-36a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:36#1","pe6-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:36"],"passage":"To follow an 80 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"How many moles of kinase went into each labeling trial?","choices":["0.3 nmol","3 μmol","0.3 mmol","0.3 μmol"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"That divides by 1000 one time too many; 24 mg is 0.024 g, one factor of 1000 only."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"That uses 8000 g/mol; 80 kDa is 80 000 g/mol, so the result is 10 times too big."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That divides grams by 80 g/mol: 1 kDa = 1000 g/mol, so the molar mass is 80 000 g/mol, not 80."}],"rule":"Mass = volume × concentration; 1 kDa = 1000 g/mol, and mg ÷ (g/mol) gives mmol. Round every number to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mg ÷ kDa gives μmol (10⁻³ g ÷ 10³ g/mol = 10⁻⁶ mol). 16 × 1.5 = 24 mg; 24 ÷ 80 = 0.3 → 0.3 μmol."},"exp":"**Mass = concentration × volume**: 16 mg/mL × 1.5 mL = 24 mg = 0.024 g. **80 kDa = 80 000 g/mol.** Moles = 0.024 g ÷ 80 000 g/mol = 3 × 10⁻⁷ mol = **0.3 μmol**.","how":{"ask":"Get the mass in grams, convert kDa to g/mol, then divide.","know":"• **mass = (mg/mL) × mL**, then mg ÷ 1000 = g.\n• **1 kDa = 1000 Da**, and Da per molecule = g/mol, so **kDa × 1000 = g/mol**.\n• **mol = g ÷ (g/mol)**; 10⁻⁶ mol = 1 μmol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the amount used: 1.5 mL at 16 mg/mL. Mass delivered = concentration × volume.","hl":"1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL","calc":["16 mg/mL × 1.5 mL = 24 mg","24 mg ÷ 1000 = 0.024 g"]},{"text":"Convert the size to a molar mass.","hl":"80 kDa","calc":["80 kDa × 1000 = 80 000 g/mol"]},{"text":"Divide grams by g/mol.","calc":["0.024 ÷ 80 000 = 3 × 10⁻⁷ mol","3 × 10⁻⁷ mol = **0.3 μmol**"]},{"text":"0.3 mmol skips the kDa conversion, 3 μmol uses 8000 g/mol, and 0.3 nmol divides by 1000 one time too many.","strike":["0.3 mmol","3 μmol","0.3 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.3 μmol."}],"trap":"Dividing by 80 instead of 80 000: kDa must become g/mol before you divide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.5 mL","note":"volume"},{"t":"16 mg/mL","note":"mass per volume"},{"t":"80 kDa","note":"molar mass, ×1000"}]},{"text":"**Mass from concentration.** A mg/mL concentration times a volume in mL gives mg directly.","calc":["16 × 1.5 = 24 mg = 2.4 × 10⁻² g"]},{"text":"**Moles from mass.** Divide by the molar mass in g/mol, after turning kDa into g/mol.","calc":["2.4 × 10⁻² g ÷ 8.0 × 10⁴ g/mol = 3.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** writing every quantity in powers of ten keeps the zeros honest: 10⁻² ÷ 10⁴ = 10⁻⁶, the micro range, as expected for milligrams of a large protein."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mol of protein = (mg/mL × mL ÷ 1000) ÷ (kDa × 1000)."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-37a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:37#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:37"],"passage":"To follow an 80 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"Where on the kinase is the labeled lysine most likely?","choices":["In the substrate cleft","On a distant surface loop","Inside the nonpolar core","Anywhere on the protein"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A tag on a loop far from the catalytic pocket leaves activity intact, as most surface labels do."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine is charged and faces water; a dye in buffer can't reach the buried core while the kinase stays folded."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Most surface labels leave activity untouched, so a total loss points to one special spot, not a random one."}],"rule":"One small label that kills activity but not folding must sit where the protein does its work; buried sites are out of reach.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Still folded yet fully dead: the tag must block function directly, so it sits in the substrate cleft. A distant loop wouldn't matter; the core is unreachable."},"exp":"A dye in water reaches exposed residues. A tag on an exposed patch **far from the catalytic pocket** leaves activity unchanged. A tag that **abolishes activity while the fold is intact** must sit where substrate binds, physically blocking it.","how":{"ask":"Activity is lost but the fold is intact: which spot must a bulky dye be blocking?","know":"• A water-soluble reagent labels **exposed** residues; buried ones are out of reach while the protein is folded.\n• Exposed sites far from the catalytic pocket: label tolerated, activity unchanged.\n• Label in or at the mouth of the catalytic pocket: substrate blocked, activity lost.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives two clues: the labeled kinase stays folded, yet all activity is gone.","hl":"stays folded but loses all activity"},{"text":"Folded means the core is closed to the dye, and a charged lysine sits at the surface anyway.","hl":"NHS-ester dye","strike":["Inside the nonpolar core"]},{"text":"A tag far from the catalytic pocket would leave activity intact, and most surface sites are like that.","strike":["On a distant surface loop","Anywhere on the protein"]},{"text":"Answer: in the substrate cleft, where the dye blocks substrate binding."}],"trap":"Assuming any label disturbs an enzyme; most surface labels are harmless, so a total loss of activity pinpoints the catalytic pocket."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"single lysine","note":"one exposed Lys tagged"},{"t":"stays folded","note":"core still closed"},{"t":"loses all activity","note":"catalytic pocket hit"}]},{"text":"**Where can a dye in water reach?** Only exposed residues; a folded protein keeps its core shut."},{"text":"**Activity lost + fold intact** → the dye sits where substrate binds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a water-borne label reaches only exposed residues; unchanged activity puts it far from the catalytic pocket, and lost activity with an intact fold puts it in the pocket."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-38a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:38#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:38"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"How many moles of chloride ion does each test cell receive from the stock?","choices":["21 μmol","42 μmol","42 mmol","42 nmol"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"mM × mL gives μmol, not mmol: (10⁻³ mol/L)(10⁻³ L) = 10⁻⁶ mol."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That applies one 10⁻³ too many; only the mM and the mL each carry a factor of 10⁻³."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the MgCl₂ itself; each formula unit releases two Cl⁻, so the ×2 was skipped."}],"rule":"mM × mL = μmol; then multiply by the ions per formula unit, read from the formula’s subscript.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mM × mL = μmol: 3.5 × 6.0 = 21 μmol MgCl₂. Two Cl⁻ per formula unit → twice that."},"exp":"**mM × mL = μmol**: 3.5 mM × 6.0 mL = 21 μmol of MgCl₂. Each **MgCl₂ gives 2 Cl⁻**, so n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 21 = **42 μmol** (4.2 × 10⁻⁵ mol).","how":{"ask":"Multiply concentration by volume with matched prefixes, then count chlorides per formula unit.","know":"• **n = M × V.** Prefix pairs: **M × L = mol**, **M × mL = mmol**, **mM × mL = μmol**.\n• Ions per formula unit come from the subscripts: MgCl₂ → 1 Mg²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the clue: each cell gets 6.0 mL of a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock. Multiply concentration by volume, keeping the prefixes.","hl":"3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock","calc":["3.5 mM × 6.0 mL = 21 μmol MgCl₂","check: 3.5 × 10⁻³ mol/L × 6.0 × 10⁻³ L = 2.1 × 10⁻⁵ mol"]},{"text":"Count chloride ions: the subscript 2 in MgCl₂ means two Cl⁻ per formula unit.","hl":"MgCl₂","calc":["2 × 21 μmol = **42 μmol Cl⁻**"]},{"text":"21 μmol is the salt, not the chloride; 42 mmol and 42 nmol misplace a factor of 1000.","strike":["21 μmol","42 mmol","42 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 42 μmol."}],"trap":"Writing mM × mL as mmol: two milli factors make micro."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"6.0 mL","note":"volume, 10⁻³ L"},{"t":"3.5 mM","note":"10⁻³ mol/L"},{"t":"chloride ion","note":"two per MgCl₂"}]},{"text":"**Milli × milli = micro.** 10⁻³ × 10⁻³ = 10⁻⁶, so the product of mM and mL comes out in μmol.","calc":["3.5 × 6.0 = 21 → 21 μmol"]},{"text":"**Ions per unit.** Dissolving one MgCl₂ gives one Mg²⁺ and two Cl⁻.","calc":["n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 21 μmol = 42 μmol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = M × V with matched prefixes (mM × mL = μmol, M × mL = mmol), then multiply by the ions per formula unit."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-39a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:39#1","pe6-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:39"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"Approximately how much iron does one electrode’s coating contain?","choices":["1.7 mg","5.2 mg","7.2 mg","8.7 mg"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That counts one Fe per formula unit; Fe₃O₄ holds three iron atoms, so the fraction uses 3 × 55.8."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"7.2 mg is the mass of magnetite in the coating; part of that oxide is oxygen, so the iron must weigh less."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That skips the 60%: it treats the whole 12.0 mg coating as magnetite."}],"rule":"Chain the fractions: coating mass × % mineral × (Fe mass in formula ÷ formula mass). Round each fraction, then multiply.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"Magnetite = 0.6 × 12 = 7.2 mg. Fe fraction ≈ 168/232 ≈ 0.7, so iron ≈ 0.7 × 7.2 ≈ 5 mg (7.2 would be all of the oxide)."},"exp":"Magnetite in the coating: 0.60 × 12.0 mg = 7.2 mg. **Fe₃O₄ = 3(55.8) + 4(16.0) ≈ 231 g/mol**, and iron’s mass fraction is **167 ÷ 231 ≈ 0.72**. Iron = 0.72 × 7.2 mg ≈ **5.2 mg**.","how":{"ask":"Find the oxide's mass, then the iron fraction of the oxide, and multiply.","know":"• **Mass fraction of an element = (atoms of it × atomic mass) ÷ formula mass.**\n• Mass of the element = fraction × mass of the compound.\n• In a mixture, first find the compound's own mass: % × total.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the clue: 12.0 mg of coating, 60% of it magnetite. First find the oxide’s own mass in the mixture.","hl":"12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite","calc":["0.60 × 12.0 mg = 7.2 mg Fe₃O₄"]},{"text":"Formula mass and iron’s share, from the atomic masses given.","hl":"Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol","calc":["3 × 55.8 ≈ 167; 4 × 16.0 = 64","167 + 64 ≈ 231 g/mol","167 ÷ 231 ≈ 0.72"]},{"text":"Multiply the fraction by the oxide’s mass.","calc":["0.72 × 7.2 mg ≈ **5.2 mg Fe**"]},{"text":"7.2 mg stops at the oxide, 8.7 mg skips the 60%, and 1.7 mg counts only one iron atom.","strike":["7.2 mg","8.7 mg","1.7 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.2 mg."}],"trap":"Counting one Fe atom per formula unit: the subscript 3 multiplies the iron's mass."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"12.0 mg","note":"whole coating"},{"t":"60% magnetite","note":"share that is oxide"},{"t":"Fe₃O₄","note":"three Fe per unit"}]},{"text":"**Peel the layers from outside in.** Coating → oxide (60%) → iron (its mass fraction in the oxide).","calc":["mass Fe = total × %oxide × (n·M_Fe ÷ M_oxide)"]},{"text":"**Mass fraction of the metal.** Count the metal atoms, multiply by the atomic mass, and divide by the formula mass.","calc":["(3 × 55.8) ÷ 231 ≈ 167 ÷ 231 ≈ 0.72"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** element mass = (atoms × atomic mass ÷ formula mass) × compound mass, after taking the compound’s % of any mixture."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-40a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:40"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"What is the most charge one electrode’s coating can deliver?","choices":["0.83 C","3.0 C","10.8 C","1.08 × 10⁴ C"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Treats 12.0 mg as 12.0 g; convert milligrams to grams before multiplying by the capacity per gram."},{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides by the mAh-to-coulomb factor instead of multiplying: one mAh is more than one coulomb."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the charge in mAh; one more step converts milliamp-hours to coulombs."}],"rule":"Charge = capacity per gram × mass in grams, then mAh → C using 1 mAh = (10⁻³ A)(3600 s).","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"0.012 g × 250 mAh/g = 3.0 mAh. 1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C, so multiply 3.0 by 3.6."},"exp":"Charge stored = **capacity × mass** = 250 mAh/g × 0.0120 g = **3.0 mAh**. One mAh is 10⁻³ A for 3600 s = **3.6 C**, so the electrode can deliver 3.0 × 3.6 = **10.8 C**.","how":{"ask":"How many mAh does the coating hold, and how many coulombs is one mAh?","know":"• Q = I × t: 1 A for 1 s = 1 C.\n• 1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C.\n• Specific capacity (mAh/g) × mass (g) = total capacity (mAh).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, and the sentence before gives 12.0 mg of coating. Convert the mass and multiply by the capacity.","hl":"stores 250 mAh per gram","strike":["1.08 × 10⁴ C"],"calc":["12.0 mg = 0.0120 g","0.0120 g × 250 mAh/g = 3.0 mAh"]},{"text":"Build the mAh-to-coulomb factor from its definition.","hl":"one milliamp flowing for one hour","calc":["1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C"]},{"text":"Multiply, do not divide, and do not stop at mAh.","strike":["0.83 C","3.0 C"],"calc":["3.0 mAh × 3.6 C/mAh = **10.8 C**"]},{"text":"Answer: 10.8 C."}],"trap":"Stopping at 3.0 because the units look finished: mAh is a unit of charge, but not coulombs."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"12.0 mg","note":"0.0120 g"},{"t":"250 mAh per gram","note":"specific capacity"},{"t":"one milliamp flowing for one hour","note":"define mAh"}]},{"text":"**Charge = current × time.**","calc":["1 mAh = 0.001 A × 3600 s = 3.6 C","1 Ah = 3600 C"]},{"text":"**Capacity × mass.** A rating per gram times the grams present gives the total."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** total charge = specific capacity × mass (in grams), then convert milliamp-hours to coulombs with 1 mAh = 3.6 C."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-41a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:41#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:41"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"Based on the passage, E° for the aluminum half-reaction, written as a reduction, is:","choices":["+2.70 V","+3.10 V","−2.30 V","−0.40 V"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Adds the two potentials; the cell potential must be subtracted from the cathode’s, not added."},{"c":0,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the whole-cell potential as if it were one electrode’s; the cathode’s share must be taken out."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the cathode value with a minus sign; the cell potential has not been used at all."}],"rule":"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, so E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell; a cell voltage larger than the cathode’s means a negative anode potential.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell = 0.40 − 2.70 = −2.30 V."},"exp":"With both half-reactions written as reductions, the **cell potential equals the cathode’s reduction potential minus the anode’s**. So the anode’s value is the cathode’s minus the cell’s: +0.40 V − 2.70 V = **−2.30 V**. The strongly negative value fits aluminum, an active metal that is easily oxidized.","how":{"ask":"Which formula links the cell potential to the two reduction potentials, and what does it give for the anode?","know":"• **E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode** (both as reduction potentials).\n• A spontaneous (galvanic) cell has E°cell > 0.\n• Active metals used as anodes (Li, Mg, Al, Zn) have negative reduction potentials.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives both numbers: the cathode’s E°red = +0.40 V and E°cell = 2.70 V. Write the cell relation and solve for the anode.","hl":"E°cell = 2.70 V","calc":["E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell"]},{"text":"Substitute the passage values.","hl":"+0.40 V","calc":["0.40 − 2.70 = **−2.30 V**"]},{"text":"+3.10 V adds instead of subtracting, +2.70 V is the whole cell’s value, and −0.40 V ignores the cell potential.","strike":["+2.70 V","+3.10 V","−0.40 V"]},{"text":"Answer: −2.30 V."}],"trap":"Using E°cell = E°cathode + E°anode, which treats the anode value as an oxidation potential; with reduction potentials, subtract."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"+0.40 V","note":"E°cathode (reduction)"},{"t":"2.70 V","note":"E°cell"},{"t":"written as a reduction","note":"E°anode as reduction"}]},{"text":"**One formula.**","calc":["E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode","→ E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell"]},{"text":"**Sense check.** A big cell voltage with a modest cathode needs a very negative anode: an active metal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with both half-cells as reduction potentials, E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, so the anode’s potential is the cathode’s minus the cell’s."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-42a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:42"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"Which form of free aspartate, shown, dominates at pH 1.2?","choices":["Cation (net +1)","Zwitterion (net 0)","Anion (net −1)","Dianion (net −2)"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-asp-plus1","cpt-pe6-asp-net0","cpt-pe6-asp-minus1","cpt-pe6-asp-minus2"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Net 0 needs the α-COOH ionized, at pH above 1.9; at pH 1.2 it still holds its H⁺."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"−1 has both carboxyls ionized, the pH 7 form that grips Ca²⁺; pH 1.2 is below both carboxyl pKa values."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"−2 needs the amine deprotonated, at pH above 9.6; at pH 1.2 the amine is NH₃⁺."}],"rule":"Below a group's pKa it keeps its H⁺: compare the pH with each pKa, then add up the charges.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"pH below every pKa → every group keeps its proton. Carboxyls neutral, amine +1 → net +1, the cation."},"exp":"pH 1.2 is **below every pKa**, so every group keeps its H⁺: α-COOH (0), side-chain COOH (0), α-NH₃⁺ (+1) → **net +1**. With its side chain as neutral –COOH instead of –COO⁻, aspartate can no longer hold Ca²⁺, so the ion is released.","how":{"ask":"For each group, is pH 1.2 above or below its pKa? Then add up the charges.","know":"• **pH < pKa → protonated** (COOH neutral, NH₃⁺ +1); pH > pKa → deprotonated (COO⁻ −1, NH₂ neutral).\n• Metal ions are held by negative **–COO⁻**; protonating it to –COOH releases the metal.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the stripping pH, 1.2, and the three pKa values. Compare 1.2 with each pKa.","hl":"pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6","calc":["α-COOH: 1.2 < 1.9 → COOH (0)","side-chain COOH: 1.2 < 3.7 → COOH (0)","α-NH₃⁺: 1.2 < 9.6 → NH₃⁺ (+1)","net = 0 + 0 + 1 = **+1**"]},{"text":"The matching drawing has two COOH groups and NH₃⁺.","fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-plus1"},{"text":"Ionized carboxyls need pH above their pKa values (1.9 and 3.7), and a neutral amine needs pH above 9.6.","strike":["Zwitterion (net 0)","Anion (net −1)","Dianion (net −2)"]},{"text":"Answer: the cation, net charge +1; the side chain is neutral –COOH and lets go of Ca²⁺."}],"trap":"Stopping after the side chain: check the α-COOH too. pH 1.2 is even below its pKa of 1.9, so the zwitterion is not the main form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hold Ca²⁺","note":"held by –COO⁻"},{"t":"the calcium is released","note":"–COO⁻ picks up H⁺"},{"t":"pH 1.2","note":"very acidic"},{"t":"pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6","note":"compare each"}]},{"text":"**Carboxylates bind metal ions.** At pH 7 the side chain is –COO⁻, and its negative oxygens grip Ca²⁺.","fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-minus1"},{"text":"**Lower the pH below the side chain's pKa** and –COO⁻ picks up H⁺; the neutral –COOH cannot hold the metal, so the ion is released."},{"text":"**Then check every group** against its own pKa and add the charges.","calc":["below pKa → H⁺ on","above pKa → H⁺ off"],"fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-plus1"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** metal-binding carboxylates release the metal once pH falls below their pKa; to find the dominant form, compare pH with each group's pKa and add the charges."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-43a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:43#1","pe6-cp:43#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:43"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"What is the main job of the paper sheet itself?","choices":["Separates the electrodes","Conducts electrons through the cell","Stores the cell’s charge","Reacts at the cathode"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Electrons take the outside wire; inside the cell, the soaked paper carries ions, not electrons."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The energy is stored chemically in the aluminum and the O₂ it reacts with; the paper holds no reactant."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Paper is inert here; the reaction at the cathode is the reduction of O₂."}],"rule":"In a cell, the electrodes react, the electrolyte carries ions between them, the external wire carries electrons, and a separator keeps the electrodes from touching.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tear it and the cell shorts out internally: the electrodes touch. So the sheet’s job is to keep them apart while its KOH lets ions pass."},"exp":"The porous sheet is the **separator**. It keeps the aluminum and the carbon cathode from touching, so electrons must take the **external circuit**, while the **KOH solution** soaked into it carries **OH⁻ and K⁺ ions** between the electrodes. If it tears, the electrodes touch, electrons flow straight across inside the cell (an internal short), and the cell heats and drains.","how":{"ask":"What goes wrong when the sheet tears, and which job does that reveal?","know":"• **Electrolyte**: carries ions between the electrodes (completes the internal circuit).\n• **External wire**: carries electrons from anode to cathode.\n• **Separator**: porous, electrically insulating; keeps electrodes apart but lets ions through.\n• Electrodes: where oxidation (anode) and reduction (cathode) happen.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says a torn sheet makes the cell heat and drain with nothing connected: the electrodes touch and short out.","hl":"If the paper tears"},{"text":"So the sheet keeps the electrodes apart; its soaked-in KOH carries ions between them.","hl":"soaked in concentrated KOH"},{"text":"Electrons go through the external circuit, the energy is stored in the aluminum and O₂, and the cathode reaction is the reduction of O₂.","strike":["Conducts electrons through the cell","Stores the cell’s charge","Reacts at the cathode"]},{"text":"Answer: it separates the electrodes."}],"trap":"Thinking electrons cross the inside of the cell: if they could, the cell would short out; they must go around through the wire."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"porous paper","note":"lets ions through"},{"t":"soaked in concentrated KOH","note":"the electrolyte"},{"t":"external circuit","note":"electron path"},{"t":"If the paper tears","note":"electrodes touch"}]},{"text":"**Four parts, four jobs.**","calc":["anode: oxidized","cathode: reduced","electrolyte: carries ions","wire: carries electrons"]},{"text":"**Why separate?** If the electrodes touched, electrons would flow directly between them, and no current would reach the outside circuit."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the solution between a cell’s electrodes is the electrolyte and carries ions, electrons travel only through the external wire, and a porous separator keeps the electrodes from touching."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-44a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:44#1","pe6-cp:44#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:44"],"q":"The four lipids shown were all extracted from human skin. Which one is NOT a structural lipid of cell membranes?","choices":["Sphingomyelin","Galactosyl ceramide","Cetyl palmitate","Phosphatidylinositol"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-sphingomyelin","cpt-pe6-galactocerebroside","cpt-pe6-cetyl-palmitate","cpt-pe6-phosphatidylinositol"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Sphingomyelin is a sphingolipid with a phosphocholine head: a classic membrane lipid, rich in the outer leaflet."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Galactosyl ceramide is sphingosine, one acyl chain and a sugar head: a glycosphingolipid of membranes."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphatidylinositol is glycerol + two acyl chains + a phosphate–inositol head: a glycerophospholipid, so it sits in membranes."}],"rule":"Membrane lipids are amphipathic, with a polar head (phosphate or sugar) on a glycerol or sphingosine backbone; a wax ester has no head.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name pattern: ‘-yl -ate’ (alcohol + fatty acid ester, e.g. cetyl palmitate) is a wax with no polar head, so no membrane role. Phospho- and sphingo- lipids are membrane lipids."},"exp":"Membrane lipids carry a polar head: **glycerophospholipids** (phosphatidylinositol), **sphingolipids** (sphingomyelin, galactosyl ceramide) and **sterols** (cholesterol). **Cetyl palmitate** is a wax: a fatty acid esterified to a long alcohol, with no polar head. Like storage fat, it is not built into membranes; it waterproofs the skin surface.","how":{"ask":"Which lipid lacks the polar head that every membrane lipid needs?","know":"• **Membrane lipids:** glycerophospholipids (PE, PS, PI), sphingolipids (sphingomyelin, cerebrosides, gangliosides) and sterols (cholesterol).\n• **Not membrane lipids:** neutral storage fats, free fatty acids (fuel), waxes (protective coatings).\n• Quick test: no polar head group → not a membrane lipid.","steps":[{"text":"Sort each lipid by its backbone and its head group.","hl":"NOT a structural lipid"},{"text":"Phosphatidylinositol: glycerol, two acyl chains and a phosphate–inositol head, so a glycerophospholipid.","strike":["Phosphatidylinositol"]},{"text":"Sphingomyelin and galactosyl ceramide: sphingosine, one acyl chain and a polar head (phosphocholine or galactose), so sphingolipids.","strike":["Sphingomyelin","Galactosyl ceramide"]},{"text":"Cetyl palmitate: palmitic acid esterified to a C16 alcohol. Two oily chains and no head: a wax.","calc":["C₁₅H₃₁–CO–O–C₁₆H₃₃: no phosphate, no sugar, no free OH"]},{"text":"Answer: cetyl palmitate."}],"trap":"Rejecting a sphingolipid because it has no glycerol; membranes are built from glycerol-based and sphingosine-based lipids alike."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NOT a structural lipid","note":"find the odd one out"},{"t":"cell membranes","note":"bilayer lipids have polar heads"}]},{"text":"**Glycerophospholipids:** glycerol, two fatty acyl chains, a phosphate and a head group (ethanolamine, serine, inositol…). The bulk of every membrane."},{"text":"**Sphingolipids:** built on sphingosine instead of glycerol. Sphingomyelin carries phosphocholine; a cerebroside carries one sugar. Both are membrane lipids."},{"text":"**Sterols:** cholesterol's four fused rings and single OH tuck between phospholipids and tune fluidity, so it is a membrane lipid too."},{"text":"**Also:** test every choice for a polar head: a lipid made only of acyl chains on a backbone, with no phosphate, sugar or OH head (a neutral storage fat or a wax), is not a membrane lipid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a membrane lipid has a polar head on an oily body: phospholipid, sphingolipid or sterol. Head-less lipids (fat, wax) store energy or waterproof."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-44b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:44#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:44"],"q":"Which lipid shown, mixed alone with water, forms micelles rather than a bilayer?","choices":["SDS","Trimyristin","Phosphatidylserine","Ergosterol"],"choiceFigs":["sodium-dodecyl-sulfate","cpt-pe6-trimyristin","cpt-pe6-phosphatidylserine","cpt-pe6-ergosterol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Trimyristin's three acyl chains carry no polar head, so in water it just separates as oil droplets."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphatidylserine has two tails under one head: a cylinder that packs into flat bilayers, not micelles."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ergosterol is fused rings with one small OH: too little head to assemble on its own."}],"rule":"Shape decides: one tail under a big head (a cone) → micelle; two tails (a cylinder) → bilayer; no head → oil.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Shape rule: one tail + big head = cone → micelle; two tails = cylinder → bilayer. SDS is the single-tail detergent."},"exp":"Assembly follows shape. **SDS** (sodium dodecyl sulfate) has **one tail under a big charged sulfate head**: a **cone**, and cones pack into spheres, so it forms **micelles**. Phosphatidylserine (two tails + head) is a cylinder and forms bilayers; trimyristin has no head and separates as droplets; ergosterol's tiny OH cannot organize it alone.","how":{"ask":"Which molecule has one tail under a big polar head, the cone shape that packs into a sphere?","know":"• **Shape decides the assembly:** one tail + big head = cone → **micelle**; two tails + head = cylinder → **bilayer**; no head → oil droplet.\n• Detergents and fatty acids form micelles; phospholipids and sphingolipids form bilayers.\n• Sterols and triacylglycerols cannot assemble alone.","steps":[{"text":"For each molecule, look for a polar head and count the oily tails.","hl":"forms micelles rather than a bilayer"},{"text":"Phosphatidylserine: two acyl tails under a charged head, a cylinder, so it forms a bilayer, not micelles.","calc":["head width ≈ 2 tails → flat sheet → bilayer"],"strike":["Phosphatidylserine"]},{"text":"Trimyristin: three tails, no head, so an oil droplet. Ergosterol: fused rings with one small OH, too little head to assemble alone.","strike":["Trimyristin","Ergosterol"]},{"text":"SDS: one C12 tail on a big sulfate head, a cone.","calc":["wide head + 1 tail → cone → packs into a sphere: micelle"]},{"text":"Answer: SDS (sodium dodecyl sulfate)."}],"trap":"Choosing phosphatidylserine because it is the most familiar membrane lipid; its two tails make a cylinder, which builds bilayers, not micelles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"micelles","note":"one-tailed cones"},{"t":"rather than a bilayer","note":"two tails would give a sheet"},{"t":"alone with water","note":"it must work alone"}]},{"text":"**Amphipathic first.** A lipid needs a water-loving head and oily tails to organize in water at all; trimyristin has no head."},{"text":"**Then count tails.** One tail (fatty acid, SDS) = cone → micelle. Two-tailed glycerophospholipids such as phosphatidylserine (and sphingolipids) are cylinders → bilayer."},{"text":"**Sterols need partners.** Ergosterol and cholesterol slip between phospholipids but cannot assemble on their own."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count tails under a polar head: one tail (cone) → micelle; two tails (cylinder) → bilayer; no head → oil."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-45a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:45#4","pe6-cp:45#1","pe6-cp:45#2","pe6-cp:45#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:45"],"q":"The deoxynucleotide dCTP is shown. How many carbon atoms does one molecule of dCTP contain?","fig":"cpt-pe6-dctp","choices":["8","9","11","12"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Deoxyribose keeps all 5 carbons; only the 2′ OH is gone, so no carbon is lost from the sugar."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Cytosine's ring has 6 atoms, but 2 of them are N; counting every ring atom as carbon gives 5 + 6 = 11."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphates hold P and O but no carbon; adding one C per phosphate is the trap."}],"rule":"Count by part: sugar (deoxyribose, 5 C) + only the carbons of the base ring (not N) + phosphates (no C).","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count by part: sugar 5 C + cytosine 4 C + phosphates 0 C = 9. Deoxy removes an O, never a carbon."},"exp":"Count by parts: **deoxyribose 5 C** (deoxy = one O missing, not a C) + **cytosine 4 C** + **three phosphates 0 C** = **9 C**.","how":{"ask":"Add the carbons of the sugar, the base and the phosphates.","know":"• **Sugar:** ribose and deoxyribose are both 5-carbon pentoses (C1′–C5′); 'deoxy' means one O fewer at C2′.\n• **Bases:** adenine, guanine and thymine have 5 C each; cytosine and uracil have 4 C.\n• **Phosphate:** PO₄ has no carbon, so one, two or three of them add 0 C.","steps":[{"text":"Split dCTP into its three parts: sugar, base, phosphates.","hl":"dCTP is shown"},{"text":"Sugar: deoxyribose keeps 5 carbons; only the 2′ OH is gone.","calc":["deoxyribose: 5 C"],"strike":["8"]},{"text":"Base: cytosine's ring holds 4 C and 2 N, with an NH₂ and a C=O attached.","calc":["cytosine: 4 C, 3 N"],"strike":["11"]},{"text":"Phosphates: three PO₄ groups, no carbon.","calc":["3 × 0 = 0 C","5 + 4 + 0 = 9 C"],"strike":["12"]},{"text":"Answer: 9 carbon atoms."}],"trap":"Counting deoxyribose as 4 carbons because 'deoxy' sounds like something was removed; it lost an oxygen, not a carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"deoxynucleotide","note":"sugar + base + phosphate(s)"},{"t":"carbon atoms","note":"count C only"}]},{"text":"**Also: the sugar.** Deoxyribose has 5 C, the same as ribose; deoxy only means no O on C2′.","calc":["deoxyribose C₅H₁₀O₄: 5 C"]},{"text":"**Also: the bases.** Adenine and guanine each have 5 C (a six-ring fused to a five-ring that share 2 C); thymine has 5 C (4 ring C + a methyl); cytosine has 4 C."},{"text":"**Also: the phosphates** have no carbon, so dCMP, dCDP and dCTP all have the same 9 C."},{"text":"**Also: purines vs pyrimidines.** Adenine and guanine bring 5 C, one more than cytosine, so a purine nucleotide has one carbon more than the matching cytosine nucleotide."},{"text":"**Also:** count N the same way, by parts: sugar and phosphate have none, so every N sits in the base; dCTP has cytosine's 3 N."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** atoms in a nucleotide = sugar + base + phosphate; sugar 5 C, adenine, guanine and thymine 5 C, cytosine and uracil 4 C, phosphate 0 C."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-46a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:46"],"q":"The walls of the atria are much thinner than the walls of the ventricles. Which feature of the atria best accounts for this difference?","choices":["They work against low pressure","They move a bigger stroke volume","They receive venous return","They hold oxygen-poor blood"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Atria pass on less blood per beat than the ventricles eject, and a larger volume would not explain thinner walls."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"True, but receiving blood takes no force; wall thickness tracks the pressure a chamber must generate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Only the right atrium does; the left atrium holds oxygen-rich blood and is just as thin."}],"rule":"Heart wall thickness tracks the pressure a chamber must generate: left ventricle (systemic) > right ventricle (lungs) > atria.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Muscle mass follows workload = pressure. Atria only top up the ventricles next door, so they need little force and little muscle."},"exp":"Muscle builds to match the **pressure a chamber must generate**. The atria only push blood a short way into the relaxed ventricles below them, at a few mmHg, so their walls are thin. The **left ventricle** must drive blood through the whole **systemic circulation** (about 120 mmHg) and is thickest; the **right ventricle** pumps only to the lungs (about 25 mmHg) and is in between.","how":{"ask":"What sets how much muscle a heart chamber needs?","know":"• Wall thickness ∝ the pressure a chamber must generate (Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure).\n• LV → systemic circuit, high resistance, ~120 mmHg; RV → pulmonary circuit, ~25 mmHg; atria → ventricles, a few mmHg.\n• Both sides pump the same volume per beat.","steps":[{"text":"Wall thickness follows pressure, not volume or blood type.","hl":"much thinner"},{"text":"Atria fill the adjacent ventricles at low pressure; no high-resistance circuit lies ahead of them.","calc":["atria: a few mmHg","RV: ~25 mmHg","LV: ~120 mmHg"]},{"text":"Stroke volume is not larger in the atria, receiving blood takes no force, and the left atrium carries oxygen-rich blood.","strike":["They move a bigger stroke volume","They receive venous return","They hold oxygen-poor blood"]},{"text":"Also: the same rule explains why the left ventricle has a thicker wall than the right: it must overcome systemic resistance, while the right pumps only to the lungs."},{"text":"Answer: they work against low pressure."}],"trap":"Linking wall thickness to blood volume: both ventricles move the same volume each beat, yet their walls differ because their pressures differ."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"atria","note":"fill the ventricles"},{"t":"much thinner","note":"less force needed"},{"t":"ventricles","note":"pump into circuits"}]},{"text":"**Pressure sets muscle.**","calc":["LV: systemic, highest","RV: pulmonary, lower","atria: lowest"]},{"text":"**Same volume, different pressure.** Left and right ventricles eject equal volumes; only the resistance ahead of them differs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a heart chamber’s wall is as thick as the pressure it must generate: the left ventricle, which must drive blood around the whole body, is thickest; the atria, which only fill the ventricles, are thinnest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-47a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:47#1","pe6-cp:47#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:47"],"q":"A beam of ground-state nitrogen atoms (1s² 2s² 2p³) passes through a nonuniform magnetic field. What is each atom's total electron spin, and how does it behave?","choices":["7/2; paramagnetic","½; paramagnetic","3/2; diamagnetic","3/2; paramagnetic"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"7/2 needs all seven spins parallel, but Pauli forces the two electrons in each of 1s and 2s to pair with opposite spins."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"½ pairs two of the 2p electrons; Hund's rule puts one electron in each 2p orbital before any pairing."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Diamagnetic means every electron is paired; any unpaired electron makes an atom paramagnetic."}],"rule":"Count only unpaired electrons, ½ spin each; Hund's rule fills p orbitals one electron each first. Any unpaired electron → paramagnetic.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Paired electrons cancel; Hund's rule leaves N's three 2p electrons unpaired: 3 × ½ = 3/2. Any unpaired electron → paramagnetic."},"exp":"By the **Pauli principle** the 1s and 2s pairs have **opposite spins** and cancel. **Hund's rule** spreads the three 2p electrons over the three 2p orbitals, all spin-up, so the total spin is **3 × ½ = 3/2**. Unpaired electrons make nitrogen **paramagnetic**: drawn toward the stronger field.","how":{"ask":"Pair the electrons orbital by orbital, add the leftover spins, then classify the magnetism.","know":"• **Pauli exclusion**: two electrons in one orbital must have opposite spins (+½ and −½), so a full orbital contributes **0**.\n• **Hund's rule**: in a partly filled subshell, electrons occupy separate orbitals with parallel spins first.\n• **Unpaired electrons → paramagnetic** (attracted); **all paired → diamagnetic** (weakly repelled).","steps":[{"text":"Read the configuration.","hl":"1s² 2s² 2p³","calc":["1s: 2 electrons, 2s: 2 electrons","2p: 3 electrons in 3 orbitals"]},{"text":"Pair the full orbitals, then add the 2p spins.","calc":["1s: +½ − ½ = 0; 2s: +½ − ½ = 0","2p: +½ +½ +½ = 3/2","total S = **3/2**"]},{"text":"Unpaired electrons mean the atom is paramagnetic.","strike":["3/2; diamagnetic"]},{"text":"7/2 ignores Pauli pairing; ½ pairs two 2p electrons against Hund's rule.","strike":["7/2; paramagnetic","½; paramagnetic"]},{"text":"Answer: 3/2; paramagnetic."}],"trap":"Adding ½ for every electron: paired electrons in the same orbital always cancel."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ground-state nitrogen atoms","note":"lowest-energy filling"},{"t":"1s² 2s² 2p³","note":"two full orbitals, a half-full p set"},{"t":"nonuniform magnetic field","note":"tests para- vs diamagnetism"}]},{"text":"**Pauli in one line.** No two electrons in an atom share all four quantum numbers, so two electrons in the same orbital must have opposite spins.","calc":["full orbital: +½ + (−½) = 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** an atom whose subshells are all filled, such as Be (1s² 2s²) or Ne, has total spin 0, so it is diamagnetic and is weakly pushed out of a strong field."},{"text":"**Also:** any unpaired electron (H, Li, N) leaves net spin, so the atom is paramagnetic and is drawn into the field."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** paired electrons cancel (Pauli); count only unpaired ones for the spin, and any unpaired electron means paramagnetic."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-48a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:48#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:48"],"passage":"A detergent maker is engineering a small starch-binding module that anchors its wash enzymes to food stains. In the binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the face of a glucose ring, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows release, so the engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position. To help the module grip greasy stains, a variant was made with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines, leaving its pocket and buried core unchanged. The wash also contains an acidic booster, a white solid that dissolves to give Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers and, at 0.20 M, a pH of about 1.5. Binding is followed by NMR: in the spectrometer’s 11.7 T magnet, ¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz and ¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz, and absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s). Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B.","q":"Which side chain at the tyrosine position best meets the engineers’ goal?","choices":["Phe","Ser","Leu","His"],"choiceFigs":["phenylalanine-sc","serine-sc","leucine-sc","histidine-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Serine has no ring to stack on the sugar, and its O–H still donates a hydrogen bond."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Leucine's branched alkyl side chain has no flat aromatic ring, so the stacking is lost."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine's ring can stack, but its ring N–H still donates a hydrogen bond."}],"rule":"Keep stacking, drop donation: pick a flat aromatic ring that carries no N–H or O–H.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two filters: flat aromatic ring? (Phe, His pass) Any N–H or O–H? (His fails). The ring with no donor wins."},"exp":"The goal keeps one feature and drops another: a **flat aromatic ring** for stacking, but **no hydrogen-bond donor**. **Phenylalanine's benzyl ring** stacks and has no N–H or O–H. Serine has no ring and still donates, leucine has no flat ring, and histidine's ring N–H still donates.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain has a flat aromatic ring but no H on N or O?","know":"• **Stacking** needs a flat aromatic ring (Phe, Tyr, Trp, His).\n• **H-bond donors** have H on N or O (Ser, Thr, Tyr, Trp, His, Asn, Gln, Lys, Arg).\n• Removing one contact while keeping another means checking both features for every choice.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 states the goal: keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor. So the new side chain needs a flat aromatic ring and no N–H or O–H.","hl":"keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor"},{"text":"Serine has no ring; leucine's side chain is alkyl, not a flat aromatic ring.","strike":["Ser","Leu"]},{"text":"Histidine stacks, but its ring N–H is a donor.","strike":["His"]},{"text":"Phenylalanine: a flat ring with only C–H bonds.","calc":["ring ✓ + no donor ✓ → stacking kept, H-bond gone"]},{"text":"Answer: the aromatic ring with no donor."}],"trap":"Picking histidine because it is aromatic; its ring N–H still donates a hydrogen bond."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"stacks on the face of a glucose ring","note":"needs a flat aromatic ring"},{"t":"H-bonds to a ring oxygen","note":"the contact to remove"},{"t":"remove every hydrogen-bond donor","note":"no N–H or O–H"}]},{"text":"**List the jobs.** Keep: a flat aromatic ring. Remove: any H on N or O."},{"text":"**Score each candidate** against both filters; only one passes both.","calc":["ring: Phe ✓, His ✓, Ser ✗, Leu ✗","no donor: Phe ✓, His ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to change one contact and keep another, test every choice against both features: ring for stacking, H on N or O for donating."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P9"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-49a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:49#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:49"],"passage":"A detergent maker is engineering a small starch-binding module that anchors its wash enzymes to food stains. In the binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the face of a glucose ring, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows release, so the engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position. To help the module grip greasy stains, a variant was made with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines, leaving its pocket and buried core unchanged. The wash also contains an acidic booster, a white solid that dissolves to give Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers and, at 0.20 M, a pH of about 1.5. Binding is followed by NMR: in the spectrometer’s 11.7 T magnet, ¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz and ¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz, and absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s). Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B.","q":"In wash water, the variant module would most likely:","choices":["clump together in solution.","form extra disulfide bonds.","lose its catalytic activity.","bind more water molecules."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Val and Leu cannot H-bond, so the variant holds less water, not more."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Disulfides need two cysteine –SH groups; valine and leucine contain no sulfur."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Residues scattered over the surface are not the active site; catalysis comes from a few positioned groups in a pocket."}],"rule":"Swapping polar surface residues for nonpolar ones makes the surface hydrophobic; molecules then hide those patches against each other.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Polar OH on the surface → hydrophobic side chains on the surface: oily patches stick together, so the protein aggregates. Val/Leu add no S, so no disulfides."},"exp":"Surface Ser and Thr **–OH groups hydrogen-bond with water**, building the hydration shell around the protein. Swapping them for **hydrophobic** Val and Leu removes those H-bonds and exposes oily patches, which stick to one another: the variant **aggregates**.","how":{"ask":"What do surface –OH groups do with water, and what happens when oily side chains replace them?","know":"• Ser and Thr carry polar, uncharged –OH groups at pH 7: they donate and accept H-bonds with water.\n• Those H-bonds build a **hydration (solvation) shell** around the protein.\n• Exposed hydrophobic side chains (Val, Leu, Ile, Phe) pull together to avoid water → aggregation.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the swap: surface serines and threonines (polar –OH) replaced by valines and leucines (oily alkyl), all on the surface.","hl":"replaced by valines and leucines"},{"text":"No sulfur enters, so no new S–S bridges; and surface residues are not the catalytic pocket.","strike":["form extra disulfide bonds.","lose its catalytic activity."]},{"text":"Val and Leu cannot H-bond, so the variant holds less water, not more.","strike":["bind more water molecules."]},{"text":"Oily patches on neighboring molecules stick together (the hydrophobic effect).","calc":["fewer –OH···H₂O bonds → thinner water shell → molecules clump"]},{"text":"Answer: it clumps together in solution."}],"trap":"Assuming any big surface change must hit catalysis; the active site is a small pocket, while surface polarity governs how the protein mixes with water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"surface serines and threonines","note":"polar –OH groups"},{"t":"valines and leucines","note":"oily, no H-bonding"},{"t":"pocket and buried core unchanged","note":"activity site untouched"}]},{"text":"**What the –OH does.** Each Ser or Thr –OH hydrogen-bonds with water molecules, adding to the protein's hydration shell."},{"text":"**Charge check.** Ser and Thr are uncharged at pH 7 (their –OH pKa is about 16), so the swap changes polarity, not charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** polar surface groups (–OH, amides, charges) hold the water shell that keeps proteins dispersed; exposed hydrophobic groups make them aggregate."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P9"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-50a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:50#2","pe6-cp:50#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:50"],"passage":"A detergent maker is engineering a small starch-binding module that anchors its wash enzymes to food stains. In the binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the face of a glucose ring, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows release, so the engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position. To help the module grip greasy stains, a variant was made with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines, leaving its pocket and buried core unchanged. The wash also contains an acidic booster, a white solid that dissolves to give Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers and, at 0.20 M, a pH of about 1.5. Binding is followed by NMR: in the spectrometer’s 11.7 T magnet, ¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz and ¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz, and absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s). Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B.","q":"Which option names the booster correctly and identifies the ion that acidifies the water?","choices":["Sodium hydrogen sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺","Sodium hydrogen sulfate; Na⁺ donates H⁺","Sodium sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺","Sodium hydrogen sulfide; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Na⁺ comes from a strong base; it neither gives nor takes protons, so it leaves the pH alone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Sulfate is SO₄²⁻; the ion here still carries an H, and the name must show it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The -ide ending means sulfur with no oxygen (HS⁻); this ion contains four O atoms."}],"rule":"Name the ions first (-ate has oxygen, -ide has none, \"hydrogen\" marks a leftover H), then ask which ion can give H⁺ to water; Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻ and NO₃⁻ do nothing.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"HSO₄⁻ = hydrogen sulfate, an acid (it still has an H to give). Na⁺ is a spectator, so the anion is the proton donor."},"exp":"HSO₄⁻ is the **hydrogen sulfate** ion (sulfate, SO₄²⁻, with one H left on it), so the salt is **sodium hydrogen sulfate**. That anion still has an acidic proton, **HSO₄⁻ + H₂O ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺**, which makes the solution strongly acidic. Na⁺ is a spectator. The same logic explains ammonium chloride: there the acidic ion is the cation, **NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺**, while Cl⁻ is a spectator.","how":{"ask":"What is the correct name of HSO₄⁻, and which of the two ions can donate a proton to water?","know":"• Naming: -ate = oxyanion (SO₄²⁻ sulfate), -ide = no oxygen (S²⁻ sulfide), \"hydrogen\" = an H still attached (HSO₄⁻ hydrogen sulfate); NH₄⁺ = ammonium.\n• Ions of strong acids and bases (Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, NO₃⁻) do not change pH.\n• Acidic ions: NH₄⁺ (weak acid), HSO₄⁻ (fairly strong acid).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the solid gives Na⁺ and HSO₄⁻ and a very low pH. Name the anion: sulfate with one H attached is hydrogen sulfate, not sulfate and not sulfide.","hl":"HSO₄⁻ ions","strike":["Sodium sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺","Sodium hydrogen sulfide; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺"]},{"text":"Find the proton donor: HSO₄⁻ can give its H to water; Na⁺ cannot.","calc":["HSO₄⁻ + H₂O ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺"],"strike":["Sodium hydrogen sulfate; Na⁺ donates H⁺"]},{"text":"Also: in ammonium chloride the roles swap: NH₄⁺, a weak acid, gives the proton, and Cl⁻ is the spectator, so its solution is only slightly acidic.","calc":["NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: sodium hydrogen sulfate, with HSO₄⁻ as the proton donor."}],"trap":"Assuming a metal cation makes a solution acidic: Na⁺ is a spectator; look for an ion that still carries a removable H."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions","note":"name each ion"},{"t":"a pH of about 1.5","note":"acidic: one ion gives H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Name by the ion.**","calc":["NH₄⁺ ammonium","SO₄²⁻ sulfate","HSO₄⁻ hydrogen sulfate","S²⁻ sulfide"]},{"text":"**Who gives the proton?** Only an ion that still holds an acidic H (NH₄⁺, HSO₄⁻); ions of strong acids and bases are spectators."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to explain a salt’s pH, name each ion and ask which can donate or accept a proton; NH₄⁺ (as in ammonium chloride) and HSO₄⁻ are acids, while Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are spectators."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P9"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-51a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:51#1","pe6-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:51"],"passage":"A detergent maker is engineering a small starch-binding module that anchors its wash enzymes to food stains. In the binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the face of a glucose ring, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows release, so the engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position. To help the module grip greasy stains, a variant was made with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines, leaving its pocket and buried core unchanged. The wash also contains an acidic booster, a white solid that dissolves to give Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers and, at 0.20 M, a pH of about 1.5. Binding is followed by NMR: in the spectrometer’s 11.7 T magnet, ¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz and ¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz, and absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s). Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B.","q":"Each ¹H spin flip absorbs a photon with what energy?","choices":["3.3 × 10⁻²⁵ J","3.3 × 10⁻³¹ J","3.3 × 10⁻²⁸ J","1.3 × 10⁻⁴² J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Uses 500 Hz instead of 500 × 10⁶ Hz; convert MHz to hertz before multiplying."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Converts MHz with 10³ instead of 10⁶: mega means a million."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides h by f; a photon’s energy is h times f."}],"rule":"E = hf with f in hertz (MHz × 10⁶). Radio-frequency NMR photons carry roughly a millionth of a visible photon’s energy.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"500 MHz = 5 × 10⁸ Hz. 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ × 5 × 10⁸ = 33 × 10⁻²⁶, one power of ten shifted into the leading digits."},"exp":"A photon’s energy is **E = hf**, with f in hertz: 500 MHz = **5.0 × 10⁸ Hz**. Multiplying Planck’s constant by 500 million cycles per second gives **about 3.3 × 10⁻²⁵ J**. That tiny energy is typical of radio waves; visible photons carry about 10⁻¹⁹ J.","how":{"ask":"What is the frequency in hertz, and what is h times that frequency?","know":"• **E = hf** (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s); f must be in Hz.\n• Prefixes: k = 10³, M = 10⁶, G = 10⁹.\n• Scale check: radio (NMR) photons ~10⁻²⁵ J; visible ~10⁻¹⁹ J.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the frequency, 500 MHz for ¹H, and Planck’s constant. Convert the frequency to hertz.","hl":"¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz","strike":["3.3 × 10⁻³¹ J","3.3 × 10⁻²⁸ J"],"calc":["500 MHz = 500 × 10⁶ Hz = 5.0 × 10⁸ Hz"]},{"text":"Multiply by Planck’s constant: E = hf, not h ÷ f.","hl":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","strike":["1.3 × 10⁻⁴² J"],"calc":["6.6 × 5.0 = 33","10⁻³⁴ × 10⁸ = 10⁻²⁶","33 × 10⁻²⁶ = **3.3 × 10⁻²⁵ J**"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.3 × 10⁻²⁵ J."}],"trap":"Dropping the 10⁶ in MHz: the answer then lands at about 10⁻³¹ J, absurdly small even for radio waves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"500 MHz","note":"f = 5.0 × 10⁸ Hz"},{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"Planck’s constant"},{"t":"one radio-frequency photon","note":"E = hf"}]},{"text":"**One equation.**","calc":["E = hf","f in Hz, h in J·s → E in J"]},{"text":"**Order-of-magnitude check.** Radio photons are about a million times weaker than visible ones, so an NMR answer near 10⁻²⁵ J is right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** photon energy E = hf with the frequency converted to hertz; an NMR (radio) photon carries roughly 10⁻²⁵ J."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P9"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:52"],"passage":"A detergent maker is engineering a small starch-binding module that anchors its wash enzymes to food stains. In the binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the face of a glucose ring, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows release, so the engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position. To help the module grip greasy stains, a variant was made with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines, leaving its pocket and buried core unchanged. The wash also contains an acidic booster, a white solid that dissolves to give Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers and, at 0.20 M, a pH of about 1.5. Binding is followed by NMR: in the spectrometer’s 11.7 T magnet, ¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz and ¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz, and absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s). Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B.","q":"Using the passage’s data, what is γ for ¹³C?","choices":["6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)","1.1 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)","9.2 × 10⁹ rad/(s·T)","6.7 × 10¹ rad/(s·T)"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Leaves the frequency in MHz; convert to hertz (× 10⁶) before dividing by the field."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplies by B instead of dividing; γ is angular frequency per tesla."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Divides f by B but leaves out the factor of two pi that turns f into angular frequency."}],"rule":"Plug into the passage’s formula exactly as defined: convert MHz to Hz, keep every constant (2π), and divide by the field, not multiply.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"γ = 2πf/B = 6.28 × 1.25 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ 7.9 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ 6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)."},"exp":"From the passage, **γ = 2πf / B**. With f = 125 MHz = **1.25 × 10⁸ Hz** and B = 11.7 T: 2π × 1.25 × 10⁸ ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ rad/s, and 7.85 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ **6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)** (that is, γ/2π ≈ 10.7 MHz/T).","how":{"ask":"What does the passage’s definition give when the frequency (in Hz) and the field are plugged in?","know":"• Translate the words into a formula first: γ = (2π × f) ÷ B.\n• Convert MHz to Hz; tesla stays as given.\n• Units follow the formula: rad/s per T.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 defines γ in words: two pi times the resonance frequency f, divided by the field B. Turn the definition into a formula.","hl":"two pi times the resonance frequency f","calc":["γ = 2πf / B"]},{"text":"Convert and substitute.","hl":"11.7 T magnet","calc":["f = 125 MHz = 1.25 × 10⁸ Hz","2πf ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ rad/s","γ ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ **6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)**"]},{"text":"Leaving MHz unconverted, multiplying by B, or dropping 2π each gives a wrong power or factor.","strike":["6.7 × 10¹ rad/(s·T)","9.2 × 10⁹ rad/(s·T)","1.1 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)."}],"trap":"Using f instead of 2πf: the passage defines γ with the angular frequency."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz","note":"f = 1.25 × 10⁸ Hz"},{"t":"11.7 T","note":"B"},{"t":"divided by the magnetic field B","note":"divide, don’t multiply"}]},{"text":"**Read the definition as an equation.** \"Two pi times f, divided by B\" is γ = 2πf/B."},{"text":"**Unit check.** Hz ÷ T → s⁻¹·T⁻¹; adding the radian from 2π gives rad/(s·T)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a passage defines a quantity in words, write it as a formula, convert every value to base units (MHz → Hz), and keep each constant such as 2π."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P9"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-53a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:53"],"passage":"A teaching lab compares reactions and materials that involve gases. Identical strips of magnesium are dropped into excess hydrochloric acid, the hydrogen is collected in a syringe, and both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume. Baker’s ammonia, used to leaven thin cookies, decomposes completely when heated, NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g), and all of the gas is caught in a closed flask. Nitroglycerin, by contrast, can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate and release a great deal of heat and gas; its energy diagram is shown. Finally, compound R, a candidate refrigerant whose phase diagram is shown, is held as a gas at a constant −28 °C and compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm.","q":"Which explanation best fits the smaller hydrogen volume in the second run?","choices":["Same moles at higher pressure","Less hydrogen was produced","The magnesium did not react","The gas was colder in the chamber"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Gas was collected in both runs, so the metal did react."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Identical strips reacted to completion with excess acid, so both runs made the same moles of H₂."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both runs were at room temperature; temperature is not what changed between them."}],"rule":"Use V = nRT/P: list which of n, T and P differ between the runs; the one that changed explains the volume difference.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"n same (identical strips, completion), T same (room temperature), P doubled → V halves (Boyle). Nothing else changed."},"exp":"Identical strips with excess acid going to completion make the **same moles of H₂** in both runs, and both runs are at room temperature. Only the **pressure** differs, and for a fixed amount of gas at constant T, **V ∝ 1/P** (Boyle’s law): doubling the pressure halves the volume. Likewise, at constant pressure, the same moles at a lower temperature would take up less volume (V ∝ T, Charles’s law).","how":{"ask":"Which of n, T and P differs between the two runs?","know":"• **PV = nRT**, so V = nRT/P.\n• Constant n and T: V ∝ 1/P (Boyle). Constant n and P: V ∝ T in kelvin (Charles).\n• Moles of gas formed are set by the limiting reagent, not by the conditions of collection.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the amounts: identical strips, excess acid, run to completion, so both runs make the same moles of H₂.","hl":"Identical strips of magnesium","strike":["Less hydrogen was produced"]},{"text":"Gas formed in both runs, and both were at room temperature.","hl":"both runs are done at room temperature","strike":["The magnesium did not react","The gas was colder in the chamber"]},{"text":"Only the pressure doubled, so the same gas fills about half the volume.","hl":"twice that pressure","calc":["V = nRT/P → P × 2 → V ÷ 2"]},{"text":"Also: the temperature version of the same idea: at constant pressure, the same moles of gas fill less volume when colder (V ∝ T)."},{"text":"Answer: the same moles of gas, compressed by the higher pressure."}],"trap":"Assuming a smaller gas volume means less gas formed: volume also depends on pressure and temperature."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"go to completion","note":"same moles"},{"t":"room temperature","note":"same T"},{"t":"twice that pressure","note":"P doubled"}]},{"text":"**Hold the variables steady.** Check n, T and P one at a time; whichever differs explains the change.","calc":["V ∝ 1/P (n, T fixed)","V ∝ T (n, P fixed)"]},{"text":"**Amount vs volume.** The reaction fixes the moles; the conditions fix the volume those moles fill."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when the same reaction gives different gas volumes, use V = nRT/P: if n is fixed, the volume change comes from T (V ∝ T) or P (V ∝ 1/P)."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P10"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-54a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:54#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:54"],"passage":"A teaching lab compares reactions and materials that involve gases. Identical strips of magnesium are dropped into excess hydrochloric acid, the hydrogen is collected in a syringe, and both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume. Baker’s ammonia, used to leaven thin cookies, decomposes completely when heated, NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g), and all of the gas is caught in a closed flask. Nitroglycerin, by contrast, can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate and release a great deal of heat and gas; its energy diagram is shown. Finally, compound R, a candidate refrigerant whose phase diagram is shown, is held as a gas at a constant −28 °C and compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm.","q":"As compound R is compressed, which changes occur, in order?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound R (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-90,"max":50,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-90,0.3],[-70,0.7],[-50,1.2],[-30,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-25,6],[-20,10]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-10,3],[10,4.5],[30,6.5],[50,9]]}]},"choices":["Condensation, then freezing","Deposition only","Freezing, then condensation","Condensation only"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A gas must condense into liquid before that liquid can freeze; going up in pressure meets the liquid–gas line first."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That stops at the first line; the solid–liquid line sits near 3.6 atm, below 8 atm, so the path crosses it too."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Deposition needs the solid–gas line, which lies only left of the triple point (−30 °C); a path at −28 °C never meets it."}],"rule":"Trace the path on the diagram: move straight along it at that temperature and name each boundary line you cross, in order.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Constant T = straight up the diagram. Above the triple-point temperature you cross gas→liquid, then liquid→solid: condensation, then freezing."},"exp":"Constant temperature = a **vertical** path at −28 °C. Going up, it first crosses the **liquid–gas line (≈ 2.1 atm): condensation**, then the **solid–liquid line (≈ 3.6 atm): freezing**, ending as a solid at 8 atm. It never touches the solid–gas line, which lies left of the triple point (−30 °C, 2 atm).","how":{"ask":"Draw the path at −28 °C from 0.5 to 8 atm: which lines does it cross, in order?","know":"• On a phase diagram, **pressure is up, temperature is across**; constant T = a vertical path, constant P = a horizontal path.\n• Each boundary crossed is one phase change: liquid–gas = vaporization/condensation; solid–liquid = melting/freezing; solid–gas = sublimation/deposition.\n• Name the change from the region you leave to the region you enter.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the path: a constant −28 °C, compressed from 0.5 to 8 atm. That is a vertical line on the diagram, starting in the gas region (low pressure, right of the triple point).","hl":"constant −28 °C"},{"text":"Move straight up. First boundary: liquid–gas, near 2.1 atm, so gas becomes liquid.","strike":["Deposition only"],"calc":["liquid–gas line at −28 °C: 2 + (2/20) × 1 ≈ 2.1 atm","gas → liquid = condensation"]},{"text":"Keep going: the solid–liquid line sits near 3.6 atm, below 8 atm, so the liquid becomes solid.","strike":["Condensation only","Freezing, then condensation"],"calc":["solid–liquid line at −28 °C: 2 + (2/5) × 4 ≈ 3.6 atm","3.6 atm < 8 atm → freezing"]},{"text":"Answer: condensation, then freezing."}],"trap":"Choosing deposition because the path starts as gas and ends as solid; at −28 °C, warmer than the triple point, a liquid region lies between them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"constant −28 °C","note":"a vertical path"},{"t":"compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm","note":"move up"},{"t":"phase diagram","note":"read regions and lines"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** solid at upper left, liquid in the wedge, gas at lower right; the three lines meet at the triple point (−30 °C, 2 atm).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound R (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-90,"max":50,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-90,0.3],[-70,0.7],[-50,1.2],[-30,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-25,6],[-20,10]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-10,3],[10,4.5],[30,6.5],[50,9]]}]}},{"text":"**Draw the path and list each line it crosses**, in order, naming the region it leaves and the one it enters.","calc":["gas → liquid (condensation) → solid (freezing)"]},{"text":"**Also:** a path held below the triple-point pressure, such as warming at 1 atm from −80 °C, crosses only the solid–gas line: sublimation, with no liquid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** follow the path (vertical for constant T, horizontal for constant P) and name each boundary it crosses in order; sublimation or deposition happens only below the triple-point pressure."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P10"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-55a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:55#1","pe6-cp:55#2","pe6-cp:55#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:55"],"passage":"A teaching lab compares reactions and materials that involve gases. Identical strips of magnesium are dropped into excess hydrochloric acid, the hydrogen is collected in a syringe, and both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume. Baker’s ammonia, used to leaven thin cookies, decomposes completely when heated, NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g), and all of the gas is caught in a closed flask. Nitroglycerin, by contrast, can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate and release a great deal of heat and gas; its energy diagram is shown. Finally, compound R, a candidate refrigerant whose phase diagram is shown, is held as a gas at a constant −28 °C and compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm.","q":"According to the diagram, nitroglycerin’s decomposition is (energies in kJ/mol):","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Energy along the reaction coordinate for the decomposition","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":350,"step":50},"series":[{"name":"Decomposition","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,150],[1,150],[2,165],[3,210],[4,270],[5,300],[6,260],[7,170],[8,90],[9,45],[10,40]]}]},"choices":["exothermic; Ea = 150.","endothermic; Ea = 150.","exothermic; Ea = 300.","exothermic; Ea = 260."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Products sit below the reactants, so energy is released; calling it endothermic flips the sign of ΔH."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the reverse barrier, peak minus products; the forward barrier is measured up from the reactants."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Reads the peak height from zero; Ea is the climb from the reactants’ level to the peak."}],"rule":"On an energy diagram: ΔH = products − reactants (products lower → exothermic); Ea = peak − reactants, never peak − zero or peak − products.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two reads: is the right end lower than the left (exothermic)? How far is the peak above the left end (Ea)? 300 − 150 = 150 kJ/mol."},"exp":"The reactants start at **150 kJ/mol** and the products end at **40 kJ/mol**, so ΔH = 40 − 150 = **−110 kJ/mol: exothermic**. The peak is at 300 kJ/mol, so the forward **Ea = 300 − 150 = 150 kJ/mol**. A large release of energy plus a **high barrier** is exactly why the compound is stable until struck, then explosive.","how":{"ask":"Where do the products sit relative to the reactants, and how high is the peak above the reactants?","know":"• ΔH (or ΔG) = products − reactants: negative → exothermic (exergonic).\n• Forward Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy; reverse Ea = transition state − products.\n• Kinetically stable but thermodynamically unstable = large energy release, high Ea.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says nitroglycerin sits unchanged for years yet releases a great deal of heat once struck: expect a high barrier and products well below the reactants. Then read the diagram.","hl":"sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years"},{"text":"Read the two ends of the curve.","calc":["reactants: 150 kJ/mol","products: 40 kJ/mol","ΔH = 40 − 150 = −110 → exothermic"],"strike":["endothermic; Ea = 150."]},{"text":"Read the peak and measure up from the reactants.","calc":["peak: 300 kJ/mol","Ea = 300 − 150 = **150 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"300 measures from zero and 260 is the reverse barrier.","strike":["exothermic; Ea = 300.","exothermic; Ea = 260."]},{"text":"This matches the passage: it sits unchanged for years (high barrier) and releases great heat once started (exothermic).","hl":"release a great deal of heat and gas"},{"text":"Answer: exothermic, with Ea = 150 kJ/mol."}],"trap":"Reading Ea as the peak’s height on the axis: the barrier is measured from where the reactants start."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sit unchanged","note":"high activation energy"},{"t":"release a great deal of heat","note":"exothermic"},{"t":"energy diagram","note":"read ends and peak"}]},{"text":"**Two separate questions.**","calc":["thermodynamics: products vs reactants → ΔH","kinetics: peak vs reactants → Ea"]},{"text":"**Stable but explosive.** A big negative ΔH with a high Ea: nothing happens until a blow or spark supplies the barrier energy, then the released heat keeps the reaction going."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an energy diagram, ΔH is products minus reactants and the forward Ea is the peak minus reactants; a compound that is stable until triggered yet releases energy is exothermic with a high Ea."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P10"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-56a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:56#1","pe6-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:56"],"passage":"A teaching lab compares reactions and materials that involve gases. Identical strips of magnesium are dropped into excess hydrochloric acid, the hydrogen is collected in a syringe, and both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume. Baker’s ammonia, used to leaven thin cookies, decomposes completely when heated, NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g), and all of the gas is caught in a closed flask. Nitroglycerin, by contrast, can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate and release a great deal of heat and gas; its energy diagram is shown. Finally, compound R, a candidate refrigerant whose phase diagram is shown, is held as a gas at a constant −28 °C and compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm.","q":"Water vapor makes up what mole fraction of the gas from the baker’s ammonia?","choices":["0.25","0.33","0.67","1.0"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the share of the other two gases, the complement, not water's share."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"A fraction of 1.0 would mean water is the only gas; the ammonia and carbon dioxide must go in the total too."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"That counts the solid in the total (1 of 4); only gases belong in a gas-phase mole fraction."}],"rule":"Mole fraction = moles of that gas ÷ moles of all gases; count gas coefficients only, never solids or liquids.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count only gas coefficients: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, water is 1 of them → 1/3 ≈ 0.33. The solid reactant is not in the gas."},"exp":"Count gas moles from the coefficients: **1 NH₃ + 1 CO₂ + 1 H₂O = 3 mol**; the solid is not part of the gas. **x(H₂O) = 1 ÷ 3 ≈ 0.33**.","how":{"ask":"Divide water's coefficient by the sum of the gas coefficients only.","know":"• **Mole fraction x_i = n_i ÷ n_total**, always between 0 and 1.\n• For a complete reaction, coefficients give mole ratios.\n• **Count only (g) species**: solids and liquids are not in the gas mixture.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the equation: NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g). Pick out the gaseous products; the solid is not part of the gas.","hl":"NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g)","calc":["gas moles = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3"]},{"text":"Divide the water's share by the total.","calc":["x(H₂O) = 1 ÷ 3 ≈ **0.33**"]},{"text":"0.25 puts the solid in the total, 0.67 is the other two gases, and 1.0 forgets them entirely.","strike":["0.25","0.67","1.0"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.33."}],"trap":"Adding the solid reactant's coefficient to the total: a gas mole fraction counts gases only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NH₄HCO₃(s)","note":"solid: not in the gas"},{"t":"H₂O(g)","note":"1 mol of the gas asked"},{"t":"mole fraction","note":"part ÷ whole"}]},{"text":"**Coefficients are moles.** Per mole of solid that decomposes, the products are 1 mol each of NH₃, CO₂ and H₂O.","calc":["n(gas) = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 mol"]},{"text":"**Part over whole.**","calc":["x(H₂O) = 1/3 ≈ 0.33","x(NH₃) + x(CO₂) = 2/3; all three sum to 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** leave every (s) and (l) out of the total; the phase labels decide what counts in a gas mixture."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mole fraction of a gaseous product = its coefficient ÷ the sum of the gas coefficients, ignoring solids and liquids."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P10"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-57a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:57#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:57"],"q":"Technetium-99m (half-life 6.0 h) arrives at a clinic with an activity of 48 mCi. What is its activity 24 h later?","choices":["1.5 mCi","3.0 mCi","6.0 mCi","12 mCi"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops after three halvings; 24 hours holds four half-lives, so halve once more."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Divides by the number of half-lives; each half-life halves the activity, so the factor is 2 raised to that number."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Halves five times; 24 h ÷ 6.0 h gives four half-lives, not five."}],"rule":"Activity after time t: n = t ÷ half-life, then A = A₀ × (1/2)ⁿ. Halve n times; never divide by n.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"24 ÷ 6.0 = 4 half-lives: 48 → 24 → 12 → 6 → 3 mCi."},"exp":"The number of half-lives is **n = 24 h ÷ 6.0 h = 4**. Activity halves each time: 48 → 24 → 12 → 6 → **3.0 mCi**, the same as 48 × (1/2)⁴ = 48 ÷ 16.","how":{"ask":"How many half-lives fit in 24 hours, and what is left after halving that many times?","know":"• n = elapsed time ÷ half-life.\n• A = A₀ × (1/2)ⁿ (the same rule for mass or number of nuclei).\n• After 1, 2, 3, 4 half-lives: ½, ¼, ⅛, 1/16 remain.","steps":[{"text":"Count half-lives.","hl":"half-life 6.0 h","calc":["24 h ÷ 6.0 h = **4**"]},{"text":"Halve four times.","hl":"48 mCi","calc":["48 → 24 → 12 → 6 → **3.0 mCi**","check: 48 × 1/16 = 3.0"]},{"text":"6.0 mCi stops one halving short, 1.5 mCi halves once too often, and 12 mCi divides by 4 instead of halving 4 times.","strike":["6.0 mCi","1.5 mCi","12 mCi"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.0 mCi."}],"trap":"Dividing the starting activity by the number of half-lives: decay is a repeated halving, (1/2)ⁿ, not 1/n."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"half-life 6.0 h","note":"one halving per 6 h"},{"t":"48 mCi","note":"starting activity"},{"t":"24 h later","note":"four half-lives"}]},{"text":"**Count, then halve.**","calc":["n = t ÷ t½","A = A₀ × (1/2)ⁿ"]},{"text":"**Fractions to know.** 1 half-life → ½, 2 → ¼, 3 → ⅛, 4 → 1/16, 5 → 1/32."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** divide the elapsed time by the half-life to get n, then multiply the starting activity by (1/2)ⁿ."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-58a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:58"],"q":"Radon-222 (Z = 86), a gas that seeps into basements, is an alpha emitter. Given Po (Z = 84), At (Z = 85) and Ra (Z = 88), what is the daughter nucleus?","choices":["Astatine-218","Radium-226","Polonium-218","Polonium-220"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Radium adds the alpha particle instead of removing it; emission makes the nucleus lighter."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"An alpha particle carries 2 protons, so Z drops by 2 (to 84), not by 1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An alpha particle has mass number 4, so A drops by 4; that subtracts only 2."}],"rule":"Alpha emission: A drops by 4 and Z drops by 2; then match the new Z to the element's name.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Alpha = lose 4 in mass, 2 in Z. 222 − 4 = 218; 86 − 2 = 84 = Po → polonium-218."},"exp":"An **alpha particle is ⁴₂He**, so **A falls by 4 and Z by 2**: A = 222 − 4 = 218, Z = 86 − 2 = 84 = **polonium**. ²²²Rn → **²¹⁸Po** + ⁴He.","how":{"ask":"Subtract 4 from A and 2 from Z, then name the element from the new Z.","know":"• **Alpha decay** emits ⁴₂He: **A − 4, Z − 2**.\n• Z alone fixes the element's identity.\n• Check: mass numbers and charges balance on both sides.","steps":[{"text":"Read the parent.","hl":"Radon-222 (Z = 86)","calc":["A = 222, Z = 86"]},{"text":"Remove a helium-4 nucleus.","calc":["A: 222 − 4 = 218","Z: 86 − 2 = 84 → Po"]},{"text":"Balance check.","calc":["222 = 218 + 4 ✓; 86 = 84 + 2 ✓"]},{"text":"Astatine drops Z by only 1, radium adds the alpha, and polonium-220 subtracts 2 from A instead of 4.","strike":["Astatine-218","Radium-226","Polonium-220"]},{"text":"Answer: polonium-218."}],"trap":"Subtracting 2 from the mass number too: the alpha carries 2 protons but 4 nucleons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Radon-222","note":"A = 222"},{"t":"Z = 86","note":"protons"},{"t":"alpha emitter","note":"loses ⁴₂He"}]},{"text":"**What leaves.** An alpha particle is two protons and two neutrons.","calc":["ΔA = −4, ΔZ = −2"]},{"text":"**Name the daughter by Z.** The new proton count picks the element from the list.","calc":["Z = 84 → polonium; A = 218"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** alpha decay lowers A by 4 and Z by 2; the new Z names the daughter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:59#1","pe6-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:59"],"q":"In the octet Lewis structure of the nitrosonium ion, NO⁺, which bond joins N and O, and what is oxygen’s formal charge?","choices":["Triple; positive","Triple; zero","Double; positive","Double; zero"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"A double bond leaves nitrogen with only six electrons around it; more sharing is needed before octets are complete."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops at a double bond, which leaves nitrogen short of an octet, and then counts oxygen’s charge for the wrong structure."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"With one lone pair and three bonds, oxygen owns 5 electrons, one fewer than its 6 valence electrons."}],"rule":"Count total valence electrons, add multiple bonds until every atom has an octet, then formal charge = valence − (lone-pair electrons + bonds).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NO⁺ has 5 + 6 − 1 = 10 valence e⁻, like CO and N₂: a triple bond plus a single lone pair on both N and O. O: 6 − (2 + 3) = +1."},"exp":"NO⁺ has 5 + 6 − 1 = **10 valence electrons**. Only **:N≡O:⁺** gives both atoms octets: a **triple bond** (6 shared electrons) plus a single lone pair on both N and O. Formal charges: **N = 5 − (2 + 3) = 0** and **O = 6 − (2 + 3) = +1**, so oxygen carries the positive charge (they sum to the ion’s +1).","how":{"ask":"How many valence electrons does NO⁺ have, which bond gives both atoms octets, and what does oxygen own in that structure?","know":"• Total valence electrons: add each atom’s, subtract one per + charge (add one per − charge).\n• Use multiple bonds until every atom has an octet.\n• **Formal charge = valence e⁻ − (lone-pair e⁻ + number of bonds)**; the charges add up to the ion’s charge.","steps":[{"text":"Count electrons.","hl":"nitrosonium ion, NO⁺","calc":["N 5 + O 6 − 1 = 10 e⁻"]},{"text":"Place them: a double bond leaves N short; a triple bond with a single lone pair on both N and O gives both octets.","calc":[":N≡O: → 6 shared + 2 + 2 = 10 ✓"],"strike":["Double; positive","Double; zero"]},{"text":"Formal charge on oxygen in that structure.","calc":["O: 6 − (2 + 3) = **+1**","N: 5 − (2 + 3) = 0","sum = +1 ✓"],"strike":["Triple; zero"]},{"text":"Answer: triple bond; oxygen is positive."}],"trap":"Putting the + charge on the less electronegative atom by instinct: formal charge comes from electron bookkeeping, and here it lands on oxygen."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NO⁺","note":"10 valence e⁻"},{"t":"octet Lewis structure","note":"forces a triple bond"},{"t":"formal charge","note":"valence − owned"}]},{"text":"**Isoelectronic family.** N₂, CO, CN⁻ and NO⁺ all have 10 valence electrons and a triple bond with a single lone pair on both N and O."},{"text":"**Bookkeeping.**","calc":["owned = lone-pair e⁻ + one per bond","formal charge = valence − owned"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count valence electrons, add bonds until every atom has an octet, then assign formal charge = valence − (lone-pair electrons + bonds); in a 10-electron diatomic the bond is triple."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-01a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:1"],"pid":"sample-cp:P1","passage":"Protease R, which a gum-disease bacterium secretes, hydrolyzes peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of lysine residues, but not when the next residue is proline. The enzyme needs bound Ca²⁺ to stay folded, so each assay received 5.0 × 10⁻³ M Ca²⁺, and the enzyme stock brought along 7.5 × 10⁻³ M sulfate left over from purification, both present before any solid could form. Calcium sulfate is only slightly soluble, with Ksp = 4.9 × 10⁻⁵ at 25 °C. Activity was confirmed with the test peptide Lys–Pro–Lys–Trp, whose fragments were identified by mass spectrometry. The team then examined Compound V, a cyclic lipopeptide from a marine sponge with six stereogenic carbons, one C=C outside its rings that can be E or Z, one C=C locked inside a five-membered ring, and no internal mirror plane. Compound V had been predicted to block Protease R. Instead, the intact compound was used up during each assay as two smaller fragments, split next to its lysine, accumulated.","q":"With respect to the activity of Protease R, Compound V acts as:","choices":["an activator of Protease R.","an inhibitor of Protease R.","a substrate of Protease R.","a catalyst of the cleavage."],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"An activator binds and comes back intact, but Compound V was used up during each assay."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Blocking was only the prediction; the result was that the enzyme cut Compound V into fragments."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Protease R is the catalyst of every cleavage; the molecule it cuts is not."}],"rule":"Role words: did the enzyme change the molecule? Changed → it is what the enzyme acts on. Unchanged → an activator (rate up) or inhibitor (rate down). The enzyme is the catalyst.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on what happened to Compound V, not on what was predicted: \"used up\" while fragments \"accumulated\" means the enzyme acted on it."},"exp":"The result in the last sentence is that intact Compound V was **used up** while two fragments, split next to its lysine, built up. Protease R therefore cuts it, so Compound V is a **substrate**. An inhibitor or activator would bind and be recovered intact, and the catalyst is Protease R itself. The prediction that it would block the enzyme was wrong.","how":{"ask":"Name the role Compound V plays for Protease R from what the experiment showed happened to it.","know":"Four role words, told apart by two questions. **Did the enzyme change it?** Yes → substrate. **No → did activity go up or down?** Up → activator (agonist); down → inhibitor (antagonist). The **catalyst** is the enzyme itself.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says the intact compound was used up as fragments built up: the enzyme changed Compound V.","hl":"the intact compound was used up during each assay"},{"text":"Concept: a molecule that an enzyme converts into products is its substrate. Inhibitors and activators only bind and come back unchanged, so neither fits.","strike":["an inhibitor of Protease R.","an activator of Protease R."]},{"text":"Protease R is what hydrolyzes the peptide bonds, so it is the catalyst; Compound V is not.","hl":"hydrolyzes peptide bonds","strike":["a catalyst of the cleavage."]},{"text":"Consumed and split next to its lysine, exactly where Protease R cuts: Compound V is a substrate."}],"trap":"Answering from the prediction (\"predicted to block\") instead of the result: the data showed the compound was cut, so it is acted on, not an inhibitor."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the result**, and keep it apart from the expectation.","marks":[{"t":"had been predicted to block Protease R","note":"an expectation, not data"},{"t":"the intact compound was used up","note":"consumed → acted on by the enzyme"},{"t":"split next to its lysine","note":"cut where Protease R cuts"}]},{"text":"**Question 1: was it changed?** A molecule the enzyme turns into products is a substrate. A molecule recovered intact is a binder."},{"text":"**Question 2 (only for binders): up or down?** Activity up → activator (agonist); activity down → inhibitor (antagonist)."},{"text":"**Also:** the enzyme is always the catalyst. In a passage, a stated prediction can be overturned by the result that follows it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Changed by the enzyme → substrate. Unchanged and activity up → agonist; unchanged and activity down → antagonist. Trust the result, not the prediction."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-02a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:2"],"pid":"sample-cp:P1","passage":"Protease R, which a gum-disease bacterium secretes, hydrolyzes peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of lysine residues, but not when the next residue is proline. The enzyme needs bound Ca²⁺ to stay folded, so each assay received 5.0 × 10⁻³ M Ca²⁺, and the enzyme stock brought along 7.5 × 10⁻³ M sulfate left over from purification, both present before any solid could form. Calcium sulfate is only slightly soluble, with Ksp = 4.9 × 10⁻⁵ at 25 °C. Activity was confirmed with the test peptide Lys–Pro–Lys–Trp, whose fragments were identified by mass spectrometry. The team then examined Compound V, a cyclic lipopeptide from a marine sponge with six stereogenic carbons, one C=C outside its rings that can be E or Z, one C=C locked inside a five-membered ring, and no internal mirror plane. Compound V had been predicted to block Protease R. Instead, the intact compound was used up during each assay as two smaller fragments, split next to its lysine, accumulated.","q":"At most, how many stereoisomers can Compound V have?","choices":["12","64","128","256"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That multiplies the number of centers by two; each stereo unit doubles the count instead."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That counts only the six stereogenic carbons and leaves out the C=C that can be E or Z."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That also counts the ring C=C, but a C=C locked in a five-membered ring cannot be E or Z."}],"rule":"Maximum stereoisomers = 2ⁿ, n = stereocenters + C=C bonds that can be E or Z. Ring C=C in small rings add nothing; no mirror plane means no meso forms.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Count units, then double once per unit: 6 carbons + 1 E/Z bond = 7 doublings: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, then once more."},"exp":"Each stereo unit has two arrangements, so the maximum is **2ⁿ**. Compound V has **six** stereogenic carbons plus **one** C=C that can be E or Z, so n = 7. The ring C=C cannot be E/Z, and with no mirror plane there are no meso forms: 2⁷ = **128**.","how":{"ask":"Count every stereo unit in the passage, then raise 2 to that number.","know":"**Maximum stereoisomers = 2ⁿ**, where n = stereocenters + double bonds that can be E or Z. A C=C inside a small ring cannot be E/Z. A mirror plane would create meso forms and lower the count.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 lists the stereo units: six stereogenic carbons and one C=C outside the rings that can be E or Z.","hl":"six stereogenic carbons, one C=C outside its rings that can be E or Z"},{"text":"Concept: each unit doubles the count. Leaving out the E/Z bond gives only 2⁶.","calc":["n = 6 + 1 = 7"],"strike":["64"]},{"text":"The C=C locked inside the five-membered ring cannot be E or Z, so it adds nothing.","hl":"one C=C locked inside a five-membered ring","strike":["256"]},{"text":"No internal mirror plane, so no meso forms to subtract; 12 is just 2 × 6, not a power of 2.","hl":"no internal mirror plane","strike":["12"]},{"text":"Use the full 2ⁿ.","calc":["2⁷ = **128**"]}],"trap":"Counting only the stereogenic carbons and forgetting the double bond that can be E or Z (or counting the ring double bond that cannot)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every stereo unit** in the passage, and the ones that do not count.","marks":[{"t":"six stereogenic carbons","note":"6 units"},{"t":"one C=C outside its rings that can be E or Z","note":"+1 unit"},{"t":"one C=C locked inside a five-membered ring","note":"not E/Z → 0"},{"t":"no internal mirror plane","note":"no meso forms"}]},{"text":"**Each unit doubles the count.** Two arrangements at each of n independent units give 2ⁿ combinations.","calc":["2⁷ = 128"]},{"text":"**Also:** a molecule with an internal mirror plane has meso forms, so its real count is below 2ⁿ."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Max stereoisomers = 2ⁿ (stereocenters + E/Z-capable C=C); ring C=C in small rings add nothing; subtract only for meso forms."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-03a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:3"],"pid":"sample-cp:P1","passage":"Protease R, which a gum-disease bacterium secretes, hydrolyzes peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of lysine residues, but not when the next residue is proline. The enzyme needs bound Ca²⁺ to stay folded, so each assay received 5.0 × 10⁻³ M Ca²⁺, and the enzyme stock brought along 7.5 × 10⁻³ M sulfate left over from purification, both present before any solid could form. Calcium sulfate is only slightly soluble, with Ksp = 4.9 × 10⁻⁵ at 25 °C. Activity was confirmed with the test peptide Lys–Pro–Lys–Trp, whose fragments were identified by mass spectrometry. The team then examined Compound V, a cyclic lipopeptide from a marine sponge with six stereogenic carbons, one C=C outside its rings that can be E or Z, one C=C locked inside a five-membered ring, and no internal mirror plane. Compound V had been predicted to block Protease R. Instead, the intact compound was used up during each assay as two smaller fragments, split next to its lysine, accumulated.","q":"Once the enzyme stock is in an assay, does calcium sulfate precipitate, and why?","choices":["Yes; Q is above Ksp","No; Q is below Ksp","Yes; [Ca²⁺] is above Ksp","No; Q equals Ksp"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiply carefully: 5.0 × 7.5 gives 37.5, so Q is 3.75 × 10⁻⁵, which sits under 4.9 × 10⁻⁵."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Ksp is compared with the product of both ion concentrations, never with one ion alone."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Q equals Ksp only in a saturated solution; this ion product is not 4.9 × 10⁻⁵."}],"rule":"Build Q = [Ca²⁺][SO₄²⁻] from the concentrations present before any solid forms, then compare with Ksp: above → solid; below → stays dissolved.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"Multiply the coefficients (5.0 × 7.5 ≈ 38) and add the exponents (10⁻³ × 10⁻³ = 10⁻⁶): Q ≈ 38 × 10⁻⁶ = 3.8 × 10⁻⁵, under 4.9 × 10⁻⁵."},"exp":"Q = [Ca²⁺][SO₄²⁻] = (5.0 × 10⁻³)(7.5 × 10⁻³) = **3.75 × 10⁻⁵**. That is **below** Ksp (4.9 × 10⁻⁵), so the solution is unsaturated and **no calcium sulfate precipitates**; the Ca²⁺ stays free for the enzyme.","how":{"ask":"Compute the ion product Q from the passage, compare it with Ksp, and say whether a solid forms.","know":"For a 1:1 salt, **Q = [cation][anion]**, using the concentrations present before any solid forms. Q > Ksp → precipitate; Q < Ksp → unsaturated, no solid; Q = Ksp → saturated.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives both ion concentrations, present before any solid could form.","hl":"5.0 × 10⁻³ M Ca²⁺"},{"text":"Concept: Ksp is compared with the product of the ions, Q. Sentence 3 gives Ksp.","hl":"Ksp = 4.9 × 10⁻⁵","calc":["Q = (5.0 × 10⁻³)(7.5 × 10⁻³) = 3.75 × 10⁻⁵"]},{"text":"Compare: 3.75 × 10⁻⁵ is less than 4.9 × 10⁻⁵, so no solid forms and Q is not equal to Ksp.","strike":["Yes; Q is above Ksp","No; Q equals Ksp"]},{"text":"One ion alone is never compared with Ksp.","strike":["Yes; [Ca²⁺] is above Ksp"]}],"trap":"Comparing a single ion concentration with Ksp, or slipping an exponent: Ksp is a product, so compare it with the product Q."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** the passage gives.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 × 10⁻³ M Ca²⁺","note":"[Ca²⁺]"},{"t":"7.5 × 10⁻³ M sulfate","note":"[SO₄²⁻]"},{"t":"Ksp = 4.9 × 10⁻⁵","note":"the limit"}]},{"text":"**Build Q like Ksp**, with the concentrations present before any solid forms.","calc":["Q = (5.0 × 10⁻³)(7.5 × 10⁻³) = 3.75 × 10⁻⁵"]},{"text":"**Compare.** 3.75 × 10⁻⁵ < 4.9 × 10⁻⁵: the solution can still hold more ions, so it stays clear."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q > Ksp → precipitate; Q < Ksp → no solid; Q = Ksp → saturated. Always compare the product of the ions."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-04a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:4"],"pid":"sample-cp:P1","passage":"Protease R, which a gum-disease bacterium secretes, hydrolyzes peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of lysine residues, but not when the next residue is proline. The enzyme needs bound Ca²⁺ to stay folded, so each assay received 5.0 × 10⁻³ M Ca²⁺, and the enzyme stock brought along 7.5 × 10⁻³ M sulfate left over from purification, both present before any solid could form. Calcium sulfate is only slightly soluble, with Ksp = 4.9 × 10⁻⁵ at 25 °C. Activity was confirmed with the test peptide Lys–Pro–Lys–Trp, whose fragments were identified by mass spectrometry. The team then examined Compound V, a cyclic lipopeptide from a marine sponge with six stereogenic carbons, one C=C outside its rings that can be E or Z, one C=C locked inside a five-membered ring, and no internal mirror plane. Compound V had been predicted to block Protease R. Instead, the intact compound was used up during each assay as two smaller fragments, split next to its lysine, accumulated.","q":"Which two fragments does Protease R release from the test peptide?","choices":["Lys and Pro–Lys–Trp","Lys–Pro–Lys and Trp","Lys–Pro and Lys–Trp","Lys–Lys and Pro–Trp"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Hydrolysis only breaks a bond; it never joins residues that were not neighbors."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That cuts on the amino side of a lysine; Protease R cuts on its carboxyl side."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That cuts after the lysine that proline follows, which the passage says Protease R does not do."}],"rule":"Find each residue the protease recognizes, check the stated exception, cut on the named side, and keep every residue in order.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mark each Lys, cross out any Lys followed by Pro, then put a slash right after the Lys that is left."},"exp":"Protease R cuts on the **carboxyl side of lysine**, **except before proline**. In Lys–Pro–Lys–Trp, the Lys at the N-terminus is followed by Pro, so it is skipped. The other Lys is followed by Trp, so that bond breaks: **Lys–Pro–Lys and Trp**.","how":{"ask":"Apply the cutting rule and its exception to the test peptide, then read off the two pieces.","know":"A protease breaks one specific peptide bond: find the recognized residue, cut on the named side (carboxyl side = the bond after it), and apply any exception. Pieces keep their original order.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the cutting rule and its exception: after lysine, but not when proline follows.","hl":"but not when the next residue is proline"},{"text":"The peptide has two lysines. The N-terminal Lys is followed by Pro, so that bond is not cut.","hl":"Lys–Pro–Lys–Trp","strike":["Lys and Pro–Lys–Trp"]},{"text":"The other Lys is followed by Trp: cut on its carboxyl side, the Lys–Trp bond, not before it.","strike":["Lys–Pro and Lys–Trp"]},{"text":"Hydrolysis never joins residues that were not neighbors.","strike":["Lys–Lys and Pro–Trp"]},{"text":"Fragments: the tripeptide ending in Lys, plus free Trp."}],"trap":"Cutting at every lysine and missing the proline exception the passage states."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the cutting rule and its exception**.","marks":[{"t":"on the carboxyl side of lysine residues","note":"cut the bond after Lys"},{"t":"but not when the next residue is proline","note":"Lys–Pro is spared"}]},{"text":"**Mark the cut.** Lys–Pro (spared) – Lys | Trp: only the bond after the second lysine breaks."},{"text":"**Also:** trypsin follows the same pattern, cutting after Lys or Arg unless Pro comes next."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Locate the recognized residue, apply the exception, break the bond on the named side, and keep the order of every residue."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-05a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:5"],"pid":"sample-cp:P2","passage":"In a myelinated axon, each internode works like a capacitor: the cytoplasm and the outside fluid are the two plates, and the axon membrane plus its myelin wraps form the insulator between them. In normal saline, the inside of a frog sciatic nerve axon rests at −75 mV relative to the outside. To measure conduction velocity, researchers stimulated one end of the nerve and recorded the impulse at two electrodes farther along; their amplifier resolves the delay between the two signals only if it is at least 0.25 ms. In untreated nerves, impulses traveled at a steady 48 m/s. A low dose of tetrodotoxin, which blocks some of the Na⁺ channels at the nodes but leaves the myelin wraps and the axon radius untouched, slowed conduction to 30 m/s. In other runs, Li⁺, which also passes through Na⁺ channels, replaced the Na⁺ of the saline, Br⁻ replaced its Cl⁻, and Cs⁺ was added to block K⁺ channels, while Ca²⁺ was kept at its usual level. Finally, the nerves were moved into a bath whose K⁺ concentration matched that of the cytoplasm, and the potential difference across each membrane fell to zero.","q":"In the final bath, the electric field within each axon membrane points:","choices":["into the axon.","out of the axon.","along the axon's length.","in no direction."],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"An outward field needs the inside to sit above the outside; here the two sides are at the same potential."},{"c":0,"tag":"copied","why":"That fits the −75 mV resting state, but the final bath removed the potential difference."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A field between capacitor plates runs across the insulator, never parallel to the plates."}],"rule":"The field inside a membrane runs from the higher-potential side to the lower one. No potential difference across it → no field.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Edge case: ΔV = 0. With E = ΔV/d, a zero voltage across the membrane gives a zero field, whatever its thickness."},"exp":"The membrane is the insulator of a capacitor, and the field in it points from the higher-potential plate to the lower one, with E = ΔV/d. In the final bath the **potential difference fell to zero**, so **E = 0**: the field points in no direction. At rest (−75 mV inside) it would have pointed inward.","how":{"ask":"Use the passage to decide which side of the membrane is at higher potential in the final bath, then give the field direction.","know":"Across a capacitor's insulator, **E points from + to −** (higher to lower potential) and **E = ΔV/d**. Inside negative → field points inward; inside positive → outward; ΔV = 0 → no field.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says the potential difference across each membrane fell to zero in the final bath.","hl":"the potential difference across each membrane fell to zero"},{"text":"Concept: the field points from the higher-potential side to the lower one, E = ΔV/d. With ΔV = 0, neither side is higher, so there is neither an inward nor an outward field.","strike":["into the axon.","out of the axon."]},{"text":"The membrane is the insulator between two plates, so any field in it runs across it, not along the axon.","hl":"the cytoplasm and the outside fluid are the two plates","strike":["along the axon's length."]},{"text":"ΔV = 0 → E = 0: the field points in no direction."}],"trap":"Reusing the resting potential from earlier in the passage: the question asks about the final bath, where the voltage across the membrane is zero."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find which condition the question asks about** and its voltage.","marks":[{"t":"rests at −75 mV relative to the outside","note":"normal saline only"},{"t":"the potential difference across each membrane fell to zero","note":"final bath: ΔV = 0"}]},{"text":"**Membrane = capacitor.** Field direction: from the higher-potential side to the lower. Size: E = ΔV/d."},{"text":"**Apply it.** Inside at −75 mV → E points inward. Inside positive → outward. ΔV = 0 → E = 0."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Field points from + to −; E = ΔV/d. Read the voltage for the exact condition asked; zero voltage means zero field."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-06a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"biology","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:6"],"pid":"sample-cp:P2","passage":"In a myelinated axon, each internode works like a capacitor: the cytoplasm and the outside fluid are the two plates, and the axon membrane plus its myelin wraps form the insulator between them. In normal saline, the inside of a frog sciatic nerve axon rests at −75 mV relative to the outside. To measure conduction velocity, researchers stimulated one end of the nerve and recorded the impulse at two electrodes farther along; their amplifier resolves the delay between the two signals only if it is at least 0.25 ms. In untreated nerves, impulses traveled at a steady 48 m/s. A low dose of tetrodotoxin, which blocks some of the Na⁺ channels at the nodes but leaves the myelin wraps and the axon radius untouched, slowed conduction to 30 m/s. In other runs, Li⁺, which also passes through Na⁺ channels, replaced the Na⁺ of the saline, Br⁻ replaced its Cl⁻, and Cs⁺ was added to block K⁺ channels, while Ca²⁺ was kept at its usual level. Finally, the nerves were moved into a bath whose K⁺ concentration matched that of the cytoplasm, and the potential difference across each membrane fell to zero.","q":"How do the internode capacitance (C) and resistance (R) of tetrodotoxin-treated nerves compare with those of untreated nerves?","choices":["C lower, R higher","C higher, R lower","C higher, R higher","C same, R same"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Lower C and higher R come from thicker insulation, i.e. more myelin, which tetrodotoxin does not add."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Slower conduction is not myelin loss here: the passage says the myelin wraps and radius were untouched."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Changing insulator thickness moves C and R in opposite directions, never both up."}],"rule":"Internode C and R depend only on the insulator: thicker myelin → C down, R up; thinner → C up, R down. Untouched myelin and radius → no change.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on what the treatment changed. Tetrodotoxin acts on node channels; the insulator (myelin, radius) is untouched, so C and R stay put."},"exp":"Internode capacitance and resistance are set by the **insulator**: its thickness (myelin wraps) and its area (axon radius). Tetrodotoxin **leaves the myelin wraps and the axon radius untouched**, so **C and R are unchanged**. It slows conduction by blocking node Na⁺ channels, not by changing the insulator.","how":{"ask":"Find what tetrodotoxin changes and what it leaves alone, then decide what happens to C and R of the internode.","know":"For the internode capacitor, **C = κε₀A/d**: thicker insulation (larger d) → lower C, and higher R. A larger area (radius) → higher C, lower R. If neither changes, C and R stay the same.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says tetrodotoxin leaves the myelin wraps and the axon radius untouched.","hl":"leaves the myelin wraps and the axon radius untouched"},{"text":"Concept (sentence 1): C and R of the internode depend only on the insulator, its thickness and area.","hl":"the axon membrane plus its myelin wraps form the insulator"},{"text":"Neither thickness nor area changed, so neither C nor R changes. The slowing comes from blocked node channels, not from lost or added myelin; and thickness never moves C and R the same way.","strike":["C lower, R higher","C higher, R lower","C higher, R higher"]}],"trap":"Reading \"slowed conduction\" as demyelination: the passage says this drug leaves the myelin alone."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what the treatment touches** and what it leaves alone.","marks":[{"t":"blocks some of the Na⁺ channels at the nodes","note":"acts at the nodes"},{"t":"leaves the myelin wraps and the axon radius untouched","note":"insulator unchanged"}]},{"text":"**Internode = capacitor.** C = κε₀A/d. More myelin (larger d) → C down, R up; less myelin → C up, R down."},{"text":"**Apply it.** d and A are the same → C and R are the same. Conduction can still slow for other reasons, such as fewer working Na⁺ channels."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ask what changed in the insulator. Thicker → C↓ R↑; thinner → C↑ R↓; unchanged → C and R unchanged."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-07a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:7"],"pid":"sample-cp:P2","passage":"In a myelinated axon, each internode works like a capacitor: the cytoplasm and the outside fluid are the two plates, and the axon membrane plus its myelin wraps form the insulator between them. In normal saline, the inside of a frog sciatic nerve axon rests at −75 mV relative to the outside. To measure conduction velocity, researchers stimulated one end of the nerve and recorded the impulse at two electrodes farther along; their amplifier resolves the delay between the two signals only if it is at least 0.25 ms. In untreated nerves, impulses traveled at a steady 48 m/s. A low dose of tetrodotoxin, which blocks some of the Na⁺ channels at the nodes but leaves the myelin wraps and the axon radius untouched, slowed conduction to 30 m/s. In other runs, Li⁺, which also passes through Na⁺ channels, replaced the Na⁺ of the saline, Br⁻ replaced its Cl⁻, and Cs⁺ was added to block K⁺ channels, while Ca²⁺ was kept at its usual level. Finally, the nerves were moved into a bath whose K⁺ concentration matched that of the cytoplasm, and the potential difference across each membrane fell to zero.","q":"Of these ions used in the altered saline, which has the smallest radius?","choices":["Cs⁺","Li⁺","Ca²⁺","Br⁻"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Anions gain electrons and swell; Br⁻ is the largest of the four."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Its 2+ charge pulls its electrons in, but they still fill three shells, more than the lithium ion has."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Cesium sits far down Group 1, so its ion has many more shells and is much larger."}],"rule":"Smallest ion: fewest occupied shells first; among the same shells, the most protons per electron. Anions are bigger than nearby cations.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on shells: the cation from period 2 has only its 1s shell left, so it beats every ion from period 4 or below."},"exp":"Ionic radius is set mainly by the **number of occupied shells**, then by charge. **Li⁺** keeps only its 1s shell, so it is the smallest. Ca²⁺ has electrons through n = 3, Cs⁺ through n = 5, and Br⁻, an anion, is the largest.","how":{"ask":"Compare the sizes of the four ions named in the passage and pick the smallest.","know":"Ion size: **more occupied shells → larger** (down a group). With the same shells, a higher positive charge → smaller; gaining electrons (anions) → larger.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 names the ions in the altered saline: Li⁺, Br⁻, Cs⁺ and Ca²⁺.","hl":"Li⁺, which also passes through Na⁺ channels"},{"text":"Concept: radius grows with each occupied shell and anions are larger than cations. Br⁻ (anion, n = 4) and Cs⁺ (n = 5) are large.","strike":["Br⁻","Cs⁺"]},{"text":"Ca²⁺ has a high charge, but its electrons still fill three shells (1s through 3p); Li⁺ has only 1s².","strike":["Ca²⁺"]},{"text":"Fewest shells wins: Li⁺ is the smallest."}],"trap":"Letting charge outweigh shells: a 2+ ion from period 4 is still larger than a 1+ ion from period 2."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **list the ions** the passage names.","marks":[{"t":"Li⁺, which also passes through Na⁺ channels","note":"period 2 cation"},{"t":"Br⁻ replaced its Cl⁻","note":"anion"},{"t":"Cs⁺ was added","note":"period 6 cation"},{"t":"Ca²⁺ was kept at its usual level","note":"period 4 cation"}]},{"text":"**Shells first.** Each extra occupied shell makes an ion much larger."},{"text":"**Then charge.** With the same shells, more protons per electron pull the cloud in; anions swell."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Smallest ion = fewest occupied shells, then highest positive charge. Anions are larger than nearby cations."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-08a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:8"],"pid":"sample-cp:P2","passage":"In a myelinated axon, each internode works like a capacitor: the cytoplasm and the outside fluid are the two plates, and the axon membrane plus its myelin wraps form the insulator between them. In normal saline, the inside of a frog sciatic nerve axon rests at −75 mV relative to the outside. To measure conduction velocity, researchers stimulated one end of the nerve and recorded the impulse at two electrodes farther along; their amplifier resolves the delay between the two signals only if it is at least 0.25 ms. In untreated nerves, impulses traveled at a steady 48 m/s. A low dose of tetrodotoxin, which blocks some of the Na⁺ channels at the nodes but leaves the myelin wraps and the axon radius untouched, slowed conduction to 30 m/s. In other runs, Li⁺, which also passes through Na⁺ channels, replaced the Na⁺ of the saline, Br⁻ replaced its Cl⁻, and Cs⁺ was added to block K⁺ channels, while Ca²⁺ was kept at its usual level. Finally, the nerves were moved into a bath whose K⁺ concentration matched that of the cytoplasm, and the potential difference across each membrane fell to zero.","q":"For untreated nerves, how far apart must the recording electrodes be, at minimum, for the amplifier to resolve the delay?","choices":["0.0075 m","0.012 m","0.12 m","190 m"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Speed ÷ delay gives m/s², not a distance; a distance is speed × time."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Off by a power of ten: 0.25 ms is 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ s, not 2.5 × 10⁻³ s."},{"c":0,"tag":"copied","why":"That uses the tetrodotoxin speed of 30 m/s; the question asks about untreated nerves."}],"rule":"Minimum spacing = speed × shortest resolvable delay, with the delay in seconds and the speed for the condition asked.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Units: m/s × s = m. Write 0.25 ms as 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ s, then 48 × 2.5 = 120 and shift four places."},"exp":"The impulse must take at least **0.25 ms** to cross the gap. In untreated nerves it moves at **48 m/s**, so d = v·t = (48 m/s)(2.5 × 10⁻⁴ s) = **0.012 m** (1.2 cm). Any closer, and the delay is too short to resolve.","how":{"ask":"Combine the shortest resolvable delay with the untreated conduction speed to find the smallest electrode spacing.","know":"**distance = speed × time**, with time in seconds (1 ms = 10⁻³ s). Use the speed for the condition the question names.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the shortest delay the amplifier can resolve.","hl":"at least 0.25 ms"},{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the speed in untreated nerves; the 30 m/s value belongs to the tetrodotoxin runs.","hl":"a steady 48 m/s","strike":["0.0075 m"]},{"text":"Concept: distance = speed × time. Dividing speed by time gives the wrong units.","calc":["0.25 ms = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ s","d = (48 m/s)(2.5 × 10⁻⁴ s) = 1.2 × 10⁻² m"],"strike":["190 m"]},{"text":"Watch the power of ten: using 2.5 × 10⁻³ s gives a spacing ten times too large.","strike":["0.12 m"]}],"trap":"Using the slowed tetrodotoxin speed, or converting ms to s with the wrong power of ten."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two numbers** for the condition asked.","marks":[{"t":"at least 0.25 ms","note":"shortest delay"},{"t":"a steady 48 m/s","note":"untreated speed"},{"t":"slowed conduction to 30 m/s","note":"treated: not asked"}]},{"text":"**Distance = speed × time.** The shortest resolvable delay sets the shortest spacing.","calc":["d = (48 m/s)(2.5 × 10⁻⁴ s) = 0.012 m"]},{"text":"**Check units.** m/s × s = m. Any other combination gives the wrong unit."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Minimum spacing = speed × minimum delay, in seconds, using the speed of the condition the question names."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-15a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:15"],"q":"Assuming it dissociates completely in water, what is the K⁺ concentration in a 0.12 M solution of potassium phosphate, K₃PO₄?","choices":["0.040 M","0.36 M","0.48 M","0.12 M"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That divides by the subscript; each formula unit releases several K⁺, so the ion level exceeds the salt level."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That counts every ion, phosphate included; the stem asks only for K⁺."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That repeats the salt concentration without using the subscript on K."}],"rule":"Ion concentration = salt concentration × that ion's subscript in the formula. Count only the ion asked for.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"K₃ means three K⁺ per unit: triple the molarity of the salt."},"exp":"K₃PO₄ → **3 K⁺** + PO₄³⁻. So [K⁺] = 3 × 0.12 M = **0.36 M**.","how":{"ask":"Multiply the salt concentration by the number of K⁺ per formula unit.","know":"Complete dissociation: each formula unit releases its ions in the ratio of the subscripts.","steps":[{"text":"Write the dissociation.","hl":"K₃PO₄","calc":["K₃PO₄ → 3 K⁺ + PO₄³⁻"]},{"text":"Multiply by the K subscript.","calc":["[K⁺] = 3 × 0.12 = 0.36 M"]},{"text":"Not divided, not all ions, not the bare salt molarity.","strike":["0.040 M","0.48 M","0.12 M"]}],"trap":"Adding in the phosphate ions: the stem asks for one ion only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the formula and molarity**.","marks":[{"t":"0.12 M","note":"formula units per liter"},{"t":"K₃PO₄","note":"three K⁺ each"}]},{"text":"**Subscript = multiplier.**","calc":["3 × 0.12 M = 0.36 M"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** [ion] = [salt] × subscript of that ion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-17a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:17"],"q":"Treating each group as fully charged or uncharged, what is the net charge of the peptide Arg–His–Lys–Glu–Lys–Arg in blood plasma?","choices":["+6","+3","+4","+5"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"This counts the basic groups and His but leaves out the negative Glu side chain."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That adds the free amino end and His but drops every negative group, including the free carboxyl end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"His (pKa about 6) is mostly neutral in plasma, so it adds no charge."}],"rule":"At blood pH: Lys and Arg +1, Asp and Glu −1, His 0, and the free ends cancel (+1 and −1). Add the side chains.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Termini cancel. Count Arg/Lys (+1 each) and Asp/Glu (−1 each); skip His. Four plus, one minus."},"exp":"At blood pH the **N-terminus (+1) and C-terminus (−1) cancel**. Side chains: Arg +1, His 0, Lys +1, Glu −1, Lys +1, Arg +1. Net = 4 − 1 = **+3**.","how":{"ask":"Add the charges of the ionizable groups at blood pH.","know":"Near pH 7.4: **Lys, Arg +1; Asp, Glu −1; His ≈ 0** (pKa ≈ 6); free amino end +1 and free carboxyl end −1.","steps":[{"text":"The two ends cancel.","calc":["+1 (N-terminus) − 1 (C-terminus) = 0"]},{"text":"Count the side chains: four basic (Arg, Lys, Lys, Arg), one acidic (Glu), His neutral.","hl":"Arg–His–Lys–Glu–Lys–Arg","calc":["+4 − 1 = +3"]},{"text":"Counting His as +1 gives one too many; dropping Glu as well, or every negative group, gives more.","strike":["+4","+5","+6"]}],"trap":"Counting His as positive: its pKa is about 6, so at blood pH it is mostly uncharged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **sort each residue** by its side chain.","marks":[{"t":"Arg–His–Lys–Glu–Lys–Arg","note":"four basic, one acidic, one His"},{"t":"blood plasma","note":"pH about 7.4"}]},{"text":"**Ends cancel**, so only the side chains matter in a linear peptide."},{"text":"**Add them.** Lys/Arg +1 each, Glu −1, His 0.","calc":["+1 + 0 + 1 − 1 + 1 + 1 = +3"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** At pH 7–7.4, net charge = (number of Lys + Arg) − (number of Asp + Glu); His and the cancelling ends add nothing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-23p","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["aamc-phys-qpack:Q23"],"q":"Heat flows steadily into a piece of gallium, melting point 30 °C, that is already at its melting point. While any solid gallium remains, the metal's temperature:","choices":["dips a little.","keeps climbing.","holds at 30 °C.","climbs, then levels out."],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Heat is flowing in, so the sample cannot cool while it absorbs energy."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"The added energy goes into breaking the solid apart (latent heat), not into raising temperature."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The sample starts at its melting point, so there is no warming stage before the plateau."}],"rule":"During a phase change at fixed pressure, added heat changes the phase, not the temperature: the reading stays flat until the change is complete.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Solid and liquid together at fixed pressure = pinned at the melting point. Pick the flat line."},"exp":"While solid and liquid coexist, the heat supplied is **latent heat of fusion**: it breaks the solid lattice rather than speeding molecules up. The temperature therefore **holds at the melting point, 30 °C**, until all the gallium has melted.","how":{"ask":"Decide what the temperature does while the substance is changing phase.","know":"Two phases in equilibrium at fixed pressure → temperature fixed at the transition temperature. **q = mL** during the change, not mcΔT.","steps":[{"text":"Start: already at the melting point, with solid present.","hl":"already at its melting point"},{"text":"Heat in cannot cool it, and there is no warming stage left.","strike":["dips a little.","climbs, then levels out."]},{"text":"Heat goes into melting, so the temperature is flat.","strike":["keeps climbing."]}],"trap":"Expecting a steady rise because heat keeps flowing in: during melting the energy changes the phase."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the state** of the sample.","marks":[{"t":"already at its melting point","note":"phase change in progress"},{"t":"While any solid gallium remains","note":"two phases coexist"}]},{"text":"**Latent heat.** Energy breaks intermolecular bonds; average kinetic energy, and so temperature, stays the same."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Two phases together at fixed pressure → temperature stays at the transition point until one phase is gone."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-27a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:27"],"q":"A roadside radar unit uses the Doppler shift of microwaves reflected from an approaching car to find the car's speed. What inputs does it need?","choices":["Car's speed, c, and distance","Sent frequency and car's distance","Echo wavelength and beam power","Both frequencies and c"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"The car's speed is the unknown being solved for, so it cannot be an input."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Without the returned frequency there is no shift to measure, and distance does not enter the Doppler equation."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Beam power affects echo strength only; one wavelength alone gives no shift."}],"rule":"A Doppler speed needs the frequency shift (emitted and received) and the wave speed in the medium; distance and power never enter.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"v_source ≈ (Δf/f) × wave speed: two frequencies and one wave speed are the only inputs."},"exp":"The Doppler shift gives speed from **Δf/f = 2v/c** for a reflected wave. The unit must know the **emitted frequency**, measure the **received frequency**, and know the **wave speed (c for microwaves)**. Distance and power do not appear.","how":{"ask":"List the inputs of the Doppler equation used to solve for the reflector's speed.","know":"Reflected wave, slow target: **Δf ≈ 2vf/c**, so v ≈ cΔf/(2f). Inputs: f emitted, f received, wave speed.","steps":[{"text":"The car's speed is the output, not an input.","hl":"to find the car's speed","strike":["Car's speed, c, and distance"]},{"text":"The shift needs both frequencies; distance plays no role.","strike":["Sent frequency and car's distance"]},{"text":"Power and a single wavelength cannot give a shift.","strike":["Echo wavelength and beam power"]}],"trap":"Thinking the distance to the car matters: Doppler measures speed from frequency, not position."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the unknown**.","marks":[{"t":"Doppler shift","note":"frequency change"},{"t":"the car's speed","note":"what is solved for"}]},{"text":"**Write the equation** and read off its inputs.","calc":["v ≈ c(f_received − f_sent)/(2f_sent)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Doppler speed ← emitted frequency + received frequency + wave speed in the medium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-28a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:28"],"q":"Pentane and neopentane share the formula C₅H₁₂, yet pentane boils at 36 °C and neopentane at 9.5 °C. Why does pentane boil so much higher?","choices":["Pentane is heavier","Neopentane is the more polar molecule","Pentane H-bonds","More chain contact in pentane"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Same molecular formula means the same molar mass, so mass cannot explain the gap."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Greater polarity would raise neopentane's boiling point, yet it boils lower."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Hydrocarbons have no H on N, O or F, so neither compound can hydrogen-bond."}],"rule":"Same formula, different boiling points in alkanes: compare shape. Long chains touch over more area → stronger dispersion → higher bp.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same mass, no polarity, no H-bonds: the only lever left is shape. The straight chain wins."},"exp":"Both are nonpolar alkanes with the same molar mass, held together only by **London dispersion forces**. Pentane's straight chain gives **more surface contact** between molecules than compact, spherical neopentane, so its attractions are stronger and its boiling point is higher.","how":{"ask":"Pick the intermolecular-force difference that explains which compound boils higher.","know":"Alkanes: dispersion only. At equal mass, **more surface area (less branching) → stronger dispersion → higher boiling point**.","steps":[{"text":"Same formula: same mass.","hl":"share the formula C₅H₁₂","strike":["Pentane is heavier"]},{"text":"Hydrocarbons are nonpolar and cannot H-bond.","strike":["Pentane H-bonds","Neopentane is the more polar molecule"]},{"text":"What differs is shape: the chain has more contact area than the ball."}],"trap":"Reaching for hydrogen bonding whenever boiling points differ: it needs H on N, O or F."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is the same** and what differs.","marks":[{"t":"share the formula C₅H₁₂","note":"same mass"},{"t":"boils at 36 °C","note":"straight chain, higher"}]},{"text":"**Rule out forces that cannot be there**: no H-bonds, no real dipoles in alkanes."},{"text":"**Shape decides dispersion.** Branching makes a molecule compact, so neighbors touch less."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For isomeric alkanes, more branching → lower boiling point."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-29a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:29"],"q":"A sodium electron is promoted from a 3s orbital to a 3p orbital. Which feature of the occupied orbital changes most?","choices":["Its shell (n)","Its shape","Its electron capacity","Its spin"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Both labels begin with the same number, so the principal level and shell size do not change."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Every orbital, s or p, holds at most a pair of electrons."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Spin belongs to the electron, not the orbital, and promotion does not have to flip it."}],"rule":"Read the orbital label: the number (n) sets size and shell; the letter (ℓ) sets shape; the subscript (mℓ) sets orientation.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same number, different letter: the letter is ℓ, and ℓ controls the shape."},"exp":"Going from **3s to 3p** keeps n = 3 (same shell and similar size) but changes ℓ from 0 to 1. ℓ sets the orbital's **shape**: spherical s to dumbbell-shaped p.","how":{"ask":"Find which quantum number changes, then name the property it controls.","know":"**n** → size and energy level; **ℓ** → shape (s, p, d, f); **mℓ** → orientation. Each orbital holds two electrons.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the labels: the number stays, the letter changes.","hl":"from a 3s orbital to a 3p orbital","strike":["Its shell (n)"]},{"text":"The letter is ℓ, which sets shape."},{"text":"Capacity is the same for every orbital, and spin is not an orbital property.","strike":["Its electron capacity","Its spin"]}],"trap":"Answering \"size\" by habit: size follows n, and n did not change."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **read both labels**.","marks":[{"t":"3s orbital","note":"n = 3, ℓ = 0"},{"t":"3p orbital","note":"n = 3, ℓ = 1"}]},{"text":"**Map each quantum number to its property.** n: size; ℓ: shape; mℓ: orientation."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Number changes → size; letter changes → shape; subscript changes → orientation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-38a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:38"],"pid":"sample-cp:P8","passage":"Phoracantholide is a lactone that a eucalyptus-boring beetle releases to repel predators. It forms when 9-hydroxydecanoic acid closes into a ring: the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon, and the water that leaves takes one of the original carboxyl oxygens with it. To confirm this route, chemists made the hydroxy acid with ¹⁸O in its hydroxyl group and let it cyclize in mild acid. The hydroxy acid itself was prepared from 9-oxodecanoic acid, which is achiral, in two ways. A ketoreductase from the beetle gave only the (R)-hydroxy acid, whereas sodium borohydride in ethanol, an achiral reagent in an achiral solvent, gave equal amounts of the (R) and (S) acids. Before cyclization, each batch of hydroxy acid was checked by infrared spectroscopy to confirm that no ketone remained. The beetle's gland secretion also holds squalene, an open-chain hydrocarbon, together with cholesterol from its diet and the molting hormones ecdysone and makisterone, which keep cholesterol's fused ring system.","q":"After the ¹⁸O-labeled hydroxy acid cyclizes, where is the ¹⁸O label?","choices":["The ring oxygen","The carbonyl oxygen","The released water","Both oxygens equally"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The carbonyl oxygen comes from the acid group; the label started on the hydroxyl."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"The leaving water takes a carboxyl oxygen, as the passage says, not the hydroxyl oxygen."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The mechanism is specific: each oxygen has one origin, so the label is not shared out."}],"rule":"Label tracing: follow the labeled atom through each bond made and broken. In ring closure to a lactone, the attacking OH oxygen becomes the ring O.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the nucleophile: the labeled oxygen is the one that attacks, and the attacking atom ends up in the new bond, inside the ring."},"exp":"The **hydroxyl oxygen** carries the ¹⁸O. It attacks the carboxyl carbon and becomes the **ring (alkoxy) oxygen** of the ester. The C=O oxygen is one of the acid's own oxygens, and the water that leaves takes the other one, so neither carries the label.","how":{"ask":"Follow the labeled hydroxyl oxygen through the ring-closing step the passage describes.","know":"In ester (lactone) formation, the **alcohol oxygen attacks the carbonyl carbon** and stays bonded to it as the ester's single-bonded oxygen. The acid loses an OH as water.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the hydroxyl oxygen attacks the carboxyl carbon and the leaving water takes a carboxyl oxygen.","hl":"the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon"},{"text":"Sentence 3 puts the label on that hydroxyl oxygen, so the label goes wherever the attacking oxygen goes.","hl":"with ¹⁸O in its hydroxyl group"},{"text":"Concept: the attacking oxygen becomes the ring oxygen; the C=O oxygen is the acid's own, so the label is not there or shared.","strike":["The carbonyl oxygen","Both oxygens equally"]},{"text":"The water carries away a carboxyl oxygen, not the labeled one.","strike":["The released water"]}],"trap":"Putting the label on the C=O oxygen: that oxygen came from the acid, not from the hydroxyl group."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find where the label starts** and what the passage says about each bond.","marks":[{"t":"with ¹⁸O in its hydroxyl group","note":"label on the OH oxygen"},{"t":"the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon","note":"OH oxygen forms the new bond"},{"t":"takes one of the original carboxyl oxygens","note":"water comes from the acid"}]},{"text":"**Follow the atom.** The attacking oxygen bonds to the carbonyl carbon and becomes the ring oxygen."},{"text":"**Also:** this is how chemists showed that esterification breaks the acid's C–O bond, not the alcohol's."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Trace a label through the bonds made and broken; the nucleophile's atom ends up in the new bond."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-39a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:39"],"pid":"sample-cp:P8","passage":"Phoracantholide is a lactone that a eucalyptus-boring beetle releases to repel predators. It forms when 9-hydroxydecanoic acid closes into a ring: the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon, and the water that leaves takes one of the original carboxyl oxygens with it. To confirm this route, chemists made the hydroxy acid with ¹⁸O in its hydroxyl group and let it cyclize in mild acid. The hydroxy acid itself was prepared from 9-oxodecanoic acid, which is achiral, in two ways. A ketoreductase from the beetle gave only the (R)-hydroxy acid, whereas sodium borohydride in ethanol, an achiral reagent in an achiral solvent, gave equal amounts of the (R) and (S) acids. Before cyclization, each batch of hydroxy acid was checked by infrared spectroscopy to confirm that no ketone remained. The beetle's gland secretion also holds squalene, an open-chain hydrocarbon, together with cholesterol from its diet and the molting hormones ecdysone and makisterone, which keep cholesterol's fused ring system.","q":"What is the total number of atoms in the phoracantholide ring?","choices":["9","10","11","12"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That counts only the ring carbons and leaves out the oxygen that closes the ring."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That counts every carbon in \"decanoic\"; the terminal methyl carbon hangs outside the ring."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The carbonyl oxygen is double-bonded outside the ring, and the methyl is a branch."}],"rule":"Lactone ring = the carbons from the carboxyl carbon to the carbon holding the OH, plus that one oxygen. Branches and C=O oxygens stay outside.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the OH locant: the hydroxyl sits on C-9, so the ring holds C-1 through C-9 plus one oxygen."},"exp":"The ring closes when the **C-9 oxygen** bonds to the **carboxyl carbon (C-1)**. The ring therefore holds **C-1 through C-9 (9 carbons) plus that oxygen = 10 atoms**. The terminal methyl carbon and the C=O oxygen are outside the ring.","how":{"ask":"Use the precursor's name and the ring-closing bond to count the ring atoms.","know":"In a lactone from an n-hydroxy acid, the ring holds **C-1 (carboxyl) through C-n plus the ring oxygen**: n + 1 atoms. Substituents and the carbonyl oxygen are outside.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 names the precursor and the bond that closes the ring: the C-9 oxygen bonds to the carboxyl carbon.","hl":"the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon"},{"text":"Concept: the ring runs from the carboxyl carbon (C-1) along the chain to C-9, then through the oxygen back to C-1. Forgetting that oxygen undercounts.","calc":["9 carbons + 1 oxygen"],"strike":["9"]},{"text":"The methyl carbon past C-9 is a branch, and the C=O oxygen is not in the ring.","strike":["11","12"]},{"text":"Ring atoms: nine carbons and one oxygen."}],"trap":"Counting all the carbons in the acid's name: only the carbons between the carboxyl carbon and the OH carbon are in the ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two atoms that bond** to close the ring.","marks":[{"t":"9-hydroxydecanoic acid","note":"OH on C-9 of a C10 chain"},{"t":"the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon","note":"O(C-9) bonds to C-1"}]},{"text":"**Walk the ring.** C-1 → C-2 → … → C-9 → O → back to C-1: 9 carbons + 1 oxygen."},{"text":"**Also:** γ-lactones (OH on C-4) have 5-membered rings and δ-lactones (OH on C-5) have 6-membered rings, by the same count."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ring size of a lactone = locant of the OH carbon + 1; branches and the C=O oxygen stay outside."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-40a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:40"],"pid":"sample-cp:P8","passage":"Phoracantholide is a lactone that a eucalyptus-boring beetle releases to repel predators. It forms when 9-hydroxydecanoic acid closes into a ring: the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon, and the water that leaves takes one of the original carboxyl oxygens with it. To confirm this route, chemists made the hydroxy acid with ¹⁸O in its hydroxyl group and let it cyclize in mild acid. The hydroxy acid itself was prepared from 9-oxodecanoic acid, which is achiral, in two ways. A ketoreductase from the beetle gave only the (R)-hydroxy acid, whereas sodium borohydride in ethanol, an achiral reagent in an achiral solvent, gave equal amounts of the (R) and (S) acids. Before cyclization, each batch of hydroxy acid was checked by infrared spectroscopy to confirm that no ketone remained. The beetle's gland secretion also holds squalene, an open-chain hydrocarbon, together with cholesterol from its diet and the molting hormones ecdysone and makisterone, which keep cholesterol's fused ring system.","q":"Which IR features should each batch of hydroxy acid show?","choices":["C=O plus broad O–H","C=O alone","Broad O–H alone","C=O plus aromatic C=C"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"\"No ketone\" does not mean no C=O: the carboxylic acid still has its carbonyl."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the spectrum of the finished lactone, which has no O–H; the batches are checked before cyclization."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The molecule is an open chain with no benzene ring, so it has no aromatic C=C bands."}],"rule":"IR: list the functional groups actually present. C=O (acid, ester, ketone) → strong sharp band; O–H (alcohol, acid) → broad band.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the name: \"hydroxy acid\" = an alcohol O–H plus a carboxylic acid (C=O and O–H). Both bands must appear."},"exp":"The batches are the **hydroxy acid, before cyclization**. Its **carboxylic acid** keeps a C=O, and both the acid and the alcohol give a **broad O–H** band. \"No ketone remained\" rules out leftover keto acid, not the acid's C=O. The lactone, made later, would show C=O with no O–H.","how":{"ask":"Decide which compound the IR check is run on, then list its bands.","know":"**C=O stretch**: strong, sharp (ketones, acids, esters). **O–H stretch**: broad (alcohols; very broad for carboxylic acids). **Aromatic C=C**: only with a benzene-type ring.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the batches checked are the hydroxy acid, before ring closure.","hl":"Before cyclization, each batch of hydroxy acid was checked"},{"text":"Concept: a hydroxy acid has an alcohol O–H and a carboxylic acid (C=O and O–H). The ketone is gone, but the acid's C=O is still there.","strike":["Broad O–H alone"]},{"text":"Both O–H groups are still present, so the spectrum cannot be C=O alone; that is the lactone's pattern.","strike":["C=O alone"]},{"text":"There is no benzene ring anywhere in the chain.","strike":["C=O plus aromatic C=C"]}],"trap":"Reading \"no ketone remained\" as \"no C=O\": a carboxylic acid has a carbonyl too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find which compound is in the IR cell**.","marks":[{"t":"Before cyclization, each batch of hydroxy acid was checked","note":"the open-chain hydroxy acid"},{"t":"to confirm that no ketone remained","note":"ketone gone, acid C=O stays"}]},{"text":"**List its groups.** Alcohol O–H + carboxylic acid (C=O + O–H) → C=O band and a broad O–H band."},{"text":"**Also:** after ring closure, both O–H groups are used up; the lactone shows C=O with no O–H."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Name the compound being measured, list its functional groups, then match each group to its IR band."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-41a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:41"],"pid":"sample-cp:P8","passage":"Phoracantholide is a lactone that a eucalyptus-boring beetle releases to repel predators. It forms when 9-hydroxydecanoic acid closes into a ring: the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon, and the water that leaves takes one of the original carboxyl oxygens with it. To confirm this route, chemists made the hydroxy acid with ¹⁸O in its hydroxyl group and let it cyclize in mild acid. The hydroxy acid itself was prepared from 9-oxodecanoic acid, which is achiral, in two ways. A ketoreductase from the beetle gave only the (R)-hydroxy acid, whereas sodium borohydride in ethanol, an achiral reagent in an achiral solvent, gave equal amounts of the (R) and (S) acids. Before cyclization, each batch of hydroxy acid was checked by infrared spectroscopy to confirm that no ketone remained. The beetle's gland secretion also holds squalene, an open-chain hydrocarbon, together with cholesterol from its diet and the molting hormones ecdysone and makisterone, which keep cholesterol's fused ring system.","q":"What best explains the outcome of the borohydride route?","choices":["Both faces react equally","The oxo acid is chiral","The (R) acid is more stable","Borohydride is a chiral reagent"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Enantiomers have identical energies, so neither is more stable; and stability would not give equal amounts."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"The passage calls 9-oxodecanoic acid achiral."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The passage calls the reagent and solvent achiral, and achiral hydride sources cannot favor a face."}],"rule":"Achiral substrate + achiral reagent + achiral solvent → racemic product. Only a chiral influence (an enzyme, a chiral catalyst) favors one face.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the word \"achiral\" for every component: nothing chiral is present, so the two mirror-image paths are equally likely."},"exp":"A planar C=O can be attacked from **either face**. With an **achiral** keto acid, an **achiral** reagent and an **achiral** solvent, the two attacks are mirror images with equal energy, so they happen **equally often** and give a 50:50 (racemic) mix. The enzyme route differs because its chiral active site favors one face.","how":{"ask":"Explain why the borohydride reduction gives equal amounts of (R) and (S) product.","know":"A flat carbonyl has **two faces**. Attack on one face gives (R), on the other (S). Only a **chiral environment** makes one path faster; otherwise the product is racemic.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says borohydride and ethanol are both achiral, and sentence 4 calls the keto acid achiral.","hl":"an achiral reagent in an achiral solvent"},{"text":"Concept: with nothing chiral present, hydride attack on either face of the flat C=O is equally likely. A chiral keto acid or reagent would contradict the passage.","strike":["The oxo acid is chiral","Borohydride is a chiral reagent"]},{"text":"Enantiomers have the same energy, so stability cannot favor one, and it could not explain equal amounts anyway.","strike":["The (R) acid is more stable"]},{"text":"Equal attack on both faces gives the 50:50 mixture."}],"trap":"Explaining a racemic result with stability: mirror-image products are equally stable, so the answer lies in how the reagent approaches."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is chiral and what is not**.","marks":[{"t":"which is achiral, in two ways","note":"substrate achiral"},{"t":"an achiral reagent in an achiral solvent","note":"no chiral influence"},{"t":"gave equal amounts of the (R) and (S) acids","note":"racemic"}]},{"text":"**Two faces.** The flat C=O can be attacked from above or below; each face leads to one enantiomer."},{"text":"**Also:** the ketoreductase gave only (R) because its chiral active site holds the ketone so hydride reaches one face."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** All achiral → racemic. One enantiomer only → a chiral influence (enzyme or chiral catalyst) chose the face."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-42a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:42"],"pid":"sample-cp:P8","passage":"Phoracantholide is a lactone that a eucalyptus-boring beetle releases to repel predators. It forms when 9-hydroxydecanoic acid closes into a ring: the C-9 hydroxyl oxygen adds to the carboxyl carbon, and the water that leaves takes one of the original carboxyl oxygens with it. To confirm this route, chemists made the hydroxy acid with ¹⁸O in its hydroxyl group and let it cyclize in mild acid. The hydroxy acid itself was prepared from 9-oxodecanoic acid, which is achiral, in two ways. A ketoreductase from the beetle gave only the (R)-hydroxy acid, whereas sodium borohydride in ethanol, an achiral reagent in an achiral solvent, gave equal amounts of the (R) and (S) acids. Before cyclization, each batch of hydroxy acid was checked by infrared spectroscopy to confirm that no ketone remained. The beetle's gland secretion also holds squalene, an open-chain hydrocarbon, together with cholesterol from its diet and the molting hormones ecdysone and makisterone, which keep cholesterol's fused ring system.","q":"Which compound in the beetle's gland secretion is NOT a steroid?","choices":["Cholesterol","Ecdysone","Squalene","Makisterone"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"The passage says ecdysone keeps cholesterol's fused ring system."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Makisterone also keeps the fused ring system, so it is a steroid."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Cholesterol is the parent steroid: four fused rings, three six-membered and one five-membered."}],"rule":"Steroid = the fused four-ring core (6-6-6-5). Open-chain lipids, even steroid precursors, and eicosanoids are not steroids.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on \"open-chain\": a steroid must have the fused four-ring core, so the one compound with no rings is out."},"exp":"A steroid has the **fused four-ring core** of cholesterol. Ecdysone and makisterone keep that ring system, and cholesterol is the parent steroid. **Squalene** is an **open-chain** hydrocarbon: it is the precursor that cyclizes into sterols, but it has no rings, so it is **not** a steroid.","how":{"ask":"Find the compound in the passage that lacks the steroid ring system.","know":"**Steroid** = four fused rings (three cyclohexane rings and one cyclopentane). Cholesterol and the hormones made from it are steroids. Squalene, the linear triterpene that is folded into that core, is not.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 describes squalene as open-chain, while the molting hormones keep cholesterol's fused ring system.","hl":"squalene, an open-chain hydrocarbon"},{"text":"Concept: a steroid is defined by the fused four-ring core. Cholesterol has it, and the passage says ecdysone and makisterone keep it.","hl":"which keep cholesterol's fused ring system","strike":["Cholesterol","Ecdysone","Makisterone"]},{"text":"Squalene has no rings, so it is the one that is not a steroid."}],"trap":"Calling a steroid precursor a steroid: squalene becomes a sterol only after it cyclizes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find how the passage describes each compound**.","marks":[{"t":"squalene, an open-chain hydrocarbon","note":"no rings"},{"t":"which keep cholesterol's fused ring system","note":"steroids"}]},{"text":"**Define the class.** Steroid = the fused 6-6-6-5 ring core. Side chains and functional groups can vary."},{"text":"**Also:** prostaglandins and leukotrienes (from arachidonic acid) are eicosanoids, not steroids, for the same reason."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For a NOT-a-steroid question, look for the compound without the fused four-ring core."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-43a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:43"],"q":"A teenager's eyeball is slightly too long, so light from distant objects focuses in front of her retina. Which corrective lens does she need?","choices":["Converging lens","Diverging lens","Prism lens","Cylindrical lens"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"A converging lens pulls the focus even farther forward; it corrects an eye that focuses behind the retina."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A prism only shifts where the image lands sideways; it does not move the focal point back."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A cylindrical lens corrects astigmatism, a direction-dependent blur, not an overall focusing error."}],"rule":"Image lands short of the retina (nearsighted) → spread the light first, negative power. Image lands past it (farsighted) → converge it, positive power.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Ask where the image lands. In front → move it back → the lens must spread rays (negative power). Behind → the opposite."},"exp":"The eye is **myopic**: its image forms **short of** the retina. A **diverging (negative-power)** lens spreads the rays slightly so the eye's optics bring them to focus farther back, on the retina.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the light must be converged or spread to move the focus onto the retina.","know":"**Myopia** (long eye, focus in front) → diverging lens. **Hyperopia** (short eye, focus behind) → converging lens.","steps":[{"text":"Where does the image form? Short of the retina.","hl":"focuses in front of her retina"},{"text":"To push the focus back, the lens must spread the rays; a converging lens would move it forward.","strike":["Converging lens"]},{"text":"Prisms and cylindrical lenses fix other problems (aim and astigmatism).","strike":["Prism lens","Cylindrical lens"]}],"trap":"Mixing up near- and farsightedness: the long eye focuses too early and needs negative power."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find where the image lands**.","marks":[{"t":"slightly too long","note":"myopic eye"},{"t":"in front of her retina","note":"focus too early"}]},{"text":"**Move the focus back** by making the incoming rays diverge a little before they enter the eye."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Image short of the retina → negative (diverging) lens; behind it → positive (converging) lens."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-47a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:47"],"pid":"sample-cp:P9","passage":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase (SCS) breaks the thioester of succinyl-CoA in the citric acid cycle and uses the energy released to make GTP from GDP. In its first step, inorganic phosphate attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon, releasing free CoA and forming succinyl phosphate; the phosphoryl group then passes to a histidine of the enzyme and on to GDP. In a crystal structure of SCS from a hot-spring bacterium, a Mg²⁺ ion bound to the GDP phosphates lies within 4 Å of five side chains: Glu197, Lys202, Thr206, Asp213 and Gln245. Activity was followed in mitochondrial extracts supplied with α-ketoglutarate, NAD⁺ and CoA, in which α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase makes the succinyl-CoA that SCS uses. Only NADH, not NAD⁺, absorbs near-ultraviolet light, and in every extract the absorbance at that wavelength rose steadily for the first five minutes. The team also tested Inhibitor T, a designed molecule that carries four carboxylic acid groups (pKa ≈ 4.2), one phosphate monoester group (pKa values ≈ 1.5 and 6.5) and one primary amino group (pKa ≈ 9.6). All assays were run at pH 7.4, and Inhibitor T slowed the rise in absorbance by about half.","q":"At the assay pH, Inhibitor T carries a net charge closest to:","choices":["−7","−6","−5","−4"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That counts the amino group as negative; a protonated amine carries +1."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"At pH 7.4 the amino group (pKa ≈ 9.6) is still protonated and adds +1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both phosphate pKa values lie below 7.4, so the monoester carries −2, not −1."}],"rule":"Net charge: for each group compare pH with pKa. pH above pKa → deprotonated (acid −1 each, amine neutral); pH below → protonated (acid neutral, amine +1).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on pH 7.4: every acid pKa below it is ionized (4 × −1, phosphate −2), and the amine, above it, is +1. Add them up."},"exp":"At **pH 7.4**: four carboxylic acids (pKa ≈ 4.2) → **4 × (−1)**; the phosphate monoester (pKa values 1.5 and 6.5, both below 7.4) → **−2**; the primary amine (pKa ≈ 9.6, above 7.4) stays protonated → **+1**. Net: −4 − 2 + 1 = **−5**.","how":{"ask":"Use each group's pKa and the assay pH to find Inhibitor T's net charge.","know":"**pH > pKa → the group has lost its proton.** Acids become negative; amines become neutral. **pH < pKa → protonated**: acids neutral, amines +1. A phosphate monoester has two acidic OH groups (up to −2).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 lists Inhibitor T's ionizable groups and pKa values; sentence 7 gives the assay pH, 7.4.","hl":"four carboxylic acid groups (pKa ≈ 4.2)"},{"text":"Concept: compare each pKa with 7.4. Carboxyls: 4 × (−1). Phosphate monoester: both pKa values are below 7.4, so −2, not −1.","calc":["−4 + (−2) = −6 from the acid groups"],"strike":["−4"]},{"text":"The amine (pKa ≈ 9.6) is still protonated at 7.4, so it adds +1; it is not negative, and it cannot be left out.","hl":"one primary amino group (pKa ≈ 9.6)","calc":["−6 + 1"],"strike":["−6","−7"]}],"trap":"Forgetting the positive group: an amine with a pKa above the pH is protonated and offsets one negative charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every ionizable group and the pH**.","marks":[{"t":"four carboxylic acid groups (pKa ≈ 4.2)","note":"−1 each"},{"t":"one phosphate monoester group (pKa values ≈ 1.5 and 6.5)","note":"−2"},{"t":"one primary amino group (pKa ≈ 9.6)","note":"+1"},{"t":"pH 7.4","note":"compare each pKa with this"}]},{"text":"**Compare pH with pKa.** pKa below the pH → proton gone; pKa above the pH → proton on."},{"text":"**Add the charges.**","calc":["4(−1) + (−2) + (+1) = −5"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For each group, pH > pKa → deprotonated, pH < pKa → protonated; then sum the charges, counting positive groups too."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-48a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:48"],"pid":"sample-cp:P9","passage":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase (SCS) breaks the thioester of succinyl-CoA in the citric acid cycle and uses the energy released to make GTP from GDP. In its first step, inorganic phosphate attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon, releasing free CoA and forming succinyl phosphate; the phosphoryl group then passes to a histidine of the enzyme and on to GDP. In a crystal structure of SCS from a hot-spring bacterium, a Mg²⁺ ion bound to the GDP phosphates lies within 4 Å of five side chains: Glu197, Lys202, Thr206, Asp213 and Gln245. Activity was followed in mitochondrial extracts supplied with α-ketoglutarate, NAD⁺ and CoA, in which α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase makes the succinyl-CoA that SCS uses. Only NADH, not NAD⁺, absorbs near-ultraviolet light, and in every extract the absorbance at that wavelength rose steadily for the first five minutes. The team also tested Inhibitor T, a designed molecule that carries four carboxylic acid groups (pKa ≈ 4.2), one phosphate monoester group (pKa values ≈ 1.5 and 6.5) and one primary amino group (pKa ≈ 9.6). All assays were run at pH 7.4, and Inhibitor T slowed the rise in absorbance by about half.","q":"Which atom is the succinyl carbonyl carbon attached to in succinyl phosphate?","choices":["CoA's thiol sulfur","An oxygen of phosphate","A histidine nitrogen","A ribose oxygen of GDP"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That bond is broken in this step: the passage says free CoA is released."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"The histidine receives the phosphoryl group later; the succinyl group never moves to it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"GDP is the last acceptor of the phosphoryl group, and it never bonds the succinyl carbon."}],"rule":"Acyl transfer: the attacking nucleophile takes the leaving group's place on the carbonyl carbon. Name the nucleophile, and that is the new partner.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the verb: \"phosphate attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon … releasing free CoA\". The attacker replaces what is released."},"exp":"Phosphate **attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon** and **CoA leaves**: a nucleophilic acyl substitution. The succinyl carbonyl carbon is now bonded to **an oxygen of the phosphate**, forming a mixed anhydride (an acyl phosphate). The histidine and GDP later receive the phosphoryl group, not the succinyl group.","how":{"ask":"Follow the first SCS step to see what replaces CoA on the succinyl carbonyl carbon.","know":"In **nucleophilic acyl substitution**, a nucleophile adds to the carbonyl carbon and a leaving group departs. In a thioester the acyl carbon is bonded to sulfur; after attack by phosphate, it is bonded to a phosphate oxygen.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says phosphate attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon and free CoA is released.","hl":"inorganic phosphate attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon, releasing free CoA"},{"text":"Concept: the nucleophile replaces the leaving group. CoA's sulfur has left, so it is no longer bonded.","strike":["CoA's thiol sulfur"]},{"text":"The histidine and GDP receive only the phosphoryl group, in later steps.","hl":"the phosphoryl group then passes to a histidine of the enzyme and on to GDP","strike":["A histidine nitrogen","A ribose oxygen of GDP"]},{"text":"Phosphate attacked with one of its oxygens, so that oxygen now holds the succinyl carbon."}],"trap":"Keeping the bond to CoA: the passage says CoA is released, so the thioester is gone in succinyl phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the bond made and the bond broken**.","marks":[{"t":"inorganic phosphate attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon","note":"new bond: phosphate O–C"},{"t":"releasing free CoA","note":"C–S bond broken"}]},{"text":"**Acyl substitution.** The nucleophile (a phosphate oxygen) takes the place of the leaving group (CoA–S⁻)."},{"text":"**Also:** thioesters and acyl phosphates are both high-energy acyl carriers; that energy ends up in GTP."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In acyl transfer, the attacking atom becomes the acyl carbon's new partner; the leaving group is no longer bonded."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-50a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:50"],"pid":"sample-cp:P9","passage":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase (SCS) breaks the thioester of succinyl-CoA in the citric acid cycle and uses the energy released to make GTP from GDP. In its first step, inorganic phosphate attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon, releasing free CoA and forming succinyl phosphate; the phosphoryl group then passes to a histidine of the enzyme and on to GDP. In a crystal structure of SCS from a hot-spring bacterium, a Mg²⁺ ion bound to the GDP phosphates lies within 4 Å of five side chains: Glu197, Lys202, Thr206, Asp213 and Gln245. Activity was followed in mitochondrial extracts supplied with α-ketoglutarate, NAD⁺ and CoA, in which α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase makes the succinyl-CoA that SCS uses. Only NADH, not NAD⁺, absorbs near-ultraviolet light, and in every extract the absorbance at that wavelength rose steadily for the first five minutes. The team also tested Inhibitor T, a designed molecule that carries four carboxylic acid groups (pKa ≈ 4.2), one phosphate monoester group (pKa values ≈ 1.5 and 6.5) and one primary amino group (pKa ≈ 9.6). All assays were run at pH 7.4, and Inhibitor T slowed the rise in absorbance by about half.","q":"Which two side chains could best bind the Mg²⁺ ion through ionic attraction?","choices":["Glu197 and Asp213","Lys202 and Gln245","Thr206 and Gln245","Lys202 and Asp213"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Lys is positive at neutral pH, so it repels a cation instead of attracting it."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Only one of these is negative; the Lys side chain is positive and repels Mg²⁺."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Thr and Gln are polar but uncharged: they can hydrogen-bond, not form ionic bonds."}],"rule":"Ionic attraction needs opposite full charges: a cation (Mg²⁺, a polyamine) pairs with Asp/Glu; an anion (phosphate) pairs with Lys/Arg.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the ligand's charge: Mg²⁺ is positive, so keep only the acidic side chains, Asp and Glu."},"exp":"Mg²⁺ is a **cation**, so ionic attraction needs **negatively charged** side chains. At neutral pH, **Glu and Asp** are carboxylates (−1). Lys is positive and would repel the ion; Thr and Gln are polar but uncharged. So **Glu197 and Asp213** bind it best.","how":{"ask":"Pick the two listed side chains whose charge is opposite to that of the bound ion.","know":"At pH ≈ 7: **Asp, Glu → −1**; **Lys, Arg → +1** (His partly); **Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln → polar, uncharged**. Ionic interactions need opposite charges.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 lists the five side chains within 4 Å of the Mg²⁺ ion.","hl":"Glu197, Lys202, Thr206, Asp213 and Gln245"},{"text":"Concept: the ion is 2+, so it needs negative partners. Lys is positive and repels it.","strike":["Lys202 and Gln245","Lys202 and Asp213"]},{"text":"Thr and Gln are uncharged; they can only hydrogen-bond.","strike":["Thr206 and Gln245"]},{"text":"The two acidic side chains, Glu and Asp, are left."}],"trap":"Picking Lys because it binds phosphates: here the question is about the Mg²⁺ ion, which is positive."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the ion and the candidate side chains**.","marks":[{"t":"a Mg²⁺ ion bound to the GDP phosphates","note":"a cation"},{"t":"Glu197, Lys202, Thr206, Asp213 and Gln245","note":"sort by charge"}]},{"text":"**Sort by charge at neutral pH.** Glu, Asp: −1. Lys: +1. Thr, Gln: uncharged."},{"text":"**Also:** the GDP phosphates themselves would pair with Lys or Arg, the positive side chains."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ionic bond = opposite full charges. Cation → Asp/Glu; anion → Lys/Arg; Ser/Thr/Asn/Gln give only hydrogen bonds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-51a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:51"],"pid":"sample-cp:P9","passage":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase (SCS) breaks the thioester of succinyl-CoA in the citric acid cycle and uses the energy released to make GTP from GDP. In its first step, inorganic phosphate attacks the thioester carbonyl carbon, releasing free CoA and forming succinyl phosphate; the phosphoryl group then passes to a histidine of the enzyme and on to GDP. In a crystal structure of SCS from a hot-spring bacterium, a Mg²⁺ ion bound to the GDP phosphates lies within 4 Å of five side chains: Glu197, Lys202, Thr206, Asp213 and Gln245. Activity was followed in mitochondrial extracts supplied with α-ketoglutarate, NAD⁺ and CoA, in which α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase makes the succinyl-CoA that SCS uses. Only NADH, not NAD⁺, absorbs near-ultraviolet light, and in every extract the absorbance at that wavelength rose steadily for the first five minutes. The team also tested Inhibitor T, a designed molecule that carries four carboxylic acid groups (pKa ≈ 4.2), one phosphate monoester group (pKa values ≈ 1.5 and 6.5) and one primary amino group (pKa ≈ 9.6). All assays were run at pH 7.4, and Inhibitor T slowed the rise in absorbance by about half.","q":"In the extracts without inhibitor, the steady rise in absorbance shows that:","choices":["NAD⁺ is reduced.","NADH is oxidized.","NADPH is formed.","NADH binds SCS."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Losing NADH would make the absorbance fall, but it rose."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Absorbance tracks how much NADH is present; binding would not make it climb for minutes."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The extracts were given NAD⁺, not NADP⁺, so no NADPH can form here."}],"rule":"Absorbance tracks the absorbing species (Beer's law). Rising → that species is being made; falling → it is being used up.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the one absorber: only NADH absorbs, so a rising signal means more NADH, made by adding electrons to NAD⁺."},"exp":"Only **NADH** absorbs at that wavelength, and absorbance is proportional to its concentration (Beer's law). A **steady rise** means NADH is accumulating, which means **NAD⁺ is being reduced** (it gains a hydride) as α-ketoglutarate is oxidized. Oxidation of NADH would make the signal fall.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a rising signal means when only NADH absorbs, and state it as a redox change.","know":"**A = εbc**: absorbance rises and falls with [NADH]. NAD⁺ + H⁻ → NADH is a **reduction**; NADH → NAD⁺ is an **oxidation**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says only NADH absorbs and the absorbance rose steadily.","hl":"the absorbance at that wavelength rose steadily"},{"text":"Concept: absorbance follows [NADH], so NADH is accumulating. Oxidizing NADH would lower the signal.","strike":["NADH is oxidized."]},{"text":"The extracts were given NAD⁺, not NADP⁺, and binding would not raise the amount of NADH for minutes.","hl":"supplied with α-ketoglutarate, NAD⁺ and CoA","strike":["NADPH is formed.","NADH binds SCS."]},{"text":"More NADH is made by adding electrons to NAD⁺: a reduction."}],"trap":"Mixing up the direction: a falling signal means NADH is oxidized; this one rises."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what absorbs and which way the signal moves**.","marks":[{"t":"Only NADH, not NAD⁺, absorbs near-ultraviolet light","note":"signal = [NADH]"},{"t":"rose steadily","note":"NADH accumulating"}]},{"text":"**Translate to redox.** NAD⁺ → NADH is a reduction (gain of a hydride); NADH → NAD⁺ is an oxidation."},{"text":"**Also:** coupled assays use this signal to report an enzyme they cannot watch directly."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Signal up → the absorbing species is made; signal down → it is used. Then name the redox change."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-53a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:53"],"pid":"sample-cp:P10","passage":"Blood flow through a calf muscle can be measured by injecting a small dose of xenon-133 in saline into the muscle and timing how fast the blood washes the tracer out. Each xenon-133 nucleus decays by β⁻ emission to cesium-133, as a neutron in the nucleus turns into a proton, and a gamma ray released after the decay is counted by a detector held over the calf. Before the study, a hospital physicist checked one vial in a calibrated counter: it read 360 MBq on delivery, 180 MBq after 5.2 days, 90 MBq after 10.4 days and 45 MBq after 15.6 days. During each session, a volunteer pushed a foot-pedal ergometer at a steady 12 W for 4.0 minutes while the washout was recorded. Ultrasound was also used during each visit: pulse-echo images gave the width of the popliteal artery, its depth below the skin and the thickness of the muscle layer, while a second probe tracked how fast the Achilles tendon slid during each push.","q":"From the vial readings, the half-life of xenon-133 is closest to:","choices":["2.6 days","5.2 days","10.4 days","15.6 days"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That halves the time instead of finding how long one halving of activity takes."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"By that reading the activity is down to one quarter: two half-lives, not one."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"By that reading the activity is down to one eighth: three half-lives."}],"rule":"Half-life = how long one halving of activity takes. Find one halving in the readings, then check that each later halving takes the same time.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Scale by halves: 360 → 180 is one halving. Read the time beside it; the later readings halve again at equal steps."},"exp":"The activity halves at each step: **360 → 180 → 90 → 45 MBq**, and each halving takes **5.2 days**. That constant halving time is the half-life. The 10.4- and 15.6-day readings are two and three half-lives.","how":{"ask":"Read the vial data and find the time it takes the activity to fall by half.","know":"**Half-life**: the time for activity (or the number of nuclei) to fall to **half**. After n half-lives, activity = A₀/2ⁿ.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the readings: 360, 180, 90 and 45 MBq over time.","hl":"360 MBq on delivery, 180 MBq after 5.2 days"},{"text":"Concept: one half-life is one halving of activity. 360 → 180 MBq takes 5.2 days, and so does each later halving."},{"text":"The 10.4-day and 15.6-day readings are one quarter and one eighth of the start: two and three half-lives.","strike":["10.4 days","15.6 days"]},{"text":"Halving the time is not the same as halving the activity.","strike":["2.6 days"]}],"trap":"Taking a later reading time as the half-life: that time covers several halvings."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the readings** and look for a halving.","marks":[{"t":"360 MBq on delivery, 180 MBq after 5.2 days","note":"one halving"},{"t":"45 MBq after 15.6 days","note":"three halvings"}]},{"text":"**Check the pattern.** Each halving (360 → 180 → 90 → 45) takes the same time: that is first-order decay."},{"text":"**Also:** after n half-lives the activity is A₀/2ⁿ, so 1/8 left means three half-lives."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Half-life = the time for one halving; confirm it with a second halving in the data."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-54a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:54"],"pid":"sample-cp:P10","passage":"Blood flow through a calf muscle can be measured by injecting a small dose of xenon-133 in saline into the muscle and timing how fast the blood washes the tracer out. Each xenon-133 nucleus decays by β⁻ emission to cesium-133, as a neutron in the nucleus turns into a proton, and a gamma ray released after the decay is counted by a detector held over the calf. Before the study, a hospital physicist checked one vial in a calibrated counter: it read 360 MBq on delivery, 180 MBq after 5.2 days, 90 MBq after 10.4 days and 45 MBq after 15.6 days. During each session, a volunteer pushed a foot-pedal ergometer at a steady 12 W for 4.0 minutes while the washout was recorded. Ultrasound was also used during each visit: pulse-echo images gave the width of the popliteal artery, its depth below the skin and the thickness of the muscle layer, while a second probe tracked how fast the Achilles tendon slid during each push.","q":"Which particle given off when a xenon-133 nucleus decays is electrically charged?","choices":["Antineutrino","Electron","Gamma ray","Proton"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"The new proton stays inside the nucleus, turning xenon into cesium; it is not emitted."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The antineutrino released with it is electrically neutral."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A gamma ray is a photon, which carries no charge."}],"rule":"β⁻: n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino; the e⁻ is the charged particle. β⁺: p → n + e⁺ + neutrino. Gamma rays and neutrinos are neutral.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the symbol: β⁻ means the emitted beta particle carries a minus sign, so it is the negative particle."},"exp":"In **β⁻ decay** a neutron becomes a proton and the nucleus emits an **electron** (the β⁻ particle) and an antineutrino; charge is conserved, 0 = (+1) + (−1). The proton stays in the nucleus. The antineutrino and the gamma ray are neutral, so the **electron** is the charged particle emitted.","how":{"ask":"Identify the decay type in the passage and name the charged particle it emits.","know":"**β⁻ decay**: n → p + e⁻ + ν̄ (emits an electron). **β⁺ decay**: p → n + e⁺ + ν (emits a positron). Gamma rays and neutrinos carry no charge.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says xenon-133 decays by β⁻ emission as a neutron turns into a proton.","hl":"decays by β⁻ emission to cesium-133"},{"text":"Concept: in β⁻ decay the new proton stays in the nucleus (that is why xenon becomes cesium); only the electron and antineutrino leave.","strike":["Proton"]},{"text":"The antineutrino and the gamma ray are neutral.","strike":["Antineutrino","Gamma ray"]},{"text":"Charge balance: neutron (0) → proton (+1) + emitted particle (−1)."}],"trap":"Picking the proton the passage mentions: it is made inside the nucleus and never leaves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the decay type**.","marks":[{"t":"decays by β⁻ emission to cesium-133","note":"β⁻ decay"},{"t":"a neutron in the nucleus turns into a proton","note":"proton stays in"}]},{"text":"**Write the decay.** n → p + e⁻ + ν̄. Charge: 0 = +1 − 1 + 0."},{"text":"**Also:** β⁺ decay, used in PET, is the mirror case: p → n + e⁺ + ν, emitting a positron."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** β⁻ emits an electron, β⁺ a positron; neutrinos and gamma rays are neutral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-55a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:55"],"pid":"sample-cp:P10","passage":"Blood flow through a calf muscle can be measured by injecting a small dose of xenon-133 in saline into the muscle and timing how fast the blood washes the tracer out. Each xenon-133 nucleus decays by β⁻ emission to cesium-133, as a neutron in the nucleus turns into a proton, and a gamma ray released after the decay is counted by a detector held over the calf. Before the study, a hospital physicist checked one vial in a calibrated counter: it read 360 MBq on delivery, 180 MBq after 5.2 days, 90 MBq after 10.4 days and 45 MBq after 15.6 days. During each session, a volunteer pushed a foot-pedal ergometer at a steady 12 W for 4.0 minutes while the washout was recorded. Ultrasound was also used during each visit: pulse-echo images gave the width of the popliteal artery, its depth below the skin and the thickness of the muscle layer, while a second probe tracked how fast the Achilles tendon slid during each push.","q":"Which of the study's measurements depended on the Doppler effect rather than on echo timing?","choices":["Popliteal artery width","Popliteal artery depth","Muscle layer thickness","Tendon sliding speed"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"A width is the distance between two echoes, found from their timing, not from a frequency shift."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Depth comes from how long an echo takes to return: ordinary pulse-echo imaging."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Thickness is the gap between two reflecting surfaces, also measured by echo timing."}],"rule":"Pulse-echo times echoes → distances and positions. Doppler compares echo frequency with emitted frequency → speed of a moving reflector.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor on the units: three choices are lengths (echo timing); only a speed needs a frequency shift."},"exp":"**Pulse-echo** imaging times returning echoes, which gives **distances**: the artery's width and depth and the muscle thickness. The **Doppler effect** shifts the echo frequency when the reflector **moves**, so measuring **how fast the tendon slid** needed the Doppler probe.","how":{"ask":"Sort the study's measurements into distances and speeds, and pick the one that needs a frequency shift.","know":"**Pulse-echo**: distance = (speed of sound × echo time) / 2 → positions, depths, thicknesses. **Doppler**: frequency shift of echoes from a moving reflector → its **speed**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says a second probe tracked how fast the tendon slid, while pulse-echo gave width, depth and thickness.","hl":"a second probe tracked how fast the Achilles tendon slid"},{"text":"Concept: echo timing gives distances; a frequency shift gives speed. Width, depth and thickness are distances.","strike":["Popliteal artery width","Popliteal artery depth","Muscle layer thickness"]},{"text":"The only speed on the list is the tendon's, so it is the Doppler measurement."}],"trap":"Assuming Doppler is only for blood: any moving reflector, such as a tendon or heart wall, shifts the echo frequency."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what each probe measured**.","marks":[{"t":"pulse-echo images gave the width of the popliteal artery, its depth below the skin and the thickness of the muscle layer","note":"distances"},{"t":"a second probe tracked how fast the Achilles tendon slid","note":"a speed"}]},{"text":"**Two ultrasound modes.** Echo time → distance. Frequency shift → speed of the reflector."},{"text":"**Also:** a reflector moving toward the probe returns a higher frequency; moving away, a lower one."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Distance or position → pulse-echo; speed of something moving → Doppler."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-56a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:56"],"pid":"sample-cp:P10","passage":"Blood flow through a calf muscle can be measured by injecting a small dose of xenon-133 in saline into the muscle and timing how fast the blood washes the tracer out. Each xenon-133 nucleus decays by β⁻ emission to cesium-133, as a neutron in the nucleus turns into a proton, and a gamma ray released after the decay is counted by a detector held over the calf. Before the study, a hospital physicist checked one vial in a calibrated counter: it read 360 MBq on delivery, 180 MBq after 5.2 days, 90 MBq after 10.4 days and 45 MBq after 15.6 days. During each session, a volunteer pushed a foot-pedal ergometer at a steady 12 W for 4.0 minutes while the washout was recorded. Ultrasound was also used during each visit: pulse-echo images gave the width of the popliteal artery, its depth below the skin and the thickness of the muscle layer, while a second probe tracked how fast the Achilles tendon slid during each push.","q":"What total work did each volunteer do on the pedal during one bout?","choices":["0.050 J","20 J","48 J","2880 J"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That multiplies watts by minutes; a watt is a joule per second, so time must be in seconds."},{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That divides power by time; work is power multiplied by time."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That divides time by power, which is not an energy at all."}],"rule":"Work = power × time, with power in W (J/s) and time in seconds. Convert minutes first.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Units: W = J/s, so J = W × s. Turn 4.0 min into 240 s, then 12 × 240."},"exp":"Power is work per time, so **W = P·t**. The volunteer delivered a steady **12 W** for **4.0 min = 240 s**: W = (12 J/s)(240 s) = **2880 J** (about 2.9 kJ).","how":{"ask":"Combine the power and the time the passage gives into work, in joules.","know":"**P = W/t**, so **W = P·t**. 1 W = 1 J/s, so time must be in **seconds** to give joules.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the power and the time: a steady 12 W for 4.0 minutes.","hl":"a steady 12 W for 4.0 minutes"},{"text":"Concept: work = power × time in seconds. Using minutes gives a number 60 times too small.","calc":["4.0 min = 240 s"],"strike":["48 J"]},{"text":"Multiply, do not divide.","calc":["W = (12 J/s)(240 s)"],"strike":["0.050 J","20 J"]}],"trap":"Multiplying watts by minutes: a watt is a joule per second."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the power and the time**.","marks":[{"t":"a steady 12 W for 4.0 minutes","note":"P and t"}]},{"text":"**Convert time.** 4.0 min × 60 s/min = 240 s."},{"text":"**Multiply.** W = P·t.","calc":["(12 W)(240 s) = 2880 J"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Work = power × time in seconds; check that W × s gives J."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-57a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:57"],"q":"A cell-free reaction must be held at pH 5.5. Which weak acid, identified by its pKa, would make the best buffer?","choices":["3.5","5.6","7.0","8.5"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is 14 minus the target pH, a pOH mix-up; the buffer pKa should match the pH itself."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That pKa sits two units below the target, outside the ±1 range where a buffer works well."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Neutral pH has nothing special for buffering; capacity peaks where pH equals the pKa."}],"rule":"Best buffer: pKa within 1 unit of the target pH, and the closer the better.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the pKa nearest the target pH; anything more than 1 unit away is out."},"exp":"A buffer resists pH change best when **pH ≈ pKa**, where [HA] = [A⁻]. Only a pKa within ±1 of 5.5 works, and **5.6** is the closest.","how":{"ask":"Choose the pKa closest to the target pH.","know":"**Henderson–Hasselbalch:** pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]). Maximum capacity at pH = pKa; useful range pKa ± 1.","steps":[{"text":"Target pH.","hl":"pH 5.5"},{"text":"Keep only pKa values within one unit of it.","strike":["3.5","7.0","8.5"]},{"text":"The remaining choice is nearly equal to the target."}],"trap":"Picking a neutral pKa because the system is biological: match the stated pH, not 7."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the target pH**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 5.5","note":"want pKa near this"}]},{"text":"**Equal acid and base forms** sit at pH = pKa, where added acid or base is absorbed best."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Buffer pKa ≈ target pH (within ±1)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-58a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:58"],"q":"A 0.40 kg aluminum block absorbs 7200 J of heat while warming from 22 °C to 42 °C. What is the specific heat of aluminum?","choices":["144 J/(kg·°C)","360 J/(kg·°C)","429 J/(kg·°C)","900 J/(kg·°C)"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That multiplies by the mass instead of dividing by it; c = q ÷ (m·ΔT)."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That divides by ΔT but leaves out the mass of the block."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That uses the final temperature, 42 °C, instead of the change in temperature."}],"rule":"c = q ÷ (m × ΔT), with ΔT = final − initial. Check units: J ÷ (kg·°C).","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Let J/(kg·°C) guide you: divide joules by kilograms and by degrees of change. 0.40 × 20 = 8."},"exp":"**q = mcΔT**, so c = q/(mΔT). ΔT = 42 − 22 = 20 °C. c = 7200 J ÷ (0.40 kg × 20 °C) = 7200 ÷ 8 = **900 J/(kg·°C)**.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange q = mcΔT for c and use the temperature change.","know":"**q = mcΔT**; ΔT = T_final − T_initial.","steps":[{"text":"Find ΔT.","hl":"from 22 °C to 42 °C","calc":["ΔT = 42 − 22 = 20 °C"]},{"text":"Divide q by (m × ΔT).","calc":["c = 7200 ÷ (0.40 × 20) = 7200 ÷ 8"]},{"text":"Multiplying by m, dropping m, or using 42 °C give the other values.","strike":["144 J/(kg·°C)","360 J/(kg·°C)","429 J/(kg·°C)"]}],"trap":"Plugging in a temperature reading instead of the change: specific heat uses ΔT."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find q, m and the two temperatures**.","marks":[{"t":"7200 J","note":"q"},{"t":"0.40 kg","note":"m"},{"t":"from 22 °C to 42 °C","note":"ΔT = 20 °C"}]},{"text":"**Rearrange and divide.**","calc":["c = q/(mΔT) = 7200/(0.40 × 20)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c = q/(m·ΔT); the units J/(kg·°C) show exactly what to divide by."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-59a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:59"],"q":"In strongly acidic solution, the keto acid oxalosuccinate (three carboxylate groups, all ionized) is reduced by NADPH to neutral isocitric acid, with NADP⁺ formed. How many H⁺ must be added to the left side to balance the equation?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That covers only the new alcohol H; each carboxylate in the neutral acid also needs its own proton."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That treats NADP⁺ as neutral; it carries +1, so the right side is +1, not 0."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That uses a −1 substrate, as for pyruvate; this keto acid carries three carboxylates, so it is −3."}],"rule":"Balance H⁺ by charge: (substrate charge) + x = +1 for NAD(P)⁺ plus a neutral product; count every carboxylate.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Charge only: left −3 + x, right +1 (NADP⁺) + 0 (neutral acid). Solve for x."},"exp":"Charge check: left = −3 (oxalosuccinate) + 0 (NADPH) + x; right = 0 (isocitric acid) + 1 (NADP⁺). So −3 + x = +1 and **x = 4**. Hydrogen check: one H arrives as hydride from NADPH; free H⁺ supply the rest: one finishes the alcohol and three protonate the carboxylates.","how":{"ask":"Find the H⁺ coefficient that balances charge (and hydrogen).","know":"NADPH → NADP⁺ + H⁻ (hydride). **NADPH is neutral, NADP⁺ is +1.** A neutral carboxylic acid needs every carboxylate protonated.","steps":[{"text":"Charges on each side.","hl":"three carboxylate groups, all ionized","calc":["left: −3 + x","right: 0 + 1 = +1"]},{"text":"Solve for x; forgetting the +1 on NADP⁺ gives a value one too small.","calc":["−3 + x = +1 → x = 4"],"strike":["Three"]},{"text":"Check H: hydride gives one H; protons must finish the alcohol and all three carboxylates, so one or two H⁺ fall short.","strike":["One","Two"]}],"trap":"Treating the substrate as −1 like pyruvate, or forgetting that NADP⁺ is +1."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **note the charges** on every species.","marks":[{"t":"three carboxylate groups, all ionized","note":"−3"},{"t":"neutral isocitric acid","note":"0"},{"t":"NADP⁺","note":"+1"}]},{"text":"**Balance charge.**","calc":["−3 + x = +1"]},{"text":"**Confirm with H.** Hydride from NADPH reduces C=O; protons finish the alcohol and every carboxylate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** With NAD(P)H → NAD(P)⁺, add H⁺ until the charges on both sides match; count every carboxylate, then double-check H atoms."}]}
];
